window.PLAIN = {"scenarios": [{"id": "s1", "title": "Scenario 1 — Customer Support Resolution Agent", "domains": "D1 · D2 · D5", "brief": "A meal-kit company runs a customer support agent on the Claude Agent SDK. Its MCP tools are <code>get_customer</code>, <code>lookup_order</code>, <code>process_refund</code>, <code>escalate_to_human</code> and <code>pause_subscription</code>. The agent may refund up to $150 per box; larger refunds go to a human."}, {"id": "s2", "title": "Scenario 2 — Code Generation with Claude Code", "domains": "D3 · D4 · D5", "brief": "A startup uses Claude Code in one TypeScript repository with <code>server/</code>, <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/</code> folders. The repository commits a <code>.claude/</code> directory and a root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Engineers use Claude Code to generate endpoints, screens and tests from tickets."}, {"id": "s3", "title": "Scenario 3 — Multi-Agent Research System", "domains": "D1 · D2 · D5", "brief": "A hospital's evidence team runs a research system on the Claude Agent SDK. A coordinator agent hands work to subagents through the Task tool: literature search, trial-registry search, document analysis, citation checking, synthesis and reporting. Journals and the trial registry are reached over MCP. Summaries go to guideline committees, and every claim must cite a paper and section."}, {"id": "s4", "title": "Scenario 4 — Developer Productivity with Claude", "domains": "D2 · D3 · D1", "brief": "A language-learning company's platform team uses Claude Code on a large Ruby on Rails app with a React front end. Engineers read and change code with the built-in tools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep and Glob. MCP servers connect Claude to Linear and a staging Postgres database. Some engineers also script Claude with the Agent SDK for internal tools."}, {"id": "s5", "title": "Scenario 5 — Claude Code for Continuous Integration", "domains": "D3 · D4 · D2", "brief": "A travel-booking company runs Claude Code in <b>GitHub Actions</b> on a Kotlin and TypeScript repository. It has four jobs: a pull-request review that posts inline comments, an autofix job for failing builds, test generation for new endpoints, and a weekly dependency-risk report. The review job uses an MCP server for code search and API guidelines. <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and <code>.claude/</code> are committed."}, {"id": "s6", "title": "Scenario 6 — Structured Data Extraction", "domains": "D4 · D5 · D1", "brief": "An insurance broker extracts data from certificates of insurance sent by many carriers. Claude returns the insured name, carrier, policy number, limits, deductibles and dates through a tool with a JSON schema. A validator checks each result before it is posted to the broker's system."}], "questions": [{"id": "m7p-s1-01", "was": "m7-s1-01", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Agentic loop and stop_reason", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's loop ends the turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block. Claude often writes a short line, such as “Let me check that box,” in the same response where it asks to call <code>lookup_order</code>. In those chats the lookup never runs. The customer waits for an answer that never comes. Which change to the loop <b>fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Tell Claude in the system prompt not to write any text in a response that calls a tool.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude would write fewer of those lines, but the loop still ends on text, so any line that slips through stops the lookup again.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> on the first request after each customer message.", "why": "Works, but wrong lever. A forced tool call carries no text, so this case stops, but Claude must now call a tool even when it should just answer.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Branch on <code>stop_reason</code>: run the tools and loop on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Best. A response can hold text and a tool request together. stop_reason says what comes next: tool_use means run the tools and continue, end_turn means the turn is over."}, {"t": "When a turn ends without an answer, send Claude a short “please continue” message.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. The nudge restarts the stalled chat, but the loop still ends early, and every stall costs an extra request.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The agent's loop ends the turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block.", "explanation": "The loop should be driven by stop_reason: continue on tool_use, stop on end_turn. Checking for text in the response is an anti-pattern, because text and tool calls can arrive together.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude says “Let me check that box” and asks for a lookup in the same breath. The loop hears the words and hangs up before the lookup runs. The clue: the loop decides by looking for text.", "answer": "Imagine a waiter who walks away as soon as you start talking, even when you were in the middle of ordering. The fix is to wait for a clear signal: “that's all”. Each Claude response carries that signal, called <b>stop_reason</b>. <b>Rule: <code>tool_use</code> → run the tool and keep going. <code>end_turn</code> → stop.</b>", "options": ["Asking Claude to stay quiet helps, but the waiter still walks off at the first word.", "Forcing a tool call stops the chatter, but now Claude must call a tool even when it should just reply.", "Correct. Listen for the real “that's all” signal.", "Calling the waiter back each time works, but he keeps walking off."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude talks too much</b> and should be told to be quiet. But the text is harmless. The bug is in the loop: it treats any text as the end of the turn. The API already says whether the turn is over, in <code>stop_reason</code>.", "diagram": "response = text + tool_use\n  stop_reason = tool_use → run tool, send result, loop\n  stop_reason = end_turn → turn over, reply to customer", "mapTitle": "What should end an agent's turn?", "map": {"head": ["Signal", "Use it to stop?", "Why"], "rows": [["<code>stop_reason == \"end_turn\"</code>", "Yes", "The API's own signal that Claude is done"], ["<code>stop_reason == \"tool_use\"</code>", "No: run tools and continue", "Claude is waiting for results"], ["Response contains text", "No", "Text and tool calls can come together"], ["Fixed number of rounds", "Only as a safety cap", "It cuts off work that is not finished"], ["Phrases like “done” in the reply", "No", "Parsing words is fragile"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent's loop ends the turn whenever Claude's response contains a text block", "means": "The decider. The stop rule looks at content, not at the signal. A prompt rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a nudge (<b>[[3]]</b>) leave that rule in place."}, {"quote": "Claude often writes a short line, such as “Let me check that box,” in the same response where it asks to call lookup_order", "means": "Text and a tool request arrive together. That is normal, so the loop must handle it."}, {"quote": "In those chats the lookup never runs", "means": "Claude did ask for the tool. Forcing tool calls (<b>[[1]]</b>) answers a different problem: Claude not asking."}, {"quote": "Which change to the loop fixes this?", "means": "Change the stop rule to stop_reason: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Loop on stop_reason: tool_use → run tools and continue; end_turn → stop. Never stop because text appeared.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops", "quote": "Continue the loop when stop_reason == \"tool_use\"; terminate when \"end_turn\"."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-02", "was": "m7-s1-02", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Explicit context passing to subagents", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator sends refund eligibility questions to a policy subagent with the Task tool. The prompt it sends reads: “Check whether this customer's claim is eligible.” The subagent's verdicts are vague and often hedge. The facts it needs came up earlier in the chat: which items spoiled, when the box arrived, and what the customer wants. Which change <b>best improves the verdicts</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Spell out the spoiled items, the arrival day and the remedy wanted in each Task call.", "why": "Best. A subagent does not see the coordinator's chat. Whatever it needs must be written into the prompt it receives, and the coordinator already has these facts."}, {"t": "Give the policy subagent read-only <code>get_customer</code> and <code>lookup_order</code> tools.", "why": "Works, but only in part. The tools would return the order and its arrival day, but which items spoiled and what the customer wants exist only in the chat.", "trap": "only part of the facts"}, {"t": "Add the refund policy and two worked verdicts to the policy subagent's system prompt.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Policy text and examples improve how the subagent reasons, but it still does not know this customer's case.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Forward the whole chat transcript to the policy subagent with each Task call.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The facts would be in there, but buried among greetings and side talk. A short, chosen summary of the case is cheaper and clearer.", "trap": "too much context"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The facts it needs came up earlier in the chat", "explanation": "Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's conversation. The coordinator must pass the facts the subagent needs, explicitly and in a focused form, in the Task prompt.", "eli5": {"question": "A manager asks a specialist “Is this claim OK?” without saying what the claim is. The clue: the facts are only in the manager's head (the chat).", "answer": "A specialist you phone has not heard your earlier conversation with the customer. You have to tell them: “two salmon packs spoiled, box came Tuesday, customer wants a refund.” A <b>subagent</b> is that specialist, and the <b>Task prompt</b> is the phone call. <b>Rule: a subagent knows only what you put in its prompt.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Tell the specialist the facts of the case.", "Letting the specialist look things up finds the order, but not what the customer said.", "A better rulebook helps the specialist judge, but they still don't know the case.", "Forwarding the whole call recording works, but they must dig through it to find the facts."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the subagent knows what the coordinator knows</b>. It does not. Each subagent starts with only its own system prompt and the prompt it is sent. Facts that came up in the chat reach it only if the coordinator writes them in.", "diagram": "chat: spoiled salmon, Tuesday, wants refund\n  coordinator ─ Task prompt ─→ policy subagent\n  (facts must be IN the prompt; nothing is inherited)", "mapTitle": "A subagent gives weak answers. What is missing?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Vague, hedged verdicts; facts were in the chat", "Facts not passed", "Write the facts into the Task prompt"], ["Facts live in backend records", "Subagent can't reach them", "Give it scoped read-only tools"], ["Facts are present but the reasoning is off", "Weak instructions", "Policy text and examples in its system prompt"], ["Prompt is huge and the answer misses key points", "Too much raw context", "Pass a focused summary of the case"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The prompt it sends reads: “Check whether this customer's claim is eligible.”", "means": "The prompt names no facts. The subagent has nothing to judge."}, {"quote": "The subagent's verdicts are vague and often hedge", "means": "That is what a reasoner does with no facts. Better policy text (<b>[[2]]</b>) doesn't add any."}, {"quote": "The facts it needs came up earlier in the chat", "means": "The decider. Only the coordinator has them, so tools (<b>[[1]]</b>) can't fetch the customer's words."}, {"quote": "Which change best improves the verdicts?", "means": "Pass the chosen facts, not the whole transcript (<b>[[3]]</b>): <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Subagents inherit nothing. Put the facts they need into the Task prompt, in a focused form.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt (no automatic inheritance / shared memory)."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-03", "was": "m7-s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Prompt guidance vs programmatic enforcement", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Customers who want to cancel are handed to a person through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Product would like the agent to suggest pausing the subscription first, before it hands the customer over. This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat. What is the <b>most appropriate</b> way to add it?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt instruction to suggest a pause, plus two short example chats.", "why": "Best. A soft behaviour, where an occasional miss does no harm, is what prompt guidance is for. One instruction and two examples are easy to maintain."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>escalate_to_human</code> until a pause has been offered.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The hook would make the offer happen every time, but it needs code to spot cancellations and track what was said. Guarantees are for business rules.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add the pause offer to the <code>escalate_to_human</code> description, so Claude offers it before calling the tool.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Claude does read tool descriptions, but they explain when and how to call a tool. How to talk to the customer belongs in the system prompt, where it is easy to find and change.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Route every cancellation request to a new retention subagent before it reaches a human.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A retention subagent could make the offer well, but it adds a handoff and another prompt to maintain, for one sentence of behaviour.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat.", "explanation": "Programmatic enforcement is for steps that must happen every time. A best-effort suggestion is shaped with a clear instruction plus a few examples.", "eli5": {"question": "Product wants the agent to ask “would you like to pause instead?” before it hands a cancellation to a person. The clue: it's a retention idea, not a business rule.", "answer": "A shop asks staff to mention the loyalty card at the till. A note and a quick demo do the job; nobody installs a till that locks until the card is mentioned. Here the note is a <b>system-prompt instruction</b> and the demo is <b>example chats</b>. <b>Rule: must happen every time → code. Nice to have → prompt and examples.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A plain instruction plus examples sets the nudge.", "A lock in code (a <b>hook</b>) is for things that must never be skipped. Here skipping is allowed.", "Tool instructions would work, but they're the wrong place for how to talk to customers.", "A whole new helper agent for one friendly sentence is too much."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the most reliable option is always the best one</b>. The question asks for the most <i>appropriate</i> one. Code-level enforcement (a hook) is right when a step must happen every time, such as checking identity before a refund. For a nice-to-have it is extra machinery: something has to detect cancellations and remember what was said.", "diagram": "Must it happen every time?\n  yes → hook or prerequisite gate (code)\n  no  → prompt instruction + examples", "mapTitle": "How firmly must this behaviour hold?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A suggestion or tone; an occasional miss is harmless", "System-prompt instruction + a few examples", "Cheap and easy to change"], ["A business rule; a miss costs money or breaks policy", "Hook or prerequisite gate in code", "Prompts have a non-zero failure rate"], ["Only some customers may get a soft offer, under a written policy", "A tool that checks the policy in code, plus a prompt for the wording", "Code decides who; a subagent would still be a model guessing"], ["The soft step grows into a long negotiation with its own tools", "A dedicated subagent", "Its own context and prompt now earn their cost"], ["Sensitive data must never reach the model", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook that strips it", "The model can't repeat what it never saw"], ["Guidance on when and how to call a tool", "The tool's description", "Claude reads it when choosing tools"], ["Instructions are clear but output still varies", "Few-shot examples", "Shows the pattern"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Product would like the agent to suggest pausing the subscription first, before it hands the customer over", "means": "The goal is a suggestion in the conversation. That belongs in Claude's instructions, not in a tool's description (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "This is a retention idea, not a business rule, so the agent does not have to do it in every chat", "means": "The decider. Missing it now and then is harmless, so a hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a new subagent (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more than the goal needs."}, {"quote": "What is the most appropriate way to add it?", "means": "“Most appropriate” means the right size: an instruction with examples, <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Business rule → enforce it in code. Suggestion or style → prompt instruction plus examples.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Programmatic enforcement (hooks, prerequisite gates) vs prompt-based guidance."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-04", "was": "m7-s1-04", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 PostToolUse hooks for transformation", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "<code>get_customer</code> returns the full card number on file, and the team that owns that server will not change it. Twice, the agent read a card number to a customer. Security says this must never happen. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to mention cards by their last four digits only, with two examples.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear rule with examples cuts the leaks, but prompts have a non-zero failure rate, and this is a must-never.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that masks the card field in results before Claude reads them.", "why": "Best. The hook changes the result before Claude reads it, so the agent can't repeat a number it never saw. The server stays as it is."}, {"t": "Route <code>get_customer</code> through a subagent told to pass on only the name, plan and box IDs.", "why": "Works, but still a model choosing. The subagent sees the full number and decides what to pass on; its instructions are not a filter.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Scan each reply for card-number patterns and block the reply before it reaches the customer.", "why": "Works, but only for the formats it knows. A number read out with spaces or in parts slips past, and Claude still has the number.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Security says this must never happen.", "explanation": "When a tool returns data the model must never use, change the result with a PostToolUse hook before the model sees it. Prompt rules only make misuse less likely.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent sometimes reads a customer's card number out loud. The clue: security says this must <i>never</i> happen.", "answer": "If a receptionist must never read out card numbers, don't hand them the number: black it out on the paper first. A <b>PostToolUse hook</b> is that black marker. It edits each tool result before Claude reads it, and Claude can't repeat what it never saw. <b>Rule: if the model must never use some data, remove it before the model sees it.</b>", "options": ["A written rule usually works, but “usually” isn't enough for a must-never.", "Correct. Black out the number before Claude ever sees it.", "A helper told to pass on only some fields still sees the number, and might pass it on.", "Checking each reply catches the obvious cases, but Claude still knows the number."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a firm instruction stops the model saying something</b>. It lowers the chance, but prompt compliance is probabilistic. For data that must never reach a customer, the reliable place to act is the tool result itself: change it before the model reads it.", "diagram": "get_customer → [PostToolUse: mask card] → Claude\nserver unchanged        model never sees the number", "mapTitle": "Which hook, and where does it act?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Mechanism", "Where it acts"], "rows": [["Model must never see or repeat a field", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook masks it", "On the result, before Claude reads it"], ["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook denies the call", "On the call, before the tool runs"], ["Messy result formats (dates, codes)", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook normalises them", "On the result"], ["A wording or tone preference", "Prompt instruction + examples", "In Claude's instructions"], ["You own the tool and nobody needs the field", "Stop returning it", "In the tool itself"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "get_customer returns the full card number on file, and the team that owns that server will not change it", "means": "The fix has to happen on the agent's side, between the tool and Claude."}, {"quote": "Twice, the agent read a card number to a customer", "means": "The number reaches Claude in ordinary chats. Scanning replies (<b>[[3]]</b>) catches only the patterns it knows."}, {"quote": "Security says this must never happen", "means": "The decider: a must-never. A prompt rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a subagent told what to pass on (<b>[[2]]</b>) both still rely on the model choosing."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Change the result before Claude sees it: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model must never use some data? Strip it from the tool result with a PostToolUse hook.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-01", "was": "m7-s3-01", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Loop termination on stop_reason", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The team wrote its own loop for the coordinator. After each response, the loop looks for a text block. If it finds one, it treats the run as finished and sends that text to the report subagent. Many runs now stop after the first search. In those runs, Claude wrote a short sentence and also asked for more Task calls in the same response. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep looping while <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code>, and end the run when it is <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Best. <code>stop_reason</code> is the API's own signal for whether Claude wants tools run. A response can hold a sentence and tool calls together, and <code>tool_use</code> says the work is not done."}, {"t": "End the run only when a response has text and no <code>tool_use</code> blocks in it.", "why": "Works, but it is still a check on the content. It handles this case, but it guesses at what <code>stop_reason</code> already says, and other stop reasons (such as hitting <code>max_tokens</code>) can look finished too.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Tell the coordinator in its system prompt to write no text in a response that calls tools.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude will mostly follow it, but the loop still trusts text as the finish signal, so one stray sentence ends a run early again.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> until the report subagent has run, then set it back to <code>auto</code>.", "why": "Works, but it fixes a different problem. Forcing a tool call stops Claude replying in text, but Claude was already calling tools here. The loop was ignoring them.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "In those runs, Claude wrote a short sentence and also asked for more Task calls in the same response.", "explanation": "The agentic loop continues while stop_reason is tool_use and ends on end_turn. Checking the reply for text is an unreliable finish signal, because Claude often writes a sentence beside its tool calls.", "eli5": {"question": "The loop decides a run is over as soon as Claude writes any words. The clue: Claude wrote a sentence <i>and</i> asked for more searches in the same reply.", "answer": "Imagine a waiter who clears the table the moment you say anything, even “mmm, and could I have dessert?”. The better waiter waits for you to say “that's all”. The API gives that signal on every reply: a field called <b><code>stop_reason</code></b>. <code>tool_use</code> means “please run these”, <code>end_turn</code> means “I'm done”. <b>Rule: the loop reads <code>stop_reason</code>, not the words.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Listen for “that's all” (<code>end_turn</code>), not for any words at all.", "Closer, but it is still the waiter guessing from what you say instead of the clear signal.", "Asking diners to stay silent while ordering mostly works, but one chatty diner still gets the table cleared.", "Forcing a tool call helps when Claude talks instead of acting. Here it was already acting."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>text in the reply means the answer is ready</b>. But Claude often writes a sentence and asks for tools in the same response. The API already says which case it is: <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code> when tools are wanted and <code>end_turn</code> when Claude is finished.", "diagram": "response: \"Searching the registry next.\" + Task call\n  text check      → stop  (wrong)\n  stop_reason     → tool_use → run Task, loop again", "mapTitle": "How should an agent loop decide it is done?", "map": {"head": ["Approach", "Verdict", "Why"], "rows": [["Read <code>stop_reason</code>: continue on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>", "Correct", "It is the API's own signal"], ["Stop when the reply has text and no tool blocks", "Fragile", "Still guesses from content"], ["Stop when the reply contains a phrase like “done”", "Anti-pattern", "Parses natural language"], ["Stop after a fixed number of turns", "Anti-pattern as the main stop", "Cuts long runs short"], ["Claude replies in text when a tool call is needed", "Different problem", "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "After each response, the loop looks for a text block", "means": "The loop's finish test is “is there text?”. The guide calls that an anti-pattern."}, {"quote": "Many runs now stop after the first search", "means": "The symptom: runs end too early, so the finish test is wrong."}, {"quote": "Claude wrote a short sentence and also asked for more Task calls in the same response", "means": "The decider. Claude did call tools, so <code>tool_choice</code> (<b>[[3]]</b>) is not needed, and a prompt asking for no text (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the faulty test in place."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Replace the content test with the API's signal: <b>[[0]]</b>. A tighter content test (<b>[[1]]</b>) is still a guess."}], "rule": "An agent loop continues on stop_reason tool_use and ends on end_turn. Never decide from the text.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "checking assistant text content as a completion indicator"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-02", "was": "m7-s3-02", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Per-request context in the Task prompt", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Each committee sets its own inclusion rules when it sends a request, such as a minimum trial size or drug classes to leave out. The coordinator agrees these rules with the committee before it delegates. Later, the analysis subagent includes studies the committee excluded. Its Task prompt names only the drug and the outcome. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "List each committee's usual rules in the analysis subagent's system prompt, by committee.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. A fixed system prompt can hold rules that rarely change, but these are agreed per request, so the list goes stale and misses one-off rules.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Add a check step after analysis that removes studies the committee's rules exclude.", "why": "Works, but too late. A filter afterwards can drop the wrong studies, but the analysis has already been done on them, and the filter still needs the same rules passed to it.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Save the agreed rules to a file for each request and tell the subagent to read it.", "why": "Works, but more moving parts. The subagent can read the file, but its Task prompt must still name the file, so you may as well put the rules in the prompt.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Write the agreed rules into each analysis Task prompt, beside the drug and the outcome.", "why": "Best. Subagents do not see the coordinator's conversation. Anything agreed for one request must be written into that request's Task prompt."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its Task prompt names only the drug and the outcome.", "explanation": "Subagents have isolated context and do not inherit what the coordinator discussed. Per-request details must be passed explicitly in the Task prompt; fixed role guidance belongs in the AgentDefinition.", "eli5": {"question": "The coordinator agrees the rules with the committee, but the analysis helper never hears them. The clue: the helper's instructions name only the drug and the outcome.", "answer": "A manager agrees special requests with a client on the phone, then hands a junior a sticky note that says only “Report on drug X”. The junior wasn't on the call. Put the special requests on the note. For a subagent, the note is the <b>Task prompt</b>; it does not hear the coordinator's conversation. <b>Rule: if a subagent needs it for this request, write it into the Task prompt.</b>", "options": ["A standing list on the junior's desk helps with usual requests, but not with what was agreed on today's call.", "Checking the work afterwards catches some mistakes, but the junior still worked without the rules.", "A separate file works, but the note must point to it anyway. Just write the rules on the note.", "Correct. Write today's rules on today's note."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the subagent knows what the coordinator knows</b>. It doesn't. A subagent starts with its own system prompt and the Task prompt, nothing else. Rules that change with each request can only reach it through that Task prompt.", "diagram": "coordinator ↔ committee: agree rules\n  Task prompt: drug + outcome        → rules lost\n  Task prompt: drug + outcome + rules → applied", "mapTitle": "Where should information for a subagent go?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of information", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Details for this one request", "The Task prompt", "Subagents don't inherit the coordinator's context"], ["Role guidance the same on every run", "The subagent's AgentDefinition system prompt", "Loaded every time"], ["Findings from an earlier subagent", "Passed in full in the next Task prompt", "No shared memory"], ["Wrong studies already included", "A later filter", "Cleans up, but too late"], ["Facts that must survive a long chat", "Case-facts block", "Protects the coordinator's own context"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each committee sets its own inclusion rules when it sends a request", "means": "The rules change per request, so a fixed system prompt (<b>[[0]]</b>) is the wrong home."}, {"quote": "The coordinator agrees these rules with the committee before it delegates", "means": "The rules live only in the coordinator's context."}, {"quote": "Later, the analysis subagent includes studies the committee excluded", "means": "The subagent never got the rules. A filter afterwards (<b>[[1]]</b>) treats the result, not the cause."}, {"quote": "Its Task prompt names only the drug and the outcome", "means": "The decider. The delegation is missing the rules. Put them there: <b>[[3]]</b>. A file (<b>[[2]]</b>) still has to be named in this prompt."}], "rule": "Subagents don't inherit context. Per-request details go in the Task prompt.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-03", "was": "m7-s3-03", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Parallel subagent spawning", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "For each request, the coordinator runs three searches: journals, the trial registry and regulatory notices. No search needs another's results. Today the coordinator sends one Task call, waits for its result, then sends the next. Committees say summaries take too long, and most of the time goes on waiting for these searches. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut the wait?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator skip any search that a request does not seem to need.", "why": "Works, but only on some requests. Skipping a search saves time when it is not needed, but most requests still wait for three searches one after another.", "trap": "only helps some requests"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator send all three Task calls in one response and run them together.", "why": "Best. The searches don't depend on each other, so they can run at the same time. The coordinator does this by sending several Task calls in one response."}, {"t": "Give each search subagent a shorter time limit, returning partial results when it runs out.", "why": "Works, but trades away quality. Each search ends sooner, but some return less evidence, and they still run one after another.", "trap": "cuts quality"}, {"t": "Have each search subagent return only the fields the coordinator needs from its results.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Smaller results save context and a little time, but the wait comes from running the searches in series.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No search needs another's results.", "explanation": "Independent subtasks should run in parallel: the coordinator emits several Task calls in one response. Skipping or trimming searches helps only around the edges.", "eli5": {"question": "Three searches run one after another, and nothing links them. The clue: no search needs another's results.", "answer": "Three friends each need to buy one thing in three different shops. Sending them one at a time, each waiting for the last to come back, is slow. Send all three at once. For the coordinator, “send all at once” means putting <b>several Task calls in one response</b>. <b>Rule: independent subagents run in parallel; send their Task calls together.</b>", "options": ["Skipping a shop you don't need helps sometimes, but the usual trip still has three shops, one after another.", "Correct. Send all three friends at once.", "Telling each friend to hurry gets them back sooner, with less shopping done.", "Smaller shopping bags are lighter, but the friends still go one at a time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the searches themselves are too slow</b>. But each search waits for the one before it to finish, even though none of them needs the others. The quickest gain is to run them at the same time, not to make each one smaller or shorter.", "diagram": "today:  journals ──▶ registry ──▶ notices\nbetter: journals ─┐\n        registry ─┼─▶ coordinator\n        notices ──┘", "mapTitle": "A multi-agent run is slow. Where does the time go?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Independent subagents run one after another", "Serial delegation", "Several Task calls in one response"], ["Requests often run subagents they don't need", "Full pipeline every time", "Select subagents by request"], ["Coordinator context fills with long results", "Verbose outputs", "Trim results to needed fields"], ["Many round trips for simple lookups", "Every check routed through the coordinator", "A scoped tool for the common case"], ["One step needs another's result", "Real dependency", "Keep that step in order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "For each request, the coordinator runs three searches", "means": "All three searches are part of the normal run, so skipping some (<b>[[0]]</b>) helps only now and then."}, {"quote": "No search needs another's results", "means": "The decider. Nothing forces them to run in order, so they can run in parallel."}, {"quote": "Today the coordinator sends one Task call, waits for its result, then sends the next", "means": "The cause: serial delegation."}, {"quote": "most of the time goes on waiting for these searches", "means": "The wait is the searches running in a row. Time limits (<b>[[2]]</b>) and smaller results (<b>[[3]]</b>) trim the edges."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to cut the wait?", "means": "Run them together: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Independent subagents? Emit all their Task calls in one response so they run in parallel.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "spawn parallel subagents by emitting multiple Task calls in one response"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-04", "was": "m7-s3-04", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Programmatic prerequisite gate", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator publishes each summary by calling <code>publish_summary</code>. Policy says no summary may reach a committee until the citation-check subagent has passed every citation in it. In a few runs, the coordinator publishes before the check has run. Each of those runs is a reportable compliance incident. Which change <b>best meets the policy</b>?", "options": [{"t": "State in the coordinator's prompt that publishing must wait for a passing citation check.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear instruction cuts early publishes, but prompts have a non-zero failure rate, and each miss is an incident.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run the citation check in a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook on <code>publish_summary</code> and recall failures.", "why": "Works, but too late. The hook runs after the call, so a bad summary has already reached the committee before it is recalled.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Make <code>publish_summary</code> refuse any summary with no passing citation check on record.", "why": "Best. A prerequisite gate in code blocks the publish until the check has passed, on every run, with no person in the loop."}, {"t": "Hold every summary for a staff member to approve before <code>publish_summary</code> sends it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A person can stop early publishes, but it adds a manual step to every run for a check that code can enforce.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each of those runs is a reportable compliance incident.", "explanation": "When one step must happen before another every time, enforce it in code: block the downstream tool until the prerequisite has a recorded result. Prompt instructions have a non-zero failure rate.", "eli5": {"question": "Summaries sometimes go out before their citations are checked. The clue: each early one is a compliance incident, so it must never happen.", "answer": "A post room has a rule: no letter leaves without a stamp from the checker. Reminding staff mostly works. A slot that won't take a letter without the stamp works every time. In code, that slot is a <b>prerequisite gate</b>: <code>publish_summary</code> refuses until a passing check is on record. <b>Rule: must happen every time → enforce it in code, not in the prompt.</b>", "options": ["A reminder mostly works, but “mostly” still means incidents.", "Checking letters after they're posted means some wrong ones already arrived.", "Correct. The slot won't accept a letter with no stamp.", "A person at the door works, but it slows every letter for a check a machine can do."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a firm instruction is enough</b> when the rule is clear. The guide says prompt instructions have a non-zero failure rate. When every miss is a compliance incident, the order must be enforced in code: the publish tool itself refuses until the check has passed.", "diagram": "publish_summary(draft)\n  check passed on record? ── no  → refuse\n                          └ yes → publish", "mapTitle": "How firmly must this step order hold?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A miss breaks policy or costs money", "Prerequisite gate or hook in code", "Holds on every run"], ["A nice-to-have; an occasional miss is harmless", "Prompt instruction plus examples", "Cheap and easy to change"], ["The problem is seen only after the action", "<code>PostToolUse</code> check", "Catches it, but too late"], ["A judgement code can't make", "Human approval", "Costly; use it where needed"], ["One tool must run before another inside a request", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code>", "Sets the first call"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Policy says no summary may reach a committee until the citation-check subagent has passed every citation in it", "means": "The check must come before publishing. A check after the call (<b>[[1]]</b>) is too late."}, {"quote": "In a few runs, the coordinator publishes before the check has run", "means": "The model sometimes gets the order wrong, which is exactly what prompt rules (<b>[[0]]</b>) cannot fully stop."}, {"quote": "Each of those runs is a reportable compliance incident", "means": "The decider. The order must hold every time, so enforce it in code."}, {"quote": "Which change best meets the policy?", "means": "A gate on the tool: <b>[[2]]</b>. A human approver (<b>[[3]]</b>) works but is far more than the rule needs."}], "rule": "A step that must come first every time → block the later tool in code until it has.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Block downstream tool calls until prerequisites complete"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-05", "was": "m7-s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Fixed pipeline vs adaptive decomposition", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Every evidence summary follows the committee template. It has four sections: efficacy, safety, cost and guideline status. Each section is researched the same way every time, and no section depends on what another finds. Auditors must be able to replay each run step by step. The team is redesigning how the coordinator splits up the work. Which approach <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Chain a fixed set of steps for each part, then run one pass that merges the four outputs.", "why": "Best. The steps are known in advance and don't depend on findings, so a fixed pipeline (prompt chaining) fits. Every run has the same shape, which auditors can replay."}, {"t": "Let the coordinator plan from early findings and add new subtasks as results come back.", "why": "Works, but less predictable. Adaptive plans suit open questions where findings decide the next step. Here the steps never change, and each run would take a different path.", "trap": "hard to audit"}, {"t": "Draft all four sections, check the draft for gaps, and send any thin section back for more work.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. A refinement loop fills coverage gaps, but it makes run length vary, which works against step-by-step replay.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Let the coordinator judge each request and call only the subagents its sections need.", "why": "Works, but there is little to skip. Choosing subagents per request helps when requests vary. Every request here has the same four sections.", "trap": "only helps varied requests"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each section is researched the same way every time, and no section depends on what another finds.", "explanation": "Fixed sequential pipelines suit work whose steps are known in advance; adaptive decomposition suits investigations whose subtasks depend on intermediate findings. Predictability and auditability favour the fixed pipeline.", "eli5": {"question": "Every summary has the same four sections, researched the same way, and auditors must replay each run. The clue: the steps never change and don't depend on each other.", "answer": "A bakery that makes the same four cakes every day uses a fixed recipe for each, then boxes them together. An inventor's lab, where each test decides the next, plans as it goes. This job is the bakery. In agent terms that's a <b>fixed pipeline</b> (prompt chaining) with one combining pass at the end. <b>Rule: steps known in advance → fixed pipeline. Next step depends on findings → adaptive plan.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Same recipes every day, then box them together.", "Planning as you go suits the inventor's lab. Here every day is the same, and inspectors want the same steps each time.", "Checking for gaps helps when you don't know what's missing. It makes every day's work a different length.", "Choosing which cakes to bake helps when orders vary. Here every order is the same four cakes."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the smarter, adaptive plan is always better</b>. Adaptive decomposition is for open-ended work, where what you find decides what you do next. When the steps are known in advance and audit needs the same shape every run, a fixed pipeline is simpler and easier to replay.", "diagram": "efficacy ─┐\nsafety   ─┤\ncost     ─┼─▶ combine pass ─▶ summary\nstatus   ─┘", "mapTitle": "How should the coordinator split up the work?", "map": {"head": ["Shape of the work", "Strategy", "Why"], "rows": [["Steps known in advance, independent", "Fixed pipeline (prompt chaining) + integration pass", "Predictable, easy to audit"], ["Next step depends on what was found", "Adaptive decomposition", "Subtasks come from findings"], ["Drafts often miss parts of the topic", "Refinement loop: check gaps, re-delegate", "Fills coverage gaps"], ["Requests vary a lot in size", "Select subagents per request", "Skips what isn't needed"], ["One big input dilutes attention", "Per-part passes, then a combining pass", "Each part gets full attention"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Every evidence summary follows the committee template", "means": "The output shape is the same every time."}, {"quote": "Each section is researched the same way every time, and no section depends on what another finds", "means": "The decider. The steps are fixed and independent, so nothing needs to adapt (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Auditors must be able to replay each run step by step", "means": "Runs must have the same shape. A gap-filling loop (<b>[[2]]</b>) varies in length."}, {"quote": "Which approach fits best?", "means": "A fixed pipeline with a combining pass: <b>[[0]]</b>. Choosing subagents (<b>[[3]]</b>) has nothing to skip."}], "rule": "Steps known in advance → fixed pipeline. Steps depend on findings → adaptive decomposition.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Fixed sequential pipelines (prompt chaining) vs dynamic adaptive decomposition based on intermediate findings"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-06", "was": "m7-s3-06", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Tool results in conversation history", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The team's own coordinator loop sends each request with the system prompt, the original question and only the latest tool result. Earlier turns are dropped to keep requests small. The coordinator often repeats searches it already ran in the same run. Runs are short and use a small part of the context window. What should the team <b>change first</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Write each search and its findings to a scratchpad file the coordinator reads every turn.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A scratchpad keeps findings across context limits or sessions. These runs are short, and the normal message history fits easily.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a case-facts block listing the searches already run, sent with the latest result.", "why": "Works, but only patches the gap. Claude would see which searches ran, but not their results or its own reasoning between turns.", "trap": "only partly works"}, {"t": "Have each search tool return a cached answer when it gets a query it already answered.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Repeats get cheaper, but Claude still doesn't know what it already did, so it keeps asking.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Send the full message history each turn, with every tool result appended in order.", "why": "Best. The agentic loop appends each tool result to the conversation, so Claude decides its next step from everything it has done."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Earlier turns are dropped to keep requests small.", "explanation": "In the agentic loop, each tool result is appended to the conversation history and sent back on the next request. Without it, the model cannot know what it has already done and repeats work.", "eli5": {"question": "Each turn, Claude only sees the latest search result, so it forgets what it already searched. The clue: earlier turns are dropped, and the runs are short.", "answer": "Imagine a detective who gets only the last page of their notebook each morning. They will re-interview the same witnesses. Give them the whole notebook. For Claude, the notebook is the <b>message history</b>: every turn and every tool result, in order. <b>Rule: append each tool result to the conversation and send it all back.</b>", "options": ["A separate filing cabinet helps when the notebook gets too thick. This notebook is thin.", "A list of who was interviewed helps, but not what they said or what the detective concluded.", "Making repeat interviews quicker saves time, but the detective still doesn't know they did them.", "Correct. Hand back the whole notebook."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>repeat searches need a memory system</b>. But the loop already has memory: the message history. This loop throws it away. When runs are short and fit easily in context, the first fix is to stop dropping turns.", "diagram": "today:  system + question + last result\nbetter: system + question + turn 1 + result 1\n        + turn 2 + result 2 + ...", "mapTitle": "The agent forgets what it already did. What do you fix?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Repeats work in a short run", "History not sent back", "Append each tool result to the conversation"], ["Forgets findings across context limits or sessions", "Context runs out", "Scratchpad file of key findings"], ["Amounts or promises drift after summarising", "Facts blurred by summaries", "Case-facts block"], ["Context fills fast with long results", "Verbose tool output", "Trim results to the needed fields"], ["Repeated calls are costly but not wrong", "Expensive lookups", "Cache results"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "only the latest tool result", "means": "Claude never sees its earlier results."}, {"quote": "Earlier turns are dropped to keep requests small", "means": "The decider. The history is being thrown away. A list of searches (<b>[[1]]</b>) restores only a part of it."}, {"quote": "The coordinator often repeats searches it already ran in the same run", "means": "The symptom. A cache (<b>[[2]]</b>) makes repeats cheap but doesn't stop them."}, {"quote": "Runs are short and use a small part of the context window", "means": "The full history fits, so a scratchpad (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "What should the team change first?", "means": "Send the full history: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "The loop sends every turn and tool result back each time. Reach for a scratchpad only when context runs out.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Tool results are appended to conversation history so the model reasons about the next action"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-08", "was": "m7-s4-08", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Resuming a named session", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer keeps several named Claude Code sessions. Last week, a session called <code>billing-audit</code> traced how invoices are rounded. Today they want to carry on that same investigation. Nobody has changed the billing code since then. Their most recent session was about something else. How should they <b>pick up the work</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Start a new session and paste a structured summary of the billing-audit findings.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. A summary is the safer start when files have changed; here nothing changed, so it only drops what Claude already saw.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Start a new session and have Claude trace the invoice rounding code again from the start.", "why": "Works, but redoes the work. Claude would reach the same findings, after repeating last week's reads.", "trap": "redoes the work"}, {"t": "Start Claude Code with <code>--resume billing-audit</code> and carry on where it stopped.", "why": "Best. Resuming by name reopens that exact conversation, and its earlier tool results are still accurate."}, {"t": "Resume billing-audit, then branch it with <code>fork_session</code> before going further.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A fork is for trying a different approach from a shared starting point; this is one line of work.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Nobody has changed the billing code since then.", "explanation": "--resume with a session name continues a specific prior conversation. When files have changed since, a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale results.", "eli5": {"question": "Someone wants to continue last week's investigation. The clues: they know its name, and <i>nobody has changed the billing code</i>, so last week's notes are still true.", "answer": "If you left a puzzle half done and nobody touched the table, you sit back down and carry on. You don't start again, or copy it onto a new table. <b>--resume &lt;name&gt;</b> is sitting back down at that table. If someone had moved pieces, a fresh table with a clear note would be safer. <b>Rule: nothing changed → resume by name. Files changed → new session with a structured summary.</b>", "options": ["Copying notes to a new table works, but you lose detail you could have kept.", "Starting the puzzle over gets there, but wastes last week's work.", "Correct. Sit back down at the same table.", "Making a copy of the table is for trying two ways at once. There's only one way here."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a fresh start with a summary is always safer</b>. It is safer when the files have changed and old tool results are stale. Here the code is unchanged, so the old results are still accurate, and resuming keeps all of them. The name matters too: the most recent session is a different one.", "mapTitle": "Resume, fork or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Continue a named past session, files unchanged", "<code>--resume &lt;name&gt;</code>", "Keeps every earlier finding"], ["Continue, but many files changed, or you can't tell which", "New session + structured summary", "Most old tool results are now wrong; starting clean is safer"], ["Continue, and only one or two files changed that you can name", "Resume and tell Claude what changed", "Most findings still hold; Claude re-reads just those files"], ["Try two approaches from one point", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches"], ["Continue the most recent session", "<code>--continue</code>", "Opens the latest one"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "a session called billing-audit traced how invoices are rounded", "means": "There is a named session holding the findings. Tracing it again (<b>[[1]]</b>) repeats them."}, {"quote": "Nobody has changed the billing code since then", "means": "The decider. The old tool results are still true, so resuming is safe and a summary (<b>[[0]]</b>) only loses detail."}, {"quote": "Their most recent session was about something else", "means": "The session must be picked by name, not by recency."}, {"quote": "How should they pick up the work?", "means": "One line of work, continued as is: <b>[[2]]</b>. A fork (<b>[[3]]</b>) is for two approaches."}], "rule": "Files unchanged → --resume by name. Files changed → new session with a structured summary.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "--resume &lt;session-name&gt; continues a specific prior conversation"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-09", "was": "m7-s4-09", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks that block outgoing tool calls", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An Agent SDK script lets Claude look into data bugs on the staging database through the MCP tool <code>run_sql</code>. Each investigation needs many read queries. Statements that change data must not run from this script. Last week Claude ran a DELETE that wiped a tester's data. Which control should the team <b>add</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A system prompt rule listing the allowed statement types, with examples of writes to refuse.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear rule makes writes rarer, but prompt compliance has a non-zero failure rate, and this must not happen.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "A permission rule that asks an engineer to approve each <code>run_sql</code> call before it runs.", "why": "Works, but too much friction. A person can stop every write, but they must also approve each of the many read queries.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "A <code>PostToolUse</code> hook on <code>run_sql</code> that checks the changed rows and stops the script.", "why": "Works, but too late. It stops the script after a write, but by then the data has already changed.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "A hook on outgoing <code>run_sql</code> calls that blocks any non-read statement and tells Claude why.", "why": "Best. The hook checks each call before it runs, so writes are refused every time and Claude can adjust its query."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Statements that change data must not run from this script.", "explanation": "Hooks give deterministic guarantees. Intercept outgoing tool calls to block actions that break a policy; PostToolUse hooks act on results after the call has already run.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude may only read from the database, but it once deleted data. The clue: writes <i>must not run</i>, and there are <i>many</i> read queries.", "answer": "A guard at the vault door checks every request before anyone goes in: “reading the ledger, fine; taking money out, no”. That guard is a <b>hook on outgoing tool calls</b>. A guard who checks the vault afterwards notices the loss but can't undo it. A written rule is a sign on the door, which most people obey. <b>Rule: if an action must not happen, block it before the call runs, in code.</b>", "options": ["A sign on the door works most of the time. Most of the time isn't enough here.", "A person approving every visit stops the bad ones, but also slows down every harmless read.", "Checking the vault afterwards notices the loss. The money is already gone.", "Correct. The guard stops writes at the door and says why."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>any hook is a guarantee</b>. Where the hook runs matters. A hook on outgoing calls acts <b>before</b> the statement runs; a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook acts <b>after</b>, when the data is already changed. A prompt rule only makes writes less likely.", "diagram": "Claude → run_sql(\"DELETE ...\")\n          ↓ hook on outgoing call: not a read → blocked\n          ↓ hook on outgoing call: SELECT → runs", "mapTitle": "Where should the control act?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Control", "When it acts"], "rows": [["An action must not happen", "Hook on outgoing tool calls", "Before the call runs"], ["Change or clean up a tool result", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook", "After the call, before Claude reads it"], ["A rare, high-stakes action needs a person", "Ask for approval", "Before the call, by a human"], ["A style or wording preference", "Prompt instruction + examples", "In Claude's instructions"], ["Spot problems for later review", "Logging", "After the fact"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each investigation needs many read queries", "means": "Reads are frequent and harmless. Approving each call (<b>[[1]]</b>) would slow all of them."}, {"quote": "Statements that change data must not run from this script", "means": "The decider. A must-not rule needs code, not a prompt (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Last week Claude ran a DELETE that wiped a tester's data", "means": "The harm happens when the statement runs. A check after the call (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes too late."}, {"quote": "Which control should the team add?", "means": "Block non-read statements before they run: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Must not happen → block the outgoing tool call in a hook. PostToolUse is for changing results, not stopping actions.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "Hooks intercept outgoing tool calls to enforce compliance"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-10", "was": "m7-s4-10", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Model-driven tool use vs fixed sequence", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "An internal Agent SDK tool answers engineers' questions about the codebase. Its code runs fixed steps: Grep for the question's keywords, Read the top three hits, then ask Claude to answer. Answers go wrong when the real logic sits in files those hits call into. Which file matters next depends on what each Read shows. Which <b>TWO</b> changes should the redesign include?", "options": [{"t": "Give Claude the Grep and Read tools, and let it pick each next call from earlier results.", "why": "Best. Claude chooses the next file from what it just read, which suits a trail that unfolds as you follow it."}, {"t": "Append each tool result to the conversation, and keep looping while stop_reason is tool_use.", "why": "Best. Each result goes back to Claude, and the loop runs until Claude ends its turn, so Claude decides when it has enough."}, {"t": "Keep the fixed steps, but add a step that follows the top hits' imports two levels deep.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Many answers sit two levels down, but any fixed depth is a guess when the next file depends on each read.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run the fixed steps once per keyword in parallel subagents, and merge their reads at the end.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. It widens the search, but each subagent still stops after the same fixed steps.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0, 1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Which file matters next depends on what each Read shows.", "explanation": "In an agentic loop the model picks the next tool call from results added to its history, continuing on tool_use and stopping on end_turn. Fixed sequences suit work whose steps are known in advance.", "eli5": {"question": "A question-answering tool always reads the same three files and stops. The clue: <i>which file matters next depends on what each Read shows</i>.", "answer": "A detective doesn't decide in advance to visit three houses and stop. Each clue tells them where to go next, and they stop when the case is solved. Here that means two things: let Claude choose its own next search or read (<b>model-driven tool use</b>), and keep feeding results back in a loop until Claude says it's done (<b>stop_reason</b> <code>end_turn</code>). <b>Rule: next step depends on what you find → let the model drive the loop. Steps known in advance → a fixed sequence.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The detective chooses where to go next from each clue.", "Correct. Keep going while the detective asks for more, stop when they say done.", "Visiting two more houses on a fixed route helps sometimes, but the right house may be three away.", "More detectives on the same fixed route cover more ground, but all stop at the same point."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better fixed recipe</b> (more steps, more parallel runs) will catch the missing files. The fault is that <b>the steps are decided before anything is read</b>. When the next file depends on what the last read showed, Claude has to choose the calls, inside a loop that keeps going until Claude ends its turn.", "diagram": "loop:\n  response = Claude(history)\n  if stop_reason == \"tool_use\": run tool, append result\n  if stop_reason == \"end_turn\": answer", "mapTitle": "Fixed steps or model-driven loop?", "map": {"head": ["Shape of the work", "Design", "Why"], "rows": [["Next step depends on what was found", "Model-driven tool calls in a loop", "Claude picks each next call"], ["Loop control", "Continue on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>", "Claude says when it has enough"], ["Steps are the same every time", "Fixed sequence (prompt chaining)", "Predictable and cheap"], ["Many independent areas to cover", "Parallel subagents", "Wider coverage, not deeper"], ["Answers are usually a fixed depth away", "Longer fixed chain", "Works only when the depth is known"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Its code runs fixed steps: Grep for the question's keywords, Read the top three hits, then ask Claude to answer", "means": "The steps are chosen before anything is read. Adding steps (<b>[[2]]</b>) or copies (<b>[[3]]</b>) keeps that."}, {"quote": "Answers go wrong when the real logic sits in files those hits call into", "means": "The answer lies further along a trail of calls than the fixed steps reach."}, {"quote": "Which file matters next depends on what each Read shows", "means": "The decider. Claude must pick each next call (<b>[[0]]</b>) and see each result in a loop that runs until it is done (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Next step depends on findings → model-driven tool calls in a stop_reason loop. Known steps → fixed sequence.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Model-driven decision-making vs pre-configured decision trees / tool sequences"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-06", "was": "m7-s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 stop_reason-driven loop", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The extraction agent runs in a loop with two lookup tools. The loop ends when Claude's reply contains the phrase “EXTRACTION COMPLETE”. Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also asking for a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs. Other runs never write the phrase and stop at the iteration cap. How should the loop <b>decide when to stop</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Continue while <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code>, and end the loop when it is <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Best. The API reports on every response whether Claude wants a tool or has finished. That is the signal the loop is built on."}, {"t": "Keep the phrase, but run any requested tools first and stop on the next turn without a tool call.", "why": "Works, but still reads text. It fixes the early exit, but runs that never write the phrase still stop at the cap.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have Claude return a <code>status</code> field, and stop when it says complete with no tool call pending.", "why": "Works, but reinvents the signal. A structured flag is sturdier than a phrase, but Claude can still forget it, and the API already reports this.", "trap": "reinvents the signal"}, {"t": "End the loop from a hook once every required schema field has a value.", "why": "Works, but wrong signal. It ends many runs on time, but fields can fill before the lookups are done, and runs with a missing field still hit the cap.", "trap": "wrong signal"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also asking for a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs.", "explanation": "An agent loop continues while stop_reason is tool_use and ends on end_turn. Parsing the reply text or using an iteration cap as the main stop are anti-patterns.", "eli5": {"question": "The loop stops when Claude writes a magic phrase. Sometimes it writes it too early, sometimes not at all. The clue: the loop is reading Claude's words to decide when the job is done.", "answer": "A waiter who waits for you to say “that's all” sometimes leaves while you're still ordering. Most restaurants just watch whether you've closed the menu. The API gives that closed menu on every reply: <b><code>stop_reason</code></b>. <code>tool_use</code> means “still ordering”; <code>end_turn</code> means “done”. <b>Rule: stop the loop on stop_reason, not on words.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Watch the menu, not the words.", "Better, but people who never say “that's all” still keep the waiter there all night.", "A “done” card on the table is better than words, but the menu already tells you.", "Leaving when the plates are full can be too early, or never happen."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the loop needs a clearer “done” message</b> from Claude. But the API already says, on every response, whether Claude wants a tool (<code>tool_use</code>) or has finished (<code>end_turn</code>). Any signal Claude writes itself, a phrase or a flag, can be missing or early.", "diagram": "response → stop_reason?\n  tool_use → run tool, append result, loop\n  end_turn → stop", "mapTitle": "How should an agent loop know it is done?", "map": {"head": ["Approach", "Problem", "Verdict"], "rows": [["<code>stop_reason</code>: continue on <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>", "None", "Use this"], ["A phrase in the reply text", "Early, missing or misread", "Anti-pattern"], ["A status field Claude writes", "Claude can forget or misjudge it", "Weaker copy of stop_reason"], ["Iteration cap as the main stop", "Cuts off real work or runs long", "Safety net only"], ["All fields filled", "Not the same as all work done", "Validation, not termination"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The loop ends when Claude's reply contains the phrase", "means": "The loop reads text to decide termination, an anti-pattern."}, {"quote": "Sometimes Claude writes that phrase while also asking for a tool call, and the loop exits before the tool runs", "means": "The decider. The response was really <code>tool_use</code>; the loop ignored it."}, {"quote": "Other runs never write the phrase and stop at the iteration cap", "means": "Any self-written signal (<b>[[1]]</b>, <b>[[2]]</b>) can go missing, and filled fields (<b>[[3]]</b>) can come before the work is done."}, {"quote": "How should the loop decide when to stop?", "means": "Use the API's own signal: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Continue on tool_use, stop on end_turn. Never parse text or rely on a cap to stop.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Continue the loop when stop_reason == \"tool_use\"; terminate when \"end_turn\""}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-07", "was": "m7-s6-07", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Explicit context in subagent prompts", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Some submissions hold a broker's cover email plus several certificates. The coordinator reads the email, which names the client and the location each certificate covers. It then spawns one subagent per certificate with the task “Extract this certificate.” Subagents often assign certificates to the wrong location. Each subagent receives only its certificate. Which change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give subagents a scoped <code>get_cover_email</code> tool, and tell them to call it before extracting.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Each subagent can read the email itself, but that adds a tool and a call per certificate for facts the coordinator already has.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Have subagents return a location guess, and let the coordinator correct it against the email.", "why": "Works, but too late. The coordinator can catch wrong guesses, but it redoes the matching after the fact instead of giving subagents the answer up front.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Run one agent over the whole submission, email and certificates together, with no subagents.", "why": "Works, but gives up the split. One agent sees everything, but long submissions crowd one context and lose the parallel, focused extraction.", "trap": "gives up the split"}, {"t": "Add the client name and the location list from the email to each subagent's task prompt.", "why": "Best. Subagents see only what their prompt gives them. The coordinator already has these facts, so it passes them on."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each subagent receives only its certificate.", "explanation": "Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's context. Facts they need, which the coordinator already holds, must be written into each subagent's prompt.", "eli5": {"question": "The coordinator read the cover email, but the helpers it sends out only get one certificate each, so they guess the location wrong. The clue: each helper receives only its certificate.", "answer": "A manager reads the client's letter, then hands each temp a single page saying “file this”. The temps don't know which site each page belongs to. Write the client and site list on each handout and they file it right. That handout is the <b>subagent's task prompt</b>. <b>Rule: subagents know only what you put in their prompt.</b>", "options": ["Letting each temp fetch the letter works, but it's extra trips for facts the manager already has.", "Fixing the filing afterwards works, but it's checking work that could have been right first time.", "One person doing everything works, but a big pile slows them down and mistakes creep in.", "Correct. Write the facts on each handout."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>subagents know what the coordinator knows</b>. They don't: a subagent starts with an empty context and sees only the prompt it is given. The coordinator <b>already holds the facts</b>, so the simplest fix is to pass them in.", "diagram": "coordinator: email → client, locations\n  ├→ subagent 1: cert 1 + client + locations\n  └→ subagent 2: cert 2 + client + locations", "mapTitle": "A subagent lacks facts it needs. How should it get them?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Coordinator already has the facts", "Put them in the subagent's prompt", "No inheritance; explicit is cheapest"], ["Subagent needs varied lookups nobody can predict", "A scoped tool for that lookup", "It can fetch what it finds it needs"], ["Later subagent needs an earlier one's findings", "Pass the complete findings in its prompt", "Context doesn't flow by itself"], ["Findings must keep their sources", "Structured format separating content and metadata", "Attribution survives the handoff"], ["Checking subagent output against known facts", "Coordinator review", "A safety net, not the main fix"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator reads the email, which names the client and the location each certificate covers", "means": "The facts are already in the coordinator's hands, so a lookup tool (<b>[[0]]</b>) is extra machinery."}, {"quote": "It then spawns one subagent per certificate with the task “Extract this certificate.”", "means": "The task prompt carries no context."}, {"quote": "Each subagent receives only its certificate", "means": "The decider: the subagent has no way to know the locations."}, {"quote": "Which change is the best fix?", "means": "Pass the facts in: <b>[[3]]</b>. Correcting afterwards (<b>[[1]]</b>) is too late; dropping subagents (<b>[[2]]</b>) changes the design."}], "rule": "Subagents inherit nothing. Put the facts they need into their prompt.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-08", "was": "m7-s6-08", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Programmatic prerequisite gate", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The agent calls <code>verify_carrier</code> to check a carrier against the licensing registry, then <code>post_certificate</code> to send the result on. One session handles several certificates in turn. Sometimes the agent posts a certificate without verifying its carrier first. Compliance requires that no certificate from an unverified carrier is ever posted. Which change <b>meets this requirement</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Make a verification ID from <code>verify_carrier</code> a required input of <code>post_certificate</code>.", "why": "Works, but checks shape, not fact. A required field makes skipping harder, but nothing confirms the ID belongs to this carrier or came from a real check.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to force <code>verify_carrier</code> on each session's first request.", "why": "Works, but only once. It verifies the first carrier, but a session handles several certificates, and later ones can still skip the check.", "trap": "covers only the first"}, {"t": "Add a hook that blocks <code>post_certificate</code> until <code>verify_carrier</code> has passed for that carrier.", "why": "Best. Code checks every post, for every certificate in the session, and refuses any carrier that has not passed."}, {"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to verify each carrier first, with two examples of verify-then-post.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The rule and examples cut the skips, but prompt instructions still fail now and then, and compliance allows no misses.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Compliance requires that no certificate from an unverified carrier is ever posted.", "explanation": "When a step must happen every time, enforce it in code: a hook or prerequisite gate that blocks the downstream tool until the check has passed for that item.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent should check each carrier before posting, but sometimes it skips the check. The clue: compliance says an unchecked carrier must not get through, not even once.", "answer": "A sign saying “show your ticket” stops most people. A turnstile that opens only for a valid ticket stops everyone. Here the turnstile is a <b>hook</b> on <code>post_certificate</code> that looks up whether this carrier passed. <b>Rule: must happen every time → code gate. Usually is good enough → prompt.</b>", "options": ["Asking for a ticket number helps, but nobody checks the number is real.", "Checking only the first person in a group lets the rest walk through.", "Correct. A turnstile on every post, for every carrier.", "A sign works most of the time, and the rule says most is not enough."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>forcing the tool</b> or a <b>required field</b> is as strong as a hook. <code>tool_choice</code> forces one call on one request, and a session here posts several certificates. A required field checks that something was passed, not that the check happened for this carrier. Only code that runs on <b>every post</b> meets an absolute rule.", "diagram": "post_certificate(cert, carrier)\n  → hook: verified[carrier]?\n       yes → allow\n       no  → block, tell agent to verify first", "mapTitle": "A step must come before another. How strong must the control be?", "map": {"head": ["Requirement", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Must hold every time, many items per session", "Hook or prerequisite gate in code", "Checks every call"], ["One call must come first in a single-item run", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code> on the first request", "Forces exactly one call"], ["Nice to have, misses do no harm", "System prompt plus examples", "Cheap; probabilistic"], ["Tool needs data from an earlier step", "Required input field", "Shapes the call, doesn't prove the check"], ["Must call some tool rather than reply in text", "<code>tool_choice: any</code>", "Forces a tool, not which one"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One session handles several certificates in turn", "means": "Forcing the first request (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers only the first certificate."}, {"quote": "Sometimes the agent posts a certificate without verifying its carrier first", "means": "The prompt-led order already fails sometimes; a stronger prompt (<b>[[3]]</b>) lowers the rate, not to zero."}, {"quote": "Compliance requires that no certificate from an unverified carrier is ever posted", "means": "The decider: zero misses, so enforcement must be in code."}, {"quote": "Which change meets this requirement?", "means": "A gate that checks this carrier on every post: <b>[[2]]</b>. A required ID (<b>[[0]]</b>) can be reused or made up."}], "rule": "Must happen every time → a hook or prerequisite gate in code, checked on every call.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Block downstream tool calls until prerequisites complete"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-01", "was": "m6-s1-01", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Prerequisite gates", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Company policy says the agent must read a box's <code>refund_eligible</code> flag before it refunds that box. Only <code>lookup_order</code> returns the flag. An audit found refunds paid on ineligible boxes where <code>lookup_order</code> was never called for that box. Many chats cover more than one box. Customers often bring up the second box late in the chat. Finance needs the rule to hold in every chat. Which change <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Force <code>lookup_order</code> with <code>tool_choice</code> as the first call of each chat, then go back to <code>auto</code>.", "why": "Works, but covers only the first box. Forcing the first call is deterministic, but a box raised later in the chat is never looked up.", "trap": "covers only part"}, {"t": "Expand the <code>process_refund</code> description to say <code>lookup_order</code> must run first for the same box.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear description makes the lookup likely, but it is still guidance the model can skip, and finance needs it every time.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>process_refund</code> until <code>lookup_order</code> returns that box.", "why": "Best. A gate on the refund call itself, checked per box, holds however many boxes the chat covers and whenever they come up."}, {"t": "Run a nightly job that checks each refund against its box's flag and reports any bad ones.", "why": "Works, but too late. It finds the bad refunds, but only after the money has gone out.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Many chats cover more than one box. Customers often bring up the second box late in the chat.", "explanation": "A prerequisite that must hold every time belongs in code on the downstream call, checked for the item being acted on. Forcing one first call cannot cover items raised later.", "eli5": {"question": "Refunds went out on boxes nobody looked up first. The clue: <i>many chats cover more than one box</i>, and the second box often comes up late.", "answer": "Think of a cashier who must scan each item's barcode before giving money back for it. Making them scan one item when the customer walks in doesn't help with the second item they pull out later. A lock on the till that opens only after <i>that</i> item is scanned works for every item (a <b>PreToolUse hook</b> on <code>process_refund</code>). <b>Rule: a check that must happen every time goes in code, on the action itself, for the thing being acted on.</b>", "options": ["Scanning once at the door covers the first item, not the one pulled out later.", "A sign by the till saying “scan first” helps, but a busy cashier can still skip it.", "Correct. The till won't open for a box until that box has been looked up.", "Checking the receipts at night finds the mistakes after the money is gone."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>forcing the lookup first</b> settles it, because <code>tool_choice</code> is deterministic. It controls one turn, not every refund. The rule is “before each refund, for that box”, so the check must sit on the refund call and be <b>keyed to the box</b>.", "diagram": "chat: box A ... later box B\nforced first call: lookup A ✓   lookup B ✗\nhook on refund:    refund A needs lookup A ✓\n                   refund B needs lookup B ✓", "mapTitle": "A step must come before an action. Where does the check go?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Must hold every time, for each item acted on", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook on the action, keyed to the item", "Deterministic, any number of items"], ["One item, known at the start", "Forced first call with <code>tool_choice</code>", "Deterministic for that one turn"], ["Nice to have, not a rule", "Prompt or description guidance", "Usually followed, cheap"], ["Need to find past mistakes", "After-the-fact audit job", "Detects, doesn't prevent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An audit found refunds paid on ineligible boxes where lookup_order was never called for that box", "means": "The prerequisite was skipped. A nightly check (<b>[[3]]</b>) finds this only after the money is paid."}, {"quote": "Many chats cover more than one box", "means": "The decider. A forced first call (<b>[[0]]</b>) covers only one lookup."}, {"quote": "Finance needs the rule to hold in every chat", "means": "Guidance in a description (<b>[[1]]</b>) is not enough. A gate in code on each refund: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Rule must hold for every item → hook on the downstream call, checked for that item.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Block downstream tool calls until prerequisites complete"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-02", "was": "m6-s1-02", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Multi-concern decomposition", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Customers often list several problems in their first message. One might report a missing ingredient in this week's box and a double charge from last month. Transcripts show the same pattern each time. The agent's first reply deals only with the first problem. The others are never mentioned again, and customers open a new chat for them. Which change <b>best addresses</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Keep a case-facts block listing every open problem and its box ID at the top of each request.", "why": "Works, but for a different failure. A case-facts block keeps facts from fading over a long chat. Here the problems are dropped on the first reply.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have the agent list every issue up front, look them all up, and answer each in one reply.", "why": "Best. The problems are lost when the message is first read. Splitting it up front, checking each part and answering together fixes that step."}, {"t": "Attach a summary of every problem and amount whenever a chat is handed to a human.", "why": "Works, but for a different moment. A good handoff helps the person who takes over. These chats were not handed over.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add example chats where the agent settles one problem per turn and confirms it before the next.", "why": "Works, but slowly. Examples would shape the habit, but one problem per turn is the pattern that lets later problems slip.", "trap": "one at a time"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The agent's first reply deals only with the first problem.", "explanation": "Requests with several concerns should be split up front, looked up together with shared context and answered in one reply. Case-facts blocks fix facts fading over long chats.", "eli5": {"question": "A customer lists several problems at once, and the agent answers only the first. The clue: it happens in the <i>first reply</i>, not after a long chat.", "answer": "A good waiter who hears “the soup is cold, and we were charged twice” writes both down, checks both, and comes back with both answers. A waiter who fixes the soup and forgets the bill makes you ask again. Writing every request down first and answering together is <b>decomposing a multi-concern request</b>. <b>Rule: several problems in one message → list them, check them together, answer them together.</b>", "options": ["A notepad for a long evening helps when things fade over time. Here the bill was forgotten on the first visit.", "Correct. Write every request down, check them all, come back with every answer.", "Briefing the manager well helps only if the manager is called. Nobody was called.", "One request per visit is exactly how the second request gets forgotten."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to see “forgotten problems” and reach for <b>memory fixes</b> like a case-facts block. Those help when facts fade over many turns. Here nothing has had time to fade: the first reply already drops the other problems. The fix is at the <b>first read</b>: split the request into its parts.", "mapTitle": "Problems get dropped. When, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Only the first problem answered in the first reply", "Request not split up front", "List each concern, investigate together, answer once"], ["Facts dropped after many turns", "Fading over a long chat", "Case-facts block in every request"], ["Person taking over lacks context", "Thin handoff", "Structured handoff summary"], ["Exact amounts blurred after summarising", "Lossy summary", "Keep figures in a case-facts block"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Customers often list several problems in their first message", "means": "Each message carries several concerns to split up."}, {"quote": "The agent's first reply deals only with the first problem", "means": "The decider. The loss happens at once, not over time, so a case-facts block (<b>[[0]]</b>) is aimed at the wrong failure."}, {"quote": "The others are never mentioned again, and customers open a new chat for them", "means": "No handoff happened (<b>[[2]]</b>), and one-per-turn examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) keep this pattern. Split, check together, answer once: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Several concerns in one message → decompose up front, look them up together, answer them together.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Decompose multi-concern requests; investigate in parallel with shared context"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-03", "was": "m6-s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Resume vs fresh session", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Spoiled-box claims often pause for about a week while the courier investigates. The team stores each Agent SDK session and resumes it by name when the customer returns. During the pause, the courier and the warehouse update many fields on the order. These include its status, refund state and claim verdict. Resumed agents have quoted old values as current. How should returning customers <b>be handled</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Resume the session and add a note naming which order fields changed since the customer last wrote.", "why": "Works, but much of the history is stale. A note on what changed suits one or two moved values. Here many fields changed, and the old results are still there to quote.", "trap": "stale history stays"}, {"t": "Resume the session and have the agent call <code>lookup_order</code> again before it answers.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A fresh read helps, but the old results stay in the history, and the agent can still quote them.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Resume the session with a case-facts block of the customer ID, claim ID and amounts promised.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A case-facts block keeps promises from being lost. The stale tool results are still in the history.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Open a fresh conversation seeded with a claim summary, then re-read the order's present state.", "why": "Best. Most earlier tool output describes a record that has since changed. A fresh session from a summary of stable facts, plus fresh reads, leaves nothing stale to quote."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "During the pause, the courier and the warehouse update many fields on the order.", "explanation": "Resume when earlier context is mostly still valid, and say what changed. When much of the earlier tool output is stale, start a new session from a structured summary of stable facts and read current state fresh.", "eli5": {"question": "A customer comes back after a week, and the agent quotes old order details as if they were current. The clue: <i>many fields</i> changed during the pause.", "answer": "Picking up last week's notebook is fine if one line changed: you cross it out. If most pages are out of date, you start a clean page with the few facts that still hold, then check the current record. That clean page is a <b>new session seeded with a structured summary</b>, followed by fresh reads. <b>Rule: a little changed → resume and say what. A lot changed → new session from a summary.</b>", "options": ["Crossing out a line works when one or two things changed. Most of these pages are out of date.", "Checking the record again helps, but the old pages are still open on the desk.", "A list of promises is useful, but it doesn't remove the stale pages.", "Correct. A clean page with the facts that still hold, then a fresh look at the record."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>resuming is always better</b> because it keeps everything the agent knew. That holds while what it knew is still true. After a week, much of the earlier tool output describes an order that has since been rewritten. Old results in the history can be quoted as current, so a <b>fresh session from a summary</b> is more reliable.", "diagram": "resume:  [old status][old verdict][old refund] + note\n          → stale values still quotable\nnew:     [summary: IDs, promises] + fresh lookup\n          → only current values", "mapTitle": "Customer returns later. Resume or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best approach", "Why"], "rows": [["Many fields changed during the pause", "New session from a structured summary, then fresh reads", "Nothing stale left to quote"], ["One or two known values changed", "Resume and tell the agent what changed", "Keeps useful context"], ["Promises at risk of being lost", "Case-facts block", "Keeps exact facts"], ["Want to try two approaches from one point", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team stores each Agent SDK session and resumes it by name when the customer returns", "means": "Today's approach keeps all the old tool results."}, {"quote": "During the pause, the courier and the warehouse update many fields on the order", "means": "The decider. Many changes, not one or two, so a change note (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves a mostly stale history."}, {"quote": "Resumed agents have quoted old values as current", "means": "Stale results in the history are the cause. A re-read (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a facts block (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves them in place. Start fresh: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Lots of stale tool output → new session from a structured summary, not a resume.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "Starting a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale tool results"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-04", "was": "m6-s1-04", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks for policy enforcement", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook rejects any <code>process_refund</code> call above $150, the per-box limit. For large claims, the agent now splits one refund into several smaller calls on the same box. Each call is under the limit, so the hook lets every one through. The total paid on one box can end up well above the limit. Which change <b>closes the gap</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Make the hook total the box's earlier refunds plus this call, and escalate above the limit.", "why": "Best. The limit is per box, but the hook checks per call. Checking the running total at the call enforces the real rule."}, {"t": "Add escalation rules for large claims to the system prompt, with two example chats on each side.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Rules and examples guide judgment, but the agent is already working around a hard limit. That needs code.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Return the hook's rejection as a business error marked not retryable, with how approval works.", "why": "Works, but for a different case. A clear business error stops retries after a rejection. Here no call is ever rejected.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Run a nightly job that totals each box's refunds and sends any over the limit to a person.", "why": "Works, but too late. It catches the overpaid boxes, but the money has already gone out.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Each call is under the limit, so the hook lets every one through.", "explanation": "A hook enforces only the condition it checks. When the policy limit is per box, the hook must check the running total for that box and redirect to escalation.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent gets round a $150 refund limit by paying in several small pieces. The clue: <i>each call is under the limit</i>, so nothing is ever rejected.", "answer": "A guard who checks each bag at the door misses someone carrying five small bags of the same stolen goods. The guard must add up what that person has already carried out. The hook should check the <b>running total per box</b>, not the single call, and send anything over the limit to a person. <b>Rule: a hook enforces only what it checks, so make it check the real rule.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Add up what this box has already received, then decide.", "A notice on the wall asks nicely. The agent is already finding a way round the rule.", "A clearer “no” helps when someone keeps asking after being refused. Nobody was refused here.", "Counting at closing time finds the loss after the goods have left."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a hook on the refund call already enforces the limit</b>. A hook enforces only the condition it checks. This one checks each call, but the policy is per box. Several small calls pass one at a time. The hook must check the <b>running total for the box</b>.", "diagram": "per-call check:   $90 ✓  $90 ✓  $90 ✓  → $270 paid\nrunning total:    $90 ✓  $180 ✗ → escalate_to_human", "mapTitle": "A hard limit is being slipped. What closes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Many small calls, each under the limit", "Hook checks the call, not the total", "Hook checks the running total, then escalates"], ["Agent retries after a rejection", "Error doesn't say it is final", "Business error, not retryable, with an explanation"], ["Agent unsure when to escalate", "Vague criteria", "Explicit criteria plus examples"], ["Limit must hold every time", "Prompt rules are probabilistic", "Hook in code"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the agent now splits one refund into several smaller calls on the same box", "means": "The limit is about a box, but the calls are split."}, {"quote": "Each call is under the limit, so the hook lets every one through", "means": "The decider. No rejection ever happens, so a better rejection error (<b>[[2]]</b>) never fires."}, {"quote": "The total paid on one box can end up well above the limit", "means": "The hook must check the total: <b>[[0]]</b>. Prompt rules (<b>[[1]]</b>) are not code, and a nightly job (<b>[[3]]</b>) acts after payment."}], "rule": "A hook enforces only what it checks. Per-box limit → check the per-box running total.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Agent SDK hooks for interception and normalization", "quote": "Hooks intercept outgoing tool calls to enforce compliance (e.g., block refunds above a threshold)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-01", "was": "m6-s3-01", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Iterative refinement loop", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "For a request on a new blood thinner, the coordinator split the work into efficacy, dosing and cost. Each subagent did its part well. But the summary missed a drug-interaction warning. The warning appeared only in footnotes of papers the analysis subagent had already read. Nothing in the request pointed to it. Last month a statin summary missed a different finding the same way. What is the <b>most effective change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a drug-interaction part to the coordinator's planning instructions for every medicine request.", "why": "Works, but only fixes this one. A wider plan helps when the missing area can be foreseen from the request. This warning showed up only inside papers already read, and the statin miss was something else again.", "trap": "only covers foreseen gaps"}, {"t": "Pass the full findings of earlier subagents into each later subagent's prompt with its task.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Passing prior findings helps subagents that depend on each other. Nobody is asked to chase what those findings point to, so the footnote stays unexamined.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator check each draft for gaps, delegate follow-ups, then redraft.", "why": "Best. An iterative refinement loop (check the synthesis for gaps, re-delegate, re-synthesise) catches gaps that only appear once the subagents have read the papers."}, {"t": "Give the synthesis subagent a scoped lookup tool for quick fact checks while it drafts.", "why": "Works, but wrong size. A scoped tool suits frequent simple checks. Investigating an interaction warning is real research, and nothing tells synthesis to look for it.", "trap": "wrong size"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The warning appeared only in footnotes of papers the analysis subagent had already read.", "explanation": "Up-front decomposition covers only what is knowable at the start. When gaps appear in what subagents find, the coordinator should evaluate the synthesis for gaps, re-delegate targeted work and re-synthesise.", "eli5": {"question": "A summary went out missing an important warning. The question asks how to stop misses like this. The clue: the warning was in footnotes of papers <i>already read</i>, and nothing in the request pointed to it.", "answer": "Think of a school project where the team splits the topic before anyone has read anything. Some important things only show up once you start reading. So someone has to read the draft, spot what it leans on but never checked, and send people back to look into it. That is an <b>iterative refinement loop</b>: check the synthesis for gaps, re-delegate, re-synthesise. <b>Rule: a gap you can only see after reading → a check-and-redo loop, not a bigger plan.</b>", "options": ["Adding one more topic to the plan helps for gaps you can predict. This one hid in footnotes, and next time it will be a different one.", "Handing each helper the earlier notes is good practice, but nobody is told to chase what the notes point to.", "Correct. The coordinator reads the draft, finds the hole, sends someone to fill it, then redrafts.", "A quick lookup tool is for small checks. An interaction warning needs real research, and nobody asks for it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a miss means the plan was too narrow</b>, so the plan should get wider. That is right when the missing area could be seen from the request. Here the warning only surfaced <i>inside</i> papers already read, and the same kind of miss happened on another drug. No up-front plan names things nobody knows yet. A <b>refinement loop</b> after the draft does.", "diagram": "plan → subagents read → draft\n   ↑                       │\n   └── re-delegate gaps ←── coordinator checks draft", "mapTitle": "Something was missed. Where should the fix go?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Whole areas missing, foreseeable from the request", "Decomposition too narrow", "Widen the coordinator's split"], ["Gap appears only inside findings already read", "No step looks for gaps", "Refinement loop: check draft, re-delegate, redraft"], ["Later subagent ignores earlier findings", "Context not passed", "Put prior findings in its prompt"], ["Synthesis needs many quick fact checks", "Round trips via coordinator", "Scoped lookup tool for simple checks"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each subagent did its part well", "means": "The subagents are not the problem. The process around them is."}, {"quote": "The warning appeared only in footnotes of papers the analysis subagent had already read", "means": "The decider. The gap shows only after reading, so a wider plan (<b>[[0]]</b>) could not have named it."}, {"quote": "Last month a statin summary missed a different finding the same way", "means": "The misses differ each time. A fixed extra part in the plan fixes one; a gap check after the draft catches each."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective change?", "means": "Check the draft and re-delegate: <b>[[2]]</b>. Passing findings (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a lookup tool (<b>[[3]]</b>) never asks anyone to look for the gap."}], "rule": "Gaps that appear only in what subagents find → coordinator checks the draft for gaps, re-delegates, re-synthesises.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "iterative refinement loops (evaluate synthesis for gaps → re-delegate → re-synthesize)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-02", "was": "m6-s3-02", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Coordinator-mediated communication", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The analysis subagent finds papers whose key data sit in supplementary files it cannot open. It reports each case to the coordinator, and each round trip adds delay. An engineer suggests a shared board where subagents post these requests for the search subagent. The right follow-up differs: find a public copy, narrow the claim, or ask the committee. It depends on what other subagents have found. Which design <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep sending each case to the coordinator as a typed field in the analysis result, and let it choose.", "why": "Best. Hub-and-spoke: the coordinator sees every subagent's results, so it is the one place that can pick between searching, narrowing the claim or asking the committee."}, {"t": "Build the shared board, have the search subagent watch it, and let the coordinator read it later.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Requests move faster, but subagents now act around the coordinator, which can no longer pick the right follow-up or handle failures.", "trap": "bypasses the coordinator"}, {"t": "Give the analysis subagent a scoped tool to find public copies, and send other cases to the coordinator.", "why": "Works, but only part of the job. A scoped tool suits one frequent, simple need. Here a search is only one of several follow-ups, and choosing it needs findings the analysis subagent cannot see.", "trap": "only part of the job"}, {"t": "Have the analysis subagent collect its cases and report them all to the coordinator once, at the end.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Fewer round trips, but every follow-up now waits until analysis is finished.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "It depends on what other subagents have found.", "explanation": "In a coordinator-subagent design, requests between subagents flow through the coordinator, which sees all results and decides routing. Scoped cross-role tools are the exception for frequent, simple needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A subagent keeps hitting files it can't open, and asking the coordinator each time is slow. Someone wants subagents to talk to each other directly. The clue: the right next step <i>depends on what other subagents have found</i>.", "answer": "Think of a hospital ward where the charge nurse knows what every nurse is doing. If nurses pass jobs to each other on a whiteboard, nobody sees the whole picture. The charge nurse is the <b>coordinator</b> in a <b>hub-and-spoke</b> design: every request goes through them, because only they know enough to choose. Sending the case as a clear, typed field keeps it quick to read. <b>Rule: a choice that needs the whole picture stays with the coordinator.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The one who sees every result makes the choice.", "A whiteboard between nurses is quicker, but the charge nurse loses track and can't pick the right next step.", "A tool for one common job is fine, but here the right job changes case by case.", "Saving all the questions for the end means fewer trips, but every answer now arrives late."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>round-trip delay should be fixed by letting subagents talk directly</b>. Subagents have <b>isolated context</b>: each sees only its own task. The coordinator is the only one that sees everything, and here the right follow-up depends on everything. A scoped tool is the exception for one frequent, simple need, which this is not.", "diagram": "analysis ──case──▶ coordinator ──▶ search / narrow / ask\n                   (sees all results)", "mapTitle": "Who should decide what happens next?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best design", "Why"], "rows": [["Next step depends on other subagents' results", "Route through the coordinator", "Only it sees every result"], ["One frequent, simple need with one answer", "Scoped cross-role tool", "Skips round trips for the common case"], ["Complex or rare follow-ups", "Coordinator re-delegates", "Keeps routing and errors in one place"], ["Subagents post work to each other", "Avoid", "Bypasses the hub; nobody owns the outcome"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It reports each case to the coordinator, and each round trip adds delay", "means": "The cost of the current design is delay, not wrong answers."}, {"quote": "The right follow-up differs: find a public copy, narrow the claim, or ask the committee", "means": "Several possible actions, so a tool for one of them (<b>[[2]]</b>) covers only part."}, {"quote": "It depends on what other subagents have found", "means": "The decider. Only the coordinator sees that. A shared board (<b>[[1]]</b>) bypasses it."}, {"quote": "Which design fits best?", "means": "Keep the hub, send the case as a typed field: <b>[[0]]</b>. Batching to the end (<b>[[3]]</b>) only moves the wait."}], "rule": "If the right next step needs the whole picture, route it through the coordinator.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Hub-and-spoke: coordinator manages all inter-subagent communication, error handling, routing"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-03", "was": "m6-s3-03", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Dependent vs parallel spawning", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "For each request, the coordinator sends four Task calls in one response: literature search, trial-registry search, safety review and cost search. The safety reviewer must check adverse-event reports for the trials in scope. Which trials are in scope is known only after the registry search returns. Today the safety review is generic and often covers the wrong trials. The other three subagents work well. Which change <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep four parallel calls, and give the safety prompt the drug, date range, output format and quality bar.", "why": "Works, but missing input. Richer context helps a subagent whose work stands alone. The trial list it needs does not exist until the registry search returns.", "trap": "missing input"}, {"t": "Add a step where the coordinator checks the safety output against the trial list and redoes mismatches.", "why": "Works, but too late. A check-and-redo loop suits gaps found late. This dependency is known up front, so redoing the work each run is avoidable.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Give the safety subagent the registry search tool, and let it look up the trials in scope itself.", "why": "Works, but duplicate work. The safety reviewer could find the trials, but it repeats a search another subagent already runs, outside its role.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Send the safety Task after the registry search returns, with its trial list; run the rest in parallel.", "why": "Best. A dependent subtask waits for its input, which goes into its prompt. Independent searches still run in parallel."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Which trials are in scope is known only after the registry search returns.", "explanation": "Subagents receive only what is in their prompt. Spawn independent work in parallel, but run a subtask after the one whose findings it needs, and include those findings in its prompt.", "eli5": {"question": "Four helpers start at the same time, but one of them needs another's result before it can do its job. The clue: the trials in scope are known <i>only after the registry search returns</i>.", "answer": "Think of a kitchen. Three cooks can start at once, but the person plating dessert must wait until they know which desserts were ordered. Starting them early just makes them guess. So the <b>dependent</b> task starts after its input exists, and that input goes into its instructions. The independent ones still run <b>in parallel</b>. <b>Rule: independent work in parallel; dependent work waits and gets the result in its prompt.</b>", "options": ["Better instructions help a cook who already has the order. This one doesn't have it yet.", "Checking the plate and redoing it works, but you knew in advance it would be wrong.", "Letting the dessert cook take the orders too works, but now two people do the same job.", "Correct. Wait for the order, hand it over, and let the other cooks carry on."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>parallel is always faster, so a weak subagent needs a better prompt</b>. Subagents get only what is in their prompt; they do not see each other's results. When one task needs another's output, starting it early forces it to guess. The fix is <b>order</b>: wait, then pass the result in explicitly.", "diagram": "lit search ─┐\ncost search ─┼─ in parallel\nregistry ────┘──▶ trial list ──▶ safety review", "mapTitle": "How should these subagents be started?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "How to spawn", "Why"], "rows": [["Tasks need nothing from each other", "Several Task calls in one response", "Run in parallel"], ["A task needs another's output", "Wait, then pass that output in its prompt", "No shared memory between subagents"], ["A task stands alone but output is vague", "Richer prompt: goal, scope, quality bar", "Its input exists already"], ["Gaps only seen after the draft", "Refinement loop", "Not knowable up front"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator sends four Task calls in one response", "means": "Everything starts at once, including the task that needs input."}, {"quote": "Which trials are in scope is known only after the registry search returns", "means": "The decider. A richer prompt (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't contain a list that doesn't exist yet."}, {"quote": "Today the safety review is generic and often covers the wrong trials", "means": "The symptom of a subagent guessing at its input. Checking and redoing (<b>[[1]]</b>) treats it after the fact."}, {"quote": "The other three subagents work well", "means": "Keep them in parallel. Wait only for the dependent one: <b>[[3]]</b>. Its own registry tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) repeats work."}], "rule": "Dependent subtask → spawn after its input exists and pass it in the prompt; independent ones stay parallel.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt (no automatic inheritance / shared memory)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-04", "was": "m6-s3-04", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Selecting subagents by query complexity", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Committees complain that every request takes several minutes. Most requests are narrow questions, such as the dose one trial used. For those, the coordinator still runs search, analysis, synthesis and reporting in order. Synthesis and reporting take most of the time and change the answer little. Summaries for broad requests are rated well. What <b>best addresses</b> the complaint?", "options": [{"t": "Run the literature and registry searches as parallel Task calls in one coordinator response.", "why": "Works, but small gain. Parallel searches save a little, but most of the time goes on synthesis and reporting.", "trap": "small part of the wait"}, {"t": "Let the coordinator pick the subagents per request and skip drafting for narrow ones.", "why": "Best. The coordinator should pick which subagents to invoke by query complexity, which removes the slow stages from most requests."}, {"t": "Give the report subagent a short-answer template for narrow questions, without the full layout.", "why": "Works, but still runs every stage. A shorter report helps a bit, but synthesis and reporting still run for a one-line answer.", "trap": "still runs every stage"}, {"t": "Cache search and analysis results, and reuse them when a committee repeats an earlier question.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Caching helps repeated questions. The slow part here is drafting, and it runs for new questions too.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Synthesis and reporting take most of the time and change the answer little.", "explanation": "A coordinator should choose which subagents to invoke based on the request, not route everything through the full pipeline.", "eli5": {"question": "Every request is slow, even simple ones. The clue: for narrow questions the slow stages (synthesis and reporting) <i>change the answer little</i>.", "answer": "Asking a hospital pharmacy what dose a trial used shouldn't trigger a full written report. A good desk lead decides how much work each question needs. That is the coordinator <b>selecting subagents by query complexity</b>: narrow questions skip the drafting stages; broad ones get the full pipeline. <b>Rule: don't run the whole pipeline for every request. Let the coordinator choose.</b>", "options": ["Starting two searches together saves a little, but the slow part is the writing afterwards.", "Correct. Small questions skip the long write-up; big ones still get it.", "A shorter report form still means writing a report for a one-line answer.", "Remembering old answers helps repeat questions, but the write-up is still slow for new ones."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a slow pipeline needs each stage to be faster</b>. The real waste is running stages that don't help. The coordinator owns decomposition, and part of that job is <b>choosing which subagents a request needs</b>. Broad requests still get the full pipeline.", "diagram": "narrow: search → analysis → answer\nbroad:  search → analysis → synthesis → report", "mapTitle": "Requests are slow. Where does the time go?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Simple requests run every stage", "Fixed full pipeline", "Coordinator picks subagents by complexity"], ["Independent searches run one after another", "Serial spawning", "Several Task calls in one response"], ["Subagents fetch the same documents", "Overlapping scope", "Partition scope between subagents"], ["Same questions asked again", "Repeated work", "Reuse earlier results"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Most requests are narrow questions, such as the dose one trial used", "means": "Most traffic needs a short answer."}, {"quote": "For those, the coordinator still runs search, analysis, synthesis and reporting in order", "means": "The cause: the full pipeline runs every time. A shorter template (<b>[[2]]</b>) still runs it."}, {"quote": "Synthesis and reporting take most of the time and change the answer little", "means": "The decider. Parallel searches (<b>[[0]]</b>) and caching (<b>[[3]]</b>) speed up stages that aren't the slow ones."}, {"quote": "Summaries for broad requests are rated well", "means": "Keep the full pipeline for those. Choose per request: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "The coordinator picks which subagents to run per request; don't route every request through the full pipeline.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "selecting which subagents to invoke by query complexity"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-05", "was": "m6-s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Resume vs fresh session with summary", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "A research session on a heart-failure drug was paused on Friday. By then the analysis subagent had read dozens of papers. Over the weekend, the journal replaced most of those papers with corrected versions. Several tables the coordinator had quoted have changed. The session history still holds the original tool results. On Monday the team wants to continue with two new questions. What is the <b>most reliable</b> way to continue?", "options": [{"t": "Resume with <code>--resume hf-drug</code>, tell the agent which papers were corrected, then add the two new questions.", "why": "Works, but stale history. Resuming and naming the changes is right when a few files changed. Here most results are out of date, and the old tables still fill the history.", "trap": "stale history"}, {"t": "Resume with <code>--resume hf-drug</code>, run <code>/compact</code> to shrink the history, then re-read the corrected papers.", "why": "Works, but keeps stale facts. Compacting shrinks the history, but it summarises the old tables instead of removing them.", "trap": "keeps stale facts"}, {"t": "Open a new session from a structured summary of what still holds, and re-read the corrected papers.", "why": "Best. When much of the earlier tool output is stale, a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming."}, {"t": "Use <code>fork_session</code> to branch Friday's session: one branch for old questions, one for new.", "why": "Works, but copies the problem. Forking suits trying different approaches from a good baseline. Both branches inherit the stale results.", "trap": "copies the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Over the weekend, the journal replaced most of those papers with corrected versions.", "explanation": "Resume when earlier context is still mostly valid and tell the agent what changed. Start fresh with a structured summary when much of the history is stale.", "eli5": {"question": "Work paused on Friday, and over the weekend most of the papers it read were corrected. The clue: <i>most</i> of the papers changed, and the history still holds the old versions.", "answer": "If one page of your notes is wrong, you fix that page. If most of the notebook copied from an old edition, you start a clean notebook with just what still holds, and re-read the new edition. That is a <b>new session with a structured summary</b>. Resuming keeps the old tables in Claude's history, and it may keep reasoning from them. <b>Rule: a little changed → resume and say what changed. Much is stale → start fresh from a summary.</b>", "options": ["Saying \"page 4 changed\" works for a few pages. Here most of the notebook is out of date.", "Shrinking an old notebook keeps a short version of the old facts.", "Correct. A clean notebook with only what still holds, plus a list of papers to re-read.", "Photocopying the old notebook twice gives you two notebooks with the same old facts."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>resuming is always best because it keeps the work</b>. It keeps the stale work too. Tool results in the history are what Claude reasons from. When most of them are out of date, a new session from a <b>structured summary</b> of what still holds is more reliable.", "mapTitle": "Continue an old session, or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Little changed since the pause", "<code>--resume</code> and say what changed", "History mostly valid"], ["Much of the tool output is stale", "New session from a structured summary", "Old results would mislead"], ["Try two approaches from a good baseline", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches"], ["Session healthy but nearly full", "<code>/compact</code>", "Frees space, keeps the work"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "By then the analysis subagent had read dozens of papers", "means": "The history is full of tool results from those papers."}, {"quote": "Over the weekend, the journal replaced most of those papers with corrected versions", "means": "The decider. <i>Most</i>, not a few, so resuming and naming changes (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves mostly stale history."}, {"quote": "The session history still holds the original tool results", "means": "Compacting (<b>[[1]]</b>) summarises them and forking (<b>[[3]]</b>) copies them."}, {"quote": "What is the most reliable way to continue?", "means": "Start fresh from what still holds: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Most tool results stale → new session from a structured summary, not --resume.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "Starting a new session with a structured summary is more reliable than resuming with stale tool results"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-06", "was": "m6-s3-06", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 PostToolUse normalization", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Three vendor-run MCP servers feed the analysis subagent. Each reports publication dates in its own format: Unix timestamps, quarter strings like 'Q3 2024', or day-first dates. The synthesis subagent sometimes puts trials in the wrong order. The vendors run these servers, and the team cannot change their responses. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that rewrites those dates to one format before Claude reads the results.", "why": "Best. A PostToolUse hook rewrites tool results in code before the model sees them, on the side the team controls."}, {"t": "Run a small MCP proxy server of the team's own in front of the vendors that converts every date.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A proxy fixes the dates, but it is a new server to run and maintain. A hook does the same inside the agent.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add few-shot examples to the analysis prompt that convert each vendor's date format to ISO 8601.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples improve consistency, but the conversion stays probabilistic, and day-first dates are easy to misread.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Show each source's publication date beside every claim drawn from it in the summary the committee gets.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Readers can spot an odd order, but the dates are still misread upstream and the order is still wrong.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The vendors run these servers, and the team cannot change their responses.", "explanation": "PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results and normalise them before the model sees them, giving deterministic behaviour where prompts give only probabilistic compliance.", "eli5": {"question": "Three outside data feeds write dates three different ways, and trials end up in the wrong order. The clue: the team <i>can't change</i> the feeds.", "answer": "If three suppliers send invoices with dates written differently, you put one clerk at the door who rewrites every date the same way before anyone files them. That clerk is a <b>PostToolUse hook</b>: code that runs on each tool result before Claude reads it. It works every time, not most times. <b>Rule: messy formats you can't fix at the source → normalise them in a hook.</b>", "options": ["Correct. One clerk at the door rewrites every date before anyone reads it.", "Opening a separate office to rewrite the dates works, but it's a whole office to run.", "Showing staff examples helps most of the time, but some dates still get misread.", "Writing the date on the final report doesn't fix the order things were filed in."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the fix belongs in the prompt or at the source</b>. The source is not the team's to change, and a prompt only usually converts correctly. A <b>PostToolUse hook</b> sits in the team's own agent and rewrites results in code before the model reasons over them.", "diagram": "vendor MCP → raw date → PostToolUse hook → ISO date → Claude", "mapTitle": "Tool results arrive in mixed formats. Where do you fix them?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["You run the server", "Fix the format at the source", "Cleanest, one place"], ["Someone else runs the server", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook in your agent", "Deterministic, on your side"], ["Need a shared fix for many agents and teams", "Your own proxy server", "Worth running only at that scale"], ["Wording or style varies, not data", "Few-shot examples", "Usually right is enough"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each reports publication dates in its own format", "means": "Mixed formats reach the model as they are."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent sometimes puts trials in the wrong order", "means": "The misread dates cause real errors. Showing dates to readers (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the order wrong."}, {"quote": "The vendors run these servers, and the team cannot change their responses", "means": "The decider. Fix on the team's side. A hook does it in code (<b>[[0]]</b>); examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) only usually; a proxy (<b>[[1]]</b>) adds a server."}], "rule": "Normalise heterogeneous tool results in a PostToolUse hook before the model sees them.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-05", "was": "m6-s4-05", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Forking from a shared baseline", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer spent an hour with Claude mapping how the lesson-sync code uses a blocking Redis client. They now want to try two migrations: one to an async client and one to a connection pool. Each should go far enough to show test failures, and the better one is kept. No files have changed since the mapping, and the context is under half full. How should they <b>go on</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Ask Claude for a structured summary of the mapping, then seed two new sessions with it, one per migration.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. A fresh session with a summary is right when tool results are stale or context is nearly full. Neither is true, and a summary drops detail.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Branch the session twice from the end of the mapping with <code>fork_session</code>, one migration per branch.", "why": "Best. Forking makes independent branches from a shared, still-valid baseline, which is what comparing two approaches needs."}, {"t": "Spawn two subagents with the Task tool, and give each the mapping findings and one migration to build.", "why": "Works, but the edits clash. Both subagents change the same working tree, and a prompt carries less than the full mapping session.", "trap": "clashing edits"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode and have Claude design both migrations side by side before picking one to build.", "why": "Works, but skips the trial. Plan mode compares designs on paper, while the engineer wants each migration far enough to see real test failures.", "trap": "skips the trial"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No files have changed since the mapping, and the context is under half full.", "explanation": "fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline. When that baseline is still valid, forking keeps all of it for each approach.", "eli5": {"question": "After an hour of good groundwork, someone wants to try two different paths from the same point. The clues: <i>nothing has changed</i> and the session still has room, so the groundwork is still good.", "answer": "Picture a save point in a game. You want to try the left door and the right door, both from the same save. You don't write notes and start a new game. You load the save twice (<b>fork_session</b>). <b>Rule: two approaches from one still-valid baseline → fork. Baseline stale or context full → new session with a summary.</b>", "options": ["Writing notes and starting new games: right when the save is broken. Here it isn't, and notes miss detail.", "Correct. Load the same save twice and try each door.", "Two helpers in the same room, both rearranging the same furniture at once.", "Planning both routes on a map. The engineer wants to actually walk them and see what breaks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a fresh start with a summary is the clean way</b> to begin new work. That is right when the old session is stale or full. Here it is neither, so the session itself is the best baseline, and forking copies all of it into two independent branches.", "diagram": "mapping session (1 hour, still valid)\n   |-- fork A: async client   -> run tests\n   |-- fork B: connection pool -> run tests\nkeep the better branch", "mapTitle": "Resume, fork or start fresh?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Two approaches from one valid baseline", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Independent branches, full context"], ["Baseline stale or context nearly full", "New session + structured summary", "Old results are unreliable"], ["Continue one named past session", "<code>--resume &lt;name&gt;</code>", "Same conversation, same work"], ["Compare designs before writing code", "Plan mode", "Explore safely before changes"], ["Hand a focused subtask to another agent", "Task subagent with findings in its prompt", "No automatic context sharing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "They now want to try two migrations", "means": "Two divergent approaches from one starting point."}, {"quote": "Each should go far enough to show test failures", "means": "They must be built, not only designed, so plan mode (<b>[[3]]</b>) stops short."}, {"quote": "No files have changed since the mapping, and the context is under half full", "means": "The decider. The baseline is still good, so a summary (<b>[[0]]</b>) only loses detail. Fork it: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Two paths from a still-valid session → fork_session. Stale or full → new session with a summary.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-06", "was": "m6-s4-06", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Per-item passes plus integration", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Before a reliability review, a staff engineer asks Claude how each of the app's 25 API clients retries failed calls. The reviewers set the questions in advance: retry count, backoff, timeout and idempotency key. Claude did it all in one long session. Early rows were detailed, and later rows were thin. It also contradicted itself about a shared retry module. What is the <b>best way</b> to restructure the work?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude draft an investigation plan from the first clients and add subtasks as new patterns turn up.", "why": "Works, but fits unknown work. Adaptive plans suit work whose shape comes from discoveries. Here the clients and the questions were set in advance.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Hand the job to an Explore subagent that reads every client in its own context and returns one table.", "why": "Works, but moves the problem. The main session stays clean, but one long pass inside the subagent still thins out.", "trap": "moves the problem"}, {"t": "Have Claude note each client's findings in a scratchpad file and check it before writing later rows.", "why": "Works, but only half the problem. The file keeps findings across context limits, but it is still one long pass with uneven attention.", "trap": "only half the problem"}, {"t": "Give each client its own focused pass, then run one comparison pass that settles the shared module question.", "why": "Best. A focused pass per client gives even depth, and a separate integration pass reconciles what they say about the shared module."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Early rows were detailed, and later rows were thin.", "explanation": "Prompt chaining: analyze each item on its own, then run a cross-item integration pass. This avoids attention dilution and resolves contradictions.", "eli5": {"question": "One long job got worse as it went on and contradicted itself. The clue: <i>early rows detailed, later rows thin</i>. Attention ran out over one long pass.", "answer": "A teacher marking 25 essays in one sitting gives the first ones care and the last ones a glance. Better: mark each essay on its own, fresh (<b>a focused pass per item</b>). Then read all the marks side by side to settle disagreements (<b>an integration pass</b>). <b>Rule: many items, same questions → one pass per item, then one pass across all of them.</b>", "options": ["Making up the plan as you go suits a job you don't understand yet. Here the questions were fixed in advance.", "Another teacher marking all 25 in one sitting gets just as tired.", "Notes help you remember, but you are still marking all 25 in one tired sitting.", "Correct. Each essay fresh, then one side-by-side check."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is memory</b>, so a notes file or a fresh context fixes it. The pattern here is attention dilution: one pass over many items gives later items less care, and nothing compares them at the end. Splitting into one pass per item plus a cross-item pass fixes both symptoms.", "mapTitle": "How should a big analysis be split?", "map": {"head": ["Shape of the work", "Strategy", "Why"], "rows": [["Known items, fixed questions", "Per-item passes + one integration pass", "Even depth, contradictions settled"], ["Shape unknown, comes from findings", "Adaptive investigation plan", "Subtasks follow discoveries"], ["Findings must survive context limits", "Scratchpad file", "Persists key facts"], ["Verbose discovery crowds the main session", "Explore subagent", "Isolates output"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The reviewers set the questions in advance", "means": "The work is known up front, so an adaptive plan (<b>[[0]]</b>) solves a problem they don't have."}, {"quote": "Claude did it all in one long session", "means": "One pass over every client is the cause."}, {"quote": "Early rows were detailed, and later rows were thin", "means": "The decider. Attention dilution, which a scratchpad (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a subagent (<b>[[1]]</b>) doing the same long pass keeps."}, {"quote": "It also contradicted itself about a shared retry module", "means": "A cross-client pass is needed to reconcile it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Many items with fixed questions → one focused pass per item, then one integration pass.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Prompt chaining: analyze each file individually, then a cross-file integration pass"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-07", "was": "m6-s4-07", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Structured handoff for a human", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Late at night, an engineer uses Claude to look into a jump in lesson load times. Claude traces it to a caching config change and drafts a rollback. The engineer's shift is ending. An engineer from the infrastructure team will do the rollback. That engineer works only from the incident ticket and does not use Claude Code. What should the engineer have Claude <b>produce</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A short incident note: service, cause, evidence with file references, revert steps, open questions.", "why": "Best. A structured handoff summary lets a person act without the session or its transcript."}, {"t": "A scratchpad file in the repository with each finding and its evidence, for a later session to load.", "why": "Works, but built for an agent. Scratchpad files carry findings across context limits for Claude, while the next person works from the ticket.", "trap": "built for an agent"}, {"t": "The full transcript of the investigation, attached to the incident ticket through the Linear server.", "why": "Works, but too much to act on. Everything is there, but the infrastructure engineer must dig the cause and the steps out of a long conversation.", "trap": "too much to act on"}, {"t": "A pull request with the rollback change, linked from the ticket, and a commit message describing it.", "why": "Works, but only half the handoff. The change is ready, but the cause, the evidence and the open questions are missing.", "trap": "only half the handoff"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "That engineer works only from the incident ticket and does not use Claude Code.", "explanation": "When a human takes over without the transcript, compile a structured handoff summary: what is affected, root cause, evidence and the recommended action.", "eli5": {"question": "Work passes from Claude's session to a person who never uses Claude Code. The clue: they work <i>only from the incident ticket</i>.", "answer": "When a night nurse hands over to the day nurse, she doesn't hand over her diary or her shopping list. She writes a short handover note: patient, problem, what was seen, what to do next, what's unclear (<b>a structured handoff summary</b>). <b>Rule: handing over to a person without the transcript → a short structured summary they can act on.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The handover note, in the ticket the next person reads.", "Notes for Claude's next session. The next person isn't using Claude.", "The whole diary. Everything is in it, but the reader has to dig.", "The medicine is ready, but nobody says why, what was seen, or what's still unclear."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more information is a better handoff</b>, or that a ready fix is enough. A person taking over needs the cause, the proof and the next steps in one short, structured place they already use. Tools for carrying context between Claude sessions don't reach them.", "mapTitle": "Who picks up the work, and what do they need?", "map": {"head": ["Next actor", "Handoff", "Why"], "rows": [["A person, from a ticket, no transcript", "Structured summary: cause, evidence, action", "Actionable without the session"], ["A later Claude session", "Scratchpad file with key findings", "Survives context limits"], ["The same engineer, same work later", "<code>--resume &lt;name&gt;</code>", "Same conversation"], ["A reviewer approving a code change", "Pull request with a clear description", "Reviews the change itself"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude traces it to a caching config change and drafts a rollback", "means": "There is a cause, evidence and an action to pass on, not only a change."}, {"quote": "An engineer from the infrastructure team will do the rollback", "means": "The next actor is a person, so agent-facing notes (<b>[[1]]</b>) miss them."}, {"quote": "That engineer works only from the incident ticket and does not use Claude Code", "means": "The decider. It must be short and in the ticket: <b>[[0]]</b>. A transcript (<b>[[2]]</b>) is too much; a PR (<b>[[3]]</b>) lacks the why."}], "rule": "Handing work to a person without the transcript → a short structured summary: cause, evidence, action, open questions.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-01", "was": "m6-s6-01", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Adaptive decomposition", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Some submissions hold a certificate plus several policy endorsements. The pipeline runs a fixed chain: the certificate, then each attached endorsement, then a step that merges the results. Certificates often cite endorsements that were not attached. The agent can fetch these with <code>fetch_endorsement</code>. A fetched endorsement may change limits or cite yet another form. Which way of splitting the work <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the fixed chain, and add a final pass that reconciles every field across all the extractions.", "why": "Works, but only covers what arrived. A reconcile pass tidies the attached documents, but nothing in the chain fetches the forms they cite.", "trap": "only covers what arrived"}, {"t": "Run one subagent per attached document in parallel, and let the coordinator merge their fields.", "why": "Works, but only covers what arrived. Parallel subagents read the attached documents faster, but none of them follows a citation to a missing form.", "trap": "only covers what arrived"}, {"t": "Let the coordinator plan from a first read, adding fetch and extract steps as citations turn up.", "why": "Best. Adaptive decomposition creates subtasks from what is found. Each citation, including one inside a fetched form, becomes a new fetch and extract step."}, {"t": "Keep the chain, but first fetch every endorsement the certificate cites before extraction starts.", "why": "Works, but one level deep. Fetching the certificate's citations up front catches most gaps, but a fetched endorsement can cite another form that the chain never fetches.", "trap": "one level deep"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "A fetched endorsement may change limits or cite yet another form.", "explanation": "Use a fixed chain when the inputs are known up front. Use adaptive decomposition when the subtasks depend on what is found along the way.", "eli5": {"question": "The pipeline always runs the same steps, but some needed documents only show up while reading. The clue is: <i>a fetched endorsement may change limits or cite yet another form</i>. The work list grows as you read.", "answer": "Think of a detective who reads a letter, finds a mention of a second letter, fetches it, and finds a third mention there. You cannot write the full to-do list before you start. That is <b>adaptive decomposition</b>: the coordinator plans from a first read and adds a fetch-and-extract step for each citation it finds. A fixed chain (<b>prompt chaining</b>) is great when you know every document in advance. <b>Rule: if the next task depends on what you just found, plan as you go.</b>", "options": ["Tidying up the documents you have is useful, but no one goes to get the ones that are missing.", "Reading the attached documents side by side is faster, but still nobody fetches the missing forms.", "Correct. Each new citation becomes a new task, however deep the chain of forms goes.", "Close. Fetching what the certificate names covers the first level, but a fetched form can name another one, and the chain stops there."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a fixed pipeline just needs one more step</b>, such as a pre-fetch or a reconcile pass. Those work when the full set of documents is known before you start. Here the set <b>grows while reading</b>: a fetched endorsement can cite another form. Only a plan that adds subtasks from what it finds can follow that.", "diagram": "certificate ─cites→ endorsement A (fetch)\n                     └─cites→ form B (fetch)\nfixed chain: stops at attachments\nadaptive:    adds a step per citation", "mapTitle": "Which way of splitting the work fits these inputs?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best split", "Why"], "rows": [["Same documents every time, known up front", "Fixed chain (prompt chaining)", "Predictable steps, easy to test"], ["One long document", "Per-section passes plus an integration pass", "Avoids attention dilution"], ["Documents cite others that must be fetched, possibly in turn", "Adaptive decomposition by the coordinator", "Subtasks come from what is found"], ["Citations go only one level deep", "Fixed chain with an up-front fetch step", "The full set is known after one read"], ["Many independent documents", "Parallel subagents, coordinator merges", "Faster, no dependencies"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The pipeline runs a fixed chain: the certificate, then each attached endorsement, then a step that merges the results", "means": "Today's design only knows about the attached documents. A reconcile pass (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps that limit."}, {"quote": "Certificates often cite endorsements that were not attached", "means": "Some needed documents are missing. Parallel subagents over the attachments (<b>[[1]]</b>) never see them."}, {"quote": "A fetched endorsement may change limits or cite yet another form", "means": "The decider. Fetching only the certificate's citations (<b>[[3]]</b>) stops one level deep."}, {"quote": "Which way of splitting the work fits best?", "means": "A plan that adds steps as citations surface: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "When the next subtask depends on what was just found, let the coordinator plan adaptively instead of running a fixed chain.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Adaptive investigation plans generate subtasks from what's discovered"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-02", "was": "m6-s6-02", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Explicit context passing between subagents", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator starts three extraction subagents in one response: header, limits and endorsements. The endorsements subagent must confirm that each endorsement belongs to this policy, so it needs the policy number. Endorsement forms often print older policy numbers too, and the subagent sometimes picks the wrong one. The header subagent reads the policy number reliably. Which change <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give the endorsements subagent a scoped <code>read_policy_number</code> tool, and keep all three running in parallel.", "why": "Works, but duplicates work. A scoped tool lets it look the number up, but the header subagent already finds it reliably, and two lookups can disagree.", "trap": "duplicates work"}, {"t": "Run header extraction first, and have the coordinator copy its result into the endorsement task.", "why": "Best. Subagents do not inherit context, so the coordinator passes the finding in the prompt. Only the dependent pair runs in order; limits stays parallel."}, {"t": "Add example endorsements to the subagent's prompt that show where the current number is printed.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. Examples help it find the right number more often, but it still searches for a value another subagent already has.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "After both finish, compare the two numbers, and rerun the endorsements subagent on any mismatch.", "why": "Works, but too late. The check catches the mistake, but every mismatch costs a second run for a value that could have been passed in first.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The header subagent reads the policy number reliably.", "explanation": "Subagents have isolated context, so the coordinator must pass earlier findings in the prompt. Run only true dependencies in order and keep the rest parallel.", "eli5": {"question": "One helper needs a fact that another helper already finds well. The clue is: <i>the header subagent reads the policy number reliably</i>. So the question is how to hand that fact over, not how to find it again.", "answer": "Think of a kitchen where one cook weighs the flour and another bakes. The baker should not weigh the flour again; the head chef passes the weight along. Subagents work the same way: they <b>do not share memory</b>, so the coordinator must <b>write the finding into the next task prompt</b>. Run the header first, then the endorsements subagent with the number in hand. Limits still runs alongside. <b>Rule: if one subagent needs another's result, the coordinator passes it in explicitly.</b>", "options": ["Close. A lookup tool fits a value needed often during a task. Here it is needed once, and another helper already has it.", "Correct. The number is passed in, so there is nothing to guess.", "Better examples help it guess right more often, but it is still re-finding a known value.", "Checking afterwards catches the error, but you pay for a second run each time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the endorsements subagent needs better ways to find the number</b>. But the number is already found, reliably, by the header subagent. The real gap is that <b>subagents do not inherit context</b>. The coordinator must pass the value in, and only the two dependent subagents need to run in order.", "mapTitle": "How should one subagent get a value another one finds?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Needs a value another subagent finds reliably, once", "Run in order, pass it in the task prompt", "No inheritance; explicit passing"], ["Needs a lookup often during its own work", "Scoped cross-role tool", "Frequent need, avoids round trips"], ["Subagents are fully independent", "Several Task calls in one response", "Parallel and fast"], ["Needs the full findings of earlier subagents", "Include them, with sources, in its prompt", "Keeps attribution"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator starts three extraction subagents in one response", "means": "Today all three run in parallel, so nothing flows between them."}, {"quote": "it needs the policy number", "means": "There is a real dependency between two of the three."}, {"quote": "Endorsement forms often print older policy numbers too, and the subagent sometimes picks the wrong one", "means": "Searching on its own is where it goes wrong. Better examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) only make that search more likely to succeed."}, {"quote": "The header subagent reads the policy number reliably", "means": "The decider. The value exists already; a second lookup tool (<b>[[0]]</b>) or a later re-check (<b>[[3]]</b>) duplicates it. Pass it in: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Subagents don't inherit context: pass earlier findings in the task prompt, and serialise only the dependent pair.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt (no automatic inheritance / shared memory)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-03", "was": "m6-s6-03", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Structured handoff to humans", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "When the agent cannot settle a certificate, it hands it to an account manager. Account managers work in the broker's system and cannot see the agent's session. Today they get the PDF and a note saying “needs review”. Most of their time goes on redoing the agent's checks to find what went wrong. What should the agent <b>attach to each handoff</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A confidence score for every field, lowest first, with the PDF pages behind the lowest ones highlighted.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Scores point at weak fields, but they say neither what the doubt is nor what was already checked.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "A source map for every extracted value, giving the page and text it came from, beside the result.", "why": "Works, but buries the issue. Provenance for every value helps checking, but the manager still has to find which field is in doubt and why.", "trap": "buries the issue"}, {"t": "The full transcript of the agent's session, attached for the account manager as a text file.", "why": "Works, but too much to read. The transcript holds everything, but the manager must read the whole session to find the problem.", "trap": "too much to read"}, {"t": "A short case summary: ID, the issue, competing values with pages, checks run and a suggested fix.", "why": "Best. A structured handoff gives someone without the session what they need to act at once."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Most of their time goes on redoing the agent's checks to find what went wrong.", "explanation": "When handing off to a person who cannot see the session, compile a structured summary: identifiers, the issue, evidence with locations, what was tried and the recommended action.", "eli5": {"question": "A person takes over work they did not see. The clue is: <i>most of their time goes on redoing the agent's checks to find what went wrong</i>. They need the story of the problem, not more raw data.", "answer": "Think of a nurse handing over a patient at shift change. She does not hand over the whole chart or every reading. She says who, what is wrong, what was tried and what she suggests. That is a <b>structured handoff summary</b>: ID, the issue, the competing values with pages, the checks already run and a recommended fix. <b>Rule: for a human without the transcript, hand over a short structured case summary.</b>", "options": ["Scores say which fields look weak, not what the doubt is or what was tried.", "Close. A page for every value helps someone re-check the whole result. Here they need the one problem, not a map of everything.", "The whole session has the answer somewhere, but reading it is the slow part.", "Correct. The manager sees the issue, the evidence and a suggestion straight away."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more information helps the reviewer</b>: every score, every source, the whole transcript. But the manager's time is going on <b>reconstructing what went wrong</b>. A handoff should state the problem, show the evidence, say what was tried and suggest a fix.", "mapTitle": "What does the person taking over need?", "map": {"head": ["Reviewer's need", "Best attachment", "Why"], "rows": [["Act on one unresolved problem without the session", "Structured summary: ID, issue, evidence, checks, suggestion", "Ready to act"], ["Re-check every value in a result", "Source map: page and text for each value", "Fast verification"], ["Decide which results need review at all", "Calibrated field confidence", "Routing, not explaining"], ["Audit how the agent behaved", "Session transcript or logs", "Complete, but slow to read"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Account managers work in the broker's system and cannot see the agent's session", "means": "Whatever they need must travel with the handoff."}, {"quote": "Today they get the PDF and a note saying “needs review”", "means": "The note gives no issue, no evidence and no history."}, {"quote": "Most of their time goes on redoing the agent's checks to find what went wrong", "means": "The decider. They need the issue and what was tried. A full source map (<b>[[1]]</b>) and a transcript (<b>[[2]]</b>) make them dig; scores (<b>[[0]]</b>) don't say what was tried."}, {"quote": "What should the agent attach to each handoff?", "means": "A short structured summary: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Humans without the transcript get a structured handoff: identifier, issue, evidence, what was tried, recommended action.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-01", "was": "s5q-s1-01", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Loop termination", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's loop stops when Claude's reply contains a closing line, such as “Anything else I can help with?”. It also stops after fifteen steps. Some refunds are left half-done: the reply opened with a polite wrap-up line and also asked to run <code>process_refund</code>. Some long claims are cut off at the step limit while every step is still making progress. Which change <b>fixes both</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Raise the step limit to forty and set it per claim type from the step counts seen in the logs.", "why": "Works, but only half the problem. Long claims get room to finish, but the loop still ends on wording, so a wrap-up line beside a refund request still cuts the refund off.", "trap": "only half the problem"}, {"t": "Loop while <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>tool_use</code>, stop on <code>end_turn</code>, and keep a high cap as a backstop.", "why": "Best. The stop reason says whether Claude asked for a tool, whatever the text says. Long claims run until Claude is done, and a high cap only guards against runaway loops."}, {"t": "Skip the closing-line check on any reply that also asks to run a tool, and keep the step limit.", "why": "Works, but treats one symptom. Half-done refunds stop, but the loop still guesses from text, and long claims are still cut off at the step limit.", "trap": "treats one symptom"}, {"t": "Have a small classifier read each reply and judge whether the customer's case is finished.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A classifier may judge endings better than a phrase list, but it is still guessing from text. The API already reports this in <code>stop_reason</code>.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Some refunds are left half-done: the reply opened with a polite wrap-up line and also asked to run process_refund.", "explanation": "The loop should continue when stop_reason is tool_use and stop on end_turn. Reading the reply's wording and using an iteration cap as the main stop are both anti-patterns.", "eli5": {"question": "The loop decides it is done by spotting a goodbye phrase or by counting steps. Both go wrong. The clue: a reply said goodbye <i>and</i> asked for a refund in the same breath.", "answer": "Think of a waiter who leaves the table as soon as a guest says “thanks”, even when the guest is still ordering. Better to look at the order pad: is there an order on it or not? Claude's order pad is <b><code>stop_reason</code></b>. <code>tool_use</code> means “run this for me and come back”. <code>end_turn</code> means “I'm finished”. <b>Rule: decide when to stop from stop_reason, not from words or a step count.</b>", "options": ["A longer shift helps the slow tables, but the waiter still walks off when someone says “thanks”.", "Correct. Look at the order pad: keep serving while there is an order, stop when there isn't. A high cap is only a safety net.", "Ignoring “thanks” when an order is on the pad fixes one case, but the shift still ends too early for long tables.", "Hiring someone to judge the mood of each table still guesses. The order pad already tells you."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to see <b>two separate bugs</b>: a phrase check that misfires and a step limit that is too low. They share one cause. The loop is guessing when Claude is done, when <b>Claude already says so</b> in <code>stop_reason</code> on every response.", "diagram": "reply → stop_reason?\n  tool_use → run tool, add result, call again\n  end_turn → done, answer the customer\ncap (high) → only for runaway loops", "mapTitle": "How should the loop decide it is finished?", "map": {"head": ["Signal used", "What goes wrong", "Better"], "rows": [["Closing phrase in the text", "Text and a tool request can come together", "<code>stop_reason</code>"], ["Fixed step count as the main stop", "Long, healthy tasks are cut off", "<code>stop_reason</code>, cap as backstop"], ["Phrase check skipped when a tool is asked for", "Patches one case, still reads text", "<code>stop_reason</code>"], ["Model judging its own text", "Still a guess, extra machinery", "<code>stop_reason</code>"], ["<code>stop_reason</code> = <code>tool_use</code> / <code>end_turn</code>", "Nothing: this is the contract", "Keep it"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent's loop stops when Claude's reply contains a closing line", "means": "Completion is read from text, an anti-pattern in the guide."}, {"quote": "It also stops after fifteen steps", "means": "A step cap is the main stop. Raising it (<b>[[0]]</b>) moves the cut-off, it doesn't remove it."}, {"quote": "the reply opened with a polite wrap-up line and also asked to run process_refund", "means": "The decider. Text and a tool request arrive together, so any text check can misfire. Skipping it only here (<b>[[2]]</b>) fixes one case."}, {"quote": "while every step is still making progress", "means": "The work is healthy; only the stopping rule is wrong."}, {"quote": "Which change fixes both?", "means": "One change for both symptoms: branch on <code>stop_reason</code>, <b>[[1]]</b>. A classifier (<b>[[3]]</b>) still guesses from text."}], "rule": "Continue on tool_use, stop on end_turn. A cap is a safety net, never the main stop.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Anti-patterns: parsing natural-language signals to determine termination; arbitrary iteration caps as the primary stop"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-03", "was": "s5q-s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks for policy enforcement", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "The $150 limit is one sentence in the system prompt. Last month several larger refunds went out with no human approval. In each chat the agent mentioned the limit, then decided the case was special, such as a spoiled anniversary dinner. Finance treats any unapproved refund above the limit as a control failure. These customers must still reach a person. Which change <b>best fits</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite the escalation section with clear rules for over-limit cases, plus two examples of escalating.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. Clear rules and examples cut down the exceptions, but a prompt is followed most of the time, and finance needs it every time.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook on <code>process_refund</code> that escalates when an issued amount is over the limit.", "why": "Works, but too late. A person hears about the case, but the hook runs after the refund has gone out, so the control failure has already happened.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Have a second Claude call review each refund the agent proposes, and approve or reject it first.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A reviewer would catch many of these, but it is another model judging the same policy, so it is still not certain, and it adds a call to every refund.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on <code>process_refund</code> that blocks amounts over the limit and points to escalation.", "why": "Best. Code checks every refund before it runs, blocks the ones over the limit, and steers the agent to <code>escalate_to_human</code>, so the customer still reaches a person."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Finance treats any unapproved refund above the limit as a control failure.", "explanation": "A rule that must hold every time belongs in code. A hook that intercepts outgoing process_refund calls blocks over-limit amounts and redirects to escalation; prompt rules have a non-zero failure rate.", "eli5": {"question": "The refund limit is written in the instructions, and the agent sometimes talks itself past it. Finance says this must not happen. The clue: <i>any</i> refund above the limit is a failure, so the rule must hold every time.", "answer": "A sign on the till saying “no refunds over $150” works most days. A till that will not open for more than $150, and shows “call the manager” instead, works every day. That till lock is a <b>PreToolUse hook</b>: code that checks each <code>process_refund</code> call before it runs. <b>Rule: if a rule must hold every time, enforce it in code before the action, not in the prompt.</b>", "options": ["A clearer sign with examples helps, but people still talk themselves past signs sometimes.", "An alarm after the till opens tells the manager, but the money has already gone.", "A second cashier double-checking helps, but they are judging too, and every refund now waits for them.", "Correct. The till itself refuses big refunds and says “call the manager”."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent misunderstood the limit</b>, so clearer wording will fix it. The transcripts show it understood and chose an exception anyway. Any prompt-based rule has a <b>non-zero failure rate</b>. When even one miss is a control failure, the check has to be code that runs before the refund.", "diagram": "agent → process_refund(amount)\n   PreToolUse hook: amount > limit?\n     yes → block, tell agent to escalate\n     no  → refund runs", "mapTitle": "How hard must this rule hold, and where is it checked?", "map": {"head": ["Requirement", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Must hold every time, before the action", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks", "Deterministic, nothing goes out"], ["Better judgement wanted, some misses fine", "Clear criteria + few-shot examples", "Cheap, steers most cases"], ["Change the data Claude sees", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook", "Runs after the tool, before Claude reads it"], ["Order of steps must hold", "Prerequisite gate", "Blocks a tool until another has run"], ["Second opinion on hard cases", "Reviewer model", "Still probabilistic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The $150 limit is one sentence in the system prompt", "means": "The rule is only a request today."}, {"quote": "In each chat the agent mentioned the limit, then decided the case was special", "means": "It knew the rule. Better wording (<b>[[0]]</b>) cuts exceptions but can't remove them."}, {"quote": "Finance treats any unapproved refund above the limit as a control failure", "means": "The decider. It must hold every time and before money moves. A hook after the refund (<b>[[1]]</b>) is too late; a reviewer model (<b>[[2]]</b>) still judges."}, {"quote": "These customers must still reach a person", "means": "Block and redirect to escalation, not just refuse: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "A business rule that must never be broken → a hook that blocks the tool call before it runs, and redirects.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "Hooks intercept outgoing tool calls to enforce compliance (e.g., block refunds above a threshold)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-08", "was": "s5q-s1-08", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Multi-step handoff", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "Escalations reach a person with a structured block: customer ID, root cause, suggested refund and next step. Staff then settle the case in the helpdesk and close it with a free-text note. Each team writes its own shorthand, such as “RFD 40 pd, TL ok”. When those customers write in again, the agent offers remedies that contradict what staff agreed. Which change fixes this with the <b>least added complexity</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give the agent a read-only helpdesk tool and have it fetch the closing note for a returning customer.", "why": "Works, but hard to read. The agent now sees the decision, but each team's shorthand must be guessed at, so it can still misread what was agreed.", "trap": "hard to read"}, {"t": "Send customers with a past escalation straight to the person who handled them, skipping the agent.", "why": "Works, but too broad. Staff know what they agreed, but a whole class of chats leaves the agent when the only gap is missing information.", "trap": "too broad"}, {"t": "Resume the original escalated session when the customer returns and continue from its history.", "why": "Works, but wrong record. The agent recalls the case up to the handoff, but the staff decision was made later in the helpdesk and isn't in that session.", "trap": "wrong record"}, {"t": "Add a resolution section to the handoff block that staff fill in on closing, and load it as case facts.", "why": "Best. The structured record that carries the case to staff now carries the decision back, in fields the agent reads without decoding shorthand."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each team writes its own shorthand, such as “RFD 40 pd, TL ok”.", "explanation": "Structured handoffs should work both ways. A resolution section in the same record brings the human's decision back to the agent in fields it can read.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent hands cases to staff with a neat form. Staff close them with scribbled notes. Later the agent doesn't know what staff decided. The clue: every team scribbles in its own shorthand.", "answer": "A GP refers you to a specialist with a referral form. If the specialist replies with a scribble, the GP has to guess. If the form has a “specialist's decision” box, the answer comes back in a shape the GP can read. That box is a <b>resolution section in the handoff record</b>. <b>Rule: the structured handoff should carry the decision back, not just the case out.</b>", "options": ["Letting the GP read the specialist's scribble helps, but the scribble is still hard to read.", "Sending the patient straight to the specialist every time skips the GP for the whole case.", "Rereading the GP's own notes doesn't show what the specialist decided later.", "Correct. A decision box on the same form, filled in when the case closes."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent just needs access</b> to the helpdesk. Access gives it the note, but the note is team shorthand. The handoff out is already structured; the <b>way back is not</b>. The smallest fix is to give the same record a place for the decision.", "diagram": "agent → [handoff: ID, cause, refund, next step] → staff\nstaff → [resolution: outcome, amount, promises] → agent\n        (loaded as case facts on the next chat)", "mapTitle": "The agent doesn't know what staff decided. What closes the loop?", "map": {"head": ["What exists today", "Gap", "Smallest fix"], "rows": [["Structured handoff out, free text back", "Decision unreadable", "Resolution section in the same record"], ["Structured notes in the helpdesk", "Agent can't see them", "Read-only helpdesk lookup"], ["No record of the decision anywhere", "Nothing to read", "Make staff record it"], ["Old agent session", "Ends at the handoff", "Not the source of the decision"], ["Every repeat goes to staff", "Agent removed", "Too broad for a data gap"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Escalations reach a person with a structured block", "means": "The outbound handoff already works."}, {"quote": "close it with a free-text note", "means": "The return path is unstructured."}, {"quote": "Each team writes its own shorthand", "means": "The decider. Reading the note (<b>[[0]]</b>) still leaves the agent decoding shorthand."}, {"quote": "the agent offers remedies that contradict what staff agreed", "means": "The decision never reaches the agent. The old session (<b>[[2]]</b>) ends before it was made."}, {"quote": "Which change fixes this with the least added complexity?", "means": "Extend the existing record: <b>[[3]]</b>. Routing all repeats to staff (<b>[[1]]</b>) is far bigger."}], "rule": "Make the handoff record two-way: the decision comes back in fields, loaded as case facts.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-09", "was": "s5q-s1-09", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Model-driven vs decision tree", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An architect wants to replace the agent's loop with a fixed decision tree. It sorts each chat into a category and runs a set tool sequence. In a pilot, the tree slightly beats the agent on common categories, where each chat follows one known path. It does worse on the rest, where chats raise several issues or the next step depends on the lookup. What does the pilot <b>best support</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use fixed sequences for the single-path categories and send every other chat to the loop.", "why": "Best. Fixed sequences suit work whose path is known in advance. The loop suits work whose next step depends on what was just found. Use each where it wins."}, {"t": "Keep the loop for all chats and have it split multi-issue messages into concerns handled in parallel.", "why": "Works, but leaves the gain unused. Splitting concerns helps the hard chats, but the pilot showed the tree is better on the common paths.", "trap": "leaves the gain unused"}, {"t": "Grow the tree with more categories, give the rare chats their own sequences, and rerun the pilot.", "why": "Works, but can't branch. More categories cover more cases, but a fixed sequence still can't choose a step from what a lookup returns.", "trap": "can't branch"}, {"t": "Move the sorting step after the order lookup, so the tree picks a sequence using the order data.", "why": "Works, but only part of the gap. Better input helps pick a category, but chats that branch later or raise several issues still don't fit one path.", "trap": "only part of the gap"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "It does worse on the rest, where chats raise several issues or the next step depends on the lookup.", "explanation": "Pre-configured sequences fit tasks with a known path; model-driven loops fit tasks whose next step depends on intermediate results. A pilot that shows both supports using each where it fits.", "eli5": {"question": "A fixed script beats the agent on common, simple chats, and loses on messy ones. The question is what to do with that. The clue: the tree wins in one place and loses in another.", "answer": "A call centre gives new staff a script for “reset my password”: it works every time. For a caller with three tangled problems, a script falls apart and you need someone who thinks. So you use the script for the simple calls and send the rest to the thinker. That is a <b>fixed sequence</b> for known paths and the <b>model-driven loop</b> for the rest. <b>Rule: fixed path known → script it. Next step depends on what you find → let the model decide.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Script the simple calls, send everything else to the thinker.", "Giving the thinker every call works, but wastes the script that was better on simple ones.", "Writing more scripts doesn't help a call whose next step depends on what you find.", "Reading the account before picking a script helps a little, but tangled calls still don't fit one script."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat this as <b>tree versus loop</b>, one winner. The pilot says both: the tree wins where the path is known, the loop wins where <b>the next step depends on what was found</b>. The guide frames these as two tools for two kinds of work.", "mapTitle": "Fixed sequence or model-driven loop?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of work", "Best fit", "Why"], "rows": [["One known path, same every time", "Fixed tool sequence", "Predictable, slightly better"], ["Next step depends on a lookup result", "Model-driven loop", "Chooses the step from what it found"], ["Several issues in one message", "Loop that splits the concerns", "One path can't cover them"], ["Mixed traffic", "Route: fixed for known paths, loop for the rest", "Each where it wins"], ["Long tail of rare cases", "Loop, not more hard-coded branches", "Branches can't predict findings"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the tree slightly beats the agent on common categories, where each chat follows one known path", "means": "Fixed sequences win on known paths. Keeping the loop for all (<b>[[1]]</b>) gives that up."}, {"quote": "It does worse on the rest, where chats raise several issues or the next step depends on the lookup", "means": "The decider. A fixed path can't depend on a result. More categories (<b>[[2]]</b>) or better sorting (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't fix that."}, {"quote": "What does the pilot best support?", "means": "Use each where it wins: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Known path → fixed sequence. Next step depends on findings → model-driven loop.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Model-driven decision-making vs pre-configured decision trees / tool sequences"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-01", "was": "s5q-s3-01", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Task tool & allowedTools", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "To answer short questions more cheaply, the team built a second coordinator called quick-answer. Its prompt says when to hand work to the document-analysis and citation-checking subagents. Its tool list was written fresh and holds only <code>Read</code> and the journal search tool. In trials, neither subagent ever runs. Quick-answer reads and checks the papers itself. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Copy the original coordinator's whole tool list into quick-answer, in place of its short list.", "why": "Works, but too broad. Delegation comes back, because the original list includes Task, but so does every other tool quick-answer was trimmed to leave out.", "trap": "too broad"}, {"t": "Give quick-answer the analysis and citation-checking tools so it can do that work well itself.", "why": "Works, but wrong action. The papers get analysed and checked, but by the coordinator. The subagents the prompt describes still never run.", "trap": "wrong action"}, {"t": "Add <code>\"Task\"</code> to quick-answer's <code>allowedTools</code>, next to its two current tools.", "why": "Best. A coordinator can only spawn subagents through the Task tool. Adding it to the short list restores delegation and keeps the list lean."}, {"t": "Send short questions back to the original coordinator, with an instruction to keep answers brief.", "why": "Works, but gives up the goal. The subagents run again, but every short question pays for the full coordinator, which is what quick-answer was built to avoid.", "trap": "gives up the goal"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its tool list was written fresh and holds only Read and the journal search tool.", "explanation": "Delegation has a hard prerequisite: the coordinator can spawn subagents only through the Task tool, so allowedTools must include \"Task\". When no subagent runs at all, check that capability before anything else.", "eli5": {"question": "A new, cheaper coordinator never hands work to its helpers, so it does the work itself. The clue: its tool list was <i>written fresh</i> and holds only two tools.", "answer": "A manager can only hand out jobs if they have the phone that calls the team. Here the new manager's desk was set up from scratch, and nobody put the phone on it. In the Agent SDK that phone is the <b>Task tool</b>: a coordinator spawns subagents only by calling it, so it must be in <code>allowedTools</code>. The fix is to add <code>\"Task\"</code> to the short list. <b>Rule: no subagent runs at all → check that the coordinator can call Task.</b>", "options": ["Copying the old manager's whole desk brings the phone back, but also all the clutter the new desk was meant to leave out.", "Teaching the manager to do the team's jobs gets the work done, but the team still never gets a call.", "Correct. Put the phone on the desk: add Task to the short tool list.", "Sending small jobs back to the old manager works, but it costs what the new desk was built to save."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a coordinator delegates because its prompt tells it to</b>. The prompt decides <i>when</i> to delegate. Whether it <i>can</i> is set by its tools: subagents are spawned through the <b>Task tool</b>, so <code>allowedTools</code> must include <code>\"Task\"</code>. A tool list written from scratch can leave it out.", "diagram": "quick-answer allowedTools: [Read, journal_search]\n  prompt: \"hand analysis to document-analysis\"\n  → no Task tool → does the work itself\nfix: [Read, journal_search, Task]", "mapTitle": "Subagents aren't doing the work. Where is the fault?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["No subagent ever runs, coordinator works alone", "Task missing from <code>allowedTools</code>", "Add <code>\"Task\"</code> to the list"], ["Coordinator needs a lean tool set", "Too many tools hurt selection", "Keep the short list and add only Task"], ["Subagents run but return thin work", "Context not passed in the Task prompt", "Put the needed findings in the prompt"], ["Coordinator picks the wrong subagent", "Vague subagent descriptions", "Sharpen the AgentDefinition descriptions"], ["Subagent misuses tools outside its role", "Tool set too wide", "Scope its tools to its role"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Its prompt says when to hand work to the document-analysis and citation-checking subagents", "means": "The prompt already asks for delegation, so prompt wording is not the gap."}, {"quote": "Its tool list was written fresh and holds only Read and the journal search tool", "means": "The decider. There is no Task tool, so the coordinator has no way to spawn a subagent. Copying the whole old list (<b>[[0]]</b>) fixes this but undoes the trimming."}, {"quote": "In trials, neither subagent ever runs", "means": "Not one call reaches a subagent: a capability problem, not a matching problem."}, {"quote": "Quick-answer reads and checks the papers itself", "means": "It falls back to the tools it has. Giving it more of them (<b>[[1]]</b>) feeds that fallback; sending work back to the old coordinator (<b>[[3]]</b>) drops the cheap path. Add Task: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "A coordinator delegates only if \"Task\" is in its allowedTools. No subagent runs at all → check that first.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Task tool spawns subagents; allowedTools must include \"Task\""}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-02", "was": "s5q-s3-02", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Scope partitioning", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "For each request, the coordinator starts both search subagents at once. One Task prompt says “find evidence on the drug”, and the other says “find trial data on the drug”. Traces show the registry subagent often follows a trial's results link and summarises the journal paper. Literature search has usually summarised that same paper already. Committees need both kinds of source. Which change <b>most directly</b> removes the duplicated work?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator judge each request and send narrow ones to one search subagent only.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Skipping a subagent saves cost on narrow requests, but these committees need both kinds of source, so both still run and still overlap.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Give each subagent named sources and exclusions: registry records to one, journals to the other.", "why": "Best. The overlap comes from two vague briefs that both cover journal papers. Partitioning the scope in the delegation stops the second fetch before it happens."}, {"t": "Have the coordinator drop papers with a repeated DOI from the two returns before synthesis.", "why": "Works, but too late. The report loses the repeats, but each paper has already been fetched and summarised twice, so the wasted work stays.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Run registry search after literature search, and pass it the full list of papers already covered.", "why": "Works, but slower. The registry subagent can skip known papers, but the two searches no longer run side by side, so every request waits longer.", "trap": "slower"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "One Task prompt says “find evidence on the drug”, and the other says “find trial data on the drug”.", "explanation": "When parallel subagents duplicate work, partition their scope in the delegation: which sources each covers and what each must leave alone. Dynamic selection helps only when a subagent isn't needed.", "eli5": {"question": "Two search helpers keep reading and summarising the same journal papers. The clue: their two instructions are vague and overlap, and both kinds of source are needed.", "answer": "Two people sent to “find out about the drug” and “find the trial data” will both end up in the same library aisle. Tell one “registry records only” and the other “journal papers only”, and they stop bumping into each other. That is <b>partitioning scope</b>: each subagent's Task prompt names its sources and what to leave alone. <b>Rule: parallel helpers doing the same work → split their scope in the brief.</b>", "options": ["Sending only one helper saves money on small jobs, but here both are needed every time.", "Correct. Give each helper its own aisle and tell them which aisle is not theirs.", "Throwing away duplicate notes at the end keeps the report tidy, but both people already did the reading.", "Making the second person wait for the first's list works, but the two can no longer work at the same time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat duplication as a <b>clean-up job</b> at the end, or to stop running one of the subagents. Both treat the symptom. The cause is two <b>overlapping briefs</b>: “evidence on the drug” and “trial data on the drug” both reach journal papers. The coordinator should partition scope when it delegates.", "diagram": "now:   literature → journal papers\n       registry   → registry + journal papers (dup)\nfix:   literature → journal papers only\n       registry   → registry records only", "mapTitle": "Subagents overlap or waste work. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Parallel subagents fetch the same sources", "Overlapping briefs", "Named sources and exclusions per subagent"], ["Later step needs an earlier step's results", "Real dependency", "Run in order, pass findings in the prompt"], ["Many requests don't need every subagent", "Full pipeline every time", "Coordinator selects subagents by need"], ["Repeats show up in the final report", "Duplication already happened", "De-duplicate, but fix the briefs too"], ["Whole topics missing from the report", "Decomposition too narrow", "Broaden the subtasks"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator starts both search subagents at once", "means": "They run in parallel, which is worth keeping. Running them in order (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives that up."}, {"quote": "One Task prompt says “find evidence on the drug”, and the other says “find trial data on the drug”", "means": "The decider. Neither brief says what to leave alone, so both reach journal papers."}, {"quote": "Literature search has usually summarised that same paper already", "means": "The waste is the second fetch and summary. Dropping repeated DOIs afterwards (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes after it is paid for."}, {"quote": "Committees need both kinds of source", "means": "Both subagents must run, so selecting just one (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't apply. Partition the scope: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Parallel subagents duplicating work → give each named sources and exclusions in its Task prompt.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "partition scope to minimize duplication"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-03", "was": "s5q-s3-03", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Coordinator prompt design", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator's system prompt is a long step-by-step procedure. It was written for a few request types, such as one drug compared with placebo. One early step says to extract the drug name. A committee asked about a nursing practice that involves no drug, and the coordinator returned an empty summary. New committees keep sending new kinds of question. How should the coordinator prompt be <b>rewritten</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a worked example of splitting each request type, including the nursing one, across subagents.", "why": "Works, but only for past cases. Examples help on the types they show, but the next new kind of question has no example yet.", "trap": "only past cases"}, {"t": "Keep the procedure, and add a branch for questions that name no drug at all.", "why": "Works, but patches one case. The nursing question would now run, but each new kind of question needs another branch.", "trap": "patches one case"}, {"t": "Add a first step that maps each question to the nearest of the known request types.", "why": "Works, but forces a fit. Every question gets a procedure, but a question unlike any type is pushed into one that doesn't suit it.", "trap": "forces a fit"}, {"t": "State the research goal, what a finished summary must contain, and when evidence is enough.", "why": "Best. Goals and quality criteria let the coordinator plan for questions nobody wrote steps for, which is what new committees keep sending."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "New committees keep sending new kinds of question.", "explanation": "Write coordinator prompts as goals and quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures. Procedures, branches and type-specific examples encode only the questions someone foresaw.", "eli5": {"question": "The coordinator follows a fixed recipe written for a few kinds of question. A question that doesn't fit the recipe comes back empty. The clue: <i>new kinds of question keep arriving</i>.", "answer": "A cook with only recipes is stuck when a guest asks for something new. A cook who knows what a good meal must be (balanced, hot, enough for everyone) can plan any dish. For the coordinator, that means a prompt that states the <b>goal and quality criteria</b>: what the summary must contain and when the evidence is enough. <b>Rule: write coordinator prompts as goals and criteria, not step-by-step procedures.</b>", "options": ["More example dishes help for those dishes, but the next new request has none.", "A new recipe for today's odd request works today, and you need another one tomorrow.", "Matching every request to the nearest recipe serves something, but often the wrong thing.", "Correct. Tell the cook what a good meal must be, and let them plan."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better procedure</b> fixes a procedure that failed: add a branch, a classifier step or more examples. Each of those still encodes only the questions someone foresaw. The exam guide says to write coordinator prompts as <b>goals and quality criteria</b>, so the coordinator can plan for questions nobody foresaw.", "mapTitle": "How should a coordinator prompt be written?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best prompt shape", "Why"], "rows": [["Questions keep changing shape", "Goal plus quality criteria", "Coordinator plans each question itself"], ["One known case is missing from a stable set", "Add a branch to the procedure", "Small patch for a closed set"], ["Coordinator splits work inconsistently", "A few worked decompositions", "Examples show the shape"], ["Fixed, repeated workflow that must be audited", "Fixed pipeline of steps", "Same steps every run"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator's system prompt is a long step-by-step procedure", "means": "The prompt is a procedure, the shape the exam guide warns against for coordinators."}, {"quote": "One early step says to extract the drug name", "means": "The steps assume a drug, so a question without one falls through."}, {"quote": "A committee asked about a nursing practice that involves no drug, and the coordinator returned an empty summary", "means": "A question outside the foreseen types fails. A branch for it (<b>[[1]]</b>) fixes only this one."}, {"quote": "New committees keep sending new kinds of question", "means": "The decider. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and mapping to known types (<b>[[2]]</b>) still rely on foreseen types. Goals and criteria: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Coordinator prompts state the goal and what good output looks like, not a step-by-step procedure.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "write coordinator prompts as goals + quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-04", "was": "s5q-s3-04", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Iterative refinement loop", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A committee asked how a drug works in adults, children and pregnant women. The summary covered adults and children in depth but gave pregnancy one sentence. The delegation log shows a subtask for each group. The pregnancy subagent returned two brief findings from a thin first search. Today the coordinator passes all returns straight to synthesis. Which addition <b>fixes this most economically</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator check the draft against the request, re-delegate thin parts, re-synthesise.", "why": "Best. The split was right; one subtask came back thin. Checking the draft for gaps and re-delegating only that part fixes it at the cost of one extra subtask."}, {"t": "Have the coordinator split every request more finely up front, across a larger number of subagents.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Finer splits help when part of a request was never assigned. Here every group had a subtask, and every run would cost more.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have synthesis mark sections with few sources, for a committee member to review by hand.", "why": "Works, but passes it on. The thin section is flagged, but a person has to find the missing evidence the system could fetch itself.", "trap": "passes it on"}, {"t": "Have every search subagent repeat with broader queries until it holds at least five findings.", "why": "Works, but wrong measure. Thin areas get more searching, but so does every well-covered one, and a count of findings doesn't check the request is answered.", "trap": "wrong measure"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The delegation log shows a subtask for each group.", "explanation": "Coordinators should evaluate the synthesis for gaps, re-delegate targeted subtasks and re-synthesise. Finer decomposition helps only when part of the request was never assigned.", "eli5": {"question": "A summary covered two of three patient groups well and the third barely. The clue: every group <i>was</i> assigned; one helper just came back with little.", "answer": "An editor reads the draft before it goes out. If one section is thin, they send that one reporter back out, then update the piece. They don't re-plan the whole paper. For the coordinator, that is the <b>iterative refinement loop</b>: check the draft against the request, re-delegate the gap, re-synthesise. <b>Rule: the split was right but one part came back thin → check, re-delegate, re-synthesise.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Read the draft, send one reporter back for the thin section, then update it.", "Hiring more reporters for every story helps if a topic was never assigned. Here it was.", "Marking the thin section for a reader to fix leaves the work to a person.", "Making every reporter file five stories wastes effort on sections already done."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to blame <b>the decomposition</b> when a part of the report is thin. Decomposition is the cause only if part of the request was never assigned. Here each group had its own subtask. The missing piece is a <b>check after synthesis</b>: evaluate the draft for gaps, re-delegate the thin part, re-synthesise.", "diagram": "split → 3 subtasks → synthesis → draft\n  check draft vs request → pregnancy thin\n  → re-delegate pregnancy only → re-synthesise", "mapTitle": "Part of the report is thin. Where is the fault?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Every part assigned, one came back thin", "No gap check after synthesis", "Check draft, re-delegate, re-synthesise"], ["A whole topic never got a subtask", "Decomposition too narrow", "Broaden the split up front"], ["Two subagents return the same material", "Overlapping scope", "Partition sources in the briefs"], ["Subagent fails to reach a source", "Access failure", "Structured error to the coordinator"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The summary covered adults and children in depth but gave pregnancy one sentence", "means": "One part is thin; the others are fine."}, {"quote": "The delegation log shows a subtask for each group", "means": "The decider. The split was right, so splitting finer (<b>[[1]]</b>) fixes a problem that isn't here."}, {"quote": "The pregnancy subagent returned two brief findings from a thin first search", "means": "One subtask needs another try. Making every subagent search more (<b>[[3]]</b>) spends effort where it isn't needed."}, {"quote": "Today the coordinator passes all returns straight to synthesis", "means": "Nothing checks the draft. Flagging for a person (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the gap open. Add the loop: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Split was right but a part came back thin → evaluate the synthesis for gaps, re-delegate, re-synthesise.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "iterative refinement loops (evaluate synthesis for gaps → re-delegate → re-synthesize)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-08", "was": "s5q-s3-08", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Dynamic subagent selection", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The hospital wants the cost per run brought down. Traces show that over half of requests ask for one fact, such as a drug's approved dose for children. Those runs still pass through all six subagents and produce a full summary. In some other runs, the two search subagents open the same papers. Committees usually need answers the same day. Which change will <b>cut cost the most</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move requests to the Message Batches API and send each answer when its batch completes.", "why": "Works, but too slow. Batches cut the price per call, but they can take up to a day with no deadline, and committees need answers the same day.", "trap": "too slow"}, {"t": "Let the coordinator judge what each request needs and call only those subagents.", "why": "Best. Over half the runs use six subagents for one fact. Selecting subagents by need removes the biggest waste."}, {"t": "Give the two search subagents separate source lists, so they stop opening the same papers.", "why": "Works, but smaller lever. It removes real duplication, but only in some runs, while the one-fact runs keep paying for all six.", "trap": "smaller lever"}, {"t": "Train a classifier on past requests that picks one of three fixed pipelines before each run.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A classifier can route one-fact requests, but the coordinator can already judge each request, without training data or fixed pipelines.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Those runs still pass through all six subagents and produce a full summary.", "explanation": "Coordinators should select which subagents to invoke by query complexity rather than always running the full pipeline. Fix the largest waste first; duplication between subagents is a smaller, separate lever.", "eli5": {"question": "Most requests ask a single fact, yet every run uses all six helpers and writes a full report. The clue: <i>over half</i> the runs do far more work than the question needs.", "answer": "If someone asks the library desk for one phone number, you don't send six researchers and write them a report. You send one person to look it up. The coordinator can do the same: judge what each request needs and <b>call only those subagents</b>. <b>Rule: don't run the full pipeline for every request; pick subagents by what the request needs.</b>", "options": ["Posting answers by slow mail is cheaper, but they arrive after the meeting.", "Correct. Send only the people the question needs.", "Stopping two researchers from reading the same book helps, but most of the waste is sending six people for one fact.", "Building a sorting machine works, but the desk clerk can already decide who to send."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to go after the <b>visible duplication</b> between the two search subagents. It is real, but it happens in only some runs. The bigger waste is the <b>full pipeline</b> running for one-fact requests. The exam guide says coordinators should select which subagents to invoke by query complexity.", "mapTitle": "Runs cost too much. Which lever is biggest?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Simple requests run every subagent", "Full pipeline every time", "Coordinator selects subagents by need"], ["Two subagents fetch the same sources", "Overlapping scope", "Partition sources per subagent"], ["Work can wait a day or more", "Paying real-time prices", "Message Batches API (50% off)"], ["Routing rules seem to need learning", "Coordinator not trusted to judge", "Let the coordinator judge first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Traces show that over half of requests ask for one fact", "means": "Most runs need very little work."}, {"quote": "Those runs still pass through all six subagents and produce a full summary", "means": "The decider. The largest waste is the full pipeline. Select by need: <b>[[1]]</b>. A trained classifier (<b>[[3]]</b>) adds machinery for the same job."}, {"quote": "In some other runs, the two search subagents open the same papers", "means": "Real but smaller. Partitioning sources (<b>[[2]]</b>) is the second lever."}, {"quote": "Committees usually need answers the same day", "means": "Batches have no latency deadline, so <b>[[0]]</b> risks missing the day."}], "rule": "Cut cost by having the coordinator invoke only the subagents a request needs; fix the biggest waste first.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Dynamically select subagents rather than always routing the full pipeline"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-09", "was": "s5q-s3-09", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Hub-and-spoke communication", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The citation-checking subagent asks the coordinator for more evidence several times per run. Each round trip adds some delay. An engineer proposes letting it call the literature-search subagent directly. Most of its asks are searches for an independent second paper. After about a third of them, the coordinator changes course, for example by dropping a claim. Which response is <b>best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep these asks going through the coordinator, each naming the claim, sources tried and the gap.", "why": "Best. The asks often change the plan, and the coordinator owns the plan. Structured asks make each round trip quicker to act on."}, {"t": "Give citation checking a scoped <code>fetch_section</code> tool for re-reading papers it already cites.", "why": "Works, but wrong need. A scoped tool suits frequent simple re-reads. Most asks here are new searches, which still go to the coordinator.", "trap": "wrong need"}, {"t": "Allow direct calls between subagents, logging each to a store the coordinator reads before synthesis.", "why": "Works, but too late. Searches get faster, but the coordinator learns about them only at synthesis, after it should have changed course.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Give citation checking the literature-search tools, so it finds independent second papers on its own.", "why": "Works, but bypasses the hub. Second papers arrive faster, but the coordinator stops seeing asks that should change the plan.", "trap": "bypasses the hub"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "After about a third of them, the coordinator changes course, for example by dropping a claim.", "explanation": "In hub-and-spoke designs the coordinator manages all inter-subagent communication, so routing, scope and error handling stay in one place. Scoped cross-role tools are for frequent simple needs; requests that change the plan belong with the coordinator.", "eli5": {"question": "A checker keeps asking the coordinator for more evidence, and someone wants it to ask the searcher directly. The clue: those asks often make the coordinator <i>change the plan</i>.", "answer": "On a building site, a worker who needs a design change goes through the site manager, because the change affects other trades. Going straight to another trade is quicker but leaves the manager blind. Here the coordinator is the manager (the <b>hub</b>). Keep the asks going through it, and make them structured so each is quick to act on. <b>Rule: subagents talk through the coordinator, especially when it can change the plan.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Go through the manager, with a clear, short request form.", "A small tool for re-checking your own drawings helps, but most requests are for new material.", "Telling the manager afterwards in a logbook is too late to change the plan.", "Handing the checker the searcher's tools speeds things up, but the manager loses sight of the change."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to see the round trip as <b>pure overhead</b> to cut. In a hub-and-spoke design, the coordinator manages all communication between subagents because it owns routing and scope. Here the asks often <b>change the plan</b>, so they belong with the coordinator. Make them faster to act on with structure, not by bypassing it.", "diagram": "citation check → {claim, sources tried, gap}\n  → coordinator → literature search\n  → coordinator may drop or widen claims", "mapTitle": "A subagent needs more. Who should it go through?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of need", "Best route", "Why"], "rows": [["Asks that can change the plan", "Through the coordinator, structured", "Hub owns routing and scope"], ["Frequent simple re-read of its own sources", "Scoped cross-role tool", "Limited tool for a common need"], ["Direct calls with a log read later", "Avoid", "Coordinator learns too late"], ["Full off-role tool set", "Avoid", "Off-role tools get misused"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer proposes letting it call the literature-search subagent directly", "means": "A spoke-to-spoke link, which bypasses the hub."}, {"quote": "Most of its asks are searches for an independent second paper", "means": "Not re-reads, so a scoped re-read tool (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers few of them."}, {"quote": "After about a third of them, the coordinator changes course, for example by dropping a claim", "means": "The decider. The coordinator must see these asks when they happen. A log read later (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a self-serve search (<b>[[3]]</b>) hides them. Keep the hub: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "In hub-and-spoke, subagents talk through the coordinator; scoped tools only for frequent simple needs.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Hub-and-spoke: coordinator manages all inter-subagent communication, error handling, routing"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-05", "was": "s5q-s4-05", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Adaptive investigation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "For error spikes, the engineer gives Claude a fixed plan: read the stack trace, the failing method and its callers, check the deploy diff, then propose a fix. Last time the method was fine. The bad value came from an upstream API client after another team's change. Claude still finished the plan and proposed a guard in the method. How should such investigations be <b>run</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Hand each step of the plan to its own subagent, and have a coordinator merge the results.", "why": "Works, but the steps are still fixed. Subagents split the work, but none of them can follow a lead the plan didn't foresee.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Begin in plan mode, and have Claude write the full step list before it reads any code.", "why": "Works, but the list is fixed before any evidence. Plan mode suits designing a change, not an investigation whose next step depends on findings.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Keep the fixed plan, and add steps for upstream API clients and other teams' recent changes.", "why": "Works, but only for causes you foresaw. The next surprise will sit somewhere the longer list doesn't cover.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Give Claude the goal and the evidence so far, and let it pick each next step as it goes.", "why": "Best. When the method proves fine, Claude can turn upstream at once instead of finishing steps that no longer fit."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Last time the method was fine.", "explanation": "Fixed sequential pipelines suit predictable work. Open-ended investigations need adaptive decomposition, where each next step comes from what the last one found.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude followed a checklist even after the evidence pointed elsewhere. The clue: <i>Last time the method was fine</i>, yet the plan carried on as if it weren't.", "answer": "Think of a doctor with a fixed checklist: check the knee, x-ray the knee, operate on the knee. If the knee is fine and the pain comes from the back, the checklist still ends in knee surgery. A good doctor picks each next test from the last result. For Claude, give the goal and the evidence, and let it choose each next step (<b>adaptive investigation</b>). <b>Rule: steps known in advance → fixed plan. Next step depends on findings → let Claude adapt.</b>", "options": ["Four doctors each doing one knee step still all look at the knee.", "Writing a fuller checklist before seeing the patient is still a checklist written blind.", "Adding a back check helps this time, but the next pain may come from somewhere else again.", "Correct. Each test is chosen from the last result."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the plan just missed a step</b>, so a longer plan will fix it. The real fault is that the steps were <b>fixed before anything was found</b>. An investigation is open-ended: each next step depends on what the last one showed. That calls for adaptive decomposition, not a better fixed list.", "diagram": "fixed:    trace -> method -> callers -> diff -> fix\n          (method fine, plan carries on)\nadaptive: trace -> method fine -> look upstream -> ...", "mapTitle": "Fixed plan or adaptive investigation?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of work", "Structure", "Why"], "rows": [["Open-ended investigation", "Goal plus evidence; Claude picks each next step", "Next step depends on findings"], ["Same steps every time, such as per-file review", "Fixed sequence (prompt chaining)", "Predictable and easy to check"], ["Known extra causes to check every time", "Longer fixed checklist", "Covers only what was foreseen"], ["Independent parts to cover in parallel", "Subagents with a coordinator", "Splits the work, not the choice of steps"], ["Designing a large code change", "Plan mode", "Explore and decide before editing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the engineer gives Claude a fixed plan", "means": "The steps are set before anything is read."}, {"quote": "Last time the method was fine", "means": "The decider. The findings changed direction, but the plan could not."}, {"quote": "The bad value came from an upstream API client after another team's change", "means": "The cause sat outside every step. Adding this step (<b>[[2]]</b>) covers this case, not the next one."}, {"quote": "Claude still finished the plan and proposed a guard in the method", "means": "The plan overrode the evidence. Only <b>[[3]]</b> lets the evidence choose the next step; <b>[[0]]</b> and <b>[[1]]</b> still fix the steps up front."}], "rule": "Open-ended investigation → give the goal and let Claude adapt each step. Predictable work → fixed sequence.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Adaptive investigation plans generate subtasks from what's discovered"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-06", "was": "s5q-s4-06", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Explicit subagent context", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 2, "question": "It is late in a long incident session, and the context window is nearly full. The engineer spawns an Explore subagent. Its prompt says: 'Investigate the issue we've been discussing and report the call paths involved.' It returns a broad tour of the serializer code. It never mentions the failing frame, the changed field or the other team's change. Which <b>TWO</b> changes would most improve the next attempt?", "options": [{"t": "Write the failing frame, the changed field and the suspect serializer into its prompt.", "why": "Best. A subagent starts with none of the session's history, so the findings must be in its prompt."}, {"t": "Fork the session and do the mapping in the branch, which already holds the incident history.", "why": "Works, but the branch starts nearly full. It has the history, but little room is left for the mapping work.", "trap": "carries the clutter"}, {"t": "Give the subagent the main session's full tool list, including the Sentry and Linear tools.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. More tools don't tell the subagent what to look for.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Say what to send back: each path from entry point to serializer, with its files and lines.", "why": "Best. A stated deliverable tells the subagent what done looks like, instead of leaving it to tour."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Its prompt says: 'Investigate the issue we've been discussing and report the call paths involved.'", "explanation": "Subagents start with isolated context: pass the relevant findings explicitly and state the deliverable and its quality criteria. Forking carries the history along, which helps only when there is room for it.", "eli5": {"question": "A helper was sent off with 'look into what we discussed', but the helper wasn't in the discussion. The clue: <i>Investigate the issue we've been discussing</i>.", "answer": "Think of sending a courier with a note that says 'fetch the thing we talked about'. The courier wasn't there, so they bring back a bit of everything. Fix the note two ways: say what you already know (the frame, the field, the suspect), and say exactly what to bring back (each path, with files and lines). A <b>subagent</b> starts with an empty memory (<b>isolated context</b>); only its prompt tells it anything. <b>Rule: a subagent knows only its prompt, so give it the findings and the deliverable.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Tell the courier what you already know.", "Cloning yourself works, but your clone arrives with a full desk and no room to work.", "A bigger toolbox doesn't tell the courier what to fetch.", "Correct. Tell the courier exactly what to bring back."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent knows what the session knows</b>. It doesn't. A subagent starts with isolated context: no history, only its prompt. 'The issue we've been discussing' points at nothing. The fix is to put the findings in the prompt and to state the deliverable.", "diagram": "main session (nearly full): frame, field, suspect\n   | prompt: 'the issue we've been discussing'\n   v\nsubagent: empty context -> broad tour", "mapTitle": "What does the subagent need from you?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Subagent needs earlier findings", "Write them into its prompt", "No automatic inheritance"], ["Subagent returns a vague tour", "State the deliverable and when it is complete", "Goal plus quality criteria"], ["Try another approach from a shared baseline, with room to spare", "Fork the session", "The branch keeps the shared history"], ["Main context nearly full", "Delegate with a short, explicit prompt", "Keeps verbose work out of the main session"], ["Subagent lacks a needed capability", "Add that tool to its list", "Tools fix ability, not direction"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the context window is nearly full", "means": "A fork (<b>[[1]]</b>) would start with that same nearly full history."}, {"quote": "Investigate the issue we've been discussing and report the call paths involved", "means": "The decider. The subagent wasn't there, and 'report the call paths' doesn't say what complete looks like."}, {"quote": "It returns a broad tour of the serializer code", "means": "No stated deliverable, so it toured. Name the output: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "It never mentions the failing frame, the changed field or the other team's change", "means": "It lacked the findings, not the tools (<b>[[2]]</b>). Give it the findings: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "A subagent knows only its prompt: pass the findings and state the deliverable.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt (no automatic inheritance / shared memory)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-07", "was": "s5q-s4-07", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 Tool results in history", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Early in a session, Claude reads the pricing service file. The engineer then changes <code>apply_regional_discount</code> in their own editor and saves. Later, Claude explains that method by quoting the old version, line numbers and all. The session is still short, and the work so far is useful. What should the engineer <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Tell Claude the file changed, and ask it to Read the file again before going on.", "why": "Best. The old Read result stays in the history Claude sees each turn. A fresh Read puts the current version there."}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code>, then ask Claude to explain the method again from its summary.", "why": "Works, but the summary still comes from the old read. It shrinks the history without bringing in the new code.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Start a new session with a summary of the findings, and have it read the file fresh.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It drops a short, useful session to fix one stale read that a single Read fixes.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a note to <code>CLAUDE.md</code> asking Claude to re-read files before it quotes them.", "why": "Works, but only usually, and it costs a re-read every time. Claude still can't know the file changed unless told.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The engineer then changes apply_regional_discount in their own editor and saves.", "explanation": "Tool results are appended to the conversation history, and Claude reasons over them on every later turn. When a file changes outside the session, tell Claude and have it read again.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude is quoting a file from memory after someone changed it. The clue: <i>changes apply_regional_discount in their own editor</i>, outside Claude's view.", "answer": "Think of a photo you took of a whiteboard. Someone rubs out a line and writes a new one, but your photo still shows the old line. Claude's earlier Read is that photo: it sits in the conversation history (<b>tool results</b>), and Claude uses it every turn. Tell Claude the board changed and take a new photo (Read again). <b>Rule: file changed outside Claude → tell it, and have it read again.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Say the board changed, and take a new photo.", "Shrinking your photo album still leaves the old photo, just smaller.", "A new notebook with a fresh photo works, but you throw away good notes to fix one picture.", "A standing reminder to re-photograph everything is slow, and Claude still doesn't know which board changed."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude has a memory that should refresh</b> when files change. There is no separate memory. The earlier Read result is part of the conversation history, sent with every turn. It stays as it was until Claude is told about the change or reads the file again.", "diagram": "turn 3: Read pricing file -> result added to history\n(engineer edits the file in their own editor)\nturn 9: Claude reasons over history -> old version", "mapTitle": "Why is Claude working from old information?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Claude quotes a file changed outside the session", "Old Read result in the history", "Tell Claude and Read again"], ["Long session, answers drift to generic patterns", "Context degradation", "<code>/compact</code> or a scratchpad of findings"], ["Resuming days later after much code changed", "Many stale tool results", "New session with a structured summary"], ["Resuming after small, known changes", "A few stale results", "Resume and tell Claude what changed"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Early in a session, Claude reads the pricing service file", "means": "That Read result is now part of the history Claude sees each turn."}, {"quote": "The engineer then changes apply_regional_discount in their own editor and saves", "means": "The decider. The change happened outside the session, so nothing tells Claude. <b>[[0]]</b> tells it and reads again."}, {"quote": "The session is still short, and the work so far is useful", "means": "Nothing is degrading. <code>/compact</code> (<b>[[1]]</b>) and a new session (<b>[[2]]</b>) solve problems this session doesn't have."}], "rule": "Tool results live in the conversation history. When a file changes outside the session, tell Claude and read again.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution", "quote": "Tool results are appended to conversation history so the model reasons about the next action"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-04", "was": "s5q-s6-04", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks for normalization", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The agent calls a <code>get_policy</code> tool to confirm dates in each carrier's system. Carriers return dates in different forms: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, or day-first text. The model sometimes reads a day-first date as month-first, and confirms the wrong expiry. Each carrier always uses the same form. Where should the dates be <b>made consistent</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the system prompt, with a note on each carrier's date form and how to read it.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The notes help the model read the dates, but it still parses them itself, and the slips continue at a lower rate.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "In a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that turns every date into one form before the model reads it.", "why": "Best. The forms are fixed, so code can convert them exactly. A hook on the tool result does it before the model ever sees a day-first date."}, {"t": "In the validator, which rejects confirmed dates that differ from extracted ones and retries.", "why": "Works, but too late. It catches some misreadings after they happen, and spends a retry on each, instead of removing the confusion.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "In a separate wrapper service for each carrier, which converts its dates before returning.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It gives the same result as one hook, with one service to build and run per carrier.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each carrier always uses the same form.", "explanation": "Fixed formats can be converted exactly in code. A PostToolUse hook normalises tool results before the model reads them, so the model never meets the ambiguity.", "eli5": {"question": "Carriers send dates in different styles, and the model sometimes reads them the wrong way round. The clue is: <i>each carrier always uses the same form</i>. Fixed styles can be converted exactly by code.", "answer": "Think of a post room that rewrites every date on incoming letters into one style before the letters reach your desk. You never see the confusing ones. That post room is a <b><code>PostToolUse</code> hook</b>: code that changes a tool's result before the model reads it. <b>Rule: if code can convert it exactly, convert it before the model sees it.</b>", "options": ["A note on how to read each style helps, but the model still does the reading.", "Correct. Code rewrites every date into one style on the way in.", "Rejecting wrong dates afterwards catches some, late, and costs a retry each.", "A service per carrier does the same job as one hook, with far more to run."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a model that misreads formats needs better instructions about them</b>. That fits when formats are open-ended and unpredictable. These are a few fixed forms, one per carrier. Converting them is <b>deterministic work with one right answer</b>, and a hook can do it on every tool result.", "diagram": "get_policy → \"04/03/2027\" (day-first)\n  PostToolUse hook → \"2027-03-04\"\n  model reads one form only", "mapTitle": "Model misreads data from tools: who should fix it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A few fixed formats in tool results", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook normalises", "Exact, before the model reads"], ["Formats vary unpredictably by layout", "Examples in the prompt", "Code cannot list every case"], ["Wrong value already produced", "Validator plus retry", "Catches after the fact"], ["Action must be blocked above a limit", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook on that tool", "Deterministic control"], ["Value needs outside data", "A tool against a real source", "Correct and traceable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Carriers return dates in different forms: Unix timestamps, ISO strings, or day-first text", "means": "Heterogeneous formats in tool results: the textbook case for normalising."}, {"quote": "The model sometimes reads a day-first date as month-first, and confirms the wrong expiry", "means": "The model is doing a parsing job. A prompt note (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves it doing that job."}, {"quote": "Each carrier always uses the same form", "means": "The decider. Fixed forms convert exactly in code. One hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers all carriers; a service each (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more machinery; the validator (<b>[[2]]</b>) acts too late."}], "rule": "Normalise fixed formats in a PostToolUse hook, before the model reads them.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "PostToolUse hooks intercept results for transformation before the model sees them."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-06", "was": "s5q-s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Routing by document type", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "One extraction prompt covers certificates, policy endorsements and carrier schedules. It grows by a paragraph with each new carrier quirk. Accuracy on certificates has slipped as the notes piled up. The prompt also contradicts itself. In the certificate notes, \"effective date\" means the policy's start date. In the endorsement notes, it means the date the change takes effect. Which restructuring is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Reorder the prompt so the certificate notes come first, under clear headings, then the rest.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. Key content first under headings helps the model find things. Both meanings of \"effective date\" still sit in the same prompt.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Classify each document first, then extract with a prompt holding only that type's notes.", "why": "Best. Classifying first and routing to a focused prompt removes guidance that is noise, or a contradiction, for this document. A new carrier note no longer affects the other types."}, {"t": "Split each document into page sections, extract each one, then merge the results at the end.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. Splitting helps with long documents. Here the prompt has grown, not the documents.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add a worked example for each document type that shows its correct effective date.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Examples steer each type toward the right date, but they lengthen the prompt and leave the two definitions in place.", "trap": "more of the same"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The prompt also contradicts itself.", "explanation": "One prompt serving several document types collects instructions that are noise or contradictions for most inputs. Classify first, then route to a focused prompt: a chain where step one decides step two.", "eli5": {"question": "One big prompt covers three document types, and accuracy slips as it grows. The clue is: <i>the prompt also contradicts itself</i>. \"Effective date\" means one thing for certificates and another for endorsements.", "answer": "Imagine one rule book for a whole hospital, where \"dose\" means one thing on the children's ward and another on the adult ward. Tidying the chapters does not stop the clash. Give each ward its own book, and check the ward first. That is <b>classify first, then route to a focused prompt</b> (prompt chaining). <b>Rule: if guidance for one case confuses another, split by case before extracting.</b>", "options": ["Headings help with finding things. Both meanings are still in the same book.", "Correct. Each document sees only the notes for its own type.", "Splitting the document helps long documents. Here the rule book grew.", "More examples add pages and leave both meanings in place."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a long prompt is a layout problem</b>: put the important parts first and add headings. That helps when content is merely hard to find. Here two sections define the same term differently, and every certificate still reads the endorsement meaning. <b>Only routing removes guidance that does not belong.</b>", "mapTitle": "One growing prompt: layout problem or fit problem?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Long prompt, key items missed", "Position effects", "Key items first, section headings"], ["Guidance for one type <b>contradicts</b> another", "Instructions do not fit every input", "Classify, then route to a focused prompt"], ["Long documents, middle fields missed", "Lost in the middle", "Split the document"], ["Output uneven under clear rules", "Uneven application", "Worked examples"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One extraction prompt covers certificates, policy endorsements and carrier schedules", "means": "Three document types share one prompt."}, {"quote": "It grows by a paragraph with each new carrier quirk", "means": "It keeps growing. More examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) grow it further."}, {"quote": "The prompt also contradicts itself", "means": "The decider. A contradiction, not a findability problem. Reordering (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps both meanings in context."}, {"quote": "In the endorsement notes, it means the date the change takes effect", "means": "The clash is in the instructions, not the documents, so splitting pages (<b>[[2]]</b>) misses it. So <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "When one prompt serves several kinds of input and its parts conflict, classify first and route each input to a focused prompt.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Fixed sequential pipelines (prompt chaining) vs dynamic adaptive decomposition based on intermediate findings."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-07", "was": "s5q-s6-07", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Gate before side effects", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The agent calls <code>post_certificate</code> once it is satisfied with a result. Policy says a certificate whose liability limit is below the client's contract minimum needs an account manager's approval before posting. The prompt states this rule. Last month a few such certificates posted anyway. In each, the agent's notes said the shortfall looked minor. Which change <b>guarantees the policy</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add explicit criteria to the prompt, with two examples of small shortfalls sent for approval.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Criteria and examples improve the agent's judgment, but compliance stays probabilistic, and the failures were exactly these small shortfalls.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that marks results below the minimum with an approval flag.", "why": "Works, but the agent still decides. Code computes the flag correctly, but the agent chooses whether to honour it before posting, which is the same weak spot.", "trap": "agent still decides"}, {"t": "Have a second Claude instance review each result below the minimum before it is posted.", "why": "Works, but the wrong approver. An independent instance catches extraction errors, but the policy asks for an account manager's approval.", "trap": "wrong approver"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on <code>post_certificate</code> that holds shortfalls for approval.", "why": "Best. The limit check is a comparison of two numbers. Code on the posting call itself holds every shortfall for approval, whatever the agent thinks."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The agent calls post_certificate once it is satisfied with a result.", "explanation": "Prompt-based compliance has a non-zero failure rate. Intercept the outgoing call and block it until the condition is met; a correct flag does not help if honouring it is optional.", "eli5": {"question": "Certificates below the contract minimum posted without approval, though the prompt states the rule. The clue is: <i>the agent calls post_certificate once it is satisfied</i>. Anything the agent must choose to obey can be skipped.", "answer": "A sign saying \"manager approval needed below the minimum\" works most days. A till that will not open without the manager's key works every day. A <b><code>PreToolUse</code> hook</b> is that lock: code that checks the posting call before it runs and holds it. <b>Rule: if a rule must hold every time, put it in code at the action.</b>", "options": ["Clearer rules improve judgment, but obeying them is still up to the agent.", "The flag is right, but the agent still decides whether to act on it.", "A second model checks the work, but the policy asks for a person.", "Correct. The posting call itself is held until a manager approves."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>moving the check into code is enough</b>, so a hook that sets a flag looks deterministic. The flag is computed by code, but it is <i>honoured</i> by the agent, which is how the shortfalls slipped through. The guarantee has to sit <b>on the posting call itself</b>.", "mapTitle": "Must-hold rule: where does the code sit?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Rule only nudges behaviour", "Prompt criteria and examples", "Probabilistic"], ["Code computes a flag the agent reads", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook on results", "Still relies on the agent"], ["Action must be held below a limit", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook on that tool", "Deterministic at the action"], ["Step B must wait for step A", "Prerequisite gate", "Blocks until A completes"], ["Need a second view of an extraction", "Independent instance", "Not a human approval"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent calls post_certificate once it is satisfied with a result", "means": "The decider. The agent controls the posting call, so a flag it reads (<b>[[1]]</b>) can be skipped."}, {"quote": "needs an account manager's approval before posting", "means": "A person, not a second model (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The prompt states this rule. Last month a few such certificates posted anyway", "means": "Prompt rules have a non-zero failure rate. More criteria (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more of the same."}, {"quote": "Which change guarantees the policy?", "means": "Code on the call itself: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Compliance rules cannot rely on the agent choosing to comply. Hold the outgoing call in code until the condition is met.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "When deterministic compliance is required (e.g., identity verification before financial ops), prompt instructions have a non-zero failure rate."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-01", "was": "s1-01", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Enforcement & gates", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "A hook blocks <code>process_refund</code> until <code>get_customer</code> returns a verified customer ID. It was added after refunds went to the wrong accounts. Gift boxes go to people who have no customer record, so every gift refund now goes to a human. Staff settle these quickly by matching the gift code and delivery address, and <code>lookup_order</code> returns both. Which change <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Exempt gift boxes from the hook, and add prompt steps with examples for matching the gift code and address.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clear steps with examples would be followed most of the time, but this check exists because refunds went to the wrong accounts, so it has to stay in code.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Keep the hook as it is, and attach a structured case summary to each gift escalation for staff.", "why": "Works, but the queue stays. A good summary helps staff work faster, but every gift refund still leaves the chat and waits for a person.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Widen the hook: a tool-confirmed match of gift code and delivery address also unlocks that refund.", "why": "Best. The gate stays in code, but it now accepts a second proof of identity that a tool confirms. Gift refunds settle in the chat without weakening the check."}, {"t": "Have checkout create a customer record for each gift recipient, so <code>get_customer</code> can verify them.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. It changes another system to fit the agent, does nothing for gifts already sent, and a record built at checkout says little about who is in the chat.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Staff settle these quickly by matching the gift code and delivery address, and lookup_order returns both.", "explanation": "When a code gate blocks a legitimate path, widen what the gate accepts rather than moving the check into the prompt. Identity checks before money moves need programmatic enforcement.", "eli5": {"question": "A guard checks ID before any refund. Gift recipients have no ID card on file, so the guard sends all of them to a person. The clue: staff already check them another way, and the tools can confirm that way.", "answer": "Think of a door guard who only accepts one kind of pass. Some honest visitors carry a different, equally good pass. You don't fire the guard or ask visitors to vouch for themselves. You teach the guard to accept the second pass too (<b>widen the prerequisite gate</b>). <b>Rule: when a code check blocks a real case, widen what the check accepts. Don't move the check into the prompt.</b>", "options": ["Sending the guard home and leaving a note works most days. This check exists because refunds went astray, so a note is not enough.", "A tidier file for the person at the desk helps, but every gift visitor still queues at the desk.", "Correct. The guard stays, and now accepts a second pass a tool can confirm.", "Printing ID cards for every gift recipient changes another office, and a card made at checkout doesn't prove who is talking."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the hook is <b>too strict</b>, so it should step aside for gift boxes. But the hook exists because refunds went to the wrong accounts. The real gap is that it accepts <b>only one kind of proof</b>. A gate can accept a second proof, as long as a tool confirms it.", "diagram": "process_refund requested\n  verified customer ID?            → allow\n  gift code + address match tool?  → allow\n  neither                          → block, escalate", "mapTitle": "A code gate blocks a legitimate case. What now?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right move", "Why"], "rows": [["Real case has another proof a tool can confirm", "Widen the gate's pass condition", "Check stays deterministic, case settles in chat"], ["Rule is a nicety, misses are acceptable", "Prompt steps with examples", "Cheap; occasional lapse is fine"], ["Rule protects money or identity", "Keep it in code (hook or gate)", "Prompts have a non-zero failure rate"], ["Case truly needs a person's judgement", "Escalate with a structured summary", "Person decides fast with the facts"], ["Data missing in another system", "Fix that system only if the agent can't confirm it", "Changing other systems is slow and wide"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It was added after refunds went to the wrong accounts", "means": "This check must hold every time. Exempting gifts and writing prompt steps (<b>[[0]]</b>) turns it into a request."}, {"quote": "so every gift refund now goes to a human", "means": "The problem is the queue. A better handoff (<b>[[1]]</b>) speeds the queue up but keeps it."}, {"quote": "Staff settle these quickly by matching the gift code and delivery address, and lookup_order returns both", "means": "The decider. A second proof already exists and a tool can confirm it, so the gate can accept it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Which change fits best?", "means": "Fix it inside the agent's own gate. Creating records at checkout (<b>[[3]]</b>) reaches into another system."}], "rule": "Identity checks before money stay in code. When the gate blocks a real case, widen what it accepts; don't swap it for a prompt.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "When deterministic compliance is required (e.g., identity verification before financial ops), prompt instructions have a non-zero failure rate."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-03", "was": "s1-03", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks & normalization", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 2, "question": "Customers can claim a refund within 30 days of delivery. The agent checks each box against that window. Delivery dates reach it in three shapes, one per warehouse system: Unix seconds, ISO dates, and a plain number such as <code>20260403</code>. The agent misjudges the plain number, mostly near month ends. Another warehouse system joins soon. Which <b>TWO</b> changes make the answer the same for every source?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that turns each delivery date into one ISO form before Claude reads it.", "why": "Best. Code converts every format before Claude sees it, so the plain number never reaches Claude. A new warehouse needs one more rule in the hook."}, {"t": "Add a <code>convert_date</code> tool, and tell the agent to call it before any refund-window question.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The tool converts dates correctly, but calling it is a request, so some chats will skip it.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Describe all three date shapes in the system prompt, with a worked conversion for each one.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples make mistakes rarer, but Claude still converts and counts the days itself, which is where the month-end errors come from.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have <code>lookup_order</code> also return <code>days_since_delivery</code> and a <code>within_window</code> flag.", "why": "Best. The tool does the date maths in code, so Claude reads the answer instead of working it out, whatever the source format."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Delivery dates reach it in three shapes, one per warehouse system", "explanation": "Deterministic data handling belongs in code. A hook normalises mixed formats before the model sees them, and a tool can return derived values instead of asking the model to compute them.", "eli5": {"question": "Dates arrive written three different ways, and the agent misreads one of them. Soon there will be a fourth. The clue: the answer must be the same whatever system the date came from.", "answer": "Imagine a post room that gets letters dated in three styles. Instead of training every clerk to read all three, you stamp one standard date on each letter as it arrives (<b>a PostToolUse hook that normalises</b>). Even better, the sender can write “arrived 12 days ago” on the envelope (<b>the tool returns the computed value</b>). <b>Rule: format conversion and date maths belong in code, not in Claude's head.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Every date is stamped in one standard form before Claude reads it.", "A conversion machine on the desk helps, but clerks still have to remember to use it.", "A wall chart of date styles helps, but the clerk still does the sums and still slips at month ends.", "Correct. The answer arrives already worked out."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think Claude needs <b>better instructions about dates</b>. But converting formats and counting days are exact jobs with one right answer. That is work for code. Claude should receive either <b>one clean format</b> or the <b>answer already computed</b>.", "diagram": "lookup_order result\n  1743638400 | 2026-04-03 | 20260403\n        ↓ PostToolUse hook\n  2026-04-03  (one ISO form)\n  + days_since_delivery, within_window from the tool", "mapTitle": "Who should handle exact data work?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "Best place", "Why"], "rows": [["Convert mixed formats to one", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook", "Runs on every result, before Claude reads it"], ["Compute a derived value (days, totals)", "The tool itself", "Claude reads the answer, no maths"], ["A step Claude must take every time", "Code, not a prompt request", "Prompts are followed most of the time, not always"], ["Explain how formats look", "System prompt", "Helps judgement, not exact work"], ["Judging an unclear customer claim", "Claude", "Needs reasoning, not a formula"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Delivery dates reach it in three shapes, one per warehouse system", "means": "The decider. Mixed formats are exactly what a normalising hook (<b>[[0]]</b>) is for."}, {"quote": "The agent misjudges the plain number, mostly near month ends", "means": "The error is in Claude's own conversion and day counting. Prompt examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) leave that work with Claude."}, {"quote": "Another warehouse system joins soon", "means": "The fix must scale. One more hook rule or tool-side calculation (<b>[[3]]</b>) handles it."}, {"quote": "Which TWO changes make the answer the same for every source?", "means": "The same answer every time needs code. A tool Claude must remember to call (<b>[[1]]</b>) is still a request."}], "rule": "Normalise formats in a PostToolUse hook and compute derived values in the tool. Don't ask Claude to do exact conversions.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Agent SDK hooks for interception and normalization", "quote": "Normalize heterogeneous formats (Unix ts, ISO 8601, numeric codes)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-04", "was": "s1-04", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Multi-concern decomposition", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Many customers raise several unrelated things in one message. For example: “My salmon was spoiled, please pause next week, and where is today's box?” The agent settles the first request and ends its turn. The customer then has to write back about the rest. Each request takes one or two tool calls, and they rarely depend on each other. Which change <b>best cuts</b> these repeat contacts?", "options": [{"t": "Spawn a subagent per request with the Task tool, and have a coordinator merge their findings into one reply.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Subagents suit long, separate investigations. These requests take one or two tool calls each, so the coordination costs more than it saves.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "End every reply by asking the customer whether anything else in their message still needs help.", "why": "Works, but leaves work undone. The question invites the customer back, but the turn still ends with requests open, so they still have to write again.", "trap": "leaves work undone"}, {"t": "Before the loop accepts <code>end_turn</code>, check that the draft reply mentions each request.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Matching words catches some misses, but a reply can mention a request without settling it.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have the agent list the separate requests first, then settle or escalate each one before it ends.", "why": "Best. A list of requests gives the agent a checklist to finish in the same contact, with no extra machinery."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The agent settles the first request and ends its turn.", "explanation": "Decompose a multi-concern message into its separate requests and handle each before ending the turn. Subagents earn their overhead only when each piece needs a long, isolated investigation.", "eli5": {"question": "A customer asks three things at once, and the agent answers only the first. The clue: each request is small and they don't depend on each other.", "answer": "Think of a waiter given three requests at one table. A good waiter jots all three on the pad first, then brings each before walking away (<b>decompose the request into a list and work through it</b>). You don't need three extra waiters for three small jobs. <b>Rule: several requests in one message → list them, then settle each before ending the turn.</b>", "options": ["Three extra waiters plus a head waiter works, but it's a lot of staff for three small jobs.", "Asking “anything else?” is polite, but the waiter still walks away with two orders unfilled.", "Checking the reply mentions each request helps, but mentioning a request is not the same as settling it.", "Correct. Write the list first, then finish each item."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to reach for <b>subagents</b> whenever a message has several parts. But subagents are for heavy, separate investigations whose output would crowd the main context. Here each request is <b>one or two tool calls</b>. The missing piece is simply a plan: list the requests, then finish them all.", "mapTitle": "A message holds several requests. How should the agent handle them?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right approach", "Why"], "rows": [["Several small, independent requests", "List them, then settle each in one turn", "A checklist, no extra machinery"], ["Several requests, each a long investigation", "Subagents via the Task tool, then merge", "Isolates heavy context"], ["Requests depend on each other", "Handle in order, using earlier results", "Later steps need earlier answers"], ["One request needs a policy exception", "Escalate that one, settle the rest", "Escalation is per request"], ["Unsure whether all were handled", "Track the list, not words in the reply", "Mentioning is not resolving"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Many customers raise several unrelated things in one message", "means": "Several requests, so the agent needs to split them up first."}, {"quote": "The agent settles the first request and ends its turn", "means": "The decider. Nothing tells the agent the others exist. Asking “anything else?” (<b>[[1]]</b>) still ends the turn early."}, {"quote": "Each request takes one or two tool calls, and they rarely depend on each other", "means": "Small work, so subagents (<b>[[0]]</b>) are more machinery than needed."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts these repeat contacts?", "means": "Finish everything in one contact: <b>[[3]]</b>. Word-matching the draft (<b>[[2]]</b>) can't tell mentioned from settled."}], "rule": "Several requests in one message: list them, then settle or escalate each before ending the turn.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Decompose multi-concern requests; investigate in parallel with shared context"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-09", "was": "s1-09", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.1 The agentic loop", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The loop checks <code>stop_reason</code>. On <code>tool_use</code>, it runs the tool and sends the output back as a new user message, rewritten as a plain sentence. The assistant turn that asked for the tool is not added to the history. The agent sometimes looks up a box it has just looked up. Once it told a customer a refund went through when none was made. Which change <b>fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Send each tool result back as its raw structured output instead of a rewritten sentence.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Raw output keeps fields that rewording can drop, but Claude still has no record of which calls it made.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block listing each tool call made so far and its result in every request.", "why": "Works, but works around the cause. It rebuilds the call record by hand, while the loop keeps dropping the real one.", "trap": "works around the cause"}, {"t": "Keep each <code>tool_use</code> request from Claude in the history, followed by its <code>tool_result</code>.", "why": "Best. Each call and its result sit in the history as a pair, so Claude can see what it already did and what came back."}, {"t": "Add a hook that blocks a second <code>lookup_order</code> call for a box already looked up.", "why": "Works, but treats one symptom. It stops repeat lookups, but Claude can still claim a refund it never requested.", "trap": "treats a symptom"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The assistant turn that asked for the tool is not added to the history.", "explanation": "In the agentic loop, the assistant's tool_use turn and the matching tool_result must both be appended to the history. Dropping either leaves Claude without a record of what it did.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent repeats lookups it just did and claims actions it never took. The clue: the turn where Claude asked for the tool is never saved.", "answer": "Imagine a diary where you write down the answers you got, but never the questions you asked. Later you can't tell what you already asked, or whether you actually did something. The fix is to write both lines every time: the request and its answer (<b>tool_use and tool_result as a pair in the history</b>). <b>Rule: every tool call goes into the history with its result.</b>", "options": ["Copying answers word for word is tidier, but the questions are still missing from the diary.", "Keeping a separate notes page works, but it rebuilds by hand what the diary should already hold.", "Correct. Write the request and its answer, every time.", "Blocking repeat lookups stops one symptom, but the agent can still claim a refund it never asked for."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to blame the <b>rewritten sentences</b>, since rewording can lose details. But losing a field does not explain repeating a lookup that just ran, or claiming a refund that was never called. Those need a <b>missing record of the calls</b>. The loop drops the assistant turn that holds each <code>tool_use</code>.", "diagram": "history must hold, in order:\n  assistant: text + tool_use(lookup_order)\n  user:      tool_result(lookup_order)\n  assistant: next step ...", "mapTitle": "The agent forgets or invents its own actions. Why?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Repeats calls, claims calls never made", "<code>tool_use</code> turns missing from history", "Append each tool_use and its tool_result"], ["Wrong details from a tool result", "Result reworded, fields dropped", "Return structured output"], ["Loop stops before tools run", "Stops on text, not <code>stop_reason</code>", "Loop on tool_use, stop on end_turn"], ["Early facts forgotten in a long chat", "Lost in the middle", "Case-facts block at the top"], ["Runs forever", "No safety cap", "Add a cap as a backstop only"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "it runs the tool and sends the output back as a new user message, rewritten as a plain sentence", "means": "Rewording can lose fields (<b>[[0]]</b>), but that doesn't explain the symptoms below."}, {"quote": "The assistant turn that asked for the tool is not added to the history", "means": "The decider. Claude never sees its own calls: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The agent sometimes looks up a box it has just looked up", "means": "It can't see that it already called the tool. Blocking repeats (<b>[[3]]</b>) only hides this one symptom."}, {"quote": "Once it told a customer a refund went through when none was made", "means": "With no record of calls, it can't tell what it did. A hand-made call list (<b>[[1]]</b>) rebuilds what the loop should keep."}], "rule": "Append both the assistant's tool_use turn and the matching tool_result to the history, every iteration.", "guide": {"obj": "1.1 Design and implement agentic loops", "quote": "Tool results are appended to conversation history so the model reasons about the next action."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-10", "was": "s1-10", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Handoff patterns", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "When the agent escalates, it attaches the full chat transcript. Staff say picking up a handoff now takes longer than handling the case from scratch. Most of that time goes on working out what the agent already tried and why it stopped. Usually the person only has to approve or decline one refund. Which handoff design <b>serves staff best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Attach only the last five turns of the chat, where the reason to escalate usually shows up.", "why": "Works, but loses history. Fewer turns are quicker to read, but the earlier steps are exactly what staff are trying to work out.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Send a structured summary: IDs, root cause, steps tried, the amount and a recommended action.", "why": "Best. Staff see what was tried, why it stopped and what is proposed in one place, so approving or declining is quick."}, {"t": "Pin the customer ID, box ID and refund amount in a header above the unchanged transcript.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. The header speeds up finding IDs, but staff still read the whole chat to learn what was tried and why it stopped.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Replace the transcript with one reason code from a fixed list of escalation categories.", "why": "Works, but too thin. A code helps route the case, but it loses the amount, the cause and what was already tried.", "trap": "loses detail"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Most of that time goes on working out what the agent already tried and why it stopped.", "explanation": "A person picking up an escalation needs a structured handoff: identifiers, root cause, what was tried, the amount and a recommended action. They should not have to read the transcript to get it.", "eli5": {"question": "Staff get the whole chat and spend ages working out what happened. The clue: the slow part is learning what the agent tried and why it stopped.", "answer": "Think of a nurse handing over at shift change. They don't hand over a recording of the whole day. They give a short sheet: who, what's wrong, what was tried, what's pending, what they suggest (<b>a structured handoff summary</b>). <b>Rule: hand a person a summary with IDs, root cause, steps tried, amount and recommended action.</b>", "options": ["The last few minutes of the recording are shorter, but the earlier steps are what the next nurse needs.", "Correct. A short handover sheet with everything needed to decide.", "A name tag on the recording helps find the patient, but you still have to play the whole recording.", "A one-word code helps send the patient to the right ward, but says nothing about what was tried."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the human just needs <b>the key IDs pulled out</b>. But finding IDs is not the slow part. The time goes on rebuilding <b>what the agent tried and why it stopped</b>. A structured summary answers that directly, so the person can decide without reading the chat.", "mapTitle": "What should a handoff to a person contain?", "map": {"head": ["What slows the person", "Best handoff", "Why"], "rows": [["Working out what was tried and why it stopped", "Structured summary with steps, cause and recommendation", "Answers the decision directly"], ["Finding the customer and box IDs", "IDs pinned at the top", "Speeds up lookup only"], ["Routing to the right team", "A reason code", "Good for queues, thin for decisions"], ["Checking a detail from the chat", "Transcript available on request", "Backup, not the main read"], ["Only the latest turns sent", "Avoid", "Drops the earlier steps"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When the agent escalates, it attaches the full chat transcript", "means": "Raw history, no structure."}, {"quote": "Most of that time goes on working out what the agent already tried and why it stopped", "means": "The decider. Pinning IDs (<b>[[2]]</b>) and a reason code (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't answer this. The last turns (<b>[[0]]</b>) cut the earlier steps."}, {"quote": "Usually the person only has to approve or decline one refund", "means": "They need the amount and a recommendation in one place: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Escalate with a structured summary (IDs, root cause, steps tried, amount, recommended action), not a raw transcript.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-01", "was": "s3-01", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Decomposition coverage", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "A committee asked how to cut readmissions after heart failure. The coordinator split the request into three searches: drug choice, dose adjustment and taking medicines as prescribed. Each search subagent returned many relevant papers. The summary barely mentioned nurse-led follow-up or home monitoring, which the committee sees as the main levers. Before synthesis, the coordinator checked the findings against its three subtasks. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have each search subagent widen its queries with synonyms, related terms and nearby topics.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Wider queries help when a search misses papers on its own topic. Each search here found plenty; nobody was asked to look at follow-up care.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator list every area of care the question raises, and plan and check from that list.", "why": "Best. The split was too narrow, and the coverage check measured findings against that same narrow plan. Splitting by the question's real areas, and checking against the committee's request, fixes both."}, {"t": "Add a synthesis step that looks for thin sections and sends them back to search for more papers.", "why": "Works, but too late. A gap loop can catch thin areas, but only after every search and a draft have run, and synthesis judges gaps from the material it was handed.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Ask each committee to list every area of care it wants covered at the time it sends a request.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. A checklist from the committee would close the gap, but splitting the question is the coordinator's job, and committees would carry it on every request.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Before synthesis, the coordinator checked the findings against its three subtasks.", "explanation": "Overly narrow decomposition leaves whole areas of a broad question uncovered, even when every subagent does its own part well. Fix the split at the coordinator, and check coverage against the original request, not against the coordinator's own plan.", "eli5": {"question": "The summary missed the topics the committee cared most about. Every search did its job. The clue: the coordinator split the question into three drug topics, then checked its work against those same three.", "answer": "A teacher sets a homework project on \"keeping a garden healthy\" and splits it into watering, soil and fertiliser. Every pupil does a great job. Nobody covers pests, because nobody was given pests. Marking against the teacher's own three topics says \"complete\". In the system, that is the coordinator's <b>task decomposition</b> being too narrow. <b>Rule: when good subagents leave a whole area out, fix the split at the coordinator and check coverage against the original question.</b>", "options": ["Better searching helps a pupil who missed books on their own topic. Nobody was given pests.", "Correct. Split by the real areas of the question, then check against the question itself.", "Spotting thin chapters after the essay is written helps, but it's late, and you only see gaps in what you were handed.", "Asking the parents for a full topic list would work, but planning the project is the teacher's job."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to blame <b>the search subagents</b> when a topic is missing. But each search found plenty on the topic it was given. The gap came from the <b>coordinator's split</b>: all three subtasks were about drugs. The check before synthesis compared findings with that same plan, so it could not see the gap.", "diagram": "request: cut readmissions\n  coordinator split → drugs | doses | adherence\n  missing            → follow-up care, home monitoring\n  check vs own plan  → \"complete\"", "mapTitle": "A summary misses part of the question. Where is the fault?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["A whole area is missing; every subagent did its part", "Coordinator's decomposition", "Split by the question's real areas; check against the request"], ["A subagent was given the topic but came back thin", "Search recall", "Better queries for that subagent"], ["Areas covered but some sections are thin after drafting", "Synthesis gaps", "Gap check, re-delegate, re-synthesise"], ["Findings retrieved, then dropped during analysis", "Analysis filtering", "Fix the analysis brief"], ["Claims lost during merging", "Synthesis", "Keep claim-source links through synthesis"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator split the request into three searches: drug choice, dose adjustment and taking medicines as prescribed", "means": "All three subtasks are about medicines. Follow-up care was never assigned to anyone."}, {"quote": "Each search subagent returned many relevant papers", "means": "Search worked on what it was given, so wider queries (<b>[[0]]</b>) are aimed at the wrong place."}, {"quote": "Before synthesis, the coordinator checked the findings against its three subtasks", "means": "The decider. The check measures the plan against itself, so the narrow split certifies itself. <b>[[1]]</b> fixes the split and the check."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "A synthesis gap loop (<b>[[2]]</b>) catches it later and at higher cost; a committee checklist (<b>[[3]]</b>) moves the coordinator's job to the committee."}], "rule": "Good subagents, missing area → the coordinator's split was too narrow. Check coverage against the request, not the plan.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Risk: overly narrow decomposition → incomplete coverage of broad topics."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-02", "was": "s3-02", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Subagent context passing", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Over several turns, the coordinator picks four trials with the committee chair. They also agree which outcome to compare. The coordinator then calls the document-analysis subagent. Its Task prompt says: \"Compare the agreed outcome across the trials we picked.\" The subagent returns a neat comparison of other trials, using a different outcome. Each request uses different trials and a different outcome. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite the Task prompt as the goal of the comparison and the quality bar it must meet.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Goals and a quality bar make a better delegation prompt, but this one is already goal-shaped. It is missing the trials and the outcome.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add the common outcomes and trial lists to the analysis subagent's AgentDefinition.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Standing instructions suit facts that hold on every run. The trials and outcome change with each request, so a fixed list would often be wrong.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Have the subagent list the trials and outcome it used, and let the coordinator reject mismatches.", "why": "Works, but too late. The coordinator would catch the wrong comparison, but only after the work is done, and the retry would hit the same missing context.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Write the trial names, the outcome measure and earlier findings into the subagent's prompt.", "why": "Best. A subagent starts with its own empty context and sees nothing of the coordinator's conversation. What it needs for this run has to be written into its prompt."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each request uses different trials and a different outcome.", "explanation": "Subagents have isolated context and do not inherit the coordinator's history. Anything specific to this run, including earlier findings and agreed choices, must be passed explicitly in the Task prompt. Standing AgentDefinition instructions suit only what stays the same.", "eli5": {"question": "The coordinator and the chair agreed on trials and an outcome. Then the coordinator told the helper \"the trials we picked\", and the helper compared other trials. The clue: the trials and outcome change every time.", "answer": "You phone a builder and say \"do the job we talked about\". But you talked about it with someone else, and the builder was never in the room. The builder guesses. A subagent is that builder: it starts fresh and has <b>isolated context</b>. So the details of this job must go in the message itself, the <b>Task prompt</b>. <b>Rule: a subagent knows only what its prompt tells it, so put this run's facts in the prompt.</b>", "options": ["A clearer goal is nice, but the builder still doesn't know which house.", "A standing list in the builder's handbook fits jobs that never change. These change every time.", "Checking the work afterwards catches the mistake, but the builder already built the wrong thing.", "Correct. Tell the builder the address, the plan and what's been decided, in the message itself."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the subagent <b>sees what the coordinator saw</b>, so \"the trials we picked\" is enough. It is not. A subagent starts with <b>isolated context</b>: no shared memory, no inherited history. Anything for this run must be written into the Task prompt.", "diagram": "coordinator history: trials + outcome (with chair)\n  Task prompt: \"the trials we picked\"\n    → subagent sees only this\n  fix: prompt carries trials + outcome + findings", "mapTitle": "Where should this piece of context go?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of information", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Facts for this run only (chosen trials, agreed outcome)", "The Task prompt", "Subagents inherit nothing"], ["Rules that hold on every run", "AgentDefinition system prompt", "Loaded each time it runs"], ["Earlier agents' findings", "The Task prompt, in full", "No shared memory between agents"], ["How to phrase the delegation", "Goals plus quality bar", "Better than step lists"], ["Source details for attribution", "Separate structured fields", "Keeps citations intact"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator picks four trials with the committee chair. They also agree which outcome to compare", "means": "The choices live only in the coordinator's conversation."}, {"quote": "Compare the agreed outcome across the trials we picked", "means": "The prompt points at that conversation, which the subagent cannot see."}, {"quote": "The subagent returns a neat comparison of other trials, using a different outcome", "means": "The work is careful; it is aimed at the wrong things. Framing (<b>[[0]]</b>) isn't the gap."}, {"quote": "Each request uses different trials and a different outcome", "means": "The decider. A standing list in the AgentDefinition (<b>[[1]]</b>) can't hold per-request choices. Put them in the prompt: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Per-run facts travel in the Task prompt; standing rules go in the AgentDefinition.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Subagent context must be provided explicitly in the prompt (no automatic inheritance / shared memory)."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-03", "was": "s3-03", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Parallel subagent spawning", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "After the searches, the coordinator hands each chosen paper to the document-analysis subagent. It sends one Task call, waits for the result, then sends the next. A typical request has about a dozen papers, and each paper is analysed on its own. Committees say summaries take too long, and most of the wait is this analysis phase. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut the wait?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator send one Task call per paper, all in a single response.", "why": "Best. The papers are analysed independently, so several Task calls in one response run together. The phase then takes about as long as the slowest paper."}, {"t": "Have one analysis subagent read all the papers in a single, larger Task call.", "why": "Works, but crowds one context. It saves some hand-offs, but one agent still works through the papers in turn, and a dozen full papers in one context dilutes its attention.", "trap": "crowds one context"}, {"t": "Have the search subagents return fewer papers by tightening their inclusion filters.", "why": "Works, but wrong action. Fewer papers means less waiting, but it cuts evidence the committee may need, and the remaining papers still run one by one.", "trap": "wrong action"}, {"t": "Have each analysis return only the fields synthesis needs, not its full notes.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Trimmed results save context later, but each paper still waits for the one before it.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "It sends one Task call, waits for the result, then sends the next.", "explanation": "Independent subtasks should run at the same time. The coordinator spawns parallel subagents by emitting several Task calls in one response. Sequential hand-offs are for work where one step needs another's result.", "eli5": {"question": "The coordinator hands out papers one at a time and waits for each. The clue: each paper is analysed on its own, so nothing needs to wait.", "answer": "A teacher has twelve essays to mark and twelve helpers, but gives out one essay, waits for it to come back, then gives out the next. Handing all twelve out at once would take about as long as the slowest helper. In the SDK, that is the coordinator sending <b>several Task calls in one response</b>. <b>Rule: independent pieces of work → send them all at once.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Hand out every essay at once.", "Giving all twelve to one helper saves trips, but that helper still reads them one by one, and gets tired.", "Marking fewer essays is faster, but you lose essays you needed.", "Shorter feedback sheets save paper, but the essays still go out one at a time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to make <b>each step smaller or cheaper</b> to save time. The real cost is <b>waiting in line</b>: the papers don't depend on each other, yet the coordinator handles them one after another. Several Task calls in one response run them side by side.", "diagram": "today:  P1 ──▶ P2 ──▶ P3 ──▶ … (sum of all)\nbetter: P1 ┐\n        P2 ├─▶ synthesis  (≈ slowest one)\n        P3 ┘", "mapTitle": "Analysis is slow. What actually cuts the wait?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Independent subtasks run one after another", "Several Task calls in one response", "They run at the same time"], ["Each step needs the previous step's output", "Keep them in sequence", "Real dependency"], ["Many documents in one agent's context", "One subagent per document, then merge", "Avoids diluted attention"], ["Some requests don't need every stage", "Pick subagents per request", "Skip what isn't needed"], ["Tool results fill the context", "Trim to the fields needed", "Saves context, not waiting"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It sends one Task call, waits for the result, then sends the next", "means": "The decider. The wait is queueing, not the work itself."}, {"quote": "each paper is analysed on its own", "means": "No paper needs another's result, so they can run together: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "most of the wait is this analysis phase", "means": "Trimming output (<b>[[3]]</b>) or cutting papers (<b>[[2]]</b>) doesn't remove the queue."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to cut the wait?", "means": "One big Task call (<b>[[1]]</b>) still reads the papers in turn, in one crowded context."}], "rule": "Independent subtasks → emit all the Task calls in one response.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "spawn parallel subagents by emitting multiple Task calls in one response"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-08", "was": "s3-08", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Adaptive decomposition", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Every request runs the full chain: searches, document analysis, citation checking, synthesis and reporting. About a third of requests are quick questions from committee members during a meeting, such as whether a newer trial changed a drug's licensed dose. These runs still take many minutes. Broad evidence reviews, the rest of the work, are well served by the full chain. What is the <b>best change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Build a separate quick-answer agent that committee members can call directly during their meetings.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second agent would answer fast, but it duplicates search and citation work the coordinator can already route.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Cap each quick run at a small number of loop turns and return the best answer found so far.", "why": "Works, but cuts blindly. A cap makes runs shorter, but an arbitrary limit is a weak stopping rule and can stop before the citation check runs.", "trap": "cuts blindly"}, {"t": "Let the coordinator choose subagents for each request, with a short cited path for quick ones.", "why": "Best. The coordinator selects subagents by the request's complexity. Quick questions get a search, a citation check and a short answer; reviews keep the full chain."}, {"t": "Keep a cache of recent answers and reply from it whenever a new question matches an old one.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Repeated questions come back fast, but new questions still take the full chain, and cached answers go stale as trials appear.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "About a third of requests are quick questions from committee members during a meeting", "explanation": "A fixed pipeline suits work whose shape is the same every time. When requests vary in size, the coordinator should choose which subagents to call for each request instead of routing everything through the full chain.", "eli5": {"question": "Quick questions in meetings go through the same long process as big reviews. The clue: a third of requests are quick questions, and the rest really do need the full process.", "answer": "A library makes everyone fill in a full research request, even for \"what time do you close?\". The fix isn't a second library. It's a librarian who looks at each question and picks the right path. That librarian is the coordinator doing <b>dynamic subagent selection</b>: choosing subagents per request. <b>Rule: requests vary in size → let the coordinator pick the subagents each one needs.</b>", "options": ["A second help desk would be quick, but now you run two libraries.", "Stopping every request after five minutes is quick, but it can cut off the important part.", "Correct. The librarian sends each question down the path it needs.", "Remembering past answers helps for repeats, but new questions still wait and old answers go out of date."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to <b>build something new</b> for the fast case. But the coordinator already exists, and choosing which subagents to call is its job. The problem is that it <b>always runs the full chain</b>. Letting it pick per request gives quick questions a short path and keeps reviews thorough.", "diagram": "quick question → search → citation check → answer\nbroad review   → searches → analysis → check\n               → synthesis → report", "mapTitle": "Requests differ in size. How should the work be split?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right design", "Why"], "rows": [["Requests vary from quick to broad", "Coordinator picks subagents per request", "Each gets the path it needs"], ["Every request has the same fixed shape", "Fixed pipeline (prompt chain)", "Predictable and easy to audit"], ["Findings reveal new questions mid-run", "Adaptive plan from findings", "New subtasks as you learn"], ["Run length needs a limit", "Stop on end_turn, not a fixed cap", "Caps cut work blindly"], ["A truly separate user group with separate tools", "A separate agent", "Only when routing can't serve it"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Every request runs the full chain", "means": "The coordinator never chooses; that is the gap."}, {"quote": "About a third of requests are quick questions from committee members during a meeting", "means": "The decider. Requests vary in size, so pick subagents per request: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Broad evidence reviews, the rest of the work, are well served by the full chain", "means": "Keep the full chain for reviews; a turn cap (<b>[[1]]</b>) would cut those too."}, {"quote": "What is the best change?", "means": "A separate agent (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a cache (<b>[[3]]</b>) both work around a routing choice the coordinator should make."}], "rule": "Requests of different sizes → let the coordinator choose subagents per request.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Dynamically select subagents rather than always routing the full pipeline"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-10", "was": "s3-10", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Session forking", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "After a long gathering phase, a session holds the full text of every paper found on a new blood thinner. The committee wants two analyses from it: one for patients with kidney disease, one for patients without. Each analysis must be able to quote any passage of the gathered papers. Neither should be shaped by the other's reasoning. Gathering should run only once. Which session strategy <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Write a structured summary of the papers and start two new sessions from that summary.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. Fresh sessions from a summary keep the analyses apart, but a summary keeps only what it chose, and each analysis must quote any passage.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Fork the session twice from its post-gathering state, one branch per patient group.", "why": "Best. Forking makes independent branches from a shared starting point: both keep every gathered paper, neither sees the other's reasoning, and gathering runs once."}, {"t": "Resume the session for the first analysis, then resume it again later for the second.", "why": "Works, but carries reasoning over. Resuming continues one line of conversation, so the second analysis sits on top of the first one's reasoning.", "trap": "carries reasoning over"}, {"t": "Ask for both analyses in a single request in the session, each one under its own heading.", "why": "Works, but mixes reasoning. One response written in one context lets each analysis shape the other.", "trap": "mixes reasoning"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each analysis must be able to quote any passage of the gathered papers.", "explanation": "--resume continues one conversation in a line; fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline. When you need separate continuations from the same full state, fork.", "eli5": {"question": "One long gathering session must feed two separate analyses. The clue: each analysis needs every word of the papers, and the two must not influence each other.", "answer": "Two students revise for different exams from the same pile of notes. Photocopy the whole pile and give each a copy: both have every page, and neither sees the other's scribbles. In sessions, that photocopy is a <b>fork</b>: <code>fork_session</code> makes independent branches from the same starting point. <b>Rule: two separate paths from the same full state → fork.</b>", "options": ["Giving each student a summary sheet keeps them apart, but they can't quote pages that aren't on it.", "Correct. Two full copies, one per student.", "Sharing one notebook, one after the other, means the second student reads the first one's scribbles.", "Writing both essays on the same page lets one bleed into the other."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think a <b>fresh session from a summary</b> is always the safer choice. It is, when old tool results are stale or the context is crowded. Here the analyses must quote <b>any passage</b>, so the full state must carry over, and the two must stay apart. That is what forking does.", "diagram": "gather (once) ──▶ fork ┬─▶ kidney disease\n                       └─▶ no kidney disease", "mapTitle": "Which session move fits?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Session move", "Why"], "rows": [["Two independent paths from the same full state", "fork_session", "Shared start, separate reasoning"], ["Carry on the same line of work later", "--resume", "Continues one conversation"], ["Many files changed, or context is crowded", "New session from a structured summary", "Clean, reliable start"], ["Only one or two named files changed", "Resume and tell the agent which files changed", "Avoids acting on stale views"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "a session holds the full text of every paper found on a new blood thinner", "means": "The full state already exists; it shouldn't be thrown away or rebuilt."}, {"quote": "Each analysis must be able to quote any passage of the gathered papers", "means": "The decider. A summary (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps only part of the text."}, {"quote": "Neither should be shaped by the other's reasoning", "means": "Resuming in sequence (<b>[[2]]</b>) or one combined request (<b>[[3]]</b>) mixes the reasoning."}, {"quote": "Gathering should run only once", "means": "Branch from the gathered state: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Separate paths from the same full state → fork_session, not resume and not a summary.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "fork_session creates independent branches from a shared baseline."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-05", "was": "m4-s4-05", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Incremental exploration", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer must explain why some learners' streaks reset a day early. To get oriented, they first had Claude read the whole progress engine, about 150 files. The session is now at 70% of its context window. Claude's answers lean on 'typical Rails patterns' rather than this code. Only a few files deal with day boundaries. What should the engineer <b>do instead</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> to shrink the loaded code, then keep asking in this session.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. It frees space, but Claude's view is still a broad read of the engine, now compressed further. The streak path was never traced.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have an Explore subagent read the whole engine and return a summary per module.", "why": "Works, but answers a different need. A module map helps with a broad overview; this is one bug, which needs the code path traced.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Grep for the streak reset and day-boundary code, then Read along that path.", "why": "Best. Find the entry points with Grep, then Read the files the flow calls into, spending context only on code that matters."}, {"t": "Keep a scratchpad file of key findings while Claude reads through the engine.", "why": "Works, but keeps the same strategy. A scratchpad saves findings, but Claude is still reading everything first.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Only a few files deal with day boundaries.", "explanation": "Build understanding incrementally: Grep for entry points, then Read to follow the flow. Reading everything up front fills the context and gives generic answers.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude read a whole package and now gives vague answers. The clue is: <b>only a few files deal with day boundaries</b>. The question is not about saving space; it is about reading the right code.", "answer": "To fix one leaky pipe, you don't read the plans for the whole building. You find the tap that leaks, then follow that one pipe. In code, <b>Grep</b> finds where the streak reset happens, and <b>Read</b> follows the files it calls. <b>Rule: one bug → search for the entry point, then follow the flow. Don't read everything first.</b>", "options": ["Squeezing the building plans into a smaller book still doesn't trace the leaky pipe.", "A summary of every room is useful for a tour. You need the one pipe.", "Correct. Find the leak, then follow that pipe.", "Taking notes while reading all the plans is still reading all the plans."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is running out of space</b>. The real problem is the <b>strategy</b>: reading everything first. Answers turn generic because the important code is buried. Build understanding <b>incrementally</b>: Grep for the entry point, then Read along the flow.", "mapTitle": "What does this exploration need?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Approach", "Why"], "rows": [["One specific bug or flow", "Grep entry points, then Read along the path", "Context goes only to relevant code"], ["A broad map of an unfamiliar area", "Explore subagent returns a summary", "Verbose discovery stays out of the main context"], ["Long session, room running out", "<code>/compact</code>", "Reduces context use"], ["Findings needed across sessions", "Scratchpad file", "Keeps key facts"], ["Read everything first", "Avoid", "Fills context, answers turn generic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "they first had Claude read the whole progress engine, about 150 files", "means": "The read-everything strategy. Compacting (<b>[[0]]</b>) or a scratchpad (<b>[[3]]</b>) keeps that strategy."}, {"quote": "Claude's answers lean on 'typical Rails patterns' rather than this code", "means": "A sign the relevant code is lost among everything else."}, {"quote": "Only a few files deal with day boundaries", "means": "The decider. The job is to trace a few files, not to map the engine (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do instead?", "means": "A different strategy: search, then follow the path: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "One specific question → Grep for the entry point, then Read along the flow. Never read it all first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Build understanding incrementally: Grep entry points → Read to follow imports/trace flows (not read-all-upfront)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-06", "was": "m4-s4-06", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.7 Resumption with stale state", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Yesterday the engineer and Claude planned a reminder-email change across nine files and edited three. Overnight a teammate's merge renamed <code>next_reminder_at</code> to <code>reminder_due_at</code> in one untouched file. Nothing else in the change moved. After <code>--resume</code>, Claude's next edit used the old name and failed. The session is at 35% of its window. What is the <b>best next step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Stay in the resumed session, tell Claude about the rename, and re-read that file.", "why": "Best. Resuming restores the conversation, not the files. When one known file changed, telling Claude and re-reading that file is enough."}, {"t": "Start a new session seeded with a summary of the design, the edits, and what remains.", "why": "Works, but gives up a good session. A fresh start beats resuming when many tool results are stale. Here only one file changed, and the session has room to spare.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Stay in the resumed session and have Claude re-read all nine files first.", "why": "Works, but more than needed. Only one file changed, and Claude's view of the other eight is still correct.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Carry on with the plan, and have Claude re-read a file whenever an edit there fails.", "why": "Works, but waits for failures. Claude already knows which file changed, so there is no reason to find out by failing again.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Nothing else in the change moved.", "explanation": "When resuming after files changed, tell the agent about the change. Start fresh from a structured summary only when much of the earlier context is stale.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude came back to yesterday's work, but one file changed overnight. The clue is: <b>nothing else in the change moved</b>, and the session still has plenty of room.", "answer": "You come back to a half-built shelf. A friend swapped one screw overnight and tells you. You don't start the whole shelf again; you check that one part. In Claude Code, <b><code>--resume</code></b> brings back the conversation, not fresh copies of the files. <b>Tell Claude what changed and re-read that file.</b> <b>Rule: one known change → tell the agent and re-read it. Much stale → start fresh with a summary.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Mention the swapped screw and check that one part.", "Starting over from notes works, but throws away a session that is mostly still right.", "Checking every screw works, but only one was swapped.", "Waiting for the shelf to wobble before you look is the slow way."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a resumed session is either fine or ruined</b>. Resuming restores the conversation, but Claude's copies of the files may be out of date. The fix scales with how much changed: <b>tell Claude about a known change</b>; start fresh with a summary only when much is stale.", "mapTitle": "How much changed since the session?", "map": {"head": ["What changed", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["One known file", "Resume, tell Claude, re-read that file", "Small fix, keeps the context"], ["Many files, or unknown changes", "New session with a structured summary", "Old tool results are unreliable"], ["Nothing", "<code>--resume</code> and carry on", "The context is still accurate"], ["Want to try two approaches", "<code>fork_session</code>", "Separate branches from one baseline"], ["Session running out of room", "<code>/compact</code>", "Reduces context use"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "renamed next_reminder_at to reminder_due_at in one untouched file", "means": "The change is known exactly, so Claude can be told."}, {"quote": "Nothing else in the change moved", "means": "The decider. Only one file is stale, so re-reading all nine (<b>[[2]]</b>) or starting over (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "Claude's next edit used the old name and failed", "means": "Waiting for more failures (<b>[[3]]</b>) repeats this."}, {"quote": "The session is at 35% of its window", "means": "There is room, so keep the session: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Resuming after a known change → tell the agent what changed and re-read that file.", "guide": {"obj": "1.7 Manage session state, resumption, and forking", "quote": "Inform the agent about file changes when resuming after modifications"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-07", "was": "m4-s4-07", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 When not to delegate", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer proposes that Claude spawn a subagent for each edit in a six-step change, so every edit gets a clean context. Each step changes a few lines and builds on the step before. The engineer reviews every diff in the main session before approving the next. The session sits at 20% of its window. What is the <b>best approach</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Spawn a subagent per edit, passing it the earlier results and the relevant CLAUDE.md rules.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Passing earlier results is right when you delegate, but each handoff costs effort for a few-line change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Hand the whole six-step change to one subagent and review its final diff.", "why": "Works, but drops the review. The engineer wants to approve each step before the next one starts.", "trap": "skips the review"}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> between edits to keep each edit's working context small.", "why": "Works, but not needed. At 20% of the window there is little to reclaim, and each summary may drop detail the next step needs.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Make the six edits in the main session one at a time, reviewing each diff.", "why": "Best. Subagents pay off by isolating noisy work or running independent tasks in parallel. Small, dependent, reviewed steps need neither."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each step changes a few lines and builds on the step before.", "explanation": "Delegate to subagents to isolate verbose output or to run independent work in parallel. Small, dependent steps reviewed in the main session gain nothing from delegation.", "eli5": {"question": "Should each small edit get its own helper? The clue is: <b>each step changes a few lines and builds on the step before</b>, and the session has lots of room.", "answer": "A helper is worth it when there is a messy job to take away, like sorting a whole box of papers. Here each step is a few lines, depends on the last one, and you check each one. Sending a helper each time means explaining the whole story again. <b>Rule: delegate noisy or independent work; keep small, dependent steps in the main session.</b>", "options": ["Briefing a new helper for every small step costs more than doing the step.", "One helper doing everything means you can't check each step as it happens.", "Tidying the desk between steps is fine, but the desk is nearly empty.", "Correct. Do the small steps yourself, checking each one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clean context is always better</b>. Subagents start with no history, so everything they need must be passed in. That cost pays off when they <b>isolate verbose output</b> or run <b>independent</b> work in parallel. Here the steps are small, depend on each other, and the main session has plenty of room.", "mapTitle": "Is a subagent worth its handoff cost?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of work", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Small, dependent steps, reviewed one by one", "Main session", "No noise to isolate; history matters"], ["Verbose discovery or search", "Subagent (e.g. Explore)", "Keeps noise out of the main context"], ["Independent tasks", "Parallel subagents in one response", "Run side by side"], ["Delegated work that needs earlier results", "Subagent with those results in its prompt", "No automatic inheritance"], ["Main session near its limit", "<code>/compact</code>", "Reduces context use"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each step changes a few lines and builds on the step before", "means": "The decider. Nothing verbose to isolate, and order matters, so a subagent per edit (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds handoff cost."}, {"quote": "The engineer reviews every diff in the main session before approving the next", "means": "One subagent for everything (<b>[[1]]</b>) skips these step reviews."}, {"quote": "The session sits at 20% of its window", "means": "Plenty of room, so compacting (<b>[[2]]</b>) solves nothing."}, {"quote": "What is the best approach?", "means": "Keep the edits in the main session: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Subagents isolate noisy or independent work. Small, dependent, reviewed steps stay in the main session.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-09", "was": "s6-09", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.6 Prompt chaining", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "One prompt does four jobs in a single call. It sorts each document by type, extracts its fields, standardises the limits and checks them against the client's contract. Accuracy dropped when the contract checks were added. When an endorsement is read as a certificate, every later step is wrong but looks consistent. Every document takes the same four steps in the same order. What is the <b>best restructuring</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give a coordinator agent the four jobs as tools, and let it pick each next step from the last result.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Dynamic decomposition suits work whose next step depends on what is found. Every document takes the same four steps, so a coordinator adds cost and variability.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep one call, and add an independent reviewer instance that checks the full output before posting.", "why": "Works, but too late. A fresh reviewer catches some errors, but a misread type produces output that looks consistent, and the one call still juggles four jobs.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Split the work into four calls in sequence, with the document type from the first picking the schema.", "why": "Best. A fixed sequence of dependent steps is what prompt chaining is for. Each call has one job, and the type becomes an output that can be checked before extraction relies on it."}, {"t": "Keep one call, and reorganise the prompt into four headed sections with the type instructions first.", "why": "Works, but only tidier. Headings make the prompt easier to follow, but four jobs still compete in one pass, with no checkpoint after sorting.", "trap": "only tidier"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Every document takes the same four steps in the same order.", "explanation": "Prompt chaining suits a fixed sequence of dependent steps: each step does one job, and each intermediate result can be checked. Adaptive decomposition is for work whose next step depends on what is found.", "eli5": {"question": "One big prompt does four jobs at once and gets worse as jobs are added. The clue: every document takes the <i>same four steps</i>, in the <i>same order</i>.", "answer": "A small kitchen where one cook takes the order, preps, cooks and plates at once makes mistakes. An assembly line, with one station per step, works better when every dish follows the same route. Each station checks what it receives. That is <b>prompt chaining</b>: a fixed sequence of focused calls. <b>Rule: same steps every time → a fixed chain. Steps depend on what you find → a coordinator.</b>", "options": ["A head chef deciding each next step is great for unusual orders. Here every dish follows the same route.", "A taster at the end helps, but a wrong order that's cooked well still looks fine.", "Correct. One station per step, and the first station's ticket decides the recipe.", "Writing the recipe in neat sections doesn't give the cook fewer jobs at once."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more agent autonomy is more robust</b>. Adaptive decomposition is for work whose next step depends on what is found. When every document follows the same dependent steps, a <b>fixed chain</b> of focused calls is simpler and more predictable, and it creates a checkpoint after each step.", "diagram": "classify → extract(schema by type) → standardise → check\n   ↑ type can be checked here", "mapTitle": "How should this multi-step work be split?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Structure", "Why"], "rows": [["Same steps in the same order every time", "Prompt chain", "Focused calls, checkpoints between"], ["Next step depends on what was found", "Coordinator with dynamic subtasks", "Adapts to findings"], ["Many files, each analysed alone", "Per-file passes plus integration", "Avoids attention dilution"], ["Subtle errors in finished output", "Independent review instance", "Fresh eyes catch more"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One prompt does four jobs in a single call", "means": "Four jobs compete in one pass. Headings (<b>[[3]]</b>) keep that."}, {"quote": "Accuracy dropped when the contract checks were added", "means": "Each job added dilutes the others."}, {"quote": "When an endorsement is read as a certificate, every later step is wrong but looks consistent", "means": "The type needs its own checkpoint. A reviewer at the end (<b>[[1]]</b>) sees consistent output."}, {"quote": "Every document takes the same four steps in the same order", "means": "The decider. A fixed sequence, so a coordinator (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more than needed. Chain it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Fixed sequence of dependent steps → prompt chaining. Steps that depend on findings → adaptive decomposition.", "guide": {"obj": "1.6 Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows", "quote": "Fixed sequential pipelines (prompt chaining) vs dynamic adaptive decomposition based on intermediate findings"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-10", "was": "s6-10", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Prerequisite ordering", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "After extraction, enrichment tools look up the carrier's financial rating and the client's contract terms. They need the carrier and document type that <code>extract_metadata</code> establishes. In about 8% of runs, the model calls an enrichment tool first, with a carrier guessed from the file name. In another 3%, it replies in prose on its first turn and calls no tool. How can metadata <b>come first on every request</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_metadata\"}</code> on the first request, then auto.", "why": "Best. Forcing the named tool makes the first call <code>extract_metadata</code> on every request. It fixes both failures: enrichment can't go first, and the model can't answer in prose."}, {"t": "Expand the enrichment tools' descriptions to say the carrier must come from <code>extract_metadata</code> output.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clearer descriptions improve tool selection but stay probabilistic, and they do nothing for the prose replies.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a pre-call hook that rejects enrichment calls until <code>extract_metadata</code> has returned, naming it.", "why": "Works, but half the problem. A prerequisite gate blocks enrichment from going first, but it can't make the model call any tool, so the prose replies still skip the metadata step.", "trap": "half the problem"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>{\"type\": \"any\"}</code> for the opening turn, and keep it until metadata exists.", "why": "Works, but half the problem. “any” stops the prose replies, but the model can still pick an enrichment tool first.", "trap": "half the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "In another 3%, it replies in prose on its first turn and calls no tool.", "explanation": "tool_choice answers different questions: auto lets the model decide, any requires some tool, and a forced named tool requires that one. Use the forced setting when a particular tool must run first.", "eli5": {"question": "The model sometimes skips the metadata step, either by calling another tool first or by just writing text. The clue: <i>two</i> different failures, and one of them calls no tool at all.", "answer": "A nurse must take your details before the doctor sees you. A guard who stops you seeing the doctor first fixes one problem, but not patients who walk out without seeing anyone. Saying “your first stop is the details desk, no exceptions” fixes both. That is a <b>forced <code>tool_choice</code></b> naming <code>extract_metadata</code>. <b>Rule: a specific tool must run first → force that tool by name.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The first stop is fixed to the details desk for everyone.", "Better signs help most patients, but some still go the wrong way, and the ones who walk out aren't helped.", "A guard at the doctor's door stops the wrong order, not patients who walk out.", "“See someone first” stops walk-outs, but they might still see the doctor before the details desk."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a prerequisite gate is always the strongest ordering fix</b>. A gate blocks a wrong call; it cannot make the model call anything. Here some runs call no tool at all. <code>tool_choice</code> can force a <b>specific named tool</b> on the first request, which fixes both failures.", "diagram": "auto  → may answer in prose\nany   → some tool, maybe enrichment\ntool: extract_metadata → that tool, first", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice (or gate) fits the failure?", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A specific tool must run first", "Forced named <code>tool_choice</code>", "That tool, every time"], ["Model replies in text, any tool is fine", "<code>tool_choice: any</code>", "Some tool, not text"], ["Wrong call order, model does call tools", "Prerequisite gate in a hook", "Blocks until the prerequisite ran"], ["Model picks the wrong similar tool", "Better tool descriptions", "Descriptions drive selection"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "They need the carrier and document type that extract_metadata establishes", "means": "A strict ordering need."}, {"quote": "In about 8% of runs, the model calls an enrichment tool first", "means": "One failure: wrong order. A gate (<b>[[2]]</b>) handles this; “any” (<b>[[3]]</b>) does not."}, {"quote": "In another 3%, it replies in prose on its first turn and calls no tool", "means": "The decider. A gate can't force a call, and descriptions (<b>[[1]]</b>) don't help prose replies."}, {"quote": "How can metadata come first on every request?", "means": "Force the named tool: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "A particular tool must run first → force it by name with tool_choice. “any” only guarantees some tool.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Force a tool to run first (e.g., extract_metadata before enrichment); \"any\" guarantees a tool call rather than text"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.2 Narrow task decomposition", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "A committee asks how to prevent falls among hospital inpatients. The coordinator splits the work into three searches: exercise programmes, balance training and strength training. Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic. The committee replies that the summary leaves out bed alarms, medication reviews and ward layout. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give each search subagent wider search terms and a higher limit on papers per topic.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagents. They did their assigned topics well. Wider terms inside “balance training” still won't turn up bed alarms or ward layout.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have synthesis check each draft for missing topics and send out more searches.", "why": "Works, but late. A gap check is a good safety net, yet here it catches the hole only after a full wasted round, and the narrow split repeats on the next request.", "trap": "catches it late"}, {"t": "Have the coordinator split the request by type of prevention first.", "why": "Best. The fault is upstream: all three topics were kinds of exercise. A split by type of prevention (exercise, alarms, medicines, environment) covers the whole question."}, {"t": "Ask the committee to list every subtopic it wants covered in each new request.", "why": "Works, but it moves the coordinator's job onto the committee. Breaking a broad question into parts is exactly what the coordinator is for.", "trap": "shifts the work to the user"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic.", "explanation": "When every subagent does its assigned job well and whole topics are still missing, the coordinator's decomposition was too narrow. Fix the split, not the subagents.", "eli5": {"question": "Every helper did a good job, and the report still misses whole topics. The clue: all three topics were kinds of exercise.", "answer": "A head chef asked for three dishes: soup, salad and bread. All three were perfect, and there was no main course. You don't retrain the cooks; you fix the chef's list. The <b>coordinator</b> is the chef. <b>Rule: if every subagent succeeded and topics are missing, the split was too narrow.</b>", "options": ["Better searching inside “balance training” still never reaches bed alarms.", "A check at the end finds the hole, but only after a wasted round.", "Correct. Fix the list of dishes, not the cooks.", "Asking the guests to write the menu is the chef's job handed back."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a gap in the report means a subagent did a poor job</b>. Here each subagent did exactly what it was asked. The gap was created one step earlier, when the coordinator chose the topics. Overly narrow decomposition gives incomplete coverage even when every subagent works perfectly.", "diagram": "falls prevention\n  -> exercise | balance | strength   (all one kind)\nmissing: alarms, medicines, ward layout", "mapTitle": "Where did the gap start?", "map": {"head": ["Symptom", "Where it started", "Fix there"], "rows": [["Whole topics missing, each subagent fine", "Coordinator's split", "Split the request more broadly"], ["Subagent ignores a rule the user gave", "Coordinator's Task prompt", "Write the rule into the prompt"], ["Subagent's results are poor for its topic", "The subagent itself", "Improve its prompt or tools"], ["Draft has a thin spot on a covered topic", "One round of research", "Gap check, then re-delegate"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator splits the work into three searches: exercise programmes, balance training and strength training", "means": "All three topics are kinds of exercise. The split itself leaves out every other kind of prevention."}, {"quote": "Each subagent returns well-cited findings within its topic", "means": "The decider: the subagents worked. Tuning them (<b>[[0]]</b>) fixes something that isn't broken."}, {"quote": "the summary leaves out bed alarms, medication reviews and ward layout", "means": "Whole topics, not thin sections. A gap check (<b>[[1]]</b>) would find them, but only after a wasted round."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the split where it was made: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Every subagent succeeded, yet topics are missing? Fix the coordinator's split.", "guide": {"obj": "1.2 Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator-subagent patterns", "quote": "Risk: overly narrow decomposition → incomplete coverage of broad topics."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Complete prior findings in the Task prompt", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "When the searches finish, the coordinator writes a short paragraph about the papers they found and sends it to the analysis subagent. Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out. The analysis subagent's findings often cite the wrong paper or no section at all. The citation checker then rejects them. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Let the analysis subagent run its own journal searches and find each of the papers again.", "why": "Works, but it repeats finished work. The searches already found the papers; the coordinator just didn't pass them on in a usable form.", "trap": "duplicate work"}, {"t": "Send the findings to it as a list, with paper titles and section numbers as fields.", "why": "Best. The subagent inherits nothing, so the coordinator passes the complete findings, with the citation details kept apart from the text where they can't be lost."}, {"t": "Add a rule to the analysis subagent's prompt to cite a paper and section for each claim.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagent. It can't cite a section number it was never sent. The gap is in the prompt the coordinator wrote.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have the citation checker find the right paper and section for each claim it rejects.", "why": "Works, but at the end of the line. It repairs citations after the fact, one by one, instead of sending the right details in the first place.", "trap": "catches it late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out.", "explanation": "Subagents receive only what the coordinator writes into the Task prompt. Pass prior agents' complete findings, in a structured form that keeps metadata such as DOI and section apart from the content.", "eli5": {"question": "The analyst cites the wrong papers. The clue: the coordinator's note mixed the titles into sentences and dropped the section numbers.", "answer": "If a manager hands a new colleague a chatty note instead of the file, the colleague can't quote page numbers that weren't in the note. Hand over the file, with the labels on. A subagent starts empty: it knows only the <b>Task prompt</b>. <b>Rule: pass complete findings, with the citation details in their own fields.</b>", "options": ["Sending the colleague to fetch every file again wastes the work already done.", "Correct. Hand over the file with its labels.", "Telling the colleague to quote pages doesn't help if the pages were never handed over.", "Fixing the quotes afterwards is slower than sending them right the first time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent that cites badly needs a stricter instruction</b>. But a subagent knows only what is in its Task prompt. Here the coordinator summarised the search results into prose and dropped the section numbers, so the details the citations need never arrived.", "diagram": "search results -> coordinator prose (no sections)\n               -> analysis subagent -> bad citations", "mapTitle": "What the coordinator should hand on", "map": {"head": ["Need downstream", "Put in the Task prompt", "Why"], "rows": [["Cite paper and section", "Each finding with title, DOI, section fields", "Metadata stays attached to the content"], ["Apply this request's rules", "The rules agreed with the user", "Subagents don't see the coordinator's chat"], ["Build on earlier work", "Complete prior-agent findings", "No re-searching, nothing lost in a summary"], ["Do the job well", "The goal and quality criteria", "Lets the subagent choose how"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator writes a short paragraph about the papers they found", "means": "Prose written by the coordinator, not the complete findings."}, {"quote": "Paper titles appear inside sentences, and section numbers are left out", "means": "The decider: the citation details were mixed in or dropped before the subagent saw them. A citing rule (<b>[[2]]</b>) can't bring them back."}, {"quote": "every claim must cite a paper and section", "means": "The details that went missing are exactly the ones every claim needs."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Send the findings complete, with metadata in separate fields: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Subagents inherit nothing. Pass complete findings, with citation details in their own fields.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "Include complete prior-agent findings in the subagent prompt; use structured formats to separate content from metadata (URLs, doc names, page numbers) for attribution"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-coordinator-split-3", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.3 Goals and quality criteria, not step lists", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The coordinator writes each search Task prompt as fixed steps: search the drug name with the outcome, take the first ten results, and summarise each one. Subagents follow the steps exactly. They miss trials that use a brand name or another word for the outcome. They also summarise reviews the committee did not need. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "State the goal and what counts as good evidence, and let each subagent plan its searches.", "why": "Best. With the goal and a quality bar, a subagent can adapt: try brand names, skip reviews nobody asked for, and stop when the evidence is good enough."}, {"t": "Add steps to also search brand names and outcome synonyms, and take the first twenty results.", "why": "Works, but it patches the list. This request's misses go away; the next request will find a gap the steps didn't foresee.", "trap": "patches the step list"}, {"t": "Give the search subagents a synonym tool that expands each drug name before they search.", "why": "Works, but it blames the subagents' tools. Brand names get found; the subagents still take the first ten results and still don't know which papers the committee needs.", "trap": "blames the subagent"}, {"t": "Have the analysis subagent drop summarised papers that don't answer the committee's question.", "why": "Works, but late, and only half. It removes unneeded reviews after they were summarised, and it can't add the trials the steps missed.", "trap": "catches it late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Subagents follow the steps exactly.", "explanation": "Coordinator prompts should state goals and quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures. Subagents that know what good looks like can adapt their searches; subagents given a recipe follow it, gaps and all.", "eli5": {"question": "The helpers did exactly what the steps said, and missed what mattered. The clue: they follow the steps exactly.", "answer": "Tell a helper “buy the first ten items on aisle 3” and they'll come back with ten items. Tell them “we're cooking for six, one is vegetarian, keep it under $40” and they'll shop well. The <b>goal and quality bar</b> let them adapt. <b>Rule: brief subagents with goals and quality criteria, not recipes.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Say what you need and what good looks like.", "A longer recipe fixes today's miss; tomorrow brings a new one.", "A better tool helps, but the helper still follows the recipe.", "Throwing out the wrong shopping later doesn't bring back what was never bought."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a subagent that misses things needs more instructions or a better tool</b>. Here the subagents followed their steps exactly; the steps were the problem. A step list can't foresee every request. A goal and a quality bar let the subagent decide how to search.", "diagram": "steps:  search X -> first 10 -> summarise  (rigid)\ngoal:   what the committee needs + quality bar\n        -> subagent plans its own searches", "mapTitle": "Recipe or goal?", "map": {"head": ["Brief style", "What the subagent does", "Result"], "rows": [["Fixed steps", "Follows them exactly", "Misses whatever the steps didn't foresee"], ["Longer fixed steps", "Follows more of them", "Fixes today's miss only"], ["Goal + quality criteria", "Plans and adapts its searches", "Finds what the goal needs"], ["Goal only, no criteria", "Adapts, but guesses the bar", "Uneven depth and sources"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator writes each search Task prompt as fixed steps", "means": "The brief is a recipe. The coordinator decides how, so the subagent can't adapt."}, {"quote": "Subagents follow the steps exactly", "means": "The decider: the subagents did what they were told. A new tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) works on the wrong layer."}, {"quote": "They miss trials that use a brand name or another word for the outcome", "means": "More steps (<b>[[1]]</b>) cover this case; the next request brings another one."}, {"quote": "They also summarise reviews the committee did not need", "means": "They don't know what the committee needs. Filtering later (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't add what was missed."}], "rule": "Brief subagents with the goal and quality criteria, not a step list.", "guide": {"obj": "1.3 Configure subagent invocation, context passing, and spawning", "quote": "write coordinator prompts as goals + quality criteria, not step-by-step procedures."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-11", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.5 Hooks that block outgoing tool calls", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Refunds above $150 must go to a human. The system prompt says so, yet a monthly review found a few larger refunds that the agent made itself. Finance wants this to stop completely. What should the team <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks <code>process_refund</code> over the limit and hands the case to a human.", "why": "Best. The hook checks every refund call before it runs. Code enforces the limit every time, which a prompt can't promise."}, {"t": "Restate the limit first in Claude's instructions, with three examples of declining big refunds.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clearer prompting cuts the misses but can't remove them, and finance wants zero.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run a nightly job that finds refunds over $150, reverses them and sends each case to a human.", "why": "Works, but after the money has gone. Reversals catch every case, but customers see a refund taken back, and the rule was still broken first.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Have a second Claude instance review each refund decision before the agent replies to the customer.", "why": "Works, but still a model's judgement. A second instance catches some cases but is probabilistic too, and it adds a model call to every refund.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Finance wants this to stop completely.", "explanation": "Business rules that must hold every time are enforced in code. A PreToolUse hook can inspect each outgoing tool call and block the ones that break the rule.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent was told “no refunds over $150”, and still made a few. The clue: finance wants <i>zero</i>.", "answer": "Telling a cashier “never give more than $150 back” works most days. A till that won't open above $150 without a manager's key works every day. A <b>PreToolUse hook</b> is that till: it checks the refund before it happens. <b>Rule: must hold every time → check it in code, before the action runs.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The till won't open above the limit.", "A bigger sign by the till still relies on the cashier remembering.", "Taking money back the next day is messier than not paying it out.", "A second cashier watching helps, but can also miss things, and costs more."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>clearer instructions will finish the job</b>. They reduce the misses, but a prompt is followed most of the time, not every time. When the requirement is “never”, put a check in code on the tool call itself, before the action runs.", "diagram": "Claude → process_refund($180)\n          → [PreToolUse: over $150? deny + escalate]\n          → tool never runs", "mapTitle": "Before, after, or in the prompt?", "map": {"head": ["Requirement", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook denies the call", "Checks before anything happens"], ["A step must come first (verify, then refund)", "Prerequisite gate in code", "Blocks the later tool until the first succeeds"], ["Data in a result must not reach Claude", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook masks it", "Acts on the result"], ["Behaviour should usually follow a style", "Prompt + examples", "Probabilistic is fine"], ["Output needs a second opinion on quality", "Independent review instance", "Fresh eyes, still probabilistic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Refunds above $150 must go to a human", "means": "A hard business rule tied to one tool call, which a hook can check."}, {"quote": "The system prompt says so, yet a monthly review found a few larger refunds", "means": "The prompt already tried. Moving or repeating it (<b>[[1]]</b>) only lowers the rate."}, {"quote": "Finance wants this to stop completely", "means": "The decider: zero misses means code, before the action. A reviewer model (<b>[[3]]</b>) is probabilistic, and a nightly reversal (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes after the refund."}, {"quote": "What should the team do?", "means": "Check every refund call before it runs: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Must never happen? Block the tool call in a PreToolUse hook, before the action runs.", "guide": {"obj": "1.5 Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool call interception and data normalization", "quote": "Hooks intercept outgoing tool calls to enforce compliance (e.g., block refunds above a threshold)."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D1 · Agentic Architecture", "obj": "1.4 Structured handoff summaries", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "When the agent escalates, staff pick up the case from a queue. Each case shows the line “Customer needs help” and a link to the chat. Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act. Some of them ask the customer to explain the problem again. What should the agent <b>send with each escalation</b>?", "options": [{"t": "The full chat transcript, pasted into the case in place of the link.", "why": "Works, but staff still read the whole chat to find the facts. It saves a click, not the minutes.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "A paragraph written by the agent that describes the problem in its own words.", "why": "Works, but free text varies. Some paragraphs leave out the box or the amount, and staff still have to hunt or ask.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "A summary with set fields: customer ID, cause, refund amount, next step.", "why": "Best. Staff see the facts they act on in the same place every time, without reading the chat."}, {"t": "The customer's account details and recent boxes, pulled fresh from <code>get_customer</code>.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It gives staff data, not the diagnosis: what went wrong and what the agent recommends.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act.", "explanation": "Escalations should carry a structured handoff summary (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) so a human can act without reading the transcript.", "eli5": {"question": "A nurse hands a patient to the next shift by saying “they need help, read the file.”", "answer": "Good handovers use a short card with the same boxes every time: who, what's wrong, what's been done, what to do next. The next nurse acts in seconds. <b>Rule: hand over to a human with a structured summary, not the transcript.</b>", "options": ["Handing over the whole file still means reading the whole file.", "A free-written note helps, but each one leaves out something different.", "Correct. The same short card, every time.", "The patient's records are useful, but they don't say what's wrong today."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more information makes a better handoff</b>. Staff don't need more; they need the few facts they act on, in a fixed place. A structured summary lets them act without reading the transcript.", "diagram": "escalate_to_human(\n  customer_id, cause, refund_amount,\n  recommended_action )\n→ staff act without opening the chat", "mapTitle": "What does a good handoff carry?", "map": {"head": ["Handoff", "What staff do", "Result"], "rows": [["Link only", "Read the whole chat", "Minutes per case"], ["Full transcript", "Read the whole chat", "Minutes per case"], ["Free-text paragraph", "Read, sometimes ask again", "Facts vary"], ["Account data only", "Work out the problem", "No diagnosis"], ["Structured summary with set fields", "Act on it", "Fast and consistent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each case shows the line “Customer needs help” and a link to the chat", "means": "The handoff carries no facts, only a pointer to them."}, {"quote": "Staff spend several minutes reading each chat before they can act", "means": "The decider: the cost is reading. Pasting the transcript in (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps that cost."}, {"quote": "Some of them ask the customer to explain the problem again", "means": "Facts are missing. Free text (<b>[[1]]</b>) sometimes misses them too; account data (<b>[[3]]</b>) has no diagnosis."}, {"quote": "What should the agent send with each escalation?", "means": "Fixed fields staff act on: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Handing a case to a human? Send a structured summary they can act on without the transcript.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "compile structured handoff summaries (customer ID, root cause, refund amount, recommended action) for humans without the transcript."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-05", "was": "m7-s1-05", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 tool_choice any vs forced", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "After each chat, a wrap-up request asks Claude to log the outcome. It should call one of three tools: <code>record_resolved</code>, <code>record_escalated</code> or <code>record_abandoned</code>. When Claude calls one, it picks the right tool. But sometimes it replies with a text summary instead, and that chat is never logged. Which change <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Ask Claude for the outcome first, then force that record tool by name in <code>tool_choice</code>.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Forcing by name does make the call happen, but it needs an extra request per chat and code to read the outcome first.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a line to the wrap-up prompt saying a record tool must be called every time.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A firm line reduces text replies, but with tool_choice on auto Claude can still answer in text.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Check <code>stop_reason</code> on the wrap-up reply and resend it with a reminder if it is <code>end_turn</code>.", "why": "Works, but too late. Catching the miss and resending gets the chat logged, but it costs extra calls when the API can require a tool call up front.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> for that final wrap-up step, and leave <code>auto</code> elsewhere.", "why": "Best. any requires a tool call but still lets Claude choose which one, and its choices are already right."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "When Claude calls one, it picks the right tool.", "explanation": "tool_choice any guarantees a tool call while leaving the choice of tool to Claude. Use it when a call must happen and selection already works.", "eli5": {"question": "At the end of each chat Claude should fill in one of three forms. It picks the right form, but sometimes writes a note instead. The clue: the choice is fine; the problem is skipping the form.", "answer": "Think of a clerk who must stamp one of three boxes before leaving. You don't tell them which box; you just say “you can't leave without stamping one.” In the API that rule is <b><code>tool_choice: any</code></b>. <b>Rule: must call some tool, Claude picks which → any. Must call one specific tool → force it by name.</b>", "options": ["Asking first, then forcing the box, works but takes two trips.", "A sign saying “please stamp” usually works, not every time.", "Checking after and sending them back works, but costs a second trip.", "Correct. Make stamping a box required, and let the clerk pick which."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a firmer instruction</b> will stop the text replies. But with <code>tool_choice</code> on <code>auto</code>, a text reply is always allowed. The API has a setting that requires a tool call while still letting Claude choose the tool.", "diagram": "auto → Claude may reply in text (logs missed)\nany  → must call a tool, Claude picks which one\ntool → must call this one named tool", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice setting fits?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Setting", "Effect"], "rows": [["Normal chat; tools are optional", "<code>auto</code>", "Claude may answer in text"], ["Some tool must be called; Claude chooses", "<code>any</code>", "A tool call, never text"], ["One specific tool must run", "Forced by name", "That tool is called"], ["No tools for this request", "<code>none</code>", "Text only"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When Claude calls one, it picks the right tool", "means": "The decider. Selection works, so Claude should keep choosing. Forcing one tool by name (<b>[[0]]</b>) needs an extra step to pick it."}, {"quote": "But sometimes it replies with a text summary instead, and that chat is never logged", "means": "The call must happen every time. A prompt line (<b>[[1]]</b>) only makes it more likely."}, {"quote": "Which change fits best?", "means": "Prevent the miss rather than catch it (<b>[[2]]</b>): <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "A tool call must happen and Claude picks the right tool? Set tool_choice to any.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "\"any\" guarantees a tool call rather than text"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-06", "was": "m7-s1-06", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Tool descriptions: input formats", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Customers usually describe a box by when it arrived, for example “last Tuesday's box”. The agent passes that date to <code>lookup_order</code> as the box ID, and the call fails. Each customer's box IDs are listed in their <code>get_customer</code> record. The description of <code>lookup_order</code> is one line: “Looks up a box.” What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add few-shot examples to the system prompt that turn a delivery date into a box ID first.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause in place. Examples would teach the date-to-ID step, but they add tokens to every request while the one-line description stays unclear. They are a later step.", "trap": "works around the cause"}, {"t": "Add a routing layer that detects dates and calls <code>get_customer</code> before <code>lookup_order</code>.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt for a first step. Code that spots dates and calls get_customer first would fix this case, but it is fragile and far more work than a better description.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Expand the <code>lookup_order</code> description with the ID format, an example, and where IDs come from.", "why": "Best. Claude learns how to call a tool mainly from its description. This one gives no ID format and no route from a date to an ID, and fixing that is cheap."}, {"t": "Add a <code>lookup_order_by_date</code> tool that takes a delivery date and returns the box.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. A date-based tool would handle this request, but it is new code, and with equally thin descriptions the agent can misuse it too.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The description of lookup_order is one line: “Looks up a box.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the main signal for how to call a tool. A minimal description should first be expanded with input format, examples, edge cases and boundaries.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent keeps putting a date where a box ID should go. The clue: the tool's instructions are four words long.", "answer": "If a form field just says “Box”, people write all sorts of things in it. Write “Box ID, like BX-20431, found on the customer record” and most mistakes stop. For Claude, the <b>tool description</b> is that label. <b>Rule: tool misused and its description is thin → fix the description first.</b>", "options": ["Examples help later, but the label itself is still unclear.", "A program that hunts for dates in messages is a lot of work for a first step.", "Correct. Fix the label: say what an ID looks like and where to find it.", "A second tool with an equally vague label can go wrong the same way."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent needs more examples or smarter routing</b>. But Claude decides how to call a tool mainly from the tool's description. “Looks up a box” gives no ID format and no hint that IDs come from <code>get_customer</code>. Fix that first: it is cheap and it fixes the cause.", "diagram": "\"Looks up a box\" → Claude guesses → date as ID → fail\n+ ID format, example, where IDs come from → right call", "mapTitle": "A tool is called wrongly. What do you fix first?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "First fix"], "rows": [["Wrong input passed; the description is one line", "Description lacks format and examples", "Expand the description"], ["Two similar tools get mixed up", "Overlapping descriptions", "Sharpen each description's boundaries"], ["Descriptions are detailed; behaviour still varies", "Ambiguous cases", "Add few-shot examples"], ["Agent prefers Grep over a better MCP tool", "The MCP description undersells it", "Improve the MCP tool's description"], ["Bad calls happen and the agent can't recover", "Error says only “failed”", "Structured error naming the expected format"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Customers usually describe a box by when it arrived", "means": "The agent gets a date, so the tool must explain how to get from a date to an ID."}, {"quote": "The agent passes that date to lookup_order as the box ID, and the call fails", "means": "The agent doesn't know what a box ID looks like. That is a description problem, not a routing problem (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Each customer's box IDs are listed in their get_customer record", "means": "The data to fix it already exists. The agent just isn't told where to look, which a description can say."}, {"quote": "The description of lookup_order is one line: “Looks up a box.”", "means": "The decider. The cause is a thin description, so examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a new tool (<b>[[3]]</b>) work around it."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "First step = the cheapest change that fixes the cause: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "A tool gets the wrong input and its description is thin? Expand the description first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for selection"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-07", "was": "m7-s1-07", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Structured business errors", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "<code>pause_subscription</code> fails when the next box is already past its packing cut-off. The cut-off depends on the delivery region, and only the backend knows it. Today the tool returns <code>isError</code> with the text “Request failed.” The agent retries several times, then tells the customer the system is down. Which change is <b>best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>check_pause_window</code> tool that the agent calls before every pause attempt.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A new tool would show the cut-off in advance, but it is new code and an extra call, while the real fault is an error that says nothing.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Return a business error with <code>isRetryable</code> false, the cut-off and next pausable box.", "why": "Best. The failure is a policy outcome, not an outage. A business category with isRetryable false stops the retries, and the details give the agent something true to tell the customer."}, {"t": "Return <code>isError</code> with the text “Too late to pause this box. Try the next one.”", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear message helps the agent explain, but without a category or retry flag it must guess whether retrying could help.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a hook that blocks a second <code>pause_subscription</code> call and tells the agent to escalate.", "why": "Works, but wrong action. The retries stop, but a human gets a case the agent could have explained itself, and still with no reason.", "trap": "wrong action"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Today the tool returns isError with the text “Request failed.”", "explanation": "MCP tools should return structured errors: a category (transient, validation, business, permission), whether a retry can help, and a human-readable explanation. Business-rule failures are not retryable.", "eli5": {"question": "The pause tool fails because it's too late for this box, but it only says “Request failed.” So the agent keeps retrying and then blames an outage. The clue: the error message tells the agent nothing.", "answer": "If a shop till just beeps “error”, the cashier tries again and again. If it says “Sale item: no returns”, the cashier stops and explains. A <b>structured error</b> is that clear message: what kind of failure, whether trying again can help, and what to tell the customer. <b>Rule: a business-rule failure → category business, not retryable, plus a plain reason.</b>", "options": ["A “check first” button helps, but it's a new button to build while the till still beeps “error”.", "Correct. Say what kind of failure it is, that retrying won't help, and what to offer instead.", "A clearer message helps, but the agent still has to guess whether to try again.", "Stopping the retries works, but the case goes to a person who didn't need to see it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent should know the rule in advance</b>. But the cut-off depends on the region and only the backend knows it. The backend already knows why the call failed. The fix is to let the error say so, in a form the agent can act on.", "diagram": "now:   \"Request failed\" → retry, retry → \"system down\"\nfixed: business, isRetryable=false, cut-off, next box\n       → \"Too late for this box; I can pause the next\"", "mapTitle": "What should a tool error tell the agent?", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "Category / retry", "What the agent does"], "rows": [["Timeout or outage", "transient · retry", "Try again, then report"], ["Bad input", "validation · fix and retry", "Correct the input"], ["Policy says no (cut-off passed)", "business · no retry", "Explain and offer the alternative"], ["Not allowed for this user", "permission · no retry", "Explain or escalate"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The cut-off depends on the delivery region, and only the backend knows it", "means": "The backend has the answer. Its error should carry it, instead of a new lookup tool (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Today the tool returns isError with the text “Request failed.”", "means": "The decider: the error carries no category, no retry hint and no reason. Better text alone (<b>[[2]]</b>) still leaves the retry question open."}, {"quote": "The agent retries several times, then tells the customer the system is down", "means": "The agent treats a policy outcome as an outage. Blocking retries (<b>[[3]]</b>) stops the loop but sends the case to a person."}, {"quote": "Which change is best?", "means": "A structured business error with a reason and an alternative: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Business-rule failure? Return a structured error: category business, not retryable, with a reason the agent can tell the customer.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "retriable: false + customer-friendly explanation for business violations"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-07", "was": "m7-s3-07", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Forcing a tool to run first", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent has two tools: <code>extract_citation_metadata</code> and <code>analyze_findings</code>. Analysis needs the page and DOI fields that extraction produces, so extraction must come first for each paper. The subagent calls a tool on every turn. On some papers it calls <code>analyze_findings</code> first, and those findings have no page numbers. Which change <b>fixes this most directly</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite both tool descriptions to say extraction comes first and which fields analysis needs.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clearer descriptions help Claude pick the right tool, but the order is still left to the model's judgement.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "On the first request for each paper, force <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>extract_citation_metadata</code>.", "why": "Best. A forced <code>tool_choice</code> makes Claude call that exact tool, so extraction runs first on every paper. Later requests go back to <code>auto</code>."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that looks up page and DOI fields and adds them to each analysis.", "why": "Works, but too late. The hook fills the fields afterwards, but the analysis already ran without them, and the skipped step is hidden.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Merge the two tools into one that extracts the metadata and then analyses the paper.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. One tool fixes the order, but it means rewriting both tools when a single setting does the job.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On some papers it calls analyze_findings first, and those findings have no page numbers.", "explanation": "tool_choice auto lets Claude choose, any forces some tool call, and a forced named tool makes Claude call that exact tool. To make a specific tool run first, force it on that request.", "eli5": {"question": "The helper sometimes analyses a paper before pulling out its page and DOI details. The clue: it does call a tool every time; it just picks the wrong one first.", "answer": "A lab says “label the sample, then test it”. A sign on the wall mostly works. Handing the technician the label printer first, and nothing else, works every time. For Claude, that is a <b>forced <code>tool_choice</code></b>: on the first request, it must call <code>extract_citation_metadata</code>. <b>Rule: a specific tool must run first → force it by name on that request.</b>", "options": ["A better sign on the wall helps, but the technician can still grab the wrong tool.", "Correct. Hand over the label printer first.", "Labelling samples after the test fills the gap, but the test already ran without a label.", "Building one machine that labels and tests works, but it's a big job when handing over the printer first does it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>better descriptions</b> will fix the order. Descriptions help Claude pick between tools, but the choice is still the model's. When one tool must run first, the API can make that call happen: set <code>tool_choice</code> to that named tool on the first request.", "diagram": "request 1: tool_choice = extract_citation_metadata\nrequest 2+: tool_choice = auto → analyze_findings", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice setting fits?", "map": {"head": ["What you need", "Setting or fix", "Effect"], "rows": [["One specific tool must run first", "Forced <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":…}</code>", "That exact tool is called"], ["Claude replies in text when it should call a tool", "<code>tool_choice</code>: <code>any</code>", "Some tool must be called"], ["Claude may answer or call a tool", "<code>tool_choice</code>: <code>auto</code>", "Claude decides"], ["Claude mixes up tools with thin descriptions", "Expand the descriptions", "Better selection, still probabilistic"], ["Results need reshaping before Claude reads them", "<code>PostToolUse</code> hook", "Changes results, not order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Analysis needs the page and DOI fields that extraction produces, so extraction must come first for each paper", "means": "The order matters every time, so a description (<b>[[0]]</b>) that only suggests it is weaker."}, {"quote": "The subagent calls a tool on every turn", "means": "It is not replying in text. The problem is which tool comes first."}, {"quote": "On some papers it calls analyze_findings first, and those findings have no page numbers", "means": "The decider. The wrong tool runs first. Fixing fields afterwards (<b>[[2]]</b>) hides the skipped step."}, {"quote": "Which change fixes this most directly?", "means": "One setting on the first request: <b>[[1]]</b>. Merging tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) is a rebuild."}], "rule": "To make a specific tool run first, force tool_choice to that tool on that request.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Force a tool to run first (e.g., extract_metadata before enrichment)"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-08", "was": "m7-s3-08", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Scoped tool access per subagent", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that holds the full tool set. The synthesis subagent only needs to read analysis results and save drafts. It often runs journal and registry searches in the middle of a draft. Sometimes it saves drafts with the wrong tool. Every tool already has a clear, detailed description. What is the <b>best fix</b> for its tool use?", "options": [{"t": "List in the synthesis system prompt which tools it may use and which belong to other roles.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude will mostly follow the list, but it still holds every tool, and prompt guidance is probabilistic.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks the synthesis subagent from calling search tools.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The hook stops the searches, but it adds code to undo a tool list that is too wide, and the wrong draft tool is still there.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give synthesis its own AgentDefinition whose tool list holds only its read and draft tools.", "why": "Best. Too many tools make selection less reliable, and agents misuse tools outside their role. Scoping the list removes both problems at the source."}, {"t": "Add sample calls and a note on when synthesis should not use each tool to every description.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. Richer descriptions help when descriptions are thin. These are already detailed; the trouble is how many off-role tools it holds.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that holds the full tool set.", "explanation": "Selection reliability drops as the tool count grows, and agents misuse tools outside their specialty. Give each subagent only the tools its role needs.", "eli5": {"question": "The writing helper has every tool in the system, and it keeps using ones that aren't its job. The clue: everyone is built from one template with all the tools.", "answer": "Give a new writer a desk with only a pen and a filing tray, and they write. Give them a desk piled with forty gadgets, and they fiddle with the gadgets. For a subagent, the desk is its <b>tool list in the AgentDefinition</b>. <b>Rule: give each subagent only the tools its role needs.</b>", "options": ["A note saying “don't touch the gadgets” mostly works, but the gadgets are still on the desk.", "A lock on some gadgets works, but it's easier to not put them on the desk.", "Correct. Clear the desk down to a pen and a tray.", "Better labels on the gadgets help when labels are poor. These labels are already good."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>misused tools mean unclear descriptions</b>. That is the first fix when descriptions are thin. Here they are already detailed. The real cause is that the synthesis subagent holds every role's tools, and agents misuse tools outside their specialty.", "diagram": "template: 15 tools → synthesis picks searches\nsynthesis AgentDefinition: read_results, save_draft", "mapTitle": "A subagent uses the wrong tools. What is the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Uses other roles' tools; descriptions are good", "Too many off-role tools", "Scope its tool list in its AgentDefinition"], ["Mixes up similar tools; descriptions are one line", "Thin descriptions", "Expand the descriptions"], ["Needs one cross-role lookup very often", "Round trips through the coordinator", "Add one limited cross-role tool"], ["A call must be blocked for policy reasons", "Compliance rule", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook"], ["Prompt wording pulls Claude towards a tool", "Keyword association", "Reword the system prompt"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "All subagents are built from one AgentDefinition template that holds the full tool set", "means": "The decider. Synthesis carries every role's tools. Telling it which to avoid (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves them all in place."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent only needs to read analysis results and save drafts", "means": "Its role needs very few tools."}, {"quote": "It often runs journal and registry searches in the middle of a draft", "means": "Off-role tools are being used. A hook (<b>[[1]]</b>) blocks the searches but leaves the wide list."}, {"quote": "Every tool already has a clear, detailed description", "means": "Descriptions are not the cause, so improving them (<b>[[3]]</b>) misses."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix for its tool use?", "means": "Give synthesis its own scoped tool list: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Each subagent gets only the tools its role needs, set in its own AgentDefinition.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Agents with off-specialization tools misuse them"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-01", "was": "m7-s4-01", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Grep for content search", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wants to remove an old helper method called <code>legacy_price_round</code>. First they need to find every place that calls it. The calls could be in models, background jobs, views or library code. The file names don't say which files use the helper. How should Claude <b>find the calls</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Glob for every Ruby and view file in the app, then Read each one and note any calls.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Reading every file would find the calls, but it fills the context with thousands of files that never mention the helper.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Grep for the helper's name across the repository, then Read the lines around each match.", "why": "Best. Grep searches inside files, so it finds every call wherever it lives, and Claude reads only the places that matter."}, {"t": "Make the helper raise an error, run the whole test suite with Bash, and list the failures.", "why": "Works, but only for tested code. Failing tests point to real calls, but any call the tests don't reach stays hidden.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Ask an Explore subagent to search the codebase for callers and send back a short list.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The subagent would run the same name search; a separate agent is for long, noisy discovery, not one search.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The file names don't say which files use the helper.", "explanation": "Finding where a name is used is a question about file contents, so Grep is the direct tool. Glob matches file names and paths, and reading everything up front wastes context.", "eli5": {"question": "Someone needs a list of every place one helper is used. The clue: <i>the file names don't say</i> which files use it, so you have to look inside the files.", "answer": "If you want every page in a library that mentions one word, you use the index, not the shelf labels and not by reading every book. <b>Grep</b> is that index: it searches the text inside files. <b>Glob</b> is the shelf label: it matches file names. Here the names give no hint, so search the text and then read only the hits. <b>Rule: looking for words inside files → Grep. Looking for files by name → Glob.</b>", "options": ["Reading every book finds the word, but takes ages and buries you in pages you didn't need.", "Correct. Search the text for the name, then look only at the matches.", "Breaking the helper and running tests finds calls the tests touch. Untested calls stay hidden.", "Sending a helper off to search works, but they would just run the same search for you."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the safest way to find every call is to read everything</b>, or to let the tests tell you. Both find calls, but neither is direct. Finding where a name appears is a question about <b>file contents</b>, and that is exactly what Grep answers. Glob only looks at file names.", "diagram": "Grep \"legacy_price_round\"  → 7 matches, file + line\n  Read those 7 spots            → confirm each call\nGlob **/*.rb → 2,000 files     → names say nothing", "mapTitle": "Which built-in tool answers this question?", "map": {"head": ["What you need", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Every place a name or text appears", "Grep", "Searches inside files"], ["Files whose name or path matches a pattern", "Glob", "Matches paths, not contents"], ["Understand one file or trace a flow", "Read, following imports", "Build understanding step by step"], ["Long, noisy discovery across a big area", "Explore subagent", "Keeps the noise out of the main context"], ["Behaviour at run time", "Bash running tests", "Shows only what tests reach"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "First they need to find every place that calls it", "means": "The task is a full list of uses: a search inside file contents."}, {"quote": "The calls could be in models, background jobs, views or library code", "means": "The calls are spread everywhere, so the search must cover the whole repository. Tests (<b>[[2]]</b>) only reach covered code."}, {"quote": "The file names don't say which files use the helper", "means": "The decider. A file-name search can't narrow it, so reading every file (<b>[[0]]</b>) is the only Glob route, and it is heavy."}, {"quote": "How should Claude find the calls?", "means": "The direct answer is one content search: <b>[[1]]</b>. A subagent (<b>[[3]]</b>) would run the same search with extra machinery."}], "rule": "Words inside files → Grep, then Read only the matches. File names or paths → Glob.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Grep = content search; Glob = file path patterns"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-02", "was": "m7-s4-02", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Structured MCP error responses", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The team's Linear MCP server has a <code>move_issue</code> tool. It fails for two reasons. Sometimes the Linear API times out. Other times the team's workflow forbids the move, such as closing an issue with no reviewer. Both failures return the text <code>Operation failed</code>. Claude retries every failure several times. What should the tool <b>return instead</b>?", "options": [{"t": "An <code>isError</code> result with an error category, a retryable flag and a plain reason.", "why": "Best. Claude can see which failures are worth retrying and which break a workflow rule, and it can explain the rule to the engineer."}, {"t": "Retry timeouts in the server with backoff, then return the same error if they still fail.", "why": "Works, but fixes only the timeouts. Most timeouts clear up inside the server, but a refused move still comes back as the same bare message.", "trap": "only half the problem"}, {"t": "An <code>isError</code> result whose text holds the HTTP status code from Linear.", "why": "Works, but leaves Claude guessing. A status code hints at the kind of failure, but it doesn't say whether to retry or which rule blocked the move.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "The same error, with the workflow rules spelled out in the tool's description.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Claude may avoid some moves that break the rules, but failures still look identical and timeouts still get mixed up with refusals.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Both failures return the text Operation failed.", "explanation": "Return isError with an error category, a retryable flag and a readable reason. Then Claude retries timeouts and stops to explain business-rule refusals instead of retrying them.", "eli5": {"question": "A tool fails for two very different reasons but says the same thing both times. The clue: <i>both failures return the text Operation failed</i>, so Claude can't tell them apart.", "answer": "Imagine a shop till that beeps the same way for “network down, try again” and “card refused”. The cashier keeps swiping the refused card. A good till says which one it is and whether trying again could help. For a tool, that is an <b>isError</b> result with an <b>error category</b>, a <b>retryable flag</b> and a plain reason. <b>Rule: tell the agent what kind of failure it was and whether a retry can help.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The tool says what went wrong and whether trying again can help.", "Retrying inside the server fixes most network blips, but a refused move still beeps the same way.", "A code number is a hint, but Claude still has to guess what to do.", "Listing the rules may prevent some refusals, but the failures still look the same."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the retries are the bug</b>, so fewer or smarter retries fix it. The real fault is that <b>two different failures look identical</b>. A timeout is transient and worth retrying; a workflow refusal is a business rule and will fail every time. Claude can only choose well if the tool tells it which is which.", "diagram": "timeout  → isError, category: transient, retryable: true\nrefused  → isError, category: business,  retryable: false\n           reason: \"needs a reviewer before closing\"", "mapTitle": "What does the agent need from a failed tool call?", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "What to return", "What Claude can then do"], "rows": [["Timeout or brief outage", "<code>isError</code>, transient, retryable", "Retry"], ["Business rule refuses the action", "<code>isError</code>, business, not retryable, plain reason", "Stop and explain"], ["Bad input", "<code>isError</code>, validation, what was wrong", "Fix the input and call again"], ["Only some failures, all transient", "Retry inside the server, report what is left", "See only failures that remain"], ["Search found nothing", "A normal result that says it is empty", "Treat as an answer, not a failure"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Sometimes the Linear API times out", "means": "One failure is transient: retrying helps. Retrying inside the server (<b>[[1]]</b>) handles this half."}, {"quote": "Other times the team's workflow forbids the move", "means": "The other failure is a business rule: retrying never helps, and the engineer needs the reason."}, {"quote": "Both failures return the text Operation failed", "means": "The decider. The fault is identical messages, so the fix is in what the tool returns. A status code (<b>[[2]]</b>) is only a hint, and rules in the description (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't change the message."}, {"quote": "What should the tool return instead?", "means": "A result that names the category and retryability: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Uniform errors cause blind retries. Return isError with a category, a retryable flag and a readable reason.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "Return structured metadata: errorCategory, isRetryable, human-readable description"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-03", "was": "m7-s4-03", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Edit for a targeted change", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Claude often changes one value in large YAML config files. Today it reads the whole file and writes the whole file back. Reviewers find these full rewrites hard to check. One rewrite also quietly changed the file's formatting. The line being changed appears only once in each file. Which tool use should the team <b>ask for</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Bash running <code>sed</code>, with the old line as the pattern and the new line as the replacement.", "why": "Works, but the wrong tool. The shell command changes the line, but it skips the editing tool's match check and is harder to read in the session.", "trap": "wrong tool"}, {"t": "Read and Write as today, with a note in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> to keep the original formatting.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude will mostly keep the formatting, but every change is still a full rewrite for reviewers to check.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Read and Write as today, then a hook that runs the YAML formatter after each write.", "why": "Works, but treats a symptom. The formatter tidies the file, but the change is still a whole-file rewrite.", "trap": "treats a symptom"}, {"t": "Edit, with the line's full current text as the match and the updated line as the replacement.", "why": "Best. Edit changes only the matched line and leaves the rest of the file untouched, which is what reviewers want."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The line being changed appears only once in each file.", "explanation": "Edit makes a targeted change through a unique text match. Read plus Write is the fallback only when the match text isn't unique.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude rewrites a whole file to change one value. The clue: <i>the line being changed appears only once</i>, so Claude can point at exactly that line.", "answer": "To fix one typo in a long letter, you use correction tape on that word. You don't retype the whole letter. <b>Edit</b> is the correction tape: it finds one unique piece of text and swaps it. <b>Read + Write</b> is retyping the letter, which is only needed when the word appears in many places and you can't point at one. <b>Rule: one unique spot → Edit. Text not unique → Read + Write.</b>", "options": ["A shell command works too, but it is a workaround for a job the editing tool already does.", "A note asking for care helps, but the whole letter is still retyped.", "Tidying after retyping fixes the look, not the size of the change.", "Correct. Correction tape on the one line."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat this as <b>a formatting problem</b> and add a rule or a formatter. The real issue is the <b>size of the change</b>: rewriting a whole file to change one value. Claude Code has a tool for small changes, and the deciding fact is that the line is unique, which is what that tool needs.", "diagram": "Edit  old: \"  timeout: 30\"\n      new: \"  timeout: 45\"\n→ one line changes, the rest of the file is untouched", "mapTitle": "Which tool for changing a file?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Change text that is unique in the file", "Edit", "Targeted change by unique match"], ["Match text appears several times", "Read, then Write", "Fallback when Edit's match isn't unique"], ["Create a new file", "Write", "Whole-file operation"], ["Run a project formatter or script", "Bash", "Runs commands"], ["Find which files contain the text", "Grep", "Content search"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Today it reads the whole file and writes the whole file back", "means": "The cause: a whole-file write for a one-value change. Keeping Read and Write (<b>[[1]]</b>, <b>[[2]]</b>) keeps the cause."}, {"quote": "Reviewers find these full rewrites hard to check", "means": "The goal is a small diff, not just tidy formatting."}, {"quote": "The line being changed appears only once in each file", "means": "The decider. A unique match is what Edit needs, so <b>[[3]]</b> works directly."}, {"quote": "Which tool use should the team ask for?", "means": "The built-in editing tool beats a shell workaround (<b>[[0]]</b>) for the same change."}], "rule": "Small change, unique text → Edit. Read + Write only when the text isn't unique.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Edit = targeted change via unique text match"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-04", "was": "m7-s4-04", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 tool_choice any", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An Agent SDK script sends each merged pull request to Claude in one request. It offers two tools: <code>record_changelog</code> and <code>request_clarification</code>. Either tool call is a good result. A plain text reply is not, because the script reads only tool input. Today some replies come back as prose. What should the script <b>change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Force <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>record_changelog</code>, and handle unclear ones in a second request.", "why": "Works, but takes away a choice. Every reply becomes a changelog call, but Claude can no longer ask for clarification, so a second request has to make up for it.", "trap": "takes away a choice"}, {"t": "Keep <code>tool_choice</code> at auto, and add examples that each end in one of the two tools.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples make tool calls more likely, but auto still allows a text reply.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to any, and let Claude pick which of the two tools to call.", "why": "Best. any means Claude must call a tool, and it still chooses between a changelog entry and a clarification."}, {"t": "Keep <code>tool_choice</code> at auto, and resend any reply with no tool call plus a reminder.", "why": "Works, but fixes it after the fact. Most resends will come back as a tool call, but each prose reply costs an extra request.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Either tool call is a good result. A plain text reply is not.", "explanation": "tool_choice auto may return text, any must call some tool, and a forced tool must call that one tool. When several tools are valid and text is not, use any.", "eli5": {"question": "A script needs Claude to answer with a tool, never with plain text, but either of two tools is fine. The clue: <i>either tool call is a good result</i>.", "answer": "Think of a form with two boxes: “add to changelog” or “I need more info”. You want people to tick one of the boxes, not write a note in the margin. <b>tool_choice any</b> says “tick a box, any box”. Forcing one named tool says “tick this box”, which removes the other good choice. <b>Rule: text not allowed, several tools fine → any. Exactly one tool fine → force it.</b>", "options": ["Forcing one box stops margin notes, but also stops the useful “I need more info” answer.", "Showing filled-in examples helps, but people can still write in the margin.", "Correct. Must tick a box, and Claude picks which one.", "Sending the form back when it has a margin note works, but costs a second round each time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>you either trust auto or force a tool</b>. There is a middle setting. <code>any</code> requires a tool call but leaves the choice of tool to Claude. It fits exactly when several tools are good answers and text is not.", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice fits the need?", "map": {"head": ["What is acceptable", "Setting", "Why"], "rows": [["Text or any tool call", "auto", "Claude decides, may answer in text"], ["Any of several tools, never text", "any", "Must call a tool, picks which"], ["Exactly one named tool", "Forced tool by name", "Must call that tool"], ["One tool must run before the others", "Forced tool on the first step", "Sets the order, then relax"], ["Tool input must match a schema", "Strict schema", "Shapes the input, not the choice"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It offers two tools: record_changelog and request_clarification", "means": "There are two valid outcomes. Forcing one (<b>[[0]]</b>) removes the other."}, {"quote": "Either tool call is a good result", "means": "The decider. Any tool is fine, so the setting should require a tool without choosing it."}, {"quote": "A plain text reply is not, because the script reads only tool input", "means": "Text must not happen at all. Examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) and resending (<b>[[3]]</b>) keep auto, which still allows text."}, {"quote": "What should the script change?", "means": "One setting fixes the cause: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Tool call required, any tool fine → tool_choice any. One specific tool required → force it by name.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_choice: \"auto\" (may return text), \"any\" (must call a tool), forced (specific named tool)"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-04", "was": "m7-s5-04", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resources for content catalogs", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The review job's MCP server has a <code>search_docs</code> tool over the team's API guidelines. Before judging a diff, the reviewer calls it many times with broad queries. It is trying to learn which guideline documents exist and how they are organised. Most of these calls return nothing useful. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut these calls?", "options": [{"t": "Expand the <code>search_docs</code> description with its input format and sample queries.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A fuller description helps the reviewer write better queries, but it still has to search to find out what documents exist.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Move the guideline lookups into an Explore subagent that returns a short summary.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. The subagent keeps the noisy results out of the reviewer's context, but it makes the same blind calls.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Publish an MCP resource that lists the guideline documents and their sections.", "why": "Best. A resource shows the catalog directly, so the reviewer knows what exists before it searches."}, {"t": "Split <code>search_docs</code> into one tool per guideline area, each with its own description.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Tool names would hint at the areas, but each still has to be searched to see what is inside, and the tool list grows.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It is trying to learn which guideline documents exist and how they are organised.", "explanation": "MCP resources expose content catalogs such as document outlines, so an agent can see what exists without exploratory tool calls. Tool descriptions fix how a tool is called, not what content exists.", "eli5": {"question": "The reviewer keeps firing vague searches just to find out what guideline documents there are. The clue: the searches are for <i>finding out what exists</i>, not for answers.", "answer": "In a library you don't search the shelves at random to learn what books exist. You read the catalog at the door. An <b>MCP resource</b> is that catalog: the server publishes the list of documents and sections, and the reviewer reads it before searching. <b>Rule: the agent is probing to learn what exists → publish a resource with the catalog.</b>", "options": ["A better search-box label helps you search well, but you still don't know which books exist.", "Sending an assistant to do the random searching: tidier for you, same wasted trips.", "Correct. Hand over the catalog first.", "More search boxes, one per shelf: a hint, but you still have to look inside each one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>wasted tool calls mean the tool is badly described</b>. Here the tool is called correctly. The calls are wasted because the reviewer is using a search tool to learn <b>what content exists</b>. That is a catalog question, and MCP has a separate feature for it: resources.", "diagram": "now:   search_docs(\"api\") × many → mostly empty\nfix:   read resource guidelines://index\n       → titles + sections → targeted search_docs", "mapTitle": "Why is the agent making so many calls?", "map": {"head": ["Cause", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["It doesn't know what content exists", "MCP resource with the catalog", "Shows the map without probing"], ["It calls the tool wrongly or vaguely", "Fuller tool description", "Primary signal for tool use"], ["It picks the wrong one of similar tools", "Rename or split into purpose-specific tools", "Clear boundaries"], ["Its results flood the main context", "Explore subagent returns a summary", "Isolates verbose output"], ["It prefers a built-in over a better MCP tool", "Enhance the MCP tool's description", "Gives it a reason to switch"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Before judging a diff, the reviewer calls it many times with broad queries", "means": "The waste is in the number of calls, not in their results flooding the context (<b>[[1]]</b> only moves them)."}, {"quote": "It is trying to learn which guideline documents exist and how they are organised", "means": "The decider. This is a catalog question, which a resource answers: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Most of these calls return nothing useful", "means": "Better-phrased queries (<b>[[0]]</b>) or more tools to probe (<b>[[3]]</b>) still leave the reviewer guessing what exists."}], "rule": "When an agent probes a tool to learn what content exists, expose a catalog as an MCP resource.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-05", "was": "m7-s5-05", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP tool descriptions vs built-ins", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The review job's MCP server offers <code>find_usages</code>. It finds every caller of a function across modules, including calls made through interfaces. The reviewer still uses Grep on function names, so it misses the interface calls. When it does call <code>find_usages</code>, the results are right. The tool's description reads only “Find usages.” What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> line: to find callers, use <code>find_usages</code>, not Grep.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause. The instruction would nudge the reviewer, but the two-word description, the main thing Claude reads when picking a tool, stays empty.", "trap": "leaves the cause"}, {"t": "Rewrite the <code>find_usages</code> description: what it covers, inputs, when to use it.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are how Claude chooses tools. A two-word description gives it no reason to prefer the MCP tool over Grep."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that turns Grep calls on symbol names into <code>find_usages</code>.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It forces the switch, but deciding which Grep calls are symbol lookups is guesswork, and it gets in the way of real text searches.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Rename <code>find_usages</code> to <code>find_all_callers</code>, which says what it finds.", "why": "Works, but only part of the fix. A clearer name helps, but it can't say that interface calls are covered or when to prefer it over Grep.", "trap": "only part of the fix"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The tool's description reads only “Find usages.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the main signal for tool selection. When an agent prefers a built-in like Grep over a more capable MCP tool, enhance the MCP tool's description first.", "eli5": {"question": "The reviewer has a great tool for finding callers but keeps using plain text search instead. The clue: the tool's description is two words.", "answer": "If a workshop has a laser measure labelled only “Measure”, people keep grabbing the tape measure they know. Put a proper label on it: what it does better and when to use it. A <b>tool description</b> is that label, and it is the main thing Claude reads when choosing tools. <b>Rule: a better MCP tool is ignored → improve its description first.</b>", "options": ["A sign on the wall saying “use the laser”: it helps, but the label on the tool is still blank.", "Correct. A full label tells Claude what the tool does and when to pick it.", "Locking the tape measure away: forceful, and it blocks real text searches too.", "A better name on the box helps, but a name can't explain everything a label can."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an ignored tool needs an order to use it</b>. Claude picks tools mainly from their descriptions. A two-word description gives it no reason to prefer the MCP tool over a familiar built-in. Fix the signal first; add orders or code only if that is not enough.", "mapTitle": "Claude uses the wrong tool. What do you change first?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "First fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Better MCP tool ignored; its description is thin", "Rewrite the description", "Main selection signal"], ["Description is full, built-in still chosen", "Add guidance in CLAUDE.md or the prompt", "Extra nudge"], ["Two tools overlap and get confused", "Rename or split with clear boundaries", "Removes ambiguity"], ["A tool must never be skipped", "Hook or prerequisite gate", "Prompts aren't guarantees"], ["Tool gives wrong results", "Fix the tool itself", "Not a selection problem"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The reviewer still uses Grep on function names, so it misses the interface calls", "means": "A selection problem: Claude chooses the built-in."}, {"quote": "When it does call find_usages, the results are right", "means": "The tool itself is fine. Only how Claude chooses it needs work."}, {"quote": "The tool's description reads only “Find usages.”", "means": "The decider. The main selection signal is nearly empty, so fix it first: <b>[[1]]</b>. A name (<b>[[3]]</b>) can carry only part of it."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "A CLAUDE.md line (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a hook (<b>[[2]]</b>) are later steps if a full description is not enough."}], "rule": "When Claude prefers a built-in over a more capable MCP tool, improve the MCP tool's description first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Enhance MCP tool descriptions so the agent doesn't prefer built-ins (Grep) over more capable MCP tools"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-05", "was": "m6-s1-05", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 System-prompt wording and tool selection", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Each tool's description now lists its inputs, sample requests and when not to use it. Even so, the agent sometimes calls <code>process_refund</code> when a customer only asks about a refund already under way. Every misrouted chat contains words like “money back” or “credit”. Status questions without those words go to <code>lookup_order</code> correctly. The system prompt's refunds section pairs “money back” with <code>process_refund</code>. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Expand both tool descriptions with refund-status cases and a sharper line between checking and issuing.", "why": "Works, but the descriptions are already good. Richer descriptions are the usual first fix. Here routing fails only on certain words, not between the tools.", "trap": "wrong layer"}, {"t": "Split <code>lookup_order</code> into order-details and refund-status tools, each with sample queries.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Purpose-built tools are good design, but status questions already route correctly. The tool boundary is not what fails.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Reword that instruction around the goal a customer is pursuing, rather than trigger phrases.", "why": "Best. Misroutes that follow particular words point to wording that ties those words to a tool. Fixing that wording removes the cause."}, {"t": "Add three example chats where a customer says “money back” and the agent only checks the status.", "why": "Works, but around the cause. Examples could offset the pull, but the instruction that causes it stays in the prompt.", "trap": "works around the cause"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The system prompt's refunds section pairs “money back” with process_refund.", "explanation": "Keyword-sensitive wording in a system prompt can create tool associations that override good descriptions. When misroutes follow particular words, fix that wording first.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent issues a refund when the customer only asks where their refund is. The clue: the mistakes follow <i>particular words</i>, and the system prompt ties those words to the refund tool.", "answer": "Picture a shop assistant whose manager left a note: “when anyone says money back, open the till.” They open the till even for someone asking where their refund is. Good labels on the drawers can't beat the manager's note. That note is <b>keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording</b>. Rewrite it around what the customer wants. <b>Rule: mistakes follow particular words → look for those words in the system prompt.</b>", "options": ["Better drawer labels are the usual first fix, but these labels are already clear.", "Splitting a drawer is good design, but people without the trigger words already get the right drawer.", "Correct. Fix the manager's note so it is about what the customer wants.", "Showing a few examples could pull against the note, but the note is still there."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>tool choice is decided only by the tool descriptions</b>. They are the main signal, not the only one. The model reads the whole context, and a system-prompt line that pairs words with a tool reads like an order. It can beat a good description.", "mapTitle": "Wrong tool picked? Look at the pattern of the mistakes", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Misroutes follow certain words; descriptions already good", "System-prompt wording", "Reword it around the customer's goal"], ["Misroutes at random; descriptions are one-liners", "Tool descriptions", "Expand: inputs, examples, boundaries"], ["One tool does two different jobs", "Tool design", "Split into purpose-specific tools"], ["Agent answers in text instead of calling a tool", "Tool choice", "<code>tool_choice</code> any or forced"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each tool's description now lists its inputs, sample requests and when not to use it", "means": "The descriptions are already good, so expanding them (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds little."}, {"quote": "Status questions without those words go to lookup_order correctly", "means": "The tools are not confused, so splitting one (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "The system prompt's refunds section pairs “money back” with process_refund", "means": "The decider. The cause is this wording. Examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) leave it in place. Reword it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Descriptions good but misroutes follow certain words → fix the system-prompt wording that names them.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces", "quote": "keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-06", "was": "m6-s1-06", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Structured error responses", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "<code>process_refund</code> pays money back through a payment provider. Some calls time out and work on a second try. Most failures, though, are declines because the customer's card is closed. Those fail the same way every time. Both kinds reach the agent as <code>isError</code> with the text “Refund failed.” The agent retries several times and then gives up. Which change to the tool <b>most improves</b> outcomes?", "options": [{"t": "Retry timed-out provider calls inside the tool with backoff, and report only when retries run out.", "why": "Works, but for the smaller part. Retrying inside the tool is right for timeouts, but most failures are closed cards, which no retry fixes.", "trap": "minor cause"}, {"t": "Return closed-card declines as business errors, not retryable, with the payout options left.", "why": "Best. Most failures are permanent. Marking them not retryable, with a note the agent can pass on, stops the retries and gives the customer a way forward."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that turns provider codes into plain reasons the agent can read.", "why": "Works, but half the signal. A readable reason helps, but without a category or retry flag the agent still has to guess whether to try again.", "trap": "only half the job"}, {"t": "Tell the agent in the system prompt to retry a refund once at most, then offer a store credit.", "why": "Works, but too blunt. Fewer retries waste less time, but timeouts lose their second try and closed cards still get no clear reason.", "trap": "too blunt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Most failures, though, are declines because the customer's card is closed.", "explanation": "A generic error hides what the agent needs to recover. Return a category and a retryable flag; business errors also need an explanation the customer can act on.", "eli5": {"question": "Refunds fail for two reasons, but the agent sees only “Refund failed” and keeps retrying. The clue: <i>most failures</i> are closed cards, which fail the same way every time.", "answer": "If a shop's card machine just beeps “failed”, the cashier keeps trying. If it says “card closed, try another method”, the cashier stops and offers something else. That message is a <b>structured error</b>: a category, a flag saying retrying won't help, and words the customer can act on. <b>Rule: tell the agent what kind of failure it is, and whether trying again can help.</b>", "options": ["Trying again quietly helps when the line is busy, but most failures are closed cards.", "Correct. Say the card is closed, that retrying won't help, and what the customer can do instead.", "Plain words are nicer, but they don't say whether to try again.", "One try only stops the waste, but busy lines lose their second chance and closed cards get no reason."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>retry logic</b> is the fix whenever an agent keeps retrying. Retrying is right for <i>transient</i> failures. Most of these failures are <b>business</b> failures that will never succeed. The tool has to say which kind it is.", "diagram": "today:  timeout / closed card → \"Refund failed\" → retry ×N\nbetter: timeout → retry inside tool\n        closed card → business, isRetryable: false\n                      + options to offer", "mapTitle": "A tool call fails. What should the error say?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of failure", "Right handling", "What the agent gets"], "rows": [["Permanent business failure (closed card)", "Business error, not retryable", "A reason and options to offer"], ["Transient (timeout)", "Retry inside the tool with backoff", "Success, or an error after retries"], ["Bad input", "Validation error", "What to fix"], ["Nothing found, call worked", "Valid empty result", "Not an error"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Most failures, though, are declines because the customer's card is closed", "means": "The decider. The bigger share is permanent, so in-tool retries (<b>[[0]]</b>) help the smaller share."}, {"quote": "Both kinds reach the agent as isError with the text “Refund failed.”", "means": "The cause: a generic error. Plain wording alone (<b>[[2]]</b>) still lacks a retry signal."}, {"quote": "The agent retries several times and then gives up", "means": "It cannot tell final from temporary. A blanket retry cap (<b>[[3]]</b>) is blunt. Say which kind: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Generic errors cause blind retries. Return errorCategory and isRetryable, plus words the customer can act on.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "Return structured metadata: errorCategory, isRetryable, human-readable description"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-07", "was": "m6-s1-07", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resources vs tools", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The agent also has a <code>search_policy</code> tool that returns handbook passages for a keyword. In most chats it makes several calls, trying broad keywords before it finds the right section. Each call returns passages that match its keyword. Once the agent knows a section's title, its next search finds that section first time. The section titles rarely change. Which change <b>best cuts</b> the number of calls?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that trims each search result down to its two best passages.", "why": "Works, but saves space, not calls. Shorter results keep the context lean, but the agent still probes with the same number of searches.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Rewrite the <code>search_policy</code> description with sample queries and the keywords it expects.", "why": "Works, but not the missing piece. Better descriptions fix badly formed calls. These calls already return relevant passages; the agent lacks a map of what exists.", "trap": "wrong layer"}, {"t": "Send policy questions to a subagent that searches in its own context and returns a summary.", "why": "Works, but moves the calls. The main context stays clean, but the subagent makes the same probing searches.", "trap": "moves the problem"}, {"t": "Publish every section title as an MCP resource the agent reads before searching.", "why": "Best. Once the agent knows the section titles, its searches land first time. A resource gives it that map from the start."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Once the agent knows a section's title, its next search finds that section first time.", "explanation": "MCP resources expose content catalogues so the agent does not spend exploratory tool calls finding out what exists. Tool descriptions help it form and choose calls.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent searches the handbook several times before it finds the right part. The clue: once it <i>knows a section's title</i>, it finds the section first time.", "answer": "In a library you wander the shelves until you find the contents list; after that you walk straight to the right shelf. Hand people the contents list at the door. For an agent, that list is an <b>MCP resource</b>: a catalogue it can read before it starts searching. <b>Rule: if the agent keeps probing to learn what exists, give it the catalogue as a resource.</b>", "options": ["Shorter answers from the librarian save reading, not trips to the shelves.", "Better instructions on how to search help bad searches. These searches are fine; the agent just doesn't know the shelves.", "Sending a helper to wander the shelves keeps you tidy, but the helper still wanders.", "Correct. Hand over the contents list at the door."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>many tool calls mean the tool description is weak</b>. Here every search returns relevant passages; the agent just doesn't know which sections exist. Knowing the titles makes the next search land first time. That missing map is what an <b>MCP resource</b> provides.", "mapTitle": "Too many exploratory calls. What's missing?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Calls work, but the agent probes to learn what exists", "No catalogue of the content", "Expose the index as an MCP resource"], ["Calls malformed or wrong keywords", "Thin tool description", "Description with formats and examples"], ["Results flood the context", "Verbose output", "Trim results, or a subagent"], ["Similar tools confused", "Overlapping descriptions", "Sharper boundaries or split tools"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each call returns passages that match its keyword", "means": "The searches work, so a better description (<b>[[1]]</b>) fixes nothing broken."}, {"quote": "Once the agent knows a section's title, its next search finds that section first time", "means": "The decider. What's missing is the list of sections. Trimming (<b>[[0]]</b>) or a subagent (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the probing calls."}, {"quote": "The section titles rarely change", "means": "A stable catalogue suits a resource: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Agent probes to discover what exists → expose the catalogue as an MCP resource.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-07", "was": "m6-s3-07", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Splitting a generic tool", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The analysis subagent searches four collections through one MCP tool, <code>search_source(source, text)</code>. The collections are journals, the trial registry, preprints and drug labels. About one call in five goes to the wrong collection. The tool's description is already long, with a worked query for each collection. Each collection has its own query syntax and returns different fields. What should the team <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add four or five examples to the subagent's prompt that show the right collection for typical questions.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples add tokens and help a little, but one tool still hides four different contracts behind a parameter.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Rewrite the description as a table that maps question types to source values, with common mistakes.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. A better description is the right first step when it is thin. This one is already long, and a parameter can't express four syntaxes.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Return <code>isError</code> with a validation category when a query's syntax doesn't match its collection.", "why": "Works, but too late. A clear error helps the agent recover after a wrong call. The wrong calls still happen.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Split it into one purpose-named tool per collection, each with its own parameters and result shape.", "why": "Best. When operations differ in input and output, purpose-specific tools with defined contracts make selection reliable."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each collection has its own query syntax and returns different fields.", "explanation": "Descriptions are the primary selection mechanism, but when one tool covers operations with different inputs and outputs, split it into purpose-specific tools.", "eli5": {"question": "One search tool covers four collections, and Claude picks the wrong one in about one call in five. The clue: the description is already detailed, and each collection <i>works differently</i>.", "answer": "Picture one hospital phone number for the lab, pharmacy, records and radiology, with a long menu. People still press the wrong key, because each department needs different details. Giving each department its own number with its own form fixes it. That is <b>splitting a generic tool</b> into purpose-specific tools with their own inputs and outputs. <b>Rule: different inputs and outputs → separate tools, not a longer description.</b>", "options": ["Showing callers sample calls helps a little, but it's still one number for four departments.", "A longer menu is the first fix for a short one. This menu is already long.", "Telling callers \"wrong department\" helps them redial, but they still dial wrong first.", "Correct. Each department gets its own number and its own form."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>misrouting always means the description needs work</b>. Descriptions are the first fix, and here it is already long and detailed. When one tool hides several operations with <b>different input syntax and output fields</b>, no description makes it clear. Split it into purpose-specific tools.", "diagram": "search_source(source, text)\n  → search_journals(...)  search_registry(...)\n  → search_preprints(...) search_labels(...)", "mapTitle": "Claude picks the wrong tool or target. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Short or vague descriptions", "Not enough to choose by", "Expand descriptions first"], ["Long description, operations differ in I/O", "One tool hides several contracts", "Split into purpose-specific tools"], ["Similar tools with overlapping wording", "Ambiguous boundaries", "Sharpen or rename to remove overlap"], ["Agent can't recover after a bad call", "Generic errors", "<code>isError</code> with a category"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "About one call in five goes to the wrong collection", "means": "A selection problem: the wrong call is made. An error after it (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes too late."}, {"quote": "The tool's description is already long, with a worked query for each collection", "means": "The first fix is done. A rewritten description (<b>[[1]]</b>) or more examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more of the same."}, {"quote": "Each collection has its own query syntax and returns different fields", "means": "The decider. Four contracts in one tool: split them, <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "One tool, several operations with different inputs and outputs → split into purpose-specific tools.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Rename/split generic tools into purpose-specific tools with defined I/O contracts"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-08", "was": "m6-s3-08", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Access failure vs valid empty result", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The journal search tool's wrapper catches connection errors and returns an empty list instead. The journal index goes offline every night for a rebuild that takes up to two hours. Committees send requests at any hour. The coordinator treats an empty list as a search that found nothing. One summary wrongly said no trials of a drug had been published. How should the tool <b>change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry connection errors in the wrapper with backoff for up to a minute, then return the empty list.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Local retries are right for brief faults. A two-hour rebuild outlasts them, and then the outage looks like no results again.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Return outages as failed calls with a category and a retry flag, and empty lists only for real searches.", "why": "Best. Telling an access failure apart from a valid empty result, with a category and retry flag, lets the coordinator wait, reroute or note the gap."}, {"t": "Fall back to the trial registry when the journal search returns nothing, and merge what it finds.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Another source may help, but the coordinator still can't tell an outage from a real absence.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Have the citation checker flag every “no evidence found” claim for a person to confirm before it goes out.", "why": "Works, but too late. A person may catch some wrong claims, but the tool still reports outages as empty searches.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The coordinator treats an empty list as a search that found nothing.", "explanation": "Tools must distinguish access failures from valid empty results and return structured error metadata (isError, category, retryability) so the agent can recover appropriately.", "eli5": {"question": "When the journal index is down, the tool says \"found nothing\" instead of \"couldn't search\". A summary then claimed no trials existed. The clue: the coordinator <i>treats an empty list as a search that found nothing</i>.", "answer": "If a librarian finds the archive locked and tells you \"there are no books on that\", you'll believe it. They should say \"the archive is locked until morning\". That is a <b>structured error</b>: the call failed, here is why, and it's worth retrying later. An empty list then only means a real search found nothing. <b>Rule: \"couldn't look\" and \"looked and found nothing\" must never look the same.</b>", "options": ["Knocking a few more times helps if the door sticks, not if it's locked for two hours.", "Correct. Say the archive is locked and when to come back. Save \"nothing found\" for real searches.", "Checking another library helps, but you still don't know the first one was closed.", "A second person double-checking \"no books\" claims catches some, but the librarian still says it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an empty list is a safe default when a call fails</b>. To the agent, it means \"searched and found nothing\", which is a claim about the evidence. An outage must be reported as a <b>failure</b> with a category and a retry flag, so the coordinator can wait, reroute or note the gap.", "diagram": "index offline → isError, category: unavailable, retryable\nsearch ran, no hits → []", "mapTitle": "A tool returns nothing. What does it mean?", "map": {"head": ["What happened", "What the tool should return", "What the coordinator can do"], "rows": [["Search ran, no matches", "Empty result, success", "Report \"no evidence found\""], ["Brief network blip", "Retry locally first", "Usually never sees it"], ["Source down for a long time", "<code>isError</code>, availability category, retryable", "Wait, reroute or note a gap"], ["Bad query", "<code>isError</code>, validation category", "Fix the query"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The journal search tool's wrapper catches connection errors and returns an empty list instead", "means": "The cause: a failure is disguised as a result."}, {"quote": "The journal index goes offline every night for a rebuild that takes up to two hours", "means": "Too long for a local retry (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The coordinator treats an empty list as a search that found nothing", "means": "The decider. The coordinator can't tell the cases apart. A fallback (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a later human check (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves that confusion in place."}, {"quote": "How should the tool change?", "means": "Report the outage as a structured failure: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Access failure → isError with category and retryability; empty result only when the search really ran.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "distinguish access failures from valid empty results"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-01", "was": "m6-s4-01", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Grep then incremental Read", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "An engineer new to the app wants to learn where the <code>X-Learner-Locale</code> header is read. They also want to see how its value reaches the lesson scheduler. Teams named their request-handling classes differently over the years. Some code reads the header through a shared constant. The engineer wants to follow the trail with Claude and see each file on the way. Which <b>TWO</b> steps should Claude take?", "options": [{"t": "Glob for <code>**/*middleware*.rb</code> and <code>**/*concern*.rb</code> to list likely classes, then Read each one.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Glob matches file names, and these classes were named differently over the years, so some places that read the header are missed.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Grep the Ruby code for the header's name, then Grep for any constant found to hold it.", "why": "Best. Grep searches file contents, so it finds the header and its constant wherever they sit, whatever the files are called."}, {"t": "Read the files the searches find and follow their calls toward the scheduler, file by file.", "why": "Best. Find the entry points by content, then Read along the flow, opening each next file only when the trail reaches it."}, {"t": "Ask an Explore subagent to trace the header to the scheduler and return a summary of the path.", "why": "Works, but hides the trail. An Explore subagent keeps the discovery out of this session and sends back only a summary, while the engineer wants to see each file.", "trap": "hides the trail"}], "answer": [1, 2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The engineer wants to follow the trail with Claude and see each file on the way.", "explanation": "Grep finds content wherever it lives, whatever the file names. Then Read follows the flow step by step, which is how you build understanding without loading everything up front.", "eli5": {"question": "Someone wants to learn how one header travels through the code, and watch it happen. The clues: class names are messy, the header hides behind a constant, and <i>they want to see each file</i>.", "answer": "Think of finding a person in a big building. Door name plates are unreliable, so you ask \"where is the word Maria written?\" (<b>Grep</b>). Then you walk from room to room, following the signs (<b>Read</b>, one file at a time). Sending a runner who comes back with a note (an <b>Explore subagent</b>) is quicker, but you don't see the rooms. <b>Rule: find by content with Grep, then follow the flow with Read.</b>", "options": ["Name plates on doors: some rooms that use the header have other names, so you miss them.", "Correct. Search for the words themselves, including the constant that hides them.", "Correct. Walk the trail one room at a time, so the engineer sees each step.", "A runner brings back a note. That wins when only the answer matters, but here the engineer wants to see the walk."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>finding files is about their names</b>, so Glob comes first. Glob matches paths. When names are inconsistent, the reliable signal is what the file <i>contains</i>, and that is Grep. After that, understanding grows by reading along the flow, not by opening everything at once.", "diagram": "Grep 'X-Learner-Locale' -> LOCALE_HEADER constant\nGrep 'LOCALE_HEADER'      -> 3 files\nRead file 1 -> calls LessonPlanner\nRead LessonPlanner -> calls Scheduler", "mapTitle": "Which built-in tool for this step?", "map": {"head": ["What you need", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Find where some text or name appears", "Grep", "Searches file contents"], ["List files by a name or path pattern", "Glob", "Matches paths, not contents"], ["Follow a flow from a found entry point", "Read, one file at a time", "Builds understanding step by step"], ["Only the conclusion, discovery kept out", "Explore subagent", "Isolates verbose output"], ["Change one unique line", "Edit", "Targeted change by unique match"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Teams named their request-handling classes differently over the years", "means": "File names are a poor guide, so a Glob on names (<b>[[0]]</b>) misses some files."}, {"quote": "Some code reads the header through a shared constant", "means": "A second content search, for the constant, is needed: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The engineer wants to follow the trail with Claude and see each file on the way", "means": "The decider. Read along the flow in this session (<b>[[2]]</b>); a subagent summary (<b>[[3]]</b>) hides the trail."}, {"quote": "Which TWO steps should Claude take?", "means": "Search by content, then read along the flow: <b>[[1]]</b> and <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Find entry points by content with Grep, then Read along the flow. Glob is for file names.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Build understanding incrementally: Grep entry points → Read to follow imports/trace flows (not read-all-upfront)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-02", "was": "m6-s4-02", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 User vs project MCP scope", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The Linear MCP server is set up in the repository's <code>.mcp.json</code>, and the whole team uses it. Priya is trying a beta build of that server which adds write tools. Until a security review ends, teammates must not get the beta when they pull. Priya needs it in this repository and in two sibling ones. Its token must stay out of git. Where should she <b>set up</b> the beta server?", "options": [{"t": "As a second entry in the repository's <code>.mcp.json</code>, with its token read from <code>${LINEAR_BETA_TOKEN}</code>.", "why": "Works, but shared on pull. Env-var expansion keeps the token out of git, yet a committed entry reaches every teammate, and it covers only this repository.", "trap": "shared on pull"}, {"t": "As a local-scope server added with <code>claude mcp add</code> inside each of the three repositories.", "why": "Works, but repeated per repo. Local scope stays private, but she must add and update it three times, where one user-level entry covers all of them.", "trap": "repeated per repo"}, {"t": "In <code>.mcp.json</code> on a personal branch of each repository that she keeps rebased on main.", "why": "Works, but fragile. Teammates miss it while the branches stay unmerged, but she keeps three branches in step, and one merge ships it to everyone.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In her user-level config file, <code>~/.claude.json</code>, with the token read from her shell.", "why": "Best. User-level config is personal, stays out of git, suits experimental servers and applies in all her repositories."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Priya needs it in this repository and in two sibling ones.", "explanation": "Project .mcp.json is shared with everyone who pulls. User-level ~/.claude.json is personal and experimental, and it applies across all of that person's repositories.", "eli5": {"question": "One person wants to try a risky new version of a shared tool, in three repositories, without anyone else getting it. The clue: <i>teammates must not get it</i>, and she needs it <i>in three places</i>.", "answer": "The team's toolbox sits in the shared office (<b>.mcp.json</b>). Anything you put there, everyone gets. Priya's own bag (<b>~/.claude.json</b>) goes with her into every room and nobody else sees it. A drawer in each room (<b>local scope</b>) is private too, but she'd have to stock three drawers. <b>Rule: team tool → .mcp.json. Personal or experimental tool → ~/.claude.json.</b>", "options": ["The shared toolbox: everyone gets the beta on their next pull.", "Private drawers work, but three of them to keep stocked.", "A private copy of the shared toolbox. It works until one merge hands it to everyone.", "Correct. Her own bag, in every repository, seen by no one else."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>MCP setup belongs in the repository</b> because that is where the team server lives. That is right for servers the team shares. A server one person is trying out belongs in that person's own config, which also follows her into every repository.", "mapTitle": "Where should this MCP server be configured?", "map": {"head": ["Who needs it", "Where", "Why"], "rows": [["The whole team, in this repo", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code> with <code>${VAR}</code> tokens", "Shared on pull, no secrets in git"], ["Only me, in all my repos", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal and experimental"], ["Only me, in this one repo", "Local scope via <code>claude mcp add</code>", "Private to one project"], ["A standard service like Jira", "A community MCP server", "No need to build your own"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "teammates must not get the beta when they pull", "means": "Anything committed, like <b>[[0]]</b>, reaches them. It must be personal config."}, {"quote": "Priya needs it in this repository and in two sibling ones", "means": "The decider. One user-level entry covers all three; local scope (<b>[[1]]</b>) needs three."}, {"quote": "Its token must stay out of git", "means": "Read it from her environment, which <b>[[3]]</b> does."}], "rule": "Shared server → project .mcp.json. Personal or experimental server → user-level ~/.claude.json.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Scoping: project-level .mcp.json (shared) vs user-level ~/.claude.json (personal/experimental)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-03", "was": "m6-s4-03", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resource for a content catalog", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Before planning work, Claude runs many <code>search_issues</code> calls on the Linear server with guessed words. It does this only to learn which projects and epics exist. The workspace holds a few hundred epics, grouped by team and project. That grouping changes only a few times a month. Which change would <b>most reduce</b> these exploratory calls?", "options": [{"t": "Publish the project and epic list, with owners and short summaries, as an MCP resource.", "why": "Best. An MCP resource exposes a content catalog, so Claude knows what exists without guessing searches."}, {"t": "Expand the <code>search_issues</code> description with the team names, the query syntax and example queries.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. A better description helps Claude call the tool well, but it can't list a few hundred epics, so the guessing goes on.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Keep a hand-written list of the projects and epics in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every session.", "why": "Works, but goes stale. It tells Claude what exists, but someone must update it by hand, and it fills every session, even with no planning.", "trap": "goes stale"}, {"t": "Send Linear lookups to an Explore subagent that searches in its own context and returns the right epic.", "why": "Works, but moves the problem. The main context stays cleaner, but the same guessing searches still run inside the subagent.", "trap": "moves the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "It does this only to learn which projects and epics exist.", "explanation": "MCP resources expose content catalogs such as issue summaries and hierarchies, so the agent knows what exists without exploratory tool calls.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude keeps searching with guesses just to find out what's there. The clue: the searches are <i>only to learn which projects and epics exist</i>. It needs a map, not a better search.", "answer": "In a new library you could guess titles at the search desk all day. Or you could read the floor plan by the door first. An <b>MCP resource</b> is that floor plan: a list of what exists, served by the server and kept current. <b>Rule: lots of calls just to discover what exists → publish a catalog as an MCP resource.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Hand Claude the floor plan before it starts.", "Teaches Claude to use the search desk well. It still has to guess what to search for.", "A floor plan drawn by hand on the wall: goes out of date, and everyone walks past it every day.", "Sends a helper to do the guessing. The guessing still happens."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a search problem needs a better search tool</b>. Here Claude searches well enough; it searches because it doesn't know what exists. That is a catalog problem, and MCP has a feature for it: resources, which expose read-only content the agent can look at directly.", "mapTitle": "Why is Claude making so many calls?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Many calls only to learn what exists", "No catalog", "MCP resource with the catalog"], ["Claude calls the tool with bad queries", "Thin tool description", "Expand the description"], ["Tool results fill the context", "Verbose output", "Trim to the relevant fields"], ["Verbose discovery crowds the main session", "Exploration in main context", "Explore subagent"], ["Claude picks Grep over a better MCP tool", "Weak MCP description", "Strengthen that description"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It does this only to learn which projects and epics exist", "means": "The decider. Discovery, not search quality, so a richer description (<b>[[1]]</b>) misses the cause."}, {"quote": "The workspace holds a few hundred epics, grouped by team and project", "means": "A structured catalog that a resource can expose, too big to write into a description."}, {"quote": "That grouping changes only a few times a month", "means": "It still changes, so a hand-kept list (<b>[[2]]</b>) drifts; a resource served by the server stays current."}, {"quote": "Which change would most reduce these exploratory calls?", "means": "Reduce, not relocate: a subagent (<b>[[3]]</b>) still makes them. <b>[[0]]</b> removes them."}], "rule": "Calls made only to discover what exists → expose a catalog as an MCP resource.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-04", "was": "m6-s4-04", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Tool descriptions on an owned server", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team also built a small MCP server for its feature-flag service, and owns its code. Its three tools have one-line descriptions: <code>get_flag</code> 'Gets flag info', <code>get_flag_rules</code> 'Gets rules' and <code>evaluate_flag</code> 'Evaluates a flag'. Asked whether a learner will see a new lesson flow, Claude calls <code>get_flag</code>. It answers from the default value and misses the targeting rules. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a section to the project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> telling Claude to use <code>evaluate_flag</code> for questions about a learner.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. It steers Claude in this repository only. The team owns the server, so the fix belongs in the descriptions every client reads.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Rename the tools to purpose-specific names such as <code>resolve_flag_for_learner</code> and <code>list_flag_rules</code>.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. Clearer names help, yet they don't state inputs, outputs or when to pick a sibling, and renames touch every caller.", "trap": "more than a first step"}, {"t": "Rewrite each tool's description with inputs, outputs, an example question and when a sibling fits.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are the main way Claude picks a tool. One-line descriptions on similar tools cause exactly this misrouting."}, {"t": "Merge the three tools into one <code>check_flag</code> tool that returns the default, rules and result.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. One tool removes the choice, but it redesigns the server, where better descriptions fix the selection at low cost.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The team also built a small MCP server for its feature-flag service, and owns its code.", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism for tool selection. Minimal descriptions on similar tools cause misrouting; expanding them is the low-effort, high-leverage first step.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude picks the wrong one of three similar tools. The clues: the descriptions are <i>one line each</i>, and the team <i>owns the server</i>, so it can change them.", "answer": "Three drawers labelled \"stuff\", \"things\" and \"items\": anyone grabs the wrong one. Writing a proper label on each drawer (<b>the tool description</b>) fixes it for everyone who opens them. A note on your own fridge (<b>CLAUDE.md</b>) only helps in your kitchen. <b>Rule: wrong tool picked among similar tools → improve the descriptions first.</b>", "options": ["A note on your own fridge. It helps here, but the drawers are yours to relabel.", "New drawer names help, but a name alone doesn't say what's inside, and everyone must relearn them.", "Correct. Proper labels on every drawer.", "Rebuilding the cupboard into one big drawer. It works, but it is a big job for a labelling problem."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude needs telling which tool to use</b>, in the prompt or in CLAUDE.md. But Claude chooses tools mainly from their descriptions. When the descriptions are one line each and the tools overlap, that is the root cause, and the team can fix it at the source because it owns the server.", "mapTitle": "Claude picks the wrong tool. Where is the fix?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "First step", "Why"], "rows": [["Similar tools, one-line descriptions, server you own", "Rewrite the descriptions", "Primary selection signal"], ["Third-party server you can't edit", "Guidance in CLAUDE.md or the prompt", "Only place you can reach"], ["Descriptions are good, names still confuse", "Rename or split into purpose-specific tools", "Clear I/O contracts"], ["A tool must run first, every time", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code> or a hook", "Deterministic order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team also built a small MCP server for its feature-flag service, and owns its code", "means": "The decider. The team can fix the descriptions themselves, so a CLAUDE.md workaround (<b>[[0]]</b>) is the wrong home."}, {"quote": "Its three tools have one-line descriptions", "means": "The root cause: minimal descriptions on similar tools."}, {"quote": "It answers from the default value and misses the targeting rules", "means": "Claude didn't know which tool covers the rules. A description that says so fixes it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "First step: renaming (<b>[[1]]</b>) or merging (<b>[[3]]</b>) are bigger changes."}], "rule": "Misrouting among similar tools → expand the descriptions first; rename or merge later if needed.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for selection; minimal descriptions → unreliable selection among similar tools"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-01", "was": "m6-s5-01", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP server scope and secrets", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The review job links findings to tickets through the tracker vendor's community MCP server. An engineer added the server to the user-level config in the CI runner image, with the API token written into it. Security has flagged the stored token. On-call engineers also rerun reviews from a fresh checkout on their laptops. There the tracker tools are missing. What is the <b>best change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the server in the runner's <code>~/.claude.json</code> and inject the token from the CI secret store at job start.", "why": "Works, but runner only. The stored token is gone, but user-level config does not travel with a checkout, so laptops still lack the tools.", "trap": "runner only"}, {"t": "Commit a setup script that runs <code>claude mcp add</code> on each machine, with the token taken from its environment.", "why": "Works, but an extra step. Each person must remember to run the script, and the server still lives in personal config. The shared home for a team server is <code>.mcp.json</code>.", "trap": "extra step"}, {"t": "Replace the community server with a small tracker tool the team writes and keeps in the repository.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The team takes on code to maintain for a standard integration that a community server already covers, and still has to configure it.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Move the definition into a root <code>.mcp.json</code>, with the credential set as <code>${TRACKER_TOKEN}</code> there.", "why": "Best. Project-level <code>.mcp.json</code> is committed, so every checkout gets the server, and the variable keeps the secret out of the repository and the image."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On-call engineers also rerun reviews from a fresh checkout on their laptops.", "explanation": "Shared MCP servers go in project-level <code>.mcp.json</code>; personal or experimental ones in <code>~/.claude.json</code>. Environment variable expansion lets a committed config carry no secret.", "eli5": {"question": "A tool connection lives in one machine's personal settings, with a password written in. Security dislikes the password, and laptops don't get the tool. The clue: people rerun reviews from a <i>fresh checkout</i>.", "answer": "Think of a shared office printer. If its setup is written on one person's desk note, only that desk can print. Pin the setup to the office wall (the committed <code>.mcp.json</code>) and leave a blank where the password goes (<code>${TRACKER_TOKEN}</code>), which each machine fills in. <b>Rule: a server the team shares goes in .mcp.json, with secrets as environment variables.</b>", "options": ["Fixes the password on one desk. Laptops still don't know the printer exists.", "Everyone must remember to run the setup, and it still ends up on personal notes.", "Building your own printer when a good one is already on the market.", "Correct. Setup on the wall, password filled in by each machine."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is only the stored token</b>. Removing it from the image fixes Security's finding but not the laptops. Where a server is declared decides who gets it: user-level config stays on one machine, project-level <code>.mcp.json</code> travels with every checkout.", "diagram": "~/.claude.json (runner image)   → runner only\n.mcp.json (committed)           → every checkout\n  token: ${TRACKER_TOKEN}       → set by CI or laptop", "mapTitle": "Where should this MCP server be declared?", "map": {"head": ["Who needs the server", "Where to declare it", "Secret"], "rows": [["The whole team and CI", "Root <code>.mcp.json</code>, committed", "<code>${VAR}</code> expansion"], ["Only one person, or an experiment", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "That person's environment"], ["Only the CI runner", "Runner config, token injected at start", "CI secret store"], ["A standard tool like a tracker", "Community MCP server", "Not hand-built code"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer added the server to the user-level config in the CI runner image, with the API token written into it", "means": "Two faults: wrong scope and a stored secret."}, {"quote": "On-call engineers also rerun reviews from a fresh checkout on their laptops", "means": "The decider. The server must come with the repository. Runner-only fixes (<b>[[0]]</b>) miss laptops; scripts (<b>[[1]]</b>) need someone to run them."}, {"quote": "through the tracker vendor's community MCP server", "means": "A community server already exists, so writing one (<b>[[2]]</b>) is extra work. Declare it in <code>.mcp.json</code>: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team-shared MCP servers go in a committed .mcp.json, with secrets as ${VARIABLES}.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Env var expansion in .mcp.json (e.g., ${GITHUB_TOKEN}) — no committed secrets."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-02", "was": "m6-s5-02", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Edit vs Read + Write fallback", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The autofix job must change a timeout value in <code>ClientFactory.kt</code>, for the payments client only. The file holds one retry helper per client. The helper bodies are identical, line for line. Only the class name far above each copy differs. Claude's Edit call fails because the target text matches several places. What should Claude do <b>next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Lengthen <code>old_string</code> upward until it starts at the payments class name, then rerun Edit.", "why": "Works, but fragile. The anchor becomes a long block that must match character for character, and one slip fails the Edit again.", "trap": "fragile anchor"}, {"t": "Read the whole file, change the payments copy's value, and save it back with Write.", "why": "Best. When Edit cannot find a unique match, reading the file and writing it back with the one change is the standard fallback."}, {"t": "Find the payments line number with Grep, then change that line with <code>sed</code> through Bash.", "why": "Works, but wrong tool. It skips Edit's exact-match check, so a wrong line number changes another client silently.", "trap": "wrong tool"}, {"t": "Stop and open an issue asking a developer to make the one-line change by hand.", "why": "Works, but gives up too soon. The change is small and fully known, and a built-in fallback exists.", "trap": "gives up too soon"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The helper bodies are identical, line for line.", "explanation": "Edit makes targeted changes through a unique text match. When no unique anchor exists, Read the file and Write it back with the change.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude needs to change one number, but the same block of text appears several times, so its find-and-replace tool can't tell which copy is meant. The clue: the copies are <i>identical, line for line</i>.", "answer": "Imagine a form with the same paragraph printed three times, and you must fix it only in the second one. Searching for the words finds all three. So you take the whole page, fix the right copy by eye, and hand in the whole page (<b>Read</b> then <b>Write</b>). <b>Rule: when Edit can't find a unique match, Read the file and Write it back.</b>", "options": ["Quoting a huge chunk of the page to point at one spot. Possible, but one typo and it fails again.", "Correct. Take the whole page, fix the right copy, hand it back.", "Counting lines and scribbling over line 214: no check that it's the right copy.", "Asking someone else to do a fix you already know how to make."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a failed Edit means you need a cleverer anchor</b>. Edit finds text by unique match. When the copies are identical, the only unique text is far away, so the anchor grows huge. The exam guide's fallback is simpler: <b>Read the file, change it, Write it back</b>.", "diagram": "class FlightsClient   ┐\n  retry helper (same) │ old_string matches\nclass PaymentsClient  │ every copy\n  retry helper (same) ┘\n→ Read whole file → change payments copy → Write", "mapTitle": "Which built-in tool fits this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Small change, unique text nearby", "Edit", "Targeted, exact match"], ["Text repeats but nearby lines differ", "Edit with a slightly longer anchor", "Short anchor becomes unique"], ["Identical copies, no unique nearby text", "Read + Write", "The standard fallback"], ["Find where something is used", "Grep", "Content search"], ["Find files by name pattern", "Glob", "Path search"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "for the payments client only", "means": "Exactly one copy must change."}, {"quote": "The helper bodies are identical, line for line", "means": "The decider. No short unique anchor exists, so stretching it (<b>[[0]]</b>) makes it long and fragile."}, {"quote": "Claude's Edit call fails because the target text matches several places", "means": "This is the non-unique match case the guide names. Fallback: <b>[[1]]</b>. Line edits by sed (<b>[[2]]</b>) skip the match check."}], "rule": "Edit needs a unique match. When none exists, Read the file and Write it back.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "When Edit fails on non-unique match → Read + Write fallback."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-02", "was": "s5q-s1-02", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Overlapping tool descriptions", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The team added an <code>issue_credit</code> tool. It gives a voucher off the next box. Since then, many customers who asked for their money back on a spoiled box got a voucher instead. These chats often open with a long story about a ruined dinner. The credit tool's description says “Make up for a bad experience.” The refund tool's says “Refund a box.” What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add four sample chats to the system prompt where an upset customer asks for money back and gets a refund.", "why": "Works, but works around the cause. Examples can steer the choice, but the two descriptions still overlap, so new kinds of upset chats keep landing on the voucher.", "trap": "works around the cause"}, {"t": "Merge both into one remedy tool with a type field, set after the agent confirms what the customer wants.", "why": "Works, but too big a first step. One tool with a clear type field can work, but it changes the tool contract and moves the same confusion into a parameter.", "trap": "too big a first step"}, {"t": "Rewrite both descriptions to say what each remedy is for, what it takes, and which requests go to the other.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are how Claude picks a tool. “A bad experience” matches almost every complaint. Clear purposes and boundaries fix the overlap at its source, with little effort."}, {"t": "Add a routing step that labels each request refund or credit and shows the agent only the matching tool.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A router can split the traffic, but it is new machinery that guesses from wording, built before the cheap fix has been tried.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The credit tool's description says “Make up for a bad experience.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the main way Claude chooses between tools. Overlapping, vague descriptions cause misrouting; the first fix is to state each tool's purpose and boundary.", "eli5": {"question": "Two tools, one gives money back and one gives a voucher. Customers who want money get vouchers. The clue: the voucher tool's label says “make up for a bad experience”, which fits almost every complaint.", "answer": "Imagine two drawers in a shop. One is labelled “Refunds”, the other “For unhappy customers”. Every unhappy customer gets sent to the second drawer. The fix is to relabel the drawers clearly: what goes in each, and what belongs in the other one. The labels are the <b>tool descriptions</b>, and they are Claude's main guide for picking a tool. <b>Rule: when Claude picks the wrong tool, fix the descriptions first.</b>", "options": ["Showing staff a few examples helps, but the drawer labels still overlap and new cases go wrong.", "Turning two drawers into one with a sorting tab can work, but it is a rebuild, not a first step.", "Correct. Clear labels saying what each drawer is for, and what belongs next door.", "A doorman who sorts customers by their words is extra staff, hired before the labels were fixed."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the long sad stories are the problem</b>, and that Claude needs examples or a router to see past them. The stories only matter because the credit tool's description, “Make up for a bad experience”, <b>matches every sad story</b>. Descriptions are Claude's main signal for choosing a tool.", "mapTitle": "Claude picks the wrong one of two tools. What do you fix first?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "First fix"], "rows": [["Two tools with vague, overlapping descriptions", "Nothing tells them apart", "Rewrite descriptions with purpose and boundaries"], ["Clear descriptions, but odd cases still misroute", "Hard edge cases", "Add a few targeted examples"], ["Many near-identical tools", "Too many choices", "Merge or split into purpose-specific tools"], ["Description is fine, input format is unclear", "Missing format detail", "Add input formats and examples to the description"], ["Prompt words pull Claude to a tool", "Keyword association in the system prompt", "Reword the system prompt"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "many customers who asked for their money back on a spoiled box got a voucher instead", "means": "A tool-selection error between two similar remedies."}, {"quote": "These chats often open with a long story about a ruined dinner", "means": "The vivid detail. It explains why “bad experience” matches, not a separate problem."}, {"quote": "The credit tool's description says “Make up for a bad experience.”", "means": "The decider. The description overlaps with every complaint, so the fix is the description: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "Low effort, high leverage. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) work around it; a merge (<b>[[1]]</b>) or router (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more than a first step."}], "rule": "Wrong tool chosen between similar tools → first make the descriptions say what each is for and where the boundary is.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Ambiguous/overlapping descriptions cause misrouting"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-07", "was": "s5q-s1-07", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 tool_choice", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A new <code>get_loyalty_tier</code> tool returns the customer's tier, and the tier decides which remedies they may get. Product wants the agent's first reply in every chat to greet the customer with their tier perks. Each chat request already carries the signed-in customer ID. An engineer proposes setting <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> on the first request. Which setup <b>best meets the need</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep <code>any</code> on the first request; at the start of a chat the tier lookup is the likely pick.", "why": "Works, but the wrong tool may run. <code>any</code> forces some tool call, but Claude still chooses which, and <code>get_customer</code> is just as likely a first move.", "trap": "wrong tool may run"}, {"t": "Force <code>get_loyalty_tier</code> by name on the first request, then go back to <code>auto</code>.", "why": "Best. A forced tool choice makes that exact tool run first, so the greeting can use the tier. Going back to <code>auto</code> lets the rest of the chat flow freely."}, {"t": "Keep <code>auto</code> and add a gate that blocks remedy tools until the tier lookup has returned.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A gate orders remedies behind the tier, but nothing makes the lookup happen before the first reply.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Keep <code>auto</code> and open the system prompt with an instruction to look up the tier first.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. The instruction makes the lookup likely, not certain, and the need is every chat.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Product wants the agent's first reply in every chat to greet the customer with their tier perks.", "explanation": "tool_choice any forces some tool call but lets Claude pick which. To make one specific tool run first, force it by name, then return to auto.", "eli5": {"question": "One particular tool must run before the first reply in every chat. Someone suggests a setting that forces <i>a</i> tool. The clue: it has to be <i>this</i> tool, every time.", "answer": "A host greeting guests is told “check the guest list before you say hello”. Saying “check something first” lets them check the coat rack instead. Saying “check <i>the guest list</i> first” leaves no room. That is forcing a tool by name: <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": ...}</code>. <code>any</code> only means “use some tool”. <b>Rule: one specific tool must run first → force it by name, then go back to auto.</b>", "options": ["“Check something first” might mean the coat rack, not the guest list.", "Correct. “Check the guest list first”, then carry on as normal.", "Locking the gift table until the list is checked is useful, but the host can still greet without it.", "A note asking the host to check the list works most nights, not every night."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b><code>any</code> means “run the right tool”</b>. It only means “call some tool rather than reply in text”. Claude still picks which. When one specific tool must run, <b>force it by name</b> for that request.", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice setting fits?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Setting", "Effect"], "rows": [["This exact tool must run now", "Forced <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":...}</code>", "That tool runs, no choice"], ["Some tool must run, Claude picks which", "<code>any</code>", "A tool call, never plain text"], ["Normal conversation", "<code>auto</code>", "Claude may call a tool or reply"], ["Tool B only after tool A has run", "Prerequisite gate", "Orders calls, doesn't start them"], ["Encourage a habit, misses are fine", "Prompt instruction", "Likely, not certain"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Product wants the agent's first reply in every chat to greet the customer with their tier perks", "means": "The decider. The tier must be fetched before the first reply, every chat. A prompt line (<b>[[3]]</b>) is only likely."}, {"quote": "Each chat request already carries the signed-in customer ID", "means": "The tool has its input ready on the first request, so forcing it works."}, {"quote": "An engineer proposes setting tool_choice to any on the first request", "means": "<code>any</code> forces a tool, not this tool (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Which setup best meets the need?", "means": "Force the tool by name, then return to <code>auto</code>: <b>[[1]]</b>. A gate (<b>[[2]]</b>) orders remedies, not the greeting."}], "rule": "any = some tool. Forced = this tool. Force by name when one tool must run first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Force a tool to run first (e.g., extract_metadata before enrichment); \"any\" guarantees a tool call rather than text"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-10", "was": "s5q-s1-10", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Tool granularity & contracts", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "<code>lookup_order</code> takes one free-text <code>query</code> field. It works out whether the text is a box ID, email, tracking number or name. Its description lists those four kinds, with an example of each. Still, the agent sometimes passes a whole sentence, such as “the box with broken eggs”. The tool cannot parse it, and the agent says no order exists. Which change <b>most reliably</b> fixes the bad calls?", "options": [{"t": "Add a line to the description saying whole sentences are not accepted, with two rejected examples.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. More text may cut the bad calls, but the description already lists the kinds with examples, and the single text field still accepts anything.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Return a structured validation error that lists the accepted kinds when a query matches none.", "why": "Works, but recovers instead of prevents. The agent stops saying “no order” and can retry, but the bad call still happens first.", "trap": "recovers, not prevents"}, {"t": "Split it into lookups by box ID, by email and by tracking number, each with one typed field.", "why": "Best. A generic tool with one free-text field invites misuse. Purpose-specific tools with typed inputs make the agent supply a real identifier."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that turns an empty result into a note to ask for a box ID.", "why": "Works, but too late. The customer gets a better reply, but the bad call has already been made, and the hook only changes what follows it.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Its description lists those four kinds, with an example of each.", "explanation": "A generic tool with a free-text parameter invites misuse. Split it into purpose-specific tools with defined input contracts.", "eli5": {"question": "One search box accepts anything, and the agent sometimes types a whole sentence into it. The label already explains what to type. The clue: the description is already good, so the box itself is the problem.", "answer": "A parcel desk with one blank line that says “anything that identifies your parcel” gets “the blue one my aunt sent”. Give the form three labelled boxes, “tracking number”, “email”, “order number”, each checking its format, and people fill in a real identifier. That is <b>splitting a generic tool into purpose-specific tools</b> with typed inputs. <b>Rule: a free-text tool that keeps being misused → split it into tools with defined inputs.</b>", "options": ["A longer note beside the blank line helps a bit, but the line still takes anything.", "Telling people what went wrong after they hand in the form helps them retry, but the bad form still came in.", "Correct. Three labelled boxes, each one checking its format.", "A kinder reply after a failed search helps the customer, but the bad search already happened."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better description</b> fixes any tool misuse. That is the usual first step, and here it has <b>already been taken</b>: the description lists the kinds with examples. When a well-described generic tool is still misused, change the tool's <b>contract</b>.", "diagram": "now:    lookup_order(query: any text)\nbetter: lookup_by_box_id(box_id)\n        lookup_by_email(email)\n        lookup_by_tracking(tracking_no)", "mapTitle": "A tool keeps being called wrongly. What do you change?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Vague or one-line description", "Claude doesn't know the format", "Expand the description first"], ["Good description, free-text field still misused", "Contract accepts anything", "Split into typed, purpose-specific tools"], ["Bad input reaches the backend", "No useful error back", "Structured validation error"], ["Tool returns messy data", "Format, not the call", "<code>PostToolUse</code> normalising hook"], ["Two tools confused with each other", "Overlapping descriptions", "Rewrite both descriptions"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "lookup_order takes one free-text query field", "means": "A generic tool with one open parameter, the shape the guide says to split."}, {"quote": "Its description lists those four kinds, with an example of each", "means": "The decider. The description fix is already done, so more text (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds little."}, {"quote": "the agent sometimes passes a whole sentence", "means": "The call itself is wrong. An error (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a hook (<b>[[3]]</b>) acts after it."}, {"quote": "Which change most reliably fixes the bad calls?", "means": "Stop the bad call from being possible: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Well-described generic tool still misused → split into purpose-specific tools with typed inputs.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Rename/split generic tools into purpose-specific tools with defined I/O contracts"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-06", "was": "s5q-s3-06", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Restricting a subagent's tools", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The reporting subagent's AgentDefinition was copied from the literature-search template. It still holds the journal search and fetch tools. While formatting a summary, it sometimes searches for newer papers and adds them. Those papers never go through citation checking. Reporting is meant to format only findings that citation checking has approved. Which change is <b>most effective</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep its tools, and require it to send any paper it adds back through citation checking.", "why": "Works, but keeps the cause. New papers get checked, but reporting keeps doing research it shouldn't, and each run grows an extra loop.", "trap": "keeps the cause"}, {"t": "Swap its search tools for a scoped <code>verify_reference</code> tool for one-off lookups.", "why": "Works, but wrong need. A scoped cross-role tool suits a frequent, simple lookup a role needs. Reporting needs no lookups at all.", "trap": "wrong need"}, {"t": "Take search and fetch out of the reporting AgentDefinition, and have it list any gaps it sees.", "why": "Best. A subagent should hold only the tools its role needs. Without search tools it can't add unchecked papers, and gaps still reach the coordinator."}, {"t": "Add a prompt section that forbids new searches, with examples of formatting approved findings only.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The prompt cuts the searches, but the tools are still there, and off-role tools get used.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Reporting is meant to format only findings that citation checking has approved.", "explanation": "Give each subagent only the tools its role needs; agents with off-role tools misuse them. Scoped cross-role tools are for frequent, simple needs the role actually has.", "eli5": {"question": "The reporting helper still has search tools left over from a template, and it uses them to add unchecked papers. The clue: its job is only to <i>format approved findings</i>.", "answer": "If the person who lays out the newspaper also has a press pass, sooner or later they add a story nobody fact-checked. Take back the press pass. In the SDK that means the reporting <b>AgentDefinition</b> lists only the tools reporting needs, and it notes any gap it sees for the coordinator. <b>Rule: give each subagent only the tools its role needs.</b>", "options": ["Sending added stories to the fact-checker catches them, but the layout person is still out reporting.", "A small look-up pass suits someone who often needs one fact. The layout person doesn't.", "Correct. Take back the press pass, and let them note what's missing.", "A sign saying “no reporting” helps, but they still hold the press pass."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to <b>manage</b> an off-role tool: check what it brings back, or tell the agent not to use it. The exam guide's point is that agents with off-specialization tools misuse them. When a role needs none of a tool's use, <b>take the tool out</b> of its AgentDefinition. A scoped cross-role tool is for a frequent, simple need the role really has.", "diagram": "reporting tools now:  read, save, journal_search, fetch\nreporting tools fix:  read, save\n  gap noticed → listed in output → coordinator", "mapTitle": "A subagent misuses a tool. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Role needs none of the tool's uses", "Remove it from the AgentDefinition", "Off-role tools get used"], ["Role often needs one simple lookup", "Scoped cross-role tool", "Limited tool for a real, frequent need"], ["Role needs bigger research now and then", "Route it through the coordinator", "Hub keeps control of scope"], ["Right tool held but picked wrongly", "Sharpen tool descriptions", "Descriptions drive selection"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The reporting subagent's AgentDefinition was copied from the literature-search template", "means": "The tool set came with the template, not from reporting's needs."}, {"quote": "While formatting a summary, it sometimes searches for newer papers and adds them", "means": "The off-role tools are being used, as the guide predicts. A prompt ban (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves them in reach."}, {"quote": "Those papers never go through citation checking", "means": "The harm is unchecked claims. Routing them back (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds a loop but keeps reporting researching."}, {"quote": "Reporting is meant to format only findings that citation checking has approved", "means": "The decider. Reporting needs no lookups, so a scoped lookup tool (<b>[[1]]</b>) answers a need it doesn't have. Remove the tools: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Give each subagent only the tools its role needs; remove off-role tools rather than asking it not to use them.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Agents with off-specialization tools misuse them"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-10", "was": "s5q-s3-10", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP tool descriptions vs built-ins", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "An MCP server over past committee summaries offers <code>search_library</code>, which searches by meaning and ranks the hits. Its description reads “Search the library.” The subagents mostly run <code>Grep</code> over a nightly text export instead. They miss summaries that use a brand name where the query used the generic name. They also use <code>Grep</code> on exported dosing tables, where an exact match is right. Which change <b>most reliably</b> redirects library searches?", "options": [{"t": "Expose the library's folder tree as an MCP resource that subagents browse before opening summaries.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A resource saves exploratory calls by showing what exists, but it doesn't make subagents search by meaning.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Remove <code>Grep</code> from the subagents' tools, leaving the MCP tool as their only search.", "why": "Works, but too blunt. Library searches move to the MCP tool, but the exact matches on dosing tables lose the right tool.", "trap": "too blunt"}, {"t": "Rewrite the tool's description: what it finds that keyword matching misses, with sample queries.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are how agents choose tools. Saying what it finds that Grep misses, with examples, steers library searches to it."}, {"t": "Rename <code>search_library</code> to <code>semantic_summary_search</code> and keep its one-line text.", "why": "Works, but only partly. The name hints at meaning-based search, but the one line still says nothing about when to prefer it over Grep.", "trap": "only partly"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Its description reads “Search the library.”", "explanation": "When agents prefer built-in tools over a more capable MCP tool, improve the MCP tool's description so its advantages and use cases are clear. Removing a tool is a blunt fix that also removes its legitimate uses.", "eli5": {"question": "A smart search tool sits unused while helpers do plain word matching and miss results. The clue: its description is one bare line, “Search the library.”", "answer": "A shop has a clever product, but its label just says “Tool”. Shoppers keep buying the plain one they know. Rewrite the label to say what it does that the plain one can't, with examples, and they pick it. For agents, the label is the <b>tool description</b>, the main thing they use to choose a tool. <b>Rule: if agents pick a built-in over a better MCP tool, improve the MCP tool's description.</b>", "options": ["A store map helps people find aisles, but doesn't tell them which tool is better.", "Taking the plain tool off the shelf works, but some jobs really need it.", "Correct. A clear label saying what it does better, with examples.", "A better name helps a little, but the label still says almost nothing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>agents ignore the MCP tool because Grep is available</b>, so Grep should go. Grep is right for exact matches on the dosing tables. The real cause is a description that says nothing about what the MCP tool does better. The exam guide says to <b>enhance MCP tool descriptions</b> so agents don't prefer built-ins.", "mapTitle": "Agents pick the wrong tool. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Built-in used where an MCP tool does better", "Thin MCP description", "Describe its strengths, with examples"], ["Built-in has no legitimate use for the role", "Off-role tool", "Remove it from the tool list"], ["Many exploratory calls to find what exists", "No catalog", "Expose an MCP resource"], ["Generic tool name confuses selection", "Vague name and contract", "Rename or split, plus a description"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "offers search_library, which searches by meaning and ranks the hits", "means": "The MCP tool is the more capable one for finding summaries."}, {"quote": "Its description reads “Search the library.”", "means": "The decider. Nothing tells the agent why to prefer it. A rename alone (<b>[[3]]</b>) keeps this line."}, {"quote": "They miss summaries that use a brand name where the query used the generic name", "means": "Keyword matching misses meaning. A folder catalog (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't change how they search."}, {"quote": "They also use Grep on exported dosing tables, where an exact match is right", "means": "Grep has a real use, so removing it (<b>[[1]]</b>) costs that. Fix the description: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Agents prefer a built-in over a better MCP tool → enhance the MCP tool's description, don't remove the built-in.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Enhance MCP tool descriptions so the agent doesn't prefer built-ins (Grep) over more capable MCP tools"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-01", "was": "s5q-s4-01", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Incremental tracing", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Errors spiked after Tuesday's deploy, and the stack trace ends in one method, <code>apply_regional_discount</code>. The engineer needs every path in the app that calls it. In this same session they will then write the fix, with the callers' real code in front of Claude. The session is fresh, and billing code is spread across many folders. How should Claude <b>trace the callers</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Glob for files with price or billing in their names, then Read the ones that look relevant.", "why": "Works, but only usually. File names hint at content, but callers in jobs, controllers or views with other names are missed.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Read every file in the billing folders in turn, noting a short summary of each in a scratchpad file.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It finds the callers, but it fills the context with files that don't matter before any tracing starts.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask an Explore subagent to map the call paths, and work from the short summary it returns.", "why": "Works, but the code stays in the subagent. The summary lists paths, but the engineer wants the callers' real code in this session for the fix.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Grep for the method's name, Read each match, then trace each caller outward one step at a time.", "why": "Best. Grep finds every call by content, Read brings the real code into this session, and each step out follows what the last read showed."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "In this same session they will then write the fix, with the callers' real code in front of Claude.", "explanation": "Build understanding incrementally: Grep for references, Read the hits, follow the trail outward. An Explore subagent suits verbose discovery when only a summary is needed, not when the code itself must be in the main session.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude must find every place that calls one method, and later fix it with that code in view. The clue: <i>with the callers' real code in front of Claude</i>. It is not about speed.", "answer": "Think of tracing a family tree. You start with one person, find their parents, then their parents' parents, one step at a time. You don't read the whole town register first. In code that means <b>Grep</b> for the method's name (search inside files), <b>Read</b> each hit, then step outward. Everything you read stays in your session, ready for the fix. <b>Rule: trace from a known point with Grep, then Read outward step by step.</b>", "options": ["Picking files by their names is judging a book by its cover. Callers with other names get missed.", "Reading the whole town register finds everyone, but it floods your desk with strangers first.", "A helper's summary would win if a list were enough. Here the real code must be on your desk for the fix.", "Correct. Start from the method and walk outward, keeping every page you read."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an Explore subagent is always the tidy way to explore</b>. It is right when the main session needs only conclusions. Here the engineer will write the fix in this session and wants the callers' code in view. A subagent's reads stay in its own context, so the main session gets only a summary.", "diagram": "Grep 'apply_regional_discount' -> matches -> Read each\n  who calls those? -> Grep again -> Read\n  (every read stays in this session for the fix)", "mapTitle": "How should Claude explore code for this task?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best approach", "Why"], "rows": [["Known starting point, code needed for the fix", "Grep, Read the hits, trace outward", "Builds the picture step by step in this session"], ["Only a conclusion needed, discovery is verbose", "Explore subagent", "Keeps noisy output out of the main context"], ["Looking for files by name or type", "Glob", "Matches file paths, not content"], ["Small, unfamiliar folder to learn fully", "Read the files", "Fine when the whole set is small"], ["Long investigation that may lose its place", "Scratchpad file of findings", "Keeps key facts across context limits"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the stack trace ends in one method, apply_regional_discount", "means": "There is a known starting point, which is exactly what Grep searches for by content."}, {"quote": "The engineer needs every path in the app that calls it", "means": "Every caller, wherever it lives. Guessing from file names (<b>[[0]]</b>) misses some."}, {"quote": "In this same session they will then write the fix, with the callers' real code in front of Claude", "means": "The decider. A subagent summary (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the code behind. <b>[[3]]</b> reads it into this session."}, {"quote": "billing code is spread across many folders", "means": "Reading everything first (<b>[[1]]</b>) fills the context with files that don't matter."}], "rule": "Known starting point → Grep for it, Read the hits, trace outward. Use a subagent only when a summary is enough.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Build understanding incrementally: Grep entry points → Read to follow imports/trace flows (not read-all-upfront)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-02", "was": "s5q-s4-02", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP config & secrets", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team is adding a Sentry MCP server so Claude can read production errors. Sentry gives each person their own token, and its audit log credits every action to the token's owner. Security wants no credential stored in the repository. New joiners should get the server as soon as they clone. How should the server be <b>set up</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Each person adds the server to their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, with their personal token inline.", "why": "Works, but not shared. Each token stays personal and out of git, but joiners don't get the server on clone and must set it up by hand.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Commit the entry to <code>.mcp.json</code>, reading the token from <code>${SENTRY_TOKEN}</code> in each shell.", "why": "Best. The entry is shared on clone, the file holds no secret, and each person's own token keeps the audit trail personal."}, {"t": "Commit the entry to <code>.mcp.json</code>, reading one shared team token from an environment variable.", "why": "Works, but every action is credited to one shared token, so the audit log can no longer say who did what.", "trap": "loses attribution"}, {"t": "Commit <code>.mcp.json</code> with an empty token field, and have each person paste in their own token.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A pasted token sits in a tracked file, and one careless commit puts it in the repository.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "New joiners should get the server as soon as they clone.", "explanation": "Shared MCP servers go in the project's .mcp.json, with secrets supplied by environment-variable expansion such as ${SENTRY_TOKEN}. User-level ~/.claude.json is for personal or experimental servers.", "eli5": {"question": "A shared tool needs a personal key for each person, and no keys may sit in the shared code. The clue: <i>New joiners should get the server as soon as they clone</i>, and the audit wants each action tied to a person.", "answer": "Think of an office door. The lock (the server entry) is the same for everyone and comes with the building. Each person carries their own key card (their token), and the log records whose card opened the door. In Claude Code the shared lock is <code>.mcp.json</code> in the repo, and <code>${SENTRY_TOKEN}</code> tells it to read each person's own key from their shell. <b>Rule: shared server in .mcp.json, secret through an environment variable.</b>", "options": ["Everyone fits their own lock at home. Keys stay private, but new people arrive to find no lock at all.", "Correct. One shared lock, each person's own key card, and no key left in the building.", "One shared card opens the door, but the log can no longer say who came in.", "A blank slot for the key works until someone tapes their key into it and the copy gets shared."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a personal token means a personal config</b>. The token is personal, but the server is shared. Claude Code splits the two: the server entry is committed in <code>.mcp.json</code>, and the token is filled in from each person's environment with <code>${VAR}</code> expansion.", "diagram": ".mcp.json (in git)\n  sentry: token = ${SENTRY_TOKEN}\neach person's shell: export SENTRY_TOKEN=<own token>", "mapTitle": "Where does this MCP setting belong?", "map": {"head": ["What you need", "Where it goes", "Why"], "rows": [["Server everyone uses, from clone", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Version-controlled and shared"], ["A secret in that config", "<code>${VAR}</code> expansion from each shell", "No credential in the repository"], ["A server only you are trying out", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal, not shared on clone"], ["Each action tied to a person", "One token per person", "A shared token hides who acted"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Sentry gives each person their own token, and its audit log credits every action to the token's owner", "means": "Each person must use their own token. One team token (<b>[[2]]</b>) blurs the audit."}, {"quote": "Security wants no credential stored in the repository", "means": "The committed file may name a variable, not hold a value. A token pasted into a tracked file (<b>[[3]]</b>) is one commit away from breaking this."}, {"quote": "New joiners should get the server as soon as they clone", "means": "The decider. The entry must be in the repo, which rules out <code>~/.claude.json</code> (<b>[[0]]</b>). <b>[[1]]</b> meets all three needs."}], "rule": "Shared server → .mcp.json in the repo. Secret → ${VAR} from each person's environment.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Env var expansion in .mcp.json (e.g., ${GITHUB_TOKEN}) — no committed secrets"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-03", "was": "s5q-s4-03", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Connection-time discovery", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Twenty minutes into a session, an engineer adds a Sentry server to the project's <code>.mcp.json</code>. They ask Claude for the latest events in an error group. Claude says none of its tools reach Sentry and offers to call Sentry's API with curl. The same entry works on a teammate's machine, and the engineer's token is set in their shell. What should the engineer <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Exit, then start Claude Code again with <code>--resume</code> on this session, and ask once more.", "why": "Best. Claude Code finds MCP servers and their tools when it connects. A restart connects to the new server, and --resume keeps the work so far."}, {"t": "Expand the Sentry tools' descriptions, and ask Claude to prefer them over Bash and curl.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. Better descriptions help Claude choose a tool it has. Here the tools were never loaded.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Accept Claude's offer, and let it fetch the events with curl through Bash for now.", "why": "Works, but a workaround. Claude gets the events this time, but the Sentry tools stay missing for the rest of the session.", "trap": "works around it"}, {"t": "Open a new terminal, start a fresh session there, and ask about the error group again.", "why": "Works, but loses the work. A new session connects to the server, but the findings so far stay behind in the old one.", "trap": "loses the context"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Twenty minutes into a session, an engineer adds a Sentry server to the project's .mcp.json.", "explanation": "Claude Code discovers MCP servers and their tools at connection time, so a server added mid-session needs a reconnect. Tell 'tool missing' apart from 'tool present but not chosen'.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude can't see a tool that was added while it was running. The clue: <i>Twenty minutes into a session, an engineer adds a Sentry server</i>. The entry itself is fine; it works elsewhere.", "answer": "Think of a TV that scans for channels only when you switch it on. Add a new channel while it is on, and it won't appear until you switch off and on again. Claude Code finds MCP tools when it connects (at <b>connection time</b>). Restarting with <code>--resume</code> rescans and keeps your place in the show. <b>Rule: tool missing after a mid-session config change → reconnect. Tool present but not chosen → fix its description.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Switch off and on to rescan, and pick up where you left off.", "A better channel guide helps you choose among channels you have. This channel isn't in the list yet.", "Watching on your phone gets you tonight's show, but the TV still lacks the channel.", "A second TV scans fresh, but you lose your place in what you were watching."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude ignored the Sentry tools</b>, so their descriptions need work. But Claude says no tool reaches Sentry: the tools are not there at all. Claude Code discovers MCP servers and their tools when it connects. A server added mid-session appears only after a reconnect.", "diagram": "start -> connect to servers in config -> tools loaded\nadd Sentry to .mcp.json    -> no new tools yet\nrestart with --resume      -> connect again -> Sentry", "mapTitle": "Is the tool missing, or present but not chosen?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Server added mid-session, no tools", "Tools load at connection time", "Restart, with <code>--resume</code> to keep the work"], ["Tools loaded, but Claude picks Bash or Grep", "Thin tool descriptions", "Expand the descriptions"], ["Works for a teammate, fails after a fresh start", "Token variable not set", "Set the variable in your shell"], ["Too many tools, wrong one chosen", "Selection gets unreliable", "Scope tools to the role"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Twenty minutes into a session, an engineer adds a Sentry server to the project's .mcp.json", "means": "The decider. The server was added after the session had connected."}, {"quote": "Claude says none of its tools reach Sentry", "means": "The tools are missing, not ignored. Better descriptions (<b>[[1]]</b>) fix a different problem."}, {"quote": "The same entry works on a teammate's machine, and the engineer's token is set in their shell", "means": "The config and the token are fine. Only the connection is stale."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do?", "means": "Reconnect and keep the work: <b>[[0]]</b>. curl (<b>[[2]]</b>) works around it; a fresh session (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the findings behind."}], "rule": "Tool missing after adding a server mid-session → reconnect. MCP tools are discovered at connection time.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "All configured MCP servers' tools discovered at connection time, available simultaneously"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-04", "was": "s5q-s4-04", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Edit vs Write", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A long pricing data file holds rate blocks for each region. The same eight-line default block appears four times, and the lines around each copy are identical too. The blocks are told apart only by their order in the file. Claude must change one rate in the third copy. Its Edit failed because the match text appears in all four places. What should Claude <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run Edit with replace-all on the rate line, then change the other three copies back.", "why": "Works, but the wrong action first. It changes three copies that should stay, then needs three more edits to undo them, each as ambiguous as the first.", "trap": "wrong action"}, {"t": "Read the whole file, then Write it back with the one rate changed in the third copy.", "why": "Best. With no unique text to match, Edit can't target one copy. Read plus Write is the fallback, and a diff review shows the single change."}, {"t": "Change the rate with sed through Bash, anchored on that copy's current line number.", "why": "Works, but fragile. A line number goes stale with any change above it, and the change skips any check that the line is the right one.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a comment naming the region above each copy, then Edit using that comment as the match.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt, and the first step hits the same problem. Adding four comments needs unique anchors too, all to make one edit possible.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The blocks are told apart only by their order in the file.", "explanation": "Edit makes a targeted change by unique text match. When the match is not unique and no unique anchor exists, fall back to Read plus Write.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude must change one of four identical copies, and nothing nearby tells them apart. The clue: <i>The blocks are told apart only by their order in the file</i>.", "answer": "Think of a form with four identical boxes, and you must correct the third one. Saying 'change the box that reads Default rate' fails, because four boxes read that. With nothing unique to point at, you take the whole form, fix the third box, and hand back the whole form. In Claude Code that is <b>Read</b> the file, then <b>Write</b> it back with the one change. <b>Rule: Edit match not unique and no unique text to anchor on → Read plus Write.</b>", "options": ["Correcting all four boxes and then undoing three is more work, and each undo has the same problem.", "Correct. Take the whole form, fix the third box, hand it back.", "'Change line 212' works until someone adds a line above it.", "Labelling every box first would help, but labelling them runs into the same problem and is a big job for one fix."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Edit can always be made to work</b> with a cleverer match or a few prep steps. Edit needs match text that appears exactly once. Here the copies and the lines around them are identical, so no such text exists. The exam guide's answer is the fallback: Read the file and Write it back with the change.", "diagram": "Edit match: default block -> 4 places -> fails\nno unique text near copy 3 -> Read file\n  -> change rate in copy 3 -> Write file -> diff", "mapTitle": "Which tool makes this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["One small change, match text unique", "Edit", "Targeted and easy to review"], ["Edit match not unique, but unique text sits nearby", "Edit with a wider match", "Keeps the change targeted"], ["Edit match not unique, nothing nearby is unique", "Read, then Write the file", "The fallback for non-unique matches"], ["Every copy should change the same way", "Edit with replace-all", "One change applied everywhere"], ["Finding where a change belongs", "Grep", "Searches file contents"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the lines around each copy are identical too", "means": "Widening the match won't help: there is no unique text near the copy."}, {"quote": "The blocks are told apart only by their order in the file", "means": "The decider. Edit matches text, not position, so it can't pick the third copy. <b>[[1]]</b> is the fallback."}, {"quote": "Claude must change one rate in the third copy", "means": "Only one copy should change. Replace-all (<b>[[0]]</b>) changes four and needs undoing."}, {"quote": "Its Edit failed because the match text appears in all four places", "means": "The guide's case: Edit failed on a non-unique match. Line numbers (<b>[[2]]</b>) are fragile, and adding comments (<b>[[3]]</b>) hits the same problem first."}], "rule": "Edit fails on a non-unique match and no unique text exists → Read plus Write.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "When Edit fails on non-unique match → Read + Write fallback"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-08", "was": "s5q-s4-08", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Scoped tool access", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The Sentry MCP server brings read tools for errors and stack traces. It also brings tools that resolve, assign and delete issues. Last week Claude resolved an issue it judged fixed, without asking anyone. Team policy says issue state changes only in Sentry's own interface, where each change is credited to a person. Which control best <b>fits the policy</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that asks the engineer to approve any Sentry call that changes issue state.", "why": "Works, but it still lets a session change issue state after one click. The policy says those changes happen only in Sentry.", "trap": "allows too much"}, {"t": "A rule in the project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> saying issue state changes only in Sentry's interface.", "why": "Works, but only usually. An instruction is followed most of the time, and the tools stay within reach.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Deny the Sentry tools that resolve, assign or delete issues in the committed project settings.", "why": "Best. The tools that change issue state are gone for everyone on clone, and the read tools stay."}, {"t": "Disconnect the server, and have engineers paste stack traces into the conversation.", "why": "Works, but it removes the read tools too, which the team uses for every incident.", "trap": "takes too much away"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Team policy says issue state changes only in Sentry's own interface, where each change is credited to a person.", "explanation": "Give an agent only the tools its role needs. When an action must not happen from the agent at all, remove access to it; approval steps fit actions that are allowed with a sign-off.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude has buttons it is never supposed to press. The clue: <i>issue state changes only in Sentry's own interface</i>. Not 'with approval'; only there.", "answer": "Think of a shop assistant who may look at the stock but must never change prices. You don't ask them to check with you each time, and you don't just put up a sign. You give them a badge that doesn't open the pricing room. In Claude Code that badge is a <b>deny list</b> in the committed project settings: the read tools stay, the state-changing tools are gone. <b>Rule: an action that must never happen from the agent → take the tool away. Allowed with sign-off → approval hook.</b>", "options": ["Asking you before each price change still lets prices change from the shop floor.", "A sign saying 'don't change prices' is usually obeyed, not always.", "Correct. The badge opens the stockroom, not the pricing room.", "Sending the assistant home stops price changes, but nobody can check the stock either."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a human approval step</b> is the safe answer to an agent that acted alone. Approval fits actions that are allowed with a sign-off. This policy says the action never happens from a session at all. The fitting control removes those tools and keeps the read tools the team needs.", "mapTitle": "What does the policy say about this action?", "map": {"head": ["Policy for the action", "Control", "Why"], "rows": [["Never from the agent", "Deny the tool in project settings", "Access removed for everyone on clone"], ["Allowed with a person's sign-off", "Approval hook or permission prompt", "A person checks each call"], ["Allowed within limits", "Hook that blocks calls past the limit", "A deterministic check on each call"], ["A preference, not a rule", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code> instruction", "Guidance, not enforcement"], ["Whole server not needed", "Remove the server", "No tools at all"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The Sentry MCP server brings read tools for errors and stack traces", "means": "The read tools are wanted. Disconnecting (<b>[[3]]</b>) loses them; a deny list (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps them."}, {"quote": "It also brings tools that resolve, assign and delete issues", "means": "The state-changing tools arrived with the server; nobody chose them."}, {"quote": "Last week Claude resolved an issue it judged fixed, without asking anyone", "means": "The tool was in reach, and Claude used it."}, {"quote": "Team policy says issue state changes only in Sentry's own interface", "means": "The decider. Never from a session, so an approval hook (<b>[[0]]</b>) still allows too much, and a CLAUDE.md rule (<b>[[1]]</b>) is only guidance."}], "rule": "An action that must never happen from the agent → remove the tool. Allowed with sign-off → approval step.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Agents with off-specialization tools misuse them"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-05", "was": "s5q-s6-05", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Forcing a first tool", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Two lookup tools, <code>get_carrier_rating</code> and <code>check_license</code>, need the <code>carrier_id</code> that <code>read_header</code> returns. All three tools are offered on every request. Each certificate is handled in its own conversation. Sometimes the agent calls a lookup first, gets an error, then calls <code>read_header</code> and tries again. Auditors now require the header to be read first on every certificate. Which change <b>meets this requirement</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite both lookup descriptions to say they need the <code>carrier_id</code> from <code>read_header</code>.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Clear descriptions are the main lever for tool choice and would cut the slips, but they cannot promise every certificate.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have the lookups return a structured error telling the agent to call <code>read_header</code> first.", "why": "Works, but too late. A clear error helps the agent recover, and it already recovers. The lookup has still run before the header.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Force <code>read_header</code> through <code>tool_choice</code> on each first request, then go back to auto.", "why": "Best. A forced tool choice makes that tool the first call. Each certificate starts its own conversation, so forcing the first request covers every certificate."}, {"t": "Add a system-prompt rule to read the header first, with two examples showing that order.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A prompt rule with examples raises compliance, but it stays probabilistic, and the auditors want no exceptions.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Auditors now require the header to be read first on every certificate.", "explanation": "A forced tool_choice makes a specific tool run on a specific request, the classic case being a metadata step that later tools depend on. Descriptions and prompt rules improve the odds but cannot promise the order.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent sometimes calls a lookup before reading the header. The clue is: <i>auditors now require the header to be read first on every certificate</i>. That is a guarantee, not an improvement.", "answer": "Telling a builder \"lay the foundation first, please\" mostly works. A site that only lets in the foundation crew on day one always works. Forcing <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>read_header</code> on the first request is that rule at the gate (<b>forced tool choice</b>). After that, the agent picks freely. <b>Rule: when one step must come first every time, force it.</b>", "options": ["Clearer tool descriptions cut the slips, but cannot promise every certificate.", "A helpful error helps recovery, but the wrong call has already happened.", "Correct. The first call has to be <code>read_header</code>.", "A prompt rule with examples is still a request, not a guarantee."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent picks the wrong tool, so improve the tool descriptions</b>. Descriptions are the main signal for tool choice, and they would cut the error rate. But the auditors demand the header first on <i>every</i> certificate. Only a setting that <b>dictates the first call</b> meets that.", "mapTitle": "A tool must run first: which lever controls the order?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Mechanism", "Strength"], "rows": [["Better tool picking in general", "Clear descriptions and boundaries", "Strong, not guaranteed"], ["Some tool call instead of text", "<code>tool_choice: \"any\"</code>", "Does not choose which"], ["A specific tool first, every time", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code> on the first request", "Guaranteed"], ["Block a call until a step is done", "Hook or prerequisite gate", "Guaranteed, in code"], ["Recover after a bad call", "Structured error result", "Recovery, not prevention"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "All three tools are offered on every request", "means": "The agent is free to pick a lookup first."}, {"quote": "Each certificate is handled in its own conversation", "means": "So the first request of each conversation is the first step for each certificate."}, {"quote": "gets an error, then calls read_header and tries again", "means": "Recovery already works. A better error (<b>[[1]]</b>) does not change the order."}, {"quote": "Auditors now require the header to be read first on every certificate", "means": "The decider. A guarantee. Descriptions (<b>[[0]]</b>) and prompt rules (<b>[[3]]</b>) only raise the odds. So <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Forced tool_choice makes a specific tool run first. Descriptions improve picking but cannot promise order.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Force a tool to run first (e.g., extract_metadata before enrichment)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-02", "was": "s1-02", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Structured error responses", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "<code>lookup_order</code> can fail in two ways. Either no box matches the ID, or the order database times out. Both return <code>isError</code> with the text “Operation failed.” The agent sometimes tells a customer their box does not exist when the lookup only timed out. Customers often mistype box IDs. The timeouts come at evening peaks and last several minutes. Which change <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry the database read inside <code>lookup_order</code> with backoff, and return the error once retries run out.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Retries ride out brief blips, but these timeouts last minutes, so the agent still gets the same vague error in the end.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Return an <code>errorCategory</code> of not_found or transient, an <code>isRetryable</code> flag and a short description.", "why": "Best. The agent can now tell a missing box from a timeout. It asks the customer to check a mistyped ID, and says “try again shortly” for a timeout."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that rewrites each failure as “The order service is briefly unavailable.”", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Timeout chats improve, but the hook sees the same text for a mistyped ID, so those customers are told the service is down.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Tell the agent in its prompt to ask the customer to recheck the box ID before saying it does not exist.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A recheck helps mistyped IDs, but the agent still cannot see which failure happened, so timeouts still end in a wrong answer.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Both return isError with the text “Operation failed.”", "explanation": "A uniform error message stops the agent from picking the right recovery. Structured errors that separate a transient timeout from a genuine not-found let it retry or explain correctly.", "eli5": {"question": "Two very different failures come back with the same words. The agent has to guess which one happened, and sometimes guesses wrong. The clue: both return the text “Operation failed.”", "answer": "Picture a shop assistant who phones the stockroom. Whether the item doesn't exist or the stockroom phone is engaged, the reply is just “no”. The fix is a reply that says which kind of “no” it is (<b>structured error with errorCategory and isRetryable</b>). Then the assistant knows whether to say “check the code” or “try again in a minute”. <b>Rule: different failures need different labels, so the agent can choose the right recovery.</b>", "options": ["Ringing again a few times helps for a quick blip. These outages last minutes, so the reply is still the same vague “no”.", "Correct. The reply now says which kind of failure it was.", "Saying “stockroom closed” every time is right for timeouts, but wrong for customers who mistyped their code.", "Asking customers to recheck helps typos, but the assistant still can't tell when the stockroom is down."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the agent is <b>careless</b> and needs better instructions or more retries. But it is doing its best with a message that says nothing. <b>“Operation failed” hides which failure happened</b>, so no prompt or hook downstream can tell them apart. The tool has to say it.", "mapTitle": "What should a failing tool tell the agent?", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "What the tool returns", "What the agent does"], "rows": [["No record matches (bad input)", "<code>errorCategory: not_found</code>, not retryable", "Ask the customer to check the ID"], ["Timeout or outage (transient)", "<code>errorCategory: transient</code>, retryable", "Say “try again shortly” or retry later"], ["Brief blip of a second or two", "Retry inside the tool first", "Usually never sees the error"], ["Policy says no (business)", "Not retryable, with a plain reason", "Explain the rule to the customer"], ["Same text for every failure", "“Operation failed”", "Guesses, often wrong"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Either no box matches the ID, or the order database times out", "means": "Two different failures that need different replies."}, {"quote": "Both return isError with the text “Operation failed.”", "means": "The decider. The message hides the difference. A hook that rewrites it (<b>[[2]]</b>) and a prompt rule (<b>[[3]]</b>) still can't see which one happened."}, {"quote": "Customers often mistype box IDs", "means": "Not-found is common and real, so treating every failure as an outage misleads many customers."}, {"quote": "The timeouts come at evening peaks and last several minutes", "means": "Retries inside the tool (<b>[[0]]</b>) run out before the outage ends."}], "rule": "Return structured errors (category, retryable flag, short description) so the agent can tell a missing record from a timeout.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "Uniform \"Operation failed\" prevents good recovery decisions."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-07", "was": "s1-07", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Tool distribution", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Over time the support agent has gained many tools. Next to the five core ones sit back-office tools such as <code>issue_credit_memo</code>, <code>adjust_inventory</code> and <code>override_price</code>. Each is needed in very few chats, and they are sometimes called when they should not be. Since the list grew, the agent picks the wrong core tool more often, spread across all five. Which change <b>best reverses</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Leave the everyday tools where they are, and move the rarely used ones to a specialist it hands off to.", "why": "Best. A short tool list on the agent that handles most chats restores reliable selection, and the rare tools stay available through the specialist."}, {"t": "Rewrite every tool description with inputs, examples and boundaries that set it apart from its neighbours.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Sharper descriptions fix confusion between look-alike tools. These mistakes are spread across all the core tools and began as the list grew.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks back-office tools unless a supervisor approved the case.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. The hook stops misuse of the back-office tools, but they stay in the list, so choosing among the core tools is still harder.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Merge all the tools into a few broad ones, each taking an action parameter that names the operation.", "why": "Works, but blurs the tools. Fewer tools shortens the list, but generic action tools hide what each operation does, so selection inside them gets harder.", "trap": "blurs the tools"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Since the list grew, the agent picks the wrong core tool more often, spread across all five.", "explanation": "Too many tools on one agent degrades selection. Keep the high-frequency tools on the front-line agent and scope rare capabilities to a specialist.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent's toolbox got crowded, and now it grabs the wrong everyday tool more often. The clue: the mistakes spread across all five core tools and began as the list grew.", "answer": "Think of a kitchen drawer stuffed with gadgets you use once a year. Finding the everyday knife gets slower and sloppier. You move the rare gadgets to a cupboard in the back room (<b>a specialist agent</b>) and keep the drawer for daily tools. <b>Rule: too many tools → keep the frequent ones on the main agent, move rare ones to a specialist.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Clear the drawer; rare gadgets live with a specialist.", "Better labels help when two tools look alike. Here the drawer is simply too full.", "Locking the rare gadgets stops misuse, but they still clutter the drawer.", "Gluing gadgets into a few multi-tools empties the drawer, but now each tool is harder to tell apart inside."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think wrong tool picks always mean <b>bad descriptions</b>. That is true when two tools look alike. But when errors are <b>spread across every tool</b> and started as the list grew, the cause is the <b>number of tools</b>. Selection gets less reliable as one agent holds more of them.", "mapTitle": "The agent picks the wrong tool. Why?", "map": {"head": ["Pattern of mistakes", "Likely cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Spread across all tools, began as the list grew", "Too many tools on one agent", "Move rare tools to a specialist"], ["Between two look-alike tools", "Vague or overlapping descriptions", "Rewrite descriptions with boundaries"], ["Rare tool used when it shouldn't be", "Tool given outside its role", "Scope it to a role, or gate it"], ["One generic tool used for many jobs", "Blurry action-parameter tool", "Split into purpose-specific tools"], ["Model answers in text instead of calling", "Tool choice left to auto", "Set <code>tool_choice</code> for that step"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Over time the support agent has gained many tools", "means": "The list is long, which points to tool count."}, {"quote": "Each is needed in very few chats, and they are sometimes called when they should not be", "means": "These tools are off the agent's main job, so they belong with a specialist: <b>[[0]]</b>. A hook (<b>[[2]]</b>) stops misuse but leaves them in the list."}, {"quote": "Since the list grew, the agent picks the wrong core tool more often, spread across all five", "means": "The decider. Not look-alike confusion, so better descriptions (<b>[[1]]</b>) miss the cause. Merging into generic tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) blurs them."}], "rule": "Errors spread across all tools as the list grew → too many tools. Keep frequent tools on the main agent; move rare ones to a specialist.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Too many tools (18 vs 4-5) degrades selection reliability."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-08", "was": "s1-08", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Tool descriptions", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The description of <code>escalate_to_human</code> is one line. It reads: “Escalate the conversation to a human manager or supervisor.” The agent calls it when customers say things like “my office manager ordered this box”. Most needless handoffs come from messages like that. The same tool also handles refunds above the agent's limit. The other tools have full descriptions with inputs and boundaries. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add example chats to the system prompt where a customer mentions a manager and the agent carries on.", "why": "Works, but works around the cause. Examples help once the description is clear. Here the one-line description is what invites the mistake, so fix it first.", "trap": "works around the cause"}, {"t": "Add a line to the system prompt saying a mention of a manager is not a request for a person.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. The prompt line patches one wording. The tool description is where Claude decides which tool to call, and it still says “manager”.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Split the tool into <code>request_person</code> and <code>escalate_large_refund</code>, each with its own inputs.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt for a first step. Purpose-specific tools can be good design, but it is more work, and each new tool still needs a proper description.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Rewrite the description: when to hand over, when not to, and what the handoff needs as input.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are the main signal for choosing a tool. Stating the real triggers and exclusions fixes the keyword match where it starts."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It reads: “Escalate the conversation to a human manager or supervisor.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism for tool selection. When a description is minimal, expanding it with when to use it, when not to, and its inputs is the highest-leverage first step.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent hands people over whenever they mention a manager, even in passing. The clue: the tool's description is one line that literally says “manager”.", "answer": "Think of a door labelled only “Manager”. People walk through it whenever the word comes up. Rewrite the sign to say who should use the door, who shouldn't, and what to bring (<b>a full tool description</b>). The sign is where the choice is made. <b>Rule: a minimal tool description is the first thing to fix when Claude picks a tool wrongly.</b>", "options": ["Showing examples helps once the sign is clear. Today the sign itself causes the mistake.", "A note elsewhere about the word “manager” patches one case, but the door still says “Manager”.", "Building two new doors can be good later, but it's more work, and each still needs a clear sign.", "Correct. Rewrite the sign: when to use it, when not to, and what to bring."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to fix tool mistakes in the <b>system prompt</b>, with rules or examples. But Claude chooses a tool mainly from its <b>description</b>. A one-line description that mentions “manager” invites keyword matching. The cheapest, most direct first step is to fix the description itself.", "mapTitle": "Claude picks a tool wrongly. Where do you start?", "map": {"head": ["What you find", "First step", "Why"], "rows": [["Description is one line or vague", "Rewrite it: when to use, when not, inputs", "Descriptions drive tool choice"], ["Description is clear, model still slips", "Add contrasting examples", "Examples sharpen an already clear rule"], ["One tool does two unrelated jobs", "Split into purpose-specific tools (later step)", "Clear input contracts per job"], ["Prompt wording pulls toward a tool", "Reword the prompt", "Keyword wording creates false links"], ["Too many tools on one agent", "Move rare tools to a specialist", "Shorter list, better choice"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The description of escalate_to_human is one line. It reads: “Escalate the conversation to a human manager or supervisor.”", "means": "The decider. A minimal description that contains the word “manager” is the root cause."}, {"quote": "The agent calls it when customers say things like “my office manager ordered this box”", "means": "Keyword matching on “manager”. A prompt line (<b>[[1]]</b>) patches this one word but leaves the description as it is."}, {"quote": "The same tool also handles refunds above the agent's limit", "means": "The tool has real uses that must keep working, which the description must name."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "First step means the cheapest fix at the cause: <b>[[3]]</b>. Splitting the tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) is a bigger later move; examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) come after the description is clear."}], "rule": "When Claude misroutes to a tool with a minimal description, rewrite the description first: when to use it, when not, and its inputs.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces", "quote": "Tool descriptions are the primary mechanism LLMs use for selection; minimal descriptions → unreliable selection among similar tools."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-04", "was": "s3-04", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Tool distribution & least privilege", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "While writing a summary, the reporting subagent often stops to confirm small details, such as a drug's approved generic name. Each check goes back through the coordinator to a search subagent, and every round trip adds a long wait. Most checks are one-field lookups. A few ask whether two papers report the same trial, which takes real research across several sources. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give the reporting subagent the full toolset that the literature-search subagent uses.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Full search tools would end the round trips, but agents misuse tools outside their speciality, and same-trial research belongs with the coordinator.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Have the reporting subagent collect its open checks and send them as one batch at the end.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. One batch means fewer round trips, but the draft is left full of open details, and simple lookups still go the long way.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Give the reporter a small tool for one-field lookups, and keep harder questions with the coordinator.", "why": "Best. A limited cross-role tool removes the round trip for the frequent, simple case, and the rarer research questions still go through the coordinator."}, {"t": "Have the coordinator look up likely names and details in advance and add them to the Task prompt.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Prefetching helps when you know what will be asked. The details come up as the summary is written, so the coordinator would be guessing.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Most checks are one-field lookups.", "explanation": "Scope each agent's tools to its role, then make a deliberate exception for a frequent, simple need: a narrow cross-role tool. Work that needs coordination keeps flowing through the coordinator.", "eli5": {"question": "The report writer keeps asking the boss to ask the researcher simple questions, and each trip is slow. The clue: most questions are one-field lookups; only a few need real research.", "answer": "A shop assistant has to ask the manager, who asks the stockroom, every time a customer wants a price. Give the assistant a price scanner for prices, and keep the tricky questions going to the manager. That scanner is a <b>narrow cross-role tool</b>: one small capability from another role, for the frequent simple need. <b>Rule: frequent simple lookup → a narrow tool for it; complex work stays with the coordinator.</b>", "options": ["Handing the assistant the stockroom keys works, but now they're doing the stockroom's job, and may get it wrong.", "Saving all questions for the end means fewer trips, but the customer waits with gaps on the receipt.", "Correct. A price scanner for the simple questions; the manager for the hard ones.", "Guessing which prices will be needed works sometimes. You can't know in advance what customers will ask."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>least privilege means no exceptions</b>, so every check must go through the coordinator. The guide allows a <b>limited cross-role tool</b> when one need is frequent and simple. The frequent case gets a direct path; the complex case keeps coordination.", "diagram": "one-field check  → lookup tool (direct)\nsame-trial question → coordinator → search subagent", "mapTitle": "A subagent needs something from another role. How do you give it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Frequent, simple lookups; rare complex ones", "Narrow cross-role tool; complex via coordinator", "Fast path for the common case"], ["Needed facts are known before the task starts", "Pass them in the Task prompt", "No tool needed"], ["Agent given another role's full toolset", "Avoid", "Off-speciality tools get misused"], ["Checks pile up until the end", "Avoid", "Blocks the work in progress"], ["Complex research mid-task", "Route through the coordinator", "It owns delegation"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each check goes back through the coordinator to a search subagent, and every round trip adds a long wait", "means": "The cost is the round trip. A batch at the end (<b>[[1]]</b>) leaves the draft blocked on open details."}, {"quote": "Most checks are one-field lookups", "means": "The decider. A frequent, simple need is exactly what a narrow tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) is for."}, {"quote": "A few ask whether two papers report the same trial, which takes real research across several sources", "means": "That work stays with the coordinator, so giving the full search toolset (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "the reporting subagent often stops to confirm small details", "means": "The details arise while writing, so prefetching them (<b>[[3]]</b>) means guessing."}], "rule": "Frequent simple need → narrow cross-role tool. Complex needs → through the coordinator.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Scoped access; limited cross-role tools for high-frequency needs."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-07", "was": "s3-07", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Error propagation to a coordinator", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent is given ten papers and reads six. For the other four, the journal's MCP server returns \"access denied: no licence for this journal\". Today the subagent retries each paper several times with backoff. Then it returns \"analysis failed\" as its only output, and the coordinator ends the run. The four abstracts are open to read. What should the subagent <b>do instead</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the backoff retries for the four papers, then pass their names up along with the six analyses.", "why": "Works, but retries a lasting error. Passing partial results up is right, but a licence denial won't clear on retry, so the wait is wasted and the cause isn't reported.", "trap": "retries a lasting error"}, {"t": "Stop retrying, and send up the six analyses with a structured error naming papers and options.", "why": "Best. A structured error (permission failure, which papers, the open abstracts as an option) plus partial results lets the coordinator choose how to go on."}, {"t": "Analyse the four open abstracts in place of the full papers, and finish the run without an error.", "why": "Works, but hides the gap. The run completes, but nobody learns that four papers were read only in outline, which matters when every claim must cite a section.", "trap": "hides the gap"}, {"t": "Ask a staff member to arrange access for each denied paper, and hold the run until it comes.", "why": "Works, but stalls the run. Access may be granted in time, but the subagent skips the coordinator, which owns recovery and may go on with partial results.", "trap": "stalls the run"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "access denied: no licence for this journal", "explanation": "Subagents should recover locally where recovery is possible, and pass up what they cannot resolve as structured error context with partial results. Generic failure strings, hidden substitutions and ending the run on one failure all deny the coordinator what it needs.", "eli5": {"question": "The helper can't open four papers because there's no licence. It keeps retrying, then says only \"failed\". The clue: \"access denied\" is a permission problem; trying again won't fix it.", "answer": "A courier finds a door locked with a sign saying \"members only\". Knocking again won't help. The courier should phone the office: \"six parcels delivered, four refused at a members-only door, the porter could take a copy\". That is a <b>structured error with partial results</b>. The office (the coordinator) then decides. <b>Rule: report what you can't fix, with its type, what you tried and what you have; let the coordinator decide.</b>", "options": ["Knocking again helps when the door is just stuck. A members-only door stays shut.", "Correct. Report what's done, what failed and why, and what else could work.", "Leaving a leaflet instead of the parcel without telling anyone hides the problem.", "Waiting at the door for a pass skips the office, which might happily go on without those four."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>retry first, then report</b> is always right. It is right for <b>transient</b> faults like timeouts. A licence denial is a <b>permission</b> error: it won't clear on retry. The subagent should stop, keep its six finished analyses, and tell the coordinator exactly what failed and what else could work.", "diagram": "{ analyses: [6 done],\n  error: { category: \"permission\", retryable: false,\n           papers: [4], alternatives: [\"open abstracts\"] } }", "mapTitle": "A subagent hits an error. What does it do?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of error", "Right response", "Why"], "rows": [["Transient (timeout, brief outage)", "Retry locally, then report with partial results", "Often clears by itself"], ["Permission or licence denied", "No retry; structured error plus partial results", "Won't clear; coordinator decides"], ["Query ran and found nothing", "Valid empty result, not an error", "Absence is an answer"], ["Silent substitution of other material", "Anti-pattern", "Hides the gap"], ["Generic \"failed\" or ending the run", "Anti-pattern", "Throws away context and work"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "access denied: no licence for this journal", "means": "The decider. A permission error, so retries (<b>[[0]]</b>) waste time."}, {"quote": "Then it returns \"analysis failed\" as its only output, and the coordinator ends the run", "means": "A generic status throws away six good analyses and the reason. <b>[[1]]</b> keeps both."}, {"quote": "The four abstracts are open to read", "means": "An alternative worth naming to the coordinator, not a quiet swap (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What should the subagent do instead?", "means": "Recovery choices belong to the coordinator, not to a pause for staff (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Unfixable error → stop retrying; return partial results plus a structured error to the coordinator.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "local recovery in subagents, propagate only unresolved errors with partial results"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-09", "was": "s3-09", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resources", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "At the start of each run, the literature-search subagent spends many MCP calls exploring the journal server. It lists collections and opens sample records to learn what each holds and how its fields are named. The server's owners add or rename collections most weeks. These exploring results also fill the subagent's context before the real search starts. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Publish the collection list and field definitions as MCP resources on the server.", "why": "Best. MCP resources expose catalogues and schemas the agent can read directly, so it skips the exploring calls, and the server keeps them current."}, {"t": "Write the collection list and field names into the search subagent's system prompt.", "why": "Works, but goes stale. A written list would end the exploring, but collections change most weeks, so someone must keep editing the prompt.", "trap": "goes stale"}, {"t": "Start each run with an exploring subagent that maps the server and returns a summary.", "why": "Works, but still explores. The search subagent's context stays clean, but the same exploring calls still happen on every run.", "trap": "still explores"}, {"t": "Add a <code>find_collections</code> tool that takes keywords and returns matching collections.", "why": "Works, but still explores. A search tool narrows the hunt, but the agent must still guess what to ask for, one call at a time.", "trap": "still explores"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The server's owners add or rename collections most weeks.", "explanation": "MCP tools perform actions; MCP resources expose content the agent can read. Catalogues, hierarchies and schemas belong in resources, which cuts exploratory tool calls and stays current as the server changes.", "eli5": {"question": "Each run starts by poking around the journal server to learn what's there. The clue: the collections change most weeks, so any list you write by hand goes out of date.", "answer": "A new visitor to a library walks every aisle to learn the layout. Better to hang a map at the door that the library itself keeps up to date. On an MCP server, that map is a <b>resource</b>: content the agent can read, like a catalogue or a schema, kept current by the server. <b>Rule: \"what's in here?\" → publish it as an MCP resource; tools are for actions.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A map at the door, kept current by the library.", "A printed map in your pocket is great until the library moves the shelves.", "Sending a scout to walk the aisles saves your legs, but someone still walks every aisle.", "Asking the desk one question at a time still means guessing what to ask."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat this as a <b>context problem</b> and hand the exploring to someone else. The real waste is that the agent has to <b>discover</b> what the server holds. MCP <b>resources</b> let the server publish that catalogue as readable content, so no exploring calls are needed.", "mapTitle": "The agent needs to know what a server holds. How?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Catalogue or schema that changes often", "MCP resource on the server", "Readable, kept current"], ["Catalogue that rarely changes", "Describe it in the system prompt", "Cheap while it stays true"], ["Exploring output crowds the context", "Explore subagent returns a summary", "Isolates verbose output"], ["Agent needs to do something", "MCP tool", "Tools are actions"], ["Agent prefers built-ins over a better MCP tool", "Improve the tool's description", "Descriptions drive selection"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "spends many MCP calls exploring the journal server", "means": "The cost is discovery itself. A delegated explorer (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a search tool (<b>[[3]]</b>) still pays it."}, {"quote": "to learn what each holds and how its fields are named", "means": "That is catalogue and schema content, which is what resources expose: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The server's owners add or rename collections most weeks", "means": "The decider. A hand-written list in the prompt (<b>[[1]]</b>) would go stale."}, {"quote": "These exploring results also fill the subagent's context", "means": "Removing the calls also removes the clutter."}], "rule": "\"What's on this server?\" → an MCP resource, not exploratory tool calls.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-01", "was": "m4-s4-01", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Built-in tool selection", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer wants to turn on a strict TypeScript check but skip the generated GraphQL files. The code generator names every output file <code>*.generated.ts</code> and writes a 'do not edit' comment at the top. An old formatter run rewrote the top lines of many files. Some generated files are leftovers that nothing imports any more. How should Claude <b>build the list</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use Glob with the pattern <code>**/*.generated.ts</code> and take every matching path.", "why": "Best. Glob matches file paths, and every generated file follows the naming rule, leftovers included."}, {"t": "Use Grep for the 'do not edit' header comment and take every file that has it.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The formatter rewrote the top lines of many files, so some generated files no longer carry the header.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Read the code generator's config file and list every output file it declares.", "why": "Works, but misses old files. The config describes today's outputs; leftovers from removed queries are not in it.", "trap": "misses leftovers"}, {"t": "Use Grep on import lines that mention <code>.generated</code> and collect those paths.", "why": "Works, but looks from the wrong side. Imports show the files somebody uses, so leftovers that nothing imports are missed.", "trap": "misses leftovers"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The code generator names every output file *.generated.ts", "explanation": "When a reliable file-name pattern identifies the files, Glob is the tool. Grep searches contents, which only works when the content marker is reliable.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude must find every generated file. The clue is: <b>every output file is named <code>*.generated.ts</code></b>, while the header comment has been damaged in many files.", "answer": "Think of a library where every cookbook has a red spine, but some title pages were torn out. To find all the cookbooks, you look at the spines, not inside the books. Looking at names is <b>Glob</b>; looking inside is <b>Grep</b>. The name rule holds for every generated file, even unused leftovers. <b>Rule: if the name tells you, use Glob; if only the contents tell you, use Grep.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Every generated file has the same kind of name, so a name pattern finds them all.", "Checking the title page works for most books. Some title pages were torn out by the formatter.", "The catalogue lists only today's books. Old leftovers were never recorded there.", "This lists books that someone borrowed. Books nobody borrows are left off."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Grep is the search tool</b> for everything. Grep searches <i>inside</i> files. Here the reliable signal is the <b>file name</b>, and Glob matches file paths. The header inside the file was damaged, so content search now misses some files.", "diagram": "Glob **/*.generated.ts  → every generated file\nGrep 'do not edit'     → only files whose header survived\nGrep imports           → only files someone still imports", "mapTitle": "What reliably marks the files you want?", "map": {"head": ["Reliable marker", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["A file-name pattern", "Glob", "Matches paths, ignores contents"], ["Text inside the file", "Grep", "Searches contents across many files"], ["One known file to change", "Edit (or Read + Write)", "Targeted change"], ["Understanding a flow", "Grep entry points, then Read", "Follow the calls step by step"], ["A list kept in config", "Read the config", "Only as complete as the config is"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The code generator names every output file *.generated.ts", "means": "The decider. A name pattern covers every generated file, so Glob (<b>[[0]]</b>) finds them all."}, {"quote": "An old formatter run rewrote the top lines of many files", "means": "The header is no longer reliable, so a Grep for it (<b>[[1]]</b>) misses some files."}, {"quote": "Some generated files are leftovers that nothing imports any more", "means": "Leftovers are missing from imports (<b>[[3]]</b>) and from today's generator config (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "How should Claude build the list?", "means": "Match the reliable name pattern: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Reliable name pattern → Glob. Reliable text inside → Grep.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "Grep = content search; Glob = file path patterns"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-02", "was": "m4-s4-02", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.5 Edit failure fallback", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A long deployment config has four environment blocks with the same settings. Each block's name sits on its first line, far above the settings. Every block contains <code>timeout: 30</code>, and only the staging block must change it to 60. Claude's <code>Edit</code> call fails because its old text matches four places. What should Claude <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry Edit with old text long enough to reach up to the staging name line.", "why": "Works, but clumsy. The old text would have to copy the whole block from its name line down to the value, which is long and easy to get wrong.", "trap": "clumsy"}, {"t": "Run <code>sed</code> through Bash on the line number of the staging timeout.", "why": "Works, but leaves the file tools. A shell edit by line number is fragile and skips the Read and Write route made for this case.", "trap": "wrong tool"}, {"t": "Read the whole file, change the staging value, then Write the file back.", "why": "Best. Edit needs a unique text match. When there is none, the fallback is Read plus Write, where Claude can use the block's position."}, {"t": "Ask the engineer to make this one change by hand in their own editor.", "why": "Works, but hands the job back. Claude can make this change itself with Read and Write.", "trap": "hands it back"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude's Edit call fails because its old text matches four places.", "explanation": "Edit makes a targeted change by unique text match. When the text is not unique, fall back to Read plus Write.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude tried to change one value with Edit, and Edit refused because the same text appears four times. The clue is: <b>the old text matches four places</b>.", "answer": "Edit is like find-and-replace that only works when the word appears once. Here the same line appears in four identical blocks. So Claude opens the whole file (<b>Read</b>), changes the line in the staging block, and saves the whole file (<b>Write</b>). <b>Rule: Edit fails on a non-unique match → Read the file and Write it back.</b>", "options": ["This can work, but Claude would have to copy a long, identical chunk exactly. Easy to get wrong.", "A shell edit by line number works, but it is fragile and skips the file tools.", "Correct. Read the whole file, change the right block, Write it back.", "The engineer could do it, but Claude can do it safely itself."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a longer match always fixes a failed Edit</b>. That works when the text near the change is different. Here the blocks are identical all the way up to their name lines. The documented fallback is <b>Read the whole file, then Write it back</b>.", "diagram": "production: ... timeout: 30\nqa:         ... timeout: 30\nstaging:    ... timeout: 30   ← change this one\ndev:        ... timeout: 30", "mapTitle": "Which file tool fits this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Why"], "rows": [["Change text that appears once", "Edit", "Targeted change by unique match"], ["Change text that appears in several identical places", "Read + Write", "Edit cannot pick one; position can"], ["Change every copy of the text", "Edit with replace_all", "All matches are meant"], ["Create a new file", "Write", "Nothing to match"], ["Find files by name", "Glob", "Path patterns, not contents"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A long deployment config has four environment blocks with the same settings", "means": "Identical blocks mean any nearby text repeats too."}, {"quote": "Each block's name sits on its first line, far above the settings", "means": "A unique match would need a long copy of the block (<b>[[0]]</b>). Possible, but clumsy."}, {"quote": "Claude's Edit call fails because its old text matches four places", "means": "The decider. Edit needs a unique match, so the fallback is Read plus Write: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "What should Claude do next?", "means": "Claude can finish the job itself with its file tools. No need for sed (<b>[[1]]</b>) or the engineer (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Edit fails because the text is not unique → Read the whole file and Write it back.", "guide": {"obj": "2.5 Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob)", "quote": "When Edit fails on non-unique match → Read + Write fallback"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-03", "was": "m4-s4-03", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP configuration scoping", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team wants the Linear MCP server ready for anyone working in this repo. That includes contractors, who must be productive in their first session. Each person signs in with their own Linear API key. Priya is also trying an early error-tracking MCP server across three repos. It sometimes crashes on start-up, and nobody else uses it. Which setup <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Each engineer adds Linear to their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, and Priya adds her server there too.", "why": "Works, but not shared. Each contractor would have to set up Linear by hand before they could use it.", "trap": "not shared"}, {"t": "Commit <code>.mcp.json</code> with both servers, each reading its key from an environment variable.", "why": "Works, but pushes Priya's trial onto everyone. Every teammate would load a server they don't use, crashes included.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Commit <code>.mcp.json</code> with the Linear server and one shared team key written into it.", "why": "Works, but puts a secret in git. It also replaces each person's own key with one shared key.", "trap": "secret in git"}, {"t": "Commit <code>.mcp.json</code> with Linear reading <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code>; Priya's stays in her <code>~/.claude.json</code>.", "why": "Best. The project file shares Linear with everyone on clone, the variable keeps each key out of git, and the personal trial stays personal."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "That includes contractors, who must be productive in their first session.", "explanation": "Shared servers go in a committed project .mcp.json with environment-variable expansion for secrets. Personal or experimental servers go in the user's ~/.claude.json.", "eli5": {"question": "Two servers, two audiences. Linear is for everyone, including new contractors. Priya's server is her own experiment. The clue is: <b>contractors must be productive in their first session</b>.", "answer": "Think of an office. Shared tools go in the common cupboard that comes with the building (<b>project <code>.mcp.json</code></b>, committed to git). Each person's key stays in their own pocket (<b>an environment variable</b>). A personal gadget stays in your own drawer (<b><code>~/.claude.json</code></b>). <b>Rule: shared server → .mcp.json with ${VAR}; personal or experimental → ~/.claude.json.</b>", "options": ["Every person stocks their own drawer. New contractors start with an empty drawer.", "Priya's half-broken gadget ends up in the common cupboard for everyone.", "The key is taped to the cupboard door, where anyone with the repo can see it.", "Correct. Shared tool in the cupboard, keys in pockets, the trial in Priya's own drawer."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>secrets mean the server can't be committed</b>. The server definition can be shared while the secret stays out: <code>.mcp.json</code> expands <code>${VAR}</code> from each person's environment. Experimental servers belong at user level, so the team doesn't inherit them.", "diagram": "repo/.mcp.json        linear → ${LINEAR_API_KEY}  (shared)\n~/.claude.json (Priya) error-tracker trial    (personal)", "mapTitle": "Who needs this server, and where does it go?", "map": {"head": ["Who needs it", "Where it goes", "Why"], "rows": [["Everyone in the repo", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code>, committed", "Arrives with a clone"], ["Everyone, but each with their own secret", "<code>.mcp.json</code> with <code>${VAR}</code>", "No secret in git"], ["Only you, in many repos", "<code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["An unstable trial", "<code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Others don't inherit its problems"], ["Instructions about how to use a tool", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Context, not server setup"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team wants the Linear MCP server ready for anyone working in this repo", "means": "A shared server, so it belongs in the project file."}, {"quote": "That includes contractors, who must be productive in their first session", "means": "The decider. Per-person setup (<b>[[0]]</b>) leaves contractors without Linear on day one."}, {"quote": "Each person signs in with their own Linear API key", "means": "Keys come from each person's environment, not from git (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "It sometimes crashes on start-up, and nobody else uses it", "means": "The trial is personal. Sharing it (<b>[[1]]</b>) hands its crashes to everyone. So: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Shared server → committed .mcp.json with ${VAR} for secrets. Personal or experimental → ~/.claude.json.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Scoping: project-level .mcp.json (shared) vs user-level ~/.claude.json (personal/experimental)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-04", "was": "m4-s4-04", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP tools vs built-ins", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The team also runs an MCP server over a code index that resolves Ruby definitions, even in vendored gems and metaprogrammed code. Its one tool, <code>lookup</code>, has the description 'Search code.' When asked where a method is defined, Claude mostly uses Grep instead. It often lands on a same-named method in a gem. What is the <b>most effective fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a CLAUDE.md section telling Claude to use lookup for every definition question.", "why": "Works, but works around the cause. A standing instruction nudges the choice, while the two-word description still gives Claude no reason to prefer the tool.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Rewrite lookup's description: what it finds that Grep misses, inputs, examples.", "why": "Best. The description is what Claude reads when choosing a tool. Saying what lookup resolves that text search cannot lets Claude pick it on its merits."}, {"t": "Rename lookup to <code>resolve_symbol</code> and split it into definition and reference tools.", "why": "Works, but more than needed. Renaming and splitting is good design for overlapping tools; here one tool simply has an empty description.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Deny Grep in the project's permission settings, leaving lookup as the code search.", "why": "Works, but too blunt. Claude would use lookup, but it would also lose Grep for the plain text searches it is right for.", "trap": "too blunt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its one tool, lookup, has the description 'Search code.'", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the main signal Claude uses to choose tools. Enhance an MCP tool's description so Claude doesn't prefer a built-in like Grep over a more capable tool.", "eli5": {"question": "A better tool exists, but Claude keeps reaching for Grep. The clue is: <b>the tool's description is just 'Search code.'</b>", "answer": "Picture two shops on a street. One sign says 'Shop'. The other says 'Hardware: keys cut, paint mixed'. You walk into the one whose sign tells you it has what you need. The sign is the <b>tool description</b>. Write what lookup can do that Grep can't, with inputs and examples. <b>Rule: wrong tool picked → fix the description first.</b>", "options": ["A note telling you to use the first shop helps a bit. Its sign still says nothing.", "Correct. A clear sign says what the shop does better than the one next door.", "Rebuilding the shop into two shops is a bigger job than painting a proper sign.", "Closing the other shop forces the choice, but you lose it for things it does well."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude needs to be told</b> which tool to use. Claude picks tools mainly from their <b>descriptions</b>. 'Search code.' sounds like Grep, so Claude takes the tool it knows. Fix the description and Claude can choose on merit.", "mapTitle": "Why is Claude picking the wrong tool?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "First fix"], "rows": [["Built-in used over a better MCP tool", "MCP description is thin", "Rewrite the description: what it does better, inputs, examples"], ["Two tools confused with each other", "Overlapping, generic tools", "Rename or split into purpose-specific tools"], ["Tool picked for a keyword in the prompt", "Wording links words to a tool", "Reword the instructions"], ["Too many tools, choices slip", "Too wide a tool set", "Give each agent fewer, scoped tools"], ["A team habit to follow", "Missing project context", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "resolves Ruby definitions, even in vendored gems and metaprogrammed code", "means": "lookup is the more capable tool. Nothing about it is broken."}, {"quote": "Its one tool, lookup, has the description 'Search code.'", "means": "The decider. The description says nothing about what lookup does better, so it is the thing to fix: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "When asked where a method is defined, Claude mostly uses Grep instead", "means": "Only one tool is involved, so splitting it (<b>[[2]]</b>) is more than this needs."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective fix?", "means": "Fix the cause. A CLAUDE.md note (<b>[[0]]</b>) or denying Grep (<b>[[3]]</b>) works around it."}], "rule": "Claude prefers a built-in over a better MCP tool → improve the MCP tool's description.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Enhance MCP tool descriptions so the agent doesn't prefer built-ins (Grep) over more capable MCP tools"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-10", "was": "m4-s4-10", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Sourcing MCP servers", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The team wants Claude to read Sentry issues, comment on them and change their status. An engineer proposes writing their own integration, since only the team knows its process. The process adds two custom tags and a 'needs repro' status step. Sentry's standard API reaches both. Several maintained open-source Sentry MCP servers already cover issues, comments, tags and status. What is the <b>best recommendation</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Adopt a community Sentry MCP server and note the tags and repro step in CLAUDE.md.", "why": "Best. Prefer community servers for standard integrations. The team's process is context, and CLAUDE.md is where shared context lives."}, {"t": "Build a small in-house Sentry MCP server whose tools encode the tags and repro step.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A custom server fits a system with no existing integration. Here the team's differences are reachable through servers that already exist.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Have Claude call Sentry's API with curl through Bash, endpoints listed in CLAUDE.md.", "why": "Works, but fragile. Raw API calls give up typed tools, clear descriptions and structured errors, and put tokens into shell commands.", "trap": "fragile"}, {"t": "Fork a community Sentry server and add tools for the team's tags and repro step.", "why": "Works, but adds upkeep. The team now maintains a fork, for differences the existing tools already reach.", "trap": "extra upkeep"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Several maintained open-source Sentry MCP servers already cover issues, comments, tags and status.", "explanation": "Prefer community MCP servers for standard integrations. Team-specific conventions are context and belong in CLAUDE.md.", "eli5": {"question": "Build or adopt? The clue is: <b>maintained open-source servers already cover issues, comments, tags and status</b>. The team's twist is only a matter of how it uses them.", "answer": "You don't build your own car because your family has its own routes. You buy a good car and write the routes down. A ready-made <b>community MCP server</b> is the car; the team's tags and repro step are the routes, written in <b><code>CLAUDE.md</code></b>. <b>Rule: standard system → community MCP server; team habits → CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Use the ready-made car and write the family's routes down.", "Building your own car works, but there are good ones on sale already.", "Driving with a toolkit instead of a car works, but every trip is fiddly.", "Modifying a bought car means maintaining your changes for good."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a custom process needs a custom integration</b>. The process differs only in how the team uses standard features, and the standard API reaches them. That is <b>context</b>, not new tooling. Adopt the community server and write the conventions in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.", "mapTitle": "Build, adopt, or describe?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Standard system, maintained servers exist", "Adopt a community MCP server", "No code to maintain"], ["Team conventions on a standard system", "Describe them in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Conventions are context"], ["In-house system with no integration", "Build a custom MCP server", "Nothing exists to adopt"], ["Read-only catalogue to browse", "MCP resources", "Content, not actions"], ["Raw API calls from the shell", "Avoid for regular use", "No typed tools or structured errors"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer proposes writing their own integration, since only the team knows its process", "means": "The tempting idea behind <b>[[1]]</b> and <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Sentry's standard API reaches both", "means": "The custom parts need no new tools, only context."}, {"quote": "Several maintained open-source Sentry MCP servers already cover issues, comments, tags and status", "means": "The decider. Adopt one instead of building, forking or calling the API by hand (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What is the best recommendation?", "means": "Community server plus CLAUDE.md: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Standard integration → community MCP server. Team-specific conventions → CLAUDE.md.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "prefer community MCP servers for standard integrations (Jira)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-08", "was": "s6-08", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Empty results vs access failures", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An MCP tool, <code>get_endorsements</code>, returns the endorsements filed with a certificate. It returns an empty list both when there are none and when the document store is unreachable. The store goes offline for about half an hour each night, during the extraction run. Some certificates then post with no endorsements although one existed. Contract holders were told the client lacked required cover. What is the <b>right fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry the store inside the tool with exponential backoff for up to a minute, then return the list.", "why": "Works, but too short. Local retry is good practice for brief blips, but these outages last half an hour, so the tool still returns an empty list that looks like “none”.", "trap": "too short"}, {"t": "Hold every certificate whose endorsement list comes back empty in a queue until a reviewer confirms it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Most empty lists are genuine, so this queues the common case to catch a rare failure, and the tool still hides which is which.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Compare endorsement counts with the carrier's portal after posting, and raise an alert when they differ.", "why": "Works, but too late. It finds the problem after the record is posted and the contract holder has already been told.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Return <code>isError</code> with a transient category and <code>isRetryable: true</code> when unreachable; keep [] for none.", "why": "Best. An outage and a genuine “no endorsements” are opposite facts sharing one return value. Separating them lets the agent hold or retry the outage and move on for the genuine case."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It returns an empty list both when there are none and when the document store is unreachable.", "explanation": "Distinguish access failures from valid empty results. Return structured errors (isError, category, retryability) so the agent can choose the right recovery; a shared empty value makes that impossible.", "eli5": {"question": "The tool says “no endorsements” both when there really are none and when it couldn't reach the store. The clue: one answer means two <i>opposite</i> things.", "answer": "If a receptionist says “no messages” both when there are none and when the phone line was down, you'll miss calls. They should say “line down, try again later” when it was down. For an MCP tool that's an <b>error result</b> (<code>isError</code>, a transient category, retryable) for the outage, and an empty list only for a true “none”. <b>Rule: a failure to look is not the same as finding nothing.</b>", "options": ["Redialling for a minute helps with a blip, not a line that's down for half an hour.", "Double-checking every “no messages” works, but most of them were true.", "Finding out next morning is after the caller was already let down.", "Correct. Say “line down” when it's down, and “no messages” only when there are none."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the fix is to retry harder or check more</b>. The real fault is that the tool reports two opposite facts the same way. Once an outage comes back as a <b>structured error</b>, and an empty list means only “none”, the agent can make the right choice.", "diagram": "store down  → isError, transient, isRetryable: true\nstore up, 0 → [] (valid empty result)", "mapTitle": "A tool came back empty. What should it have said?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool should return", "Agent can then"], "rows": [["Store reachable, nothing filed", "Empty list, success", "Proceed"], ["Store unreachable for a long time", "<code>isError</code>, transient, retryable", "Hold or retry later"], ["Brief blip of a few seconds", "Retry inside the tool first", "Never see the blip"], ["Bad input to the tool", "Validation error, not retryable", "Fix the input"], ["Policy forbids the action", "Business error with an explanation", "Explain or escalate"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It returns an empty list both when there are none and when the document store is unreachable", "means": "The decider: two opposite facts share one value. Holding all empties (<b>[[1]]</b>) works around it."}, {"quote": "The store goes offline for about half an hour each night, during the extraction run", "means": "Outages are long, so a one-minute retry (<b>[[0]]</b>) still ends in a false “none”."}, {"quote": "Contract holders were told the client lacked required cover", "means": "The harm happens at posting. A check after posting (<b>[[2]]</b>) is too late."}, {"quote": "What is the right fix?", "means": "Report the outage as an error: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Distinguish access failures from valid empty results; return a structured, retryable error for an outage.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "distinguish access failures from valid empty results"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-1", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Project .mcp.json with env var expansion", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "One engineer connected Claude to the Linear MCP server through their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, with their API key written in it. Teammates now want the same Linear tools, and so does a new CI job. Each person and the CI job have their own key. No key may be committed to the repository. How should the team <b>set it up</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have each engineer add the server to their own <code>~/.claude.json</code>, as a README describes.", "why": "Works, but personal. Each engineer configures it by hand, the entries drift, and the CI job needs its own setup too.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Add the server to a committed <code>.mcp.json</code> that reads the key from <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code>.", "why": "Best. Project <code>.mcp.json</code> is shared through git, so every clone and CI get the server, and each sets the variable from its own environment or secret store."}, {"t": "Add a script to the repo that calls the Linear API, and let Claude run it through Bash.", "why": "Works, but loses the MCP tools. Claude gets one script instead of described tools with structured errors, and the team maintains it.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Copy the engineer's server entry into the CI runner's <code>~/.claude.json</code> as well.", "why": "Works, but for CI only. Teammates still have nothing, and the server now has two hand-made entries to keep in step.", "trap": "covers only CI"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "No key may be committed to the repository.", "explanation": "Shared MCP servers belong in the project-level .mcp.json. Env var expansion such as ${LINEAR_API_KEY} keeps secrets out of the committed file.", "eli5": {"question": "One engineer's laptop knows how to reach Linear. The team and CI want it too, but nobody's key may go into the repo.", "answer": "Put the address book in the shared office, but leave a blank where the door code goes. Each person fills in their own code from their own pocket. The committed <code>.mcp.json</code> is the shared address book; <code>${LINEAR_API_KEY}</code> is the blank. <b>Rule: a server the team shares → project <code>.mcp.json</code>, with secrets as environment variables.</b>", "options": ["Everyone writing their own address book works, but they drift, and CI needs one too.", "Correct. One shared address book, with each person's code filled in from their own pocket.", "A hand-written phone script works, but it is clumsier than the real phone and someone has to maintain it.", "Giving a copy to CI helps CI only; the teammates still have nothing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a secret means the config must stay personal</b>. Claude Code separates the two: the server entry goes in the committed project <code>.mcp.json</code>, and the secret stays outside git as an environment variable that the file expands.", "diagram": ".mcp.json (in git):  \"LINEAR_API_KEY\": \"${LINEAR_API_KEY}\"\nlaptop env / CI secret → fills in the real key", "mapTitle": "Who needs the server, and where is it configured?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team and CI, needs a secret", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code> with <code>${VAR}</code>", "Shared config, no committed secret"], ["Whole team, no secret needed", "Project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Arrives with the clone"], ["Only me, or an experiment", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["A standard service like Jira or Linear", "An existing community server", "No custom code to maintain"], ["Only one CI job needs it", "Still <code>.mcp.json</code>; CI sets the variable", "One config for all"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One engineer connected Claude to the Linear MCP server through their own ~/.claude.json", "means": "User-level config: personal, so nobody else has it."}, {"quote": "Teammates now want the same Linear tools, and so does a new CI job", "means": "Shared need. Per-person entries (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a CI-only copy (<b>[[3]]</b>) each cover part of it."}, {"quote": "No key may be committed to the repository", "means": "The decider: the committed file names a variable, not the key: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "How should the team set it up?", "means": "Keep the MCP server and its tools; a Bash script (<b>[[2]]</b>) replaces them with something weaker."}], "rule": "Team-shared MCP server → project .mcp.json, secrets as ${VARS}. Personal or experimental → ~/.claude.json.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "Env var expansion in .mcp.json (e.g., ${GITHUB_TOKEN}) — no committed secrets."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-2", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 Community MCP servers for standard integrations", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The review job should read the Jira ticket linked in each pull request and check the change against it. It also needs to search related tickets and add a comment. These are ordinary Jira tasks. A developer plans to write the team's own MCP server for them. What is the <b>best approach</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Write the planned server, with detailed tool descriptions and structured errors.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A well-made custom server does the job, but the team now builds and maintains a Jira client that already exists.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give the job a script that calls the Jira REST API with <code>curl</code> through Bash.", "why": "Works, but fragile. Claude gets raw HTTP output instead of described tools, and the script grows with every new need.", "trap": "fragile"}, {"t": "Configure an existing community Jira MCP server in the project <code>.mcp.json</code>.", "why": "Best. Jira is a standard integration with maintained community servers. Configured in the project file, the review job and every developer get it."}, {"t": "Add the Jira calls as new tools on the team's existing code-search MCP server.", "why": "Works, but still custom code. There is no new server, yet the team writes and maintains Jira code that already exists, inside a server built for something else.", "trap": "still custom code"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "These are ordinary Jira tasks.", "explanation": "For standard integrations such as Jira, prefer an existing community MCP server over writing a custom one.", "eli5": {"question": "The team needs Claude to read and comment on Jira tickets, and someone wants to build a connector from scratch.", "answer": "If you need a kettle, you buy a good one from the shop; you don't build one from parts. Jira is the kettle: lots of teams need the same thing, so a well-tested <b>community MCP server</b> already exists. Build your own only for systems nobody else has. <b>Rule: standard integration → community server; your own system → your own server.</b>", "options": ["A hand-built kettle works, but you'll be fixing it for years.", "Boiling water over a campfire works, but it's clumsy every time.", "Correct. Buy the good kettle everyone uses, and put it in the shared kitchen.", "Bolting a hand-built kettle onto the toaster: still yours to fix."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a custom server is the careful choice</b> because you control it. For standard integrations the exam guide says the opposite: prefer a community MCP server. Custom code is for your own systems, where nothing exists to adopt.", "mapTitle": "Adopt, build, or work around?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best choice", "Why"], "rows": [["Standard service (Jira, GitHub)", "Community MCP server", "Already built and maintained"], ["Your own internal system", "Your own MCP server", "Nothing exists to adopt"], ["The team and CI need it", "Configure it in project <code>.mcp.json</code>", "Shared on clone"], ["Only you, trying it out", "User-level <code>~/.claude.json</code>", "Personal"], ["Claude explores to learn what exists", "Add an MCP resource", "A catalog cuts exploratory calls"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review job uses an MCP server for code search and API guidelines", "means": "The team already works through MCP; adding another server fits."}, {"quote": "It also needs to search related tickets and add a comment", "means": "It must act, not only read, so it needs real Jira tools."}, {"quote": "These are ordinary Jira tasks", "means": "The decider: a standard integration, so a community server exists."}, {"quote": "A developer plans to write the team's own MCP server for them", "means": "Building it (<b>[[0]]</b>), bolting it onto another server (<b>[[3]]</b>) or scripting around it (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more to maintain. Adopt: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Standard integration (Jira)? Configure a community MCP server; build your own only for your own systems.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "prefer community MCP servers for standard integrations (Jira)"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-mcp-where-what-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.4 MCP resources for schema catalogs", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Engineers ask Claude questions about data in the staging database. Before each answer, Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist. Most of those queries find nothing useful. The database has several hundred tables, and its structure changes only when a migration runs. What is the <b>best way</b> to cut these exploratory calls?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>describe_table</code> tool that returns the columns of one named table.", "why": "Works, but still exploring. Claude must already know a table's name, so it still calls once per guess.", "trap": "still exploratory"}, {"t": "Paste the full schema into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every session to load.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Hundreds of tables load into every session, even ones about other work, and the copy drifts after each migration.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Expand the query tool's description with sample queries on the system tables.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. The queries get better aimed, but Claude still runs them to explore.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Expose the schema as an MCP resource: tables, columns and a summary of each.", "why": "Best. A resource is a catalog Claude reads when it needs it. One read shows what exists, so the exploratory queries go away."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist.", "explanation": "MCP resources expose content catalogs, such as schemas, so the agent can see what exists without exploratory tool calls.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude keeps knocking on doors to find out which rooms the building has.", "answer": "Give it the floor plan instead. A floor plan on the wall shows every room at a glance, and you only walk to the room you need. An <b>MCP resource</b> is that floor plan: a catalog Claude can read. Tools are the doors you open to <i>do</i> something. <b>Rule: Claude explores to learn what exists → publish a resource.</b>", "options": ["A door you can open only if you know the room's name: still guessing.", "Printing the whole floor plan on everyone's desk every day, even when they work elsewhere.", "Better knocking technique, but still knocking.", "Correct. One floor plan, read once, and no more knocking."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every MCP feature is a tool</b>, so the fix must be a better or new tool. MCP has a second primitive: a <b>resource</b>, content the client can read, such as a schema or a document list. It answers “what exists?” in one read, which is exactly what the exploratory queries were doing.", "diagram": "Today:  query, query, query … (most find nothing)\nAfter:  read schema resource → one targeted query", "mapTitle": "Tool, resource, or memory?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "MCP primitive", "Why"], "rows": [["Learn what exists (schemas, doc lists)", "Resource", "A catalog read once"], ["Do something with inputs (run a query)", "Tool", "An action with parameters"], ["Claude picks Grep over a better tool", "Better tool description", "Descriptions drive selection"], ["A fact every session needs, short and stable", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded"], ["Standard service such as Jira", "Community server", "Adopt, don't build"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude runs many queries on the system tables to learn which tables and columns exist", "means": "The decider: the calls are exploration. Better-aimed queries (<b>[[2]]</b>) are still exploration."}, {"quote": "Most of those queries find nothing useful", "means": "Wasted calls, not wrong answers. The fix is to make the calls unnecessary."}, {"quote": "The database has several hundred tables", "means": "Too big for always-loaded memory (<b>[[1]]</b>), and too many to guess one by one (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to cut these exploratory calls?", "means": "A catalog Claude reads when needed: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Claude explores to learn what exists? Publish an MCP resource (a catalog); keep tools for actions.", "guide": {"obj": "2.4 Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows", "quote": "MCP resources expose content catalogs (issue summaries, doc hierarchies, schemas) to reduce exploratory tool calls."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-2", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.2 Access failure vs valid empty result", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The extraction agent calls a <code>verify_carrier</code> MCP tool to check each carrier against the licensing registry. The registry sometimes goes offline for an hour or more. During those outages the tool returns “Carrier not found”, the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence. Certificates from licensed carriers are then rejected as unlicensed. Which change is <b>best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Retry the registry with backoff inside the tool before it returns “Carrier not found”.", "why": "Works, but only for short blips. After an hour-long outage the retries run out and the tool still says “not found”, a wrong answer that looks like a real one.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Send each “Carrier not found” certificate to a reviewer before it is rejected.", "why": "Works, but it treats the symptom. Reviewers catch the wrong rejections, at the cost of checking every real miss too, while the tool keeps saying something false.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Answer from a cache of earlier registry lookups while the registry is offline.", "why": "Works, but only for carriers seen before, and the cached answer may be out of date. A new carrier still comes back “not found”.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return a transient, retryable <code>isError</code> result when the registry is offline.", "why": "Best. The agent can now tell “the registry couldn't be reached” from “the registry says no licence”, and hold the certificate until the lookup works."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence", "explanation": "A tool must distinguish an access failure from a valid empty result. Return an isError result with an error category and a retryable flag, and keep “not found” for lookups that actually worked.", "eli5": {"question": "When the registry is down, the tool says “not found”, the same words it uses for a real “no licence”. The clue: two very different events give the same answer.", "answer": "If you phone a shop and nobody picks up, that doesn't mean the shop doesn't sell bread. “No answer” and “we have none” are different news. A tool must say which one happened: an <b>isError</b> result marked <b>transient, retryable</b> for “couldn't reach it”. <b>Rule: never report an access failure as an empty result.</b>", "options": ["Ringing again helps if the line is busy for a minute, not for an hour.", "Asking a person to double-check every “none” works, but the phone is still lying.", "Remembering last week's answer helps only for shops you've rung before.", "Correct. Say “nobody answered”, not “they have none”."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>“not found” is a safe default when a lookup fails</b>. It isn't: it turns “we don't know” into a confident wrong answer. Downstream logic acts on it as fact. The tool has to report the failure as a failure.", "diagram": "offline -> \"Carrier not found\" -> rejected (wrong)\noffline -> isError, transient, retryable\n        -> hold, retry later", "mapTitle": "What the tool returns, and what the agent can do", "map": {"head": ["What happened", "What the tool returns", "What the agent can do"], "rows": [["Registry answered: no licence", "Success, “not found”", "Reject as unlicensed"], ["Registry offline", "<code>isError</code>, transient, retryable", "Hold and retry later"], ["Bad input (malformed carrier ID)", "<code>isError</code>, validation", "Fix the input and call again"], ["Registry offline, reported as “not found”", "A false empty result", "Rejects good certificates"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The registry sometimes goes offline for an hour or more", "means": "Too long for retries inside the tool (<b>[[0]]</b>) to wait out."}, {"quote": "the same text it returns for a carrier with no licence", "means": "The decider: an access failure and a valid empty result look identical. Only the tool can tell them apart."}, {"quote": "Certificates from licensed carriers are then rejected as unlicensed", "means": "Wrong answers acted on as fact. A reviewer (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a cache (<b>[[2]]</b>) works around the false answer instead of removing it."}, {"quote": "Which change is best?", "means": "Report the outage as an error with its category: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Couldn't reach it is not the same as found nothing. Return isError with a category and a retryable flag.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "distinguish access failures from valid empty results."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Descriptions that separate similar tools", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The search subagent has two MCP tools, <code>search_journals</code> and <code>search_trial_registry</code>. Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.” Committees often ask about trials that are still running. The subagent sends these questions to <code>search_journals</code>, which holds only published papers, and reports that nothing was found. What is the most effective <b>first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a routing step that sends questions about ongoing or recruiting trials to the registry tool.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Keyword code catches the phrasings it was written for and misses the rest, and it has to be kept up as questions change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Rewrite both descriptions: what each source holds, example queries, and when to use the other.", "why": "Best. Descriptions are what Claude reads to choose a tool. Saying what each one covers, with examples and a boundary, fixes the cause in two edits."}, {"t": "Merge the two tools into one <code>search_studies</code> tool that queries both sources at once.", "why": "Works, but more than a first step. Every question now pays for two searches, and the server and its callers have to change.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add five example questions to the subagent's prompt, each showing which search tool to call.", "why": "Works, but misses the cause. Examples help on questions like the ones shown, add tokens to every call, and leave two identical descriptions in place.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.”", "explanation": "Tool descriptions are the primary way Claude chooses between tools. When two tools share a vague description, the first step is to say what each one covers, with input formats, example queries and boundaries.", "eli5": {"question": "Two drawers carry the same label, “Studies”. One holds published papers; the other holds trials still running. The helper keeps opening the wrong one.", "answer": "Before you hire a sorter or knock the drawers together, <b>write better labels</b>: “Published papers: use for finished studies” and “Trials still running: use for ongoing or recruiting trials”. Claude picks tools by their descriptions the way you pick drawers by their labels. <b>Rule: similar tools, wrong picks → fix the descriptions first.</b>", "options": ["A sorter at the door helps, but only for the words it was told to look for.", "Correct. Clear labels on both drawers.", "One big drawer works, but you search everything every time and have to rebuild the cabinet.", "Showing a few examples helps, but the labels still say the same thing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs extra machinery to choose</b>: a router, a merged tool, or examples. Claude chooses a tool mainly from its description. Two tools with the same one-line description give it nothing to choose by, so the first fix is the descriptions themselves.", "diagram": "question → Claude reads descriptions → picks a tool\n\"Searches for studies on a drug\" x2 → coin toss\nsay what each holds + when to use the other → right pick", "mapTitle": "What fixes a wrong tool choice?", "map": {"head": ["Cause", "Fix", "Size"], "rows": [["Similar tools, vague descriptions", "Formats, examples, edge cases, boundaries in each description", "Small, first step"], ["Descriptions are clear, choices still inconsistent", "A few targeted examples in the prompt", "Small, later step"], ["Too many tools for one agent", "Give the agent only the tools its role needs", "Medium"], ["One generic tool doing several jobs", "Split it into purpose-specific tools", "Medium"], ["Picking by keywords in code", "Routing layer", "Large; usually overbuilt"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Both descriptions read “Searches for studies on a drug.”", "means": "The decider: Claude has nothing to tell the two tools apart. That is the cause, and examples in the prompt (<b>[[3]]</b>) work around it."}, {"quote": "which holds only published papers", "means": "A boundary the description should state: running trials belong in the registry."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective first step?", "means": "The cheapest change that fixes the cause: <b>[[1]]</b>. A router (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a merged tool (<b>[[2]]</b>) are bigger builds."}], "rule": "Claude picks the wrong one of two similar tools? Fix the descriptions first.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Include input formats, example queries, edge cases, boundaries."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.1 Keyword wording that pulls the wrong tool", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The system prompt says: “When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use <code>pause_subscription</code>.” Company policy is that cancellations go to a person through <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Customers who write “stop sending boxes, I want to cancel” now get a paused plan instead. They are billed again when the pause ends. The <code>pause_subscription</code> description says only “Pauses deliveries.” What is the best <b>fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep the line and add three example chats where cancelling customers are sent to a person.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The examples pull one way and the keyword line still pulls the other, so some cancellations keep getting paused.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks a pause when the message mentions cancelling.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Code now guesses intent from words, and it blocks customers who say “don't cancel, just pause”.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a <code>cancel_request</code> tool that hands cancellations to staff, described for that case.", "why": "Works, but a new tool for a job <code>escalate_to_human</code> already does. Now three tools overlap instead of two.", "trap": "new tool"}, {"t": "Drop the keyword line, and say in each tool's description which requests it is for.", "why": "Best. The line ties a word to a tool, which is what causes the wrong pick. Clear descriptions with boundaries let Claude choose by what the customer wants."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use pause_subscription.", "explanation": "Keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations. Remove the association and put each tool's purpose and boundaries in its description.", "eli5": {"question": "A sign says “Anyone who says <i>stop</i>, send to the pause desk.” So people who say “stop, I'm leaving” end up paused, not cancelled.", "answer": "Take the sign down, and label each desk by what it does: “Pause desk: a break from deliveries. Leaving for good? Go to the person at the front.” <b>Rule: a prompt line that ties a word to a tool causes misrouting → remove it and put the boundary in the tool descriptions.</b>", "options": ["Showing people the right desk helps, but the sign still points the wrong way.", "A guard who stops anyone saying “cancel” also stops people who said “don't cancel”.", "A new desk for leavers works, but the front desk already does that job.", "Correct. Remove the misleading sign and label the desks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a misrouting needs new code or a new tool</b>. Here the system prompt itself causes it: a keyword rule sends any mention of stopping to one tool. Remove the cause, and give each tool a description that says what it is for and what it is not for.", "diagram": "\"stop ... cancel\" → prompt rule: stop = pause → paused\nrule gone + clear descriptions → escalate_to_human", "mapTitle": "Where does the wrong pick come from?", "map": {"head": ["Source of the misroute", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["A prompt line ties a keyword to a tool", "Remove or reword it by intent", "The line causes the pull"], ["Tool descriptions are one line", "Add purpose, examples and boundaries", "Descriptions drive the choice"], ["Two tools do the same job", "Keep one, describe it clearly", "Overlap causes misrouting"], ["An action must never run in some case", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook", "Code for must-hold rules"], ["Clear descriptions, still inconsistent", "A few targeted examples", "A later step"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When a customer mentions stopping deliveries, use pause_subscription", "means": "The decider: a keyword rule in the prompt. Examples added beside it (<b>[[0]]</b>) still compete with it."}, {"quote": "cancellations go to a person through escalate_to_human", "means": "A tool for this already exists, so a new one (<b>[[2]]</b>) adds overlap."}, {"quote": "The pause_subscription description says only “Pauses deliveries.”", "means": "No boundary to choose by. A hook guessing intent from words (<b>[[1]]</b>) is code for a wording problem."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Remove the keyword line and describe each tool clearly: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "A prompt line ties a word to a tool? Remove it, and put the boundary in the tool descriptions.", "guide": {"obj": "2.1 Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries", "quote": "Ambiguous/overlapping descriptions cause misrouting; keyword-sensitive system-prompt wording creates unintended tool associations."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-tool-picker-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D2 · Tool Design & MCP", "obj": "2.3 Too many tools for one agent", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An Agent SDK script triages new bug reports in Linear. It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools. Its job needs about five of them. It often calls <code>update_issue</code> when it should call <code>move_issue</code>. Sometimes it queries staging when no data is involved. Every tool already has a detailed description. What is the best <b>fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite all the tool descriptions with sharper boundaries between issue and database tools.", "why": "Works, but misses the cause. The descriptions are already detailed; the number of tools is what lowers the choice quality.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Add a classifier that reads each bug report and picks which tools to offer for it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The script does one job, so its tool set can be fixed once instead of chosen per report.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Give the triage script only the tools its job needs and leave the rest out.", "why": "Best. Fewer tools make the choice more reliable, and a triage script has no reason to reach the database."}, {"t": "Merge <code>update_issue</code> and <code>move_issue</code> into one <code>change_issue</code> tool with a mode field.", "why": "Works, but fixes one pair. The choice moves into a parameter, and the database calls still happen.", "trap": "fixes one symptom"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools.", "explanation": "Giving an agent many tools degrades selection reliability, and tools outside its role get misused. Scope each agent to the tools its job needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A helper sorting mail has been handed every tool in the building, and keeps grabbing the wrong one.", "answer": "Better labels on 18 tools still leave 18 tools to pick from. Give the mail sorter the five things a mail sorter needs. <b>Rule: wrong picks among many tools, with good descriptions already → cut the agent's tool set to its job.</b>", "options": ["Clearer labels help, but the labels are already good and the pile is still too big.", "A helper who hands out tools for each letter works, but the job never changes.", "Correct. Give the sorter only the tools for sorting.", "Gluing two tools together fixes one mix-up, not the pile."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every wrong pick is a description problem</b>. Descriptions come first when they are vague. Here they are already detailed, and the agent holds far more tools than its job needs. Too many tools degrade the choice, and tools outside the role get misused.", "diagram": "18 tools + built-ins → triage script → wrong picks\n5 triage tools → triage script → right picks", "mapTitle": "Descriptions, or the tool set?", "map": {"head": ["Symptom", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Similar tools, one-line descriptions", "Better descriptions", "Primary selection mechanism"], ["Many tools, good descriptions, wrong picks", "Scope the tool set to the role", "Too many tools degrade selection"], ["Agent uses tools outside its job", "Remove them from its definition", "Off-role tools get misused"], ["One role often needs one outside tool", "Give a limited cross-role tool", "Keeps the common case fast"], ["Tool must run first", "Forced <code>tool_choice</code>", "Fixes order, not choice"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It was given every tool from both MCP servers, 18 in all, plus the built-in tools", "means": "The decider: the tool count is the cause."}, {"quote": "Sometimes it queries staging when no data is involved", "means": "A tool outside its job gets misused. Merging two issue tools (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves this in place."}, {"quote": "Every tool already has a detailed description", "means": "Rewriting them (<b>[[0]]</b>) does not reach the cause."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "A fixed, smaller tool set for this one job: <b>[[2]]</b>. A per-report classifier (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more than a fixed job needs."}], "rule": "Good descriptions and still wrong picks among many tools? Give the agent only the tools its job needs.", "guide": {"obj": "2.3 Distribute tools across agents and configure tool choice", "quote": "Too many tools (18 vs 4-5) degrades selection reliability."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-01", "was": "m7-s2-01", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Personal vs project commands", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wrote a <code>/standup</code> command. It summarises their own commits and reads notes from their home folder. They want to use it in every repository they open, not only this one. Teammates have their own standup habits. They have asked not to see this command in their lists. Where should the command file <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In this repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed, with a README note that it is optional.", "why": "Works, but wrong scope. The engineer gets the command here, but only in this repository, and every teammate sees it after their next pull.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> in the engineer's home folder, outside every repository.", "why": "Best. User-level commands are personal. They follow the engineer into every project, and nobody else sees them."}, {"t": "In <code>.claude/commands/</code> of each repository, with the file listed in each <code>.gitignore</code>.", "why": "Works, but copies to maintain. It stays private, but the file must be copied and ignored in every repository, and the copies drift apart.", "trap": "copies to maintain"}, {"t": "As a section in the engineer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> that describes the standup steps.", "why": "Works, but wrong kind of file. Claude would follow the steps when asked, but memory loads into every session and there is no <code>/standup</code> to invoke.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "They want to use it in every repository they open, not only this one.", "explanation": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ are shared through git. User commands in ~/.claude/commands/ are personal and follow the user across projects.", "eli5": {"question": "One engineer has a personal <code>/standup</code> command. They want it everywhere they work, and teammates don't want to see it. The clue: <i>every repository</i>, and <i>only for them</i>.", "answer": "Think of a personal toolbox you carry from job to job, versus the shared tools left on site. The site tools are <code>.claude/commands/</code> in the repository: everyone who clones gets them. Your own toolbox is <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> in your home folder: it comes with you to every project and nobody else sees it. <b>Rule: shared with the team → project commands. Just for me, everywhere → user commands.</b>", "options": ["Leaving your tool on site means it stays at this one site, and everyone else trips over it.", "Correct. Your own toolbox goes with you to every repository, and only you see it.", "A hidden copy at every site works, but you have to make and update every copy yourself.", "A note describing the steps is not a tool you can pick up; there is no <code>/standup</code> to run."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a command belongs with the code it is used on</b>. That is true for team workflows. This command is personal: it reads the engineer's own notes and is wanted in every repository. Claude Code has a <b>user-level</b> commands folder exactly for this.", "diagram": "~/.claude/commands/standup.md  → this engineer, every repo\nrepo/.claude/commands/*.md     → everyone, this repo only", "mapTitle": "Who needs the command, and where?", "map": {"head": ["Who and where", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team, this repository", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed", "Arrives for everyone on clone or pull"], ["Only me, every repository", "<code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Personal; follows me across projects"], ["Only me, only this repository", "Repo command file ignored by git", "Private, but one copy per repo"], ["A standard Claude must always follow", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded, not invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It summarises their own commits and reads notes from their home folder", "means": "The command is personal to one engineer."}, {"quote": "They want to use it in every repository they open, not only this one", "means": "The decider. A file in one repository (<b>[[0]]</b>) or copied into each (<b>[[2]]</b>) doesn't follow the engineer by itself."}, {"quote": "They have asked not to see this command in their lists", "means": "A committed project command (<b>[[0]]</b>) shows up for everyone."}, {"quote": "Where should the command file live?", "means": "It is a command, so it lives in a commands folder, not in memory (<b>[[3]]</b>). The user-level one: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team command → .claude/commands/ in the repo. Personal command for every project → ~/.claude/commands/.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ (shared) vs user ~/.claude/commands/ (personal)"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-02", "was": "m7-s2-02", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Path-scoped rules", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's accessibility conventions apply to every screen file. Screen files are named <code>*.screen.tsx</code> and live in <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/ui/</code>. The conventions sit in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they also load during server work. Where should they <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in <code>mobile/</code>, and one in <code>packages/shared/ui/</code> that imports it.", "why": "Works, but tied to folders. Folder CLAUDE.md files would load the conventions for screens, but they need one per tree and also load for every other file in those folders.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "An <code>/a11y</code> skill that engineers invoke when they work on a screen.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. A skill loads when it is invoked, so screens edited without it miss the conventions.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A file in <code>.claude/rules/</code> whose <code>paths:</code> frontmatter is <code>**/*.screen.tsx</code>.", "why": "Best. A path-scoped rule loads whenever Claude edits a matching file, in any folder, and stays out of server work."}, {"t": "A separate markdown file pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. @import keeps the conventions in their own file, which is tidier, but they still load in every session, including server work.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Screen files … live in mobile/ and packages/shared/ui/.", "explanation": "Conventions tied to a file type that spans folders belong in .claude/rules/ with a paths glob. They load only for matching files, wherever those files are.", "eli5": {"question": "Some rules apply only to screen files, which sit in two different folders. Right now Claude reads those rules even when it works on the server.", "answer": "Picture a sticky note that pops up only when you open a certain kind of file, wherever it is. That's a <b>rule file with a path pattern</b> in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is a note stuck on a folder: fine when everything is in one folder, clumsy when the files are spread out. <b>Rule: convention follows a file pattern → path-scoped rule. Convention follows a folder → folder CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["Notes stuck on two folders: copies to keep in sync, and they show up for other files in those folders too.", "A skill waits until someone asks for it. People forget.", "Correct. The note appears for screen files only, in any folder.", "Importing into the root file still loads it for everyone, every time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>conventions belong in a CLAUDE.md near the code</b>. That works when the code sits in one folder. These conventions follow a <i>file name pattern</i> across two trees. Claude Code can load a rule file only when the file being edited matches a glob, wherever it lives.", "diagram": ".claude/rules/a11y.md   paths: **/*.screen.tsx\n  mobile/home.screen.tsx          → loaded\n  packages/shared/ui/x.screen.tsx → loaded\n  server/api.ts                   → not loaded", "mapTitle": "Where does this instruction live?", "map": {"head": ["Where it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Files matching a pattern, in many folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a <code>paths:</code> glob", "Loads by file, in any folder"], ["Everything inside one folder", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working there"], ["The whole project, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or an @import from it)", "Shared with the team through git"], ["Only you, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["A task someone runs on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team's accessibility conventions apply to every screen file", "means": "The convention follows a kind of file, which is what a <code>paths:</code> glob matches."}, {"quote": "Screen files are named *.screen.tsx and live in mobile/ and packages/shared/ui/", "means": "The decider. Two trees, so folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files (<b>[[0]]</b>) need a copy per tree."}, {"quote": "The conventions sit in the root CLAUDE.md, so they also load during server work", "means": "The goal is to stop loading them everywhere. An @import from the root (<b>[[3]]</b>) still loads everywhere."}, {"quote": "Where should they go?", "means": "A rule that loads by itself for matching files: <b>[[2]]</b>. A skill (<b>[[1]]</b>) needs someone to invoke it."}], "rule": "Convention follows a file pattern across folders → .claude/rules/ with a paths glob.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-03", "was": "m7-s2-03", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Plan mode for open design choices", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A ticket asks to replace the hand-written API client in <code>mobile/</code> with one generated from the server's OpenAPI spec. Many screens call the old client. The team has not yet chosen between two generator libraries. Each library shapes the calls differently, so the choice changes every screen. How should the engineer <b>start</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use plan mode: have Claude survey call sites, compare both libraries and agree a design first.", "why": "Best. An open design choice that touches many files is what plan mode is for: explore and decide first, then edit once."}, {"t": "Start editing one feature folder with the likelier library, run its tests, then roll the pattern out.", "why": "Works, but too early. A small tested spike is good once the library is chosen. Here it bakes in a guess, and the other folders copy it.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to map the call sites and how each library would change them, then edit.", "why": "Works, but stops halfway. The subagent gathers the facts without filling the main context, but nobody agrees a design before the edits start.", "trap": "only half the job"}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about the old client's edge cases, then make the change directly.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviewing collects requirements. The open question here is which library, and that needs the code surveyed.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The team has not yet chosen between two generator libraries.", "explanation": "Plan mode fits large changes with several valid approaches or an architectural decision. Direct execution fits well-scoped changes whose approach is settled.", "eli5": {"question": "A big change touches many screens, and the team hasn't picked which of two libraries to use. The clue: the choice is still open, and it changes every screen.", "answer": "Before rewiring a whole house, an electrician walks every room and agrees the plan with you. Rewiring one room first and copying it everywhere is risky if you later pick a different fuse box. Walking the house and agreeing first is <b>plan mode</b>: Claude explores and proposes a design, and nothing is edited until it is agreed. <b>Rule: open choice + many files → plan mode first. Settled approach + small change → edit directly.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Look at every screen, compare both libraries, agree the design, then edit.", "A tested trial room is a good habit, but only after the fuse box is chosen.", "A helper who walks the rooms brings back a good map, but nobody agrees the plan before work starts.", "Asking about the old wiring's quirks is useful, but it doesn't pick the library."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a small tested spike is always the safe start</b>. It is, once the approach is settled. Here the approach is the open question, and whatever the spike picks gets copied into every screen. Plan mode lets Claude explore and design <i>before</i> committing to edits, which avoids rework.", "diagram": "plan mode:  survey call sites → compare libs → agree\n            → then edit all screens once\ndirect:     edit → test → copy pattern (guess baked in)", "mapTitle": "How should Claude start this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best start", "Why"], "rows": [["Many files, approach not yet chosen", "Plan mode", "Explore and decide before edits"], ["Approach chosen, change across folders", "Direct edits, one folder at a time with tests", "Nothing left to design"], ["One small, well-scoped fix", "Direct execution", "Planning adds nothing"], ["Discovery would flood the context", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary, keeps context clean"], ["Requirements unclear to Claude", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Many screens call the old client", "means": "A multi-file change, one of plan mode's signs."}, {"quote": "The team has not yet chosen between two generator libraries", "means": "The decider. A spike with the likelier library (<b>[[1]]</b>) bakes in a guess."}, {"quote": "Each library shapes the calls differently, so the choice changes every screen", "means": "This is a design decision, not missing requirements (<b>[[3]]</b>). Mapping alone (<b>[[2]]</b>) doesn't make the decision."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer start?", "means": "Start by exploring and agreeing a design: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Several valid approaches across many files → plan mode before any edits.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Plan mode: large-scale changes, multiple valid approaches, architectural decisions, multi-file mods"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-04", "was": "m7-s2-04", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skills vs always-loaded memory", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team stores money as whole cents in server, mobile and shared code. That rule lives inside a <code>/money</code> skill, so Claude sees it only when someone invokes the skill. Generated code often uses decimal numbers for prices instead. The team wants every engineer to get the rule in every session. Where should the rule <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths:</code> globs for the server, mobile and shared price modules.", "why": "Works, but too narrow. The rule would load for price modules, but prices turn up in new code anywhere, and the team wants it in every session.", "trap": "too narrow"}, {"t": "In each engineer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, copied from a snippet kept in the team wiki.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Each person who copies it gets the rule, but user-level memory is not shared through git, so copies drift and new joiners miss it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In the <code>/money</code> skill, with a description telling Claude to use it for any pricing work.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude may pick the skill up from its description, but a skill is still on demand, and price code often doesn't look like pricing work.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "In the committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, as a short rule next to the team's other conventions.", "why": "Best. A standard for all code and all sessions belongs in always-loaded project memory, which every engineer gets from the repository."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The team wants every engineer to get the rule in every session.", "explanation": "Skills are on-demand workflows. CLAUDE.md holds always-loaded standards. A rule every session needs belongs in the project CLAUDE.md.", "eli5": {"question": "A money rule (store prices as whole cents) only reaches Claude when someone opens a skill, so Claude often forgets it. The clue: the team wants it in <i>every session</i>, for <i>everyone</i>.", "answer": "A recipe card you pull out for one dish is a skill. The house rules on the kitchen wall are <code>CLAUDE.md</code>: everyone sees them every time they cook. “Prices are whole cents” is a house rule, so it goes on the wall: the committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. <b>Rule: always applies, for everyone → project CLAUDE.md. A task you run on request → skill.</b>", "options": ["A note that appears only at certain stations misses prices cooked anywhere else.", "Personal copies of the house rules drift, and new staff never get one.", "Hoping the cook pulls out the right recipe card works some of the time, not every time.", "Correct. House rules on the wall, seen by everyone in every session."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a narrower home is always tidier</b>. Path rules and skills keep context small, which is good when a convention applies to some files or some tasks. This rule applies to any code that touches a price, and the team wants it in every session. That is the job of always-loaded <b>project memory</b>.", "mapTitle": "When should Claude see this instruction?", "map": {"head": ["When it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Every session, every engineer", "Committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded; shared through git"], ["Only when editing certain files", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads for matching files only"], ["A task someone runs on request", "Skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code>", "Loads when invoked"], ["Only me, every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal; not in git"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team stores money as whole cents in server, mobile and shared code", "means": "A team-wide standard across the whole repository."}, {"quote": "so Claude sees it only when someone invokes the skill", "means": "The cause: an on-demand home. A better skill description (<b>[[2]]</b>) is still on demand."}, {"quote": "Generated code often uses decimal numbers for prices instead", "means": "Prices appear in new code anywhere, not only in known price modules (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The team wants every engineer to get the rule in every session", "means": "The decider. Shared and always loaded: <b>[[3]]</b>. Personal memory (<b>[[1]]</b>) isn't shared."}], "rule": "A standard every session needs goes in the committed project CLAUDE.md, not in a skill.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Skills = on-demand task workflows; CLAUDE.md = always-loaded universal standards"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-05", "was": "m7-s2-05", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 User-level memory is not shared", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "A new engineer's Claude ignores the team's test-naming conventions, while a senior engineer's Claude follows them. The senior looks and finds the conventions in their own <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>. They are not anywhere in the repository. The team wants every engineer, including future hires, to get them. What should the team <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Make the conventions a <code>/test-names</code> command in <code>.claude/commands/</code> to run on tests.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. Everyone gets the command, but the conventions apply only when it is run, not while tests are written.", "trap": "only when remembered"}, {"t": "Move the conventions into the committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and delete the senior's copy.", "why": "Best. Project memory reaches everyone on clone, including future hires. Deleting the personal copy leaves one version to maintain."}, {"t": "Put the conventions in a shared file and <code>@import</code> it from each engineer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. There is one source file, but each engineer must add the import by hand, and new hires start without it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Have the senior paste the conventions into the new engineer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> as well.", "why": "Works, but for one person. The new engineer is fixed today, but the next hire has the same gap, and two personal copies drift.", "trap": "fixes one person"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "They are not anywhere in the repository.", "explanation": "User-level CLAUDE.md is personal and not shared through version control. Team conventions belong in project-level memory.", "eli5": {"question": "One engineer's Claude follows the test-naming rules and another's doesn't. The rules turn out to live in the senior's personal file. The clue: <i>not anywhere in the repository</i>.", "answer": "The senior kept the team's rules in their own notebook, so only they had them. The fix is to pin the rules on the team noticeboard, the committed project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, and throw away the private copy so there is only one version. <b>Rule: if the whole team needs it, it goes in the repository, not in someone's home folder.</b>", "options": ["A tool people must remember to use helps, but the rules should apply while tests are written.", "Correct. Put the rules on the team board everyone gets with the code.", "Everyone pointing their own notebook at one page still needs every person, and every new hire, to set it up.", "Copying the notebook for one newcomer fixes them, not the next one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a team convention reaches the team once someone writes it down</b>. Where it is written matters. <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> lives in one person's home folder and is not in git. Only files committed to the repository reach every clone.", "diagram": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (senior only)   ✗ not in git\nrepo/CLAUDE.md (committed)          ✓ every clone", "mapTitle": "Who gets this instruction?", "map": {"head": ["Where it is written", "Who gets it", "Use it for"], "rows": [["Committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Everyone who clones", "Team conventions"], ["<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Only that person", "Personal preferences"], ["@import from each user file", "Only those who add the line", "Personal setups sharing a source"], ["A command in <code>.claude/commands/</code>", "Everyone, when they run it", "Workflows run on request"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The senior looks and finds the conventions in their own ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md", "means": "The cause is found: user-level memory, which only the senior loads."}, {"quote": "They are not anywhere in the repository", "means": "The decider. Nothing reaches the team through git. Fixes that stay in home folders (<b>[[2]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) keep that gap."}, {"quote": "The team wants every engineer, including future hires, to get them", "means": "It must arrive on clone and apply without anyone remembering a command (<b>[[0]]</b>). That is project memory: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team conventions go in committed project memory; user-level CLAUDE.md is never shared.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "User-level is not shared via version control"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-06", "was": "m7-s2-06", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Machine-readable CLI output", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 2, "question": "A script runs <code>claude -p</code> to generate tests for each new endpoint. It then reads Claude's reply to learn which files were created and whether the tests passed. The wording of the reply changes from run to run, and the parser keeps breaking. The script needs named fields it can rely on. Which <b>TWO</b> changes fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Add <code>--output-format json</code> to the command and read the result from the JSON it prints.", "why": "Best. The CLI prints a machine-readable JSON envelope instead of free text, so the script no longer parses prose."}, {"t": "Put a sample JSON reply in the prompt and ask Claude to answer with only that JSON object.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A format example gets the shape right most runs, but the structure is requested, not enforced, and one stray line breaks the parser.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Send each reply to a second <code>claude -p</code> call that rewrites it as the JSON the script expects.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second call can tidy the reply, but it doubles the calls and is still a prompt asking for a format.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Pass a <code>--json-schema</code> that defines a list of new files and a pass or fail field.", "why": "Best. The schema fixes which named fields the result contains. With JSON output, the script reads exactly those fields."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The script needs named fields it can rely on.", "explanation": "For scripts, use the CLI's structured output: --output-format json for a machine-readable envelope and --json-schema for the fields. Prompt-level format requests stay probabilistic.", "eli5": {"question": "A script reads Claude's reply, but the wording changes each run and the script breaks. The clue: a <i>script</i> needs <i>named fields it can rely on</i>.", "answer": "Asking someone to “please write it neatly” is not the same as handing them a form with labelled boxes. The CLI can hand Claude the form: <code>--output-format json</code> makes the output JSON, and <code>--json-schema</code> says which boxes must be filled (files created, pass or fail). <b>Rule: a program reads it → structured output from the CLI. A person reads it → a format example is enough.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The output comes back as JSON a script can read.", "Asking nicely for neat writing works most of the time, but a script needs it every time.", "Paying a second person to copy out the first one's notes neatly works, but it is a long way round.", "Correct. The schema is the form with labelled boxes: the fields are fixed."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clear format example in the prompt is enough</b> to get parseable output. For a person reading it, it is. A parser breaks on the one run that drifts. The Claude Code CLI has flags that make the output structured instead of hoping the prose is.", "diagram": "claude -p \"...\" --output-format json --json-schema s.json\n  → {\"files\": [...], \"testsPassed\": true}  → script", "mapTitle": "Who reads the output, and what do they need?", "map": {"head": ["Reader and need", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Script needs a machine-readable reply", "<code>--output-format json</code>", "JSON envelope, not prose"], ["Script needs specific named fields", "<code>--json-schema</code>", "Fixes which fields appear"], ["A person reads the reply", "Format example in the prompt", "Usually right is good enough"], ["Run hangs waiting for input in CI", "<code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code>", "Non-interactive mode"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It then reads Claude's reply to learn which files were created and whether the tests passed", "means": "A program, not a person, reads the output, and it wants two specific facts."}, {"quote": "The wording of the reply changes from run to run, and the parser keeps breaking", "means": "The output is free text. A prompt example (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a rewrite call (<b>[[2]]</b>) still asks rather than enforces."}, {"quote": "The script needs named fields it can rely on", "means": "The decider. JSON output (<b>[[0]]</b>) plus a schema for the fields (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}], "rule": "A script parsing Claude Code output → --output-format json plus --json-schema, not prompt wording.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "--output-format json + --json-schema for machine-parseable findings"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-05", "was": "m7-s4-05", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Plan mode for multi-approach changes", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The team must move the app from one background-job library to another. Job classes, retry settings and tests in many folders will change. Engineers disagree on whether to move job by job or switch everything at once. Nobody has yet mapped which jobs use features found only in the old library. How should the engineer <b>start</b> with Claude?", "options": [{"t": "Use an Explore subagent to list jobs that use old-library features, then convert them directly.", "why": "Works, but skips the design step. The list answers the mapping question, but converting straight away settles the job-by-job debate by default.", "trap": "skips the design"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode, have Claude map the job code and compare both paths, then approve a plan.", "why": "Best. Plan mode lets Claude explore and weigh both strategies before any file changes, so the team agrees a design first."}, {"t": "Convert the simplest job directly, review its diff, then repeat for each job in turn.", "why": "Works, but picks a path without deciding. Direct execution suits small, clear changes; here it quietly chooses job by job and finds the hard jobs late.", "trap": "decides by default"}, {"t": "Have Claude write tests for each job first, then convert the jobs until all the tests pass.", "why": "Works, but in the wrong order. Tests make each conversion safer, but they don't choose the strategy or map the old-library features.", "trap": "wrong order"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Engineers disagree on whether to move job by job or switch everything at once.", "explanation": "Use plan mode for large changes across many files with several valid approaches: explore and design before editing. Direct execution suits simple, well-scoped changes.", "eli5": {"question": "A big change touches many folders, the team hasn't picked a strategy, and nobody knows the risky jobs yet. The clue: <i>engineers disagree</i> on how to do it.", "answer": "Before moving house, you walk through every room and agree a plan: move it all in one day, or room by room? You don't start carrying boxes while still arguing. <b>Plan mode</b> is that walk-through: Claude explores and proposes a design, and nothing changes until you approve. <b>Rule: big change, several valid ways, unknowns → plan mode first. Small clear change → just do it.</b>", "options": ["Making a list of tricky rooms helps, but then you start carrying boxes without agreeing the plan.", "Correct. Walk through, compare the two ways, agree, then move.", "Moving one small box at a time works for small moves. Here it picks a plan without deciding.", "Checking each box is safe is good, but it doesn't settle how to move."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>careful step-by-step work is always safe</b>. Small reviewed steps are safe for well-scoped changes. This change has an <b>open strategy question</b> and <b>unknown dependencies</b>. Any approach that starts editing quietly answers the strategy question by default. Plan mode explores and designs first.", "mapTitle": "Plan mode or direct execution?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Approach", "Why"], "rows": [["Many files, several valid strategies", "Plan mode", "Explore and design before edits"], ["Architectural decision still open", "Plan mode", "Avoids rework"], ["Simple, well-scoped change", "Direct execution", "No design needed"], ["Big discovery job with noisy output", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary to the main session"], ["Behaviour must be pinned down while changing", "Tests first, then iterate", "Refinement, after the plan"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Job classes, retry settings and tests in many folders will change", "means": "A large, multi-file change: plan-mode territory, not single direct steps (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Engineers disagree on whether to move job by job or switch everything at once", "means": "The decider. There are two valid strategies, and a design must choose. Converting directly after a list (<b>[[0]]</b>) skips that choice."}, {"quote": "Nobody has yet mapped which jobs use features found only in the old library", "means": "Exploration is needed before editing. Writing tests first (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't map these features."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer start with Claude?", "means": "The first move is to explore and design: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Large change + several valid approaches + unknowns → plan mode first. Simple, scoped change → direct execution.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Plan mode: large-scale changes, multiple valid approaches, architectural decisions, multi-file mods"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-06", "was": "m7-s4-06", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Background job classes follow strict team conventions, such as safe-to-repeat perform methods and set retry limits. Job files all end in <code>_job.rb</code>, but they sit in many folders across the app's engines. The conventions are long and matter only for job files. Today they live in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and load in every session. Where should they <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in <code>app/jobs/</code>, the folder where new job classes are created.", "why": "Works, but tied to one folder. It loads for jobs in that folder, but the jobs in the engines' folders miss it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A conventions file that the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> pulls in with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. The import keeps the conventions in their own file, but they still load in every session.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A <code>/job-conventions</code> skill that engineers invoke before they edit a job class.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. A skill loads when invoked, so a job edited without it misses the conventions.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "A <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> frontmatter is <code>**/*_job.rb</code>.", "why": "Best. A path-scoped rule loads whenever Claude works on a matching file, in any folder, and stays out of other work."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Job files all end in _job.rb, but they sit in many folders across the app's engines.", "explanation": "Rules in .claude/rules/ with a paths glob load only for matching files, whatever folder they are in. They beat folder CLAUDE.md files for conventions that span folders.", "eli5": {"question": "Long rules apply only to job files, which share a name ending but sit in many folders. The clue: <i>they sit in many folders</i>.", "answer": "Picture a sticky note that pops up whenever you open a file ending in <code>_job.rb</code>, wherever that file is. That's a <b>rule file with a path pattern</b> in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is a note stuck on one folder: fine if every job lived there, but these don't. <b>Rule: convention follows a file pattern → path-scoped rule. Convention follows a folder → folder CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["A note on one folder misses the job files in the other folders.", "Importing into the root file still loads it for everyone, every time.", "A skill waits until someone asks for it. People forget.", "Correct. The note appears for job files only, in any folder."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>conventions belong in a CLAUDE.md near the code</b>. That works when the code sits in one folder. Job files follow a <i>file name pattern</i> across many folders. Claude Code can load a rule file only when the file being edited matches a glob, wherever it lives.", "diagram": ".claude/rules/jobs.md   paths: **/*_job.rb\n  app/jobs/send_email_job.rb        → loaded\n  engines/billing/.../invoice_job.rb → loaded\n  app/models/user.rb                 → not loaded", "mapTitle": "Where does this instruction live?", "map": {"head": ["Where it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Files matching a pattern, in many folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a <code>paths:</code> glob", "Loads by file, in any folder"], ["Everything inside one folder", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working there"], ["The whole project, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or an @import from it)", "Shared and always loaded"], ["A task someone runs on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"], ["Only you, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Job files all end in _job.rb, but they sit in many folders across the app's engines", "means": "The decider. A shared name pattern across many folders is what a <code>paths:</code> glob matches. One folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (<b>[[0]]</b>) misses the engines."}, {"quote": "The conventions are long and matter only for job files", "means": "They should load only for job files, and by themselves. A skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) needs someone to invoke it."}, {"quote": "Today they live in the root CLAUDE.md and load in every session", "means": "The goal is to stop loading them everywhere. An @import from the root (<b>[[1]]</b>) still loads everywhere."}, {"quote": "Where should they go?", "means": "A rule that loads by itself for matching files: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Convention follows a file pattern across folders → .claude/rules/ with a paths glob.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s4-07", "was": "m7-s4-07", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skills vs always-loaded memory", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds a long runbook for restoring the staging database from a snapshot. Engineers run a restore about once a month, when they ask Claude for one. The runbook isn't tied to any file being edited. Even so, it loads into every session and fills the context. Where should the runbook <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Into <code>.claude/skills/</code> as a <code>SKILL.md</code> that engineers invoke for a restore.", "why": "Best. A project skill is an on-demand workflow: it is shared through the repository and loads only when someone asks for a restore."}, {"t": "Into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches the database config files.", "why": "Works, but the wrong trigger. It loads when someone edits those files, which has nothing to do with when a restore is asked for.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Into a separate runbook file that the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> pulls in with <code>@import</code>.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. The import tidies the root file, but the runbook still loads in every session.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Into a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> inside <code>db/</code>, next to the snapshot scripts it uses.", "why": "Works, but the wrong trigger. It loads whenever Claude works in db/, and a restore asked from elsewhere may not see it.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Engineers run a restore about once a month, when they ask Claude for one.", "explanation": "CLAUDE.md is for standards that should always load. Skills are on-demand task workflows. Path-scoped rules load guidance when matching files are edited.", "eli5": {"question": "A long how-to for a monthly task loads every single time. The clue: engineers use it <i>when they ask Claude for one</i>, not while editing any file.", "answer": "A fire-drill manual doesn't belong on every desk every day. It sits on a shelf and you take it down when you need it. A <b>skill</b> is that shelf: shared with the team, loaded only when someone calls for it. <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is the noticeboard everyone reads every day. <b>Rule: a task run on request → skill. A standard that always applies → CLAUDE.md. Guidance for certain files → path rule.</b>", "options": ["Correct. On the shelf, taken down when someone asks for a restore.", "A rule for config files pops up when editing them, which is not when people ask for a restore.", "Moving the manual into a binder that's still on every desk saves nothing.", "A note on the db folder shows up while working there, not when someone asks for a restore."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>long content just needs a smaller, nearer home</b>, like a folder file or a path rule. The real question is <b>what should trigger loading</b>. This runbook is needed when someone asks for a restore, not when certain files are edited. That trigger is a request, which is what skills are for.", "mapTitle": "What should make this content load?", "map": {"head": ["Trigger", "Home", "Loads when"], "rows": [["Someone asks for a task", "Skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code>", "Invoked"], ["Editing files that match a pattern", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "A matching file is edited"], ["Working in one folder", "Folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Claude works in that folder"], ["Always, for everyone", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or an @import", "Every session"], ["Only you, every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Your sessions only"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Engineers run a restore about once a month, when they ask Claude for one", "means": "The decider. The trigger is a request, which is a skill: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The runbook isn't tied to any file being edited", "means": "File-based triggers miss the point: a path rule (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a db/ CLAUDE.md (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "it loads into every session and fills the context", "means": "The goal is to stop always-loading. An @import (<b>[[2]]</b>) still loads every session."}], "rule": "On-request task → skill. Always-true standard → CLAUDE.md. File-pattern guidance → path rule.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Skills = on-demand task workflows; CLAUDE.md = always-loaded universal standards"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-01", "was": "m7-s5-01", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy and scope", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A senior engineer wrote the team's review criteria into her own <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>. Her local reviews follow them well. The CI review job and her teammates' sessions do not pick them up. The criteria apply to every file in the repository, in both languages. Where should the criteria <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> globs match the Kotlin and TypeScript files.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. It is committed and shared, and the globs would match most files. Path rules are for conventions tied to certain files; criteria for every file belong in the file that loads every time.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A new section in the committed project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> at the repo root.", "why": "Best. The project CLAUDE.md is committed, so every clone and the CI job get it, and it loads in every session, local or headless."}, {"t": "A committed <code>docs/review-criteria.md</code> that the CI job's <code>-p</code> prompt names.", "why": "Works, but only for CI. The CI job would read it, but developers' own sessions never see a file that only the CI prompt mentions.", "trap": "covers only CI"}, {"t": "A project skill in <code>.claude/skills/review/</code> that CI and developers invoke.", "why": "Works, but only when invoked. Skills load on demand, so any session that doesn't call it reviews without the criteria.", "trap": "only when invoked"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The criteria apply to every file in the repository, in both languages.", "explanation": "User-level CLAUDE.md is personal and never reaches teammates or CI. Standards for the whole repository go in the committed project CLAUDE.md, which every session loads.", "eli5": {"question": "Good review rules are stuck in one person's private notes, so CI and her teammates never see them. The clue: the rules apply to <i>every</i> file.", "answer": "Her <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> is like a note in her own pocket. The team needs a poster on the office wall that everyone sees every day: the <b>project CLAUDE.md</b>, committed to the repository. Other homes work too, but each has a catch: a note that only appears for some files, a note only the robot reads, or a manual someone has to open. <b>Rule: rules for everyone and every file → project CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["A note that pops up for matching files. Fine for file-type rules; for rules that cover everything, the wall poster is simpler.", "Correct. Committed, shared, and loaded in every session, including CI.", "Only the CI job is told about this file. Developers' sessions never read it.", "A manual on the shelf: it helps only when someone opens it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>any committed file will reach everyone</b>. Committing only shares the file. What matters next is <b>when it loads</b>: the project CLAUDE.md loads in every session, a path rule loads for matching files, a skill loads when invoked, and a plain doc loads only when a prompt points at it.", "diagram": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md   → only her sessions\nCLAUDE.md (project)   → every session, local and CI\n.claude/rules/ paths: → only for matching files\n.claude/skills/       → only when invoked", "mapTitle": "Who needs it, and when should it load?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team, every file, every session", "Project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Committed and loaded every time"], ["Whole team, only certain file types", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads only for matching files"], ["Only me, every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["A task run on request", "Skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"], ["Only one CI job", "That job's <code>-p</code> prompt", "Other sessions never see it"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A senior engineer wrote the team's review criteria into her own ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md", "means": "User-level memory is personal and is not in git. That is why nobody else gets it."}, {"quote": "The CI review job and her teammates' sessions do not pick them up", "means": "Both CI and developers need them. A file named only in the CI prompt (<b>[[2]]</b>) reaches CI alone."}, {"quote": "The criteria apply to every file in the repository, in both languages", "means": "The decider. This is a universal standard, not a file-type convention (<b>[[0]]</b>), and it should not wait for someone to invoke a skill (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Where should the criteria live?", "means": "In the committed project CLAUDE.md: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team-wide standards for every file go in the committed project CLAUDE.md. User-level CLAUDE.md is personal.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "User-level is not shared via version control."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-02", "was": "m7-s5-02", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 Verifying loaded memory", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds one line, <code>@docs/testing-standards.md</code>, which should pull in the team's testing rules. The CI test generator keeps writing tests that break those rules. A developer can reproduce this in a local session. Nobody has checked yet whether the imported file loads at all. What should the developer do <b>first</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/memory</code> in that session and see whether the standards file is listed.", "why": "Best. /memory is the built-in way to see which memory files loaded. It tells “not loaded” apart from “loaded but not followed”, and each needs a different fix."}, {"t": "Add two finished tests that follow the rules to the generator's prompt as examples.", "why": "Works, but too early. Examples help once the rules are known to be loaded. If the import is broken, they cover it up instead of fixing it.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Ask Claude in that session to quote the testing rules it was given, then compare.", "why": "Works, but unreliable. It may give a hint, but the model's account of its own context can paraphrase or invent rules. /memory shows the real list.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Paste the rules inline in place of the <code>@import</code> line and rerun the generator.", "why": "Works, but guesses the cause. If the import was broken this fixes it, but the rules lose their own file and nobody learns what went wrong.", "trap": "skips the check"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Nobody has checked yet whether the imported file loads at all.", "explanation": "When Claude Code seems to ignore guidance, first confirm what it actually loaded. /memory lists the loaded memory files; only then choose between fixing the import and improving the prompt.", "eli5": {"question": "Tests keep breaking the team's rules. The rules sit in a file that CLAUDE.md pulls in with one line. The clue: nobody knows yet whether that file is even being read.", "answer": "If a cook ignores a recipe, first check the recipe is actually on the kitchen wall. Only then teach the cook. <code>/memory</code> is that look at the wall: it lists every memory file Claude Code loaded. <b>Rule: guidance looks ignored → check it loaded (/memory) before changing the prompt.</b>", "options": ["Correct. It shows whether the rules file was loaded at all.", "A good next step, but only once you know the rules are loaded.", "Asking the cook what's on the wall: they might misremember. Look yourself.", "Rewriting the recipe before checking it was on the wall. It may help, but you learn nothing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>rules that are ignored need a stronger prompt</b>. There are two different faults: the rules never loaded, or they loaded and were not followed. They need different fixes, so the first step is to <b>find out which</b>.", "diagram": "rules ignored\n  /memory → file missing → fix the @import\n  /memory → file listed → examples, clearer rules", "mapTitle": "Claude Code seems to ignore guidance. Which fault is it?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Likely fault", "Next step"], "rows": [["Not sure the file loads", "Unknown", "Run <code>/memory</code>"], ["<code>/memory</code> does not list it", "Broken import or wrong location", "Fix the path or move the file"], ["Listed, but output still drifts", "Rules unclear or not shown", "Add examples or clearer criteria"], ["Listed only in some folders", "Directory-level CLAUDE.md", "Move to project level or <code>.claude/rules/</code>"], ["Works for you, not for teammates", "User-level CLAUDE.md", "Move to the committed project file"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The project CLAUDE.md holds one line, @docs/testing-standards.md", "means": "The rules arrive through an import. If the import fails, nothing else in the prompt matters."}, {"quote": "A developer can reproduce this in a local session", "means": "So the developer can run <code>/memory</code> right there and see the loaded files."}, {"quote": "Nobody has checked yet whether the imported file loads at all", "means": "The decider. Examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) and inlining (<b>[[3]]</b>) are fixes for a cause nobody has found. Asking the model (<b>[[2]]</b>) is a weaker check than the real list."}, {"quote": "What should the developer do first?", "means": "Check what loaded: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Before tuning a prompt, confirm the guidance loaded. /memory shows which memory files Claude Code loaded.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "/memory command verifies which memory files are loaded."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-03", "was": "m7-s5-03", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Machine-parseable CLI output", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The review job runs <code>claude -p</code> and asks Claude to end its reply with a JSON block of findings. A script cuts out that block and posts each finding as an inline comment. On some runs Claude adds a sentence after the block, and the script posts nothing. The script needs a file, line, severity and message for every finding. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add two complete sample replies to the prompt, each ending in a well-placed findings block.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples make the layout more consistent, but a script needs a format it can count on every run.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have the script rerun the job with the parse error added whenever it finds no block.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. Many reruns would succeed, but each one pays for a second review, and the fragile text format stays.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Make the script search the whole reply for the last JSON block, not only the end.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. A looser parser rescues these runs, but nothing checks that each finding has a file, line, severity and message.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Run the CLI with <code>--output-format json</code> and a <code>--json-schema</code> for the findings.", "why": "Best. The CLI then returns machine-parseable output that matches the schema, with the fields each inline comment needs."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The script needs a file, line, severity and message for every finding.", "explanation": "In CI, claude -p with --output-format json and --json-schema gives findings a script can parse. Asking for JSON in prose, even with examples, leaves the format to chance.", "eli5": {"question": "A script reads Claude's reply and posts comments. Sometimes Claude adds a chatty sentence and the script chokes. The clue: a <i>script</i>, not a person, reads the reply.", "answer": "A person can read a letter with a P.S. at the bottom. A sorting machine needs a form with fixed boxes. <code>--output-format json</code> with <code>--json-schema</code> is that form: Claude Code returns JSON in the shape you defined. <b>Rule: a program reads the output → ask the CLI for schema-shaped JSON, don't ask nicely in the prompt.</b>", "options": ["Showing a sample letter makes the P.S. rarer, not impossible.", "Sending the letter back when the machine chokes: it often works, but you pay twice.", "A smarter machine that hunts for the form: it copes, but nobody checks the boxes are filled in.", "Correct. The CLI hands over a filled-in form, every time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clear request plus examples is enough to get JSON</b>. For a person reading the reply, it is. A script needs a <b>contract</b>: output that always parses and always has the required fields. Claude Code's CLI has flags for exactly that.", "diagram": "claude -p \"review\" --output-format json \\\n  --json-schema findings.json\n→ [{file, line, severity, message}, …]\n→ script posts inline comments", "mapTitle": "Who reads the output, and how sure must the format be?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Script parses Claude Code CLI output in CI", "<code>--output-format json</code> + <code>--json-schema</code>", "Machine-parseable, schema-shaped"], ["Messages API call must return fixed fields", "Tool with JSON schema + forced <code>tool_choice</code>", "Most reliable schema output"], ["People read it; layout varies", "Few-shot examples of the format", "Shows the pattern"], ["Output parses but values are wrong", "Validate, retry with the specific error", "Schema can't catch meaning errors"], ["CI job hangs waiting for input", "<code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code>", "Non-interactive mode"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review job runs claude -p and asks Claude to end its reply with a JSON block of findings", "means": "The format is only a request in the prompt. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) make it likelier, not certain."}, {"quote": "On some runs Claude adds a sentence after the block, and the script posts nothing", "means": "A format fault. Reruns (<b>[[1]]</b>) and a looser parser (<b>[[2]]</b>) cope with it after the fact."}, {"quote": "The script needs a file, line, severity and message for every finding", "means": "The decider. Required fields for a program: a schema the CLI enforces, <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "When a script consumes Claude Code output in CI, use --output-format json with --json-schema.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "--output-format json + --json-schema for machine-parseable findings → inline PR comments."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-01", "was": "m6-s2-01", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 @import for shared standards", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The platform group owns the team's TypeScript style guide. It lives at <code>docs/standards/style.md</code> in this repository. The group revises it every couple of weeks. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds a hand-written summary of part of the guide. That summary is already out of date. The guide applies to every file Claude writes. How should Claude <b>get the guide</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Copy the guide's rules into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file for <code>**/*.ts</code> and drop the summary.", "why": "Works, but another copy. The rules would load for every TypeScript file, but the group keeps revising the original, so this copy goes out of date just like the summary did.", "trap": "copies to maintain"}, {"t": "Replace the summary in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line for the guide.", "why": "Best. The import loads the guide as it stands today, so each revision reaches Claude and nobody maintains a copy."}, {"t": "Wrap the guide in a <code>/ts-style</code> skill that loads the document when an engineer invokes it.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. The skill reads the live file, but it loads on demand, and the guide applies to every file Claude writes.", "trap": "only when remembered"}, {"t": "Ask each engineer to <code>@import</code> the guide from their own <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> file.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. Each engineer who adds the line gets the live guide, but user-level memory is not shared through git, so new joiners start without it.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "That summary is already out of date.", "explanation": "When a standard is owned and revised in another file, import it with @import instead of keeping a copy. The import always loads the current version.", "eli5": {"question": "The team keeps a hand-written summary of a style guide in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, and the real guide keeps changing. The clue: the summary is <i>already out of date</i>, and the guide is revised often.", "answer": "Think of a noticeboard. You can copy the rules onto it by hand, or you can pin up a note that says “see the current rulebook”. A hand copy goes stale every time the rulebook changes. An <code>@import</code> line is the pointer: Claude reads the guide file itself, as it stands today. <b>Rule: a standard owned and revised elsewhere → import it, don't copy it.</b>", "options": ["A neater copy on a different board is still a copy. It goes stale like the first one.", "Correct. A pointer to the real rulebook, so Claude always reads the current version.", "A rulebook you must ask for by name is only read when someone remembers to ask.", "Personal pointers work for each person who sets one up, but new joiners don't have one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the fix is a better home for the copy</b>, such as a tidy rules file. The problem is not where the copy sits; it is that there is a copy at all. The guide lives in the repository and changes every couple of weeks. <code>@import</code> pulls that file into memory, so there is one source and Claude reads it as it is now.", "diagram": "CLAUDE.md\n  @docs/standards/style.md   → loads the live guide\nrules/style.md (copied text)  → stale after next revision", "mapTitle": "A standard lives in another file. How does Claude get it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Owned and revised in a repo file, applies everywhere", "<code>@import</code> from the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "One source, always current"], ["Team-owned, applies only to some files", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads for matching files only"], ["A task run on request", "Skill or slash command", "Loads when invoked"], ["Only me, every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal; not in git"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The group revises it every couple of weeks", "means": "Any copy of the guide will keep falling behind."}, {"quote": "That summary is already out of date", "means": "The decider. Copying is the cause, so a fresh copy in a rules file (<b>[[0]]</b>) repeats it."}, {"quote": "The guide applies to every file Claude writes", "means": "It must always load. An on-demand skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) and personal imports (<b>[[3]]</b>) both leave gaps."}, {"quote": "How should Claude get the guide?", "means": "Point project memory at the real file: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "A standard that lives and changes in another file → @import it from project memory; don't keep a copy.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "@import syntax for modular references"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-02", "was": "m6-s2-02", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Path-scoped rules", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Tenant isolation adds conventions for data-access code. Every repository function must take a <code>tenantId</code>, and raw SQL is banned. These files are named <code>*.repo.ts</code>. Most sit in <code>server/src/repositories/</code>, but others live in <code>server/jobs/</code> and <code>packages/shared/db/</code>. The conventions should load whenever Claude edits one of these files, and not for other work. Where should they <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, under a “Tenant data access” heading with one scoped function as a model.", "why": "Works, but loads everywhere. Claude would see the conventions in every repo file, and also in every other session where they don't apply.", "trap": "too broad"}, {"t": "In a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> inside <code>server/src/repositories/</code>, with one scoped function as a model.", "why": "Works, but tied to one folder. It covers most repo files, but the ones in jobs and the shared package never load it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths: **/*.repo.ts</code> and one scoped function as a model.", "why": "Best. A path-scoped rule loads whenever Claude edits a matching file, in any folder, and stays out of other work."}, {"t": "In a <code>tenant-repo</code> skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code> that engineers invoke for data-access work.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. A skill loads on request, so repo files edited without it miss the conventions.", "trap": "only when remembered"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Most sit in server/src/repositories/, but others live in server/jobs/ and packages/shared/db/.", "explanation": "Conventions for a kind of file that is spread across folders belong in .claude/rules/ with a paths glob. They load only for matching files, wherever they are.", "eli5": {"question": "New rules apply to data-access files, and those files sit in three different folders. The clue: <i>most</i> are in one folder, <i>but others</i> live elsewhere.", "answer": "Picture a reminder that pops up only when you open a certain kind of file, wherever it sits. That is a <b>rule file with a path pattern</b> in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is a note stuck on one folder's door: it misses the same kind of file in other rooms. <b>Rule: a convention follows a file pattern → path-scoped rule. It follows one folder → folder CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["A note on the front door is seen by everyone, even people who never touch data access.", "A note on one room's door works for that room, but some of these files are in other rooms.", "Correct. The reminder appears for every <code>*.repo.ts</code> file, in any folder, and nowhere else.", "A manual you must ask for only helps when someone remembers to ask."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a CLAUDE.md in the main folder is close enough</b>, since most of the files are there. These conventions follow a <i>kind of file</i>, not a folder, and some of those files sit elsewhere. Claude Code can load a rule file only when the file being edited matches a glob, wherever it lives.", "diagram": ".claude/rules/tenant.md   paths: **/*.repo.ts\n  server/src/repositories/a.repo.ts → loaded\n  server/jobs/b.repo.ts             → loaded\n  mobile/home.screen.tsx            → not loaded", "mapTitle": "Where does this convention live?", "map": {"head": ["Where it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Files matching a pattern, in many folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a <code>paths:</code> glob", "Loads by file, in any folder"], ["Everything inside one folder", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working there"], ["The whole project, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded for everyone"], ["A task someone runs on request", "Skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "These files are named *.repo.ts", "means": "The convention follows a file name pattern, which is what a <code>paths:</code> glob matches."}, {"quote": "Most sit in server/src/repositories/, but others live in server/jobs/ and packages/shared/db/", "means": "The decider. One folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (<b>[[1]]</b>) misses the others."}, {"quote": "The conventions should load whenever Claude edits one of these files, and not for other work", "means": "Not everywhere (<b>[[0]]</b>), and not on request (<b>[[3]]</b>). Loaded by file: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Convention follows a file pattern across folders → .claude/rules/ with a paths glob.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-03", "was": "m6-s2-03", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Plan mode", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer must change how background jobs in <code>server/jobs/</code> receive the current tenant. The requirements are settled: each job runs for one tenant, and child jobs inherit it. How to build it is not settled. The job modules open database connections in several different ways. A base class, a middleware and a decorator have all been proposed. How should the engineer <b>start</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use plan mode: Claude surveys the job modules, weighs the designs and proposes one for sign-off.", "why": "Best. An open design choice across many files is what plan mode is for: explore, decide, then edit once."}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to survey how job modules open connections and return a summary of patterns.", "why": "Works, but stops halfway. The survey keeps the main context clean, but nobody weighs the designs or agrees one before edits start.", "trap": "only half the job"}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about tenant rules and edge cases, then implement from the answers.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviewing draws out requirements, and those are already settled. The open question is the design.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have Claude write failing tests for tenant inheritance, then edit module by module until they pass.", "why": "Works, but too early. Tests pin down the behaviour, but editing before a design is chosen bakes in a guess across every module.", "trap": "too early"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "How to build it is not settled.", "explanation": "Plan mode fits multi-file changes with several valid approaches. It lets Claude explore and agree a design before editing, which avoids rework.", "eli5": {"question": "A change touches many job files. The team knows <i>what</i> it wants, but three ways to build it are on the table. The clue: <i>how to build it is not settled</i>.", "answer": "Before re-plumbing a whole building, a plumber walks every floor, compares the options and agrees one with you. Then the work is done once. Walking and agreeing first is <b>plan mode</b>: Claude explores and proposes a design, and nothing is edited until it is agreed. <b>Rule: open design choice + many files → plan mode first.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Walk every floor, compare the three designs, agree one, then start work.", "A helper who maps the pipes brings back a good picture, but nobody picks the design.", "Asking what you want is useful when the wishes are unclear. Here they are already agreed.", "Testing the taps first is good, but if you start re-plumbing before choosing, you may redo it all."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a survey or a test-first loop is enough to start safely</b>. Each helps with one part. Here the requirements are clear and the open question is a design choice that touches many modules. Plan mode covers both parts: Claude explores the code <i>and</i> proposes a design before any edit.", "diagram": "plan mode: survey jobs → weigh 3 designs → agree\n           → then edit every module once\ntests first: edit → guess design → maybe redo", "mapTitle": "How should Claude start this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best start", "Why"], "rows": [["Many files, design not chosen", "Plan mode", "Explore and decide before edits"], ["Only need to understand code, no decision", "Explore subagent", "Summary back, clean context"], ["Requirements unclear to Claude", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Design chosen, behaviour well defined", "Tests first, then direct edits", "Failures guide each step"], ["One small, exact fix", "Direct execution", "Nothing to design"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The requirements are settled: each job runs for one tenant, and child jobs inherit it", "means": "Nothing to interview about (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "How to build it is not settled", "means": "The decider. A design must be chosen. A survey alone (<b>[[1]]</b>) doesn't choose it."}, {"quote": "A base class, a middleware and a decorator have all been proposed", "means": "Several valid approaches across many modules. Editing first (<b>[[3]]</b>) bakes in a guess."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer start?", "means": "Explore and agree a design first: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Several valid designs across many files → plan mode before any edit.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Plan mode: large-scale changes, multiple valid approaches, architectural decisions, multi-file mods"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-04", "was": "m6-s2-04", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Test-driven iteration", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Claude is writing <code>tenantQuota()</code> from a spec with eight worked input and output cases. Each round, the engineer reports a failing case and Claude fixes it. But each fix breaks a different case that had been passing. The engineer finds these breaks only by checking every case by hand. What should the engineer do <b>next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add more input and output pairs to the spec at each boundary, with the exact expected result for each.", "why": "Works, but different problem. More examples help when Claude misreads the behaviour. Here the cases are clear; the breaks go unseen.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Send the two most recently broken cases in one message and ask for one change that fixes both.", "why": "Works, but only for two. Interacting problems belong in one message, but each round breaks a different case, so the next fix breaks a third.", "trap": "only part of it"}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about any quota rules it is unsure of before the next revision.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviewing surfaces unclear requirements. These are written down as worked cases.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have Claude turn the eight cases into automated tests, run them after every edit, and fix what fails.", "why": "Best. Tests run after each edit show Claude every break at once, so it fixes the cases together instead of one at a time."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The engineer finds these breaks only by checking every case by hand.", "explanation": "Test-driven iteration: write the cases as tests first and share the failures. Claude then sees every regression after each change.", "eli5": {"question": "Each fix breaks a different case, and the engineer only finds out by checking by hand. The clue: the breaks are <i>found by hand</i>, so Claude never sees them.", "answer": "Imagine fixing one light in a house and unknowingly blowing another, because nobody checks the other rooms. A test suite is someone who flips every switch after each fix. <b>Test-driven iteration</b> means turning the worked cases into tests, running them after every edit, and giving Claude the failures. <b>Rule: fixes keep breaking other cases → write the tests, run them every time.</b>", "options": ["More drawings of how the lights should work don't help if nobody flips the switches.", "Fixing two rooms together helps those two, but a third room breaks next round.", "Asking about the wiring helps when the plan is unclear. Here it is written down.", "Correct. Flip every switch after each fix, and show Claude which ones failed."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the spec or the instructions need more detail</b>. The behaviour is already pinned down by worked cases. What's missing is feedback: Claude sees only the one case reported each round, so it can't notice the others breaking. Tests run after every edit give it the full picture.", "mapTitle": "Iterating with Claude isn't converging. What helps?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Each fix breaks a different passing case", "No automatic check of all cases", "Tests first, run after every edit"], ["Two known problems affect each other", "Fixed one at a time", "Both in one message"], ["Claude misreads the transformation", "Prose underspecifies it", "Concrete input/output examples"], ["Requirements unclear to Claude", "Missing information", "Interview pattern"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "from a spec with eight worked input and output cases", "means": "The behaviour is specified. More examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) or an interview (<b>[[2]]</b>) add what's already there."}, {"quote": "But each fix breaks a different case that had been passing", "means": "Not one pair of interacting cases. Sending two together (<b>[[1]]</b>) fixes two and leaves the rest."}, {"quote": "The engineer finds these breaks only by checking every case by hand", "means": "The decider. Claude has no automatic feedback. Tests run each edit: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Fixes keep breaking other cases → write the cases as tests, run them every edit, share failures.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Test-driven iteration (write tests first, share failures)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-05", "was": "m6-s2-05", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Command vs always-loaded memory", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's pre-PR checklist sits in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. Its items are about migration files and the shared package. Claude now raises checklist points during routine work. Engineers use the checklist once, as the last step before opening a pull request. It changes about monthly, and new joiners should get the current version on clone. Where should the checklist <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths:</code> globs for migrations and the shared package.", "why": "Works, but wrong trigger. It loads less often, but still during routine edits to those files, not once before a PR.", "trap": "wrong trigger"}, {"t": "Into a <code>/pre-pr</code> command in the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code>, run before a pull request.", "why": "Best. A project command arrives on clone, is updated in git, and loads only when run, which matches a once-before-PR check."}, {"t": "Into <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> on each laptop, copied there by the onboarding script for new joiners.", "why": "Works, but copies drift. Each new joiner gets it on day one, but the monthly changes never reach the copies already installed.", "trap": "copies to maintain"}, {"t": "Into <code>docs/pre-pr.md</code>, pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. The file is tidier, but imported memory loads in every session, so the points keep popping up.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Engineers use the checklist once, as the last step before opening a pull request.", "explanation": "Something run on request belongs in a command or skill, not in always-loaded memory. A project command in .claude/commands/ is shared through git.", "eli5": {"question": "A checklist meant for the very end of the work loads all the time, so Claude keeps bringing it up. The clue: it is used <i>once, as the last step</i>.", "answer": "A pre-flight checklist is read just before take-off, not taped over the windscreen for the whole flight. A <b>project slash command</b> is that checklist kept in the cockpit drawer: everyone on the crew has it, the latest version is always there, and it comes out only when you call for it. <b>Rule: used on request → command. Must apply all the time → CLAUDE.md.</b>", "options": ["Taping it near certain controls still shows it during the flight, not only before take-off.", "Correct. Kept in the shared drawer, current for everyone, opened when you need it.", "Handing each pilot their own copy works on day one, but the copies miss every update.", "A neater page, still taped over the windscreen the whole time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is that the checklist loads too broadly</b>, so a narrower path rule fixes it. The real mismatch is timing. The checklist is a task run once at the end, and any always-on or path-based home loads it during normal work. A <b>project command</b> loads only when invoked and is shared through git.", "mapTitle": "When should Claude see this checklist?", "map": {"head": ["When it is needed", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Once, when someone asks for it", "Project command in <code>.claude/commands/</code>", "Loads on request; shared in git"], ["Whenever matching files are edited", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads during those edits"], ["Every session, every engineer", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (or <code>@import</code>)", "Always loaded"], ["Only me, every project", "<code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Personal; not in git"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude now raises checklist points during routine work", "means": "The symptom: always-loaded memory. An <code>@import</code> (<b>[[3]]</b>) still loads it."}, {"quote": "Engineers use the checklist once, as the last step before opening a pull request", "means": "The decider. It is a task on request, not a rule for edits (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "It changes about monthly, and new joiners should get the current version on clone", "means": "It must live in the repository. Copies on laptops (<b>[[2]]</b>) drift. Project command: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Run once on request → project command in .claude/commands/. Always applies → CLAUDE.md.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ (shared) vs user ~/.claude/commands/ (personal)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-06", "was": "m6-s2-06", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Direct execution", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "An incident review produced an exact fix for <code>server/jobs/invoice.ts</code>. The ticket gives the before and after lines for one retry setting. No other file needs to change. A regression test for the fix already exists. The file is long and holds several other billing jobs. How should the engineer <b>have Claude make</b> the change?", "options": [{"t": "Send an Explore subagent through the billing jobs to summarise how retries work, then make the edit.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. An Explore subagent is for open-ended discovery. The fix is already known to the line.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode so Claude reads the whole file, maps the other jobs and proposes the edit for sign-off.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Plan mode suits open or multi-file changes. This one is decided, single-file and tested.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Work directly: give Claude the ticket's lines, have it edit and run the test, then review the diff.", "why": "Best. A small, exact, tested change is what direct execution is for. The diff review confirms nothing else moved."}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about the incident and retry behaviour before it changes anything.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviewing draws out unclear requirements. The incident review already settled them.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The ticket gives the before and after lines for one retry setting.", "explanation": "Direct execution fits simple, well-scoped changes. Plan mode and Explore subagents are for open or large changes.", "eli5": {"question": "The exact fix is written in the ticket, it touches one file, and a test already exists. The clue: the ticket gives the <i>before and after lines</i>.", "answer": "If the mechanic's note says “replace this one bolt with that one”, you don't survey the whole engine first. You swap the bolt, run the check, and look it over. That is <b>direct execution</b>: Claude makes the exact edit, runs the test, and you review the diff. The file being long doesn't change that. <b>Rule: decided, small, tested → edit directly.</b>", "options": ["Sending a scout to map the engine is for when you don't know where the problem is. You do.", "Drawing up a plan for one bolt swap works, but the plan is already in the note.", "Correct. Swap the bolt, run the check, look over the result.", "Asking what happened helps when nobody knows the fix. The review already wrote it down."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a big file calls for planning or exploring first</b>. What matters is whether the change is open or decided. Here it is decided to the line, touches one file, and has a test. That is the definition of a <b>simple, well-scoped change</b>, and direct execution fits it.", "mapTitle": "How much process does this change need?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best approach", "Why"], "rows": [["Exact fix known, one file, test exists", "Direct execution + diff review", "Nothing to design or discover"], ["Open design or many files", "Plan mode", "Decide before editing"], ["Need to understand unfamiliar code", "Explore subagent", "Summary back, clean context"], ["Requirements unclear", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The ticket gives the before and after lines for one retry setting", "means": "The decider. The change is fully decided, so there is nothing to discover (<b>[[0]]</b>) or ask (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "No other file needs to change. A regression test for the fix already exists", "means": "Single file, already testable: the signs of a well-scoped change."}, {"quote": "The file is long and holds several other billing jobs", "means": "Length tempts toward plan mode (<b>[[1]]</b>), but it doesn't make the change open. A diff review covers it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Decided, single-file, tested change → direct execution, then review the diff.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Direct execution: simple well-scoped changes (single validation check)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-08", "was": "m6-s4-08", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skill with context: fork", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Once a month, an engineer upgrades the app's gems with Claude. Claude reads the release notes for dozens of gems, about 150,000 tokens of text. It then returns a short list of required code edits. The engineer makes those edits in the same conversation afterwards. Several engineers take turns doing this task. What is the <b>best way</b> to package it?", "options": [{"t": "A project slash command in <code>.claude/commands/upgrade-gems.md</code> with the steps, checks and edit-list format.", "why": "Works, but fills the conversation. A shared command suits a repeatable team task, but the release notes would load into the conversation the engineer keeps using.", "trap": "fills the conversation"}, {"t": "A user-level skill in <code>~/.claude/skills/upgrade-gems/</code> with <code>context: fork</code> and Read and Grep allowed.", "why": "Works, but not shared. The forked context keeps the reading out, but a user-level skill lives on one machine, and the task rotates.", "trap": "not shared"}, {"t": "A skill in the repository's <code>.claude/skills/</code> with <code>context: fork</code> set, ending with the edit list.", "why": "Best. A project skill is shared through git, and context: fork runs the reading in an isolated sub-agent context, so only the edit list comes back."}, {"t": "A procedure file in <code>docs/</code> pulled into the project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but loads every session. @import keeps CLAUDE.md tidy, but a monthly task then fills every session, and the reading is not isolated.", "trap": "loads every session"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The engineer makes those edits in the same conversation afterwards.", "explanation": "Skills are on-demand team workflows. context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context, so heavy reading does not pollute the main conversation.", "eli5": {"question": "A heavy monthly task produces a short result that people keep working with. The clues: <i>150,000 tokens</i> of reading, the edits happen <i>in the same conversation</i>, and <i>several engineers</i> do it.", "answer": "Send someone to the archive to read a mountain of papers and bring back one page. Your desk stays clear (<b>context: fork</b>). Put the instructions on the shared team shelf, not in your own drawer, so whoever is on duty finds them (<b>.claude/skills/ in the repo</b>). <b>Rule: on-demand team task with heavy reading → project skill with context: fork.</b>", "options": ["Shared instructions, but the mountain of paper lands on the desk you keep working at.", "The archive trip is right, but the instructions sit in one person's drawer.", "Correct. Shared instructions, reading done in the archive, one page back.", "Pinning the instructions to every desk, every day, for a monthly job."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a slash command and a skill are the same thing</b>. Both package a repeatable task. A skill can also set <code>context: fork</code>, which runs it in its own sub-agent context. That matters when the work reads far more than it returns.", "mapTitle": "How should this workflow be packaged?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Package", "Why"], "rows": [["Team task, heavy reading, short result", "Project skill with <code>context: fork</code>", "Shared, and isolates the reading"], ["Team task, output fine in the conversation", "Project command in <code>.claude/commands/</code>", "Shared through git"], ["Personal task only", "<code>~/.claude/skills/</code> or <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Not shared"], ["Standard every session needs", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>, with @import for modules", "Always loaded"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "about 150,000 tokens of text", "means": "The reading is huge; it should not land in the working conversation."}, {"quote": "The engineer makes those edits in the same conversation afterwards", "means": "The decider. Only the edit list should come back, so isolate the reading; a plain command (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't."}, {"quote": "Several engineers take turns doing this task", "means": "It must be shared through the repo, which rules out the user-level skill (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Once a month", "means": "On demand, not always loaded, so @import into CLAUDE.md (<b>[[3]]</b>) is the wrong home. <b>[[2]]</b> fits."}], "rule": "Shared on-demand task with heavy reading → project skill with context: fork.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context (no pollution of main conversation)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-09", "was": "m6-s4-09", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 Verifying loaded memory files", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer says Claude ignores <code>app/billing/CLAUDE.md</code> and keeps adding a <code>LogWrapper</code> call that the file forbids. Teammates in the same folder don't see this. The engineer's personal <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> says to wrap outbound calls in <code>LogWrapper</code>. They also recently started working in a worktree made from an older branch. Either one might explain it. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Remove the <code>LogWrapper</code> line from their <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> and restate the ban in the billing file.", "why": "Works, but fixes a guess. It is right once the personal file is the cause. The old-branch worktree may lack the billing file, so check what loads first.", "trap": "fixes a guess"}, {"t": "Run <code>/memory</code> in that engineer's own session, inside the worktree they use, and see which files load.", "why": "Best. /memory shows which memory files are loaded, so one look tells a missing billing file apart from a conflicting personal one."}, {"t": "Recreate the worktree from the current main branch, then ask Claude to make the billing change again.", "why": "Works, but fixes a guess. It helps if the old branch is the cause, but the personal instruction stays, and it shows nothing about what loaded.", "trap": "fixes a guess"}, {"t": "Ask Claude in that session to list the billing rules it is following before it makes the next edit.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Claude's own account of its instructions can be incomplete, while /memory shows the loaded files for certain.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Either one might explain it.", "explanation": "/memory verifies which memory files are loaded. When two causes are possible, checking what is loaded comes before changing either one.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude ignores a folder's rules for one engineer only. The clue: <i>either one might explain it</i>. Two suspects, so the first step is to find out which, not to fix one.", "answer": "Two people give a cook opposite orders, and you're not sure the cook even got the second note. Before you change anyone's orders, you look at the notes pinned on the cook's board (<b>/memory</b>). Then you know which problem you have. <b>Rule: instructions seem ignored → /memory first, to see what is actually loaded.</b>", "options": ["Takes down one suspect's note. Right if that was the cause, but maybe the other note never arrived.", "Correct. Look at the board and see which notes are pinned.", "Replaces the board. Helps if the note was missing, but the other order is still up.", "Asking the cook to recite the orders. Useful, but the cook may not remember them all."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the conflicting personal instruction is obviously the cause</b>, so remove it. But the worktree came from an older branch, which may not have the billing file at all. When causes compete, the first step is a cheap check that tells them apart, and Claude Code has one: <code>/memory</code>.", "diagram": "/memory in the engineer's worktree\n  ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md      loaded (LogWrapper rule)\n  CLAUDE.md                loaded\n  app/billing/CLAUDE.md    ?  <- present or missing", "mapTitle": "Claude ignores an instruction. What do you check?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Likely cause", "First step"], "rows": [["Only one person sees it, two causes possible", "Unknown which file loads", "Run <code>/memory</code> in their session"], ["Personal file confirmed to conflict", "User-level instruction overrides intent", "Fix the personal file"], ["Directory file missing in a worktree", "Old branch lacks it", "Update the worktree"], ["Everyone sees it", "Project instruction unclear", "Fix the project CLAUDE.md"], ["Convention needed across many folders", "Directory file can't reach", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a glob"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Teammates in the same folder don't see this", "means": "The project files are fine; the cause is in this engineer's setup."}, {"quote": "The engineer's personal ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md says to wrap outbound calls in LogWrapper", "means": "Suspect one, which <b>[[0]]</b> fixes."}, {"quote": "worktree made from an older branch", "means": "Suspect two, which <b>[[2]]</b> fixes."}, {"quote": "Either one might explain it", "means": "The decider. Check before fixing: <b>[[1]]</b> shows which files load. Asking Claude (<b>[[3]]</b>) is less certain."}], "rule": "Instructions seem ignored → run /memory first to see which memory files are loaded.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "/memory command verifies which memory files are loaded"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s4-10", "was": "m6-s4-10", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Interview pattern", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A product manager wants a daily CSV export of tutor payouts for finance. It replaces a spreadsheet an analyst built by hand, and the manager pastes a few rows. The engineer knows the schema but not payout accounting. Finance says the old sheet got some cases wrong, but not which. Nobody has decided how refunds after payout or lessons crossing midnight are handled. What is the <b>best way</b> to start?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude begin by asking the engineer about unusual cases the export must handle, then take open ones to finance.", "why": "Best. The interview pattern has Claude ask first, surfacing the cases the engineer hasn't thought about in an unfamiliar domain."}, {"t": "Give Claude the pasted spreadsheet rows with their source records, and ask for an export that matches them exactly.", "why": "Works, but copies known errors. Input/output examples are the best way to show a transformation when the outputs are right. These are wrong in unnamed cases.", "trap": "copies known errors"}, {"t": "Write failing tests for the export's columns and header first, share the failures with Claude, and iterate on them.", "why": "Works, but tests the easy part. Test-first works when the expected behaviour is known. These tests cover layout, not the unsettled payout rules.", "trap": "tests the easy part"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode and have Claude design the full export, including refunds and midnight lessons, before any coding.", "why": "Works, but invents the rules. Plan mode designs an approach, but Claude would have to make up rules nobody has set.", "trap": "invents the rules"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Nobody has decided how refunds after payout or lessons crossing midnight are handled.", "explanation": "In an unfamiliar domain with unsettled rules, the interview pattern has Claude ask questions first, so the missing requirements surface before code is written.", "eli5": {"question": "Build a report in an area nobody fully understands, from an example known to be wrong. The clue: <i>nobody has decided</i> the tricky cases, and the old sheet is wrong in unknown places.", "answer": "A tailor making a suit for someone new doesn't copy a badly fitting old suit. They ask questions and take measurements first (<b>the interview pattern</b>). The answers they can't get from you, they check with the person paying. <b>Rule: unfamiliar domain, unsettled rules → have Claude ask questions first.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Ask first, and send the open questions to the people who decide.", "Copying the old suit, including the bits that don't fit.", "Checking the buttons and pockets first. The hard part is the fit, and nobody knows it yet.", "Designing the whole suit now, guessing the measurements nobody took."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>examples or tests are always the best start</b>. They are, when you trust the expected output. Here the example is known to be wrong somewhere and key rules don't exist yet. The work starts by finding out what the requirements are, which is what the interview pattern does.", "mapTitle": "How do you start an unclear build with Claude?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Technique", "Why"], "rows": [["Unfamiliar domain, rules not settled", "Interview pattern: Claude asks first", "Surfaces missing requirements"], ["Trusted input/output examples exist", "Concrete examples", "Show the transformation exactly"], ["Expected behaviour is known", "Tests first, share failures", "Tight feedback loop"], ["Big change, several valid approaches", "Plan mode", "Design before editing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The engineer knows the schema but not payout accounting", "means": "The domain is unfamiliar; questions will find what the engineer hasn't thought of."}, {"quote": "Finance says the old sheet got some cases wrong, but not which", "means": "The pasted rows aren't trustworthy, so matching them (<b>[[1]]</b>) copies errors."}, {"quote": "Nobody has decided how refunds after payout or lessons crossing midnight are handled", "means": "The decider. The rules must be found before tests (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a plan (<b>[[3]]</b>) can use them: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Unfamiliar domain with unsettled rules → interview pattern: Claude asks questions first.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "the interview pattern (Claude asks questions first)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-03", "was": "m6-s5-03", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Path-scoped rules across directories", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Database migration conventions sit in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so they load on every review. Most pull requests touch no migrations, so the section wastes context. Each service keeps its migrations in its own folder, such as <code>services/billing/db/migrations/</code>. There are many services. The team wants the conventions loaded only when a migration file is involved. Where should they <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in each service's migrations folder, with a CI check that keeps the copies identical.", "why": "Works, but one copy per folder. Folder files load only there, which is right, but every service needs its own copy and a check to stop drift.", "trap": "one copy per folder"}, {"t": "A <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob is <code>services/*/db/migrations/**</code>.", "why": "Best. One rule file loads for any matching migration file, in every service, and stays out of other reviews."}, {"t": "A project skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code> that the review prompt calls when the diff has a migration.", "why": "Works, but only when invoked. The conventions load only if the prompt decides to call the skill, which a path rule does by itself.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "A separate <code>docs/migrations.md</code> pulled into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. The file is tidier, but imported content loads on every review.", "trap": "still loads everywhere"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each service keeps its migrations in its own folder", "explanation": "<code>.claude/rules/</code> files with <code>paths:</code> globs load conventions only for matching files, and beat folder CLAUDE.md files when those files span many folders. <code>@import</code> organises content but does not make it conditional.", "eli5": {"question": "Rules about migration files load on every review, even when no migration changed. The clue: migrations sit in <i>a folder per service</i>, many folders.", "answer": "Picture a sticky note that pops up only when you open a migration file, wherever it is. That's a <b>rule file with a path pattern</b> in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A folder <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is a note stuck on one folder: you'd need one per service. <b>Rule: convention follows a file pattern across folders → path-scoped rule.</b>", "options": ["A note on every folder: many copies, and a check to keep them the same.", "Correct. One note that pops up for migration files in any service.", "A manual someone must remember to open.", "Moving the note to a separate page that is still read every time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>conventions belong in a CLAUDE.md next to the code</b>. That works for one folder. These files are spread across many service folders. A rule file with a <code>paths:</code> glob loads only when a matching file is in play, wherever it lives.", "diagram": ".claude/rules/migrations.md\n  paths: services/*/db/migrations/**\nservices/billing/db/migrations/V3.sql → loaded\nservices/search/src/Api.kt            → not loaded", "mapTitle": "Where does this convention live?", "map": {"head": ["Where it applies", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Files matching a pattern, in many folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads by file, any folder"], ["Everything in one folder", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working there"], ["The whole project, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or an @import", "Loads every session"], ["A task run on request", "A skill or command", "Loads only when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "so they load on every review", "means": "The goal is to stop loading them everywhere. An @import (<b>[[3]]</b>) still loads everywhere."}, {"quote": "Each service keeps its migrations in its own folder", "means": "The decider. Folder files (<b>[[0]]</b>) need a copy per service."}, {"quote": "The team wants the conventions loaded only when a migration file is involved", "means": "Loading tied to files, by itself: <b>[[1]]</b>. A skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) waits for someone to call it."}], "rule": "Conventions that follow a file pattern across folders → .claude/rules/ with a paths glob.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-04", "was": "m6-s5-04", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skill with context: fork", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Developers and the release job both draft the changelog with a long procedure. It walks the git log since the last tag and groups commits by service. The procedure lives in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, so every review run loads it too. In a developer's session it prints thousands of lines of log, and later answers get worse. It must stay versioned with the repository. Where should it <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A project command at <code>.claude/commands/changelog.md</code>, run as a slash command by CI and developers.", "why": "Works, but the log still floods. A project command is shared and on demand, but it runs inside the current conversation.", "trap": "log still floods"}, {"t": "The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, with a line telling Claude to hand the git-log walk to the Explore subagent.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. The log is kept out of the session, but every review run still loads the procedure.", "trap": "still loads everywhere"}, {"t": "A skill in each developer's <code>~/.claude/skills/</code> folder, with <code>context: fork</code> in its frontmatter.", "why": "Works, but not shared. Forking keeps the log out, but personal skills are not in the repository, so CI and new developers lack it.", "trap": "not shared"}, {"t": "A project skill in <code>.claude/skills/changelog/</code> whose <code>SKILL.md</code> sets <code>context: fork</code>.", "why": "Best. A project skill is versioned and loads on demand, and <code>context: fork</code> runs it in its own context, keeping the log out of the session."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "In a developer's session it prints thousands of lines of log, and later answers get worse.", "explanation": "CLAUDE.md holds always-loaded standards; skills are on-demand workflows, and <code>context: fork</code> runs one in an isolated sub-agent context.", "eli5": {"question": "A long changelog procedure sits in the file every session reads, and when it runs it floods the chat with log lines. The clue: <i>thousands of lines of log</i>.", "answer": "Think of a messy job like sorting a big box of receipts. You don't keep the instructions taped to every desk, and you don't tip the box out on your work table. You keep the instructions in the team cupboard (a <b>project skill</b>) and do the sorting in a back room (<code>context: fork</code>), bringing back only the summary. <b>Rule: on-demand procedure with noisy output → project skill with context: fork.</b>", "options": ["Instructions in the cupboard, but you still tip the box out on your desk.", "Back room for the sorting, but the instructions stay taped to every desk.", "Back room, but the instructions are in your own drawer, not the team's.", "Correct. Team cupboard, back room, summary returned."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>moving the procedure out of CLAUDE.md is the whole fix</b>. There are two problems: it loads everywhere, and its output floods the session. A slash command fixes the first only. A project skill with <code>context: fork</code> fixes both.", "diagram": "/changelog skill (context: fork)\n  sub-context: git log ... 1000s of lines\n  returns: grouped summary → main session", "mapTitle": "Where does a repeatable procedure live?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Home", "Why"], "rows": [["Always-on standards", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads every session"], ["On demand, little output, shared", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>", "Runs in the current session"], ["On demand, noisy output, shared", "<code>.claude/skills/</code> with <code>context: fork</code>", "Output stays in its own context"], ["Personal workflow", "<code>~/.claude/skills/</code>", "Not in the repository"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Developers and the release job both draft the changelog", "means": "Shared by people and CI, so personal skills (<b>[[2]]</b>) don't reach everyone."}, {"quote": "so every review run loads it too", "means": "It should load only on demand. Keeping it in the root file (<b>[[1]]</b>) doesn't."}, {"quote": "In a developer's session it prints thousands of lines of log, and later answers get worse", "means": "The decider. Output must be isolated, which a command (<b>[[0]]</b>) does not do. Forked skill: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Noisy on-demand procedures go in a project skill with context: fork.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context (no pollution of main conversation)."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-05", "was": "m6-s5-05", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Test-driven iteration", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A scheduled job asks Claude Code to fix a fare-rounding function that gives wrong totals for some currencies. The function has a test file with one case per currency, and several cases fail today. The job's runner can run the tests. The job runs with <code>-p</code>, with no one watching, and the fix touches one function. How should the prompt <b>direct the work</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude run the suite, fix the code against what fails, and repeat until it all passes.", "why": "Best. The failing tests are the spec and the feedback, and the full set catches breakage in other currencies."}, {"t": "List worked amounts, with the currency and the expected rounded total, for each failing case.", "why": "Works, but less checking. Examples beat prose, but a handful of pairs cannot catch a fix that breaks another currency, and the tests already exist.", "trap": "less checking"}, {"t": "Describe each currency's rounding rule in a short paragraph before asking for the fix.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. Prose rules are easy to misread; examples and tests say the same thing exactly.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Start in plan mode, so Claude explores the rounding code and proposes an approach first.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Plan mode suits large changes with many options; a one-function fix gains little, and nobody is there to approve.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The function has a test file with one case per currency, and several cases fail today. The job's runner can run the tests.", "explanation": "Concrete examples and test-driven iteration both beat prose. When runnable tests exist, iterating against them gives a checkable stop point.", "eli5": {"question": "An unattended job must fix a rounding bug. Which way of steering Claude gets a correct fix? The clue: <i>a test file exists, cases fail, and the runner can run it</i>.", "answer": "Think of a student with a practice sheet and the answer key. The best way to learn is to try, check against the key, fix, and check again until all match. The failing tests are that key (<b>test-driven iteration</b>), and they also show if a fix breaks another currency. <b>Rule: when runnable tests exist, iterate against them.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Try, check against the key, fix, repeat.", "A few worked answers help, but the full key is already there and checks more.", "Explaining the rules in words: easier to misunderstand than the key.", "Writing an essay plan for a one-line fix, with no teacher to read it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the best prompt explains the rules well</b>. Explaining helps, and examples help more. But when a runnable test file already exists, <b>tests are examples that check themselves</b>. Claude can loop against them until they pass, and they catch side effects too.", "diagram": "run tests → 3 fail\nchange code → run tests → 1 fails\nchange code → run tests → all pass → stop", "mapTitle": "How do I tell Claude what correct looks like?", "map": {"head": ["What you have", "Best technique", "Why"], "rows": [["Runnable tests, some failing", "Test-driven iteration", "Checks itself and catches breakage"], ["No tests, a clear transformation", "Concrete input/output examples", "Clearer than prose"], ["Unclear requirements, a person present", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks first"], ["Large change, many approaches", "Plan mode", "Design before editing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The function has a test file with one case per currency, and several cases fail today", "means": "The decider. The spec and the feedback already exist: <b>[[0]]</b>. Hand-picked pairs (<b>[[1]]</b>) check less."}, {"quote": "The job runs with -p, with no one watching", "means": "No one approves a plan (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "the fix touches one function", "means": "Small and well scoped, so no planning step is needed."}], "rule": "Runnable failing tests exist → have Claude iterate against them until they pass.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Test-driven iteration (write tests first, share failures)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-01", "was": "s5q-s2-01", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.3 Diagnosing a rule that never loads", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team moved its mobile test conventions out of the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> into <code>.claude/rules/mobile-tests.md</code>. The rule's <code>paths:</code> glob is <code>mobile/**/*.test.tsx</code>. The mobile test files are really named <code>*.spec.tsx</code>. Shared test helpers in <code>packages/shared/testing/</code> must follow the same conventions. Claude ignores the conventions in both places. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Put the conventions in a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> in <code>mobile/</code> and a copy in <code>packages/shared/testing/</code>.", "why": "Works, but tied to folders. Folder CLAUDE.md files would load the conventions, but they need two copies kept in step and load for every other file in those folders.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Pull the rule file into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with an <code>@import</code> line, as the one source.", "why": "Works, but loads everywhere again. The conventions reach the test files, but they load in every session, which undoes the move out of the root file.", "trap": "undoes the goal"}, {"t": "Turn the conventions into a <code>/test-style</code> skill that engineers invoke when writing tests.", "why": "Works, but only when someone remembers. A skill loads on request, so tests written without invoking it miss the conventions.", "trap": "only when remembered"}, {"t": "Change the rule's <code>paths:</code> to match <code>**/*.spec.tsx</code> and the shared testing folder.", "why": "Best. The rule never loads because its glob matches no real file. Fixing the pattern keeps one rule that loads for every matching file, in both trees, and nowhere else."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The mobile test files are really named *.spec.tsx.", "explanation": "A path-scoped rule loads only for files its glob matches, and a glob that matches nothing fails silently. Fix the pattern; glob rules suit conventions that span folders.", "eli5": {"question": "A rule file was set up to load for test files, but Claude never uses it. The clue: the glob looks for <code>*.test.tsx</code>, and the files are really named <code>*.spec.tsx</code>. This is not about where rules should live; the home is already right.", "answer": "Think of a doorbell wired to the wrong door. The bell works; nobody presses it. The rule file (a <b>path-scoped rule</b>) is fine, but its address pattern matches no real file, so it never loads. Fix the address to <code>**/*.spec.tsx</code> and add the shared testing folder. <b>Rule: a path-scoped rule that never fires usually has a glob that matches nothing. Fix the glob, not the home.</b>", "options": ["Notes stuck on two folders would work, but you keep two copies in step, and they show for every file there.", "Importing into the root file makes the rule load for everyone, every time. That is what the move was meant to stop.", "A skill waits to be asked. Tests written without asking miss the conventions.", "Correct. Rewire the bell to the right doors: one rule, both trees."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the rule is in the wrong place</b> and to move it. The home is right: conventions that follow a file type across folders belong in <code>.claude/rules/</code>. A rule loads only when the edited file matches its <code>paths:</code> glob, and <b>a glob that matches nothing gives no error</b>. Here the glob names the wrong suffix and misses a folder.", "diagram": "paths: mobile/**/*.test.tsx\n  mobile/cart/Cart.spec.tsx      -> no match\n  packages/shared/testing/x.ts   -> no match\nfixed: **/*.spec.tsx, packages/shared/testing/**\n  both                           -> loaded", "mapTitle": "Conventions are not applied: where is the fault?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Rule file never loads for the files it targets", "Glob matches no real file", "Correct the <code>paths:</code> glob"], ["Convention belongs to one folder only", "Nothing wrong with a folder note", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>"], ["Convention spans several folders", "Folder notes need copies", "One rule with a glob"], ["Convention loads in unrelated work", "It sits in the root file", "Move it to a path-scoped rule"], ["Workflow someone runs on request", "Not a convention", "A skill or command"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team moved its mobile test conventions out of the root CLAUDE.md", "means": "The goal was to stop loading them everywhere. An @import from the root (<b>[[1]]</b>) brings that back."}, {"quote": "The mobile test files are really named *.spec.tsx", "means": "The decider. The glob looks for *.test.tsx, so it matches nothing. The home is fine; the pattern is wrong."}, {"quote": "Shared test helpers in packages/shared/testing/ must follow the same conventions", "means": "Two trees. Folder CLAUDE.md files (<b>[[0]]</b>) need a copy in each."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the cause in the existing rule: <b>[[3]]</b>. A skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) needs someone to invoke it."}], "rule": "A path-scoped rule that never loads → check its glob against the real file names first.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-02", "was": "s5q-s2-02", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 User vs project scope", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Reviewers keep finding <code>// TODO(me):</code> markers in merged code. Every marker comes from sessions run by one engineer. Their <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> has a line asking for these markers, left over from a previous job. The project's own memory files say nothing about TODO style. Nobody else on the team wants the markers. What should the team <b>do</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a line to the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> that forbids personal TODO markers in this codebase.", "why": "Works, but fights the cause. The project rule now argues with the personal line in every session of that engineer, and the line itself stays.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Have that engineer delete the line from their own file, then check it with <code>/memory</code>.", "why": "Best. User-level memory loads only in that engineer's sessions. Removing the line removes the cause, and /memory shows which files are loaded."}, {"t": "Add a CI lint check that fails any pull request whose diff contains a <code>TODO(me)</code> marker.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It catches the markers late, after Claude writes them, and adds a pipeline check for one stray personal line.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add an item to the review checklist so reviewers remove <code>TODO(me)</code> markers before merging.", "why": "Works, but too late. Reviewers keep cleaning up after each session, and the line that causes the markers stays.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Their ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md has a line asking for these markers, left over from a previous job.", "explanation": "User-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only on that person's machine and is not shared through version control. When one person's sessions misbehave, look there first and remove the cause.", "eli5": {"question": "Odd markers show up in code, and all of them come from one person's sessions. The clue: a line in that person's own personal file asks for them. Nothing in the shared project files does.", "answer": "Picture a personal sticky note on one desk that says “mark everything TODO(me)”. Nobody else has the note. You don't put up a sign for the whole office; you take the note off that one desk. The personal file is <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> (<b>user-level memory</b>), and <code>/memory</code> shows what is loaded. <b>Rule: a problem from one person's sessions → check their user-level memory and remove the cause there.</b>", "options": ["An office-wide sign works, but it argues with the sticky note, which stays on the desk.", "Correct. Take the note off the one desk where it is.", "A machine at the door that rejects the markers works, but it is a lot of kit for one sticky note.", "Asking reviewers to clean up works, but they clean up forever while the note stays."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a bad habit in merged code needs a team-wide rule</b>. Here the habit has one source: a line in one engineer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>. User-level memory is <b>personal and not shared</b>, so nobody else gets it. Removing that line fixes the cause at the right size.", "mapTitle": "Unwanted output: where does it come from, and where is the fix?", "map": {"head": ["Where the instruction comes from", "Who gets it", "Fix"], "rows": [["One person's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Only that person", "Edit that file; check with <code>/memory</code>"], ["Root or project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Everyone who clones", "Edit the committed file"], ["Nowhere: many people's sessions drift", "Everyone", "Add a project rule"], ["A rule that must hold every time", "Everyone", "A deterministic check in CI or a hook"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Every marker comes from sessions run by one engineer", "means": "One source, not a team-wide drift."}, {"quote": "Their ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md has a line asking for these markers, left over from a previous job", "means": "The decider. The cause is a single personal line. A project rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) argues with it instead of removing it."}, {"quote": "The project's own memory files say nothing about TODO style", "means": "The shared files are not at fault."}, {"quote": "What should the team do?", "means": "Remove the cause: <b>[[1]]</b>. A CI check (<b>[[2]]</b>) and a checklist (<b>[[3]]</b>) catch the markers after they are written."}], "rule": "Problem from one person's sessions → look in their user-level memory and fix it there.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "User-level is not shared via version control"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-03", "was": "s5q-s2-03", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 context: fork", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An <code>/auth-audit</code> skill finds endpoints in <code>server/</code> that still use the old auth helper. It reads about 200 files and ends with a short report that engineers like. After it runs, Claude brings up files from the scan and loses track of the engineer's task. This happens even in a fresh session. Engineers run the audit several times a day. Which change <b>most directly</b> fixes this?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> straight after each audit, before going back to the engineer's own task.", "why": "Works, but cleans up after every run. Compaction is a manual, lossy step that someone must repeat each time the audit floods the context.", "trap": "cleans up after"}, {"t": "Add <code>context: fork</code> to the skill's frontmatter, so it runs apart and returns its report.", "why": "Best. The skill runs in an isolated sub-agent context. The files it reads never enter the main conversation; only the report comes back."}, {"t": "Add an <code>argument-hint</code> asking for one folder, and audit a single folder per run.", "why": "Works, but changes the job. Smaller scans flood less, but the report engineers like needs the whole server tree.", "trap": "changes the job"}, {"t": "Have the skill save its report to a file, then start a new session that reads the file.", "why": "Works, but adds a restart. The engineer gets a clean context, but loses the session they were working in, several times a day.", "trap": "extra steps"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It reads about 200 files and ends with a short report that engineers like.", "explanation": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context, so its verbose work does not pollute the main conversation. Anything that reads a lot and reports a little is a candidate.", "eli5": {"question": "A skill reads a huge pile of files to write a short report, and the pile is left on Claude's desk. The clue: it reads a lot and hands back a little, every time it runs.", "answer": "Send an assistant to the archive room. They read the 200 files there and bring back one page. Your desk stays clear. That is <code>context: fork</code>: the skill runs in its own <b>sub-agent context</b> and returns only the report. <b>Rule: a skill that reads a lot and reports a little → context: fork.</b>", "options": ["Tidying the desk after every trip works, but you do it by hand each time and lose some detail.", "Correct. The reading happens in another room; only the page comes back.", "Reading one shelf at a time keeps the desk clearer, but the report no longer covers the whole archive.", "Moving to a new desk after every trip works, but you leave your own work behind each time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context needs cleaning up</b>. That treats each flood after it happens. The cause is one skill that pulls hundreds of files into the main conversation every run. <code>context: fork</code> moves that reading into an isolated sub-agent, so the flood never happens.", "diagram": "main session --/auth-audit--> forked sub-agent\n                                reads ~200 files\nmain session <-- short report --", "mapTitle": "Context is crowded: prevent it or clean it up?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["One skill floods context every run", "Verbose work in the main session", "<code>context: fork</code> on the skill"], ["Long session slowly fills, answers still good", "General build-up", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Answers vague, contradicting earlier work", "Context degradation", "Notes file, then a fresh session"], ["One-off big exploration of unknown code", "Discovery output", "Explore subagent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It reads about 200 files and ends with a short report that engineers like", "means": "The decider. Reads a lot, returns a little: the shape context: fork is for. Narrowing the scan (<b>[[2]]</b>) changes the report."}, {"quote": "This happens even in a fresh session", "means": "It is not general build-up. The skill itself causes it."}, {"quote": "Engineers run the audit several times a day", "means": "A manual fix after each run (<b>[[0]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) repeats all day."}, {"quote": "Which change most directly fixes this?", "means": "Stop the flood at its source: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "A skill that reads a lot and reports a little → context: fork.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-04", "was": "s5q-s2-04", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skill frontmatter & tools", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The <code>/auth-audit</code> skill is meant to be read-only. Its <code>SKILL.md</code> body opens with a sentence saying it must report and not change files. Twice this month it edited a file it was auditing, both times where the fix looked trivial. The skill's folder is also copied as-is into two other repositories, each with its own settings. Which change makes the <b>read-only property hold</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Register a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook in project settings that blocks edits while the audit runs.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. The hook is a firm block here, but settings stay behind when the folder is copied, so the other two repositories get no protection.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Run the audit only in plan mode, where Claude can read and plan but not edit files.", "why": "Works, but only when remembered. Plan mode blocks edits, but each engineer must switch to it before every audit.", "trap": "only when remembered"}, {"t": "Move the read-only line into a heading at the top of <code>SKILL.md</code> and repeat it at the end.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A louder instruction is still an instruction, and the skill already broke the one it had.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "List only <code>Read</code>, <code>Grep</code> and <code>Glob</code> in the <code>allowed-tools</code> field of the skill's frontmatter.", "why": "Best. allowed-tools limits what the skill can call, so it cannot edit. The frontmatter travels with the skill folder into every repository."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The skill's folder is also copied as-is into two other repositories, each with its own settings.", "explanation": "Skill frontmatter carries its own limits: allowed-tools restricts what the skill can call. When a skill must not do something, remove the tool rather than forbid its use, and keep the limit with the skill.", "eli5": {"question": "A skill that should only read sometimes edits files. The clue: the skill's folder is copied into other repositories, and their settings files are not.", "answer": "Give the inspector a badge that only opens doors to look, not a full key. The badge goes wherever the inspector goes. That badge is the <code>allowed-tools</code> list in the skill's <b>frontmatter</b> (the settings block at the top of <code>SKILL.md</code>). <b>Rule: a skill must not do X → remove the tool in its own frontmatter, so the limit travels with it.</b>", "options": ["A guard at this building's door works here, but the inspector also visits two other buildings with no guard.", "Asking everyone to switch on look-only mode first works, until someone forgets.", "A bigger sign saying “do not touch” is still a sign. The inspector already ignored one twice.", "Correct. The badge cannot open edit doors, and it travels with the skill."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a hook is always the firmest block</b>. It is firm, but it lives in a repository's settings. This skill is copied into other repositories without those settings. The skill's own frontmatter (<code>allowed-tools</code>) limits what it can call and <b>travels with the skill folder</b>.", "mapTitle": "A skill must not do something: where does the limit go?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Skill copied between repositories", "<code>allowed-tools</code> in its frontmatter", "Travels with the folder"], ["Block needed in one repository, for any work", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook in settings", "Firm, but stays in that repo"], ["Skill floods the main context", "<code>context: fork</code>", "Isolates context, not files"], ["Skill needs an input from the user", "<code>argument-hint</code>", "Guides the invocation"], ["Nice-to-have behaviour", "Instruction in <code>SKILL.md</code>", "Usually followed, not certain"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Its SKILL.md body opens with a sentence saying it must report and not change files", "means": "An instruction already exists. Saying it louder (<b>[[2]]</b>) is still only an instruction."}, {"quote": "Twice this month it edited a file it was auditing", "means": "The property must hold, not usually hold."}, {"quote": "The skill's folder is also copied as-is into two other repositories, each with its own settings", "means": "The decider. A hook in this repository's settings (<b>[[0]]</b>) does not travel with the folder."}, {"quote": "Which change makes the read-only property hold?", "means": "Remove the edit tools from the skill itself: <b>[[3]]</b>. Plan mode (<b>[[1]]</b>) depends on each engineer."}], "rule": "A skill must not do something → remove the tool in its own allowed-tools.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Skills in .claude/skills/ with SKILL.md frontmatter: context: fork, allowed-tools, argument-hint"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-05", "was": "s5q-s2-05", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Interview pattern", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wrote a prose spec for a codemod that moves <code>mobile/</code> to a new date library. Three runs each made different guesses about cases the spec never mentions: commented-out calls, calls in test fixtures, and import aliases. Each time, the engineer had not thought of the case until a run tripped over it. They want the gaps found before the next run. What is the <b>most effective next step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add before-and-after file pairs for the three known cases to the spec, then rerun the codemod.", "why": "Works, but only for known cases. Examples beat prose for the three cases already found, but each run uncovers a new case nobody had thought of.", "trap": "only known cases"}, {"t": "Have Claude ask the engineer about edge cases and open choices before it writes any code.", "why": "Best. The interview pattern has Claude surface the questions the author has not thought of, before any code is written."}, {"t": "Run the next attempt in plan mode and review the proposed change for each file before approving.", "why": "Works, but slow. Plan mode would show some odd cases inside per-file plans, but reading many plans is a slow way to find missing requirements.", "trap": "indirect"}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to list unusual call patterns across <code>mobile/</code> before the next run.", "why": "Works, but half the job. It finds odd code patterns, but not what the engineer wants done with each one.", "trap": "half the job"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each time, the engineer had not thought of the case until a run tripped over it.", "explanation": "When each attempt exposes a requirement the author had not considered, use the interview pattern: Claude asks questions before implementing. Concrete examples or tests come once the cases are known.", "eli5": {"question": "Every run of a code change trips over a case nobody planned for. The clue: the engineer had not thought of those cases at all. The missing piece is in the engineer's head, not in the code.", "answer": "Before a builder starts, a good one sits down and asks: “What about the back door? What about the old pipes?” Questions find the gaps before the work begins. That is the <b>interview pattern</b>: Claude asks the engineer questions first. <b>Rule: requirements you haven't thought of yet → have Claude interview you before it builds.</b>", "options": ["Showing examples helps for the three cases you know. The fourth case is still unknown.", "Correct. The builder asks questions first and finds the gaps.", "Reading the builder's plan for every room might show a gap, but it is a slow way to find them.", "A survey of the house finds odd corners, but not what you want done in each."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clearer spec fixes inconsistent runs</b>. Clearer examples help only for cases the author already knows. Here every run reveals a case the author had <i>not thought of</i>. The interview pattern turns that around: Claude asks about edge cases and open choices before coding.", "mapTitle": "Runs keep going wrong: what is missing?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "What is missing", "Technique"], "rows": [["Each run hits a case the author never considered", "Unknown requirements", "Interview pattern"], ["Known cases described in prose are misread", "A clear picture of the change", "Before-and-after examples"], ["Known behaviour must stay correct", "A check that can fail", "Tests written first"], ["Large change with several valid designs", "An agreed approach", "Plan mode"], ["Unfamiliar code to explore", "A map of the code", "Explore subagent"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Three runs each made different guesses about cases the spec never mentions", "means": "The spec has gaps, and Claude fills them with guesses."}, {"quote": "Each time, the engineer had not thought of the case until a run tripped over it", "means": "The decider. The gaps are unknown to the author, so examples of known cases (<b>[[0]]</b>) cannot close them."}, {"quote": "They want the gaps found before the next run", "means": "Find gaps before coding. A code survey (<b>[[3]]</b>) finds patterns but not decisions; plan review (<b>[[2]]</b>) is slow."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective next step?", "means": "Claude asks first: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Requirements the author hasn't thought of → interview pattern before implementation.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "the interview pattern (Claude asks questions first)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-06", "was": "s5q-s2-06", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Message batching strategy", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Four small tasks remain. Rename the <code>Ledger</code> type in <code>packages/shared/</code> to <code>Journal</code>. Update the 40 files that import it. Add a CHANGELOG line, whose wording the release template fixes. Bump a version constant in an unrelated package. The import paths depend on whether the type moves to a new module during the rename. The reviewer wants all four in one pull request. How should the engineer <b>send these to Claude</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Send the rename, the import updates and the CHANGELOG line in one message, and the version bump next.", "why": "Works, but groups by theme. The CHANGELOG wording is fixed by the template, so it does not interact with the rename and only adds noise to that message.", "trap": "groups by theme"}, {"t": "Send all four tasks in one message, let Claude pick the order, and review the whole diff at the end.", "why": "Works, but dilutes attention. One pull request is a review grouping, not a reasoning one, and the two tasks that interact get less focus.", "trap": "dilutes attention"}, {"t": "Send the rename and the import updates together, then the CHANGELOG line and the bump one at a time.", "why": "Best. The imports depend on how the rename is done, so those two go in one message. The other two depend on nothing and go one at a time."}, {"t": "Send each of the four tasks as its own message, in order, and review the diff after each one.", "why": "Works, but splits a pair. Sent alone, the rename settles where the type lives without weighing the imports that depend on it.", "trap": "splits a pair"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The import paths depend on whether the type moves to a new module during the rename.", "explanation": "Send interacting problems in one message and independent ones in sequence. Group by dependency, not by theme or by which pull request they land in.", "eli5": {"question": "Four jobs, one pull request. The clue: only the rename and the imports depend on each other. The CHANGELOG wording is fixed in advance, and the version bump is in another package.", "answer": "When you move a cupboard, you tell the movers about the cupboard and the things that hang on it in the same breath. The shopping list and the bin can wait for their own turns. Tell Claude about <b>interacting problems in one message</b> and the independent ones in sequence. <b>Rule: group by what depends on what, not by topic or pull request.</b>", "options": ["Mentioning the CHANGELOG with the rename feels natural, but its wording is already fixed, so it only adds noise.", "One big message works, but the two tasks that matter together get less attention.", "Correct. The pair that depends on each other goes together; the rest go one by one.", "One at a time works, but the rename gets decided without thinking about its imports."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>tasks that ship together should be sent together</b>. A pull request groups work for review. Claude reasons best when <b>tasks that interact share a message</b> and independent ones come one at a time. Only the rename and its imports interact here.", "mapTitle": "Which tasks share a message?", "map": {"head": ["Relationship", "How to send", "Example here"], "rows": [["One task's result shapes another", "Same message", "Rename + imports"], ["Fully independent", "Its own message, in sequence", "Version bump"], ["Same topic, but output fixed in advance", "Its own message", "CHANGELOG line from the template"], ["Same pull request only", "Not a reason to group", "All four"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Add a CHANGELOG line, whose wording the release template fixes", "means": "The note does not depend on the rename, so grouping it with the rename (<b>[[0]]</b>) is grouping by theme."}, {"quote": "Bump a version constant in an unrelated package", "means": "Independent: its own message."}, {"quote": "The import paths depend on whether the type moves to a new module during the rename", "means": "The decider. The rename and the imports interact, so splitting them (<b>[[3]]</b>) loses that link."}, {"quote": "The reviewer wants all four in one pull request", "means": "A review grouping, not a reasoning one. One big message (<b>[[1]]</b>) dilutes the pair that matters."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer send these to Claude?", "means": "Pair together, the rest in sequence: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Interacting problems in one message; independent ones in sequence.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "single message for interacting problems, sequential for independent ones"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-09", "was": "s5q-s4-09", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Direct execution", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The fix is agreed with the code owner: a two-line guard at the top of <code>apply_regional_discount</code> plus one unit test. The guard returns the full price when the region is missing. The error spike is still live. The pricing file is shared by several teams, and last month a large refactor of it went well in plan mode. How should the engineer <b>make this change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Start with the interview pattern, so Claude asks about regional pricing edge cases first.", "why": "Works, but the design is already agreed. Questions about edge cases slow a fix the owner has signed off.", "trap": "delays the fix"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode, so Claude explores the shared file and proposes an approach before editing.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Plan mode suits large changes with several valid approaches; this one is two agreed lines.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask Claude for the guard and its test directly, then review the diff before committing.", "why": "Best. The change is small and its approach is settled, so direct execution with a diff review is the right size."}, {"t": "Have an Explore subagent map the method's callers, then make the change once it reports.", "why": "Works, but the callers don't change the fix. Mapping them adds time during a live incident without changing the two agreed lines.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The fix is agreed with the code owner: a two-line guard at the top of apply_regional_discount plus one unit test.", "explanation": "Plan mode suits large, multi-file or architectural changes with several valid approaches. Direct execution suits simple, well-scoped changes whose approach is settled.", "eli5": {"question": "A tiny, already-agreed fix must go in during a live incident. The clue: <i>The fix is agreed with the code owner: a two-line guard</i>. The shared file and last month's refactor are not about this change.", "answer": "Think of a builder told exactly which loose tile to glue back. They don't redraw the house plans first; they glue the tile and you check it. Plan mode is for redrawing plans, when there are several ways to build. A small change with a settled approach goes straight in (<b>direct execution</b>), and the diff review is your check. <b>Rule: small and settled → direct, then review. Big or several valid approaches → plan mode.</b>", "options": ["Asking about every tile in the house slows a repair that's already agreed.", "Redrawing the plans suited last month's rebuild, not one tile.", "Correct. Glue the tile, then check the work.", "Surveying the neighbours doesn't change how you glue one tile."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a shared, important file needs plan mode</b>, especially when plan mode helped there before. Plan mode fits the <i>change</i>, not the file. Last month's refactor was large, with several approaches. This fix is two agreed lines, so direct execution and a diff review fit.", "mapTitle": "How big and how settled is the change?", "map": {"head": ["The change", "Way of working", "Why"], "rows": [["Small, approach agreed", "Direct execution, then review the diff", "Nothing left to design"], ["Large, many files, several valid approaches", "Plan mode", "Explore and design before editing"], ["Unclear requirements or edge cases", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Need a summary of a big code area", "Explore subagent", "Keeps verbose discovery out of context"], ["Behaviour defined by examples or tests", "Test-driven iteration", "Failures guide each change"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The fix is agreed with the code owner: a two-line guard at the top of apply_regional_discount plus one unit test", "means": "The decider. Small and settled: <b>[[2]]</b>. Nothing is left for an interview (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The error spike is still live", "means": "Extra steps such as mapping callers (<b>[[3]]</b>) cost time now."}, {"quote": "last month a large refactor of it went well in plan mode", "means": "That was a large change. Plan mode (<b>[[1]]</b>) fits the size of the change, not the file."}], "rule": "Small, settled change → direct execution and a review. Large, or several valid approaches → plan mode.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Direct execution: simple well-scoped changes (single validation check)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s4-10", "was": "s5q-s4-10", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Command arguments", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer wants <code>/triage &lt;group-id&gt;</code> to run the team's first-response checklist. It pulls the group's latest events, finds the frames in the code and lists recent commits that touched them. Every joiner should get it on clone, and it must not be able to edit files. People usually carry on in the same conversation, asking about what it found. Which setup <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A Triage section in the project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with the checklist and a rule against edits.", "why": "Works, but it loads in every session and the no-edits line is only a request. CLAUDE.md is for standards every session needs.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A committed skill with <code>context: fork</code>, an argument-hint and read-only allowed-tools.", "why": "Works, but the findings stay in a forked context. People who want to ask about them in the same conversation lose them.", "trap": "wrong context"}, {"t": "A committed <code>.claude/commands/triage.md</code> with argument-hint and read-only allowed-tools.", "why": "Best. It is shared on clone, takes the group ID, can't edit files, and its findings stay in the conversation for follow-up."}, {"t": "A committed <code>.claude/commands/triage.md</code> whose body tells Claude not to modify files.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The no-edits rule is an instruction; allowed-tools would make it a limit.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "People usually carry on in the same conversation, asking about what it found.", "explanation": "Project commands and skills under .claude/ are shared on clone; argument-hint documents the input and allowed-tools limits capability. context: fork keeps a workflow's detail out of the main conversation, which is wrong when people want to follow up on it.", "eli5": {"question": "A shared shortcut must take an ID, never edit files, and leave its findings where people can ask about them. The clue: <i>People usually carry on in the same conversation, asking about what it found</i>.", "answer": "Think of a recipe card in the team kitchen drawer. Everyone who joins finds it there (<b>.claude/commands/</b> in the repo). The card says what to bring (<b>argument-hint</b>) and which tools you may use, knives excluded (<b>allowed-tools</b>). The dish is served at your own table, so you can ask about it. A <code>context: fork</code> skill cooks in a separate kitchen and sends back only a note. <b>Rule: findings needed in the conversation → no fork. Tool limits → allowed-tools, not a written request.</b>", "options": ["A note on the fridge that everyone reads every day, saying please don't use knives.", "Cooked in another kitchen: you get a note about the dish, not the dish to ask about.", "Correct. Shared card, set ingredients, no knives, served at your table.", "The card says please don't use knives, but the knives are still in reach."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>context: fork is the cleaner choice</b> for any workflow. It keeps a workflow's detail out of the main conversation, which is right when nobody needs it afterwards. Here people carry on asking about the findings, so they must stay in the conversation. The no-edits need is a capability limit, which is allowed-tools, not a sentence in the prompt.", "mapTitle": "Which mechanism meets each need?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Shared on clone", "<code>.claude/commands/</code> or <code>.claude/skills/</code> in the repo", "Version-controlled"], ["Takes an input", "<code>argument-hint</code>", "Shows what to pass"], ["Must not edit files", "<code>allowed-tools</code> without write tools", "A limit, not a request"], ["Detail should stay out of the main chat", "Skill with <code>context: fork</code>", "Isolated sub-agent context"], ["Standard every session needs", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Every joiner should get it on clone", "means": "It must be committed under <code>.claude/</code>."}, {"quote": "it must not be able to edit files", "means": "A capability limit: allowed-tools. A written rule (<b>[[3]]</b>, <b>[[0]]</b>) is only a request."}, {"quote": "People usually carry on in the same conversation, asking about what it found", "means": "The decider. A forked skill (<b>[[1]]</b>) keeps the findings out of the conversation. <b>[[2]]</b> keeps them in."}], "rule": "Shared workflow with an input and limits → committed command with argument-hint and allowed-tools. Use context: fork only when the detail isn't needed afterwards.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context (no pollution of main conversation)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-03", "was": "s5q-s5-03", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 --json-schema", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A security dashboard reads the review job's findings in a fixed shape: severity, file, line and description. The job runs <code>claude -p</code> with <code>--output-format json</code>. A small adapter maps whatever keys come back onto the dashboard's shape. Every few weeks a key comes back renamed, such as <code>location</code> instead of <code>file</code>. Then the adapter breaks. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Pass the dashboard's schema with <code>--json-schema</code> and remove the key-mapping adapter.", "why": "Best. The keys drift because the output shape is not constrained. A schema on the CLI run makes the output match the dashboard's shape."}, {"t": "Check each result against the schema and rerun the review with the errors added on failure.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. Retry with error feedback usually fixes a bad run, but each failure costs a second review and the shape is still unconstrained.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "List the exact field names and severity values with the review criteria in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Naming the fields makes drift rarer, but an instruction is a tendency, not a constraint.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add a contract test that fails the build when the keys in the review output change.", "why": "Works, but too late. The test turns a silent break into a loud one, but it detects drift after it happens instead of removing it.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "A small adapter maps whatever keys come back onto the dashboard's shape.", "explanation": "--output-format json gives valid JSON; --json-schema gives your JSON. When a consumer needs a fixed shape, constrain the output at generation instead of repairing it afterwards.", "eli5": {"question": "The JSON is valid, but its key names sometimes change, and the code that reads it breaks. The clue: an adapter maps <i>whatever keys come back</i>. Nothing fixes the shape at the source.", "answer": "Asking someone to “fill in a form” without giving them the form means each one looks a bit different. Hand them the printed form and every answer lands in the same box. <code>--output-format json</code> says “answer in JSON”; <code>--json-schema</code> hands over the form. <b>Rule: a consumer needs a fixed shape → give the CLI the schema.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Hand out the printed form, and stop translating answers afterwards.", "Sending back a messy answer to redo usually works, but you pay twice and the form is still missing.", "Describing the form in a note helps, but people still drift now and then.", "An alarm when a box name changes tells you fast, but doesn't stop it changing."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>JSON output is already structured</b>, so the fix belongs in the code that reads it. <code>--output-format json</code> makes the reply valid JSON, but it doesn't fix the <b>keys</b>. <code>--json-schema</code> does, at the source.", "diagram": "claude -p --output-format json   → valid JSON, keys may vary\n  + --json-schema dashboard.json  → valid JSON, your keys", "mapTitle": "CI output breaks the consumer. Where does the fix go?", "map": {"head": ["What breaks", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Reply is prose, not JSON", "No output format", "<code>--output-format json</code>"], ["JSON is valid but keys or enums drift", "No schema", "<code>--json-schema</code>"], ["Shape is right but a value is wrong", "Semantic error", "Validate and retry with the error"], ["Job hangs waiting for input", "Interactive mode", "<code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A security dashboard reads the review job's findings in a fixed shape", "means": "The consumer has a contract."}, {"quote": "The job runs claude -p with --output-format json", "means": "Valid JSON is already there; the shape is not."}, {"quote": "A small adapter maps whatever keys come back onto the dashboard's shape", "means": "The decider. The shape is repaired afterwards instead of fixed at the source: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Every few weeks a key comes back renamed", "means": "Rare drift: a prompt note (<b>[[2]]</b>) makes it rarer; a retry (<b>[[1]]</b>) or contract test (<b>[[3]]</b>) reacts to it."}], "rule": "Valid JSON is not your JSON. Pin the shape with --json-schema.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "--output-format json + --json-schema for machine-parseable findings → inline PR comments."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-04", "was": "s5q-s5-04", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Headless permissions", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A new nightly job runs a technical-debt sweep with <code>claude -p</code>. It finishes in seconds and posts an empty findings list every night. The same prompt in an engineer's local session finds dozens of issues. In the CI log, Claude's last turn asks to run a Bash search across the repository. The next line is the empty result, and the job exits without an error. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run the sweep in an interactive session each night, with an engineer on call to approve tools.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Someone could approve the search, but a nightly job that needs a person awake has lost the point of automation.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Run the sweep with the flag that skips every permission check for the whole session.", "why": "Works, but too broad. The search would run, but so would any other command, with nobody watching.", "trap": "too broad"}, {"t": "Run the search in an earlier workflow step and pass its results into the sweep's prompt.", "why": "Works, but works around it. The first search arrives, but the sweep can't run follow-up searches as it investigates.", "trap": "works around it"}, {"t": "Allow that search command for the job, with <code>--allowedTools</code> or the committed settings.", "why": "Best. A headless run has nobody to approve a tool, so an unpermitted tool is refused. Allowing exactly that command fixes the cause."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "In the CI log, Claude's last turn asks to run a Bash search across the repository.", "explanation": "A non-interactive (-p) run cannot ask anyone for approval. Every tool a CI job needs must be allowed up front, or the run quietly does nothing. Allow the narrowest set that works.", "eli5": {"question": "The nightly job always finds nothing, but the same prompt finds plenty on a laptop. The clue: the log stops right after Claude <i>asks</i> to run a search.", "answer": "Think of a night guard who must phone the manager before opening a locked room. At night nobody answers, so the room stays shut and the report is empty. Fix it by giving the guard a key to that one room ahead of time: an <b>allow rule</b> for that command. A run with <code>-p</code> is the night shift: nobody is there to say yes. <b>Rule: headless job → allow the tools it needs up front, and only those.</b>", "options": ["Keeping a manager awake every night works, but defeats the point of a night guard.", "A master key to every room opens the right one, and all the others too.", "Bringing the guard one box from the room helps, but they can't go back for more.", "Correct. A key to exactly the room the job needs."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an empty result with no error means there was nothing to find</b>. In a <code>-p</code> run, a tool that needs approval is simply refused, because nobody is there to approve it. The run then ends quickly and quietly. The fix is to <b>allow the needed tool up front</b>, as narrowly as works.", "mapTitle": "A CI run of Claude Code misbehaves. What's the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Job hangs until timeout", "Interactive mode in CI", "Run with <code>-p</code>"], ["Fast, empty result right after a tool request", "Tool not permitted; nobody to approve", "Allow that tool for the job"], ["Results differ from one engineer's laptop", "Criteria in user-level memory", "Move them to project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>"], ["Output shape varies", "No schema", "<code>--json-schema</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It finishes in seconds and posts an empty findings list every night", "means": "Too fast and too empty for a real sweep."}, {"quote": "The same prompt in an engineer's local session finds dozens of issues", "means": "The prompt is fine. Something about the CI run differs."}, {"quote": "In the CI log, Claude's last turn asks to run a Bash search across the repository", "means": "The decider. The run stops right at the tool request: a permission refusal. Allow it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "the job exits without an error", "means": "A refused tool is not a crash. A person on call (<b>[[0]]</b>) or skipping all checks (<b>[[1]]</b>) would also get past it, at a cost."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the cause, at the right size. Pre-running the search (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the sweep unable to search again."}], "rule": "A -p run can't ask for approval. Allow the tools a CI job needs up front, and no more.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "-p / --print = non-interactive mode (no input hang)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-07", "was": "s5q-s5-07", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skills for shared procedures", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The review procedure is a long prompt with criteria, severity levels and exclusions. It is pasted into the Actions workflow, a pre-push script and a wiki page, and the copies now disagree. Developers also run the review by hand inside their working Claude Code sessions. Its long file-by-file reasoning then fills the conversation they were using for the change. Where should the procedure <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A project slash command in <code>.claude/commands/</code>, run by the workflow and the script.", "why": "Works, but only half the fix. One shared, versioned file ends the drift, but run from a working session, its reasoning still fills that session.", "trap": "only half the fix"}, {"t": "A Review section in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, loaded in every session and every run.", "why": "Works, but loads everywhere. One copy ends the drift, but a long on-demand procedure would weigh on every unrelated session.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "A <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob covers the files it reviews.", "why": "Works, but wrong kind of file. The criteria would load while matching files are edited, but a review is a task you run, not a convention to load.", "trap": "wrong kind of file"}, {"t": "A project skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code> with <code>context: fork</code>, run by name.", "why": "Best. One versioned definition ends the drift, and <code>context: fork</code> runs it in an isolated sub-agent context, so the reasoning stays out of the developer's conversation."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its long file-by-file reasoning then fills the conversation they were using for the change.", "explanation": "Skills are on-demand workflows and can run isolated with context: fork. CLAUDE.md holds always-loaded standards. One versioned definition invoked by name stops copies drifting.", "eli5": {"question": "Two problems: three copies that disagree, and a review that floods the developer's chat. The clue: the long reasoning <i>fills the conversation they were using</i>.", "answer": "Think of a recipe kept in one shared cookbook (no more differing copies). Some recipes are messy, so you cook them in a side kitchen and bring back only the dish. A <b>skill</b> is the shared recipe; <code>context: fork</code> is the side kitchen, so your own counter stays clean. <b>Rule: shared on-demand procedure that is noisy → skill with <code>context: fork</code>.</b>", "options": ["One shared recipe card, but you still cook it on your own counter.", "A recipe stuck on the fridge door is in front of everyone at every meal, even when nobody is cooking it.", "A note that appears when you touch certain pans is for habits, not for a dish you choose to cook.", "Correct. One shared recipe, cooked in a side kitchen."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to see only the <b>drift</b> and reach for any shared file. There is a second problem: the review's long reasoning <b>pollutes the developer's session</b>. Only a skill with <code>context: fork</code> fixes both, because it is shared and versioned and also runs in its own isolated context.", "mapTitle": "Where does a shared instruction set live?", "map": {"head": ["What it is", "Home", "Loads when"], "rows": [["Always-on team standard", "Project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Every session"], ["Convention for a file pattern", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> + <code>paths:</code>", "Matching files are edited"], ["Short on-demand team task", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>", "Invoked; runs in the session"], ["On-demand task with noisy output", "Skill with <code>context: fork</code>", "Invoked; runs isolated"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review procedure is a long prompt with criteria, severity levels and exclusions", "means": "A procedure you run, not an always-on standard (<b>[[1]]</b>) or file convention (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "the copies now disagree", "means": "Needs one shared, versioned definition."}, {"quote": "Developers also run the review by hand inside their working Claude Code sessions", "means": "It runs inside live sessions, not only in CI."}, {"quote": "Its long file-by-file reasoning then fills the conversation they were using for the change", "means": "The decider. A command (<b>[[0]]</b>) runs in that same session; a forked skill doesn't: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "On-demand and noisy → skill with context: fork. Always-on → CLAUDE.md.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context (no pollution of main conversation)."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-08", "was": "s5q-s5-08", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Workload-appropriate config", "trap": "Non-existent or misunderstood feature", "fam": 9, "select": 1, "question": "All four CI jobs call one wrapper script. It runs <code>claude -p</code> with the same tool list and turn limit for every job. Last month the limit was raised for the autofix job, which follows call chains across many files. Since then the pull-request review often runs past its time budget. Lowering the limit again makes autofix stop halfway. What is the <b>best approach</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Split the review into per-file passes plus one cross-file pass, keeping the shared limit.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Multi-pass review sharpens attention on large diffs, but each pass still runs under the limit set for autofix.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add a timeout to the review step that stops the run and posts whatever findings exist.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. The review meets its budget, but it posts a partial review and the shared setting stays wrong.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Give each job its own <code>claude -p</code> call, with a turn limit and tool list chosen for that job.", "why": "Best. A quick blocking review and a deep autofix need opposite settings. Separate invocations let each one fit its own job."}, {"t": "Have autofix save its progress to a scratchpad file and resume next run at a lower limit.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Autofix eventually finishes, but a failing build waits several runs for a fix that one run could make.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "It runs claude -p with the same tool list and turn limit for every job.", "explanation": "A time-bound blocking check and a deep investigation need different settings; one shared configuration puts each at the wrong point. Configure each CI workload on its own.", "eli5": {"question": "Two jobs share one setting, and each needs it set differently. The clue: <i>the same tool list and turn limit for every job</i>.", "answer": "One alarm clock for the whole house doesn't work when one person catches a 6 a.m. train and another works nights. Give each their own alarm. Here that means each CI job gets its own <code>claude -p</code> call with its own limits. <b>Rule: different workloads → different settings, not one shared compromise.</b>", "options": ["Splitting the morning routine into steps doesn't change when the shared alarm rings.", "Pulling the commuter out of bed half-dressed gets them out on time, but not ready.", "Correct. One alarm per person.", "Letting the night worker finish tomorrow works, but the job waits a day longer."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to tune <b>the one shared setting</b> until both jobs are happy. They want opposite things: the review must be quick, and autofix must go deep. No single limit fits both. Give each workload <b>its own configuration</b>, just as the exam guide splits a blocking pre-merge check from an overnight report.", "mapTitle": "CI jobs pull a shared setting in different directions. What now?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "Needs", "Setting"], "rows": [["Blocking pull-request review", "Fast, bounded", "Low turn limit, review tools, output schema"], ["Autofix for failing builds", "Deep investigation", "Higher turn limit, edit tools"], ["Weekly or nightly report", "Cheap, nobody waits", "Message Batches API (single-turn)"], ["Very long exploration", "Survives across runs", "Scratchpad file and resume"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It runs claude -p with the same tool list and turn limit for every job", "means": "The decider. One setting for jobs with different needs. Split it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Last month the limit was raised for the autofix job, which follows call chains across many files", "means": "Autofix needs depth."}, {"quote": "Since then the pull-request review often runs past its time budget", "means": "The review needs speed. A timeout (<b>[[1]]</b>) or multi-pass split (<b>[[0]]</b>) works around the shared limit."}, {"quote": "Lowering the limit again makes autofix stop halfway", "means": "Resuming over runs (<b>[[3]]</b>) makes the build wait longer for its fix."}], "rule": "Different CI workloads get different claude -p settings. Don't share one compromise.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Batch for the overnight report only; keep real-time for pre-merge"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-01", "was": "s2-01", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1/3.3 Memory hierarchy & path rules", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> has grown very long. It holds server conventions, mobile conventions, testing conventions and a security checklist. Engineers say Claude quotes mobile conventions while it edits server code. The testing conventions apply to every test file, and test files sit next to their sources in all three folders. The security checklist applies everywhere. How should the team <b>restructure</b> it?", "options": [{"t": "Give <code>server/</code>, <code>mobile/</code> and <code>packages/shared/</code> each a <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, and keep shared items at the root.", "why": "Works, but tied to folders. Folder files would stop mobile rules leaking into server work, but the testing conventions cover test files in all three folders, so they must be copied three times.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Keep the checklist in the root file, and move area and testing rules into <code>.claude/rules/</code> with globs.", "why": "Best. Universal items stay always loaded. Path-scoped rules load only for matching files, and a glob like <code>**/*.test.ts</code> follows test files into every folder."}, {"t": "Split each section into its own file under <code>docs/claude/</code>, and <code>@import</code> every one from the root file.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. Imports make the file tidy and modular, but every imported file still loads in every session, so mobile rules still reach server work.", "trap": "still loads everywhere"}, {"t": "Turn each area's conventions into a skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code> that engineers invoke before working there.", "why": "Works, but only when remembered. A skill keeps the conventions out of other work, but it loads only when invoked, and conventions should apply while editing without anyone asking.", "trap": "only when remembered"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The testing conventions apply to every test file, and test files sit next to their sources in all three folders.", "explanation": "Universal standards stay in the root CLAUDE.md. Conventions that apply to some files go in .claude/rules/ with paths: globs, which load only for matching files and can follow a file type across folders.", "eli5": {"question": "One huge rule book loads for everything, so Claude reads mobile rules while doing server work. The clue: the testing rules apply to test files that sit in <i>all three folders</i>.", "answer": "Think of a school with one giant noticeboard at the gate. Everyone reads every notice, even the ones for other classes. Better: keep only school-wide notices at the gate (the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>), and give other notices a label that says who they are for, wherever those people are (<code>.claude/rules/</code> files with a <code>paths:</code> pattern). <b>Rule: applies everywhere → root CLAUDE.md. Applies to a kind of file → path-scoped rule.</b>", "options": ["A noticeboard in each classroom helps, but the test notice then has to be pinned up three times.", "Correct. School-wide notices at the gate, and labelled notices that find the right readers in any room.", "Stapling the notices into one booklet is tidier, but everyone still reads the whole booklet.", "Notices that people must ask for get skipped by people who forget to ask."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>each folder needs its own CLAUDE.md</b>, since the complaint is about server and mobile rules mixing. That fixes the area rules. But the testing conventions follow a <i>kind of file</i>, and test files sit in every folder. A <code>paths:</code> glob in <code>.claude/rules/</code> matches files by pattern, wherever they are.", "diagram": "CLAUDE.md (root)        security checklist, always\n.claude/rules/server.md paths: server/**\n.claude/rules/mobile.md paths: mobile/**\n.claude/rules/tests.md  paths: **/*.test.ts", "mapTitle": "Who needs this instruction, and when?", "map": {"head": ["Instruction applies to", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Every file, every session", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded, shared through git"], ["A file pattern spread over folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with a <code>paths:</code> glob", "Loads for matching files anywhere"], ["Everything in one folder, nothing outside", "That folder's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loads when working in that folder"], ["Same as today, just tidier", "<code>@import</code> from the root", "Modular, but still always loaded"], ["A task someone runs on request", "A skill", "Loads only when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Engineers say Claude quotes mobile conventions while it edits server code", "means": "Everything loads everywhere. An <code>@import</code> split (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps that."}, {"quote": "The testing conventions apply to every test file, and test files sit next to their sources in all three folders", "means": "The decider. Folder files (<b>[[0]]</b>) would need three copies; a glob follows the files."}, {"quote": "The security checklist applies everywhere", "means": "One item really is universal, so it stays in the root file."}, {"quote": "How should the team restructure it?", "means": "Conventions should load by themselves while editing, not on request (<b>[[3]]</b>). Root for universal, path rules for the rest: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Universal standards in the root CLAUDE.md; conventions for a file pattern across folders in .claude/rules/ with paths globs.", "guide": {"obj": "3.3 Path-specific rules for conditional convention loading", "quote": "Glob-pattern rules beat directory CLAUDE.md for conventions spanning directories"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-02", "was": "s2-02", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Skills vs commands vs memory", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A long, step-by-step procedure for moving an old endpoint onto the new router sits pasted in the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. A few engineers run it each week. One step scans every handler in <code>server/</code> and prints a long list that the rest of the session never needs. The team wants the procedure to run on request, with read, edit and test tools only. Where should it <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "As a project command in <code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed to the repository and run by name.", "why": "Works, but leaves the noise in. A shared command runs on request, but the scan's long list still lands in the main conversation, and nothing limits its tools.", "trap": "leaves the noise in"}, {"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches handler files in <code>server/</code>.", "why": "Works, but wrong trigger. A path rule would show the steps while handlers are edited, but it loads on every handler edit instead of when someone asks for the procedure.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a separate markdown file that the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> pulls in with an <code>@import</code> line.", "why": "Works, but still loads everywhere. The procedure gets its own file, but an imported file loads into every session, so the long text stays always on.", "trap": "still loads everywhere"}, {"t": "As a skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code> whose <code>SKILL.md</code> sets <code>context: fork</code> and <code>allowed-tools</code>.", "why": "Best. A skill runs on demand. <code>context: fork</code> keeps the scan output in its own sub-agent context, and <code>allowed-tools</code> limits it to read, edit and test."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "One step scans every handler in server/ and prints a long list that the rest of the session never needs.", "explanation": "An on-demand procedure belongs in a skill, not in always-loaded memory. context: fork isolates its verbose output from the main conversation, and allowed-tools restricts what it may use.", "eli5": {"question": "A long recipe sits in the always-loaded file, and one of its steps prints a huge list nobody needs afterwards. The clue: <i>run on request</i>, <i>keep the long list out</i>, <i>limit the tools</i>.", "answer": "Picture a specialist you call in for one job. They work in their own room, use only the tools you hand them, and come back with the result, not their pile of scrap paper. That is a <b>skill</b> with <code>context: fork</code> (its own room) and <code>allowed-tools</code> (the tools you hand over). <b>Rule: on-demand procedure with noisy steps → skill with context: fork; limit tools with allowed-tools.</b>", "options": ["A shared recipe card runs on request, but the specialist works at your desk and leaves the scrap paper there.", "A note that pops up whenever you touch a handler appears at the wrong moments, not when you ask for the job.", "Moving the recipe to its own page that is stapled into the handbook still means everyone reads it every day.", "Correct. The specialist works in their own room with only the tools you hand over."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a command and a skill are the same thing</b>: both run on request and both are shared through git. The difference here is the noisy scan and the tool limit. A skill's <code>SKILL.md</code> can set <code>context: fork</code>, which runs it in an isolated sub-agent context, and <code>allowed-tools</code>, which restricts what it can use.", "diagram": "main session ──/migrate──▶ skill (context: fork)\n                            scans 100s of handlers\n             ◀── result ── (scan stays in fork)", "mapTitle": "Where does a team procedure live?", "map": {"head": ["What it needs", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["On request, noisy steps, tool limits", "Skill with <code>context: fork</code> + <code>allowed-tools</code>", "Isolated context, restricted tools"], ["On request, small output, shared", "Command in <code>.claude/commands/</code>", "Simple, invoked by name"], ["A convention for certain files", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads when matching files are edited"], ["A standard for every session", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded"], ["Tidy a long always-on file", "<code>@import</code>", "Modular, still always loaded"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "sits pasted in the root CLAUDE.md", "means": "Today it loads in every session. An <code>@import</code> (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps that."}, {"quote": "One step scans every handler in server/ and prints a long list that the rest of the session never needs", "means": "The decider. A plain command (<b>[[0]]</b>) would put that list in the main conversation; <code>context: fork</code> keeps it out."}, {"quote": "The team wants the procedure to run on request, with read, edit and test tools only", "means": "On request, not on file edits (<b>[[1]]</b>), and a tool limit: <code>allowed-tools</code> in a skill, <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "On-demand procedure with verbose steps or tool limits → skill with context: fork and allowed-tools.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "context: fork runs a skill in an isolated sub-agent context (no pollution of main conversation)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-03", "was": "s2-03", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A ticket asks for soft delete on orders: a <code>deleted_at</code> column, plus filtering at dozens of query sites in <code>server/</code>. The ticket thread suggests three designs: filter in the base repository, add a check at each query, or read from a filtered view. The reporting module reads deleted rows on purpose, and each design treats it differently. The engineer wants to avoid redoing the edits. How should they <b>start</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Work in direct execution one module at a time, and run the existing tests after each module.", "why": "Works, but too early. Small tested steps are right once the design is chosen. Here three designs are still open, so the edits may have to be redone.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Have Claude write failing soft-delete tests for the query sites first, then edit until they pass.", "why": "Works, but decides too soon. Tests first is a good habit, but what the tests expect depends on which design is picked, and that is still open.", "trap": "decides too soon"}, {"t": "Use plan mode: have Claude map the query code, weigh the three designs and get approval first.", "why": "Best. Many files, several valid designs and a trade-off for the reporting module are what plan mode is for: explore and decide before any edit."}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to list every query site, then apply the base-repository filter to them.", "why": "Works, but skips the decision. The list is useful, but this picks one design without weighing it, and a base filter hides the deleted rows reporting needs.", "trap": "skips the decision"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The ticket thread suggests three designs: filter in the base repository, add a check at each query, or read from a filtered view.", "explanation": "Plan mode fits large changes with several valid approaches and architectural trade-offs. It explores and designs before committing, which prevents rework. Direct execution fits once the approach is settled.", "eli5": {"question": "A change touches dozens of places, and there are three ways to do it. One module needs the deleted rows, and each way treats it differently. The clue: <i>three designs</i> are still on the table.", "answer": "Before re-plumbing a whole building, a plumber walks every floor and agrees the plan with the owner. Starting on one floor with a guess means ripping pipes out later. Walking and agreeing first is <b>plan mode</b>: Claude maps the code, compares the designs and waits for approval before editing. <b>Rule: several valid designs + many files → plan mode first.</b>", "options": ["Floor by floor with checks is good once the plan is agreed. It isn't yet.", "Writing the inspection list first is good, but the list depends on which plan you pick.", "Correct. Walk the building, compare the three plans, agree, then start.", "A helper's map of every pipe is useful, but jumping to one plan unchecked breaks the floor that needs the old water."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>good tests make it safe to just start</b>. Tests catch regressions in an approach you have chosen. They don't choose the approach. Here three designs are open and each treats the reporting module differently, so the first job is a design decision. Plan mode explores and designs before committing, which prevents rework.", "mapTitle": "How should Claude start this change?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best start", "Why"], "rows": [["Many files, several valid designs", "Plan mode", "Decide before editing"], ["Design settled, change across modules", "Direct execution, tests after each step", "Nothing left to design"], ["Behaviour settled, needs pinning down", "Tests first, then iterate", "Tests state the target"], ["Discovery output would flood context", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary"], ["One small well-scoped fix", "Direct execution", "Planning adds nothing"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "plus filtering at dozens of query sites in server/", "means": "A multi-file change, one sign of plan mode."}, {"quote": "The ticket thread suggests three designs", "means": "The decider. The approach is open, so module-by-module edits (<b>[[0]]</b>) or tests (<b>[[1]]</b>) would assume an answer."}, {"quote": "The reporting module reads deleted rows on purpose, and each design treats it differently", "means": "A real trade-off. Picking the base filter unweighed (<b>[[3]]</b>) would hide those rows."}, {"quote": "The engineer wants to avoid redoing the edits", "means": "Decide first, edit once: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Several valid designs across many files → plan mode before any edits.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Plan mode enables safe exploration/design before committing → prevents rework"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-04", "was": "s2-04", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "An engineer has rewritten a prose spec for a log-redaction function four times. Each run, Claude builds it differently. Sometimes it masks the whole object that holds a secret, and sometimes only the value. Nested arrays and values that are already masked come out differently each time. The engineer has a firm answer for every case. They also want later changes checked against those answers. Which <b>TWO</b> changes fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Show Claude two or three before-and-after log records, with a nested array and a masked value.", "why": "Best. Concrete input and output pairs show the transformation directly and settle the cases that prose leaves open."}, {"t": "Ask Claude to interview the engineer about the unclear cases before it writes any more code.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviewing draws out requirements nobody has settled. The engineer already has every answer; the gap is in showing them to Claude.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Write failing tests for the disputed cases first, share the failures, and iterate until they pass.", "why": "Best. Tests state each answer exactly, give Claude concrete feedback, and keep checking later changes."}, {"t": "Expand the prose spec with exact definitions for each disputed case and a glossary of its key terms.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Sharper prose helps a little, but four prose rewrites have already failed to pin the behaviour down.", "trap": "more of the same"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The engineer has a firm answer for every case.", "explanation": "Concrete input/output examples communicate a transformation better than prose, and test-driven iteration states the target objectively and keeps checking it. The interview pattern is for requirements that are not yet settled.", "eli5": {"question": "Four rewrites of a written spec, and Claude still builds the function differently each time. The engineer knows every answer. The clue: the answers are <i>known</i> but the <i>prose</i> isn't getting them across, and later changes must be <i>checked</i>.", "answer": "Explaining a haircut in words rarely works; showing a before-and-after photo does. And a checklist you tick at the end tells you whether the cut matches. For code, the photos are <b>input/output examples</b> and the checklist is <b>failing tests</b> that Claude iterates against, which keep checking later changes too. <b>Rule: prose keeps failing → show examples and write tests first.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Before-and-after photos show exactly what you want.", "Asking questions helps when you don't know what you want. Here you already do.", "Correct. A checklist that tells you, now and later, whether the result matches.", "A longer written description is the thing that has already failed four times."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>unclear output means the requirements are unclear</b>, so Claude should ask questions. Here the engineer has every answer; prose is the weak channel. The exam guide's tools for this are concrete input/output examples, which beat prose for transformations, and test-driven iteration, which also keeps checking later changes.", "diagram": "{user:{token:\"abc\"}}  →  {user:{token:\"***\"}}\n[{key:\"x\"},{key:\"y\"}]  →  [{key:\"***\"},{key:\"***\"}]\n\"***\"                  →  \"***\" (not re-masked)", "mapTitle": "Claude keeps building it differently. Which lever?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Lever", "Why"], "rows": [["Answers known, prose fails to convey them", "Input/output examples", "Shows the transformation"], ["Answers known, must stay checked later", "Tests first, share failures", "Objective and repeatable"], ["Answers not yet decided", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Prose is vague on terms", "Sharper definitions", "Helps only if wording is the gap"], ["Several problems that interact", "One message with all of them", "Fixes depend on each other"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer has rewritten a prose spec for a log-redaction function four times", "means": "More prose (<b>[[3]]</b>) is the lever that has already failed."}, {"quote": "Nested arrays and values that are already masked come out differently each time", "means": "Specific cases that a before-and-after example settles directly (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The engineer has a firm answer for every case", "means": "The decider. Nothing is left to discover, so an interview (<b>[[1]]</b>) asks for what is already known."}, {"quote": "They also want later changes checked against those answers", "means": "Tests keep checking: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Prose fails to convey a known transformation → input/output examples plus tests written first.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Concrete input/output examples communicate transformations better than prose"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-05", "was": "s2-05", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Command scoping", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team wants an <code>/rfc</code> command that drafts a design record. It must work in this repository and in a second one the team owns. Many people who open these repositories are contractors on their own laptops. They never run the internal setup script. A few engineers want a personal version that also drafts a Slack announcement. Which arrangement <b>meets every need</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep one copy in <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>, installed on each person's laptop by the setup script.", "why": "Works, but misses people. One copy avoids duplication and reaches everyone who runs the script. Contractors never run it, so they don't get the command.", "trap": "misses people"}, {"t": "Describe the RFC steps in each repository's root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, with the Slack step marked optional.", "why": "Works, but wrong kind of file. Claude would follow the steps when asked, but memory loads in every session and there is no <code>/rfc</code> to run.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "Commit it to <code>.claude/commands/</code> in both repositories, with the Slack step behind an argument.", "why": "Works, but wrong scope for the extra. Everyone gets the command, but a personal habit ends up in the team's shared file for everyone to maintain.", "trap": "wrong scope"}, {"t": "Commit it in each repo's <code>.claude/commands/</code>, plus a renamed variant in <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.", "why": "Best. Project commands arrive with every clone, contractors included. The personal variant lives at user level under its own name."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "They never run the internal setup script.", "explanation": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ are version-controlled and arrive with every clone. Personal variants belong in ~/.claude/commands/ under a separate name.", "eli5": {"question": "A team command must reach everyone in two repositories, including contractors who skip the setup script. A few people want a personal extra. The clue: contractors <i>never run the setup script</i>.", "answer": "Tools left in the workshop are there for anyone who walks in. Tools handed out at orientation only reach people who come to orientation. The workshop is <code>.claude/commands/</code> in each repository: it arrives with every clone. Your own toolbox is <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>: right for a personal variant. <b>Rule: everyone who clones needs it → project command. Only me → user command.</b>", "options": ["Handing tools out at orientation misses everyone who skips orientation.", "A note describing the steps on the wall is read every day, but it isn't a tool you can pick up.", "Putting your personal gadget on the shared bench works, but now the whole team looks after it.", "Correct. Shared tools in each workshop, your own variant in your own toolbox."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>one central copy is always cleaner</b> than a copy per repository. A user-level copy is central, but it only reaches machines where someone installs it. Committed project commands travel with the code, so they reach anyone who clones, including contractors. Personal extras stay personal at user level.", "diagram": "repo-A/.claude/commands/rfc.md  → everyone who clones A\nrepo-B/.claude/commands/rfc.md  → everyone who clones B\n~/.claude/commands/rfc-slack.md → just me, everywhere", "mapTitle": "Who needs the command, and how does it reach them?", "map": {"head": ["Who needs it", "Best home", "How it arrives"], "rows": [["Everyone who clones a repository", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed", "With the code, on clone or pull"], ["Only me, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Already on my machine"], ["Everyone, if setup is always run", "User-level copy installed by a script", "Only where the script ran"], ["A standard to always follow", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loaded every session, not invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It must work in this repository and in a second one the team owns", "means": "Two repositories, so it is committed to each."}, {"quote": "Many people who open these repositories are contractors on their own laptops", "means": "Reaching people's machines is the hard part."}, {"quote": "They never run the internal setup script", "means": "The decider. A script-installed user copy (<b>[[0]]</b>) misses them; a committed command doesn't."}, {"quote": "A few engineers want a personal version that also drafts a Slack announcement", "means": "Personal, so user level, not a flag in the team file (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a memory section (<b>[[1]]</b>). Both needs together: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team command → committed .claude/commands/ in each repo; personal variant → ~/.claude/commands/ under its own name.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ (shared) vs user ~/.claude/commands/ (personal)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-06", "was": "s2-06", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 Memory precedence & /memory", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer prefers a naming style the team has not adopted. They added it to <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, which a dotfiles repository links into place. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> states a different style. A rule file for <code>mobile/</code> screens states a third. While editing a mobile screen, Claude ignores the engineer's style. What is the <b>most useful first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move the engineer's style into the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, replacing the entry it conflicts with.", "why": "Works, but wrong scope. Claude would follow it, but the team has not adopted this style, and nobody yet knows whether the personal file even loads.", "trap": "wrong scope"}, {"t": "Run <code>/memory</code> in that session to see which files loaded, then compare each file's naming style.", "why": "Best. <code>/memory</code> shows which memory files are actually in context. That settles whether the linked user file loads before anyone argues about conflicts."}, {"t": "Restate the preferred style in the opening prompt of each session that works on mobile code.", "why": "Works, but skips the diagnosis. Claude will usually follow a fresh instruction, but nobody learns why the memory file had no effect.", "trap": "skips the diagnosis"}, {"t": "Replace the dotfiles link with a real copy of the file in <code>~/.claude/</code>, then restart.", "why": "Works, but guesses the cause. It might help, but it changes the setup before checking what loads, and there are two other conflicting files to explain.", "trap": "guesses the cause"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "While editing a mobile screen, Claude ignores the engineer's style.", "explanation": "/memory lists the memory files a session has loaded. Check what is in context before changing files or reasoning about precedence.", "eli5": {"question": "Three files say three different things about naming, and Claude ignores the engineer's personal one. The clue: nobody has checked which files Claude actually <i>has</i>.", "answer": "Before arguing about which note on the fridge wins, look at what is actually on the fridge. Maybe your note never got stuck up. <code>/memory</code> is that look: it lists the memory files loaded in this session. Then you compare what they say. <b>Rule: instructions seem ignored → run /memory first to see what is loaded.</b>", "options": ["Putting your note in the family rules makes everyone follow it, and the family never agreed.", "Correct. Look at the fridge first, then compare the notes.", "Saying it out loud every morning works, but you never find out what happened to your note.", "Re-sticking your note might help, but you haven't checked yet whether it fell off."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to jump to <b>which file wins</b> when three files disagree. That question only matters once you know all three are loaded. The personal file arrives through a link, so it may not be loading at all. The exam guide's tool for this is <code>/memory</code>, which verifies which memory files are loaded.", "diagram": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  (dotfiles link)  loaded?  ← check\nroot CLAUDE.md                        style B\n.claude/rules/ mobile screens         style C", "mapTitle": "Claude ignores an instruction. What do you do first?", "map": {"head": ["What you know", "First step", "Why"], "rows": [["Unsure which memory files load", "Run <code>/memory</code>", "Shows what is in context"], ["File loads; it is a team standard", "Put it in project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Shared through git"], ["File loads; it is personal", "Keep it in <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Not shared with the team"], ["File not loading", "Fix the file or its location", "Now the cause is known"], ["Needed once, right now", "Say it in the prompt", "Quick, but fixes nothing lasting"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer prefers a naming style the team has not adopted", "means": "Personal, so moving it into the shared root file (<b>[[0]]</b>) is the wrong scope."}, {"quote": "which a dotfiles repository links into place", "means": "A possible reason it isn't loading, but only a guess until checked (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The root CLAUDE.md states a different style. A rule file for mobile/ screens states a third", "means": "Three sources. Which of them are in context?"}, {"quote": "What is the most useful first step?", "means": "Find out before changing anything. Restating in each prompt (<b>[[2]]</b>) hides the question. Check what loaded: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Instruction seems ignored → /memory first to see which memory files are loaded.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "/memory command verifies which memory files are loaded"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-08", "was": "m4-s4-08", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.x Tool permissions", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The repo also holds the Terraform for production, under <code>infra/</code>. The team lead's limits: Claude may read anything and edit app code without asking. It must never change a file under infra/ or run <code>terraform apply</code>. The limits depend only on paths and commands. They must reach every teammate on clone. What is the <b>simplest setup</b> that enforces them?", "options": [{"t": "Add a root CLAUDE.md section saying infra/ is read-only and Terraform is off-limits.", "why": "Works, but only usually. CLAUDE.md is context Claude usually follows. A must-never rule needs enforcement.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Commit deny rules in <code>.claude/settings.json</code> for edits under infra/ and terraform apply.", "why": "Best. Permission deny rules block paths and commands every time, and a committed project settings file reaches everyone on clone."}, {"t": "Commit a PreToolUse hook script that checks each Edit, Write and Bash call against the rules.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A hook enforces too, and fits rules about what a change contains. For plain paths and commands, deny rules do it with no code.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Put the same deny rules in each engineer's own <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>.", "why": "Works, but not shared. User settings don't travel with a clone, so new teammates start without the rules.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The limits depend only on paths and commands.", "explanation": "Rules that must hold every time need enforcement, not instructions. When they depend only on paths and commands, committed permission deny rules are the simplest enforcement.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude must never touch production Terraform. The clue is: <b>the limits depend only on paths and commands</b>, and they must reach everyone on clone.", "answer": "A sign saying 'staff only' is usually obeyed (<b>CLAUDE.md</b>). A locked door is always obeyed (<b>permission deny rules</b>). A guard who inspects every bag (<b>a hook</b>) also works, but you only need one when the rule depends on what's in the bag. Here the rule is just 'this door, this command', so a lock is enough. Putting it in the project file shares it with everyone. <b>Rule: path or command limit → committed deny rule; content-dependent rule → hook.</b>", "options": ["A sign on the door. Most people obey it; this must hold every time.", "Correct. A lock on the door, fitted in the building everyone shares.", "A guard checking every bag works, but nobody needs to look inside the bags here.", "Each person fits their own lock. Newcomers arrive without one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a hook is the only real enforcement</b>. Permission rules enforce too: they block named paths and commands every time, with no code. A hook earns its place when the decision depends on <b>what</b> a call contains. Where the rule lives also matters: project settings are shared, user settings are not.", "mapTitle": "How should this limit be enforced?", "map": {"head": ["Kind of rule", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Block certain paths or commands, every time", "Deny rules in <code>.claude/settings.json</code>", "Declarative, shared on clone"], ["Block based on what a call contains", "<code>PreToolUse</code> hook", "Code can inspect the content"], ["A preference or convention", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Context, usually followed"], ["A limit only for you", "<code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["A rare risky step a person should judge", "Ask for approval", "Human in the loop"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It must never change a file under infra/ or run terraform apply", "means": "A must-never rule needs enforcement, not a CLAUDE.md note (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The limits depend only on paths and commands", "means": "The decider. Deny rules cover this without a hook script (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "They must reach every teammate on clone", "means": "So the rules go in the committed project file, not user settings (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "What is the simplest setup that enforces them?", "means": "Committed deny rules: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Must-never limit on paths or commands → committed permission deny rules. Content-dependent → hook.", "guide": {"obj": "1.4 Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns", "quote": "Programmatic enforcement (hooks, prerequisite gates) vs prompt-based guidance"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s4-09", "was": "m4-s4-09", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Plan mode + Explore delegation", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer must work out how certificate PDFs flow from course completion to the learner's inbox, then propose a refactor. The team lead wants at least two designs to choose from. The flow spans about thirty files in four engines. Old export jobs and dead feature flags share its method names, so searches return many false hits. Which approach <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Enter plan mode and run the searches and file reads directly in the main session.", "why": "Works, but crowds the context. Plan mode is right, but the many false search hits would fill the session the designs are built in.", "trap": "crowds the context"}, {"t": "Have an Explore subagent map the flow, then refactor directly from its summary.", "why": "Works, but skips the design step. Discovery is handled well, but the lead asked for designs to compare first.", "trap": "skips the design"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode, have an Explore subagent do discovery, then draft designs.", "why": "Best. Plan mode suits work with several valid designs. The Explore subagent keeps noisy discovery out of the main context and returns a summary."}, {"t": "Start direct execution on the PDF step, and switch to plan mode as it grows.", "why": "Works, but too late. The size and the need for designs are known now, so starting by editing risks rework.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Old export jobs and dead feature flags share its method names, so searches return many false hits.", "explanation": "Plan mode is for architectural work with several valid approaches. An Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery and returns a summary.", "eli5": {"question": "Two needs at once: compare designs, and dig through noisy code. The clues are: <b>two designs to choose from</b>, and <b>searches return many false hits</b>.", "answer": "Planning a kitchen rebuild: first you sketch options on paper (<b>plan mode</b>). Someone else measures the room and clears the junk, and hands you a clean floor plan (<b>an Explore subagent</b>). You sketch from that plan, not from a pile of junk. <b>Rule: several designs → plan mode. Noisy discovery → Explore subagent.</b>", "options": ["Sketching in the middle of the junk works, but the junk fills your table.", "A clean floor plan, but you start building without comparing designs.", "Correct. Clean floor plan from a helper, then sketch the options.", "Starting to build and planning later means undoing work."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>plan mode alone handles exploration</b>. Plan mode decides <i>how</i> you work: explore and design before editing. It doesn't keep noisy search output out of the context. An <b>Explore subagent</b> does that, returning only a summary.", "diagram": "main session (plan mode) ── asks ──▶ Explore subagent\n      ▲                                 │ many noisy hits\n      └──────── summary of the flow ◀───┘", "mapTitle": "What does this task need?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Approach", "Why"], "rows": [["Several valid designs, many files", "Plan mode", "Design before editing, avoid rework"], ["Noisy, verbose discovery", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary, keeps the context clean"], ["Plan mode with small, clean discovery", "Read directly in the main session", "Little noise to isolate"], ["Simple, well-scoped change", "Direct execution", "Nothing to design"], ["Requirements only a person knows", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team lead wants at least two designs to choose from", "means": "Design before editing, so plan mode. Refactoring straight away (<b>[[1]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) skips it."}, {"quote": "The flow spans about thirty files in four engines", "means": "A large, multi-file change, which is plan-mode work from the start."}, {"quote": "Old export jobs and dead feature flags share its method names, so searches return many false hits", "means": "The decider. Noisy discovery would crowd the main session (<b>[[0]]</b>), so hand it to an Explore subagent."}, {"quote": "Which approach fits best?", "means": "Plan mode plus an Explore subagent: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Designs to compare → plan mode. Noisy discovery → Explore subagent, summary back.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output, returns summaries"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-01", "was": "s5-01", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Non-interactive CI invocation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The review job runs <code>claude \"Check this diff for injection risks\"</code> in a workflow step. The engineer tested it in a terminal, where she read the answer and typed <code>/exit</code>. On the runner, the step sits idle until the job's time limit cancels it. The next step, which turns the reply into comments, then has nothing to read. What should the review step <b>run instead</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Wrap the command in an <code>expect</code> script that sends <code>/exit</code> once the answer appears.", "why": "Works, but a workaround. It fakes the person at the keyboard, and it must guess when the answer is finished. The CLI already has a mode that needs no person.", "trap": "workaround"}, {"t": "Set a short time limit on the step so a stuck run fails within minutes, not hours.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. The job fails sooner and wastes fewer runner hours, but it still produces no review.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Run <code>claude -p</code> with the same prompt, and have the next step read its standard output.", "why": "Best. <code>-p</code> (<code>--print</code>) is non-interactive mode: Claude answers once, prints the reply and exits, so nothing waits for a person."}, {"t": "Replace the step with a script that sends the diff to the Messages API and prints the reply.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A direct API call does finish, but it rebuilds the job by hand and gives up the Claude Code setup the repository already commits.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The engineer tested it in a terminal, where she read the answer and typed /exit.", "explanation": "In CI, Claude Code runs in print mode (-p / --print): one prompt in, one answer out, then exit. Without it the CLI waits for a person to type the next turn.", "eli5": {"question": "A command that worked in a terminal hangs forever in CI. The clue: in the terminal, a person <i>typed /exit</i> to finish it. On the runner, nobody is there to type.", "answer": "Think of a phone call that only ends when someone hangs up. In a terminal, the engineer hung up by typing <code>/exit</code>. In CI nobody is on the line, so the call stays open. Claude Code has a “leave a message” mode instead: <code>claude -p</code> (print mode) answers once, prints the reply and hangs up by itself. <b>Rule: Claude Code in a script or CI → always use -p.</b>", "options": ["A robot that hangs up for you works, but it has to guess when the other side is done talking.", "Cutting the call off sooner saves time, but you still get no message.", "Correct. Leave-a-message mode: answer once, then hang up by itself.", "Writing your own phone works, but you already own one with the right button."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a hang in CI needs a timeout or a trick to end the session</b>. The real cause is simpler: plain <code>claude \"...\"</code> starts an <i>interactive</i> session that waits for the next turn. The supported answer is <b>print mode</b>, <code>-p</code>, which is built for scripts.", "diagram": "claude \"...\"     → answers, then waits for input (hang)\nclaude -p \"...\"  → answers, prints, exits (CI-safe)", "mapTitle": "Claude Code in CI misbehaves. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Step hangs until the job is cancelled", "Interactive session waiting for input", "<code>claude -p</code>"], ["Step finishes, script can't parse the reply", "Free-text output", "<code>--output-format json</code> + <code>--json-schema</code>"], ["CI ignores team conventions", "Context not in the repository", "Project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>"], ["Review misses problems in its own code", "Same session wrote and reviews", "Independent review instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The engineer tested it in a terminal, where she read the answer and typed /exit", "means": "The decider. The command is interactive: it only ends when a person ends it. An <code>expect</code> script (<b>[[0]]</b>) fakes that person instead of not needing one."}, {"quote": "On the runner, the step sits idle until the job's time limit cancels it", "means": "Nobody types the next turn on the runner. A shorter timeout (<b>[[1]]</b>) only makes the failure quicker."}, {"quote": "The next step, which turns the reply into comments, then has nothing to read", "means": "The empty input is a symptom of the hang, not a separate problem."}, {"quote": "What should the review step run instead?", "means": "Keep Claude Code and its committed setup; switch it to print mode: <b>[[2]]</b>. Rebuilding on the raw API (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more work than the fix needs."}], "rule": "Claude Code in a pipeline → claude -p. It answers once and exits.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "-p / --print = non-interactive mode (no input hang)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-02", "was": "s5-02", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Machine-parseable output", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The review job runs <code>claude -p</code>, and its findings come back as markdown. A Python script uses regular expressions to pull out the file, line and message for each inline comment. Some comments land on the wrong line. On some runs nothing posts, because a heading came back in a style the patterns don't expect. Only the script reads this output. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add two sample findings to the prompt, in the exact heading and bullet style the patterns expect.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples make the layout steadier, but the script still parses prose, and the next unexpected shape breaks it again.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add <code>--output-format json</code> and a <code>--json-schema</code> with file, line, severity and message.", "why": "Best. A program is the only reader, so it should get named fields, not prose to re-parse. The schema makes file and line values instead of patterns to guess."}, {"t": "Make the patterns accept more heading and list styles, with a test for each failure seen so far.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. Each fix covers one shape the script has already met, and the next new shape fails again.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Review each changed file in its own run, so the script knows the file and parses only line and message.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Per-file runs help attention on big diffs, but line and message are still pulled out of free text.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Only the script reads this output.", "explanation": "When a program consumes Claude Code output in CI, declare its shape: --output-format json with --json-schema gives the posting script fields to read instead of prose to parse.", "eli5": {"question": "A script reads Claude's markdown with patterns and keeps breaking. The clue: <i>only the script reads this output</i>, so nobody needs nice prose.", "answer": "Asking someone to write a letter you then cut up with scissors is fragile. Handing them a form with labelled boxes is not. For Claude Code the form is <code>--output-format json</code> plus a <code>--json-schema</code> naming the boxes: file, line, severity, message. <b>Rule: a program reads it → structured output from the CLI, not prose.</b>", "options": ["A sample letter helps the writer, but you are still cutting up letters.", "Correct. A form with labelled boxes the script can read directly.", "Sharper scissors cut the letters you've seen; the next odd letter still tears.", "One letter per file is shorter, but it is still a letter to cut up."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>parsing failures mean the prose needs to be more regular</b>, through examples or better patterns. That is still parsing prose. When a program is the only reader, the CLI can hand it <b>structured output</b>: JSON whose fields a schema fixes.", "diagram": "claude -p ... --output-format json --json-schema f.json\n  → [{\"file\":..., \"line\":..., \"message\":...}]", "mapTitle": "Who reads the output, and what do they need?", "map": {"head": ["Reader and need", "Mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["A script needs named fields", "<code>--output-format json</code> + <code>--json-schema</code>", "Fields, not prose"], ["A person reads it; layout varies", "Format examples in the prompt", "Steady enough for people"], ["Large diff, uneven attention", "Per-file passes + integration pass", "Focus, not format"], ["Run hangs waiting for input", "<code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code>", "Non-interactive mode"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A Python script uses regular expressions to pull out the file, line and message for each inline comment", "means": "A program is parsing free text to recover fields."}, {"quote": "On some runs nothing posts, because a heading came back in a style the patterns don't expect", "means": "The layout drifts. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and wider patterns (<b>[[2]]</b>) chase the drift instead of removing it."}, {"quote": "Only the script reads this output", "means": "The decider. No human needs prose, so ask for fields: <b>[[1]]</b>. Per-file runs (<b>[[3]]</b>) still leave line and message in prose."}], "rule": "A script consuming Claude Code findings → --output-format json + --json-schema.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "--output-format json + --json-schema for machine-parseable findings → inline PR comments"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-03", "was": "s5-03", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Project context in CI", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The test generator keeps writing tests for generated API client code, which the team leaves untested on purpose. It also uses mock factories that the team replaced with builders last year. Developers get the same advice from Claude Code on their laptops. The testing conventions are already written up in <code>docs/testing.md</code>, which the platform team keeps current. Where should the fix <b>go</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the test generator's workflow step, as testing conventions passed with <code>--append-system-prompt</code>.", "why": "Works, but covers only CI. The generator would improve, but laptops keep the old advice, and a second copy lives in a workflow file.", "trap": "covers only CI"}, {"t": "In a project skill in <code>.claude/skills/testing/</code> that the generator's prompt invokes.", "why": "Works, but only when invoked. CI would call it, but developers on laptops must remember to, and standards should apply in every session.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with a copy of the conventions and <code>paths:</code> for test files.", "why": "Works, but a second copy. It would load for test files everywhere, but it duplicates a document the platform team already maintains, and the two drift.", "trap": "second copy"}, {"t": "In the committed project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, as an <code>@import</code> of <code>docs/testing.md</code>.", "why": "Best. Project memory loads in every Claude Code session in the repository, in CI and on laptops. The import reuses the document the platform team already keeps current."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The testing conventions are already written up in docs/testing.md, which the platform team keeps current.", "explanation": "CLAUDE.md is where a repository gives every Claude Code session its testing standards and fixtures, in CI and locally. @import lets it reference an existing document instead of copying it.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude writes outdated tests both in CI and on laptops. The team already has a good testing guide. The clue: <i>everyone</i> gets the bad advice, and the guide <i>already exists</i>.", "answer": "The team already has a handbook, but nobody gave Claude a copy. The fix is to clip the handbook into the team's noticeboard, the committed project <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, with an <code>@import</code> line. Every session reads the noticeboard, and the platform team keeps editing the one handbook. <b>Rule: project standards → project CLAUDE.md, and @import a document that already exists.</b>", "options": ["Telling only the CI worker fixes CI; people on laptops still get the old advice.", "A manual someone has to open helps only the people who remember to open it.", "A photocopy pinned by the test files works until the handbook changes and the copy doesn't.", "Correct. Pin the real handbook to the board everyone reads."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a CI problem needs a CI fix</b>. Laptops show the same wrong advice, so the gap is in the project context every session shares. That is the job of the committed <b>project CLAUDE.md</b>, and <code>@import</code> lets it point at the document the team already maintains.", "diagram": "CLAUDE.md:  @docs/testing.md\n  → CI test job, CI review, every laptop session", "mapTitle": "Where should a team convention live?", "map": {"head": ["Who needs it, when", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Every session, CI and laptops", "Project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> (with <code>@import</code>)", "Always loaded; one source"], ["Only files matching a pattern, written fresh", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads for matching files"], ["One CI job only", "<code>--append-system-prompt</code> in the workflow", "Job-specific instruction"], ["A task someone runs on request", "Skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code>", "Loads when invoked"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The test generator keeps writing tests for generated API client code, which the team leaves untested on purpose", "means": "Claude doesn't know the team's testing standards."}, {"quote": "Developers get the same advice from Claude Code on their laptops", "means": "Not a CI-only gap. A workflow flag (<b>[[0]]</b>) fixes one job; a skill (<b>[[1]]</b>) needs someone to invoke it."}, {"quote": "The testing conventions are already written up in docs/testing.md, which the platform team keeps current", "means": "The decider. Reuse that document: <b>[[3]]</b>. A copy in a rules file (<b>[[2]]</b>) drifts from it."}], "rule": "Testing standards for every session → project CLAUDE.md; @import an existing document instead of copying it.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "CLAUDE.md provides project context (testing standards, fixtures, review criteria)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-04", "was": "s5-04", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.6 Session context isolation", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "To save a runner start-up, one <code>claude -p</code> call does two jobs. It writes tests for the new endpoints, then reviews the whole pull request, new tests included. Human reviewers keep finding generated tests that assert on the wrong thing. The automated review has not flagged one of them. Its prompt lists only general quality checks. What would <b>most improve</b> the review of generated tests?", "options": [{"t": "Run the review as a separate <code>claude -p</code> call that gets the diff but no generation turns.", "why": "Best. The combined session still holds the reasoning that wrote the tests, so it reads them as settled. A fresh instance checks them the way the human reviewers do."}, {"t": "Add a review check that each assertion tests the behaviour that the test's name describes.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause. Explicit criteria help any reviewer, but this reviewer is checking tests it has just decided were right.", "trap": "leaves the cause"}, {"t": "Turn on extended thinking for the review half, with room to re-derive each test's intent.", "why": "Works, but same blind spot. More reasoning in the same context still starts from the generation reasoning.", "trap": "same blind spot"}, {"t": "Split the review into per-file passes plus one cross-file pass over source and new tests.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Multi-pass review fixes thin attention on large diffs. The problem here is that the reviewer wrote what it reviews.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "one claude -p call does two jobs", "explanation": "A session is a weak reviewer of its own output, because it keeps the reasoning that produced it. An independent instance with no generation history catches more than self-review, even with extended thinking.", "eli5": {"question": "The same Claude call writes tests and then reviews them, and its review never finds the bad tests humans find. The clue: <i>one call does two jobs</i>.", "answer": "Marking your own homework rarely finds your mistakes: you read what you meant, not what you wrote. A different marker catches them. Here that means a separate <code>claude -p</code> call that sees the diff but not the reasoning that wrote the tests (an <b>independent review instance</b>). <b>Rule: never let the writer review its own work in the same session.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A fresh marker who never saw the working.", "A sharper marking scheme helps, but the student is still marking their own homework.", "Thinking longer about your own answers mostly convinces you they're right.", "Marking page by page helps with long homework, but it's still your own homework."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a review that misses problems needs better criteria or more thinking</b>. Those help an independent reviewer. Here the reviewer is the same session that wrote the tests, so it keeps the reasoning behind them and tends to agree with itself. The fix is <b>who reviews</b>.", "diagram": "now:  one session: write tests → review PR → approve\nfix:  session A writes → fresh claude -p reviews diff", "mapTitle": "A review step misses problems. What's wrong with it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Reviewer is the session that wrote the code", "Separate review instance", "No shared reasoning"], ["Independent reviewer lacks a specific check", "Explicit criteria for that check", "Says what counts"], ["Large diff reviewed in one pass", "Per-file passes + cross-file pass", "Avoids thin attention"], ["Hard reasoning, independent reviewer", "Extended thinking", "More room to reason"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "one claude -p call does two jobs", "means": "The decider. The writer and the reviewer share one context."}, {"quote": "Human reviewers keep finding generated tests that assert on the wrong thing", "means": "Independent readers catch what the session misses."}, {"quote": "Its prompt lists only general quality checks", "means": "Criteria (<b>[[1]]</b>) would help, but the same session would still apply them to its own tests."}, {"quote": "What would most improve the review of generated tests?", "means": "Change the reviewer, not its effort: <b>[[0]]</b>. Extended thinking (<b>[[2]]</b>) and more passes (<b>[[3]]</b>) keep the same reviewer."}], "rule": "Generated code is reviewed by a separate instance, not by the session that wrote it.", "guide": {"obj": "3.6 Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD", "quote": "the session that generated code is less effective at reviewing its own changes than an independent instance"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-1", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 User-level memory for personal preferences", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A developer added a line to the committed root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>. It says: “Explain every change in detail, I am new to Kotlin.” Now the CI review job posts long teaching comments on every pull request. Teammates' own sessions have started explaining everything too. The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions. Where should the line <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches only the Kotlin files.", "why": "Works, but still shared. The rule is committed, so CI and teammates load it whenever a Kotlin file is touched, which is most of the time.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In the developer's own <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, removed from the committed project file.", "why": "Best. User-level memory is personal and not shared through git. It loads in every one of the developer's sessions, in every project, and nobody else's."}, {"t": "In a project skill that the developer invokes at the start of each of their sessions.", "why": "Works, but only when remembered. It is also committed, so the skill shows up for the whole team.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "In the CI job's <code>-p</code> prompt, as an override asking for short review comments.", "why": "Works, but for CI only. The review gets shorter, yet teammates' sessions still load the line from the root file.", "trap": "treats one symptom"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions.", "explanation": "A personal preference belongs in user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. It is not shared through version control, so it changes only that person's sessions.", "eli5": {"question": "One developer's personal wish (“explain everything to me”) went into the shared file. Now CI and teammates get it too.", "answer": "A sticky note on the office noticeboard is read by everyone. A note in your own notebook is read only by you, wherever you go. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is the noticeboard; <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> is your notebook. <b>Rule: only for me → user-level memory. For the team → the project file.</b>", "options": ["A note that pops up on Kotlin files is still on the noticeboard: CI and teammates see it.", "Correct. Your own notebook: you get it everywhere, and nobody else does.", "A note you must remember to fetch each morning, and the whole team can see it.", "Telling CI to ignore it helps CI, but teammates still read the noticeboard."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the problem is CI's output</b>, so the fix goes in CI. The real fault is the <i>home</i> of the line: a personal wish sits in a file that every clone and every CI run loads. Move it to the file that only this person loads.", "diagram": "~/.claude/CLAUDE.md  → this developer, every project\nrepo/CLAUDE.md       → every teammate and every CI run", "mapTitle": "Who should this instruction reach?", "map": {"head": ["Who it is for", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Only me, in every project", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not in git"], ["The whole team and CI, always", "Root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Committed, loads for everyone"], ["Files matching a pattern", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Committed, loads for matching files"], ["A task run on request", "A skill or slash command", "Loads only when invoked"], ["One CI job's run", "That job's <code>-p</code> prompt", "Affects that run only"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A developer added a line to the committed root CLAUDE.md", "means": "Committed means shared: every clone and CI load it."}, {"quote": "Teammates' own sessions have started explaining everything too", "means": "The leak is not only in CI, so a CI-only override (<b>[[3]]</b>) fixes one symptom."}, {"quote": "The developer still wants detailed answers in all of their own sessions", "means": "The decider: personal, and in every project. A committed rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) or skill (<b>[[2]]</b>) is still shared, and stays in this repo."}, {"quote": "Where should the line live?", "means": "In the file only this developer loads: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Only for me, everywhere → ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. For the team and CI → the committed project CLAUDE.md.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "User-level is not shared via version control."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-2", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.2 Project commands shared through git", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer wrote a <code>/new-screen</code> command that creates a screen, its test and its story in the team's layout. It lives in their <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>. Teammates copied it from a chat message, and the copies have drifted, so new screens come out in different layouts. The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code. Where should the command <b>live</b>?", "options": [{"t": "In the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code> folder, committed alongside the code.", "why": "Best. Project commands live in the repository, so every engineer gets the same version on clone or pull, and changes go through review."}, {"t": "In the root <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, as a section that lists the steps for a new screen.", "why": "Works, but wrong kind of file. Claude can follow the steps, but they load into every session and there is no <code>/new-screen</code> to run.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches <code>*.screen.tsx</code>.", "why": "Works, but loads too late. A path rule loads when a matching file is touched, and the screen does not exist until the command creates it.", "trap": "wrong home"}, {"t": "In a team dotfiles repo that copies it into each engineer's <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.", "why": "Works, but outside the project. Each engineer must install and update the dotfiles, so versions can drift again.", "trap": "copies to maintain"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code.", "explanation": "A command the whole team should share lives in the project's .claude/commands/, committed to git. ~/.claude/commands/ is for personal commands.", "eli5": {"question": "A handy command lives on one person's laptop. Everyone copied it, and the copies no longer match.", "answer": "If the whole crew needs the same tool, leave it in the shared toolshed on site, not in one person's van. The repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code> is the shared toolshed: whoever clones gets the same tool, and any change is reviewed. <b>Rule: a team command → <code>.claude/commands/</code> in the repo. A personal command → <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>.</b>", "options": ["Correct. One tool in the shared toolshed; everyone gets the same one.", "Written instructions on the wall aren't a tool you can pick up, and everyone reads them all day.", "A note that appears only next to finished screens can't help you make a new one.", "Copying the tool into every van works until someone forgets to update theirs."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the drift is a sharing problem to solve with a sync tool</b>. Claude Code already has a shared home for commands: the repository's <code>.claude/commands/</code>. Committed there, the command arrives with the code and changes go through review.", "diagram": "repo/.claude/commands/new-screen.md → every engineer\n~/.claude/commands/new-screen.md     → one engineer", "mapTitle": "Who needs the command, and where does it live?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best home", "Why"], "rows": [["Whole team, this repository", "<code>.claude/commands/</code>, committed", "Same version for everyone, reviewed"], ["Only me, every repository", "<code>~/.claude/commands/</code>", "Personal, follows me"], ["A standard that always applies", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Always loaded, not invoked"], ["Conventions for a kind of file", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads when a matching file is touched"], ["Shared copies kept in sync by a script", "Dotfiles repo", "Outside the project; can drift"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It lives in their ~/.claude/commands/", "means": "A user-level command: personal, so teammates had to copy it."}, {"quote": "the copies have drifted, so new screens come out in different layouts", "means": "Any copy-based fix, such as a dotfiles sync (<b>[[3]]</b>), keeps the copies."}, {"quote": "The team wants one version that every engineer gets with the code", "means": "The decider: shared through the repository. A CLAUDE.md section (<b>[[1]]</b>) is shared but isn't a command."}, {"quote": "Where should the command live?", "means": "It must run on request before any screen exists, which rules out a path rule (<b>[[2]]</b>). Answer: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Team command → .claude/commands/ in the repo. Personal command → ~/.claude/commands/.", "guide": {"obj": "3.2 Custom slash commands and skills", "quote": "Project commands in .claude/commands/ (shared) vs user ~/.claude/commands/ (personal)"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-instruction-home-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.1 @import for modular references", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "The team's API style guide lives in <code>docs/api-style.md</code>, and the API team updates it often. The root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds a pasted copy of the guide. That copy is months out of date, so Claude follows rules the guide has since changed. The guide applies to every endpoint in the app. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace the copy with an <code>@import</code> line naming the guide file in <code>docs/</code>.", "why": "Best. The import pulls the live guide into memory, so there is one copy to maintain and Claude always reads the current rules."}, {"t": "Add a CI step that copies the latest guide into <code>CLAUDE.md</code> after every merge.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It keeps the copy fresh with a moving part you must build and maintain, when an import line does it with no copy.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Move the guide into an <code>/api-style</code> skill that engineers invoke for endpoint work.", "why": "Works, but only when invoked. Endpoints written without the skill miss the guide, and the skill is another copy.", "trap": "only when invoked"}, {"t": "Paste the guide into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with a <code>paths:</code> glob on the controllers.", "why": "Works, but still a copy. It loads more precisely, yet it drifts from <code>docs/</code> exactly like the pasted copy did.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "That copy is months out of date, so Claude follows rules the guide has since changed.", "explanation": "@import lets CLAUDE.md reference a file instead of copying it, so memory stays in step with the source document.", "eli5": {"question": "The rule book was photocopied into Claude's notes. The real rule book changed; the photocopy didn't.", "answer": "Instead of a photocopy pinned to the wall, pin a note that says “read the rule book on shelf 3”. Whoever reads the note always gets the current book. An <code>@import</code> line in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> is that note. <b>Rule: the content already lives in a file → import it, don't copy it.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Point at the real book, so there's nothing to keep in sync.", "A machine that re-photocopies the book after every change works, but it's a machine to keep running.", "Locking the book in a cupboard means people only read it when they remember to open the cupboard.", "A neater photocopy in a better spot is still a photocopy that goes stale."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>this is about where the guide should load</b>. The guide applies to every endpoint, so always-loaded memory is already the right place. The fault is that memory holds a <i>copy</i>. <code>@import</code> makes <code>CLAUDE.md</code> point at the source file instead.", "diagram": "CLAUDE.md:  @docs/api-style.md\n            ↓ loads the current file\ndocs/api-style.md  (the API team edits only this)", "mapTitle": "Copy it, import it, or load it somewhere else?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Content already lives in a repo file", "<code>@import</code> it from <code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "One source, no copy to drift"], ["Conventions for a file pattern across folders", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>", "Loads only for matching files"], ["A workflow run now and then", "A skill", "Loads only when invoked"], ["Only you need it", "<code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>", "Personal, not shared"], ["Memory doesn't seem to load", "Check with <code>/memory</code>", "Shows which files loaded"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team's API style guide lives in docs/api-style.md, and the API team updates it often", "means": "The source of truth is a file that changes."}, {"quote": "The root CLAUDE.md holds a pasted copy of the guide", "means": "Memory holds a copy, not the source."}, {"quote": "That copy is months out of date", "means": "The decider: drift. Another copy (<b>[[2]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) drifts the same way; a sync job (<b>[[1]]</b>) is extra machinery."}, {"quote": "The guide applies to every endpoint in the app", "means": "Always-loaded memory is the right place; only the copy is wrong. Import the file: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Content already in a repo file? @import it from CLAUDE.md instead of pasting a copy.", "guide": {"obj": "3.1 CLAUDE.md hierarchy, scoping, modular organization", "quote": "@import syntax for modular references"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-1", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Direct execution for a well-scoped change", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A ticket asks for one new check on the sign-up endpoint in <code>server/</code>. Passwords shorter than twelve characters must be rejected with the team's usual error response. The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists. How should the engineer <b>approach</b> this with Claude?", "options": [{"t": "Enter plan mode, have Claude survey the auth code, then approve a written plan.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The plan would say what the ticket already says. Plan mode is for open design choices across many files, and here there are none.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Send an Explore subagent to map every input check in <code>server/</code>, then edit.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. The Explore subagent keeps long discovery output out of the session. Here there is nothing to discover: the engineer already knows both files.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask Claude to add the check and a test case directly, then run the tests.", "why": "Best. One rule, two known files, an existing error format. A simple, well-scoped change like this is what direct execution is for."}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about password rules before writing code.", "why": "Works, but nothing is left to ask. The interview pattern draws out unclear requirements. The ticket already states the rule and the error.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists.", "explanation": "Direct execution fits simple, well-scoped changes such as a single validation check. Plan mode fits large changes with several valid approaches or architectural decisions.", "eli5": {"question": "A small, clear ticket: add one password check to one endpoint. The clue: the engineer already knows which files to change, and the error format exists.", "answer": "Hanging one picture doesn't need an architect. You know the wall, you have the nail, you hang it. <b>Direct execution</b> is hanging the picture: Claude makes the change and runs the tests. <b>Rule: small and settled → just do it. Big or undecided → plan first.</b>", "options": ["Drawing up a plan for one picture: correct in the end, but slow for no gain.", "Sending someone to measure every wall when you already know which wall.", "Correct. Make the change, add a test, run the tests.", "Asking questions when the ticket has already answered them."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>planning first is always the safer choice</b>. Plan mode costs time: Claude explores, writes a plan, and waits for approval. That pays off when the approach is open or many files change. For one clear rule in two known files, the plan adds time and changes nothing.", "diagram": "known files + settled rule\n  -> edit endpoint + add test case\n  -> run tests -> done", "mapTitle": "Which mode fits the task?", "map": {"head": ["Task looks like", "Mode", "Why"], "rows": [["One rule, known files, settled approach", "Direct execution", "Nothing to decide, so just do it"], ["Many files and two valid approaches", "Plan mode", "Agree the design before any edits"], ["Discovery output would flood the session", "Explore subagent", "Returns a summary, not the raw output"], ["Requirements are unclear", "Interview pattern", "Claude asks questions first"], ["Known right answers, fixes keep breaking", "Tests first, share failures", "Each round has a clear target"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A ticket asks for one new check on the sign-up endpoint", "means": "One rule in one place: a small, well-scoped change."}, {"quote": "The engineer knows the endpoint file and its test file, and the error format already exists", "means": "The decider. There is nothing to explore (<b>[[1]]</b>) and no design to agree (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Passwords shorter than twelve characters must be rejected with the team's usual error response", "means": "The requirement is already stated, so an interview (<b>[[3]]</b>) has nothing to ask."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer approach this with Claude?", "means": "Make the change and test it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Small, well-scoped and settled? Direct execution. Plan mode is for big or undecided changes.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Direct execution: simple well-scoped changes (single validation check)."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-2", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.4 Explore subagent for verbose discovery", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer is in plan mode with Claude, designing how to split the large <code>User</code> model into smaller pieces. The design depends on every place in the app that reads or writes user fields. Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context. The design talk that followed lost track of earlier decisions. What should the engineer <b>do</b> for the search?", "options": [{"t": "Have an Explore subagent run the searches and return a summary per field.", "why": "Best. The subagent does the noisy searching in its own context and hands back only the summary. The planning session keeps its room for the design."}, {"t": "Let Claude run the searches in the session, then use <code>/compact</code> before designing.", "why": "Works, but after the damage. Compacting frees space, but the raw output already crowded the session, and the summary it keeps is not chosen for this design.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Leave plan mode and change the model in small steps, searching as each step needs.", "why": "Works, but wrong mode. Splitting a central model is an architectural change. Without a plan, early steps pick a design by default, and later steps redo them.", "trap": "wrong mode"}, {"t": "Save the full usage list in <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for every later session to load.", "why": "Works, but wrong home. The list is loaded into every session, for every task, and goes stale as soon as the code changes.", "trap": "wrong home"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context.", "explanation": "The Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output and returns a summary, so the main session keeps its context for planning and decisions.", "eli5": {"question": "Planning a big change needs a long search through the app, and last time the search output crowded out the planning. The clue: the searches filled the session's context.", "answer": "When you plan a kitchen, you send someone to measure every room. They come back with one sheet of numbers, not a pile of tape-measure notes. The <b>Explore subagent</b> is that helper: it does the messy searching elsewhere and hands back a short summary. <b>Rule: noisy discovery goes to the Explore subagent; the planning session gets the summary.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The helper measures; you get one tidy sheet.", "Letting the notes pile up on the table, then tidying: the mess already got in the way.", "Starting to knock down walls before the plan is agreed.", "Pinning the notes to the fridge for every future job, where they go out of date."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context can be fixed afterwards</b>. Compacting helps later, but the design talk already ran in a crowded session. The Explore subagent keeps the long output out of the session from the start. Only its summary comes back.", "diagram": "plan session -> Explore subagent: find user-field usages\n               (long output stays in the subagent)\nplan session <- summary per field", "mapTitle": "Where should long output go?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Tool", "Effect on the main session"], "rows": [["Long discovery before a design", "Explore subagent", "Only the summary comes back"], ["Session already full of old output", "<code>/compact</code>", "Shrinks what is there"], ["Facts every task needs, every time", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code>", "Loaded into every session"], ["Architectural change, open design", "Plan mode", "Design agreed before edits"], ["One small, settled edit", "Direct execution", "No discovery needed"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer is in plan mode with Claude, designing how to split the large User model", "means": "An architectural change: plan mode is right. Leaving it (<b>[[2]]</b>) trades one problem for another."}, {"quote": "Last time, those searches filled most of the session's context", "means": "The decider: the problem is long discovery output landing in the planning session."}, {"quote": "The design talk that followed lost track of earlier decisions", "means": "Compacting after the search (<b>[[1]]</b>) comes after the damage. Keep the output out from the start."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do for the search?", "means": "Run it in an Explore subagent and take back a summary: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Long discovery output? Give the search to an Explore subagent and keep only its summary.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output, returns summaries."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-plan-or-do-3", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D3 · Claude Code Configuration", "obj": "3.5 Test-driven iteration", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Claude wrote a function in <code>packages/shared/</code> that prices a booking from its start and end times. Prices come out wrong around midnight and around daylight-saving changes. Each fix, asked for in prose, breaks another case. The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each. How should the engineer <b>get</b> the function right?", "options": [{"t": "Describe the midnight and daylight-saving rules in more detail, and ask for a rewrite.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Better prose helps a little, but prose is what keeps breaking other cases. Nothing checks every case each round.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Paste three of the bookings into the prompt as input and expected-price examples.", "why": "Works, but covers only some cases. Concrete examples beat prose, but three of twenty leaves the rest unchecked, and nothing runs them after each change.", "trap": "covers only some cases"}, {"t": "Enter plan mode and have Claude design a new pricing approach before any edits.", "why": "Works, but different problem. The approach is not in question; the edge cases are. A new design still needs checking against the same bookings.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Turn the list into tests first, have Claude run them, and share the failures.", "why": "Best. Every known case is checked on every round, and each failure tells Claude exactly what is still wrong. Fixes can't quietly break another case."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each.", "explanation": "When the right answers are known, write them as tests first and share the failures with Claude. Test-driven iteration gives each round a clear target and catches fixes that break other cases.", "eli5": {"question": "A pricing function keeps getting edge cases wrong, and each fix breaks another. The clue: the team already knows the right price for twenty tricky bookings.", "answer": "If a student keeps getting sums wrong, you don't explain the rules again in longer words. You give them the answer sheet as a checklist, and after each try you show which sums are still wrong. <b>Tests first, then share the failures</b> is that checklist. <b>Rule: known right answers → tests first, and feed Claude the failures.</b>", "options": ["Explaining the rules again in longer words.", "Showing three worked sums out of twenty, and never checking the rest.", "Redesigning the method when the problem is a handful of tricky sums.", "Correct. Mark every sum after every try, and show what is still wrong."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a clearer description will finally get it right</b>. The description is what keeps failing: each prose fix breaks another case. When the right answers are known, turn them into tests. Every round is then checked against every case, and the failures say exactly what to fix.", "diagram": "write tests from the twenty bookings\n  -> Claude edits -> run tests\n  -> share failures -> repeat until all pass", "mapTitle": "How do you tell Claude what “right” means?", "map": {"head": ["You have", "Technique", "What it gives Claude"], "rows": [["Known right answers for many cases", "Tests first, share failures", "A target checked every round"], ["A few clear before/after examples", "Input and output examples", "The shape of the change"], ["Unclear requirements", "Interview pattern", "Questions answered before code"], ["Several problems that affect each other", "One message with all of them", "Fixes made together"], ["Independent problems", "One at a time, in sequence", "Clean, separate fixes"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each fix, asked for in prose, breaks another case", "means": "More prose (<b>[[0]]</b>) repeats what is failing."}, {"quote": "The team has a list of about twenty such bookings with the correct price for each", "means": "The decider: the right answers are known, so they can become tests. Three examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) leave most of them unchecked."}, {"quote": "Prices come out wrong around midnight and around daylight-saving changes", "means": "Edge cases, not an open design question, so plan mode (<b>[[2]]</b>) aims at the wrong thing."}, {"quote": "How should the engineer get the function right?", "means": "Tests first, then share the failures each round: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Know the right answers? Write them as tests first, then share the failures with Claude.", "guide": {"obj": "3.5 Iterative refinement techniques", "quote": "Test-driven iteration (write tests first, share failures)"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-07", "was": "m7-s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for consistent output", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The <code>/new-endpoint</code> command describes the team's error-response format in prose. On simple endpoints Claude follows it. On endpoints with several failure cases, the error shapes differ from run to run. Asked about the format, Claude repeats the rules correctly. What is the <b>most effective</b> change?", "options": [{"t": "Rewrite the prose as a numbered checklist, one rule per failure case, with the exact error JSON.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Sharper rules help when rules are vague, but Claude already knows these rules, and complex cases still drift.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Move the error rules into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file whose <code>paths:</code> glob matches route handlers.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A path rule fixes rules that don't load for some files. These rules load: Claude can repeat them.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have a fresh Claude session review each new endpoint against the error rules before it merges.", "why": "Works, but too late. An independent review catches wrong shapes after the code is written; it doesn't make the output consistent.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Add two or three complete handlers from the repo showing the error format for varied failures.", "why": "Best. When rules are loaded but output still varies, a few concrete examples are the most effective fix, especially for the hard cases."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Asked about the format, Claude repeats the rules correctly.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples are the most effective technique for consistent output when instructions alone are inconsistent. Pick examples that cover the hard cases.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude knows the error-format rules and can repeat them, but on complex endpoints its error shapes still vary. The clue: the rules are <i>known</i>, yet the output is <i>inconsistent</i>.", "answer": "A new cook can recite the plating rules but still plates complex dishes differently every time. Showing them two or three finished plates fixes it faster than rewriting the rules. Those finished plates are <b>few-shot examples</b>: real handlers that show the error format across different failures. <b>Rule: rules understood but output inconsistent → show examples.</b>", "options": ["Neater rules don't help a cook who already knows the rules.", "Putting the rules where the cook stands helps only if they couldn't see them. They can.", "A taster at the pass catches bad plates, but the kitchen keeps making them.", "Correct. Show finished plates for the tricky dishes."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>inconsistent output means the instructions are unclear or not loading</b>. Here they are clear enough to repeat and they load. When instructions alone give inconsistent output, the exam guide's answer is <b>few-shot examples</b>, chosen to cover the hard cases.", "mapTitle": "Why is the output wrong, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Rules known, output varies on hard cases", "Prose alone underspecifies the shape", "2–3 examples covering varied cases"], ["Rules missing when editing some files", "Not loading for those paths", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>"], ["Too many false flags", "Vague criteria", "Explicit categorical criteria"], ["Mistakes slip into merged code", "No independent check", "Review by a fresh instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "On simple endpoints Claude follows it", "means": "The rules are not wrong; they work on easy cases."}, {"quote": "On endpoints with several failure cases, the error shapes differ from run to run", "means": "Inconsistency on the hard cases, which examples address. A reviewer (<b>[[2]]</b>) catches it later rather than fixing it."}, {"quote": "Asked about the format, Claude repeats the rules correctly", "means": "The decider. The rules load and are understood, so moving them (<b>[[1]]</b>) or rewording them (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds little."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective change?", "means": "Show, don't tell: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Instructions loaded and understood but output inconsistent → add 2–3 examples covering the hard cases.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Few-shot is the most effective technique for consistent, actionable output when instructions alone are inconsistent"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-08", "was": "m7-s2-08", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria for precision", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "A <code>/check-migration</code> command asks Claude to “flag risky Prisma migrations”. It flags almost every migration, so engineers have started to skip its output. The team counts only three changes as risky: dropping a column, changing a column's type, or adding a required column with no default. Which change <b>best improves precision</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Name those three changes as the flag criteria, each with a snippet, and say all else passes.", "why": "Best. The team can state exactly what counts. Explicit categories with concrete snippets replace the vague word “risky” and cut false flags."}, {"t": "Add several past migrations labelled risky or safe, each with a line on why.", "why": "Works, but indirect. Labelled examples teach the line by showing it. Here the team can state the line outright, which is clearer and shorter.", "trap": "indirect"}, {"t": "Tell Claude to be conservative and to flag a migration only when it is highly confident of risk.", "why": "Works, but only a little. Fewer flags come out, but “risky” is still undefined, and confidence wording doesn't sharpen precision like categories do.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have Claude give each finding a severity score, and show engineers only the high-scoring ones.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Fewer flags reach engineers, but scores without criteria are as vague as the flags they rank.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The team counts only three changes as risky: dropping a column, changing a column's type, or adding a required column with no default.", "explanation": "Vague instructions like “risky” produce false positives that erode trust. Replace them with explicit categorical criteria, ideally with a concrete example of each.", "eli5": {"question": "A migration checker flags almost everything, so people ignore it. The clue: the team can name the <i>exact three</i> changes that count as risky.", "answer": "Telling a guard “stop anyone suspicious” gets everyone stopped. Handing them a list (“no bags, no drinks, no pets”) gets the right people stopped. The list is <b>explicit criteria</b>: the three risky changes, each with a small snippet, and everything else passes. <b>Rule: if you can name what counts, name it. Vague words make false alarms.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Give the guard the exact list, with a picture of each item.", "Showing the guard past cases works, but you can just give them the list.", "“Only stop people you're really sure about” still leaves “suspicious” undefined.", "Ranking the alarms hides most of them, but the guard still doesn't know what counts."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>examples or a confidence threshold</b> are the way to cut false positives. Examples are the right tool when the line is hard to put in words. Here the team can state the line in one sentence. The exam guide says explicit categorical criteria beat vague instructions, and “be conservative” doesn't improve precision the way categories do.", "mapTitle": "Too many false flags. What sharpens the line?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Team can list exactly what counts", "Explicit criteria with a snippet each", "Replaces the vague word"], ["Line is real but hard to put in words", "Labelled examples with reasons", "Shows ambiguous cases"], ["Prompt says “be careful” or “high confidence”", "Swap for categories", "Confidence wording barely helps"], ["Flags right but noisy in one category", "Drop or tighten that category", "Protects trust in the rest"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "asks Claude to “flag risky Prisma migrations”", "means": "The cause: “risky” is undefined, so Claude flags anything that might be."}, {"quote": "so engineers have started to skip its output", "means": "False positives are costing trust. Hiding low scores (<b>[[3]]</b>) treats the symptom."}, {"quote": "The team counts only three changes as risky", "means": "The decider. The line can be stated outright, so labelled examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) are the long way round and confidence wording (<b>[[2]]</b>) stays vague. Name them: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "If the team can name what counts, write it as explicit criteria; don't say “be conservative”.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-06", "was": "m7-s5-06", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria over confidence wording", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Developers dismiss most of the reviewer's performance findings. The prompt's only guidance for this category is “flag performance problems”. Last month the team added “report only findings you are highly confident about”, and the dismissal rate did not change. The team wants to keep the category, since real performance bugs have reached production. What should they change <b>next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace it with concrete conditions, such as a database query run inside a loop.", "why": "Best. Explicit criteria tell the reviewer exactly what counts as a performance problem. Vague wording plus a plea for confidence does not."}, {"t": "Add three sample findings to the prompt, each with flagged code and why.", "why": "Works, but too early. Examples sharpen criteria that are applied unevenly. Here there are no criteria yet, so the examples would have to carry the whole definition.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Switch the performance category off until its precision improves, then bring it back.", "why": "Works, but wrong goal. Pausing a noisy category protects developers' trust, but the team wants to keep catching real performance bugs.", "trap": "wrong goal"}, {"t": "Tag each finding with the pattern it matched, and study which patterns get dismissed.", "why": "Works, but delays the fix. Tracking patterns shows where the noise comes from, but it measures the problem while the vague guidance stays in place.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The prompt's only guidance for this category is “flag performance problems”.", "explanation": "Vague instructions plus “only high-confidence” wording do not raise precision. Define the category with concrete, checkable conditions; add examples once criteria exist.", "eli5": {"question": "The reviewer's performance comments are mostly ignored. Asking it to be “highly confident” didn't help. The clue: the only guidance is “flag performance problems”.", "answer": "Telling a new referee “call bad fouls, but only when you're sure” changes nothing. Giving them the rulebook does: “a push from behind is a foul”. Concrete conditions are the <b>explicit criteria</b> for the category. <b>Rule: too many false alarms → define exactly what counts, don't ask for more confidence.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Spell out what counts as a performance problem.", "Showing past calls helps a referee who has a rulebook. Without one, they are guessing what the examples have in common.", "Benching the referee stops bad calls, and good ones too.", "Keeping a tally of bad calls is useful later, but the referee still has no rulebook."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>false positives mean the model is not careful enough</b>, so you ask it to be careful. Precision comes from knowing <b>what counts</b>. “Flag performance problems” leaves that open, and “only if highly confident” doesn't close it.", "mapTitle": "The reviewer raises too many false alarms. What's missing?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Category is vague; no criteria", "Concrete, checkable conditions", "Says exactly what counts"], ["Criteria exist but are applied unevenly", "2–4 examples with reasoning", "Shows how to apply them"], ["Prompt asks for “high confidence”", "Replace with criteria", "Confidence wording doesn't raise precision"], ["Not sure which findings are noise", "Track <code>detected_pattern</code> on findings", "Shows dismissal patterns"], ["Category is noisy and not needed", "Pause it", "Protects trust in the rest"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Developers dismiss most of the reviewer's performance findings", "means": "A precision problem in one category."}, {"quote": "The prompt's only guidance for this category is “flag performance problems”", "means": "The decider. There are no criteria, so define them: <b>[[0]]</b>. Examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) illustrate criteria; they come after."}, {"quote": "the dismissal rate did not change", "means": "Confidence wording failed, as the exam guide predicts."}, {"quote": "The team wants to keep the category, since real performance bugs have reached production", "means": "Pausing it (<b>[[2]]</b>) gives up the real catches. Tracking patterns (<b>[[3]]</b>) measures, but doesn't fix."}], "rule": "To cut false positives, define explicit criteria. “Only high-confidence” wording does not raise precision.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-07", "was": "m7-s5-07", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for generalisation", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The reviewer's dead-code rule says code is not dead when a feature flag can turn it on. Flags in this repository take many forms, such as config lookups, annotations and environment checks. Teams add new forms as they adopt new libraries. The reviewer follows the rule for some forms and flags the rest as dead. What should the team add to the prompt <b>first</b>?", "options": [{"t": "A full list of the flag forms in use today, with the exact call or annotation for each.", "why": "Works, but goes stale. The list fixes today's forms, but new forms keep arriving, so it is always behind.", "trap": "goes stale"}, {"t": "A sharper definition: any runtime check that decides whether code runs.", "why": "Works, but same gap. The rule is already clear; the reviewer fails to recognise unfamiliar forms, and more words describe the pattern less well than cases do.", "trap": "same gap"}, {"t": "A second pass in a fresh instance that rechecks each dead-code finding before posting.", "why": "Works, but different problem. An independent pass catches self-review bias, but it applies the same rule with the same blind spot about unfamiliar forms.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "A few worked cases in different flag forms, each with its verdict and the reason.", "why": "Best. Varied examples with reasoning teach the pattern, so the reviewer can recognise forms it has not seen."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Teams add new forms as they adopt new libraries.", "explanation": "The rule is clear; the reviewer fails to recognise unfamiliar cases. Few-shot examples that show reasoning help the model generalise to new patterns, which a list of today's forms cannot do.", "eli5": {"question": "The reviewer knows the rule but doesn't recognise every kind of feature flag. The clue: new kinds keep appearing.", "answer": "You teach a child to spot dogs by showing a few very different dogs and saying why each is a dog. A list of today's breeds is out of date when a new breed turns up. A few <b>worked examples with reasons</b> (few-shot) teach the pattern, not the list. <b>Rule: the model must handle new variants → show varied examples with reasoning.</b>", "options": ["A breed list works today and falls behind tomorrow.", "A tighter definition of “dog” is still words. The child knows the definition and still misses odd-looking dogs.", "A second child with the same training misses the same odd dogs.", "Correct. Varied examples with reasons teach the pattern."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a model that misses cases needs more precise wording</b>. The rule here is already clear. The failure is <b>recognising unfamiliar forms</b>, and that is what few-shot examples with reasoning are for: they let the model generalise to cases nobody listed.", "mapTitle": "The model misapplies a rule. What helps?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best help", "Why"], "rows": [["Clear rule, many and changing forms", "2–4 varied examples with reasoning", "Generalises to new forms"], ["Clear rule, small fixed set of forms", "List them", "Complete and cheap"], ["Rule itself is vague", "Explicit criteria", "Defines what counts"], ["Session reviews its own work", "Independent review instance", "No shared reasoning"], ["Output format varies", "Examples of the format", "Shows the shape"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Flags in this repository take many forms, such as config lookups, annotations and environment checks", "means": "The pattern is varied, so a single definition (<b>[[1]]</b>) hasn't helped Claude recognise it."}, {"quote": "Teams add new forms as they adopt new libraries", "means": "The decider. A list of today's forms (<b>[[0]]</b>) is always behind."}, {"quote": "The reviewer follows the rule for some forms and flags the rest as dead", "means": "It knows the rule; it doesn't recognise every form. A second pass (<b>[[2]]</b>) shares that blind spot. Worked cases teach recognition: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "When the model must recognise new variants of a pattern, give 2–4 varied examples that show the reasoning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "enable generalization to novel patterns"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-08", "was": "m7-s5-08", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch fit and request trimming", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 2, "question": "The weekly dependency-risk report sends one request per module on Saturday night. Nobody reads it until the Monday planning meeting. Each request carries the module's whole source tree, but the report uses only its build file and lockfile. The report must rate every module every week. Which <b>two changes</b> cut its cost the most?", "options": [{"t": "Send the requests as one Message Batches API job, one <code>custom_id</code> per module.", "why": "Best. Nobody needs the results before Monday, so the batch discount costs nothing in practice. <code>custom_id</code> matches each result to its module."}, {"t": "Skip modules whose build file and lockfile have not changed since last week's run.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves a lot, but the report must rate every module weekly, and a new advisory can hit an unchanged lockfile.", "trap": "drops coverage"}, {"t": "Send each module's build file and lockfile alone, and leave its source code out.", "why": "Best. Most tokens are source code the report never reads. Trimming the input cuts cost without losing coverage."}, {"t": "Merge the modules into a few large requests, one for each product area.", "why": "Works, but saves little. The same source still goes in, so tokens barely drop, and bigger requests spread attention across many modules.", "trap": "saves little"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Nobody reads it until the Monday planning meeting. Each request carries the module's whole source tree, but the report uses only its build file and lockfile.", "explanation": "Latency-tolerant work like a weekly report fits the Message Batches API. Sending only the content the task uses cuts tokens. Neither reduces coverage, unlike skipping work.", "eli5": {"question": "A weekly report costs too much. Which two changes save the most and still rate every module? The clues: nobody reads it until Monday, and it only uses the build file and lockfile.", "answer": "Think of posting parcels. If nobody needs them until Monday, use the cheap slow service (the <b>Message Batches API</b>: half price, results within a day). And don't ship the whole house when only two letters are needed: send just those files (<b>trim the input</b>). Skipping some parcels would save even more, but every module must be rated. <b>Rule: batch work nobody waits for, and send only what the task reads.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Half price, and Monday is plenty of time. <code>custom_id</code> links each result to its module.", "Cheap, but it breaks “rate every module every week”.", "Correct. Most of each request is code the report doesn't use.", "Fewer, bigger parcels with the same contents: little saving, and each module gets less attention."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the biggest saving is doing less work</b>, like skipping unchanged modules. The requirement is full coverage every week. The savings that keep coverage are <b>paying less per request</b> (batch) and <b>sending fewer tokens per request</b> (trim the input).", "mapTitle": "Cutting cost: which levers keep the output complete?", "map": {"head": ["Lever", "When it fits", "Watch out for"], "rows": [["Message Batches API", "Nobody waits on results (overnight, weekly)", "No latency SLA; no multi-turn tools"], ["Trim input to what the task uses", "Requests carry content the task ignores", "Don't drop what's used"], ["Skip unchanged items", "Old results stay valid", "Breaks full-coverage requirements"], ["Pack many items per request", "Rarely", "Attention spread thin"], ["Real-time requests", "Someone waits (pre-merge check)", "Full price"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Nobody reads it until the Monday planning meeting", "means": "Nobody waits on it, so batch fits: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Each request carries the module's whole source tree, but the report uses only its build file and lockfile", "means": "Most tokens are wasted. Trim the input: <b>[[2]]</b>. Merging (<b>[[3]]</b>) keeps the same tokens."}, {"quote": "The report must rate every module every week", "means": "Skipping modules (<b>[[1]]</b>) breaks this."}], "rule": "Batch what nobody waits for; trim what the task doesn't read. Don't skip work a requirement says to cover.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Good for non-blocking/latency-tolerant (overnight reports, weekly audits); bad for blocking (pre-merge)."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-09", "was": "m7-s5-09", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retry with specific error feedback", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The autofix job patches failing builds. When a patch does not compile, the job reruns Claude with the message “The patch failed. Try again.” Most second attempts fail the same way. The compiler output names each broken file, line and error. Every fix needed lies in the repository's own code. What is the <b>best change</b> to raise the fix rate?", "options": [{"t": "After one failed retry, open a draft pull request with the patch and build log for a developer.", "why": "Works, but gives up too soon. A hand-off is right when the fix needs information the job can't reach. Here everything is in the repository, and the retry never got the errors.", "trap": "gives up too soon"}, {"t": "On each retry, send the compiler's error lines for the failed patch with the original request.", "why": "Best. The errors are specific and fixable from the repository, so feeding them back gives the retry what it needs."}, {"t": "Have a separate review instance read each failed patch, and pass its comments into the retry.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It adds feedback, but a reviewer's opinion is weaker than the compiler's exact errors, which already exist for free.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "On each retry, attach the current contents of each broken file the compiler named.", "why": "Works, but more context, not the error. The files help Claude see the code, but they don't say what broke.", "trap": "more context, not the error"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The compiler output names each broken file, line and error.", "explanation": "Retries succeed when they carry the specific validation errors and the needed information is in the source. A bare “try again” gives the model nothing new.", "eli5": {"question": "When a fix fails, the job just says “try again”, and Claude fails the same way. The clue: the compiler already says exactly what broke.", "answer": "A teacher who hands back homework marked only “wrong, redo” gets the same mistakes back. Circle the errors and the student can fix them. The compiler's messages are those circles. <b>Retry with the specific errors</b>. <b>Rule: retry with the exact error when the fix is findable in the source; hand to a person when it isn't.</b>", "options": ["Calling in a tutor after one bare “redo”: the student never saw the circles.", "Correct. Hand back the exact errors with the task.", "A second teacher's opinion, when the marking key already says what's wrong.", "Giving back the whole exercise book without circling anything."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a failing retry means the task is too hard for the model</b>. Here the retry got no new information: “try again” only. When the error is specific and the fix is in the source, <b>feeding back the error</b> is what makes a retry work.", "diagram": "patch → build fails → compiler: File.kt:42 type mismatch\nretry(request + patch + compiler errors) → new patch", "mapTitle": "A retry keeps failing. What should the next attempt get?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Next step", "Why"], "rows": [["Specific error, fix is in the source", "Retry with the error messages", "Model sees what broke"], ["Fix needs information that isn't there", "Hand to a person", "Retrying can't create it"], ["Retry with errors still fails repeatedly", "Hand off with the log", "Diminishing returns"], ["Output parses but values are wrong", "Validate and retry with the specific error", "Schemas can't catch meaning"], ["Bare “try again”", "Add the error", "Gives nothing new"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the job reruns Claude with the message “The patch failed. Try again.”", "means": "The retry carries no information about what broke."}, {"quote": "The compiler output names each broken file, line and error", "means": "The decider. Precise feedback already exists: <b>[[1]]</b>. Opinions (<b>[[2]]</b>) and raw files (<b>[[3]]</b>) are weaker substitutes."}, {"quote": "Every fix needed lies in the repository's own code", "means": "The information is reachable, so handing off early (<b>[[0]]</b>) gives up too soon."}], "rule": "Retry with the specific error when the fix can be found in the source. Hand to a person when the information is missing.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retry-with-error-feedback: append specific validation errors on retry."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s5-10", "was": "m7-s5-10", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.6 Independent review instance", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "After the autofix job's patch compiles, the same session is asked to check the patch for side effects. It approves nearly every patch. Several approved patches later broke callers in other modules. The check's prompt already lists the kinds of side effect to look for. What is the <b>best way</b> to improve this check?", "options": [{"t": "Add worked examples of patches that broke callers, each with the clue the check missed.", "why": "Works, but leaves the cause. Examples sharpen a reviewer that misses a category, but a session checking its own patch keeps the reasoning that wrote it.", "trap": "leaves the cause"}, {"t": "Turn on extended thinking for the check and have it trace each changed signature.", "why": "Works, but same blind spot. Longer reasoning finds some problems, but in the same session it tends to confirm the patch it just wrote.", "trap": "same blind spot"}, {"t": "Require a <code>side_effects</code> entry in the check's output schema for each changed function.", "why": "Works, but form, not judgment. A schema makes the check report on every function, but a session reviewing its own patch fills it with “none” just as easily.", "trap": "form, not judgment"}, {"t": "Run the check as a fresh <code>claude -p</code> call that sees the patch and code, not the session.", "why": "Best. An independent instance has no stake in the earlier reasoning and catches what self-review misses."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "the same session is asked to check the patch for side effects", "explanation": "A model reviewing its own work in the same session keeps its generation reasoning and rarely questions it. An independent instance without that history catches more than self-review, extended thinking or examples.", "eli5": {"question": "The session that wrote a patch is asked to check it, and it approves almost everything. The clue: it's the <i>same session</i>.", "answer": "Writers miss their own typos because they read what they meant, not what they wrote. A fresh reader catches them. A new <code>claude -p</code> call that never saw the writing session is that fresh reader (an <b>independent review instance</b>). <b>Rule: don't let a session mark its own homework; use a fresh instance.</b>", "options": ["Examples help a reader who lacks the knowledge. This one has it, but is reading its own work.", "Thinking longer about your own work mostly convinces you it's right.", "A form with a box for each change: the writer still writes “none”.", "Correct. A fresh reader with no memory of writing it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a check that misses things needs more guidance or more thinking</b>. The prompt already lists what to look for. The problem is <b>who is checking</b>: the session that wrote the patch keeps its own reasoning and tends to agree with itself.", "diagram": "now:  session A writes patch → session A checks it → approve\nfix:  session A writes patch → fresh claude -p checks it", "mapTitle": "A review step misses problems. What's wrong with it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Reviewer is the session that wrote the code", "Independent review instance", "No shared reasoning"], ["Independent reviewer misses one category", "Worked examples of that category", "Teaches the pattern"], ["Criteria for the check are vague", "Explicit criteria", "Says what counts"], ["Large change reviewed in one pass", "Per-file passes + a cross-file pass", "Avoids spread-thin attention"], ["Findings must reach a script", "Output schema", "Shapes the report, not the judgment"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the same session is asked to check the patch for side effects", "means": "The decider. Self-review in the same session: <b>[[3]]</b> removes that."}, {"quote": "It approves nearly every patch", "means": "The signature of self-review: it agrees with its own reasoning. Extended thinking (<b>[[1]]</b>) in that session tends to do the same."}, {"quote": "The check's prompt already lists the kinds of side effect to look for", "means": "Guidance is present. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a schema (<b>[[2]]</b>) refine it but keep the same reviewer."}], "rule": "A session reviewing its own work keeps its reasoning. Use an independent instance for review.", "guide": {"obj": "4.6 Multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures", "quote": "Independent review instances (no prior reasoning) catch subtle issues better than self-review/extended thinking."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-01", "was": "m7-s6-01", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Nullable fields against fabrication", "trap": "Prompt where enforcement was required", "fam": 1, "select": 1, "question": "Many certificates do not show a deductible at all. In the schema, deductible is a required number. For these certificates, the extraction returns 0 or copies the coverage limit into the field. Those values are posted as if they were printed, and reviewers cannot tell them apart from real ones. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add two example certificates with no deductible to the prompt, each with its expected output.", "why": "Works, but only partly. Examples show what to do when no deductible is printed, but while the schema demands a number the model has no honest value to return.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make deductible nullable in the schema, and state in its description when to return null.", "why": "Best. A required number forces the model to invent one. A nullable field gives it an honest way to say “not printed”."}, {"t": "Keep deductible required and have the model enter -1 whenever no deductible is printed.", "why": "Works, but fragile. -1 marks the gap, but it is still a number: every system downstream must know the convention, or -1 posts as a real value.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Add a <code>deductible_printed</code> flag beside the number, and have reviewers ignore the number when it is false.", "why": "Works, but the invented number stays. The flag tells reviewers to ignore it, but the required field still puts a made-up value in the record, where other systems can use it.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "In the schema, deductible is a required number.", "explanation": "Required fields push the model to invent values when the source lacks them. Optional or nullable fields, with a description of when to leave them empty, prevent that.", "eli5": {"question": "When a certificate has no deductible, the extraction makes one up (0, or the limit). The clue: the schema says the field must always hold a number.", "answer": "A form with a box that must contain a number, and no “not applicable” option, makes people write something, anything. Add “leave blank if not printed” and the made-up values stop. In a JSON schema that's a <b>nullable field</b>. <b>Rule: the model invents values when the schema leaves it no honest answer. Make missing values nullable.</b>", "options": ["Showing examples doesn't help while the form still has no blank option.", "Correct. Let the field be empty when nothing is printed.", "-1 is still a number someone can mistake for a real one.", "A “please ignore” sticker helps, but the made-up number is still on the form."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>made-up values are a prompting problem</b>. Here the schema causes them: a required number has no valid way to say “not printed”, so the model fills it. Fix the schema first; examples help only once the schema allows an honest answer.", "diagram": "required number + nothing printed → model invents 0\nnullable number + nothing printed → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "Extraction output is wrong. Schema, prompt or retry?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Values invented when the source lacks them", "The field is required", "Make it optional or nullable"], ["Categories forced into the nearest option", "Closed enum", "Add <code>other</code> plus a detail field"], ["Field allows null but the model still guesses", "Unclear when to leave it empty", "Describe the rule and add examples"], ["Accuracy drops on unusual layouts", "Too few patterns shown", "Few-shot examples of each layout"], ["A value fails a validation check", "The model misread the document", "Retry with the specific error"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Many certificates do not show a deductible at all", "means": "Missing is a normal case, so the output needs a way to say so."}, {"quote": "In the schema, deductible is a required number", "means": "The decider: the schema forces a number. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't fix a schema with no honest answer, and a flag (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the invented number in the record."}, {"quote": "Those values are posted as if they were printed, and reviewers cannot tell them apart from real ones", "means": "Invented values look real. A marker like -1 (<b>[[2]]</b>) has the same problem."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Change the schema: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model inventing values for missing data? Make the field nullable and say when to use null.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-02", "was": "m7-s6-02", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Extensible enums with other + detail", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "Each coverage line has a <code>line_type</code> enum with five values, such as general liability, auto and umbrella. Certificates now often list other lines, such as cyber or professional liability. The extraction forces these into the nearest listed value, so cyber often posts as general liability. Brokers need to know what each of these lines really is. Which schema change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add cyber and professional liability to the enum, and add more values as carriers bring new lines.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The two new values fix today's lines, but the next new line type is forced into the nearest value again until someone changes the schema.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Make <code>line_type</code> nullable, and leave it empty when a line matches none of the listed values.", "why": "Works, but loses the answer. An empty value stops the wrong label, but brokers still cannot see what the line is, and the type was printed on the certificate.", "trap": "loses information"}, {"t": "Change <code>line_type</code> to a free-text string that holds the carrier's own wording.", "why": "Works, but drops the structure. Every line keeps its real name, but the common types now arrive spelled many ways, and reports that group by type break.", "trap": "overcorrects"}, {"t": "Add <code>other</code> to the enum, with a <code>line_type_detail</code> field for the carrier's wording.", "why": "Best. Known types stay clean enum values, and any new type lands in <code>other</code> with its real name kept beside it."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Brokers need to know what each of these lines really is.", "explanation": "A closed enum forces unknown values into the nearest option. Add an other value plus a detail field so new categories are captured without losing the known ones.", "eli5": {"question": "A list of insurance types has five choices. New types like cyber get squeezed into the closest choice, so they post wrong. The clue: brokers need to know what each line really is.", "answer": "Think of a form with tick boxes for five fruits. Someone brings a mango and ticks “apple”. The usual fix is a sixth box, “other”, with a line to write “mango”. In a schema that's an <b>enum with <code>other</code> plus a detail field</b>. <b>Rule: a list that keeps growing gets an “other” value and a field for the real name.</b>", "options": ["Adding boxes for today's new fruits works until next week's new fruit arrives.", "Leaving the box blank stops the wrong tick, but nobody knows it was a mango.", "Letting people write anything loses the neat tick boxes everyone counts by.", "Correct. Keep the boxes, add “other”, and write the real name next to it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a wrong category means the list is missing a value</b>, so you add the value. That fixes today, but carriers keep inventing new lines. The schema needs a <b>place for values nobody listed yet</b>, while known values stay clean for reporting.", "diagram": "enum: gl | auto | wc | umbrella | property | other\n  “Cyber Liability” → other + detail: “Cyber Liability”\n  “Auto”            → auto", "mapTitle": "A category field gets the wrong value. What does the schema need?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["New, printed types forced into the nearest value", "Closed enum with no escape", "Add <code>other</code> plus a detail field"], ["The same one or two missing values, and the list is stable", "A gap in a fixed list", "Add those values to the enum"], ["The type is not printed at all", "Nothing to extract", "Make the field nullable"], ["The document itself is unclear about the type", "Real ambiguity", "Add <code>unclear</code> and route for review"], ["Values chosen inconsistently between similar documents", "Unclear boundaries", "Describe each value and add examples"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Certificates now often list other lines, such as cyber or professional liability", "means": "New types keep appearing, so adding two values (<b>[[0]]</b>) only fixes today's list."}, {"quote": "The extraction forces these into the nearest listed value", "means": "The cause is a closed list with no place for unknown values."}, {"quote": "Brokers need to know what each of these lines really is", "means": "The decider. Null (<b>[[1]]</b>) would hide the real type."}, {"quote": "Which schema change is the best fix?", "means": "Keep the known values clean, which free text (<b>[[2]]</b>) gives up, and add an escape with detail: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "A category list that keeps growing gets an other value plus a detail field.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "enum \"unclear\"/\"other\" + detail for extensible categories"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-03", "was": "m7-s6-03", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retry with specific validation errors", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A validator rejects any policy whose expiry date is earlier than its effective date. On failure, the pipeline resends the certificate with the message “Validation failed, please retry.” Most retries fail the same way. In sampled failures, both dates are printed clearly, but the model has put each date in the other's field. Which change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Resend with the specific error: the two fields, the values returned and the rule they broke.", "why": "Best. Both dates are on the page, so the model can fix the mix-up once it is told exactly what went wrong."}, {"t": "Swap the two dates in code whenever the expiry date comes out earlier than the effective date.", "why": "Works, but hides the cause. It fixes this exact swap, but a date that was misread, not swapped, would also be flipped and posted as if correct.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Send each certificate that fails the date check to a person in the review queue.", "why": "Works, but gives up too early. A person will fix it, but the information is on the page and a clear retry fixes most of these without anyone's time.", "trap": "gives up too early"}, {"t": "Add example certificates to the prompt that show where several carriers print the two dates.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples may cut the first-pass swaps, but the retry still sends a vague message, so the failures that remain still repeat.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "On failure, the pipeline resends the certificate with the message “Validation failed, please retry.”", "explanation": "When the information is in the document, a retry works if it carries the specific validation error. A generic retry message gives the model nothing to correct.", "eli5": {"question": "When the dates come back swapped, the system just says “try again”, and the model makes the same mistake. The clue: the retry message says nothing about what was wrong.", "answer": "A teacher hands back homework marked only “wrong, redo it”. The pupil guesses and gets it wrong again. Write “you swapped the start and end dates” and it's fixed in one go. That is <b>retry with the specific validation error</b>. <b>Rule: when the answer is on the page, retry with the exact error. When it isn't on the page, retrying won't help.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Tell the model exactly which fields broke which rule.", "Quietly swapping in code fixes this case, but also “fixes” dates that were simply misread.", "A person can fix it, but the model could have fixed it with a clear note.", "Better examples help the first try, but the retry note is still useless."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>retries don't work</b>, because most of them fail. They fail here because the message is vague. The guide's line is about <i>where</i> the information is: if it is in the document, a retry with the <b>specific error</b> fixes it; if it is absent, no retry can.", "diagram": "extract → validator: expiry < effective\n  vague:    “Validation failed” → same swap again\n  specific: “expiry 2026-01-01 < effective 2027-01-01,\n             fields may be swapped” → fixed", "mapTitle": "An extraction fails validation. Retry, fix in code, or send to a person?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Value is on the page but in the wrong field or format", "Mapping or format slip", "Retry with the specific error"], ["Value is missing or unreadable in the source", "Information absent", "Don't retry; null or human review"], ["Same mistake across many first passes", "Unclear layouts", "Few-shot examples of those layouts"], ["A fixed, harmless format difference", "Known variation", "Normalise in code"], ["Totals don't add up", "Semantic error", "Extract a calculated total beside the stated one"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the pipeline resends the certificate with the message “Validation failed, please retry.”", "means": "The decider. The retry gives no detail, so the model has nothing to correct."}, {"quote": "Most retries fail the same way", "means": "Retrying itself is not the problem; the content of the retry is."}, {"quote": "both dates are printed clearly, but the model has put each date in the other's field", "means": "The information is on the page, so a retry can work. A person (<b>[[2]]</b>) isn't needed, and a code swap (<b>[[1]]</b>) would also flip misread dates."}, {"quote": "Which change is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the failing step, the retry: <b>[[0]]</b>. Examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) help the first pass only."}], "rule": "Information on the page but extracted wrong? Retry with the specific validation error.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retry-with-error-feedback: append specific validation errors on retry"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-04", "was": "m7-s6-04", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for varied document layouts", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Each schema field has a detailed description of what to extract. Accuracy is high on certificates that use the standard industry form. It drops on carriers' own policy schedules, which put limits in paragraphs, footnotes or tables with several columns. The prompt has instructions only, with no complete document worked through. What is the <b>most effective</b> way to raise accuracy on these schedules?", "options": [{"t": "Add notes to each field description on where values sit in non-standard layouts.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. The descriptions are already detailed, and prose about layouts teaches less than seeing a few documents worked through.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Classify each document first, and send schedules to a separate prompt written only for them.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second prompt adds a classifier and another prompt to maintain, and it still has no worked examples.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add three or four worked schedules in different layouts, showing where each limit is.", "why": "Best. Worked examples are the strongest way to show how to handle varied layouts, and the prompt has none yet."}, {"t": "Make the limit fields nullable and send schedules with empty limits to a reviewer.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It stops guessed limits from posting, but accuracy on schedules stays where it is and reviewers do the work.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The prompt has instructions only, with no complete document worked through.", "explanation": "When detailed instructions still give uneven results across document layouts, a few worked examples covering those layouts are the most effective next step.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude does well on the standard form but badly on carriers' own layouts. The clue: the instructions are already detailed, and there are no worked examples at all.", "answer": "A new clerk reads a thick manual and still gets lost in an unusual invoice. Sit with them through three odd invoices, pointing at where each number is, and they get it. That's <b>few-shot examples</b>: a few documents worked through in the prompt. <b>Rule: instructions already detailed but layouts vary? Add 2–4 worked examples of those layouts.</b>", "options": ["Adding more pages to the manual helps a little; the clerk already has plenty of words.", "A separate manual for odd invoices is more work, and it still has no worked examples.", "Correct. Show a few odd layouts worked through, step by step.", "Leaving hard numbers blank for a person stops guesses, but doesn't make the clerk better."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>better instructions</b> fix accuracy. Here the descriptions are already detailed. What is missing is <b>examples</b>: showing a few varied documents and where each value was found teaches layouts that prose cannot describe well.", "mapTitle": "Accuracy is uneven. What is missing from the prompt?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Detailed instructions, accuracy drops on unusual layouts", "No worked examples", "2–4 examples across the layouts"], ["Field descriptions are thin or vague", "Unclear instructions", "Expand the descriptions first"], ["Values invented when absent", "Required field", "Make it nullable"], ["Very different document families need different fields", "One schema for unlike jobs", "Classify, then route to separate prompts"], ["Values fail a validation check", "Misread or swapped values", "Retry with the specific error"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each schema field has a detailed description of what to extract", "means": "Instructions are already detailed, so more notes (<b>[[0]]</b>) add little."}, {"quote": "It drops on carriers' own policy schedules, which put limits in paragraphs, footnotes or tables with several columns", "means": "Same fields, varied layouts. The schema is fine, so a separate prompt (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "The prompt has instructions only, with no complete document worked through", "means": "The decider: the strongest tool for varied layouts is unused."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective way to raise accuracy on these schedules?", "means": "Raise accuracy, not route around it as <b>[[3]]</b> does: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Detailed instructions but uneven results across layouts? Add a few worked examples of those layouts.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Few-shot is the most effective technique for consistent, actionable output when instructions alone are inconsistent"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-05", "was": "m7-s6-05", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch for latency-tolerant volume", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A schema change means the archive of past certificates must be extracted again, about 40,000 documents. The results are due at the end of next week. Each certificate needs one extraction call and no other tools. Brokers keep using the live pipeline for new certificates in the meantime. Which approach <b>best fits</b> this re-extraction?", "options": [{"t": "Run the archive through the live pipeline at night, in chunks that leave room for daytime traffic.", "why": "Works, but full price. It finishes in time, but every call costs the normal rate and someone has to schedule and watch the chunks.", "trap": "full price"}, {"t": "Submit the archive with the Message Batches API, one certificate per request, keyed by <code>custom_id</code>.", "why": "Best. Results are needed in days, not seconds, and each request is one call with no tools. Batches cost half as much, and <code>custom_id</code> matches each result to its certificate."}, {"t": "Have a coordinator spawn parallel extraction subagents across the archive, several per response.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It is fast, but it adds an orchestration layer and pays full price for speed nobody needs this week.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Send the archive to the live API with ten certificates in each request to cut the number of calls.", "why": "Works, but crowds each request. Fewer calls, but full price, attention spread across ten documents, and one bad file spoils the rest of its request.", "trap": "crowds each request"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The results are due at the end of next week.", "explanation": "Large, latency-tolerant jobs of single-call requests belong on the Message Batches API: half the cost, up to a 24-hour window, results matched by custom_id.", "eli5": {"question": "A big pile of old certificates must be redone, and the deadline is next week. The clue: nobody needs the answers right now, and each one is a single call.", "answer": "Posting 40,000 letters by express courier is fast and expensive. Bulk mail costs half and arrives within a day, which is fine when the deadline is next week. That bulk service is the <b>Message Batches API</b>, and the label on each envelope is the <b><code>custom_id</code></b>. <b>Rule: not urgent and one call each → batch. Someone waiting → live.</b>", "options": ["Express courier at night works, but you still pay express prices.", "Correct. Bulk mail: half the price, done within a day, each letter labelled.", "A team of couriers running in parallel is quick, but costly and not needed this week.", "Stuffing ten letters in one envelope saves stamps, but they get mixed up."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a big job needs the fastest setup</b>. The deadline is a week away, so speed is worth nothing here and cost is what matters. The Batches API gives <b>half the cost</b> and returns within a day. Its limit, no multi-turn tool calls, does not bite: each certificate is one call.", "diagram": "40,000 requests, each custom_id = certificate ID\n  → Message Batches API (≤ 24 h, 50% cost)\n  → results matched back by custom_id", "mapTitle": "Live API or Batches API?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Large job, results needed in days, one call each", "Message Batches API", "Half price, latency doesn't matter"], ["Someone is waiting, such as a broker uploading now", "Live API", "Batches have no latency promise"], ["Each document needs several tool-call turns", "Live API agent loop", "Batches don't support multi-turn tool calls"], ["Large job due within the hour", "Live API in parallel", "Speed is worth the price"], ["Some batch requests fail", "Resubmit only those, by <code>custom_id</code>", "No need to redo the rest"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "about 40,000 documents", "means": "Large volume, so cost per call matters."}, {"quote": "The results are due at the end of next week", "means": "The decider. Days of slack, so paying full price for speed (<b>[[2]]</b>, <b>[[0]]</b>) buys nothing."}, {"quote": "Each certificate needs one extraction call and no other tools", "means": "The Batches limit on multi-turn tool calls does not apply."}, {"quote": "Which approach best fits this re-extraction?", "means": "One document per request, matched by ID: <b>[[1]]</b>. Packing ten into one request (<b>[[3]]</b>) mixes documents."}], "rule": "Big, not urgent, one call per document → Message Batches API with custom_id.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Good for non-blocking/latency-tolerant (overnight reports, weekly audits)"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-07", "was": "m6-s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria for precision", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The <code>/review</code> command asks Claude to “flag anything that could cause problems, but be conservative”. Engineers ignore most of its findings. Over half are style or naming points the linter already enforces. Tenant-scoping findings, by contrast, are nearly always acted on. The team wants engineers to trust that a raised finding is worth reading. Which change <b>helps most</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three example findings that fix the layout and severity scale, one showing a borderline case.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Examples steady the format and severity, but they don't say that style points shouldn't be raised.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Have an independent Claude instance check each finding without the review context, keeping agreed ones.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second reviewer drops some noise, but it works from the same vague brief and accepts style points too.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a <code>detected_pattern</code> field to findings and track which patterns engineers dismiss this month.", "why": "Works, but delays the fix. Tracking dismissals finds noisy patterns, and this one is already found.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Rewrite the command to name the issue classes to raise and those to skip, with a code sample for each.", "why": "Best. Explicit categories with a code sample each replace “anything” and “be conservative”, and cut the noisy class at the source."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Over half are style or naming points the linter already enforces.", "explanation": "Vague instructions and “be conservative” don't improve precision. Name the categories to report and to skip, with concrete code examples, to keep trust in real findings.", "eli5": {"question": "A review command flags lots of things, and people ignore it. The clue: over half the noise is <i>one known kind</i> (style points the linter already checks).", "answer": "A smoke alarm that also beeps at toast gets its battery pulled, and then nobody hears the real fire. You fix it by telling it exactly what to react to: smoke, not toast. For Claude, that is <b>explicit criteria</b>: which issue classes to raise, which to skip, with a small code sample for each. “Be conservative” is like saying “beep less”. <b>Rule: you can name the noise → name it in the criteria.</b>", "options": ["Showing what a good beep sounds like doesn't stop it beeping at toast.", "A second alarm with the same settings beeps at the same toast.", "Logging every false alarm finds the cause, but you already know it is toast.", "Correct. Tell the alarm exactly what counts and what to ignore."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>noisy output needs better examples, a second check or more data</b>. Each of those has a place. Here the noise has a name: style and naming points that the linter covers. The exam guide says “be conservative” doesn't improve precision; <b>explicit categories with code examples</b> do.", "mapTitle": "A reviewer raises too much noise. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["One known class makes most of the noise", "Vague “flag anything” brief", "Name classes to raise and skip"], ["Format or severity varies run to run", "No shared model of output", "Few-shot example findings"], ["Noise source unknown", "Not yet measured", "Track <code>detected_pattern</code> dismissals"], ["Subtle misses in generated code", "Self-review bias", "Independent review instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "flag anything that could cause problems, but be conservative", "means": "The cause: a vague brief, with a confidence word that doesn't sharpen it."}, {"quote": "Over half are style or naming points the linter already enforces", "means": "The decider. The noise is a named class, already found, so tracking (<b>[[2]]</b>) isn't needed. Skip it explicitly."}, {"quote": "Tenant-scoping findings, by contrast, are nearly always acted on", "means": "Precision is the issue, not format (<b>[[0]]</b>). A second checker with the same brief (<b>[[1]]</b>) keeps the same line."}, {"quote": "Which change helps most?", "means": "Name what to raise and what to skip: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Noise from a nameable class → explicit raise/skip categories with code examples, not “be conservative”.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "High false-positive categories erode developer trust in accurate ones; define explicit severity criteria with concrete code examples"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-08", "was": "m6-s2-08", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for ambiguous cases", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "A <code>/new-job</code> command generates background jobs that must be safe to run twice. Its instructions have been revised several times and now fill a page. On simple jobs the output is right every time. Jobs that mix side effects are different. For a database write followed by a carrier API call, repeated runs chose different strategies. Which change is <b>most likely</b> to make the output consistent?", "options": [{"t": "Add two or three complete reference jobs for the mixed cases, each noting why its strategy fits.", "why": "Best. When rules alone give inconsistent output, a few targeted examples with their reasoning show Claude how to handle the hard cases."}, {"t": "Restate the rules as a decision table mapping each mix of side effects to its required strategy.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. A table is sharper prose, but the rules have been refined several times and still leave mixed cases open.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Have a separate Claude instance review each generated job for safe reruns before it is merged.", "why": "Works, but too late. An independent review catches bad jobs after they are written; it doesn't make generation consistent.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Have Claude state which strategy it will use, and why, before it writes each new job file.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Reasoning first helps a little, but Claude still picks from the same rules that leave these cases open.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Its instructions have been revised several times and now fill a page.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples are the most effective fix when instructions alone give inconsistent output. Use two to four targeted examples that show the reasoning on ambiguous cases.", "eli5": {"question": "A long page of rules works on easy jobs but gives different answers on the tricky mixed ones. The clue: the rules have <i>already been revised several times</i>.", "answer": "A trainee chef has read the rulebook many times and still plates the tricky dishes differently each night. Rewriting the rulebook again won't help much. Showing two or three finished tricky dishes, with a note on why each was done that way, will. Those are <b>few-shot examples</b>. <b>Rule: rules refined but tricky cases still vary → show a few worked examples with reasoning.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Show finished tricky dishes and why each was made that way.", "A neater rulebook is still a rulebook. The trainee has read it many times.", "A taster at the door catches bad plates, but the kitchen keeps making them.", "Saying your plan out loud helps a little, but you still plan from the same rulebook."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>inconsistent output means the rules need one more rewrite</b>. That is the right first step, and it has already been taken several times. When rules are clear on simple cases but leave ambiguous cases open, the exam guide's answer is <b>2–4 targeted examples that show the reasoning</b>.", "mapTitle": "Output varies. What makes it consistent?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Rules refined, hard cases still vary", "Prose can't pin down ambiguous cases", "2–4 examples with reasoning"], ["Rules vague or first draft", "Underspecified instructions", "Sharper rules or a table"], ["Bad output reaches merge", "No independent check", "Review by a fresh instance"], ["Output shape varies, choice is fine", "No structure enforced", "Schema or format example"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Its instructions have been revised several times and now fill a page", "means": "The decider. Rewording has been tried, so a table (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more of the same."}, {"quote": "On simple jobs the output is right every time", "means": "The rules work; the gap is only in the hard cases."}, {"quote": "For a database write followed by a carrier API call, repeated runs chose different strategies", "means": "An ambiguous case, which examples address. A reviewer (<b>[[2]]</b>) acts later; stated reasoning (<b>[[3]]</b>) uses the same rules."}, {"quote": "Which change is most likely to make the output consistent?", "means": "Show the hard cases: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Rules refined but ambiguous cases still vary → 2–4 targeted examples that show the reasoning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "2-4 targeted examples showing reasoning for the chosen action"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-06", "was": "m6-s5-06", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria for a retained category", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The reviewer's error-handling category causes most of the comments developers dismiss. A sample of dismissed comments shows two kinds. Some flag calls that the framework's error middleware already wraps. Others flag errors rethrown on purpose. The prompt only says “flag missing or inadequate error handling”. The category is a compliance control and must run on every pull request. What is the <b>most effective change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Switch the category off while a rewritten version is tested on past pull requests, then restore it.", "why": "Works, but breaks the control. Pausing a noisy category restores trust, but this one must run on every pull request.", "trap": "breaks the control"}, {"t": "Add a <code>detected_pattern</code> field to each finding, and tune the category from dismissal data.", "why": "Works, but delays the fix. Pattern tracking finds unknown causes of noise; the sample has already named both.", "trap": "delays the fix"}, {"t": "Redefine it as an exception caught and dropped, or a returned error left unused, with code samples.", "why": "Best. Concrete criteria with code cut the false positives while the check keeps running."}, {"t": "Have a second, independent Claude instance confirm each finding in this category before it is posted.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second judge reading the same vague rule inherits its vagueness, at twice the cost.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "A sample of dismissed comments shows two kinds.", "explanation": "Vague instructions produce false positives that erode trust. Replace them with categorical criteria and concrete code, not more review layers.", "eli5": {"question": "A review category gets dismissed a lot, and the team already knows the two reasons why. The clue: <i>the sample shows two kinds</i>, and the check can't be switched off.", "answer": "Think of a smoke alarm that goes off when you make toast. You know the cause, so you move or tune the sensor; you don't unplug it, because it is required. Here, the vague rule is the badly placed sensor. Write down exactly what counts (<b>explicit criteria</b>) with code samples. <b>Rule: when the noise has known causes, rewrite the vague instruction as concrete criteria.</b>", "options": ["Unplugging a required alarm while you tune it.", "Logging every false alarm to study later, when you already know it's the toaster.", "Correct. Tell the alarm exactly what counts as smoke.", "Hiring a second person to double-check every alarm, using the same vague idea of smoke."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a noisy category needs more checking or more data</b>. Here the cause is known: the instruction is vague, and the sample names both false-positive kinds. The fix is <b>categorical criteria with code examples</b>, which the exam guide puts ahead of review layers.", "mapTitle": "A review category is noisy. What do I do?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Causes known, category must stay", "Rewrite as explicit criteria with code", "Fixes the cause in place"], ["Category optional, trust collapsing", "Pause it, then fix it", "Stops the damage first"], ["Causes unknown", "Track <code>detected_pattern</code>", "Finds the causes"], ["Errors are judgement, rule is clear", "Independent review instance", "A fresh eye helps"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A sample of dismissed comments shows two kinds", "means": "The decider. The causes are known, so gathering data (<b>[[1]]</b>) delays the fix."}, {"quote": "The prompt only says “flag missing or inadequate error handling”", "means": "The vague rule is the cause. A second judge (<b>[[3]]</b>) reads the same vague rule."}, {"quote": "The category is a compliance control and must run on every pull request", "means": "It can't be paused (<b>[[0]]</b>). Fix it in place: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Known false-positive causes → rewrite the instruction as explicit criteria with code examples.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "High false-positive categories erode developer trust in accurate ones; define explicit severity criteria with concrete code examples."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-07", "was": "m6-s5-07", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for actionable output", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The review job returns findings through <code>--json-schema</code>. Each finding has file, line, severity, issue and <code>suggested_fix</code>. Parsing has not failed in a month. The trouble is what <code>suggested_fix</code> says. Some entries show replacement code, but many say things like “consider refactoring”. The prompt already describes the wanted fix in a paragraph. What is the <b>most effective first step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Make <code>suggested_fix</code> an object with required <code>replacement_code</code> and <code>explanation</code> strings.", "why": "Works, but shape, not content. A required string can still say “consider refactoring”; the schema already parses.", "trap": "shape, not content"}, {"t": "Add three complete sample findings to the prompt, each with a code fix, one spanning several lines.", "why": "Best. When written instructions give uneven output, a few targeted examples are the most effective way to get consistent, usable content."}, {"t": "Check each fix for a code block before posting, and re-prompt with that finding when one is missing.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. A retry catches vague fixes after the fact and pays a second call, instead of fixing the first answer.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Send each finding to a second Claude instance that rewrites its fix as replacement code.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It doubles calls on every run to repair output the first pass can be shown how to write.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The prompt already describes the wanted fix in a paragraph.", "explanation": "Schemas govern shape, not whether a field's content is useful. Few-shot examples are the strongest first lever for consistent, actionable content once instructions exist.", "eli5": {"question": "The findings are well-formed, but the suggested fixes are often vague. The clue: <i>a paragraph already describes what's wanted</i>, and it isn't working.", "answer": "Think of telling a new cook “make it look nice”. They try, but results vary. Show them three finished plates and they copy the standard. Examples in the prompt (<b>few-shot</b>) do that for Claude. <b>Rule: when instructions alone give uneven output, add 2–4 examples.</b>", "options": ["A stricter plate shape still lets the cook serve a vague dish.", "Correct. Show three finished plates.", "Sending back vague dishes works, but costs a second cook each time.", "A second cook re-plates every dish: double the work."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a schema can force good content</b>. A schema controls shape: fields, types, required. It cannot tell a useful fix from “consider refactoring”. When a written description already exists and output is still uneven, <b>examples</b> are the next lever.", "mapTitle": "Output is wrong. Which lever?", "map": {"head": ["What is wrong", "Lever", "Why"], "rows": [["Fields missing or JSON broken", "Schema / <code>--json-schema</code>", "Fixes shape"], ["Shape fine, content vague, instructions exist", "Few-shot examples", "Shows the standard"], ["Specific check fails after output", "Validate and retry with the error", "Safety net"], ["Judgement errors on hard cases", "Independent review", "Fresh eye"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Parsing has not failed in a month", "means": "Shape is fine, so a tighter schema (<b>[[0]]</b>) targets the wrong thing."}, {"quote": "Some entries show replacement code, but many say things like “consider refactoring”", "means": "The content is uneven."}, {"quote": "The prompt already describes the wanted fix in a paragraph", "means": "The decider. Instructions alone aren't enough. Examples: <b>[[1]]</b>. Retries (<b>[[2]]</b>) and rewriters (<b>[[3]]</b>) come later, if at all."}], "rule": "Instructions exist but output is uneven → add a few targeted examples before adding checks.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Few-shot is the most effective technique for consistent, actionable output when instructions alone are inconsistent."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-08", "was": "m6-s5-08", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch suitability for non-blocking work", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "A new post-merge job scans each merged change for risky database migrations. It posts results to the on-call channel and blocks no merge. Finance wants to move it to the Message Batches API for the discount. On-call engineers must read each scan within 90 minutes, before the next deploy goes out. A late scan lets a risky change ship. How should the team <b>respond</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move the scan to batches, one request per merged change, with its commit SHA as the <code>custom_id</code>.", "why": "Works, but no time limit. Batch results usually arrive sooner, but the API allows up to a day and promises no latency.", "trap": "no time limit"}, {"t": "Move the scan to batches, and resend a change as a normal request when no result comes within 45 minutes.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It adds a fallback path, eats half the window waiting, and pays full price for any change that falls back.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep normal requests, but scan all merges waiting for the next deploy together in one request.", "why": "Works, but dilutes attention. Packing many changes into one request saves a little and weakens the scan of each.", "trap": "dilutes attention"}, {"t": "Keep this scan on normal requests, and use batches for work read the next day, like the weekly report.", "why": "Best. A 90-minute deadline needs a time guarantee the Batches API does not give, even for a job that blocks no merge."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On-call engineers must read each scan within 90 minutes, before the next deploy goes out.", "explanation": "The Batches API saves 50% but has up to a 24-hour window and no latency SLA. Fit depends on whether someone needs the result by a deadline, not on whether the job blocks a merge.", "eli5": {"question": "Finance wants the cheap, slow lane for a scan. The scan doesn't block merges, but people must read it within a set time. The clue: <i>within 90 minutes, before the next deploy</i>.", "answer": "Think of cheap post that arrives “some time in the next day”. Fine for a weekly newsletter, bad for a ticket you need tonight. The <b>Message Batches API</b> is that cheap post: half price, up to 24 hours, no promise. <b>Rule: batch only work nobody needs by a deadline; “non-blocking” is not the same as “no deadline”.</b>", "options": ["Cheap post for something needed tonight. Usually fine, sometimes too late.", "Cheap post plus a courier backup: more moving parts, and still a long wait.", "Stuffing many letters in one envelope: each gets less care.", "Correct. Normal post for the urgent letter, cheap post for the newsletter."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>non-blocking means batch-friendly</b>. The real test is whether someone needs the result by a deadline. Batches can take up to 24 hours with <b>no latency promise</b>, so a 90-minute need rules them out even though no merge waits.", "diagram": "merge → scan → on-call reads → deploy (≤ 90 min)\nbatch: result in minutes ... or up to 24 h", "mapTitle": "Should this job use the Message Batches API?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "Batch?", "Why"], "rows": [["Weekly report read next week", "Yes", "Nobody waits"], ["Overnight report read in the morning", "Yes", "Hours of slack"], ["Non-blocking, but read within a deadline", "No", "No latency promise"], ["Blocking pre-merge check", "No", "Someone waits now"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It posts results to the on-call channel and blocks no merge", "means": "Non-blocking, which makes batch tempting (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "On-call engineers must read each scan within 90 minutes, before the next deploy goes out", "means": "The decider. A deadline needs a time guarantee. Fallback timers (<b>[[1]]</b>) patch around it."}, {"quote": "A late scan lets a risky change ship", "means": "Lateness matters. Keep it synchronous and batch the weekly report: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Batch only work with no deadline; non-blocking is not the same as latency-tolerant.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Message Batches API: 50% cost savings, up to 24-hour window, no latency SLA."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-09", "was": "m6-s5-09", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.6 Cross-file integration pass", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The review job checks each changed file in its own pass, and it catches bugs inside a single file well. It runs in a fresh session, apart from the job that wrote the code. Yet bugs that span files keep slipping through. In one case, the settings loader renamed a config key, while two other files still read the old key. What change <b>fixes the cause</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep per-file passes, then add one pass comparing each changed key or signature with its callers.", "why": "Best. Per-file passes cannot see contracts between files; a separate cross-file pass is the pattern for these misses."}, {"t": "Add a second, independent reviewer for each file, and merge both reviewers' comments before posting.", "why": "Works, but same blind spot. Independent review beats self-review, but the reviewer is already fresh, and a second per-file view still sees one file.", "trap": "same blind spot"}, {"t": "List shared config keys and function signatures with their users in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.", "why": "Works, but goes stale. The list helps, but someone must keep it current, and per-file passes still never compare two changed files.", "trap": "goes stale"}, {"t": "Replace the per-file passes with one pass over the whole diff, on a larger-context model.", "why": "Works, but dilutes attention. One pass sees across files, but attention thins over many files, and a bigger window is not better attention.", "trap": "dilutes attention"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Yet bugs that span files keep slipping through.", "explanation": "Split large reviews into per-file local passes plus a cross-file integration pass. Independent instances fix self-review bias, a different failure.", "eli5": {"question": "Each file is reviewed well alone, but bugs between files are missed. The clue: the misses <i>span files</i>, and the reviewer is already independent.", "answer": "Think of proofreading a book chapter by chapter. Each chapter reads fine, but chapter 2 renamed a character and chapter 5 still uses the old name. You need one extra read that checks names across chapters (a <b>cross-file integration pass</b>). <b>Rule: per-file passes for local bugs, plus one cross-file pass for contracts between files.</b>", "options": ["Correct. One more read that checks names across chapters.", "A second proofreader per chapter still never compares chapters.", "A list of character names helps, until someone forgets to update it.", "Reading the whole book in one go: you see everything but notice less."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>missed bugs mean the reviewer needs fresh eyes</b>. Fresh eyes fix self-review bias, and this reviewer already has them. These misses live <b>between files</b>, which no per-file pass can see.", "diagram": "pass 1: Settings.kt   ✓ (key renamed)\npass 2: Booking.kt    ✓ (reads old key)\npass 3: cross-file    ✗ key mismatch found", "mapTitle": "Review misses bugs. Which fix?", "map": {"head": ["Where the misses are", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Between files", "Cross-file integration pass", "Sees contracts across files"], ["Inside files, reviewer is the author's session", "Independent review instance", "No generation bias"], ["Inside files, large diff in one pass", "Split into per-file passes", "Attention dilution"], ["Project context missing", "<code>CLAUDE.md</code> context", "Background for every pass"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "it catches bugs inside a single file well", "means": "Per-file passes work; keep them."}, {"quote": "It runs in a fresh session, apart from the job that wrote the code", "means": "Already independent, so another per-file reviewer (<b>[[1]]</b>) adds little."}, {"quote": "Yet bugs that span files keep slipping through", "means": "The decider. A cross-file pass: <b>[[0]]</b>. One big pass (<b>[[3]]</b>) dilutes attention."}], "rule": "Per-file passes plus a cross-file integration pass; independent review solves a different problem.", "guide": {"obj": "4.6 Multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures", "quote": "Multi-pass: per-file local analysis + cross-file integration passes (avoid attention dilution, contradictions)."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s5-10", "was": "m6-s5-10", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retry with specific feedback", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 2, "question": "Some review findings cite a line outside every diff hunk, so the inline-comment API rejects them. Most are off by the hunk's starting line. The reviewer reads the raw diff and counts lines from each hunk header itself. A posting script already parses those headers. The team can afford an extra model call only on runs that need one. Which <b>two changes</b> fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Number every diff line given to the reviewer with its new-file line, taken from the parsed headers.", "why": "Best. The error is mechanical counting; giving Claude the numbers removes most of it at no extra call."}, {"t": "Send all findings to a second Claude instance that checks each cited line against the diff before posting.", "why": "Works, but costs every run. It catches some errors, but spends a call on every run, and reading the same raw diff it can miscount too.", "trap": "costs every run"}, {"t": "Check cited lines against the parsed ranges, and send one follow-up listing valid ranges for misses.", "why": "Best. A code check with specific feedback fixes what is left, and spends the extra call only on runs that need it."}, {"t": "Add two sample findings to the prompt that cite the right line deep inside a long hunk.", "why": "Works, but still counting. Examples show the format, but Claude still counts lines itself, so offset errors remain.", "trap": "still counting"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The reviewer reads the raw diff and counts lines from each hunk header itself.", "explanation": "Give the model the facts it is miscomputing, validate its output against them, and retry with the specific error. Spend extra calls only where validation shows a need.", "eli5": {"question": "Comments get rejected because Claude counts diff lines wrong. Which two changes fix it within budget? The clues: Claude <i>counts lines itself</i>, and an extra call is allowed <i>only when needed</i>.", "answer": "Think of a reader giving page numbers from a book with no page numbers printed. They miscount. First, print the page numbers (<b>number the diff lines</b>). Then check each cited page and, if one is wrong, tell the reader exactly which pages are valid (<b>retry with specific feedback</b>). <b>Rule: supply the facts the model miscomputes, then validate and retry with the specific error.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Print the page numbers so nobody counts.", "A second reader on every book costs every time, and may miscount too.", "Correct. Check the numbers and send back only the wrong ones, with the valid range.", "Showing good examples, while the reader still counts pages by hand."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>wrong output needs a second reviewer</b>. These errors are not judgement; they are <b>mechanical miscounts</b>. Give Claude the line numbers so it doesn't count, then check with code and retry with the specific error only when something is still wrong.", "diagram": "diff → script adds line numbers → reviewer\nfindings → script checks ranges\n  miss → one follow-up: “line 88 not in hunk; valid 40–62”", "mapTitle": "The model gets a mechanical fact wrong. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Cause", "Fix", "Cost"], "rows": [["Model computes a fact code knows", "Give it the fact", "No extra call"], ["Some errors remain", "Code check + retry with the specific error", "Extra call only on misses"], ["Judgement errors", "Independent review instance", "Extra call every run"], ["Format unclear", "Few-shot examples", "No extra call"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Most are off by the hunk's starting line", "means": "A counting error, not a judgement error."}, {"quote": "The reviewer reads the raw diff and counts lines from each hunk header itself", "means": "The decider. Hand it the numbers: <b>[[0]]</b>. Examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) leave it counting."}, {"quote": "A posting script already parses those headers", "means": "Code knows the valid ranges, so it can check and give specific feedback: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The team can afford an extra model call only on runs that need one", "means": "A verifier on every run (<b>[[1]]</b>) breaks the budget."}], "rule": "Supply facts the model miscomputes; validate with code and retry with the specific error.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retry-with-error-feedback: append specific validation errors on retry."}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-04", "was": "m6-s6-04", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Extensible enum vs nullable", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The schema's <code>insured_type</code> field is an enum with four values: individual, corporation, LLC and partnership. Many certificates name other kinds of insured, such as a trust, a joint venture or a city council. The wording is printed clearly. The extraction forces these into corporation. New kinds keep turning up as the broker signs new clients. Which schema change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add <code>other</code> to the enum, plus an <code>insured_type_detail</code> string for the printed text.", "why": "Best. An extensible enum keeps the four common values and records any other kind exactly as printed."}, {"t": "Make <code>insured_type</code> nullable, and leave it empty when no listed value matches.", "why": "Works, but loses information. Null stops the wrong label, but the kind is printed clearly and null throws it away.", "trap": "loses information"}, {"t": "Add an <code>unclear</code> value with a note field, for insureds whose kind is hard to read.", "why": "Works, but wrong label. It stops the forcing, but these kinds are clear, not hard to read, so they get mislabelled as unclear.", "trap": "wrong label"}, {"t": "Keep the four values, and add examples to the prompt showing how other kinds map onto them.", "why": "Works, but standardises a wrong answer. Examples make the mapping consistent, but no listed value is correct for a trust or a council.", "trap": "standardises a wrong answer"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "New kinds keep turning up as the broker signs new clients.", "explanation": "Use nullable fields when the source lacks the information, <code>unclear</code> when the text is ambiguous, and <code>other</code> plus a detail field when a clear value falls outside the list.", "eli5": {"question": "A fixed list of choices doesn't cover what the documents say. The clue is: <i>the wording is printed clearly</i> and <i>new kinds keep turning up</i>. The value exists and is readable; it just isn't on the list.", "answer": "Think of a form with tick boxes for Mr, Mrs and Ms. Someone writes Dr. You add a box marked <i>Other: ____</i>, not a blank and not a guess. That is an <b>extensible enum</b>: an <code>other</code> value plus a detail field. Null is for when the information is missing; <code>unclear</code> is for when it can't be read. <b>Rule: clear value outside the list → other + detail.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Common kinds stay tidy, and anything new is kept word for word.", "Close. Null is right when the certificate says nothing. Here it says clearly, so null discards it.", "Unclear is for smudged or ambiguous text. These are clear, so the label would be wrong.", "Examples make the wrong mapping consistent; a trust is still not a corporation."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>any value that doesn't fit should be left empty</b>. Empty (null) means the source lacks the information. Here the kind of insured is <b>present and clear</b>, just outside the list, and new kinds keep coming. The schema needs room for them.", "mapTitle": "The value doesn't fit the enum. What is it really?", "map": {"head": ["What the document shows", "Schema answer", "Why"], "rows": [["Nothing about this field", "Nullable field, return null", "Prevents fabrication"], ["Text that is ambiguous or illegible", "<code>unclear</code> value", "Honest about doubt"], ["A clear value outside the list, new ones keep coming", "<code>other</code> + detail field", "Keeps the real value"], ["A clear value that fits a listed one", "The listed value", "Normal case"], ["A few stable new values", "Add them to the enum", "Simple when the set settles"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Many certificates name other kinds of insured, such as a trust, a joint venture or a city council", "means": "The values are real and outside the four."}, {"quote": "The wording is printed clearly", "means": "Not missing, so null (<b>[[1]]</b>) loses it; not ambiguous, so <code>unclear</code> (<b>[[2]]</b>) mislabels it."}, {"quote": "The extraction forces these into corporation", "means": "Examples of how to map them (<b>[[3]]</b>) keep forcing them."}, {"quote": "New kinds keep turning up as the broker signs new clients", "means": "The decider. The list can't be finished; leave room: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Clear value outside a fixed list → add other plus a detail field; null only when the source lacks it.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "enum \"unclear\"/\"other\" + detail for extensible categories"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-05", "was": "m6-s6-05", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retry with specific error feedback", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A validator checks each policy number against the format its carrier uses. On a mismatch, the pipeline resends the certificate with “Output failed validation. Try again.” Second attempts rarely pass. In sampled failures, the right number is printed on the certificate. The model took a nearby number instead, such as the certificate number or the carrier's NAIC code. Which change <b>most improves</b> second attempts?", "options": [{"t": "Send each certificate that fails the format check to a reviewer, and stop retrying it.", "why": "Works, but gives up too early. A person will fix it, but the number is printed, so an informed retry could fix most of these without one.", "trap": "gives up too early"}, {"t": "Add a <code>matches_carrier_format</code> flag that the model fills in beside the policy number.", "why": "Works, but no new information. A self-check flag can make the model look twice, but the retry still does not say what failed.", "trap": "no new information"}, {"t": "Resend with the specific error: the value returned, the carrier's format and the field it broke.", "why": "Best. Appending the specific validation error tells the model what was wrong and what to look for."}, {"t": "Add example certificates to the prompt that mark the policy number box apart from nearby numbers.", "why": "Works, but helps first tries. Examples may cut the misreads, but the retry message still says only “try again”.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "In sampled failures, the right number is printed on the certificate.", "explanation": "Retry with error feedback appends the specific validation failure. It works for misreads and format errors, not for information missing from the source.", "eli5": {"question": "Retries fail because they say nothing useful. The clue is: <i>the right number is printed on the certificate</i>. The answer is there to find, if the model is told what went wrong.", "answer": "Think of a teacher who hands back homework marked only \"wrong, try again\". The pupil tends to make the same mistake. If the teacher writes \"you copied the page number, not the answer\", the second try is far better. That is <b>retry with error feedback</b>: send the value returned and the rule it broke. It only helps when the right value is in the document. <b>Rule: when the answer is in the source, retry with the specific error.</b>", "options": ["Close. Handing it to a person is right when the number is missing. Here it is printed.", "A self-check box may help a little, but the model still isn't told what failed.", "Correct. The model learns exactly what was wrong on the second try.", "Examples may help first attempts, but the retry is still blind."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a retry that fails needs a person</b> or a better prompt. But these failures are misreads: the correct value is <b>on the page</b>. The retry fails because its message carries <b>no detail</b>. Tell the model what it returned and what rule it broke.", "mapTitle": "A retry keeps failing. What is the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Value is in the document, retries repeat the error", "Generic retry message", "Append the specific validation error"], ["Value is not in the document", "Information absent", "Null or route to a person"], ["First attempts misread one layout", "Model hasn't seen it", "Few-shot examples"], ["Totals don't add up", "Semantic error", "Calculated vs stated fields, conflict flag"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "On a mismatch, the pipeline resends the certificate with “Output failed validation. Try again.”", "means": "The retry carries no detail. Examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't change that message."}, {"quote": "Second attempts rarely pass", "means": "The model repeats its mistake without guidance. A self-check flag (<b>[[1]]</b>) adds no guidance either."}, {"quote": "In sampled failures, the right number is printed on the certificate", "means": "The decider. The information is present, so a reviewer (<b>[[0]]</b>) isn't needed for most."}, {"quote": "The model took a nearby number instead, such as the certificate number or the carrier's NAIC code", "means": "A misread, which a specific error message can correct: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Value present but misread → retry with the specific validation error. Value absent → don't retry.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retry-with-error-feedback: append specific validation errors on retry"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-06", "was": "m6-s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 tool_choice any vs forced", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "Each document goes alone to a request with three tools: <code>extract_certificate</code>, <code>extract_endorsement</code> and <code>extract_schedule</code>. Uploads carry no type label. With <code>tool_choice</code> on auto, some replies are a prose summary with no tool call, and the parser drops them. When the model does call a tool, it picks the right one. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Expand each extractor's description with the headings and features of its document type.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Richer descriptions help the model pick a tool, but it already picks well; the gap is replies with no tool at all.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add a first call that labels the document type, then force the matching extractor by name.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Classify-then-force is right when the model picks the wrong tool. Here it picks well, so the extra call adds cost for nothing.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep auto, and when <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>end_turn</code>, resend saying a tool call is required.", "why": "Works, but patches after. Detecting and resending recovers the dropped replies, but each costs a second call when the API can require a tool call on the first.", "trap": "patches after"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> on every extraction call, with the schemas unchanged.", "why": "Best. <code>any</code> requires a tool call and leaves the choice of tool to the model, which already chooses well."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "When the model does call a tool, it picks the right one.", "explanation": "<code>auto</code> may return text, <code>any</code> requires some tool call, and a forced tool requires one named tool. Use <code>any</code> when a call is needed but the right tool is not known in advance.", "eli5": {"question": "Some replies come back as plain text instead of a tool call. The clue is: <i>when the model does call a tool, it picks the right one</i>. Choosing is fine; the only problem is not calling at all.", "answer": "Think of a post room with three trays. The clerk always picks the right tray, but sometimes writes a note instead of filing the letter. You don't need to tell the clerk which tray; you just say \"every letter goes in a tray\". That is <code>tool_choice: any</code>: <b>a tool call is required, and the model still picks which</b>. Forcing one named tool is for when you already know which tray. <b>Rule: must call a tool, model picks well → any.</b>", "options": ["Better labels on the trays help picking, but picking is already right.", "Close. Labelling first and forcing a tool fits when the model picks the wrong tool. It doesn't.", "Catching text replies and asking again works, but every miss costs an extra call.", "Correct. Every reply is a tool call, and the model keeps choosing the right one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a missing tool call means the model is confused about which tool</b>. But when it calls one, it chooses correctly. The only failure is <b>no call at all</b>, which <code>auto</code> allows. The API setting <code>any</code> removes that option on the first try.", "mapTitle": "What should tool_choice be?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Setting", "Why"], "rows": [["Text replies are acceptable", "<code>auto</code>", "Model may answer without a tool"], ["A tool call is required, model picks well", "<code>any</code>", "Must call some tool"], ["A tool call is required, model picks the wrong one", "Classify, then force the named tool", "You decide the tool"], ["One step must run first, such as metadata", "Forced tool on that call", "Fixed order"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Uploads carry no type label", "means": "The code can't know the right tool in advance, so forcing a named one needs an extra classify call (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "With tool_choice on auto, some replies are a prose summary with no tool call, and the parser drops them", "means": "The failure is text instead of a call. Resending after <code>end_turn</code> (<b>[[2]]</b>) repairs it a call late."}, {"quote": "When the model does call a tool, it picks the right one", "means": "The decider. Selection is fine, so better descriptions (<b>[[0]]</b>) aim at the wrong thing. Require a call: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Model picks well but sometimes replies in text → tool_choice any.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_choice: \"auto\" (may return text), \"any\" (must call a tool)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-07", "was": "m6-s6-07", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria vs few-shot", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Each result includes a <code>coverage_concerns</code> list for account managers. The instruction says “list anything unusual about the coverage”. Most listed items turn out to be routine wording, such as standard exclusions and the usual disclaimer. Real problems get lost among them. The broker's compliance checklist already defines a concern as five cases, each with how it reads on a certificate. What <b>most improves precision</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three annotated certificates to the prompt that mark which items belonged on the list.", "why": "Works, but only usually works. Examples help where a line is hard to put into words, but the checklist already states the line.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Swap the open request for the checklist's definitions, and treat everything else as routine.", "why": "Best. Explicit categorical criteria are the main lever for precision; a vague word like “unusual” produces false positives."}, {"t": "Tag each item with a <code>detected_pattern</code>, track which ones managers dismiss, and prune those.", "why": "Works, but slow. Tracking dismissals finds unknown noise over time, but the definition of a real concern already exists and can be used now.", "trap": "slow"}, {"t": "Have a second, independent Claude instance drop routine items before the list reaches managers.", "why": "Works, but doubles the vague rule. A second reviewer still judges “routine” without a definition, at twice the cost.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The broker's compliance checklist already defines a concern as five cases, each with how it reads on a certificate.", "explanation": "Precision comes from explicit categorical criteria. Examples support criteria; review layers don't fix a vague definition.", "eli5": {"question": "A list is full of noise because the instruction is vague. The clue is: <i>the compliance checklist already defines a concern as five cases</i>. The rule exists; the prompt just doesn't use it.", "answer": "Think of telling a new shop assistant \"tell me about anything odd\". You get told about everything. Hand them the store's list of five things to report, and they report those. That is <b>explicit criteria</b>: replace a vague word with the defined categories and how each looks. <b>Rule: vague instruction, many false alarms → write the categories down.</b>", "options": ["Close. Examples teach a line that is hard to describe. This line is already written down.", "Correct. The model reports only the five defined cases.", "Learning from dismissals takes time to rediscover a definition you already have.", "A second checker with the same vague idea of routine makes the same mistakes, at twice the cost."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>noise needs filtering or more examples</b>. The cause is the word <b>“unusual”</b>: it has no boundary, so the model lists anything that might count. The boundary already exists in the checklist. <b>Put the categories in the prompt.</b>", "mapTitle": "Too many false positives. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best lever", "Why"], "rows": [["Vague instruction, written definition exists", "Explicit categorical criteria", "Draws the line"], ["Line is hard to put into words", "Few-shot examples with reasoning", "Shows the line"], ["Noise source unknown", "Track <code>detected_pattern</code> on dismissals", "Finds the cause"], ["Subtle errors a self-review misses", "Independent review instance", "Fresh eyes"], ["“Be conservative” in the prompt", "Doesn't help precision", "No categories"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The instruction says “list anything unusual about the coverage”", "means": "The cause: a vague instruction."}, {"quote": "Most listed items turn out to be routine wording, such as standard exclusions and the usual disclaimer", "means": "False positives. A second filter (<b>[[3]]</b>) uses the same vague idea of routine."}, {"quote": "The broker's compliance checklist already defines a concern as five cases, each with how it reads on a certificate", "means": "The decider. The line can be stated, so examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and dismissal tracking (<b>[[2]]</b>) are slower routes to it. State it: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "When a definition exists, put explicit categories in the prompt; it beats examples or filters for precision.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-08", "was": "m6-s6-08", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for ambiguous cases", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Auto liability limits go into an <code>auto_limits</code> object. It has a <code>basis</code> of combined or split, plus fields for each amount and any sublimit. The brokers confirm every wording seen so far fits this shape. The prompt defines each basis and where sublimits go. Yet mixed wordings, such as a combined limit with a per-person sublimit, come out differently from run to run. What is the <b>most effective next step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three worked examples of these cases to the prompt, with the expected JSON and a reason.", "why": "Best. When written definitions still give inconsistent results, a few worked cases that show the reasoning are the most effective fix."}, {"t": "Add an <code>other</code> basis with a detail field, and send those limits to a reviewer.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Other-plus-detail suits values outside the schema. These fit it, so this sends data that could be structured to a person.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Split it into two calls: classify the basis first, then extract the amounts with a basis prompt.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Chaining isolates the steps, but the unstable part is the basis decision itself, which the first call still makes.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Check that the amounts and sublimits are consistent, and retry with any problem described.", "why": "Works, but partly. A consistency check catches some bad encodings, but many inconsistent mappings are still valid, so it can't tell which is right.", "trap": "only partly"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The brokers confirm every wording seen so far fits this shape.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples, including ambiguous cases with the reasoning shown, are the most effective way to get consistent output when instructions alone are inconsistent.", "eli5": {"question": "The schema is fine and the rules are written, yet results wobble. The clue is: <i>every wording seen so far fits this shape</i> and <i>the prompt defines each basis</i>. Instructions alone aren't enough; the model needs to see it done.", "answer": "Think of learning to fold a shirt from written steps. The tricky collar still comes out different each time, until someone shows you three times. That is <b>few-shot prompting</b>: a few worked mixed cases, each with the expected JSON and a line on why. <b>Rule: instructions given, output still inconsistent → show 2–4 worked examples of the hard cases.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Worked hard cases show how to map them the same way every run.", "Close. Other-plus-detail fits values the schema can't hold. These fit it.", "Splitting the call still leaves the shaky decision in the first step.", "A check catches some mistakes but often can't tell two valid shapes apart."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>inconsistent output means the schema is wrong</b> or the task needs splitting. But the brokers confirm every wording fits, and the rules are already written. What's missing is <b>demonstration</b>: worked examples of the hard, mixed cases.", "mapTitle": "Output varies run to run. What is the lever?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best lever", "Why"], "rows": [["Schema fits, rules written, hard cases vary", "2–4 worked examples with reasoning", "Shows the mapping"], ["Values fall outside the schema", "<code>other</code> + detail", "Room for new values"], ["One prompt does several jobs badly", "Prompt chaining", "One job per call"], ["Values break arithmetic rules", "Validate and retry with the error", "Catches semantic errors"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The brokers confirm every wording seen so far fits this shape", "means": "The decider. The schema is not the problem, so <code>other</code> (<b>[[1]]</b>) answers a different question."}, {"quote": "The prompt defines each basis and where sublimits go", "means": "Instructions alone are already in place."}, {"quote": "Yet mixed wordings, such as a combined limit with a per-person sublimit, come out differently from run to run", "means": "Inconsistency on ambiguous cases. Splitting calls (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps the same decision; a check (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't always see it. Show it: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Instructions alone inconsistent on hard cases → a few worked examples with the reasoning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Few-shot is the most effective technique for consistent, actionable output when instructions alone are inconsistent"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-09", "was": "s5q-s2-09", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for ambiguous cases", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A codemod's runs disagree only on three awkward call sites. One is in a deprecated module, one sits behind a feature flag that is off, and one is in a fixture testing old behaviour. The engineer described the handling of each in prose, yet runs still vary. Each case turns on judgement about intent. More variants like these will turn up. What communicates the intended handling <b>most reliably</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three worked examples from the repo, each with the code, the action taken and a line on why.", "why": "Best. Few-shot examples that show the reasoning behind each choice give the most consistent handling of awkward cases. The reasoning carries over to new variants."}, {"t": "Add a table that maps each awkward condition to its required action, with one row per known case.", "why": "Works, but only for listed cases. Explicit criteria suit a complete list. New variants will turn up, and a table says nothing outside its rows.", "trap": "only listed cases"}, {"t": "Have Claude interview the engineer about the awkward cases, then add the answers to the prose.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Interviews find requirements the author has not thought of. Here the engineer knows the handling and has written it down.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return each decision through a schema whose action enum has an <code>unclear</code> value with a detail field.", "why": "Works, but defers the call. An unclear value reports the ambiguity for a person to settle, instead of handling it the way the engineer intends.", "trap": "defers the call"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "More variants like these will turn up.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples are the most effective technique when instructions alone give inconsistent results. Two to four examples that show the reasoning for each choice let the model handle new variants.", "eli5": {"question": "Clear cases go fine; judgement calls vary from run to run, even with written instructions. The clue: each case needs judgement, and new ones will appear that nobody has listed.", "answer": "A new referee learns tricky calls best by watching replays with the senior referee explaining each decision. Rules on paper don't cover every play; the explained replays teach how to think. That is <b>few-shot examples with reasoning</b>. <b>Rule: prose instructions give inconsistent judgement calls → show 2–4 worked examples that explain why.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Explained replays teach the thinking, which carries to new plays.", "A rule card works for the plays it lists. New kinds of plays aren't on it.", "Asking the senior referee questions helps when the rules are unknown. Here they are known and written down.", "Flagging hard calls for review works, but the referee is meant to make the call."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>writing the rules more precisely</b> will fix inconsistent judgement. A table helps when the cases can be listed in full. These cases turn on intent, and new variants will appear. <b>Worked examples that show the reasoning</b> teach the judgement itself, so it carries over to cases nobody listed.", "mapTitle": "Output is inconsistent: what will steady it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best technique", "Why"], "rows": [["Judgement calls vary; new variants expected", "Few-shot examples with reasoning", "Teaches the judgement"], ["A complete, fixed list of cases", "Explicit criteria or a table", "Covers every listed case"], ["Author hasn't thought of the cases yet", "Interview pattern", "Finds the requirements"], ["Some cases truly cannot be decided", "Enum with <code>unclear</code> + detail", "Flags them honestly"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "disagree only on three awkward call sites", "means": "Clear cases work; the problem is judgement on edge cases."}, {"quote": "The engineer described the handling of each in prose, yet runs still vary", "means": "Instructions alone are inconsistent. The handling is known, so an interview (<b>[[2]]</b>) answers a different problem."}, {"quote": "Each case turns on judgement about intent", "means": "The engineer wants these decided, not flagged (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "More variants like these will turn up", "means": "The decider. A table (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers only its rows; examples with reasoning carry over."}, {"quote": "What communicates the intended handling most reliably?", "means": "<b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Inconsistent judgement on ambiguous cases → 2–4 few-shot examples that show the reasoning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Demonstrate ambiguous-case handling; enable generalization to novel patterns"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-10", "was": "s5q-s2-10", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.6 Multi-pass review architecture", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A CI job reviews the finished codemod by running <code>claude -p</code> on a clean checkout of the branch. Its report on about 70 changed files is thorough for the first few and thin in the middle. It missed a moved module whose caller still uses the old import. The team wants the review to catch problems in every file and between files. Which review design <b>best fits</b> this change?", "options": [{"t": "Have a separate Claude instance, with no access to the codemod's session, review the full diff.", "why": "Works, but already done. An independent reviewer beats self-review, but the CI job already starts fresh on a clean checkout. It is still one pass over everything.", "trap": "already in place"}, {"t": "Run the review with <code>--output-format json</code> and a schema, and post findings as PR comments.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Structured findings improve how the review is delivered, not what a single pass notices.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add the team's review criteria and the new API's contract to <code>CLAUDE.md</code> for the CI reviewer.", "why": "Works, but more context, same spread. CLAUDE.md is the right home for review criteria, but one pass is still spread thin across every file.", "trap": "still one pass"}, {"t": "Review each file on its own for local issues, then do a second pass over calls between files.", "why": "Best. Per-file passes give each file full attention, and a separate integration pass checks contracts between files, where the missed caller sits."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Its report on about 70 changed files is thorough for the first few and thin in the middle.", "explanation": "Large multi-file changes need per-file passes for local correctness and a cross-file integration pass for contracts between files. One pass over everything dilutes attention.", "eli5": {"question": "One reviewer reads 70 files in one go. They are careful at the start, skim the middle, and miss a problem that sits between two files. The clue: attention thins as the pile grows.", "answer": "An exam marker with a huge stack marks the first scripts carefully and rushes the rest. Better: mark each script on its own, then do one separate check that the answers fit together. That is a <b>multi-pass review</b>: per-file passes plus a cross-file integration pass. <b>Rule: big multi-file change → review each file alone, then review the links between them.</b>", "options": ["A fresh marker helps when the author marks their own work. This marker is already fresh; the stack is the problem.", "A neater report form changes how marks are written down, not how carefully scripts are read.", "A better marking guide helps, but the marker still rushes through a huge stack.", "Correct. Each script gets full attention, and a separate check catches answers that don't fit together."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a weak review means the reviewer is not independent</b>. This CI reviewer already starts fresh on a clean checkout. The real problem is <b>attention spread across too many files in one pass</b>. Split it: one pass per file, then an integration pass for the links between files.", "diagram": "file 1 -> pass   file 2 -> pass   ...   file 70 -> pass\n          \\________ integration pass ________/\n             calls and imports between files", "mapTitle": "A code review misses things: what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Thin in the middle, cross-file bugs missed", "Attention spread across one pass", "Per-file passes + integration pass"], ["Reviewer agrees with the author's reasoning", "Self-review in the same session", "Independent instance"], ["Findings hard to post or parse", "Free-text output", "<code>--output-format json</code> + schema"], ["Reviewer ignores team standards", "Missing project context", "Criteria in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "running claude -p on a clean checkout of the branch", "means": "Already independent of the session that wrote the code, so a separate instance (<b>[[0]]</b>) changes little."}, {"quote": "Its report on about 70 changed files is thorough for the first few and thin in the middle", "means": "The decider. Attention dilution in one pass. More context (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a new format (<b>[[1]]</b>) keeps one pass."}, {"quote": "It missed a moved module whose caller still uses the old import", "means": "A bug between files: needs an integration pass."}, {"quote": "Which review design best fits this change?", "means": "Per-file plus cross-file passes: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Big multi-file change → per-file passes plus a cross-file integration pass.", "guide": {"obj": "4.6 Multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures", "quote": "Multi-pass: per-file local analysis + cross-file integration passes (avoid attention dilution, contradictions)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-01", "was": "s5q-s5-01", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Criteria for test quality", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The test generator writes tests for each new endpoint. Every new test passes, and coverage has gone up a lot. A reviewer read a sample and found the same habit in nearly all of them. Each test calls the endpoint and checks only that the response is not empty. The prompt's only guidance on quality is “write thorough tests”. What is the <b>most effective</b> change?", "options": [{"t": "Add three finished tests from the repository to the prompt, each checking an exact expected response.", "why": "Works, but too early. Good examples would raise the bar, but the model has no stated rule to apply, so it must guess what the examples have in common.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Replace “thorough” with checkable rules, such as asserting the exact status and body for each input.", "why": "Best. “Thorough” gives the model nothing to check itself against. Explicit criteria say what a real assertion is."}, {"t": "Have a separate Claude instance, without the generator's context, review each test before commit.", "why": "Works, but same vague bar. A fresh reviewer catches some weak tests, but it judges against the same undefined idea of “thorough”.", "trap": "same vague bar"}, {"t": "Fail the job when mutation testing shows the new tests miss bugs injected into the endpoint.", "why": "Works, but too late. Mutation testing exposes weak tests after they are written, but the generator still doesn't know what a good test checks.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The prompt's only guidance on quality is “write thorough tests”.", "explanation": "Vague quality words such as “thorough” do not change behaviour; explicit, checkable criteria do. Define what counts first, then add examples where defined criteria are applied unevenly.", "eli5": {"question": "Generated tests pass but check almost nothing. The clue: the only guidance is “write thorough tests”. This is not about the volume of tests; it's about what each one checks.", "answer": "Telling a new cook “make it tasty” gets you anything. A recipe card that says “two teaspoons of salt, bake until golden” gets you the dish. The recipe card is <b>explicit criteria</b>: rules the model can check its own work against. <b>Rule: vague quality word in the prompt → replace it with checkable criteria.</b>", "options": ["Showing finished dishes helps once the recipe exists. Without it, the cook guesses what made them good.", "Correct. Write the recipe: what each test must assert.", "A taster with no recipe judges by the same vague word “tasty”.", "A strict food inspector catches bad dishes afterwards, but the cook still has no recipe."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>weak output means the model needs more help seeing good output</b>, so you add examples or a reviewer. The real gap is that nobody has said <b>what a good test checks</b>. “Thorough” is a vague word, and the exam guide is clear: explicit criteria beat vague instructions.", "mapTitle": "Generated output is weak. What's missing?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix first", "Why"], "rows": [["Only a vague quality word in the prompt", "Explicit, checkable criteria", "Says exactly what counts"], ["Criteria exist but are applied unevenly", "2–4 finished examples", "Shows how to apply them"], ["Criteria and examples exist; subtle errors slip", "Independent review instance", "Fresh eyes catch what the author missed"], ["Need proof that tests catch bugs", "Mutation testing in CI", "Measures test strength after the fact"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Every new test passes, and coverage has gone up a lot", "means": "The numbers look good, so the problem is quality, not quantity."}, {"quote": "Each test calls the endpoint and checks only that the response is not empty", "means": "The same weak habit everywhere. The model is consistent; it just doesn't know what to assert."}, {"quote": "The prompt's only guidance on quality is “write thorough tests”", "means": "The decider. There are no criteria to apply, so define them: <b>[[1]]</b>. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a reviewer (<b>[[2]]</b>) both lean on a definition that doesn't exist yet."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective change?", "means": "Fix the cause in the prompt. Mutation testing (<b>[[3]]</b>) catches the result later."}], "rule": "Vague words like “thorough” don't steer the model. Replace them with checkable criteria.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-02", "was": "s5q-s5-02", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot examples", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "Generated tests now check real values, but their layout changes from run to run. About half use the team's table-driven layout with one shared fixture. The rest write one function per case and rebuild the fixture each time. The project <code>CLAUDE.md</code> describes the table-driven layout in two paragraphs. <code>/memory</code> shows that this file loads in the job. What is the <b>most effective</b> change?", "options": [{"t": "Move the test conventions into a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with a <code>paths:</code> glob for tests.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Path-scoped rules are a tidy home for test conventions, but the conventions already load; moving them doesn't make them followed.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Rewrite the two paragraphs as a numbered checklist covering case tables, fixtures and names.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. A checklist is a little clearer, but the model already reads a written description and still follows it only half the time.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Add two or three finished tests from the repository to the generation prompt as models.", "why": "Best. When written instructions give inconsistent output, a few concrete examples are the most effective way to show a layout."}, {"t": "Add a CI lint check that fails any new test file not written in the table-driven layout.", "why": "Works, but catches, doesn't teach. The check stops bad layouts merging, but the generator keeps producing them and the job keeps failing.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "About half use the team's table-driven layout with one shared fixture.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples are the most effective technique when instructions alone give inconsistent output. They carry structure that prose describes poorly. First check the instructions load (here /memory shows they do), then show, don't tell.", "eli5": {"question": "The test layout is right about half the time. The rules are written down and do load. The clue: <i>half</i> follow the description, so the words are read but not reliably applied.", "answer": "If half your class gets a layout wrong after reading the instructions, you don't rewrite the instructions again. You pin two finished examples on the board. Those are <b>few-shot examples</b>: finished tests in the prompt that show the exact shape. <b>Rule: written rules followed only some of the time → show 2–3 examples.</b>", "options": ["Moving the instructions to a new folder doesn't help; the class already has them.", "Rewriting the instructions as a list is still more instructions, which is what half-works today.", "Correct. Pin finished examples on the board; copying a shape is easy.", "A marker who fails bad layouts catches them, but the class keeps making them."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>inconsistent output means the instructions are missing or unclear</b>. Here they are present (<code>/memory</code> shows them) and followed half the time. That is the exact case the exam guide names for <b>few-shot examples</b>: instructions alone give inconsistent output.", "mapTitle": "The model doesn't follow a convention. Why?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Likely cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Convention never followed; not in /memory", "Instructions don't load", "Fix the load (import, rules file)"], ["Convention followed about half the time", "Prose shows shape poorly", "2–3 finished examples"], ["Output uniform but vague", "No criteria", "Explicit criteria"], ["Convention loads for the wrong files", "Wrong scope", "<code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code>"], ["Must never merge in the wrong shape", "Needs a hard gate", "CI check (on top of the fix)"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Generated tests now check real values, but their layout changes from run to run", "means": "The problem is consistency of shape."}, {"quote": "About half use the team's table-driven layout with one shared fixture", "means": "The decider. The description works sometimes, so the words are read; they just don't pin the shape. Examples do: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The project CLAUDE.md describes the table-driven layout in two paragraphs", "means": "Prose already exists. A checklist (<b>[[1]]</b>) is more prose."}, {"quote": "/memory shows that this file loads in the job", "means": "Loading is fine, so moving the file (<b>[[0]]</b>) changes nothing."}, {"quote": "What is the most effective change?", "means": "Fix the generator. A lint gate (<b>[[3]]</b>) only catches the failures."}], "rule": "Instructions load but are followed unevenly → add 2–3 finished examples of the shape you want.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Few-shot is the most effective technique for consistent, actionable output when instructions alone are inconsistent."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-05", "was": "s5q-s5-05", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Dismissal pattern tracking", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 2, "question": "Developers dismiss about a fifth of the reviewer's inline comments. The dismiss button stores only a timestamp. Nobody can say which kinds of finding get dismissed most. The team wants a false-positive rate for each kind of finding, updated daily without anyone reading comments. It will use these rates to decide which review criteria to rewrite. Which <b>two changes</b> achieve this?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>detected_pattern</code> field to each finding that names the review rule behind it.", "why": "Best. Without a rule label, a dismissal can't be tied to a kind of finding. With it, “which rules are noisy” becomes a query."}, {"t": "Each week, sample findings from every category and have an engineer label each one.", "why": "Works, but manual. Labelled samples give good per-category precision, but the team wants daily figures without anyone reading comments.", "trap": "manual"}, {"t": "Record each dismissal with the finding's ID in a table that joins to the stored findings.", "why": "Best. The label only helps if the outcome is captured against it. Joined, they give a dismissal rate per rule that updates by itself."}, {"t": "Ask a separate Claude instance to label each dismissed finding as a true or false positive.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second model adds an opinion on each dismissal, but it supplies no categories, and dismissals are still not recorded.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The dismiss button stores only a timestamp. Nobody can say which kinds of finding get dismissed most.", "explanation": "To improve precision per category you need to know which rule produced each finding and what happened to it. A detected_pattern field plus dismissals recorded against it turns developer clicks into a per-rule signal.", "eli5": {"question": "The team wants to know which kinds of comment get dismissed most, every day, automatically. The clue: today a dismissal is just a timestamp. It doesn't say <i>which</i> finding, or <i>what kind</i>.", "answer": "Think of a shop's returns desk. To know which products come back most, each sale needs a product label, and each return must be logged against that sale. Label plus log gives a return rate per product. Here the label is <code>detected_pattern</code> and the log is the dismissal joined by finding ID. <b>Rule: label each finding with its rule, record the outcome against it, and the noisy rules show themselves.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The product label on each sale.", "Asking a staff member to inspect a sample works, but it's hand work every week.", "Correct. Logging each return against the sale it came from.", "Hiring a second opinion on each return costs more and still doesn't say which product it was."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>measuring precision needs someone to judge findings</b>, a human or a second model. The developers already judge every finding by dismissing it. What is missing is <b>data plumbing</b>: a category on each finding and the dismissal tied to it.", "diagram": "finding {id, detected_pattern}  ⋈  dismissal {finding_id}\n        → dismissal rate per pattern, refreshed daily", "mapTitle": "We want per-category precision. What do we need?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Mechanism", "Note"], "rows": [["Know the kind of each finding", "<code>detected_pattern</code> field", "Set at generation"], ["Know the outcome of each finding", "Dismissal logged with finding ID", "Join to findings"], ["Precise figure, hand labelling allowed", "Stratified sample per category", "Weekly, manual"], ["Second opinion on each finding", "Independent review instance", "Costs a call; no categories by itself"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The dismiss button stores only a timestamp", "means": "The outcome isn't tied to a finding. Log it with the finding ID: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Nobody can say which kinds of finding get dismissed most", "means": "Findings have no category. Add one: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "updated daily without anyone reading comments", "means": "Rules out weekly hand labelling (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Which two changes achieve this?", "means": "A second model (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives opinions but neither the category nor the outcome record."}], "rule": "Tag each finding with detected_pattern and log its outcome against it. Dismissal patterns become a query.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Track detected_pattern fields to analyze dismissal/false-positive patterns."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-06", "was": "s5q-s5-06", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch API & custom_id", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "A nightly technical-debt sweep uses the Message Batches API, with one request per file. A script attaches the first result to the first file sent, the second to the second, and so on. This week a comment about SQL strings appeared on a YAML file. Some requests also come back as errors, and the script then resends the whole batch. What is the <b>best fix</b> for the result handling?", "options": [{"t": "Have the model repeat the file path in each reply, and match results to files by that path.", "why": "Works, but misses errors. It pairs successful replies correctly, but an errored request has no reply, so the script can't tell which files to resend.", "trap": "misses errors"}, {"t": "Set each request's <code>custom_id</code> to its file path, then match and resend failures by it.", "why": "Best. Batch results don't come back in order. <code>custom_id</code> is the field built to pair results, and it names exactly which requests to resend."}, {"t": "Move the sweep to synchronous calls from a worker pool, saving each reply beside its file.", "why": "Works, but loses the discount. Pairing becomes trivial, but the sweep gives up the 50% batch saving on work nobody waits for.", "trap": "costs more"}, {"t": "Add a final step that checks each finding's quoted code against its file and drops mismatches.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Some misplaced comments are caught, but the pairing stays wrong and good findings are dropped with it.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "A script attaches the first result to the first file sent, the second to the second, and so on.", "explanation": "Batch results can arrive in any order; correlate them by custom_id, never by position. Resubmit only the failed requests by their custom_id, which keeps the cost saving.", "eli5": {"question": "Comments land on the wrong files, and every error resends everything. The clue: results are matched to files <i>by position</i>.", "answer": "Think of a laundry. If you hand in ten shirts and match them up by the order they come back, someone gets the wrong shirt. Laundries pin a name tag on each item. In a batch, the name tag is <code>custom_id</code>: you set it on each request and it comes back on each result, even an error. <b>Rule: pair batch results by <code>custom_id</code>, and resend only the failed ones.</b>", "options": ["Writing your name inside the shirt works, until a shirt is lost and nothing comes back to read.", "Correct. A name tag on every item, including the ones that go missing.", "Washing each shirt at home while you wait works, but costs twice as much.", "Checking each shirt's size catches some mix-ups, but the system still hands them out wrong."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a batch returns results in the order you sent them</b>. It doesn't promise that. The Batches API gives each request a <b><code>custom_id</code></b> for exactly this pairing, and the same ID tells you which failed requests to resend.", "mapTitle": "Batch results: how do I pair and recover them?", "map": {"head": ["Problem", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Results land on the wrong item", "Pair by <code>custom_id</code>", "Order isn't guaranteed"], ["Some requests error", "Resend only those, by <code>custom_id</code>", "Keeps cost down"], ["Pairing by a value in the reply", "Works for successes only", "Errors have no reply"], ["Someone waits on the result", "Real-time calls, not batch", "Batch has no latency promise"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A nightly technical-debt sweep uses the Message Batches API, with one request per file", "means": "Nobody waits on it, so batch is the right tool; keep it (unlike <b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "A script attaches the first result to the first file sent, the second to the second, and so on", "means": "The decider. Pairing by position is the bug. Use <code>custom_id</code>: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "This week a comment about SQL strings appeared on a YAML file", "means": "The symptom. Filtering mismatches (<b>[[3]]</b>) hides it."}, {"quote": "Some requests also come back as errors, and the script then resends the whole batch", "means": "Errors need an ID that exists without a reply. An echoed path (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't."}], "rule": "Pair batch results by custom_id, never by order. Resend only the failures by custom_id.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "custom_id correlates request/response; resubmit only failed docs by custom_id."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-09", "was": "s5q-s5-09", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 tool_choice for structured output", "trap": "Non-existent or misunderstood feature", "fam": 9, "select": 1, "question": "The security team added a second review stage built on the Claude API. It sends the gathered context with three tools attached: <code>get_diff</code>, <code>read_file</code> and <code>report_findings</code>. It then asks for the report. On clean pull requests, some replies are a plain sentence such as “No issues found.” The dashboard's parser rejects these. Which change makes this stage <b>always</b> return a structured report?", "options": [{"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>\"any\"</code> and leave all three tool definitions attached.", "why": "Works, but only usually. <code>\"any\"</code> rules out a plain-text reply, but the model may meet it by calling <code>get_diff</code> or <code>read_file</code> instead.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Set <code>tool_choice</code> to force the <code>report_findings</code> tool by name on this stage's request.", "why": "Best. A forced tool makes that call the reply. A clean diff becomes an empty findings list inside the schema."}, {"t": "Add a system prompt line requiring a <code>report_findings</code> call even for clean diffs.", "why": "Works, but only a request. The line raises the rate, but an instruction can't make the call mandatory.", "trap": "only a request"}, {"t": "Check each reply and retry with the parser's error added when no tool call is present.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The retry usually succeeds, but it costs an extra call and lowers the failure rate rather than removing it.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "It sends the gathered context with three tools attached: get_diff, read_file and report_findings.", "explanation": "auto allows text, \"any\" requires some tool, and a forced tool requires that tool. When output must be one schema every time, force the tool and express the empty case inside it.", "eli5": {"question": "Sometimes the stage answers in a sentence instead of the report. The fix must make the report happen every time. The clue: <i>three</i> tools are attached, not one.", "answer": "Saying “use a form” to someone holding three different forms means they might fill in the wrong one. Handing them the one form and saying “this one” settles it. In the API, <code>tool_choice</code> <code>\"any\"</code> means “use a form”; forcing <code>report_findings</code> by name means “this one”. <b>Rule: one exact output every time → force that tool by name.</b>", "options": ["“Use a form” rules out a chatty reply, but with three forms on the desk they may pick the wrong one.", "Correct. Hand over the one form and say “this one”.", "A reminder to use the form helps, but it is a request, not a rule.", "Sending back a chatty reply to redo usually works, but costs another round."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b><code>\"any\"</code> already forces structured output</b>. It forces <i>some</i> tool call, and three tools are attached. Only a <b>forced named tool</b> makes the report the reply every time.", "diagram": "tool_choice auto            → text or any tool\ntool_choice any             → some tool (maybe read_file)\ntool_choice report_findings → report_findings", "mapTitle": "Which tool_choice gives the output I need?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "<code>tool_choice</code>", "Note"], "rows": [["Model decides whether to use tools", "<code>auto</code>", "May return text"], ["Must call a tool; only one is attached", "<code>\"any\"</code>", "Same as forcing it"], ["Must call a tool; several attached; any is fine", "<code>\"any\"</code>", "Model picks which"], ["Must produce this exact report", "Forced named tool", "Empty case goes inside the schema"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It sends the gathered context with three tools attached: get_diff, read_file and report_findings", "means": "The decider. With three tools, <code>\"any\"</code> (<b>[[0]]</b>) can be met by the wrong one."}, {"quote": "On clean pull requests, some replies are a plain sentence such as “No issues found.”", "means": "Text replies are allowed today. A prompt line (<b>[[2]]</b>) asks; it doesn't force."}, {"quote": "Which change makes this stage always return a structured report?", "means": "“Always” needs a mechanism: force the tool: <b>[[1]]</b>. A retry (<b>[[3]]</b>) only lowers the rate."}], "rule": "auto may give text, \"any\" gives some tool, a forced tool gives that tool. Need one schema every time → force it.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_choice: \"auto\" (may return text), \"any\" (must call a tool), forced (specific named tool)."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-01", "was": "s5q-s6-01", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Syntactic vs semantic validity", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The extraction tool now uses a strict schema, and schema failures have dropped to zero. Wrong values still reach the broker's system. In sampled errors, the carrier field holds the name of the agency that issued the certificate. Some per-occurrence limits hold the aggregate amount instead. The validator checks only the schema. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add tighter patterns to the schema for carrier names, policy numbers and every limit amount.", "why": "Works, but on the wrong layer. Patterns catch values with a bad shape. An agency name and an aggregate amount have the same shape as the right values, so they still pass.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add example certificates to the prompt that mark the agency box and the insurer box.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples would cut the carrier mix-up, but not the limit error, and nothing would stop the wrong values that still slip through.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Add checks on meaning to the validator, such as known carriers and occurrence under aggregate.", "why": "Best. A strict schema removes shape errors, not wrong meanings. Checks on meaning (is this a real carrier, is the occurrence limit at or below the aggregate) stop these before posting."}, {"t": "Have a reviewer label a weekly sample of posted certificates and report errors by field.", "why": "Works, but too late. A sample measures how often errors happen. It looks after posting, so each wrong value still reaches the broker's system.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The validator checks only the schema.", "explanation": "Strict schemas eliminate syntax errors but not semantic ones. Once the shape is guaranteed, the remaining errors look correct, so the validator needs checks on meaning.", "eli5": {"question": "The schema is strict, yet wrong values still post. The clue is: <i>the validator checks only the schema</i>. The wrong values have the right shape, so nothing that checks shape can see them.", "answer": "A form can insist that the date box holds a date. It cannot tell whether it is the <i>right</i> date. A strict schema is that form: it checks the <b>shape</b> of each answer, not its <b>meaning</b>. To catch an agency in the carrier box, you need a check that knows what a carrier is. <b>Rule: a schema proves the form is filled in properly, not truthfully. Add checks on meaning.</b>", "options": ["Stricter shape rules still accept an agency name or an aggregate amount. They look right.", "Examples help with one of the two errors, and nothing catches what still slips through.", "Correct. The validator now checks what the values mean, before anything posts.", "A sample tells you the error rate later. It stops nothing today."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a stricter schema would have caught these</b>, as if the schema were just too loose. A strict schema removes <i>syntax</i> errors: broken JSON, missing fields, wrong types. Every error here is well-formed. An agency name is a valid string and an aggregate is a valid number. <b>No rule about shape can detect a wrong meaning.</b>", "diagram": "schema check:   carrier = \"ABC Agency\"   ✓ string\nmeaning check:  is ABC Agency a carrier?  ✗ stop", "mapTitle": "Schema passes but the value is wrong: what catches it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Kind of error", "What catches it"], "rows": [["Broken JSON, missing fields", "Syntax or shape", "Strict schema via tool use"], ["Value has a bad format (letters in a number)", "Format", "Patterns in the schema"], ["Right format, wrong value", "Semantic", "Checks on meaning in the validator"], ["Value not printed at all", "Absent information", "Nullable field"], ["Need to know error rates by field", "Measurement", "Stratified sample of results"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The extraction tool now uses a strict schema, and schema failures have dropped to zero", "means": "Shape is solved. What is left must be something shape cannot see."}, {"quote": "the carrier field holds the name of the agency that issued the certificate", "means": "A valid string with the wrong meaning. A tighter pattern (<b>[[0]]</b>) accepts it."}, {"quote": "Some per-occurrence limits hold the aggregate amount instead", "means": "A second, different error. Examples about the agency box (<b>[[1]]</b>) do not touch it."}, {"quote": "The validator checks only the schema", "means": "The decider. The gap is a missing meaning check before posting: <b>[[2]]</b>. A weekly sample (<b>[[3]]</b>) only measures afterwards."}], "rule": "Strict schemas remove syntax errors, not semantic ones. After adopting one, add checks on meaning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Strict schemas eliminate syntax errors but not semantic errors (line items not summing, wrong fields)."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-02", "was": "s5q-s6-02", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retries and absent information", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The schema requires a <code>naic_code</code> for each carrier, and the validator rejects results without one. A failed result is resent with the validator's message. Certificates from surplus-lines carriers print no NAIC code at all. These use every retry, and some end with a code that looks real but is made up. Which change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Check the code against the official five-digit format, and put that rule in the retry message.", "why": "Works, but only partly. A format check catches crude mistakes, but a made-up code usually has the right format, and the field is still demanded from certificates that cannot contain it.", "trap": "only partly"}, {"t": "Add example certificates to the prompt that show where carriers usually print their code.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. Examples help when a value is printed in a hard-to-find spot. These certificates have no code to find.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Allow more retries for these certificates, with the validator's message on every attempt.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. More attempts mean more pressure to fill a field that has no answer, so more invented codes.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Make <code>naic_code</code> nullable, and stop retrying when the certificate does not print a code.", "why": "Best. Retries fix errors where the value is there. When it is absent, a nullable field lets the model say so instead of inventing a code."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Certificates from surplus-lines carriers print no NAIC code at all.", "explanation": "Retry with feedback works for format and structure errors. It fails when the information is absent from the source. Make such fields nullable instead of pressing for a value.", "eli5": {"question": "Retries keep failing on one field, and some end with an invented code. The clue is: <i>surplus-lines carriers print no NAIC code at all</i>. The value is not on the page, so there is nothing to find.", "answer": "Ask a student for an answer that is not in the book, and keep saying \"try again\". Sooner or later they make one up. The fix is to allow \"not in the book\" as an answer. Here that is a <b>nullable field</b>: the model returns null when no code is printed. <b>Rule: retrying helps when the answer is there. When it is absent, allow \"absent\".</b>", "options": ["A format check stops crude fakes, but a made-up code usually looks right.", "Examples show where a value sits. These certificates have no value to show.", "More tries means more pressure, and more made-up codes.", "Correct. \"Not printed\" becomes a valid answer, and the retries stop."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>failing retries mean the model needs more help finding the value</b>: better examples, stricter checks, more attempts. That holds when the value is on the page. Here the certificates never carry the code. Pressure to fill a required field that cannot be filled is exactly <b>what produces invented codes</b>.", "mapTitle": "Retries keep failing: is the value there to find?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Retry fixes it on the second attempt", "Mis-extraction", "Retry with the specific error"], ["Value printed in an odd spot on some layouts", "Model does not know where to look", "Examples of those layouts"], ["Value <b>not on the document</b>", "Absent information", "Nullable field, stop retrying"], ["Invented values appear after retries", "Pressure to fill a required field", "Allow null"], ["Wrong but well-formed values", "Semantic error", "Checks on meaning"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The schema requires a naic_code for each carrier, and the validator rejects results without one", "means": "The field is required, so an empty answer always fails."}, {"quote": "Certificates from surplus-lines carriers print no NAIC code at all", "means": "The decider. The value is absent from the source. Examples of where it sits (<b>[[1]]</b>) have nothing to point at."}, {"quote": "These use every retry, and some end with a code that looks real but is made up", "means": "Fabrication under pressure. A format check (<b>[[0]]</b>) passes a real-looking fake; more retries (<b>[[2]]</b>) add pressure. So <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Retry with feedback fixes format and structure errors, not missing information. Make such fields nullable.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retries are ineffective when info is absent from the source (vs format/structural errors)."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-03", "was": "s5q-s6-03", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Criteria for uncertainty", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Some certificates arrive as faxed scans. On these, a digit in the policy number is often smudged, such as an 8 that might be a 3. The model always picks one reading. A wrong policy number links the certificate to the wrong policy, which takes hours to untangle. The schema has no way to mark a value as unsure. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>needs_review</code> flag, with written rules for setting it, like any unclear digit.", "why": "Best. The model needs a defined way to say \"unsure\", and explicit criteria for when to use it. Flagged certificates go to a quick review instead of a costly wrong link."}, {"t": "Add three examples of smudged policy numbers to the prompt, each reasoning through the digits.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples can sharpen the guess, but the model still has to commit to one reading, because the schema gives it no other answer.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Extract each faxed certificate twice, and send it for review when the two numbers differ.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Two runs can make the same misreading, so a smudge read wrongly both times still posts.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Send every faxed certificate to the review queue, and keep auto-posting for clean PDFs.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It pays for a review on every fax, including the many with clear numbers, to catch a few.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The schema has no way to mark a value as unsure.", "explanation": "Give the model a defined way to say \"unsure\" and explicit criteria for using it. Otherwise every unclear case becomes a confident guess.", "eli5": {"question": "The model must guess between a smudged 8 and 3, and a wrong guess is costly. The clue is: <i>the schema has no way to mark a value as unsure</i>. Whatever you teach it, the model can only commit.", "answer": "A cashier who cannot read a cheque can guess, or ring a bell for the manager. With no bell, they guess. Give them a bell and a rule for when to ring it. Here the bell is a <b>review flag</b> in the schema, and the rule is <b>explicit criteria</b> for setting it. <b>Rule: give the model a defined \"unsure\" answer, and say when to use it.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A bell, plus a written rule for when to ring it.", "Examples make the guess better, but it is still a guess.", "Two runs can misread the same smudge the same way.", "Checking every fax pays for many clean ones to catch a few."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs to get better at reading smudges</b>, with examples or a second run. But no skill makes a smudge readable. The real gap is the output: the schema only lets the model commit. It needs <b>a defined way to say \"unsure\"</b>, with written criteria for when to say it.", "mapTitle": "Model forced to guess: what is missing?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Model always commits, schema has no \"unsure\"", "No way to express doubt", "Review flag plus criteria"], ["Flag exists but is used unevenly", "Uneven judgment", "Examples of borderline cases"], ["Value not printed at all", "Absent information", "Nullable field"], ["Two runs agree on a wrong value", "Shared blind spot", "Do not treat agreement as proof"], ["One document type is often unclear", "Whole type is risky", "Route that type, if errors are common"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "a digit in the policy number is often smudged, such as an 8 that might be a 3", "means": "The source is truly ambiguous. A second run (<b>[[2]]</b>) can read it the same wrong way."}, {"quote": "The model always picks one reading", "means": "It never says it is unsure."}, {"quote": "A wrong policy number links the certificate to the wrong policy, which takes hours to untangle", "means": "A wrong guess costs far more than a quick review, but only the unclear ones need it, not every fax (<b>[[3]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The schema has no way to mark a value as unsure", "means": "The decider. Examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) can only teach a better guess. A flag with criteria (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds the missing answer."}], "rule": "If the model can only commit, it will guess. Add an \"unsure\" outcome and write criteria for using it.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-09", "was": "s5q-s6-09", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Self-checking fields", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "The team wants to stop well-formed but wrong values before they post, without a person checking every certificate. Most errors sit in two fields: carrier and policy number. The broker's system already holds each client's known policies, with carrier and policy number. An engineer suggests auto-posting any result whose self-reported confidence is above 0.9. Which <b>two changes</b> catch these errors before posting?", "options": [{"t": "Label a monthly sample of posted certificates, stratified by carrier and by field, to track errors.", "why": "Works, but too late. Stratified sampling is the right way to measure error rates by carrier and field. It looks after posting, so it stops no single wrong value.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Score confidence for each field, calibrate it on labelled data, and review fields below threshold.", "why": "Best. Errors cluster in two fields, so field-level scores route them. Calibration on labelled data is what makes the scores mean something."}, {"t": "Auto-post results whose self-reported confidence is above 0.9, and send the rest to review.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated: a confidently misread field still scores high.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Compare carrier and policy number with the client's known policies, and review mismatches.", "why": "Best. Well-formed errors are caught against an outside source of truth. The broker's own records check the two error-prone fields with no person involved."}, {"t": "Extract each certificate twice with different prompts, and review fields where they disagree.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Two runs can make the same mistake on the same layout and agree.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The team wants to stop well-formed but wrong values before they post.", "explanation": "Self-reported confidence and agreement between runs are proxies, not measures. Catch tidy-but-wrong values with outside records and with field-level confidence calibrated on labelled data.", "eli5": {"question": "The team wants to catch wrong-but-tidy values before they post, without checking every certificate by hand. The clue is: <i>before they post</i>. A method that only measures errors afterwards does not stop any of them.", "answer": "A shop can catch fake notes two ways: a machine that checks each note against known features, and a trained eye on only the doubtful ones. Counting fakes at month end tells you the rate but stops none. Here the machine is the <b>broker's own records</b>, and the trained eye is <b>per-field confidence checked against labelled data</b> (calibration). <b>Rule: catch tidy-but-wrong values with an outside source of truth.</b>", "options": ["A stratified sample measures error rates well, but only after posting.", "Correct. Field scores, checked against labelled data, send only doubtful fields to review.", "The model's own score is unreliable: a confident misread scores high.", "Correct. Records the broker already holds catch a wrong carrier or policy number.", "Two runs can make the same mistake twice."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>measuring errors is the same as catching them</b>. A stratified sample is the right way to learn error rates by field. But it looks at results after the fact. To stop each wrong value before it posts, every certificate needs <b>a check against something outside the extraction</b>.", "mapTitle": "Tidy but wrong values: measure them or stop them?", "map": {"head": ["Goal", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Stop wrong values on each certificate", "Cross-check against own records", "Outside source of truth"], ["Send only doubtful fields to people", "Per-field confidence, calibrated on labelled data", "Scores that mean something"], ["Learn error rates by field or carrier", "Stratified sample", "Measures, does not stop"], ["Is the model sure?", "Not self-reported confidence", "Poorly calibrated"], ["Do two runs agree?", "Not a correctness check", "Same mistake twice"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team wants to stop well-formed but wrong values before they post", "means": "The decider. Prevention on each certificate. A sample (<b>[[0]]</b>) measures after posting."}, {"quote": "Most errors sit in two fields: carrier and policy number", "means": "Field-level scores can route these: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The broker's system already holds each client's known policies, with carrier and policy number", "means": "An outside source of truth for exactly those fields: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "auto-posting any result whose self-reported confidence is above 0.9", "means": "Self-reported confidence (<b>[[2]]</b>) is unreliable; so is agreement between runs (<b>[[4]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Self-reported confidence and run agreement do not measure correctness. Catch tidy-but-wrong values with outside records and calibrated per-field confidence.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "field-level confidence calibrated on labeled sets routes review."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-10", "was": "s5q-s6-10", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for the long tail", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "A large new client sends certificates from dozens of carriers the broker has never seen. Their layouts range from multi-column forms to dense paragraphs to tables with almost no labels. Today, for each new carrier, the team collects samples and writes carrier notes into the prompt. That takes days per carrier, and accuracy is poor until it is done. Which approach <b>scales best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three or four worked certificates with very different layouts, showing where each field is.", "why": "Best. A few examples chosen for structural variety let the model generalise to layouts it has not seen. One set replaces per-carrier notes."}, {"t": "Classify certificates by carrier, and route each one to a prompt with that carrier's notes.", "why": "Works, but only for known carriers. Routing helps when each route has notes written for it. A new carrier has none yet, so it falls back to today's problem.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Rewrite each field's description with its label variants and where it tends to appear.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Better descriptions help with what to extract, but prose about position struggles across layouts this different. Examples show it better.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make the fields nullable, and send certificates with empty fields to a reviewer for now.", "why": "Works, but for a different problem. Nullable fields stop invented values, but this hands new carriers to people instead of extracting them well.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "A large new client sends certificates from dozens of carriers the broker has never seen.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples chosen for structural variety generalise to layouts the system has never seen. Per-carrier guidance grows with every carrier and does nothing for a carrier on day one.", "eli5": {"question": "Dozens of unseen carriers arrive at once, and notes take days per carrier. The clue is: <i>carriers the broker has never seen</i>. The system must cope with layouts nobody has written notes for.", "answer": "Teach a new clerk with three very different sample forms, and they can handle a fourth they have never seen. Give them one cheat sheet per company, and they are stuck on any company without a sheet. Varied samples are <b>few-shot examples</b> chosen for variety. <b>Rule: for a stream of new layouts, teach with varied examples, not per-case notes.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Varied worked examples let the model handle layouts it has not seen.", "Routing works when every carrier has notes. New carriers have none yet.", "Written rules about position struggle across very different layouts.", "This stops made-up values, but sends new carriers to people instead."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>routing to carrier-specific prompts is the scalable design</b>, since it keeps each prompt focused. It is, for known inputs. Here dozens of carriers arrive that nobody has written notes for. Only something that <b>generalises to unseen layouts</b> helps from the first certificate.", "mapTitle": "Many layouts: do you know them in advance?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Few, stable carriers with known quirks", "Route to carrier-specific prompts", "Focused guidance"], ["Many unseen layouts", "Few-shot examples chosen for variety", "Generalises"], ["Guidance for types contradicts", "Classify, then route", "Removes conflicts"], ["Field may be absent", "Nullable field", "Prevents invention"], ["Unclear what counts as the value", "Explicit criteria", "Defines it"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "certificates from dozens of carriers the broker has never seen", "means": "The decider. No notes exist, so routing by carrier (<b>[[1]]</b>) falls back to the generic prompt."}, {"quote": "Their layouts range from multi-column forms to dense paragraphs to tables with almost no labels", "means": "Variety of structure: prose rules (<b>[[2]]</b>) struggle; varied examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) carry it."}, {"quote": "That takes days per carrier, and accuracy is poor until it is done", "means": "Per-carrier work does not scale. Sending them to people (<b>[[3]]</b>) does not scale either."}], "rule": "Few-shot examples chosen for structural variety generalise to unseen layouts. Per-case notes grow with every case.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "enable generalization to novel patterns"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-09", "was": "s2-09", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Engineers run a pre-push review with the prompt “review this diff for anything questionable”. Each run returns dozens of comments. Most are about things the team has no rule on, such as variable names or import order. The few findings that matter get buried, and engineers have started skipping the step. The output is well formatted and much the same each run. Which change <b>most improves precision</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Define which findings count, like a comment that contradicts its code, and which to skip.", "why": "Best. Explicit categories tell Claude what qualifies and what to leave out, which removes the preference comments at their source."}, {"t": "Add three sample reviews, each showing a diff, the findings worth raising and the points left out.", "why": "Works, but indirect. Examples are strongest when output is inconsistent. Here it is already consistent; the prompt never says what counts.", "trap": "indirect"}, {"t": "Add the line “be conservative and report only high-confidence issues” to the end of the prompt.", "why": "Works, but only a little. Fewer comments come out, but “questionable” is still undefined, and confidence wording doesn't sharpen precision like categories do.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run the review three times per diff and keep only the findings that appear in at least two runs.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Voting removes random noise. This output is the same each run, so the same preference comments survive.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Most are about things the team has no rule on, such as variable names or import order.", "explanation": "Vague review prompts produce false positives that erode trust. Define explicit categories of what to report and what to skip; “be conservative” does not improve precision the way categorical criteria do.", "eli5": {"question": "A review step produces dozens of comments, most about things nobody cares about, so people skip it. The output is consistent. The clue: the prompt never says <i>what counts</i>.", "answer": "Ask a proofreader to mark “anything questionable” and you get red ink everywhere. Tell them “mark wrong facts and broken links, ignore style” and you get the marks that matter. That list is <b>explicit criteria</b>. <b>Rule: too many false flags → say exactly what counts and what to skip.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Give the proofreader the exact list of what to mark and what to ignore.", "Showing marked-up samples works, but you can just tell them the list.", "“Only mark what you're sure about” still leaves “questionable” undefined.", "Asking three proofreaders who all mark the same style points doesn't remove those marks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to reach for <b>few-shot examples</b> whenever output is disappointing. Examples are the strongest lever when output is <i>inconsistent</i> despite clear instructions. Here output is consistent; the instruction never defines what counts. The exam guide says explicit criteria beat vague instructions, and “be conservative” doesn't help the way categories do.", "mapTitle": "Review output is noisy. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Consistent, but flags things nobody cares about", "Vague instruction", "Explicit categories to report and skip"], ["Instructions clear, output varies run to run", "Prose alone underspecifies", "2–4 targeted examples"], ["Prompt says “be careful”", "Still vague", "Swap for categories"], ["Random one-off flags", "Run-to-run noise", "Several runs, keep agreed findings"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "review this diff for anything questionable", "means": "The cause: “questionable” is undefined."}, {"quote": "Most are about things the team has no rule on, such as variable names or import order", "means": "The decider. The fix is to say what counts and what to skip: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "engineers have started skipping the step", "means": "False positives are eroding trust; “be conservative” (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the line undefined."}, {"quote": "The output is well formatted and much the same each run", "means": "Consistent already, so examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) and voting (<b>[[3]]</b>) aim at a problem that isn't here."}], "rule": "Too many false positives from a vague prompt → explicit categories of what to report and what to skip.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "\"Be conservative\" / \"only high-confidence\" don't improve precision like categorical criteria"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-10", "was": "s2-10", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.6 Independent review instances", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A session generates a billing module in <code>server/</code>. The engineer then asks the same session to review it, and it says the code looks right. A colleague opens a fresh session with the plain prompt “review this for bugs”. It finds an off-by-one at a proration boundary and a rounding error. Both sessions used the same model and settings. What should <b>change</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Give review sessions explicit criteria for boundary conditions and the rounding of money amounts.", "why": "Works, but not the difference. Criteria sharpen reviews, but the colleague found both bugs with an equally plain prompt.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Run reviews in a fresh session that has none of the generating session's history.", "why": "Best. A session that wrote the code keeps its own reasoning and tends to accept it. An independent instance reviews with fresh eyes."}, {"t": "Turn on extended thinking for review passes in the generating session before it concludes.", "why": "Works, but same assumptions. Longer reasoning helps a little, but it starts from the same generation reasoning that missed the bugs.", "trap": "same blind spot"}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> in the generating session, then repeat the same review in that session.", "why": "Works, but keeps the bias. Compacting frees space, but the generation reasoning stays in summary form, so the review is still a self-review.", "trap": "same blind spot"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "A colleague opens a fresh session with the plain prompt “review this for bugs”.", "explanation": "A session that generated code retains its reasoning and is less effective at reviewing its own changes. Independent review instances catch subtle issues better than self-review or extended thinking.", "eli5": {"question": "The session that wrote the code says it's fine. A fresh session with a plain prompt finds two bugs. The clue: the only real difference is <i>fresh</i>.", "answer": "Proofreading your own essay is hard: you read what you meant, not what you wrote. A friend who never saw your drafts spots the typos at once. For Claude, the friend is an <b>independent instance</b> with none of the generation history. <b>Rule: review code in a fresh instance, not in the session that wrote it.</b>", "options": ["A better checklist helps, but the friend found the typos without one.", "Correct. A fresh reader with no memory of your drafts.", "Thinking longer about your own essay still reads what you meant.", "Shortening your notes still leaves you reading your own essay."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a missed bug means the review prompt was weak</b>. Here the prompts were equally plain, the model and settings were the same, and only one session found the bugs. The difference is history: the generating session keeps its own reasoning and won't question it. An independent instance has none.", "diagram": "session A: write → review (same reasoning) → \"looks right\"\nsession B: fresh  → review                → 2 bugs", "mapTitle": "Review missed bugs. What changes the result?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Same prompt; fresh session finds bugs", "Self-review bias", "Independent review instance"], ["Fresh session also misses them", "Vague review criteria", "Explicit criteria per category"], ["Self-review with more thinking", "Same assumptions", "Still a self-review"], ["Large change, many files", "Attention dilution", "Per-file pass + integration pass"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The engineer then asks the same session to review it, and it says the code looks right", "means": "A self-review: the reviewer holds the generation reasoning."}, {"quote": "A colleague opens a fresh session with the plain prompt “review this for bugs”", "means": "The decider. A plain prompt worked when the session was fresh, so criteria (<b>[[0]]</b>) weren't the gap."}, {"quote": "Both sessions used the same model and settings", "means": "Only the history differs. More thinking (<b>[[2]]</b>) or compacting (<b>[[3]]</b>) keeps that history. Use a separate instance: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Review generated code in an independent instance; self-review keeps the generation reasoning.", "guide": {"obj": "4.6 Multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures", "quote": "Independent review instances (no prior reasoning) catch subtle issues better than self-review/extended thinking"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-05", "was": "s5-05", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria & precision", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Developers act on few of the review's findings. The biggest group says a doc comment is out of date, and most of those comments are fine. That group comes from one prompt line: “keep code documentation trustworthy”. Developers now skim past the whole review, including its accurate security findings. What change <b>most improves precision</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add three sample findings of stale doc comments, each with its file, line and suggested fix.", "why": "Works, but too early. Examples steady the shape of findings, but they rest on a goal with no boundary, so Claude still guesses what counts.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Have a second instance score each finding, and post only the ones it rates high.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A grader cuts some noise, but it judges against the same vague “trustworthy” and doubles the cost of every review.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Reword that line: flag a doc comment only when what it claims differs from what the code does.", "why": "Best. “Trustworthy” has no boundary, so almost any comment can be flagged. A condition comparing claim and code can be checked, and explicit criteria are what raise precision."}, {"t": "Post doc-comment findings in a folded summary at the end, not as inline comments.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Security findings stand out again, but the doc-comment findings are as wrong as before.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "That group comes from one prompt line: “keep code documentation trustworthy”.", "explanation": "Vague goals produce vague findings. Replace a goal like “trustworthy” with a condition checkable against the code, because a noisy category erodes trust in the accurate findings too.", "eli5": {"question": "One vague prompt line creates lots of wrong findings, and people now ignore the whole review. The clue: the line says <i>trustworthy</i>, which has no clear edge.", "answer": "Telling a proofreader “make sure the captions are good” gets every caption flagged. Telling them “flag a caption only when it describes something not in the picture” gets the real errors. That is an <b>explicit criterion</b>: a test Claude can check against the code. <b>Rule: replace vague goals with conditions you can check.</b>", "options": ["Showing sample corrections helps, but the proofreader still doesn't know which captions count.", "A second proofreader with the same vague brief is just as unsure, and twice the cost.", "Correct. Give a test: flag it only when the words don't match the picture.", "Moving the corrections to the back hides them, but they are still wrong."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a noisy category needs examples, a filter or a quieter place</b>. Those treat its output. The cause is the instruction: “trustworthy” has no boundary. The exam guide's fix is an <b>explicit criterion</b>, for example flag a comment only when its claim contradicts the code.", "mapTitle": "Too many false findings. What sharpens them?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["One category is mostly false flags", "Vague goal in the prompt", "Explicit, checkable criterion"], ["Criterion clear, findings' shape varies", "Prose underspecifies output", "A few worked examples"], ["Prompt says “only if highly confident”", "Confidence wording", "Swap for categories"], ["Category can't be made precise yet", "No good test exists", "Turn it off for now"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The biggest group says a doc comment is out of date, and most of those comments are fine", "means": "One category is mostly false positives."}, {"quote": "That group comes from one prompt line: “keep code documentation trustworthy”", "means": "The decider. The cause is a vague goal. Examples (<b>[[0]]</b>) and a grader (<b>[[1]]</b>) both inherit it."}, {"quote": "Developers now skim past the whole review, including its accurate security findings", "means": "The noise costs trust in good findings. Folding it away (<b>[[3]]</b>) hides it without fixing it."}, {"quote": "What change most improves precision?", "means": "Precision comes from what counts as a finding: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "A vague goal makes false positives; rewrite it as a condition Claude can check against the code.", "guide": {"obj": "4.1 Explicit criteria to improve precision / reduce false positives", "quote": "Explicit criteria beat vague instructions (\"flag comments only when claimed behavior contradicts actual code\" vs \"check comments are accurate\")"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-06", "was": "s5-06", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot for consistency", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "The review prompt defines each severity level with a clear test, such as “reachable from an unauthenticated request”. Most findings get the same level on every run. A possible null dereference on a guarded path was marked critical on one pull request and minor on another. The swings happen where two of the tests seem to apply at once. What <b>most improves</b> consistency?", "options": [{"t": "Add more conditions to each level's definition to cover guarded and partly guarded paths.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. Sharper wording helps when levels are vague. These are already clear tests, and more conditions add more to weigh at the boundary.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Add a few borderline findings, each with the level chosen and why the other level lost.", "why": "Best. The written tests are fine; the inconsistency sits where two of them overlap. Examples that show the reasoning on borderline cases make those calls consistent."}, {"t": "Run the review three times, and post each finding at the level most runs gave it.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Voting smooths some swings, but it triples the cost of a check developers wait on, and it hides the boundary rather than teaching it.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Ask Claude to reason step by step through each test before it picks a level.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Reasoning helps a little, but at the boundary two tests still fit, and Claude has nothing showing which one wins.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The swings happen where two of the tests seem to apply at once.", "explanation": "When instructions are already explicit but output still varies on borderline cases, few-shot examples are the most effective fix. Choose boundary cases and show why one level beat the other.", "eli5": {"question": "Severity rules are clear and work most of the time, but borderline findings flip between levels. The clue: the swings happen where <i>two tests apply at once</i>.", "answer": "A referee who knows the rulebook still calls borderline fouls differently. What helps is watching replays of borderline calls with the reason for each decision. For Claude, those replays are <b>few-shot examples</b>: a few boundary findings, each with the level chosen and why the other lost. <b>Rule: clear rules but inconsistent borderline calls → show worked examples.</b>", "options": ["A thicker rulebook gives the referee more rules to weigh against each other.", "Correct. Replays of borderline calls, with the reason for each.", "Three referees voting smooths some calls, at three times the cost, and none of them learns the boundary.", "Thinking harder helps a little, but two rules still fit and nothing shows which wins."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>inconsistent output means the rules need more words</b>. Here the rules are clear tests and work on most findings. The swings sit at the <b>overlap</b> between two tests. The exam guide's fix for that is <b>few-shot examples</b> that show the reasoning on ambiguous cases.", "mapTitle": "Output varies. Is it the rule or the boundary?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Clear tests, swings on borderline cases", "Overlap between tests", "2–4 boundary examples with reasons"], ["Levels described in vague words", "No real test to apply", "Explicit severity criteria"], ["One category mostly false flags", "Vague goal", "Checkable condition"], ["Review misses its own mistakes", "Same session reviews", "Independent instance"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The review prompt defines each severity level with a clear test", "means": "The criteria are already explicit, so adding more conditions (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more of the same."}, {"quote": "Most findings get the same level on every run", "means": "The rules work in clear cases."}, {"quote": "The swings happen where two of the tests seem to apply at once", "means": "The decider. A boundary problem, which examples teach: <b>[[1]]</b>. Voting (<b>[[2]]</b>) and step-by-step reasoning (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't show which test wins."}], "rule": "Clear criteria, inconsistent borderline calls → a few worked boundary examples with the reason for each.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "2-4 targeted examples showing reasoning for the chosen action"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-07", "was": "s5-07", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch processing suitability", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Finance wants the Message Batches discount used wherever it fits. Three jobs cost the most. The pull-request review is a required check, and developers wait for it before they can merge. A new nightly flaky-test summary is read at the morning stand-up. The weekly dependency-risk report is read at Monday planning. Which plan should the team <b>adopt</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Batch the nightly summary and the weekly report, and keep the review on real-time calls.", "why": "Best. The summary and the report are read hours later, so they take the discount. The review blocks a merge while someone waits, and batches promise no response time."}, {"t": "Batch all three, and have the review job poll for its result before it posts a status.", "why": "Works, but blocks the merge. It saves the most, but batches can take up to a day with no latency promise, and the merge button waits that long.", "trap": "blocks the merge"}, {"t": "Batch only the weekly report, and keep the nightly summary and review on real-time calls.", "why": "Works, but misses savings. The report is a good fit, but the overnight summary is too, and it stays at full price.", "trap": "misses savings"}, {"t": "Batch all three, and fall back to a real-time call when a review result is slow.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. For the blocking review the fallback would fire on most runs, so the team pays for both paths and keeps two code paths.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The pull-request review is a required check, and developers wait for it before they can merge.", "explanation": "Message Batches save 50% but can take up to 24 hours with no latency guarantee. They suit overnight and weekly work, not checks that block a merge.", "eli5": {"question": "Finance wants the cheap, slow option wherever it fits. The clue: developers <i>wait</i> for the review before they can merge; nobody waits for the summary or the report.", "answer": "Overnight post is half price but arrives whenever it arrives. It's fine for a letter read next week, not for a key someone is standing at the door waiting for. The <b>Message Batches API</b> is that overnight post: half price, up to a day, no promised time. <b>Rule: someone waiting → real-time. Read later → batch.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Send the two letters read later by cheap post, and hand-deliver the key.", "Sending the key by cheap post and checking the mailbox leaves someone stuck at the door.", "Only one letter goes by cheap post; the other one could have too.", "Cheap post with a courier backup for the key means paying for the courier almost every time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the biggest saving is the best plan</b>. The Message Batches API is half price but has no latency promise, up to 24 hours. It fits work read later and does not fit a <b>blocking</b> check that people wait on.", "mapTitle": "Does this job fit the Message Batches API?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "Who waits?", "Interface"], "rows": [["Required pre-merge review", "A developer, now", "Real-time"], ["Nightly summary read at stand-up", "Nobody until morning", "Batch"], ["Weekly report read Monday", "Nobody for days", "Batch"], ["Advisory review nobody waits on", "Nobody", "Batch could fit"], ["Job needing multi-turn tool calls", "Any", "Real-time (no tool loop in a batch)"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Finance wants the Message Batches discount used wherever it fits", "means": "The goal is to save where it fits, not everywhere."}, {"quote": "The pull-request review is a required check, and developers wait for it before they can merge", "means": "The decider. Blocking work stays real-time. Polling (<b>[[1]]</b>) holds the merge; a fallback (<b>[[3]]</b>) runs two paths."}, {"quote": "A new nightly flaky-test summary is read at the morning stand-up", "means": "Latency-tolerant, so it should be batched too, which <b>[[2]]</b> misses."}, {"quote": "Which plan should the team adopt?", "means": "Batch the two read-later jobs, keep the review real-time: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Batch what is read later; keep anything someone waits on real-time.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Good for non-blocking/latency-tolerant (overnight reports, weekly audits); bad for blocking (pre-merge)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-08", "was": "s5-08", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch API limitations", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A new nightly job sorts each file's technical debt into classes through the Message Batches API. Each request offers a <code>get_git_blame</code> tool, since who changed the code and when affects the class. Every result now ends with <code>stop_reason</code> “tool_use”, a blame call for that file, and no class. The workflow has the repository checked out. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move the job back to real-time requests that run the tool loop, and accept the higher cost.", "why": "Works, but gives up the discount. Real-time calls run the tool loop, but every request needs the same predictable data, which can be fetched first.", "trap": "gives up the discount"}, {"t": "Drop the blame tool, and have Claude classify each file from its content alone.", "why": "Works, but loses data. Every request finishes, but the classes ignore who changed the code and when, which the team says matters.", "trap": "loses data"}, {"t": "Answer each stopped request in a second batch, with its blame result added as a new turn.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A second round can finish the job, but each tool call costs another batch wait, and the data was known up front.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Run <code>git blame</code> before submitting, and put each file's output in its own request.", "why": "Best. A batch request cannot run a tool loop. The blame data is predictable, so supplying it up front keeps the job on the cheaper interface."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Every result now ends with stop_reason “tool_use”, a blame call for that file, and no class.", "explanation": "The Message Batches API does not support multi-turn tool calling within one request. When the tool data is predictable, fetch it first and put it in the request; when it is not, use real-time requests.", "eli5": {"question": "Batch requests stop as soon as Claude asks for a tool. The clue: every request asks for the <i>same, predictable</i> thing, a blame for its own file, and the workflow can get it.", "answer": "A batch is like a letter: you can't phone mid-letter to ask a question. If you already know the writer will ask “who last changed this file?”, you put the answer in the envelope. Here that means running <code>git blame</code> first and putting each file's output in its request. <b>Rule: batches can't run tool loops; if the tool data is predictable, send it in the request.</b>", "options": ["Switching to phone calls works, but costs double when one sheet in the envelope would do.", "Removing the question means the letter is answered without facts the team says matter.", "Mailing the answer back and waiting for another letter works, but it's slow and the answer was known all along.", "Correct. Put the blame output in the envelope from the start."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a batch that stops on <code>tool_use</code> needs a different interface</b>. The Message Batches API cannot continue a tool loop inside one request. But when the tool data is <b>predictable</b>, you can fetch it first and include it, and keep the batch discount.", "diagram": "now:  batch req → Claude: tool_use(get_git_blame) → stops\nfix:  git blame in workflow → blame text in each request\n      → Claude classifies → end_turn", "mapTitle": "A job needs extra data. How do you get it to Claude?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Batch job, tool data predictable", "Fetch first, include in the request", "Keeps the discount"], ["Data depends on what Claude finds", "Real-time requests with a tool loop", "Batches can't loop"], ["Tool data adds nothing to the result", "Drop the tool", "Simpler request"], ["Some batch requests failed", "Resubmit those by <code>custom_id</code>", "Only redo what failed"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each request offers a get_git_blame tool, since who changed the code and when affects the class", "means": "The blame data matters, so dropping it (<b>[[1]]</b>) lowers quality."}, {"quote": "Every result now ends with stop_reason “tool_use”, a blame call for that file, and no class", "means": "The decider. Each request needs the same predictable data, and a batch can't run the tool loop. Extra batch rounds (<b>[[2]]</b>) work around that slowly."}, {"quote": "The workflow has the repository checked out", "means": "The data can be fetched before submitting: <b>[[3]]</b>. Going real-time (<b>[[0]]</b>) isn't needed."}], "rule": "Batch requests can't call tools in a loop; fetch predictable tool data first and include it.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Batch API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-09", "was": "s5-09", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Schema-compliant output", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The weekly report's summariser calls the Messages API. Its system prompt asks for a JSON array of risks in the reply text. Some replies will not parse: a trailing comma, an unescaped quote, or a sentence before the bracket. When that happens, the posting script drops that module from the report. Which change <b>removes these parse failures</b> at the source?", "options": [{"t": "Define a <code>record_risks</code> tool whose input schema is the array, and force it in <code>tool_choice</code>.", "why": "Best. Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant output, and it removes JSON syntax errors at the source."}, {"t": "Add two complete sample replies to the prompt, with escaped quotes and no text before the array.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples make valid JSON more likely, but nothing enforces it, and a few replies still break.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Prefill the reply with an opening bracket, and strip trailing commas before parsing.", "why": "Works, but patches symptoms. Prefill stops the preamble and the repair fixes one shape, but an unescaped quote still breaks the parse.", "trap": "patches symptoms"}, {"t": "Retry each reply that won't parse, with the parser's error added to the request.", "why": "Works, but too late. Retry with specific errors often repairs the reply, but it pays for a second call to fix what a schema prevents.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Which change removes these parse failures at the source?", "explanation": "Tool use with a JSON schema eliminates JSON syntax errors. Retries, repairs and examples treat failures after or around the fact. Values still need checking in code, since schemas don't catch semantic errors.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude writes JSON as plain text and some replies are broken: a stray comma, a quote, a sentence before it. The clue: fix it <i>at the source</i>, not after.", "answer": "Asking someone to type a table neatly still gives typos. Giving them a form with fixed boxes means the layout can't break. For the API, that form is a <b>tool with a JSON schema</b>: define <code>record_risks</code> and force it with <code>tool_choice</code>, and Claude's answer arrives as well-formed tool input. <b>Rule: need valid JSON every time → tool use with a schema.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A form with fixed boxes; the layout can't break.", "A neat sample helps most typists, but some replies still have typos.", "Fixing the two typos you've seen leaves the third kind, the stray quote.", "Sending it back with the error fixes it, but only after a wasted round."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>broken JSON needs better instructions or a repair step</b>. Those reduce or patch errors. <b>Tool use with a JSON schema</b> removes the whole class of syntax errors. It doesn't check the meaning of values, which still needs validation in code.", "diagram": "now:  prompt → reply text \"[ {...}, ]\" → parse fails\nfix:  tool_choice = record_risks → tool input (valid JSON)", "mapTitle": "Output goes wrong. Syntax or meaning?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Kind of error", "Fix"], "rows": [["Commas, quotes, preamble break parsing", "Syntax", "Tool use with a schema, forced"], ["Valid JSON, wrong or impossible values", "Semantic", "Validate in code, retry with the error"], ["Required field missing in source", "Absent information", "Nullable field, no retry"], ["Model replies in text, not a tool", "Tool not forced", "<code>tool_choice</code> any or named"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Its system prompt asks for a JSON array of risks in the reply text", "means": "The format is only requested. Examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) still only request it."}, {"quote": "a trailing comma, an unescaped quote, or a sentence before the bracket", "means": "Several kinds of syntax error. A repair for two of them (<b>[[2]]</b>) leaves the quote."}, {"quote": "Which change removes these parse failures at the source?", "means": "The decider. At the source means before a reply is ever wrong, not a retry afterwards (<b>[[3]]</b>): <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Valid JSON every time → tool use with a JSON schema; check the values in code.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_use with JSON schemas = most reliable schema-compliant output (eliminates JSON syntax errors)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-01", "was": "s6-01", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Optional & nullable fields", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Some certificates are issued while a policy is still being bound. On those, the policy number box says “to be assigned”. The schema makes <code>policy_number</code> a required string. For these certificates, the extraction returns the binder number or a string built from the carrier code and date. Those values post as real policy numbers, and renewals later link to the wrong policy. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Check each policy number against the carrier's records before posting, and queue any that have no matching policy.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. A lookup catches some invented numbers, but the number was never printed, and an invented one that happens to match a real policy slips through.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Make <code>policy_number</code> nullable, not required, and say that unbound certificates may leave it blank for now.", "why": "Best. Nothing is printed, yet the schema demands a value, so the model invents one. A nullable field gives the honest answer somewhere to go."}, {"t": "Add two unbound certificates to the prompt as examples, with the field filled with an empty string.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. An empty string is a workaround that dresses up “absent” as a value, and the schema still demands something.", "trap": "hides the problem"}, {"t": "Add a validator rule rejecting a policy number equal to the binder number, then retry extraction.", "why": "Works, but only for one symptom. It stops the copied binder number, but a retry cannot find a number that is not printed, so the model invents a different one.", "trap": "only one symptom"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On those, the policy number box says “to be assigned”.", "explanation": "When a value can be legitimately missing from the source, the schema must be able to say so. Optional or nullable fields prevent fabrication; a required field reads as “produce something”.", "eli5": {"question": "Some certificates have no policy number yet, but the schema demands one, so the extraction makes one up. The clue: the box literally says “to be assigned”.", "answer": "Picture a form where the policy number box must be filled before you can hand it in. If you don't have the number, you scribble something plausible. Add a “not yet assigned” option and the scribbling stops. In a JSON schema that option is a <b>nullable field</b>. <b>Rule: if the source can lack a value, the schema must allow “none”.</b>", "options": ["Checking the scribbled number against the carrier helps, but a made-up number can still match a real policy.", "Correct. Let the box be empty when nothing is printed.", "Writing an empty string still fills the box with a pretend value.", "Rejecting one kind of scribble just makes the next scribble different."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>made-up values should be caught after extraction</b>, by a lookup or a validator. But the cause is upstream: the schema gives the model no honest way to say “not printed”, so it produces something plausible. Fix the schema, and the invention stops at the source.", "diagram": "required string + “to be assigned” → binder no. posted\nnullable string + “to be assigned” → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "A value is wrong in the output. Where is the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Plausible values where the source has none", "Field is required", "Make it nullable, describe when"], ["Value is printed but sometimes misread", "Extraction error", "Check against the system of record"], ["Odd categories forced into the nearest slot", "Closed enum", "Add <code>other</code> plus a detail field"], ["Value printed but in the wrong format", "Format slip", "Retry with the specific validation error"], ["Accuracy drops on unusual layouts", "Too few patterns shown", "Varied few-shot examples"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Some certificates are issued while a policy is still being bound", "means": "A missing policy number is a normal, expected case."}, {"quote": "On those, the policy number box says “to be assigned”", "means": "The decider. The value is absent from the source. A validator retry (<b>[[3]]</b>) cannot find what is not printed."}, {"quote": "The schema makes policy_number a required string", "means": "The cause: the schema forces a value. An empty string (<b>[[2]]</b>) obeys the schema by pretending."}, {"quote": "Those values post as real policy numbers", "means": "Invented values look real. A lookup (<b>[[0]]</b>) only catches the ones that fail to match."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Fix the cause in the schema: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Value can be absent from the source → make the field nullable. A required field invites invention.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-02", "was": "s6-02", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Extensible enums", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Each coverage limit has a <code>limit_basis</code> enum with three values: per occurrence, per claim and aggregate. Some certificates print other bases. Most are “per project” or “per location” aggregates, which the extraction posts as plain aggregate. The rest are carriers' own wordings, and new ones appear every few months. Reviewers cannot tell a forced value from a printed one. Which schema change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add an <code>other</code> value to the enum, plus a <code>limit_basis_detail</code> string for the printed wording.", "why": "Best. The enum keeps validating the common bases, and anything else lands in <code>other</code> with the printed text kept. Nothing is forced, and new wordings need no schema change."}, {"t": "Add per project aggregate and per location aggregate to the enum, next to the three existing values.", "why": "Works, but only for today's list. It handles the biggest group cleanly, but the carriers' own wordings still get forced into a slot, and that list keeps growing.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Make <code>limit_basis</code> nullable, and return null when the printed basis is not one of the three.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. Nothing is forced any more, but a basis that was printed is thrown away, so reviewers still can't see what the certificate said.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Ask for a confidence score on <code>limit_basis</code>, and send values scored below a threshold to a reviewer.", "why": "Works, but leans on a weak signal. Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated, and the forced value is still the one stored.", "trap": "unreliable signal"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The rest are carriers' own wordings, and new ones appear every few months.", "explanation": "For a category that is mostly closed but sometimes open, use an enum with an <code>other</code> value plus a detail field. Validation stays where it works and odd values stop being forced into the nearest slot.", "eli5": {"question": "A three-value list can't hold every limit basis carriers print, so odd ones get squeezed into “aggregate”. The clue: carriers keep inventing <i>new</i> wordings.", "answer": "A sorting rack with three labelled shelves works until odd parcels arrive. Adding two more shelves helps this month, but next month brings new shapes. A shelf marked “other”, with a note saying what each parcel really is, handles every future surprise. That is an <b>enum with <code>other</code> plus a detail field</b>. <b>Rule: mostly-closed list that keeps growing → add “other” and keep the printed text.</b>", "options": ["Correct. An “other” shelf with a note never runs out of room.", "Two more shelves fit today's odd parcels; new shapes next month still get squeezed.", "Leaving odd parcels unlabelled stops the squeezing, but you lose what they were.", "Asking the sorter how sure they feel doesn't change which shelf the parcel went on."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a forced category means the list is missing values</b>, so add them. That works for a closed set. Here part of the set is open: carriers keep inventing wordings. An enum needs an escape hatch, <code>other</code>, plus a field that keeps the printed text.", "diagram": "per occurrence | per claim | aggregate | other\n  other → limit_basis_detail: “per project aggregate”", "mapTitle": "Values don't fit the enum. What kind of misfit?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Odd values, and new ones keep appearing", "Open-ended category", "<code>other</code> plus a detail field"], ["A fixed, known set of extra values", "Enum is incomplete", "Add those values to the enum"], ["No value printed at all", "Absent from source", "Nullable field"], ["Printed text is ambiguous", "Unclear category", "<code>unclear</code> value plus detail"], ["Routing on how sure the model feels", "Uncalibrated confidence", "Calibrate on labelled data"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Most are “per project” or “per location” aggregates, which the extraction posts as plain aggregate", "means": "Values are being forced into the nearest slot. Adding these two (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers the biggest group."}, {"quote": "The rest are carriers' own wordings, and new ones appear every few months", "means": "The decider. The set is open, so any fixed list (<b>[[1]]</b>) falls behind."}, {"quote": "Reviewers cannot tell a forced value from a printed one", "means": "The printed text must be kept. Null (<b>[[2]]</b>) drops it; a confidence score (<b>[[3]]</b>) still stores the forced value."}, {"quote": "Which schema change is the best fix?", "means": "Add an escape hatch that keeps the wording: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Mostly-closed category that keeps growing → enum with “other” plus a detail field.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "enum \"unclear\"/\"other\" + detail for extensible categories"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-03", "was": "s6-03", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Self-checking extraction", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Property certificates list each location's insured value and then a printed total. In spot checks, some printed values did not add up to the printed total. Some carriers had made errors; elsewhere a row on a continuation page was skipped. Yet most extracted records still added up, because the model had changed one figure. Reviewers want these held, with enough detail to see why. Which change <b>does that</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Recompute the sum of location values in the posting service, and hold any record that differs from the total.", "why": "Works, but sees too little. A code check is sound, but the model has already changed a figure in most records, so the extracted numbers agree and the check passes them.", "trap": "too late in the chain"}, {"t": "Tell the model to copy every figure exactly as printed and to leave a carrier's arithmetic errors alone.", "why": "Works, but only usually. It may reduce the quiet edits, but it gives no signal that a mismatch exists, so nothing is held for a reviewer.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Send certificates with continuation pages to a second pass that reads only the location rows again.", "why": "Works, but for one cause. It helps with skipped rows, but does nothing for carrier errors or for the model quietly changing a figure.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Add fields for the total as printed and the total summed from the extracted rows, plus a mismatch flag.", "why": "Best. The model reports both numbers instead of reconciling them, and the flag holds the certificate. The gap between the two also hints at the cause."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Yet most extracted records still added up, because the model had changed one figure.", "explanation": "Build self-checking into the output: extract the stated total alongside a calculated total, plus a conflict flag. Discrepancies are reported as data instead of being reconciled inside the model.", "eli5": {"question": "The printed figures on some certificates don't add up, but the model quietly fixes one number so its record looks tidy. The clue: the extracted records <i>still added up</i>.", "answer": "A cashier whose till is short shouldn't quietly move a coin to make it balance. They should write down “counted 98, receipt says 100” and flag it. For extraction that means two fields, the <b>stated total</b> and the <b>calculated total</b>, plus a <b>mismatch flag</b>. <b>Rule: report the conflict as data; don't let the model reconcile it.</b>", "options": ["A manager recounting later sees a balanced till, because the cashier already moved the coin.", "Asking the cashier not to fiddle may help, but nobody is told when the till is short.", "Recounting one drawer helps with one cause, not the others.", "Correct. Write both counts down and raise a flag when they differ."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a sum check in code catches arithmetic problems</b>. It does, if the extracted figures are the printed ones. Here the model edits a figure so its output balances, so the check sees nothing. A strict schema stops syntax errors, not this kind of semantic error. The fix is to make the model <b>report both totals</b> and a flag.", "diagram": "printed rows sum 9.6m, printed total 10m\n  stated_total 10m | calculated_total 9.6m\n  → conflict_detected: true → held for review", "mapTitle": "Numbers don't reconcile. Where should it be caught?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Output balances but source doesn't", "Model reconciles silently", "Stated total, calculated total, flag"], ["Output figures copied faithfully, some don't sum", "Source or reading error", "Recompute in code and hold"], ["Rows missed on continuation pages", "Attention on long input", "Focused pass on those pages"], ["Valid JSON with wrong values", "Semantic error", "Self-check fields and validation"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "In spot checks, some printed values did not add up to the printed total", "means": "The mismatch is real and in the source."}, {"quote": "Some carriers had made errors; elsewhere a row on a continuation page was skipped", "means": "Two causes. A second pass on continuation pages (<b>[[2]]</b>) covers only one."}, {"quote": "Yet most extracted records still added up, because the model had changed one figure", "means": "The decider. A code check on extracted numbers (<b>[[0]]</b>) sees balanced records."}, {"quote": "Reviewers want these held, with enough detail to see why", "means": "A signal is needed, which an instruction (<b>[[1]]</b>) does not give. Both totals plus a flag: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Don't let the model reconcile figures. Extract the stated total and a calculated total, and flag conflicts.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops", "quote": "Self-correction: extract calculated_total alongside stated_total; conflict_detected booleans"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-04", "was": "s6-04", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Validation & retry loops", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "When validation fails, the pipeline retries up to five times. Each retry says only “Output failed validation, please try again”. Most documents that use all five attempts are policy schedules from two carriers. Those schedules have no place for the carrier's licence number, which the schema requires. These documents use about a fifth of extraction spend, and reviewers still key them in by hand. What change <b>cuts that spend most</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Replace the generic retry message with the validator's specific errors, naming each field and its rule.", "why": "Works, but for a different failure. Specific feedback is the right loop for values printed but misformatted. Here the value isn't on the page, so retries still fail.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Give these two carriers' schedules their own prompt with worked examples of where each field appears.", "why": "Works, but for a different failure. Layout examples help when a value is present but hard to find. They cannot find a box the form doesn't have.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Allow a null value for that field, and route those documents straight to manual entry without a retry.", "why": "Best. No retry can find a value that is not printed. Allowing null stops both the invention pressure and the wasted retries, and the documents go straight to the reviewers who handle them anyway."}, {"t": "Lower the retry limit from five attempts to two for every document, and send what fails to review.", "why": "Works, but too blunt. It trims spend on these documents, but also cuts retries that rescue fixable ones, and still spends two attempts on documents that can never pass.", "trap": "blunt cut"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Those schedules have no place for the carrier's licence number, which the schema requires.", "explanation": "Retry with specific error feedback works when the model can use the feedback. It is useless when the information is absent from the source; then the schema should allow absence and the document should leave the loop.", "eli5": {"question": "Retries burn money on two carriers' schedules that always fail. The clue: those forms have <i>no place</i> for the licence number at all.", "answer": "Sending a clerk back five times to find a box that isn't on the form wastes five trips. Better instructions won't help; the box isn't there. Mark the field as “may be empty” and pass those forms straight to the person who fills it in by hand. That is a <b>nullable field plus routing</b>, with no retry. <b>Rule: retries fix misread values, not missing ones.</b>", "options": ["Clearer instructions help a clerk who misread a box, not one looking for a box that isn't there.", "A map of the form helps find a box that exists. This one doesn't.", "Correct. Stop sending them back; pass the form to the person who handles it.", "Fewer trips for everyone saves some money, but also stops trips that would have worked."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>retries fail because the feedback is too vague</b>. Specific feedback is the fix when the value is on the page but misformatted. Here the value is <b>absent from the source</b>, so every retry fails however good the message is. Let the schema say “absent” and take the document out of the loop.", "diagram": "value printed, wrong format → retry with specific error\nvalue not on the form       → null → reviewer, no retry", "mapTitle": "Validation keeps failing. Will a retry help?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Value not on the form at all", "Absent from source", "Nullable field, route without retry"], ["Value printed but misformatted", "Format or structure slip", "Retry with the specific error"], ["Value present but hard to locate", "Unusual layout", "Few-shot layout examples"], ["Retries rescue many documents", "Recoverable errors", "Keep the retry limit"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each retry says only “Output failed validation, please try again”", "means": "The loop retries blindly, which looks like the problem, and better messages (<b>[[0]]</b>) are tempting."}, {"quote": "Those schedules have no place for the carrier's licence number, which the schema requires", "means": "The decider. The value is absent from the source. Feedback (<b>[[0]]</b>) and layout examples (<b>[[1]]</b>) can't conjure it."}, {"quote": "reviewers still key them in by hand", "means": "These documents end with a person anyway, so sending them there directly loses nothing. A lower limit for all (<b>[[3]]</b>) hits fixable documents too."}, {"quote": "What change cuts that spend most?", "means": "Stop retrying what cannot succeed: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Information absent from the source → allow null and route it out. Retries help only when the value is there.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops", "quote": "Retries are ineffective when info is absent from the source"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-05", "was": "s6-05", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.2 Few-shot across varied structures", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The prompt holds eight worked certificates, all picked from the six largest carriers because they were to hand. Accuracy is high for those carriers and much lower for the rest. Smaller carriers put limits in footnotes, in tables split across pages, or under labels like “Each Claim”. New carriers join often, and their first certificates arrive before anyone has seen the layout. What <b>most improves</b> accuracy on the smaller carriers?", "options": [{"t": "Swap most of the eight for small-carrier certificates with unusual layouts, each noting how fields were found.", "why": "Best. Examples teach generalisation when they show varied, awkward structures and the reasoning. Varied layouts carry over to carriers nobody has seen yet."}, {"t": "Add written layout rules: limits may sit in footnotes, tables may split, and Each Claim means per claim.", "why": "Works, but only for what's been seen. Rules cover the variations already listed. They don't show how to reason about a new layout, where examples do better.", "trap": "only what's seen"}, {"t": "Keep a prompt per carrier with that carrier's own samples, picked by the sender when a certificate arrives.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It lifts accuracy on known carriers, but a new carrier has no prompt yet, and there are many prompts to maintain.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Extend the prompt to one sample from every current carrier, ordered from the highest volume to the lowest.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It adds a large token cost to every document and teaches recall of known layouts, not how to handle the next one.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "New carriers join often, and their first certificates arrive before anyone has seen the layout.", "explanation": "Few-shot examples should show varied and ambiguous structures so the model can generalise to new patterns. Choose them where the model fails, not where it already succeeds.", "eli5": {"question": "All the worked examples come from the big carriers, and accuracy is poor on everyone else. The clue: new carriers arrive <i>before anyone has seen their layout</i>, so the model must cope with the unknown.", "answer": "If you train a new clerk only on the neatest forms, they freeze on the first messy one. Show them a few awkward forms and talk through how you found each box, and they learn to cope with forms they've never seen. Those are <b>varied few-shot examples with reasoning</b>. <b>Rule: pick examples from where the model struggles, and show how the answer was found.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A few awkward forms, with the reasoning, teach the clerk to handle new ones.", "A list of known quirks helps with those quirks, not with the next new one.", "A binder per carrier works for carriers you know; a new carrier has no binder yet.", "One example of every form is a huge binder, and still says nothing about the next new one."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>more examples, or more specific examples, means better accuracy</b>. What matters is which examples. Eight variations of layouts the model already reads well teach nothing new. A few <b>varied, awkward examples with the reasoning shown</b> help the model generalise to layouts it has never seen.", "mapTitle": "Accuracy is low on some documents. What do examples need to show?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["New layouts keep arriving unseen", "Varied, awkward examples with reasoning", "Generalises to novel layouts"], ["Fixed set of known senders", "Per-sender prompts or examples", "Each layout covered directly"], ["Known quirks, few in number", "Written rules can do", "Nothing new to generalise"], ["Instructions alone give inconsistent output", "Add 2–4 targeted examples", "Most effective for consistency"], ["Examples all from easy cases", "Swap for hard cases", "Easy ones teach nothing new"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "all picked from the six largest carriers because they were to hand", "means": "The examples were chosen by convenience, not by where the model fails."}, {"quote": "Smaller carriers put limits in footnotes, in tables split across pages, or under labels like “Each Claim”", "means": "Varied structures, the case few-shot handles. Listing them as rules (<b>[[1]]</b>) covers only these."}, {"quote": "New carriers join often, and their first certificates arrive before anyone has seen the layout", "means": "The decider. Per-carrier prompts (<b>[[2]]</b>) and one sample per carrier (<b>[[3]]</b>) can't cover an unseen carrier."}, {"quote": "What most improves accuracy on the smaller carriers?", "means": "Varied examples that generalise: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Choose few-shot examples that show varied, awkward layouts with reasoning, so the model generalises to new ones.", "guide": {"obj": "4.2 Few-shot prompting for consistency and quality", "quote": "Demonstrate ambiguous-case handling; enable generalization to novel patterns"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-1", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Blocking work stays synchronous", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments. Each review request sends every file in the changed modules, though comments go only on changed lines. A manager wants to move the job to the Message Batches API to halve its cost. Which change <b>best</b> cuts the review's cost?", "options": [{"t": "Move the job to the Message Batches API and poll for its results every minute.", "why": "Works, but blocks the merge. It halves the price, but a batch can take up to 24 hours with no latency promise. Polling faster doesn't make it finish sooner.", "trap": "blocks the merge"}, {"t": "Keep the job on the standard API and send only the diff and changed files.", "why": "Best. Developers are waiting, so the review stays synchronous. Most of the tokens are files the review never comments on, so trimming them cuts the cost."}, {"t": "Send the job as a batch, with a timeout that falls back to a standard call.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Developers still wait out the timeout, slow batches end up paid for twice, and there are now two paths to run and test.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Review only larger pull requests, and let small ones merge without a review.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves money by not doing the job. Small changes can still break things.", "trap": "drops coverage"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments.", "explanation": "Batch processing suits latency-tolerant work, not blocking checks like a pre-merge review. To cut the cost of a blocking job, keep it synchronous and send only what it needs.", "eli5": {"question": "A review that blocks merging costs too much, and a manager wants the cheap batch service. The clue: developers are <i>waiting</i> for it.", "answer": "A half-price dry cleaner that returns shirts “within a day” is no use if you need the shirt for a meeting in an hour. Keep the fast service, and stop sending the whole wardrobe when only one shirt is dirty. <b>Rule: someone waiting → synchronous. To save money, trim what you send.</b>", "options": ["Phoning the slow cleaner every minute doesn't get the shirt back sooner.", "Correct. Fast service, and send only the dirty shirt.", "Trying the slow cleaner first, then paying for express when it's late.", "Saving money by not cleaning some shirts at all."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>batch is simply the cheaper way to call Claude</b>. It is half price because it has no latency promise: results arrive within 24 hours. A check that people wait on can't take that deal. The saving for a blocking job has to come from somewhere else, such as sending fewer tokens.", "diagram": "push -> review (standard API, diff + changed files)\n     -> comments in minutes -> merge", "mapTitle": "Batch or now?", "map": {"head": ["Job", "API", "Why"], "rows": [["Blocks a merge or a person", "Standard (synchronous)", "Batch has no latency promise"], ["Report read days later", "Message Batches API", "Half price, results within 24 hours"], ["Large backfill due next week", "Message Batches API", "Half price, time to spare"], ["Needs tool calls mid-task", "Standard (synchronous)", "A batch request can't loop through tools"], ["Sends far more than it reads", "Trim the request", "Fewer tokens on either API"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The pull-request review job must pass before a branch can merge, and developers wait for its comments", "means": "The decider: a blocking job. Batch (<b>[[0]]</b>) and batch with a fallback (<b>[[2]]</b>) both make people wait."}, {"quote": "Each review request sends every file in the changed modules, though comments go only on changed lines", "means": "Most of the tokens are never used. Trim them: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts the review's cost?", "means": "Cut cost without dropping reviews, which rules out <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Someone waiting on it? Keep it synchronous, and cut cost by trimming the request.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Good for non-blocking/latency-tolerant (overnight reports, weekly audits); bad for blocking (pre-merge)."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-2", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch for latency-tolerant work", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "When a support chat ends, a script sends the transcript to Claude to tag the reason for contact. The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review. Each tag needs a single call with no tools. The script uses the standard Messages API, one call per chat as it ends. Which change <b>best</b> cuts the tagging cost?", "options": [{"t": "Send each day's transcripts every night as one Message Batches API job.", "why": "Best. Nobody reads the tags until Friday, and each tag is a single call. The batch costs half as much, and results are back within a day."}, {"t": "Tag a random sample of chats each week instead of tagging every chat.", "why": "Works, but drops coverage. It saves money by doing less, and the quality team loses the full picture.", "trap": "drops coverage"}, {"t": "Put twenty transcripts into each request to cut the number of calls made.", "why": "Works, but crowds each request. Fewer calls, but still full price, attention spread across twenty chats, and one odd chat can spoil the rest.", "trap": "crowds each request"}, {"t": "Have the support agent tag the reason itself as the last step of each chat.", "why": "Works, but still full price. The tag moves into the live chat, which still pays the standard rate for speed nobody needs.", "trap": "full price"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review.", "explanation": "Work nobody waits on, made of single calls with no tools, belongs on the Message Batches API: half the cost, results within 24 hours.", "eli5": {"question": "Every chat gets tagged straight away at full price, but the tags are only read on Friday. The clue: nobody waits for them.", "answer": "If a letter only has to arrive by Friday, you don't send it by express courier. You drop the day's letters in the cheap bulk mail each evening. The <b>Message Batches API</b> is that bulk mail: half the price, delivered within a day. <b>Rule: nobody waiting + one call each → batch.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Bulk mail each evening, still well before Friday.", "Saving stamps by sending only some of the letters.", "Stuffing twenty letters in one express envelope: still express, and they get mixed up.", "Handing letters to the courier at the door: still express price."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>sending each item as soon as it exists is the normal way</b>. It is only needed when someone is waiting. When results are read days later, the instant answer is speed you pay full price for and never use.", "diagram": "chats end -> collect the day's transcripts\n  -> nightly Message Batches job (custom_id per chat)\n  -> tags ready long before Friday", "mapTitle": "Is anyone waiting?", "map": {"head": ["When the result is used", "API", "Cost"], "rows": [["Seconds later, by a person or a merge", "Standard (synchronous)", "Full price"], ["Next day or later", "Message Batches API", "Half price"], ["Next week, in bulk", "Message Batches API", "Half price"], ["Mid-task, after a tool call", "Standard (synchronous)", "Full price"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The quality team reads these tags once a week, at its Friday review", "means": "The decider: nobody waits for a tag. Instant answers (<b>[[3]]</b>) are speed nobody uses."}, {"quote": "Each tag needs a single call with no tools", "means": "A batch request can do this; it doesn't need a tool loop."}, {"quote": "Which change best cuts the tagging cost?", "means": "Cut cost and still tag every chat: <b>[[0]]</b>, not a sample (<b>[[1]]</b>) or crowded requests (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}], "rule": "Nobody waiting, one call each? Send it as a Message Batches API job at half the cost.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Message Batches API: 50% cost savings, up to 24-hour window, no latency SLA."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-batch-or-now-3", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.5 Batch has no multi-turn tool calling", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Each quarter the team audits about 3,000 past summaries. For each one, the citation-check subagent fetches the cited sections over MCP, then confirms each claim matches its source. Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites. The audit is due in two weeks, and the team wants it to cost less. What is the <b>best</b> approach?", "options": [{"t": "Keep running the audit through the subagent on the standard API, as it runs today.", "why": "Works, but full price. It finishes in time, but nobody needs the answers this week, and every call pays the standard rate.", "trap": "full price"}, {"t": "Fetch the quoted passages in code, then batch one request per summary.", "why": "Best. The summaries already say what to fetch, so code can fetch it. Each request then needs no tools, which a batch can run at half the cost."}, {"t": "Batch one request per summary, and send each tool result back in a follow-up batch.", "why": "Works, but slow round trips. A batch request can't loop through tools, so every fetch becomes a new batch that can take up to 24 hours.", "trap": "slow round trips"}, {"t": "Run several citation-check subagents in parallel to finish the audit within a day.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It is fast, but it pays full price and adds coordination for speed nobody needs.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites.", "explanation": "The Message Batches API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request. When the data a step needs is known in advance, fetch it in code and send single-call requests as a batch.", "eli5": {"question": "A big, slow-deadline audit uses tool calls to fetch papers. The clue: each summary already says exactly which sections to fetch.", "answer": "The cheap bulk service can't phone you halfway to ask for a missing page. So put every page it needs in the envelope before you post it. Fetching the sections in code first turns each check into one self-contained request, which the <b>Message Batches API</b> runs at half price. <b>Rule: batch requests can't call tools, so hand them everything up front.</b>", "options": ["Keeps working, at express price, for an audit due in two weeks.", "Correct. Put the pages in the envelope, then use bulk mail.", "Posting a letter, waiting a day for a question, posting the answer, and so on.", "Hiring more couriers to finish in a day when you have two weeks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>an agent job can move to batch as it is</b>. A batch request returns once. If Claude needs a tool result mid-task, the request can't loop. But when the tool calls are predictable, code can make them first, and each request no longer needs a tool.", "diagram": "code: fetch cited sections for each summary\n  -> batch: one request per summary (custom_id)\n  -> results within 24 h, half price", "mapTitle": "Can this job go to batch?", "map": {"head": ["Job shape", "Fit for batch?", "What to do"], "rows": [["One call, no tools, nobody waiting", "Yes", "Batch it"], ["Tool calls Claude must choose as it goes", "No", "Keep it synchronous"], ["Tool calls known in advance", "Yes, after a change", "Fetch in code, then batch"], ["Someone waiting on the answer", "No", "Keep it synchronous"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the citation-check subagent fetches the cited sections over MCP", "means": "Today the job uses tool calls, which a single batch request can't loop through (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Every summary already lists the papers and sections it cites", "means": "The decider: the fetches are known in advance, so code can do them first."}, {"quote": "The audit is due in two weeks, and the team wants it to cost less", "means": "Nobody is waiting. Full price (<b>[[0]]</b>) and parallel speed (<b>[[3]]</b>) buy nothing."}, {"quote": "What is the best approach?", "means": "Fetch in code, then batch: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Batch can't run a tool loop. If the tool calls are predictable, make them in code, then batch.", "guide": {"obj": "4.5 Efficient batch processing strategies", "quote": "Batch API does not support multi-turn tool calling in a single request."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-1", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 tool_use with a JSON schema", "trap": "Fixing text instead of using a tool", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An internal Agent SDK script reads each incident write-up in Linear. It asks Claude to reply with a JSON object holding the service, severity, start time and root cause. A parser then reads the reply. On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket. The parser skips those incidents. The values themselves are usually right. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a complete example reply to the prompt that shows the JSON and nothing else.", "why": "Works, but only usually. An example cuts the stray sentences, but text replies can still break, and each break loses an incident.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Resend any reply that fails to parse, along with the error from the parser.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The retry usually fixes the reply, but every broken reply costs a second call that a schema would avoid.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Define a <code>record_incident</code> tool with a JSON schema and have Claude call it.", "why": "Best. Tool input arrives as structured JSON that matches the schema, so syntax errors and stray sentences go away at the source."}, {"t": "Make the parser cut out the text between the first and last brace before reading.", "why": "Works, but only for one kind of break. It drops the stray sentence, but a missing bracket still fails, and the next new quirk will too.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket.", "explanation": "Tool use with a JSON schema is the most reliable way to get schema-compliant output; it removes JSON syntax errors. Prompts, retries and lenient parsers only reduce them.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude writes the JSON as text, and sometimes the text breaks. The clue: the values are right; only the format fails.", "answer": "Asking someone to write a form out by hand, neatly, mostly works. Handing them a form with boxes always gives you the boxes. A <b>tool with a JSON schema</b> is the form with boxes. <b>Rule: if structured output breaks as text, return it through a tool.</b>", "options": ["A neat example helps, but handwriting still slips sometimes.", "Sending messy forms back works, but costs a second round each time.", "Correct. Use the form with boxes.", "Tidying up the handwriting fixes one kind of mess, not every kind."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>better instructions will fix the format</b>. Text replies are probabilistic; a tool call with a JSON schema is structured by design. The guide: tool use with a schema <b>eliminates JSON syntax errors</b>. It does not fix wrong values, but here the values are fine.", "diagram": "text reply:  “Here it is: {…”  → parser fails\ntool call:   record_incident({service, …})\n             → structured input, no parsing", "mapTitle": "What goes wrong, and which layer fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Problem", "Layer", "Fix"], "rows": [["JSON breaks: stray text, missing bracket", "Output format", "Tool with a JSON schema"], ["Field has no value on the page", "Schema design", "Optional or nullable field"], ["Category not in the list", "Schema design", "Enum with other + detail"], ["Value on the page, extracted wrong", "Validation loop", "Retry with the specific error"], ["Odd layouts read wrong", "Prompt", "Worked examples of those layouts"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It asks Claude to reply with a JSON object", "means": "The output is text, so its syntax can break."}, {"quote": "On some write-ups Claude adds a sentence before the JSON or drops a closing bracket", "means": "The decider: syntax failures. A tool call removes them: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The values themselves are usually right", "means": "Nothing to fix in the content. An example (<b>[[0]]</b>), a retry (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a lenient parser (<b>[[3]]</b>) works around the format instead of removing the cause."}], "rule": "Structured output breaking as text? Return it through a tool with a JSON schema.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "tool_use with JSON schemas = most reliable schema-compliant output (eliminates JSON syntax errors)"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.3 Nullable fields against fabrication", "trap": "Forcing a value that isn't there", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent extracts each paper's details through a tool with a JSON schema. The schema makes <code>trial_registration_id</code> a required string. Many older papers do not print a registration ID. For those papers, the subagent returns IDs in the right format that do not exist. Reviewers then spend time searching the registry for them. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a prompt rule telling the subagent not to guess an ID that isn't printed.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The rule lowers the guessing, but the schema still demands a string, so the model still has to put something there.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Make <code>trial_registration_id</code> nullable, to be left null when not printed.", "why": "Best. The schema now has an honest answer for “not printed”, so the model has no reason to invent an ID."}, {"t": "Have the citation-check subagent look up every extracted ID in the registry.", "why": "Works, but catches it late. The lookup finds the fake IDs, but they are still invented first, and every paper costs an extra registry call.", "trap": "catches it late"}, {"t": "Add worked examples of papers both with and without a printed registration ID.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Examples show the right behaviour, but a required field still leaves no valid way to say “none”.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The schema makes trial_registration_id a required string.", "explanation": "When a field may be absent from the source, make it optional or nullable. A required field pushes the model to fabricate a plausible value.", "eli5": {"question": "Old papers have no registration ID, but the form insists on one, so the model makes one up. The clue: the field is required.", "answer": "A screen that won't save until every box is filled gets fake answers in the boxes. Add a “not given” option and people stop faking. A <b>nullable field</b> is that option. <b>Rule: if a value may be missing, let the schema say so.</b>", "options": ["Asking people not to fake helps, but the box still has to be filled.", "Correct. Give the box a “not given” option.", "Checking every answer later finds the fakes after they were made.", "Showing examples helps, but the screen still won't save an empty box."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the model needs to be told not to invent</b>. Here the schema is what demands a value. Give the schema an honest answer for “not printed” and the pressure to invent goes away.", "diagram": "required string, nothing printed → plausible fake ID\nnullable, nothing printed        → null (honest)", "mapTitle": "A value may be missing. How does the schema handle it?", "map": {"head": ["Schema choice", "What the model returns", "Effect"], "rows": [["Required field", "A plausible value", "Fabrication that looks real"], ["Sentinel such as 0 or N/A", "A fake value", "Read downstream as real"], ["Optional or nullable field", "null", "Honest gap, easy to route"], ["Enum with other + detail", "other, plus what it is", "Honest new category"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The schema makes trial_registration_id a required string", "means": "The decider: the schema has no valid way to say “not printed”. Make it nullable: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Many older papers do not print a registration ID", "means": "The value is absent from the source, so a required field forces a guess."}, {"quote": "the subagent returns IDs in the right format that do not exist", "means": "Fabrication. A rule (<b>[[0]]</b>) or examples (<b>[[3]]</b>) fight the schema; a registry check (<b>[[2]]</b>) catches fakes after they're made."}], "rule": "A value may be missing? Make the field optional or nullable, not required.", "guide": {"obj": "4.3 Structured output via tool use and JSON schemas", "quote": "Optional/nullable fields prevent fabrication; enum \"unclear\"/\"other\" + detail for extensible categories"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-extraction-honesty-3", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D4 · Prompt Engineering & Structured Output", "obj": "4.4 Retries can't recover absent information", "trap": "Retrying the impossible", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The broker's system needs a policy number on every certificate. When none is extracted, the pipeline retries with the specific error, up to two times. On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge. These fail each retry. Sometimes the last retry returns a number that is not on the page, and it gets posted. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Raise the retry limit and say in the message that the number must be found.", "why": "Works, but presses harder for the impossible. More retries help only when the number is on the page; here they invite more invented numbers.", "trap": "retrying the impossible"}, {"t": "Check each policy number against the carrier's format before it is posted.", "why": "Works, but catches only some cases. A broken number fails the check, but an invented one in the right format passes.", "trap": "catches only some cases"}, {"t": "Add worked examples that show where each carrier prints its policy number.", "why": "Works, but a different problem. Examples help find a number that is printed somewhere unusual; this one is not on the page.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return null when the policy number isn't printed and send it to a person.", "why": "Best. A retry can't recover what the scan lost. Null says so honestly, and a person can get the number from the carrier."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge.", "explanation": "Retries with error feedback fix values that are in the document. When the information is absent from the source, retries fail or invite fabrication; return null and route to human review.", "eli5": {"question": "The policy number is torn off the scan, and the system keeps asking for it until the model makes one up. The clue: the number isn't on the page.", "answer": "If the date is torn off a form, telling the temp “find it” three times won't bring it back. It might get you a made-up date. Hand that form to someone who can phone the customer. <b>Rule: retry only when the answer is on the page. If it isn't, say so and send it to a person.</b>", "options": ["Asking more firmly makes a made-up answer more likely, not a real one.", "Checking the format catches messy numbers, not neat fakes.", "Examples help find a number printed in an odd place, not one that's missing.", "Correct. Say it's missing and hand it to someone who can find it."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a specific retry fixes any validation failure</b>. It fixes values that are on the page but extracted wrong. When the information is absent, every retry fails, and pressure to fill the field produces an invention.", "diagram": "scan: Policy NG-44▒▒▒▒\n  retry “policy number required” → NG-448126 (fake)\n  null + person                  → real number found", "mapTitle": "Validation failed. Is the value on the page?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Fix", "Why"], "rows": [["On the page, wrong field or format", "Retry with the specific error", "The model can correct it"], ["On the page, odd layouts every time", "Worked examples", "Fixes the first pass"], ["Not on the page", "Null, then a person", "No retry can find it"], ["Invented value in the right format", "Not caught by format checks", "Prevent it at the schema"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the pipeline retries with the specific error", "means": "The retry is already the good kind. Its content is not the problem."}, {"quote": "On some scanned certificates, the policy number is cut off at the page edge", "means": "The decider: the information is absent from the source. Examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) and more retries (<b>[[0]]</b>) can't bring it back."}, {"quote": "the last retry returns a number that is not on the page, and it gets posted", "means": "Fabrication under pressure. A format check (<b>[[1]]</b>) passes a well-formed fake. Return null and route it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Value missing from the source? Don't retry; return null and send it to a person.", "guide": {"obj": "4.4 Validation, retry, and feedback loops for extraction quality", "quote": "Retries are ineffective when info is absent from the source (vs format/structural errors)"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-08", "was": "m7-s1-08", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Explicit request for a human", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "A customer writes: “My salmon was missing again. I want to speak to a real person.” The refund is well inside the agent's limit, so the agent could settle the case alone. The customer sounds calm, and the request for a person is clear. How should the agent <b>respond</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Say sorry, offer the refund it can issue now, and escalate if the customer asks for a person again.", "why": "Works, but wrong action. Many customers would take the refund, but this one asked for a person and now has to ask twice.", "trap": "wrong action"}, {"t": "Run <code>lookup_order</code> to confirm the missing salmon, then escalate with what it found.", "why": "Works, but delays the request. The check adds a detail to the handoff, but the customer waits for a decision that is already made.", "trap": "delays the request"}, {"t": "Call <code>escalate_to_human</code> at once, with a note that the customer asked for a person.", "why": "Works, but thin handoff. The customer reaches a person quickly, but the person must ask again which box and item it was about.", "trap": "thin handoff"}, {"t": "Call <code>escalate_to_human</code> at once, passing the customer ID, the box and the missing item.", "why": "Best. An explicit request for a person is a reason to escalate on its own. Passing the facts lets the person pick up without asking again."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "I want to speak to a real person.", "explanation": "When a customer explicitly asks for a human, escalate immediately, even if the agent could resolve the case. Hand over the key facts in a structured way so the human does not need to re-ask.", "eli5": {"question": "A customer says “I want a real person.” The agent could fix the problem itself. The clue: the customer asked directly.", "answer": "If you ask a shop assistant for the manager, you don't want to hear “but I can help you!” first. A good assistant fetches the manager and says “this customer's salmon was missing from box 3.” That's <b>escalate at once, with a handoff that has the facts</b>. <b>Rule: explicit request for a human → escalate now, and pass the case details.</b>", "options": ["Offering a fix first is kind, but the customer already said what they want.", "Checking the order first makes them wait for something they didn't ask for.", "Fetching the manager is right, but the manager has to ask everything again.", "Correct. Fetch the manager now and tell them what happened."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the agent should resolve what it can</b>, because the refund is within its limit. That is right when a customer is unhappy but hasn't asked for a person. An explicit request for a human is its own trigger: honour it straight away.", "diagram": "\"I want a real person\" → escalate now\n  handoff: customer ID, box, missing item\n  person picks up without asking again", "mapTitle": "When should the agent hand over to a person?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Agent's move", "Why"], "rows": [["Customer explicitly asks for a person", "Escalate at once, with the facts", "The request is the trigger"], ["Frustrated, simple case, no request for a person", "Offer the fix it can make", "Resolve when it's straightforward"], ["Refund above the agent's limit", "Escalate", "Policy limit"], ["Policy doesn't cover the case", "Escalate", "A gap needs a human call"], ["Request is unclear", "Ask a clarifying question", "Don't guess"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "I want to speak to a real person", "means": "The decider: an explicit request. Offering a refund first (<b>[[0]]</b>) makes the customer ask twice."}, {"quote": "The refund is well inside the agent's limit, so the agent could settle the case alone", "means": "Being able to resolve it is not a reason to override the request, or to stop for a lookup (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "How should the agent respond?", "means": "Escalate now, with the facts so the person doesn't re-ask (unlike <b>[[2]]</b>): <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Customer asks for a human? Escalate immediately and pass the case facts in the handoff.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Escalate immediately on explicit demand; offer resolution when straightforward."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-09", "was": "m7-s1-09", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Access failure vs valid empty result", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The policy subagent checks a customer's past refunds through a payments API. When that API times out, the subagent tells the coordinator “No previous refunds found.” The coordinator then approves refunds that repeat earlier ones. The API's outages usually last about an hour. How should the subagent <b>report this case</b>?", "options": [{"t": "As a failed lookup, naming the query it tried and any past refunds it did get back.", "why": "Best. A failed lookup must look different from a real empty result. With the failure, the query and any partial results, the coordinator can hold the refund, escalate or try later."}, {"t": "Retry with backoff first, then report “history unavailable” if every retry fails.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Retries help with short blips, but hour-long outages outlast them, and a bare status drops what was tried and found.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "As an error marked “transient”, so the coordinator reruns the subagent later.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. It separates failure from empty, which is half the fix, but drops the query and any partial results the coordinator could use now.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "As before, but the coordinator escalates every refund where no past refunds come back.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. No repeat refunds slip through, but every genuine first-time claim goes to a person too, and the timeout still looks like “none”.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "When that API times out, the subagent tells the coordinator “No previous refunds found.”", "explanation": "Subagents must distinguish access failures from valid empty results, and report failures with context: what was attempted, the error type and any partial results, so the coordinator can decide what to do.", "eli5": {"question": "The refund-history check sometimes can't reach the payments system, and then reports “no past refunds.” So the boss approves repeat refunds. The clue: “couldn't check” is reported as “nothing there.”", "answer": "If you can't open a filing cabinet, you don't tell your boss “the folder is empty.” You say “the cabinet was locked; I looked for Jo's refunds; I found two in the other drawer.” That's reporting an <b>access failure</b> with its <b>context</b>. <b>Rule: “couldn't check” must never look like “checked, found nothing.”</b>", "options": ["Correct. Say the check failed, what you tried, and what you did find.", "Trying the locked cabinet a few more times doesn't help when it stays locked for an hour.", "Saying “try again later” is better than “empty”, but leaves out what you already found.", "Sending every “empty” folder to a person catches repeats, but buries them in real first-timers."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the answer is to retry harder</b>. But the bug is in the report: a timeout comes back looking like a real empty result, so the coordinator trusts it. The subagent must say the lookup failed, and pass on what it tried and found.", "diagram": "timeout → \"no refunds\"      → coordinator approves (wrong)\ntimeout → failed + query + partial → hold, escalate or retry", "mapTitle": "How should a subagent report a lookup?", "map": {"head": ["What happened", "Report as", "Coordinator can"], "rows": [["Lookup worked, nothing found", "Valid empty result", "Trust it and proceed"], ["Lookup timed out, nothing retrieved", "Failure, with the query tried", "Hold, retry later or escalate"], ["Lookup failed partway", "Failure, plus the partial results", "Use what's there and decide"], ["Short blip, retry worked", "Normal result", "Proceed"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When that API times out, the subagent tells the coordinator “No previous refunds found.”", "means": "The decider: a failure reported as an empty result. Escalating every empty result (<b>[[3]]</b>) works around this without fixing it."}, {"quote": "The coordinator then approves refunds that repeat earlier ones", "means": "The coordinator trusted a result that wasn't real. It needs to know the check failed."}, {"quote": "The API's outages usually last about an hour", "means": "Local retries (<b>[[1]]</b>) won't outlast the outage, so the report itself must change."}, {"quote": "How should the subagent report this case?", "means": "A failure with the query and partial results, not a bare transient flag (<b>[[2]]</b>): <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Never report “couldn't check” as “found nothing.” Report the failure with what was tried and any partial results.", "guide": {"obj": "5.3 Error propagation across multi-agent systems", "quote": "Distinguish access failures (retry) from valid empty results."}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s1-10", "was": "m7-s1-10", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Case facts across summarisation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Some refund disputes go on for a long time. When a chat gets close to the context limit, the SDK replaces older turns with a summary. After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier. It also forgets which items the customer said were missing. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Before each refund offer, have the agent call <code>lookup_order</code> again to reload the order and its items.", "why": "Works, but only for what the order system knows. It reloads the order, but the promised amount and the items the customer reported missing were said in the chat, not stored there.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Raise the threshold for summarising older turns, so more of each chat stays word for word.", "why": "Works, but only delays it. More of each chat stays word for word, but long disputes still cross the threshold and the same facts get blurred.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Tell the summariser to quote every amount and item mentioned in the earlier turns.", "why": "Works, but still lossy. A careful summary keeps most amounts, but the facts go through a summary on every pass, and each pass can drop or change them.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block of promised amounts, missing items and order IDs in every request.", "why": "Best. Facts kept outside the summarised history survive every summary, and the agent sees them on every turn."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier.", "explanation": "Summarising blurs numbers, dates and promises. Pull them into a persistent case-facts block that is sent with every request instead of being summarised.", "eli5": {"question": "To save space, old parts of the chat get squeezed into a summary. After that, the agent forgets what it promised. The clue: the facts get lost <i>in the summary</i>.", "answer": "When a long meeting is summarised, exact numbers get rounded off. A good assistant keeps a separate card with the key facts: “promised $40 credit; salmon and rice missing”. The summary can be rough; the card stays exact. That card is a <b>case-facts block</b> sent with every request. <b>Rule: numbers and promises that must survive go in a facts block, not the summary.</b>", "options": ["Re-reading the order helps, but the promises were made in the chat, not in the order.", "Summarising later only moves the problem further down the chat.", "A better summary is still a summary; numbers can still slip.", "Correct. Keep the key facts on a separate card that never gets summarised."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary</b> will keep the facts. But every summary is a lossy step, and a long chat gets summarised again and again. Exact facts such as amounts, items and promises should not depend on a summary at all. Keep them in a separate block that goes with every request.", "diagram": "older turns   → summary (blurry)     ┐\ncase facts: $40 credit, salmon      ├→ every request\nrecent turns  (word for word)       ┘", "mapTitle": "Facts go missing in a long session. Where are they lost?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Promised amounts or items drift after summarising", "Facts blurred by summaries", "Persistent case-facts block"], ["Context fills fast with long tool output", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the needed fields"], ["Details in the middle of a long input are missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key facts first, clear section headers"], ["Claims lose their sources after summarising", "No claim-to-source mapping", "Keep the source with each claim"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "When a chat gets close to the context limit, the SDK replaces older turns with a summary", "means": "Facts from early turns now exist only inside summaries."}, {"quote": "After that, the agent sometimes offers a smaller refund than it promised earlier", "means": "The decider. The loss starts after summarising, so the summary is the cause. Reloading the order (<b>[[0]]</b>) doesn't bring back what was promised in the chat."}, {"quote": "It also forgets which items the customer said were missing", "means": "More than one kind of fact is lost, so a better summary prompt (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a later threshold (<b>[[1]]</b>) only reduces or delays it."}, {"quote": "Which change best fixes this?", "means": "Take the facts out of the summarised history: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Exact facts that must survive a long chat go into a case-facts block, not into summaries.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Extract transactional facts into a persistent \"case facts\" block"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-09", "was": "m7-s2-09", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 /compact for context pressure", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer is midway through generating a set of screens in one long session. The context is nearly full. Most of it is long test output from earlier runs. Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to the right files. Several screens are still left to build. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude write its decisions to a notes file, then start a new session that reads it first.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt for now. A notes file and a fresh session are the fix when answers have turned vague or work spans sessions. This session is still healthy.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Route future test runs through a subagent that returns only the failing tests and their causes.", "why": "Works, but for later. It keeps future test output small, but it doesn't free the space that is already full.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> to condense the conversation so far, then carry on with the screens.", "why": "Best. The session is accurate; only space is short. Compacting frees context and keeps the work in the same session."}, {"t": "Run <code>/clear</code>, then give Claude a short list of the screens still to build and continue.", "why": "Works, but throws away good work. Space is freed, but the decisions and file details Claude is using correctly are gone.", "trap": "loses the work"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to the right files.", "explanation": "/compact reduces context use within a healthy session. Scratchpad files and fresh sessions are for degraded sessions or work across sessions; subagents prevent future bloat.", "eli5": {"question": "The session is almost out of space, mostly from old test output. Claude is still giving good, specific answers. The clue: the session is <i>healthy</i>; it is only <i>full</i>.", "answer": "Your desk is covered in old printouts, but you are working well. You don't move to a new desk; you clear the clutter into a short pile of notes and keep going. That is <code>/compact</code>: it condenses the conversation so far and keeps the session. <b>Rule: healthy but full → /compact. Confused and drifting → notes file and a fresh session.</b>", "options": ["Moving to a new desk with a notes file is the fix when you've lost the thread. You haven't.", "A rule to print less from now on is good, but the desk is already covered.", "Correct. Tidy the desk and keep working.", "Sweeping the desk clean frees space, but throws away work you still need."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context means starting again</b>. A fresh session with a notes file is the fix for <i>degradation</i>: vague answers, “typical patterns” instead of specific files. Here Claude's answers are still specific, so only space is short. <code>/compact</code> reduces context use inside the same session.", "mapTitle": "Context trouble: what kind, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Nearly full, answers still specific", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Answers vague or inconsistent", "Context degradation", "Notes file, then a fresh session"], ["Verbose output will keep piling up", "Discovery in the main context", "Subagent returns summaries"], ["Work must survive a crash or a new day", "No saved state", "Scratchpad or manifest file"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Most of it is long test output from earlier runs", "means": "Old output already fills the space. Routing future runs (<b>[[1]]</b>) doesn't remove it."}, {"quote": "Claude's recent answers are still accurate and refer to the right files", "means": "The decider. No degradation, so a fresh session (<b>[[0]]</b>) or a full clear (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives up working context for nothing."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do next?", "means": "Free space, keep the session: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Session still accurate but full → /compact. Session degraded → scratchpad notes and a fresh session.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "/compact to reduce context usage"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s2-10", "was": "m7-s2-10", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Lost in the middle", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer pastes a long product spec into Claude to build a booking endpoint. The spec opens with an overview and ends with acceptance criteria. The generated code meets both. It misses rules from the middle sections, such as the late-cancellation window. The whole spec fits easily in the context. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Split the spec by section, have Claude build each part in its own pass, then run an integration pass.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Separate passes give each section full attention, but they suit input too big for one context. This spec fits, and the miss is about position.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Put a short list of the key rules at the top of the prompt, with the full spec below under clear headings.", "why": "Best. Models read the start and end of long input reliably and can miss the middle. Key rules first, with headings, counters that directly."}, {"t": "Ask Claude to list every rule in the spec first, then check its code against that list.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A rule list helps, but Claude builds it from the same long input, so middle rules can be missed in the list too.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have Claude summarise the spec first, then build the endpoint from that summary instead.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. A summary is shorter, but summaries tend to drop exact details like time windows, the very rules being missed.", "trap": "loses detail"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "It misses rules from the middle sections, such as the late-cancellation window.", "explanation": "Long inputs suffer from “lost in the middle”: beginnings and ends are processed reliably, middles less so. Put key facts first and use explicit section headings.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude follows the start and end of a long spec but misses rules in the middle. The clue: the misses are about <i>where</i> the rules sit, not how long the spec is.", "answer": "In a long briefing, people remember the opening and the closing, and the middle blurs. A good briefing starts with “the five things you must remember” and uses clear headings. For Claude, that means a short list of key rules at the top, with the full spec below under headings. <b>Rule: lost in the middle → key points first, clear section headings.</b>", "options": ["Splitting the briefing into separate meetings works, but it's for briefings too long for one sitting.", "Correct. Lead with the must-remember rules, then the full spec with headings.", "Asking the listener to write their own list helps, but they can miss the same middle parts.", "A summary is shorter, but it's where small details like time limits get dropped."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>missed details mean the input is too long</b>, so it should be split or shortened. Here it fits easily. The problem is position: models reliably use the beginning and end of long input and can skip the middle. Move the key rules to where attention is strong and add <b>clear section headers</b>.", "diagram": "KEY RULES (late-cancel window, ...)   ← read reliably\n## Overview\n## Booking rules ...  (middle, easy to miss)\n## Acceptance criteria                 ← read reliably", "mapTitle": "Details go missing from long input. Why?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Start and end used, middle missed; input fits", "Lost in the middle", "Key points first, clear headers"], ["Input too large for one context", "Size", "Per-section passes plus an integration pass"], ["Numbers and dates blurred after summarising", "Lossy summary", "Keep exact facts in a facts block"], ["Context fills with long tool output", "Verbose results", "Trim to the needed fields"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The spec opens with an overview and ends with acceptance criteria. The generated code meets both", "means": "The start and the end are used correctly."}, {"quote": "It misses rules from the middle sections, such as the late-cancellation window", "means": "The decider: a position problem. A summary (<b>[[3]]</b>) tends to drop exactly this kind of detail, and a self-made list (<b>[[2]]</b>) reads the same middle."}, {"quote": "The whole spec fits easily in the context", "means": "Size is not the problem, so splitting into passes (<b>[[0]]</b>) is more than needed."}, {"quote": "Which change best fixes this?", "means": "Put the key rules where attention is strong: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Lost in the middle → key points at the start and explicit section headers.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "place key summaries at the beginning + explicit section headers"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-09", "was": "m7-s3-09", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Lost in the middle", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "The synthesis subagent gets one long input: findings from every analysed paper, in the order the papers were found. Each finding is already cut down to a few relevant fields. Reviewers say the summaries cover the first and last papers well. Findings from papers in the middle of the input are often missing. Which change <b>best addresses this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Split the findings into batches, summarise each batch, then merge the batch summaries.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. Shorter inputs lose less, but it adds passes and a merge step, where claims can lose their sources.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Have an independent reviewer instance list the papers each draft leaves out.", "why": "Works, but too late. A fresh reviewer can catch missing papers, but it finds the gap after the draft and adds a pass to every run.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Start the input with a digest of the key findings, and put the details under headings.", "why": "Best. Models read the start and end of a long input most reliably. A leading digest and clear section headings keep the middle from being lost."}, {"t": "Order the findings so the strongest studies sit at the start and the end of the input.", "why": "Works, but moves the problem. The strongest studies get read, but whatever lands in the middle is still skipped.", "trap": "moves the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Findings from papers in the middle of the input are often missing.", "explanation": "Models reliably process the beginning and end of long inputs but may omit the middle. Put key findings first and organise the rest under explicit headers.", "eli5": {"question": "In one long input, the start and end get used, but the middle gets skipped. The clue: what goes missing depends on <i>where</i> it sits.", "answer": "When you skim a long report, you remember the first and last pages. A good report opens with a one-page summary and uses clear headings, so nothing in the middle is lost. That's the fix here: a <b>digest of key findings at the start</b> and <b>section headings</b> for the rest. <b>Rule: lost in the middle → key points first, clear headings throughout.</b>", "options": ["Splitting the report into small booklets works, but now you also have to combine them.", "A second reader who lists what was skipped helps, but only after the report is written.", "Correct. A summary page up front and clear headings.", "Putting the best pages first and last helps those pages, but the middle is still skimmed."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the input is too big and needs cutting</b>. But the findings are already trimmed. What's missing depends on position: start and end are covered, the middle isn't. That is the “lost in the middle” effect, and the guide's fix is a summary at the start plus clear section headers.", "diagram": "[digest: key findings from every paper]\n## Efficacy   ...\n## Safety     ...\n## Cost       ...", "mapTitle": "Details go missing from a long input. Why?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Start and end used, middle missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key digest first + section headings"], ["Input bloated with fields nobody uses", "Verbose tool output", "Trim to relevant fields"], ["Facts drift after summarising a long chat", "Lossy summaries", "Case-facts block"], ["Claims lose their sources after merging", "No claim-source mapping", "Keep sources with each claim"], ["One pass over many items misses some", "Attention diluted", "Per-item passes, then integration"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "findings from every analysed paper, in the order the papers were found", "means": "No structure: important findings can land anywhere, including the middle."}, {"quote": "Each finding is already cut down to a few relevant fields", "means": "Size has already been tackled, so the cause is layout, not bulk."}, {"quote": "Reviewers say the summaries cover the first and last papers well", "means": "Coverage depends on position, the signature of “lost in the middle”."}, {"quote": "Findings from papers in the middle of the input are often missing", "means": "The decider. Reordering (<b>[[3]]</b>) only moves what's in the middle; a reviewer (<b>[[1]]</b>) catches it late; batches (<b>[[0]]</b>) add machinery."}, {"quote": "Which change best addresses this?", "means": "Digest first, headings for the rest: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Middle of a long input gets missed → put key findings first and use clear section headers.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "place key summaries at the beginning + explicit section headers"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s3-10", "was": "m7-s3-10", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Structured error propagation", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "The trial-registry subagent sometimes cannot reach the registry, and these outages can last for hours. Today it raises an exception that ends the whole run and throws away finished work from the other subagents. The team wants the coordinator to decide what happens next: wait, use another source, or publish with a noted gap. Which <b>TWO</b> changes should the subagent make?", "options": [{"t": "Retry the registry call with backoff for a short while before reporting back.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. A short retry rides out brief blips, but these outages last hours, and the coordinator still learns nothing it can decide with.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Return a structured failure with the error type, the query it tried and possible alternatives.", "why": "Best. Structured error context tells the coordinator what failed and what else could work, so it can choose to wait, switch source or publish with a gap."}, {"t": "Log the full error to the team's monitoring dashboard and page the on-call engineer.", "why": "Works, but solves a different problem. People hear about the outage, but the coordinator gets nothing to decide with, and the run still ends.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Return any trial records it had already fetched before the registry went down.", "why": "Best. Passing partial results keeps finished work, and lets the coordinator publish with a noted gap instead of starting over."}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The team wants the coordinator to decide what happens next: wait, use another source, or publish with a noted gap.", "explanation": "Recover locally from brief errors, but pass unresolved ones up with structured context: failure type, attempted query, partial results and alternatives. Crashing the run and hiding the failure are both anti-patterns.", "eli5": {"question": "When the registry is down, the helper crashes the whole job. The team wants the coordinator to choose what to do next. The clue: the coordinator needs enough information to choose.", "answer": "A courier can't get into a building. Phoning the office to say “building locked, tried the front door, the back entrance might work, here are the parcels I did deliver” lets the office decide. Throwing the whole van away does not. For the subagent that means a <b>structured error</b> (what failed, what was tried, alternatives) plus its <b>partial results</b>. <b>Rule: pass failures up with context and partial results; don't crash and don't hide them.</b>", "options": ["Knocking again for a minute helps with a brief delay. These outages last hours.", "Correct. Say what failed, what was tried and what else might work.", "Telling the IT team is good practice, but it doesn't help the office decide what to do now.", "Correct. Hand back the parcels already delivered."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the subagent should just try harder</b> before giving up. Local retry is right for brief blips. These outages last hours, and the team wants the coordinator to make the call. That needs information: what failed, what was tried, what else might work, and what was already found.", "diagram": "registry down\n  → { error: \"registry_unreachable\", query: ...,\n      alternatives: [...], partial: [records] }\n  → coordinator: wait / switch / publish with gap", "mapTitle": "A subagent hits an error. What does it send back?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right response", "Why"], "rows": [["Brief, transient blip", "Retry locally with backoff", "Usually clears on its own"], ["Outage that won't clear soon", "Structured error + partial results to the coordinator", "Coordinator chooses recovery"], ["Query ran and found nothing", "A valid empty result, not an error", "Absence is an answer"], ["Any failure ends the whole run", "Anti-pattern", "Loses finished work"], ["Failure reported as success or as “operation failed”", "Anti-pattern", "Hides what happened"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "these outages can last for hours", "means": "A short retry (<b>[[0]]</b>) won't outlast the outage."}, {"quote": "Today it raises an exception that ends the whole run and throws away finished work from the other subagents", "means": "Ending the run on one failure is an anti-pattern, and finished work should be kept: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The team wants the coordinator to decide what happens next", "means": "The coordinator needs facts to decide with: <b>[[1]]</b>. Paging a person (<b>[[2]]</b>) doesn't give it any."}, {"quote": "wait, use another source, or publish with a noted gap", "means": "Alternatives support “use another source”; partial results support “publish with a noted gap”."}], "rule": "Unresolved errors go up with structure: failure type, query tried, alternatives, partial results.", "guide": {"obj": "5.3 Error propagation across multi-agent systems", "quote": "Structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, alternatives) enables coordinator recovery"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-09", "was": "m7-s6-09", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Stratified sampling and field-level routing", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "The team wants to send only doubtful fields to reviewers and post the rest directly. Accuracy was measured on a uniform random sample and is high overall. Scans from small carriers are rare in that sample. Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before any field skips review. Which <b>two steps</b> together achieve this?", "options": [{"t": "Label a new sample with a quota for each document type, including enough small-carrier scans.", "why": "Best. A quota per type gives enough examples of each, including the rare scans, to measure each type's error rate."}, {"t": "Label a larger uniform random sample, and report accuracy for each field across all documents.", "why": "Works, but rare types stay rare. A bigger sample and per-field numbers help, but small-carrier scans are still too few to measure, and results are not split by type.", "trap": "only partly"}, {"t": "Have the model report a confidence for each field, and send any field below a fixed cutoff to a reviewer.", "why": "Works, but unchecked. Field-level routing is the right shape, but self-reported confidence is not matched against real errors, so the cutoff is a guess.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Calibrate each field's confidence on the labelled set, and send fields below its threshold to review.", "why": "Best. Thresholds set from labelled results route each field by its measured error rate, which is the routing the team wants."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before any field skips review.", "explanation": "High overall accuracy can hide weak document types. Measure with a stratified sample, then route fields by confidence calibrated on that labelled set.", "eli5": {"question": "The team wants to skip review for fields the model is sure about. The clue: compliance wants each type of document measured, and rare scans barely appear in the sample.", "answer": "A school says “95% passed”, but only two pupils from the small village took the test. To know how the village does, you test a set number from each place. That's a <b>stratified sample</b>. Then you only trust a pupil's “I'm sure” once you've checked how often “sure” was right. That's <b>calibrated confidence</b>. <b>Rule: measure each type on purpose, then route by confidence checked against real answers.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Test a set number from each type, rare ones included.", "A bigger random test still has hardly any village pupils.", "Trusting “I'm sure” without checking how often it's right is a guess.", "Correct. Set each cut-off from how often “sure” was really right."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>high overall accuracy</b> means it is safe to skip review. An average can hide a weak document type, and rare types barely show up in a uniform sample. Two things are needed: <b>measure each type</b> on purpose, and <b>route each field</b> by confidence that has been checked against labelled answers.", "diagram": "stratified sample (quota per type) → labels\n  → error rate per type and field\n  → calibrated thresholds → route doubtful fields", "mapTitle": "Is it safe to let some extractions skip human review?", "map": {"head": ["Need", "Right step", "Why not the alternative"], "rows": [["Error rate for each document type, rare ones too", "Stratified sample with a quota per type", "Uniform samples barely include rare types"], ["Route individual fields, not whole documents", "Field-level confidence calibrated on labels", "Raw self-reported confidence is uncalibrated"], ["Check high-confidence results stay accurate", "Ongoing stratified random sampling", "Errors drift without a check"], ["A single headline accuracy number", "Not enough on its own", "Masks weak types and fields"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team wants to send only doubtful fields to reviewers and post the rest directly", "means": "Routing is per field, so the confidence must be per field and trustworthy: <b>[[3]]</b>, not a raw score (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Accuracy was measured on a uniform random sample and is high overall", "means": "An overall number can hide weak types."}, {"quote": "Scans from small carriers are rare in that sample", "means": "A larger uniform sample (<b>[[1]]</b>) still holds few of them."}, {"quote": "Compliance wants error rates measured for each document type before any field skips review", "means": "The decider: sample by type with a quota, <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Measure by type with a stratified sample, then route fields by confidence calibrated on labels.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Stratified random sampling measures high-confidence error rates; field-level confidence calibrated on labeled sets routes review"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m7p-s6-10", "was": "m7-s6-10", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Provenance for human review", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Reviewers check flagged certificates against the source PDF. Policy schedules can run to dozens of pages. Most of each review is spent hunting for where a value was printed. The extraction output holds the values and nothing else. Which change <b>most speeds up</b> these reviews?", "options": [{"t": "Return a calibrated confidence for each field, and open each review on the lowest-scoring ones.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It tells reviewers which fields to check first, but not where on the dozens of pages each value is.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Highlight every place in the PDF where each extracted value's text appears.", "why": "Works, but noisy. Exact matches help, but a date or limit can appear many times, and a misread value appears nowhere.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Return each value with the page number and the exact text span it was read from.", "why": "Best. Reviewers jump straight to the source of each value, which removes the hunting."}, {"t": "Return the values grouped under the schedule's own section headings, in document order.", "why": "Works, but only narrows it. Section headings point to the right area, but reviewers still search within each section.", "trap": "only narrows it"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Most of each review is spent hunting for where a value was printed.", "explanation": "Keep the source with each extracted value: page and exact span. Reviewers then verify instead of search.", "eli5": {"question": "Reviewers spend most of their time looking for where each number came from in a long document. The clue: the output gives values but no sources.", "answer": "A student's essay quotes facts with no page numbers, so the teacher hunts through the book to check each one. Ask for “page 12, line 4” beside each fact and checking takes seconds. That's <b>source provenance</b>: each value carries the page and exact text it came from. <b>Rule: if people check values against a document, return the location with each value.</b>", "options": ["Saying which facts are shaky helps choose what to check, not find it.", "Highlighting every match helps, but common numbers light up everywhere, and misread ones nowhere.", "Correct. Give the page and the exact words for each value.", "Chapter titles narrow the search, but you still read the chapter."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>reviews are slow because there are too many fields</b>, so you prioritise them. The stem says the time goes into <i>finding</i> each value. The fix is to keep the <b>source with each value</b>, the same way a research report keeps a source with each claim.", "diagram": "limit: 1,000,000\n  source: page 14, “Each Occurrence $1,000,000”\n  → reviewer opens page 14, checks, done", "mapTitle": "Human review is slow. Where does the time go?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Time spent finding where each value is", "No source kept with values", "Page and exact span per value"], ["Time spent checking fields that were fine", "No way to pick which fields to check", "Calibrated field confidence"], ["Reviewers unsure what a value means", "Values lack context", "Field descriptions in the review screen"], ["Errors cluster in some document types", "Uneven accuracy", "Stratified sampling by type"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Policy schedules can run to dozens of pages", "means": "Searching by eye is slow, and a section grouping (<b>[[3]]</b>) still leaves pages to read."}, {"quote": "Most of each review is spent hunting for where a value was printed", "means": "The decider: the cost is finding, not choosing what to check (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The extraction output holds the values and nothing else", "means": "The source is thrown away at extraction. Searching for the text afterwards (<b>[[1]]</b>) misses misread values."}, {"quote": "Which change most speeds up these reviews?", "means": "Keep the source with each value: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "If people check values against a document, return the page and exact span with each value.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Preserve structured claim-source mappings through synthesis"}}, "source": "Mock 7"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-08", "was": "m6-s1-08", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Trimming tool output", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "In chats about several boxes, the context fills after only a few lookups. The SDK then starts summarising older turns, and the summaries blur the box totals the agent quotes later. Each <code>lookup_order</code> result carries dozens of fields, such as warehouse codes and tax lines. The agent's replies use about six of them. These results take up most of the context. Which change <b>best addresses</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Add a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that strips each order payload down to the attributes replies need.", "why": "Best. Unused fields fill the context and force early summarising, which is where totals blur. Trimming results as they arrive removes that pressure."}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block with the box totals the agent quotes, sent along with every request.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. It protects the totals, but the context still fills with unused fields after a few lookups.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Put a summary of the key figures at the top of the context under clear section headings.", "why": "Works, but for a different issue. Position and headings help with things missed in the middle. The figures come from the same blurring summary.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Start a new session from a structured summary once a chat passes a set number of turns.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The new session fills with the same long results within a few lookups.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "These results take up most of the context.", "explanation": "Tool results can take up context far out of proportion to their value. Trim them to the relevant fields as they arrive; use case-facts blocks for values that must survive long chats.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent misquotes box totals after the SDK summarises older turns. The clue: the lookups carry dozens of fields, the agent uses about six, and they <i>take up most of the context</i>.", "answer": "If every delivery note is a fifty-page printout and you need one line, your desk fills up fast and you start throwing pages away in a hurry. Cut each printout to the lines you use as it arrives. That is <b>trimming tool output to the relevant fields</b>, done by a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook. <b>Rule: tool results crowding the context → trim them as they arrive.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Cut each printout to the lines you use.", "A sticky note of key totals protects them, but the desk still fills with printouts.", "Putting key figures on top helps when things get lost in a pile. These figures come from the blurred notes.", "A clean desk fills up again with the same printouts."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat <b>blurred totals as a memory problem</b> and protect them with a case-facts block. That protects the figures, but the cause is upstream: results with dozens of unused fields fill the context and force early summarising. <b>Trimming tool output</b> removes the pressure at its source.", "diagram": "lookup_order → dozens of fields → context full\n             → early summary blurs totals\nhook trims   → about six fields → room to spare", "mapTitle": "Details get lost in long chats. What is the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Tool results fill most of the context", "Verbose results", "Trim to the relevant fields as they arrive"], ["Lean results, but a long chat loses figures", "Lossy summarising", "Case-facts block in every request"], ["Middle of a long input missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key points first, clear headings"], ["Old session has stale tool results", "Staleness", "New session from a summary"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the summaries blur the box totals the agent quotes later", "means": "The symptom. A case-facts block (<b>[[1]]</b>) treats this, not what fills the context."}, {"quote": "Each lookup_order result carries dozens of fields, such as warehouse codes and tax lines", "means": "Most of each result is never used. A fresh session (<b>[[3]]</b>) fills with the same fields."}, {"quote": "These results take up most of the context", "means": "The decider. The pressure comes from tool output, so trim it: <b>[[0]]</b>. Moving figures to the top (<b>[[2]]</b>) solves position, not size."}], "rule": "Tool results crowding the context → trim to the fields used, with a PostToolUse hook.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "trim verbose tool outputs to relevant fields"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-09", "was": "m6-s1-09", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Validating by category before automating", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Today a person approves every redelivery the agent proposes for a damaged box. Leadership wants the agent to approve redeliveries on its own. They point to last month's audit, where reviewers agreed with the agent in 96% of randomly sampled chats. Only about 20 of the sampled chats were redelivery cases. What is the <b>most appropriate</b> step before making the change?", "options": [{"t": "Add confidence scores for box ID and delivery date, calibrated on labelled chats, and route low ones.", "why": "Works, but too early. Calibrated confidence is a good way to route review once redelivery accuracy is known. It hasn't been measured yet.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Write clear criteria for when a redelivery needs a person, with an example chat for each.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Criteria improve the agent's judgment, but they don't show whether its redelivery decisions are accurate enough.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Sample more redelivery chats until their own agreement rate is measured, then decide.", "why": "Best. The 96% rests on about twenty redelivery chats and says little about them. Measure the category being automated before automating it."}, {"t": "Add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that sends redeliveries above a set value to <code>escalate_to_human</code>.", "why": "Works, but guards only the big ones. A hook enforces a limit well, but it says nothing about the quality of decisions below it.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Only about 20 of the sampled chats were redelivery cases.", "explanation": "Overall accuracy can hide poor performance on one type of case. Measure accuracy for the category being automated, with enough samples, before automating it.", "eli5": {"question": "Leadership wants to let the agent approve redeliveries alone, citing a 96% overall score. The clue: only about <i>20</i> of the audited chats were redeliveries.", "answer": "A student who scores 96% overall might have got most of the few geometry questions wrong. Before you let them teach geometry, you test them on geometry. That is <b>validating accuracy by category</b>: sample enough redelivery chats to measure that type on its own. <b>Rule: before automating a type of case, measure accuracy for that type.</b>", "options": ["Grading how sure the student feels is useful later, once you know their geometry score.", "A clearer marking guide helps the student, but it doesn't tell you how good they are now.", "Correct. Test geometry on its own before trusting them with it.", "Checking only the hardest problems leaves the rest of their geometry untested."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to trust <b>a high overall accuracy</b>. An overall figure can hide poor results on one type of case, and here redeliveries were a small slice of the sample. The exam guide says to <b>validate by type before automating</b>, then use calibrated confidence to route review.", "mapTitle": "Ready to automate a type of case?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Next step", "Why"], "rows": [["Overall score high, this type barely sampled", "Sample this type until its accuracy is measured", "Overall figures hide weak types"], ["This type measured and accurate", "Calibrated confidence routes the doubtful ones", "Review where it is needed"], ["Agent's judgment on edge cases is poor", "Explicit criteria with examples", "Sharpens decisions"], ["A hard money limit must hold", "Hook in code", "Deterministic"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Leadership wants the agent to approve redeliveries on its own", "means": "A decision to automate one type of case."}, {"quote": "reviewers agreed with the agent in 96% of randomly sampled chats", "means": "An overall figure, across all types."}, {"quote": "Only about 20 of the sampled chats were redelivery cases", "means": "The decider. Redelivery accuracy is unknown, so routing by confidence (<b>[[0]]</b>), criteria (<b>[[1]]</b>) and a value hook (<b>[[3]]</b>) all come before it is measured. Measure first: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Before automating a type of case, measure accuracy for that type with enough samples.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Validate accuracy by document type and field before automating"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s1-10", "was": "m6-s1-10", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Escalation calibration", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 2, "question": "First-contact resolution is below target. A review of recent escalations shows two patterns. The agent hands off routine cases that a person closes in one step. It also keeps working on requests the refund policy does not cover. The fix has to ship this sprint. Only a few hundred past chats are labelled with the right outcome. Which <b>TWO</b> changes together best recalibrate the agent?", "options": [{"t": "State when to escalate: the customer asks for a person, policy is silent, or no path is left.", "why": "Best. Explicit triggers draw the line the agent is guessing at today."}, {"t": "Add a few example chats that contrast a handoff with a resolution on similar borderline cases.", "why": "Best. Contrasting examples show how to apply the criteria where the line is hard to read."}, {"t": "Train a classifier on the labelled chats that decides before each reply whether to hand off.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A classifier can work with lots of labelled data and time. With a few hundred labels and one sprint, fixing the agent's own criteria is proportionate.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Attach a summary with customer ID, cause, amount and next step to each handoff call.", "why": "Works, but different problem. A structured handoff helps the person who takes over, but it doesn't change which cases are handed off.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [0, 1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The agent hands off routine cases that a person closes in one step. It also keeps working on requests the refund policy does not cover.", "explanation": "Unclear escalation boundaries are best fixed with explicit criteria plus a few contrasting examples. A classifier is disproportionate here; handoff summaries improve the handoff, not the decision.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent hands off easy cases and holds on to cases it should hand off. The clue: it is <i>wrong in both directions</i>, so it doesn't know where the line is.", "answer": "A new staff member who calls the manager for easy returns, but argues over unusual ones, needs two things: a clear list of when to call the manager, and a few side-by-side examples of near-identical cases handled each way. That is <b>explicit escalation criteria plus few-shot examples</b>. <b>Rule: unclear escalation line → state the triggers and show contrasting examples.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A clear list of when to call the manager.", "Correct. Side-by-side examples show where the line falls on close calls.", "Hiring a separate screener could work with lots of history and time. You have neither.", "A better note for the manager helps once they are called, not with deciding when to call."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat a calibration problem as a <b>routing problem</b> and add a separate classifier. The agent errs both ways because its <b>decision line is unclear</b>. Writing the line down, then showing borderline examples, is the proportionate fix.", "mapTitle": "Escalation is off. What kind of fix?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Escalates easy cases, keeps hard ones", "Unclear decision line", "Explicit criteria plus contrasting examples"], ["Lots of labelled data, time to build", "Routing could be learned", "A classifier may compete"], ["Humans lack context after handoff", "Thin handoff", "Structured handoff summary"], ["Escalates on angry tone", "Sentiment used as a proxy", "Criteria based on the case"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent hands off routine cases that a person closes in one step", "means": "Escalating too much."}, {"quote": "It also keeps working on requests the refund policy does not cover", "means": "Escalating too little. Both directions point to an unclear line: criteria (<b>[[0]]</b>) and examples (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Only a few hundred past chats are labelled with the right outcome", "means": "Too little data and time for a new classifier (<b>[[2]]</b>). A handoff summary (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't change who gets handed off."}], "rule": "Unclear escalation boundary → explicit criteria plus a few contrasting examples, not a classifier.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Triggers: customer requests a human; policy exceptions/gaps; inability to progress"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-09", "was": "m6-s2-09", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Scratchpads and session handover", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "An engineer has spent hours auditing tenant leaks across the job modules in <code>server/jobs/</code>. The context is nearly full. Claude's recent answers mention “typical” patterns that contradict its own earlier findings. The engineer will finish the audit tomorrow. A merge this morning changed many modules that were already reviewed. Tomorrow's work must start from accurate findings. Which <b>TWO</b> actions fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Have the engineer <code>--resume</code> this session tomorrow, opening with a list of the merged modules.", "why": "Works, but stale. Resuming keeps the history, but it brings back a degraded context full of results the merge has made out of date.", "trap": "stale context"}, {"t": "Have Claude write a per-module findings file (path, leak, line) into the repository before stopping.", "why": "Best. A findings file keeps the specific results outside the degrading context, ready for tomorrow."}, {"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> now with instructions to keep the findings, then carry on in this session tomorrow.", "why": "Works, but keeps the drift. Compacting frees space, but it condenses a context that is already mixing up its findings.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Start a fresh session tomorrow from a structured summary, rechecking the merged modules first.", "why": "Best. A clean session from a structured summary is more reliable than a degraded one, and rechecking covers the merge."}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude's recent answers mention “typical” patterns that contradict its own earlier findings.", "explanation": "Context degradation shows as answers about “typical patterns” instead of specific findings. Persist findings in a scratchpad file and start a new session from a structured summary, instead of resuming stale context.", "eli5": {"question": "A long audit session is full, and Claude has started mixing up its own findings. Work continues tomorrow, and some files changed overnight. The clue: answers about <i>“typical” patterns</i> that contradict earlier findings.", "answer": "After a long day, a detective's memory blurs. The fix is to write the case notes down while they are still right, then start fresh tomorrow from those notes, re-checking anything that changed. For Claude, that is a <b>findings file</b> (a scratchpad) plus a <b>fresh session from a structured summary</b>. <b>Rule: answers drifting → write findings down, start clean from them.</b>", "options": ["Picking up the same tired memory tomorrow brings back the blur, plus notes the merge made stale.", "Correct. Write the case notes down while they are still accurate.", "Squeezing a blurry memory into fewer pages makes it shorter, not clearer.", "Correct. Start fresh from the notes, and re-check what changed overnight."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context is only a space problem</b>, so compacting or resuming will do. The answers about “typical” patterns show <b>degradation</b>: the session is no longer reliable. And the merge has made some results stale. The fix is to save the good findings outside the context and start a new session from a structured summary.", "diagram": "today:    findings → findings file (path, leak, line)\ntomorrow: fresh session ← summary + file\n          → recheck merged modules first", "mapTitle": "Context trouble: what kind, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Answers vague, contradict earlier findings", "Context degradation", "Findings file, then a fresh session"], ["Nearly full, answers still specific", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Same work, nothing changed, session healthy", "Interrupted only", "<code>--resume</code> the session"], ["Verbose output piling up", "Discovery in main context", "Subagent returns summaries"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude's recent answers mention “typical” patterns that contradict its own earlier findings", "means": "The decider: degradation. Compacting (<b>[[2]]</b>) or resuming (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps the confused context."}, {"quote": "The engineer will finish the audit tomorrow", "means": "The findings must survive the break: a file in the repository (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "A merge this morning changed many modules that were already reviewed", "means": "Old tool results are stale. Resuming brings them back; a fresh start that rechecks those modules doesn't."}, {"quote": "Tomorrow's work must start from accurate findings", "means": "A clean session from a structured summary: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Session degraded and work continues later → save findings to a file, start fresh from a structured summary.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s2-10", "was": "m6-s2-10", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Isolating verbose output", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "The engineer is converting <code>server/</code> endpoints to tenant-scoped queries. Claude runs the integration suite after each endpoint. Each run prints thousands of lines, and only the few failures matter. Many endpoints remain in this session. Claude is already losing track of which endpoints it has converted. Which change <b>best keeps the session working</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> after every suite run, asking it to keep the converted list and failing tests.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. Each compact frees space, but the next run refills it, and detail is lost each time.", "trap": "only for a moment"}, {"t": "Keep a progress file of converted endpoints in the repo, and have Claude update and reread it each time.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The file fixes the forgetting, but the test logs still flood the context and crowd out the work.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Have a subagent run the suite each time and return a short list of failures and their causes.", "why": "Best. The subagent takes each long run in its own context and hands back only what matters, so the main session stays small."}, {"t": "Start a fresh session every ten endpoints, seeded with a summary of what is done and what is failing.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Each fresh session fills up again with the same verbose logs.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Each run prints thousands of lines, and only the few failures matter.", "explanation": "Subagent delegation isolates verbose output: the subagent reads it in its own context and returns a short summary. That removes the cause of context overflow.", "eli5": {"question": "Every test run dumps thousands of lines into the session, and Claude is starting to forget its own progress. The clue: only the <i>few failures</i> in each run matter.", "answer": "If a printer spits a thick report onto your desk after every task, your desk drowns. Better to have an assistant read each report and hand you a sticky note with the problems. That assistant is a <b>subagent</b>: it reads the long output in its own space and returns a short summary. <b>Rule: verbose output keeps flooding the session → delegate it to a subagent.</b>", "options": ["Tidying the desk after every report works for a moment, and each tidy loses a little.", "A progress list helps you remember, but the reports still pile up on the desk.", "Correct. An assistant reads the reports and hands you only the problems.", "Moving to a new desk every so often still lets the reports pile up again."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the forgetting is the problem</b>, so a progress file or a restart fixes it. Forgetting is the symptom. The cause is thousands of lines of test output entering the context every run. <b>Subagent delegation</b> keeps that output out of the main session and returns only the failures.", "diagram": "main session → subagent: run suite\n  subagent reads 1000s of lines\n  ← returns: 3 failures + causes", "mapTitle": "Context keeps filling up. What fixes the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Verbose output every step, little matters", "Logs flooding the context", "Subagent returns a short summary"], ["Progress must survive a break", "No saved state", "Progress or scratchpad file"], ["One-off build-up, session healthy", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Answers degraded, work continues", "Context degradation", "Fresh session from a summary"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude runs the integration suite after each endpoint", "means": "The flooding repeats every step, so one-off fixes (<b>[[0]]</b>, <b>[[3]]</b>) keep coming back."}, {"quote": "Each run prints thousands of lines, and only the few failures matter", "means": "The decider. The cause is verbose output entering the context."}, {"quote": "Claude is already losing track of which endpoints it has converted", "means": "The symptom. A progress file (<b>[[1]]</b>) helps memory but leaves the cause. Isolate the output: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Verbose output floods the session every step → have a subagent run it and return a summary.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-09", "was": "m6-s3-09", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Presenting conflicting sources", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Two recent sources disagree on the rate of major bleeding with a new drug. A large trial gives one figure, and a national registry report gives a higher one. The synthesis subagent kept only the trial figure. The committee needs to see both figures to judge them. Summaries go straight from the report subagent to the committee. Which change to the synthesis output <b>fits best</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Add a section for conflicting results that lists each estimate with its source, date and method.", "why": "Best. Conflicting statistics are kept with source attribution in a clear section, not settled by picking one."}, {"t": "Keep the figure from the stronger study design, and mention the other source's figure in a footnote.", "why": "Works, but still picks one. The other figure survives, but the summary has already chosen, and a footnote is easy to miss.", "trap": "still picks one"}, {"t": "Show every source's publication and data-collection dates next to the claims drawn from it.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Dates stop time gaps being read as conflicts. These sources are both recent and still disagree.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Present numeric findings as tables with a source column, and keep narrative findings as prose.", "why": "Works, but only the format. Tables suit numbers, but a table can still hold only the one figure synthesis chose.", "trap": "only the format"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The committee needs to see both figures to judge them.", "explanation": "When credible sources conflict, keep both values with attribution in a structured section rather than picking one.", "eli5": {"question": "Two good sources give different bleeding rates, and the summary kept only one. The clue: the committee <i>needs to see both figures</i> to judge them.", "answer": "If two thermometers give different readings, you don't throw one away. You write down both, which thermometer, when, and how it was used, and let the doctor decide. In a summary, that is a <b>conflicts section with source attribution</b>. <b>Rule: credible sources disagree → show every figure with its source. Don't pick one.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Both readings, each labelled with where, when and how it was taken.", "Keeping the \"better\" thermometer's reading up front still makes the choice for the doctor.", "Writing the time on each reading helps when the readings were taken at different times. Here both are recent.", "A neat table still only shows the one reading someone chose to keep."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a good synthesis resolves disagreements</b> by choosing the stronger source. When credible sources conflict and the reader must judge, picking one hides the disagreement. The exam guide says to <b>annotate conflicting statistics with source attribution</b>, not to pick one.", "mapTitle": "Sources differ. What should the synthesis do?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best output", "Why"], "rows": [["Credible sources give different figures", "Conflicts section, each figure with source and method", "Reader judges; nothing hidden"], ["Figures differ because of different dates", "Publication and collection dates beside claims", "Time gap, not a conflict"], ["Numbers mixed into long prose", "Tables for numbers, prose for narrative", "Right format per content"], ["Claims lose their sources in summaries", "Claim-source mappings kept through synthesis", "Every claim stays cited"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Two recent sources disagree on the rate of major bleeding with a new drug", "means": "Both are recent, so dates (<b>[[2]]</b>) don't explain the gap."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent kept only the trial figure", "means": "The fault: it picked one. A footnote approach (<b>[[1]]</b>) still picks one."}, {"quote": "The committee needs to see both figures to judge them", "means": "The decider. Both figures, with source and method: <b>[[0]]</b>. Table format (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't bring the lost figure back."}, {"quote": "Summaries go straight from the report subagent to the committee", "means": "Nobody adds the second figure later, so the synthesis output must carry it."}], "rule": "Conflicting credible figures → keep each one with its source in a conflicts section; don't pick one.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "annotate conflicting statistics with source attribution (don't arbitrarily pick one)"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s3-10", "was": "m6-s3-10", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Preserving figures across long runs", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "A large request produces many subagent results. The coordinator keeps a rolling summary of them and rewrites it as each result arrives. The synthesis subagent works from that summary. Audits show exact figures, such as a hazard ratio, turned into vague words like “lowered risk”. The full results are too long to give synthesis in one input. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator summarise each result once, on its own, instead of one rolling rewrite of them all.", "why": "Works, but still summarises numbers. Fewer rewrites lose less, but each summary can still turn a figure into vague words.", "trap": "still summarises numbers"}, {"t": "Tell the coordinator in its prompt to keep every exact number when it rewrites the summary.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The instruction helps, but repeated rewriting keeps condensing figures now and then.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Keep a separate facts block of exact figures with date and source, and pass it to synthesis as is.", "why": "Best. A persistent facts block keeps numbers, dates and sources out of progressive summarisation."}, {"t": "Trim each subagent result to the fields the summary needs before the coordinator reads it.", "why": "Works, but different problem. Trimming saves space, but the kept figures are still reworded in the rolling summary.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Audits show exact figures, such as a hazard ratio, turned into vague words like “lowered risk”.", "explanation": "Progressive summarisation loses exact figures. Extract them into a persistent facts block that is passed on unchanged.", "eli5": {"question": "A summary that keeps getting rewritten turns exact numbers into vague words. The clue: figures like a hazard ratio become <i>“lowered risk”</i>.", "answer": "Pass a story along a line of people and the exact numbers are the first thing to blur. So you write the numbers on a card that is handed along untouched, next to the story. That card is a <b>persistent facts block</b>: exact figures with date and source, kept out of the summary and given to synthesis as they are. <b>Rule: numbers that must survive go in a facts block, not in a summary.</b>", "options": ["Retelling each part once blurs less, but each retelling can still blur a number.", "Asking people to remember the numbers works most of the time, not every time.", "Correct. The numbers travel on a card nobody rewrites.", "Shortening each part makes it quicker to pass on, but the numbers still blur in the retelling."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary keeps the numbers</b>. Summarising is condensing, and exact figures, percentages and dates are what condensing turns vague. The fix is to keep them <b>outside</b> the summary: a persistent facts block passed on unchanged.", "diagram": "results ─▶ rolling summary (prose, may blur) ─┐\n        └▶ facts block (exact, as is) ──────┴▶ synthesis", "mapTitle": "Information is lost on the way to synthesis. Why?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Exact figures become vague words", "Progressive summarisation", "Persistent facts block, passed as is"], ["Middle items missing from long input", "Lost in the middle", "Key points first, clear section headers"], ["Context full of unused fields", "Verbose tool results", "Trim to the relevant fields"], ["Claims lose their sources", "No claim-source mapping", "Keep mappings through synthesis"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The coordinator keeps a rolling summary of them and rewrites it as each result arrives", "means": "Progressive summarisation: each rewrite can blur a figure. Summarising once (<b>[[0]]</b>) is still summarising."}, {"quote": "Audits show exact figures, such as a hazard ratio, turned into vague words", "means": "The decider. The loss is exact figures. Trimming fields (<b>[[3]]</b>) fixes size, not wording."}, {"quote": "The full results are too long to give synthesis in one input", "means": "Some condensing is needed, so the figures must be kept apart from it: <b>[[2]]</b>. An instruction (<b>[[1]]</b>) only usually holds."}], "rule": "Exact numbers, dates and sources go in a persistent facts block outside any rolling summary.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Extract transactional facts into a persistent \"case facts\" block"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-09", "was": "m6-s6-09", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Temporal differences vs conflicts", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "A submission can show different liability limits for the same policy: one on the certificate, another on an endorsement, another on a reissued certificate. The merge step keeps whichever figure its prompt finds most authoritative and drops the rest. Each document states the date it takes effect. A document issued later can take effect earlier. What should the merge step do <b>with these figures</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Mark the limit as disputed, store each with its source, and send it to a reviewer to choose.", "why": "Works, but over-escalates. Flagging with sources is right for a real conflict. These are values over time, and their dates order them.", "trap": "over-escalates"}, {"t": "Set a <code>conflict_detected</code> flag, keep the most recently issued figure, and log the rest.", "why": "Works, but wrong date. Issue order usually matches effect order, but a later document can take effect earlier.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run a second, independent extraction, and keep the figure both passes judge most authoritative.", "why": "Works, but ignores the dates. Agreement between passes makes the pick steadier, but it still drops the other values and never uses the dates.", "trap": "ignores the dates"}, {"t": "Keep each limit with its document and effective date; the latest is current, the rest history.", "why": "Best. Capturing dates with sources shows a sequence over time, not a contradiction, and keeps provenance for every value."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each document states the date it takes effect.", "explanation": "Keep claim-to-source mappings and capture dates so changes over time aren't misread as contradictions. Real conflicts get annotated with sources, not picked arbitrarily.", "eli5": {"question": "Several documents give different limits. The clue is: <i>each document states the date it takes effect</i>. They aren't arguing; they are a timeline.", "answer": "Think of a price tag that has been re-stickered twice. The three prices don't contradict each other; each was right from a certain date. Keep all three with their dates, and the newest by date is the price today. That is <b>capturing dates with sources</b>, so <b>changes over time aren't misread as conflicts</b>. Use the effective date, not the date it was issued. <b>Rule: dated values → order them by when they take effect and keep the history.</b>", "options": ["Close. Sending to a person fits a true clash, such as two limits in force at once. Dates settle this one.", "The newest document isn't always the one in force; use the effective date.", "Two passes agreeing still throws away the history and ignores the dates.", "Correct. Every value keeps its source, and the dates say which is current."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>different figures mean a conflict</b> that someone must settle. But each document <b>states when it takes effect</b>, so the figures form a sequence. Record each with its source and effective date; the latest effective one is current.", "mapTitle": "Sources disagree. Conflict or change over time?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "What it is", "Right handling"], "rows": [["Values with effective dates", "Change over time", "Keep all with dates; latest effective is current"], ["Two values in force at the same time", "Real conflict", "Annotate both with sources; escalate"], ["Values without dates", "Unknown", "Keep sources; flag for review"], ["One source cited for a value", "Provenance", "Claim-to-source mapping"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The merge step keeps whichever figure its prompt finds most authoritative and drops the rest", "means": "The cause: values and sources are thrown away. A second pass (<b>[[2]]</b>) still drops them."}, {"quote": "Each document states the date it takes effect", "means": "The decider. The values can be ordered, so a reviewer (<b>[[0]]</b>) isn't needed."}, {"quote": "A document issued later can take effect earlier", "means": "Issue order (<b>[[1]]</b>) is the wrong date. Use effective dates: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Capture dates with sources so changes over time aren't misread as contradictions.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Require publication/collection dates so temporal differences aren't misread as contradictions"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m6p-s6-10", "was": "m6-s6-10", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Validation before automating", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "The broker wants some fields to post without review from launch day. Compliance requires a measured error rate under 1% for any field that skips review, before it posts. A labelled set of past certificates exists. It mixes standard forms with a smaller share of carriers' own schedules. The model returns a confidence for each field. Which TWO together <b>meet this at launch</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Break labelled results out by document type and field; only pairs under the bar skip review.", "why": "Best. Aggregate accuracy can hide weak document types or fields; measuring each pair shows which can skip review."}, {"t": "Sample posted fields each week, stratified by document type, and track the error rate per type.", "why": "Works, but after launch. Stratified sampling is the right ongoing monitor, but it measures only what has already posted.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Fit each field's confidence cut-off on the labelled set, and send values below it to a reviewer.", "why": "Best. Confidence calibrated on labelled data routes individual doubtful values to review."}, {"t": "Check each certificate's dates and limits for internal consistency, and send failures to a reviewer.", "why": "Works, but measures nothing. Consistency checks catch some errors, but they give no error rate, and many fields have nothing to check against.", "trap": "measures nothing"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Compliance requires a measured error rate under 1% for any field that skips review, before it posts.", "explanation": "Aggregate accuracy masks weak segments. Before automating, validate by document type and field and calibrate field-level confidence on labelled data; after launch, monitor with stratified sampling.", "eli5": {"question": "They want to skip review safely from day one. The clue is: <i>a measured error rate … before it posts</i>. Only data you already have can measure that, and it must be measured per type of document.", "answer": "Think of a restaurant inspector. A good average score can hide one dirty kitchen. So you check each kitchen separately (<b>accuracy by document type and field</b>), and on the day you set a bar each dish must pass before it leaves (<b>calibrated confidence thresholds</b>). Weekly spot checks come later. <b>Rule: before automating, measure per type and field on labelled data, and calibrate field confidence.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Per-type, per-field numbers show which fields can safely skip review.", "Close. Weekly stratified sampling is the right check after launch, but it can't measure anything before the first post.", "Correct. Each doubtful value goes to a person; the rest post.", "Consistency checks catch some errors but give no error rate, and most fields can't be checked this way."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>one good accuracy number</b> or ongoing sampling is enough. But the rate must be measured <b>before the first post</b>, and an average over mostly standard forms can hide weak schedules. Use the labelled set: break it out by type and field, and calibrate per-field confidence to route doubtful values.", "mapTitle": "When can a field skip review?", "map": {"head": ["Question", "Mechanism", "When"], "rows": [["Which type-field pairs are accurate enough?", "Accuracy by document type and field on labelled data", "Before launch"], ["Which single values are doubtful?", "Confidence calibrated on labelled data", "Before launch, then each value"], ["Is quality holding up?", "Stratified sampling of posted output", "After launch"], ["Do values agree with each other?", "Consistency checks", "Any time; partial cover"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Compliance requires a measured error rate under 1% for any field that skips review, before it posts", "means": "The decider. It must be measured before launch; weekly sampling of posted output (<b>[[1]]</b>) comes too late."}, {"quote": "A labelled set of past certificates exists", "means": "The data to measure with is already here."}, {"quote": "It mixes standard forms with a smaller share of carriers' own schedules", "means": "An average can hide weak schedules: break it out (<b>[[0]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The model returns a confidence for each field", "means": "Calibrate it on the labelled set to route values: <b>[[2]]</b>. Consistency checks (<b>[[3]]</b>) give no rate."}], "rule": "Validate by document type and field, and calibrate field confidence on labelled data, before automating.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Validate accuracy by document type and field before automating"}}, "source": "Mock 6"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-04", "was": "s5q-s1-04", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Explicit escalation triggers", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Today the agent hands a chat to a person when a sentiment score on the customer's messages passes a threshold. A review of past chats found two kinds of mistake. Angry customers asking where their box is get passed on at once, though a tracking lookup would settle it. Polite customers who ask to speak to someone stay with the agent for several more turns. Which change <b>most improves</b> escalation?", "options": [{"t": "Escalate on clear triggers: a request for a person, a case policy doesn't cover, or no progress.", "why": "Best. Escalation should rest on these categories, and a request for a person is honoured at once. Mood is a poor guide to how hard a case is."}, {"t": "Resolve simple issues whatever the tone, and hand over only when a request for a person is repeated.", "why": "Works, but delays the request. It fixes the angry tracking questions, but a polite customer who asks for a person still has to ask twice.", "trap": "delays the request"}, {"t": "Retune the sentiment threshold on the reviewed chats and refresh it each month as reviews come in.", "why": "Works, but wrong signal. A tuned threshold trims some mistakes, but calm requests for a person and angry simple questions still fall on the wrong sides of any mood line.", "trap": "wrong signal"}, {"t": "Train a classifier on past chats labelled escalate or resolve and use it instead of the score.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A model trained on history may beat the score, but it is heavy machinery for a problem clear criteria solve, and it copies past inconsistency.", "trap": "overbuilt"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Polite customers who ask to speak to someone stay with the agent for several more turns.", "explanation": "Escalate on categorical triggers: an explicit request for a human (honoured at once), a policy gap, or inability to progress. Sentiment is an unreliable proxy for case complexity.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent passes chats to a person based on how upset the customer sounds. Shouty customers with easy questions get passed on, and calm ones who ask for a person don't. The clue: mood and difficulty are different things.", "answer": "A shop assistant who calls the manager whenever someone raises their voice gets it wrong both ways. The loud customer only wanted to know where their parcel was. The quiet one politely asked for the manager and was ignored. Better rules: call the manager when asked, when the rulebook doesn't cover it, or when you're stuck. Those are <b>explicit escalation triggers</b>. <b>Rule: escalate on what the case needs, not on how the customer sounds.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Clear reasons to call the manager, and “I want the manager” counts straight away.", "Helping the loud customer first is right, but making the quiet one ask twice is not.", "Tuning the volume meter still measures volume, not difficulty.", "A trained judge of past cases is a big build and learns past mistakes too."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the <b>threshold is just set wrong</b>. The mistakes go both ways, though: loud easy cases escalate, calm hard requests don't. No single mood line can fix that, because <b>sentiment does not measure complexity</b>. The guide lists the real triggers.", "mapTitle": "When should the agent hand over to a person?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right move", "Why"], "rows": [["Customer asks for a person", "Escalate at once", "Explicit demand is honoured"], ["Upset customer, simple issue", "Offer the fix first", "Tone isn't complexity"], ["Case outside policy or a policy gap", "Escalate", "The agent has no rule to apply"], ["Agent cannot make progress", "Escalate", "Stuck is a trigger"], ["Mood score or self-rated confidence", "Don't use as the trigger", "Unreliable proxies"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "when a sentiment score on the customer's messages passes a threshold", "means": "The trigger is mood. Retuning it (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps the wrong signal."}, {"quote": "Angry customers asking where their box is get passed on at once, though a tracking lookup would settle it", "means": "Mood says escalate; the case says resolve."}, {"quote": "Polite customers who ask to speak to someone stay with the agent for several more turns", "means": "The decider. An explicit request must be honoured at once. Making them repeat it (<b>[[1]]</b>) still delays them."}, {"quote": "Which change most improves escalation?", "means": "Replace the signal with clear triggers: <b>[[0]]</b>. A trained classifier (<b>[[3]]</b>) is more than the problem needs."}], "rule": "Escalate on a request for a person, a policy gap, or no progress. Not on mood.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Sentiment-based escalation and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-05", "was": "s5q-s1-05", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Ambiguity resolution", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "<code>get_customer</code> searches by the name the customer gives. For common names it often finds several accounts. It then returns the one with the latest box. Sometimes that is the wrong person, and a refund goes to a stranger's card. Most chats start in the help widget before the customer signs in. What should the agent do when a search <b>finds several accounts</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Use the account tied to the signed-in session, and fall back to the name search when there is none.", "why": "Works, but only some chats. A signed-in account beats any name match, but most chats start before sign-in, so most still fall back to the same guess.", "trap": "only some chats"}, {"t": "Take the top match only when its match score is high, and escalate the rest with the candidate list.", "why": "Works, but still guesses. Fewer wrong picks get through, but high-scoring guesses can still be wrong, and many easy chats go to a person.", "trap": "still guesses"}, {"t": "Show the agent every match and have it ask the customer for a box ID or postcode before acting.", "why": "Best. With several matches, the deciding fact is one only the customer knows. One question replaces a guess with a lookup."}, {"t": "Rank matches by recent boxes and city, then read the chosen name and city back to confirm.", "why": "Works, but can confirm the wrong one. Reading back catches some mistakes, but two people with a common name in the same city will both say yes.", "trap": "can confirm the wrong one"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Most chats start in the help widget before the customer signs in.", "explanation": "When a lookup returns several customer matches, ask the customer for an identifier instead of choosing one by heuristic.", "eli5": {"question": "A name search finds several people, and the agent picks one by guessing. Sometimes it picks the wrong one and refunds a stranger. The clue: most customers aren't signed in yet, so the agent has nothing but the name.", "answer": "At a busy pharmacy, three people called Priya Shah are waiting. The pharmacist doesn't hand the bag to whoever came most recently. They ask for a date of birth. That is <b>asking for an identifier</b> instead of picking by a rule of thumb. <b>Rule: several matches → ask the customer for something only they know.</b>", "options": ["Using the name on a loyalty card works for card holders, but most people at the counter don't show one.", "Handing over only when you're quite sure still guesses, and sends many easy cases to the manager.", "Correct. Ask for the date of birth before handing over the bag.", "Reading the name back doesn't help when two Priyas live on the same street."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better way to pick</b> will fix it: a score, a ranking, or a read-back. Each is still a guess. When several accounts match, the fact that decides it is one <b>only the customer knows</b>, and asking costs one turn.", "mapTitle": "A lookup is ambiguous. How do you pick the account?", "map": {"head": ["What you have", "Best move", "Why"], "rows": [["Several matches, customer not signed in", "Ask for a box ID or postcode", "Replaces a guess with a lookup"], ["Customer signed in to a verified account", "Use the session's account", "Identity already proven"], ["One clear match", "Use it", "Nothing to resolve"], ["Match score or ranking rule", "Don't pick by it", "Heuristic selection"], ["Refund before identity is verified", "Prerequisite gate", "Blocks money moves until verified"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "For common names it often finds several accounts", "means": "An ambiguous lookup: the classic case for asking."}, {"quote": "It then returns the one with the latest box", "means": "Heuristic selection. Scores (<b>[[1]]</b>) and rankings (<b>[[3]]</b>) are more of the same."}, {"quote": "a refund goes to a stranger's card", "means": "The cost of a wrong pick is high."}, {"quote": "Most chats start in the help widget before the customer signs in", "means": "The decider. A session account (<b>[[0]]</b>) helps only the few signed-in chats. Asking works for all: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Several matches → ask for an identifier. Never pick by heuristic.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Multiple customer matches → ask for identifiers (not heuristic selection)"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s1-06", "was": "s5q-s1-06", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Case facts across sessions", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 2, "question": "Many chats are follow-ups on a case the agent handled before. The agent gets the earlier chat as a short prose summary, placed after a long refund policy and before the new message. On follow-ups it quotes wrong refund amounts and repeats offers already made. A promised callback and a held replacement box exist only in the earlier chat. Which <b>TWO</b> changes together make follow-ups reliable?", "options": [{"t": "Replace the prose with a case record: customer ID, box ID, amount paid, offers made and open promises.", "why": "Best. The prose turned exact amounts and promises into vague phrases. A record of transactional facts carries the numbers and the open callback forward intact."}, {"t": "Have the agent call <code>lookup_order</code> at the start of each follow-up and rebuild the case from it.", "why": "Works, but misses chat-only facts. The order system gives the true amounts, but the callback and the held box were never recorded there.", "trap": "misses chat-only facts"}, {"t": "Resume the earlier chat's session on a follow-up and carry on with its full transcript and tool results.", "why": "Works, but brings back stale results. The facts are all there, but old tool results may be out of date, and a fresh start with a structured summary is more reliable.", "trap": "stale results"}, {"t": "Put the case record first in the context under its own heading, ahead of the policy and new message.", "why": "Best. The summary now sits in the middle, behind a long policy, where models read least reliably. Key facts belong at the start with a clear heading."}], "answer": [0, 3], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "A promised callback and a held replacement box exist only in the earlier chat.", "explanation": "Summaries blur numbers and promises, and facts buried mid-context get missed. Keep a structured case-facts block and place it at the start of the context under its own heading.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent forgets details from a customer's last chat. It gets a vague paragraph, tucked behind a long policy. The clue: some promises were made only in that earlier chat, and the paragraph is buried in the middle.", "answer": "A nurse handing over a shift doesn't say “the patient seemed fine”. They fill in a chart: drug, dose, time, what was promised. And the chart goes on the front of the folder, not page 40. Two fixes: <b>write the facts as a case record</b>, and <b>put it first</b> under its own heading. <b>Rule: exact facts in a structured block, at the top.</b>", "options": ["Correct. A chart with the exact numbers and open promises, not a vague note.", "The pharmacy record has the doses, but not the promise the last nurse made at the bedside.", "Reading the whole old shift log works, but some of it is out of date now.", "Correct. Put the chart on the front of the folder, not buried behind the policy pages."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the summary is just too short</b>. Two things go wrong. Prose <b>blurs exact facts</b> (amounts, promises) into vague phrases. And it sits in the <b>middle</b> of a long context, after a big policy, where models attend least. One change fixes the content; the other fixes the place.", "diagram": "now:    [long policy] [vague summary] [new message]\nbetter: [## Case facts: IDs, paid, offers, promises]\n        [policy] [new message]", "mapTitle": "Facts from an earlier chat get lost. Where is the leak?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Wrong amounts, vague promises", "Prose summary blurs numbers", "Structured case-facts record"], ["Facts present but ignored", "Buried in the middle of the context", "Put them first, with a heading"], ["Facts live in a system of record", "Not loaded", "Look them up with a tool"], ["Facts only in the old chat", "No record outside the chat", "Carry them in the case record"], ["Old session resumed", "Stale tool results", "Fresh start + structured summary"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent gets the earlier chat as a short prose summary", "means": "Prose loses exact numbers. Replace it with a record: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "placed after a long refund policy and before the new message", "means": "Lost in the middle. Move it to the start: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "it quotes wrong refund amounts and repeats offers already made", "means": "Exact transactional facts are missing."}, {"quote": "A promised callback and a held replacement box exist only in the earlier chat", "means": "The decider. <code>lookup_order</code> (<b>[[1]]</b>) can't find them. Resuming (<b>[[2]]</b>) finds them along with stale results."}], "rule": "Carry exact case facts in a structured block, and put it at the start under its own heading.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Extract transactional facts into a persistent \"case facts\" block; trim verbose tool outputs to relevant fields; place key summaries at the beginning + explicit section headers"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-07", "was": "s5q-s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Scratchpads & manifests", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "A codemod changes about 70 files and runs for over an hour. It has been cut off twice, once by a crash and once when the engineer needed the machine. Before restarting, the engineer hand-fixed some converted files and reverted one. Both times the run started over, as Claude could not tell which files were done. Which change makes the work resumable with the <b>least added complexity</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude keep a progress file of each target file and its status, read on every restart.", "why": "Best. A manifest on disk survives crashes and records progress outside the conversation. The engineer can note their own changes in it too."}, {"t": "Resume the interrupted session by name with <code>--resume</code> and have Claude carry on.", "why": "Works, but stale. Resuming suits a session where nothing changed since. The engineer edited and reverted files, so the old tool results describe a state that is gone.", "trap": "stale view"}, {"t": "Have Claude pause after every few files and print a summary for the engineer to keep by hand.", "why": "Works, but manual. The engineer becomes the progress record and must paste it back after each crash.", "trap": "manual record"}, {"t": "Split the work into runs of about ten files, each started fresh with its own list of files.", "why": "Works, but only shrinks the loss. A crash still loses part of a batch, and nothing records which files inside it were done.", "trap": "shrinks the loss"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Before restarting, the engineer hand-fixed some converted files and reverted one.", "explanation": "Long mechanical work needs durable state outside the conversation. A manifest file survives crashes and context limits; a resumed session restores what was said, not what is now on disk.", "eli5": {"question": "A long job keeps getting cut off and starts again from zero. The clue: between runs, the engineer changed some files by hand, so the old conversation no longer matches the disk.", "answer": "Painters tick off each room on a sheet taped to the door. If they go home, the sheet tells tomorrow's crew where to start, even if the owner repainted one room overnight. That sheet is a <b>manifest</b> (a progress file on disk). <b>Rule: long work that must survive crashes → keep a progress file on disk, not just a conversation.</b>", "options": ["Correct. The sheet on the door survives anything and can be updated.", "Replaying yesterday's chat works if nothing changed. Here the owner changed rooms overnight.", "Asking the painter to phone you after every few rooms works, but you become the sheet.", "Smaller jobs lose less, but you still don't know which rooms in a batch were done."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>resuming the old session restores progress</b>. It restores the conversation, including tool results from before the hand edits. A <b>manifest file on disk</b> records progress outside the conversation, survives a crash, and can be updated when files change.", "mapTitle": "Long work keeps getting cut off: how do you keep its place?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best mechanism", "Why"], "rows": [["Long mechanical work across many files", "Manifest file on disk", "Survives crashes and edits"], ["Short pause, nothing changed since", "<code>--resume</code> the named session", "Context is still true"], ["Files changed since the last session", "Fresh session with a structured summary", "Avoids stale tool results"], ["Context getting full mid-task", "<code>/compact</code>", "Space, not progress"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It has been cut off twice, once by a crash and once when the engineer needed the machine", "means": "Progress must live somewhere that survives a crash."}, {"quote": "Before restarting, the engineer hand-fixed some converted files and reverted one", "means": "The decider. The old session's tool results are stale, so <code>--resume</code> (<b>[[1]]</b>) carries a wrong picture."}, {"quote": "Claude could not tell which files were done", "means": "The missing piece is a record of status per file. Smaller batches (<b>[[3]]</b>) don't add one."}, {"quote": "Which change makes the work resumable with the least added complexity?", "means": "One file Claude updates and reads: <b>[[0]]</b>. Manual summaries (<b>[[2]]</b>) put the job on the engineer."}], "rule": "Long work that must survive interruptions → a manifest on disk that the next run reads.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Structured state persistence (manifests) for crash recovery"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s2-08", "was": "s5q-s2-08", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Context degradation signals", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer has spent three hours in one session tracing the payment handlers in <code>server/</code>. Claude now says things like “typically an Express service would…” instead of naming the handlers it read that morning. It has also contradicted decisions made earlier in the session. This afternoon's work changes those handlers and must follow those decisions exactly. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> now, then carry on in the same session from a condensed history of the day.", "why": "Works, but blurs detail. Compaction frees space and suits a healthy session that needs only the gist. This one needs exact names and decisions, and it is already drifting.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Send the rest of the handler reading to an Explore subagent that returns short summaries.", "why": "Works, but too late. A subagent keeps future reading out of the context, but this session has already degraded and still holds the blurred decisions.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Have Claude save its findings and decisions in a notes file, then open a new session on it.", "why": "Best. Generic answers and contradictions are signs of context degradation. A notes file keeps the specifics, and a fresh session reads them clearly."}, {"t": "Run <code>/clear</code>, then have the engineer retype the morning's key decisions from memory.", "why": "Works, but loses work. A clean context helps, but the engineer recalls only some of the decisions and none of the file details.", "trap": "loses the work"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Claude now says things like “typically an Express service would…” instead of naming the handlers it read that morning.", "explanation": "Answers that drift to typical patterns and contradict earlier findings signal context degradation. Persist the key findings in a scratchpad file and start a fresh session seeded with it.", "eli5": {"question": "After hours of work, Claude gives vague textbook answers and contradicts itself. The clue: it talks about “typical” services instead of the files it actually read. The session is not only full; it is drifting.", "answer": "After a very long meeting, people start repeating general ideas and forget what they agreed. You don't keep talking. You write the decisions down, take a break, and start the next meeting from that page. That page is a <b>scratchpad file</b>, and the next meeting is a fresh session. <b>Rule: vague or contradicting answers → write key findings to a file and start fresh from it.</b>", "options": ["A quick summary of the meeting saves time, but it blurs exact decisions, and the meeting is already confused.", "Sending someone else to read the rest helps from now on, but the confusion already in the room stays.", "Correct. Write it down, then start a clear meeting from the page.", "Starting over from what you remember loses the details that only the notes would keep."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a long session just needs compacting</b>. <code>/compact</code> fits a session that is full but still accurate. Here Claude names “typical patterns” instead of real files and contradicts itself: that is <b>context degradation</b>. The fix is to save the specifics to a file and continue in a fresh session that reads it.", "mapTitle": "Context trouble: what kind, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Vague “typical” answers, contradictions", "Context degradation", "Notes file, then a fresh session"], ["Nearly full, answers still specific", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Verbose reading will keep piling up", "Discovery in the main context", "Subagent returns summaries"], ["Work must survive a crash", "No saved state", "Manifest file"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "An engineer has spent three hours in one session tracing the payment handlers in server/", "means": "Long session, lots of material."}, {"quote": "instead of naming the handlers it read that morning", "means": "The decider. Generic answers are a degradation sign, not only a space problem."}, {"quote": "It has also contradicted decisions made earlier in the session", "means": "The session's own memory is unreliable, so condensing it (<b>[[0]]</b>) keeps the confusion."}, {"quote": "This afternoon's work changes those handlers and must follow those decisions exactly", "means": "Exact specifics must survive. Retyping from memory (<b>[[3]]</b>) loses them; a subagent (<b>[[1]]</b>) only helps future reading."}, {"quote": "What should the engineer do next?", "means": "Save, then start fresh: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Degraded session → scratchpad file with the specifics, then a fresh session. Healthy but full → /compact.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-05", "was": "s5q-s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Structured claim-source mapping", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 2, "question": "Spot checks find citations that name a real, relevant paper, but the cited section lacks the claim. Document analysis returns prose paragraphs that mention papers in passing. Synthesis merges and rewrites those paragraphs. The reporting subagent then picks a paper and section for each sentence by matching it against every paper in the run. Runs must not get longer. Which <b>TWO</b> changes together fix attribution?", "options": [{"t": "Have reporting re-open each cited paper and re-match any citation whose cited section lacks the claim.", "why": "Works, but slower. Bad matches get caught, but every run now re-reads its papers, and reporting is still guessing the link.", "trap": "slower"}, {"t": "Have document analysis return each finding as fields: claim, DOI, paper title and section.", "why": "Best. The link between claim and source is recorded where it is known, as data, instead of as names buried in prose."}, {"t": "Give synthesis examples of merged paragraphs that keep each paper's name beside its sentence.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Names stay nearer their sentences most of the time, but the mapping is still prose that the reporter must parse.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have synthesis pass reporting a claim-to-source list as data, one entry for each claim it keeps.", "why": "Best. The mapping survives the rewrite, so reporting reads the source for each claim instead of matching sentences by guesswork."}, {"t": "Have reporting match sentences against only the twenty top-ranked papers of each run.", "why": "Works, but hides the problem. Fewer candidates mean fewer wrong matches, but it is still matching, and claims from lower-ranked papers lose their source.", "trap": "hides the problem"}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The reporting subagent then picks a paper and section for each sentence by matching it against every paper in the run.", "explanation": "Source attribution survives only if claim-source mappings are kept as structured data from the analysis step through synthesis to reporting. Fixing one end leaves the other to rebuild the link by guesswork.", "eli5": {"question": "Citations point at the right paper but the wrong section. The clue: the link between claim and source is lost along the way, and reporting has to <i>guess</i> it back.", "answer": "Picture a parcel whose label is torn off at the first depot. The last depot guesses the address from what's inside. The fix is a label that stays on all the way. Here the label is a <b>claim-source mapping</b> kept as data: analysis records each claim with its DOI and section, and synthesis passes that list on to reporting. <b>Rule: keep claim-source links as structured data at every hand-off.</b>", "options": ["Checking every parcel at the last depot catches wrong addresses, but slows every run and still guesses.", "Correct. Print the address on the parcel at the first depot.", "Asking the middle depot to write addresses neatly helps, but it is still handwriting someone must read.", "Correct. Keep the label on through the middle depot and hand it to the last one.", "Guessing from fewer addresses makes fewer mistakes, but it is still a guess."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to fix attribution <b>at the end</b>, where the wrong citations appear. The link was lost earlier: findings arrive as prose and synthesis rewrites them. The exam guide says to keep <b>structured claim-source mappings</b> through synthesis. That needs both ends: structured findings going in, and the mapping passed on coming out.", "diagram": "analysis  → {claim, DOI, title, section}\nsynthesis → keeps list: claim → source\nreporting → cites from the list (no matching)", "mapTitle": "Citations are wrong. Where does the link break?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the link breaks", "Fix"], "rows": [["Findings arrive as prose with sources in passing", "At the analysis step", "Return claim and source as fields"], ["Synthesis rewrites and drops the links", "At the synthesis step", "Pass a claim-to-source list on as data"], ["Reporter matches sentences to sources", "Link rebuilt by guessing", "Read sources from the mapping"], ["A few wrong citations, time to spare", "Late check is acceptable", "Re-open and verify each citation"], ["Two sources give different figures", "Real conflict or time gap", "Attribute both, add dates"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Spot checks find citations that name a real, relevant paper, but the cited section lacks the claim", "means": "The link between claim and source is wrong, not the source itself."}, {"quote": "Document analysis returns prose paragraphs that mention papers in passing", "means": "The link is never recorded as data. Fix it here: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Synthesis merges and rewrites those paragraphs", "means": "Any link that survives analysis is lost here unless passed on as data: <b>[[3]]</b>. Examples (<b>[[2]]</b>) keep it as prose."}, {"quote": "The reporting subagent then picks a paper and section for each sentence by matching it against every paper in the run", "means": "The decider. Reporting rebuilds the link by guessing. A shorter list (<b>[[4]]</b>) still guesses."}, {"quote": "Runs must not get longer", "means": "Re-opening every cited paper (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds time to each run."}], "rule": "Keep claim-source mappings as structured data from analysis through synthesis to reporting.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Preserve structured claim-source mappings through synthesis"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s3-07", "was": "s5q-s3-07", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Temporal & conflicting sources", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "Synthesis flagged a conflict about surgical infections. One paper puts the infection rate at 4%, and another puts it at 9%. Both are reputable, and the committee could not tell which to trust. A reviewer later found the two papers studied patients a decade apart. Each source record holds a title, a DOI and a section, and nothing else. Which change <b>best handles</b> this kind of conflict in future summaries?", "options": [{"t": "Have synthesis show both figures side by side, each with its source, and mark the disagreement.", "why": "Works, but only half. Showing both with attribution is right for a real conflict, but readers still can't see that the figures cover different years.", "trap": "only half"}, {"t": "Have citation checking confirm each figure in its paper, and drop any it can't find word for word.", "why": "Works, but different problem. It catches figures the papers don't contain. Both figures here are real, so both pass.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Tell synthesis that when two reputable figures disagree, the more recently published paper wins.", "why": "Works, but picks blindly. Newer is often better, but the rule throws away a valid figure and hides the time difference.", "trap": "picks arbitrarily"}, {"t": "Record each source's publication date and study period, and have synthesis compare periods first.", "why": "Best. With dates on every source, synthesis can tell a time difference from a real conflict, and attribute both when periods match."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each source record holds a title, a DOI and a section, and nothing else.", "explanation": "Require publication and data-collection dates on sources so temporal differences are not read as contradictions. Genuine conflicts over the same period are shown with attribution, not resolved arbitrarily.", "eli5": {"question": "Two good papers give different infection rates. They looked at patients ten years apart, but nothing in the system records dates. The clue: the source record holds <i>nothing else</i> but title, DOI and section.", "answer": "Two weather reports disagree: one says snow, one says sun. If you see one is from January and one from July, there's no conflict. Without dates you can't tell. So add <b>publication date and study period</b> to every source record, and have synthesis compare periods before calling it a conflict. Real conflicts over the same period are then shown side by side. <b>Rule: date every source so a time gap isn't mistaken for a contradiction.</b>", "options": ["Showing both reports with their names is fair, but readers still don't see one is from winter.", "Checking each report says what it claims is good, but both do.", "“Trust the newest report” sometimes picks right, but it hides why they differ.", "Correct. Put a date on every report, then compare."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to treat two different figures as a <b>contradiction to resolve</b>: show both, verify both, or pick one. Often they measure <i>different periods</i>. The exam guide says to require publication and collection dates so time differences aren't misread as contradictions. Showing both with attribution is the rule for a real conflict over the same period.", "mapTitle": "Two sources disagree. What should synthesis do?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Figures differ; studies cover different years", "Time gap, not conflict", "Record dates, compare periods first"], ["Figures differ for the same period", "Real conflict", "Show both with attribution"], ["A figure isn't in the cited paper", "Bad extraction or citation", "Verify against the source"], ["Pressure to give one number", "Arbitrary choice", "Don't pick one; annotate the conflict"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "One paper puts the infection rate at 4%, and another puts it at 9%", "means": "Two real figures that look like a conflict."}, {"quote": "Both are reputable, and the committee could not tell which to trust", "means": "Both pass a check (<b>[[1]]</b>), and choosing one by rule (<b>[[2]]</b>) is arbitrary."}, {"quote": "A reviewer later found the two papers studied patients a decade apart", "means": "It is a time difference. Side-by-side figures alone (<b>[[0]]</b>) don't show that."}, {"quote": "Each source record holds a title, a DOI and a section, and nothing else", "means": "The decider. No dates, so synthesis can't compare periods. Add them: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Require publication and study-period dates on sources; compare periods before calling figures a conflict.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Require publication/collection dates so temporal differences aren't misread as contradictions"}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s5-10", "was": "s5q-s5-10", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Stratified sampling", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 1, "question": "Each week the team labels a random sample of review findings, and precision has held steady for weeks. The security team now wants findings rated critical to open tickets by themselves. Criticals are a small share of findings, so each weekly sample holds only a couple. The security lead will agree only after seeing a measured error rate for critical findings. What should the team do <b>first</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Label a sample drawn heavily from critical findings and measure precision for that rating alone.", "why": "Best. The decision is about one group, so that group's error rate is the number needed. A stratified sample gets enough criticals to measure it."}, {"t": "Calibrate each finding's confidence on labelled data and ticket only criticals above a threshold.", "why": "Works, but too early. Calibrated confidence is how you route items once automation runs, but it doesn't give the measured critical error rate asked for first.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Grow the random weekly sample several times over and report overall precision with a range.", "why": "Works, but wrong measure. A bigger random sample sharpens the overall figure, which is mostly non-critical findings, and needs far more labelling.", "trap": "wrong measure"}, {"t": "Use the developer dismissal rate on critical findings as their measured false-positive rate.", "why": "Works, but a proxy. Dismissals are a useful signal, but developers also dismiss correct findings they don't want to deal with.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The security lead will agree only after seeing a measured error rate for critical findings.", "explanation": "Aggregate accuracy can hide poor performance on a subgroup. Before automating one stratum, measure that stratum with a stratified sample; calibrated confidence comes later, for routing.", "eli5": {"question": "A rare kind of finding is about to trigger tickets automatically. The lead wants its error rate measured first. The clue: the random sample holds <i>only a couple</i> of those findings each week.", "answer": "If you want to know how good a school's top-set maths class is, testing random pupils from the whole school gives you two top-set pupils a week. Test mostly top-set pupils instead. That is a <b>stratified sample</b>: draw extra from the group you care about and measure it on its own. <b>Rule: before automating one group, measure that group's error rate directly.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Test the top set on its own.", "Grading pupils' confidence helps decide who to trust later, but first you need the class's real score.", "Testing more random pupils sharpens the school average, not the top-set score.", "Counting complaints is a rough signal; some pupils complain about correct marks."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a steady overall precision covers every kind of finding</b>. An overall number is mostly the common findings. A rare group can be much worse and stay hidden. The exam guide's answer is a <b>stratified sample</b> of the group you are about to automate.", "mapTitle": "We need an error rate. Which measurement?", "map": {"head": ["Question", "Method", "Note"], "rows": [["Overall precision", "Random sample", "Mostly common findings"], ["Error rate of one rare group", "Stratified sample of that group", "Needed before automating it"], ["Which items to route to humans", "Calibrated confidence threshold", "Once automation is running"], ["Cheap ongoing signal", "Dismissal rate", "A proxy, not a measurement"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each week the team labels a random sample of review findings, and precision has held steady for weeks", "means": "An overall figure, dominated by common findings."}, {"quote": "Criticals are a small share of findings, so each weekly sample holds only a couple", "means": "Too few to measure criticals. A bigger random sample (<b>[[2]]</b>) is a costly way to get a few more."}, {"quote": "The security lead will agree only after seeing a measured error rate for critical findings", "means": "The decider. Measure that group directly: <b>[[0]]</b>. Dismissals (<b>[[3]]</b>) are a proxy."}, {"quote": "What should the team do first?", "means": "Calibration and thresholds (<b>[[1]]</b>) come after the measurement."}], "rule": "Before automating one group, measure that group with a stratified sample. Overall precision can hide it.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Stratified random sampling measures high-confidence error rates; field-level confidence calibrated on labeled sets routes review."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m5p-s6-08", "was": "s5q-s6-08", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Position in long documents", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Property schedules list many insured locations across many pages. Each schedule goes to the model in one request. The request opens with the list of fields to extract, and each page starts with a page marker. Values for locations on the first and last pages come out right. Values for locations on the middle pages are often missed. Which change is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Extract each range of pages in its own request, then merge the results in a final step.", "why": "Best. Strong at both ends and weak in the middle is the lost-in-the-middle pattern. Separate requests put every location near the start or end of its own input."}, {"t": "Add a heading for each location, and repeat the field list again at the end of the request.", "why": "Works, but only partly. Headings and key items at the edges are the usual light fixes. The request already has a field list and page markers, and the middle still lags.", "trap": "only partly"}, {"t": "Ask the model to list every location first, then fill in the fields for each one.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A list of locations helps the model track them, but it still reads one long input, and the middle pages stay in the middle.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Run the extraction twice, and keep middle-page values only when both runs agree.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Both runs share the same weak spot, so they can miss or misread the same values.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The request opens with the list of fields to extract, and each page starts with a page marker.", "explanation": "Models handle the start and end of a long input most reliably and may under-use the middle. When structure is already in place, split the input so nothing sits in the middle.", "eli5": {"question": "Locations on the middle pages are missed far more often than those at the start or end. The clue is: <i>the request opens with the list of fields, and each page starts with a page marker</i>. The usual light fixes are already there.", "answer": "Read a long letter in one go, and you remember the opening and the ending best. Page dividers help a bit, but the middle still blurs. Read it in short parts, and there is no middle. That is <b>splitting the document</b>: one request per page range, then a merge. <b>Rule: if the middle still lags after headings, make the middle into its own start.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Each page range is the whole input of its own request.", "Headings and a field list are already there, and the middle still lags.", "A list of locations helps tracking, but the middle pages are still in the middle.", "Two runs share the same blind spot."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the middle is missed because the input lacks structure</b>. But the request already lists the fields first and marks every page. Strong ends and a weak middle is the <b>lost-in-the-middle</b> pattern. It is about position, so change the positions.", "mapTitle": "Middle of a long input under-used: what is already in place?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Unstructured text, middle fields missed", "Position and no structure", "Headings, key items first"], ["Headings already there, middle still lags", "Position itself", "Split: one request per part"], ["Document does not fit", "Capacity", "Chunk it"], ["Tool output buries key facts", "Too much context", "Trim output, keep a facts block"], ["Mixed document types in one prompt", "Instruction fit", "Classify and route"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each schedule goes to the model in one request", "means": "One long input, with the middle pages in the middle."}, {"quote": "The request opens with the list of fields to extract, and each page starts with a page marker", "means": "The decider. Headings and a field list (<b>[[1]]</b>) are already present."}, {"quote": "Values for locations on the first and last pages come out right", "means": "Strong at the ends."}, {"quote": "Values for locations on the middle pages are often missed", "means": "Weak in the middle. A second run (<b>[[3]]</b>) or a location list (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps the same long input. So <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Strong ends and a weak middle is a position problem. If headings are already there, split the input.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "models reliably process beginning/end but may omit middle sections."}}, "source": "Mock 5"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-05", "was": "s1-05", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Escalation calibration", "trap": "Disproportionate fix", "fam": 2, "select": 1, "question": "The agent often hands customers to a person when the person then settles the case with no exception. It also handles some cases that needed an exception. In those it promises remedies the company does not offer. The escalation guidance in its system prompt is one line: “Escalate when appropriate.” An engineer proposes having Claude rate its own confidence and escalating low scores. What is the <b>best next step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Write clear escalate-or-resolve criteria into the prompt, with a few contrasting example cases.", "why": "Best. The agent has no real definition of when to escalate. Explicit criteria with contrasting examples fix both kinds of mistake at low cost."}, {"t": "Have Claude rate its confidence each turn, and escalate cases below a cut-off tuned on past chats.", "why": "Works, but weak signal. A tuned cut-off moves the balance, but Claude's own confidence does not track whether a case needs an exception, so one error trades for the other.", "trap": "wrong signal"}, {"t": "Train a classifier on past escalation labels to route each case before the agent replies.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. A classifier is worth its cost once clear criteria are in place and still fall short. Here the guidance is one vague line, so the cheap fix comes first.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Add a sentiment check that escalates customers who sound frustrated, whatever else is going on.", "why": "Works, but wrong signal. Some upset customers do need a person, but mood does not track complexity: calm customers can need exceptions, angry ones often have simple problems.", "trap": "wrong signal"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The escalation guidance in its system prompt is one line: “Escalate when appropriate.”", "explanation": "Self-reported confidence and sentiment are unreliable proxies for case complexity. When the escalation boundary is unclear, define it with explicit criteria and contrasting examples first.", "eli5": {"question": "The agent escalates the wrong cases in both directions. Someone suggests a confidence score. The clue: the only guidance it has is “Escalate when appropriate.”", "answer": "Imagine a new shop assistant told only “call the manager when appropriate”. Sometimes they call for nothing, sometimes they promise things they can't. Asking them how sure they feel won't help. Give them a clear list of when to call and when not to, with a few real examples (<b>explicit criteria plus few-shot examples</b>). <b>Rule: unclear escalation → write the criteria first. Confidence and mood are poor signals.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Tell the assistant exactly when to call the manager, with examples.", "Asking the assistant how sure they feel moves the line, but their feeling doesn't match which cases need the manager.", "Hiring a second person to sort cases works, but costs far more than writing down the rule first. It wins only once the rule exists and still falls short.", "Calling the manager for every grumpy customer misses calm customers with tricky problems."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the agent needs a <b>number</b> to decide with, like a confidence score. But the agent's own sense of confidence does not track whether a case needs a policy exception. The real gap is that <b>nobody has told it where the line is</b>. One vague sentence is all it has.", "mapTitle": "Escalation is off. What fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Guidance is vague or missing", "Explicit criteria + contrasting examples", "Defines the boundary at low cost"], ["Clear criteria exist and still fail", "A trained classifier on labelled cases", "Worth its cost only after the cheap fix"], ["Customer asks for a person", "Escalate at once", "An explicit request is a trigger"], ["Routing by self-rated confidence", "Avoid", "Poorly matched to real complexity"], ["Routing by sentiment", "Avoid", "Mood is not complexity"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The agent often hands customers to a person when the person then settles the case with no exception", "means": "Over-escalation: the agent doesn't know which cases it can settle."}, {"quote": "It also handles some cases that needed an exception", "means": "Under-escalation too. Both errors point to an undefined boundary, not a missing score."}, {"quote": "The escalation guidance in its system prompt is one line: “Escalate when appropriate.”", "means": "The decider. The cheap, direct fix is to write the boundary down: <b>[[0]]</b>. A classifier (<b>[[2]]</b>) comes after that."}, {"quote": "An engineer proposes having Claude rate its own confidence and escalating low scores", "means": "Self-rated confidence (<b>[[1]]</b>) and sentiment (<b>[[3]]</b>) are unreliable signals of complexity."}], "rule": "When escalation is miscalibrated, write explicit criteria with contrasting examples first. Self-rated confidence and sentiment are poor proxies.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Sentiment-based escalation and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s1-06", "was": "s1-06", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Preserving critical facts", "trap": "More context or reasoning instead of structure", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "In long chats the agent starts to slip. It rounds refund amounts and asks again for box IDs the customer already gave. Each <code>get_customer</code> call returns over 40 fields, and the agent calls it several times per chat. The workflow uses only a handful of those fields. The slips begin while the context window is still under half full. Which change <b>best fixes this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move to a model with a larger context window, and keep the full history in every request.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. More room helps when history gets cut off, but the window is under half full. The facts are getting buried, not dropped.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Strip each <code>get_customer</code> result to the values the agent needs before it joins the history.", "why": "Best. Repeated 40-field results bury the few facts that matter. Trimming them removes the noise around the amounts and IDs."}, {"t": "Every ten turns, summarise the older turns and replace them in the history with that summary.", "why": "Works, but loses detail. A shorter history has less noise, but summaries tend to round amounts and drop IDs, which are the very slips seen here.", "trap": "loses detail"}, {"t": "Call <code>get_customer</code> again before each reply, so the agent works from fresh values.", "why": "Works, but adds noise. Fresh values sit near the end where Claude reads well, but each call adds another 40-field result, and it can't recover IDs the customer typed.", "trap": "adds noise"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Each get_customer call returns over 40 fields, and the agent calls it several times per chat.", "explanation": "Tool results pile up and swamp the context long before it is full. Trim verbose tool output to the fields the workflow uses, so key facts are not lost in the middle.", "eli5": {"question": "Late in long chats, the agent rounds amounts and forgets IDs. The clue: each customer lookup dumps over 40 fields, many times per chat, and the window isn't even half full.", "answer": "Imagine a desk where every phone call adds a 40-page printout, though you only need five lines from it. After a few calls, the important note is buried under paper. A bigger desk doesn't help. Cut each printout down to the five lines you need (<b>trim tool results to the relevant fields</b>). <b>Rule: when facts get lost before the window is full, cut the noise, not the history.</b>", "options": ["A bigger desk holds more paper, but the note is still buried under it.", "Correct. Keep only the lines you use from each printout.", "Writing a short summary clears the desk, but summaries round numbers and lose IDs.", "Fetching a fresh printout puts the numbers on top, but adds another 40 pages each time."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think the agent is <b>running out of room</b>. But the window is under half full. The problem is <b>noise</b>: big tool results repeated many times bury the few facts that matter in the middle of the context, where models read least reliably.", "mapTitle": "Facts get lost in a long chat. Why, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Slips while the window is far from full", "Verbose tool results bury key facts", "Trim results to the fields used"], ["Amounts and IDs lost after summarising", "Summary condensed the exact values", "A case-facts block kept outside the summary"], ["Early turns cut off at the limit", "History really is too long", "Bigger window, or a structured summary"], ["Facts in the middle overlooked", "Lost-in-the-middle effect", "Key facts at the top, with clear headers"], ["Stale values used", "Data changed since the lookup", "Fetch again before acting"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It rounds refund amounts and asks again for box IDs the customer already gave", "means": "Exact facts are being lost. Summaries (<b>[[2]]</b>) are known to round amounts and drop IDs."}, {"quote": "Each get_customer call returns over 40 fields, and the agent calls it several times per chat", "means": "The decider. Lots of repeated noise. Calling it more (<b>[[3]]</b>) adds more."}, {"quote": "The workflow uses only a handful of those fields", "means": "Most of each result can go: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The slips begin while the context window is still under half full", "means": "Not an overflow, so a bigger window (<b>[[0]]</b>) misses the cause."}], "rule": "Before the window is full, lost facts mean too much noise. Trim tool results to the fields you use.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Tool results accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately (40+ fields when 5 are relevant)."}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-07", "was": "s2-07", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Large-codebase exploration", "trap": "Wrong scope or wrong home", "fam": 8, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer has spent a long session exploring the payments code in <code>server/</code>. Claude now gives different answers about where requests are authenticated. It talks about “typical middleware patterns” instead of the file it named earlier. The exploration will continue over several more sessions this week. Two other engineers will pick up parts of it. What is the <b>best response</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Run <code>/compact</code> now and keep exploring in this session with the condensed history.", "why": "Works, but stays in one session. Compacting frees space today, but it carries nothing to later sessions or to the other engineers, and it can blur exact file names.", "trap": "stays in one session"}, {"t": "Have Claude re-read the authentication code end to end so the exact details are fresh again.", "why": "Works, but more of the same. The details return for a while, but more volume goes into an already degraded context, and nothing reaches later sessions.", "trap": "more of the same"}, {"t": "Resume this session tomorrow with <code>--resume</code> so the full history carries over.", "why": "Works, but carries the problem. Resuming keeps every earlier finding, but it brings back the same degraded context too.", "trap": "carries the problem"}, {"t": "Write confirmed findings to a scratchpad file, and give broad searches to subagents.", "why": "Best. A scratchpad file keeps findings across sessions and people, and subagents keep verbose search output out of the main context."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The exploration will continue over several more sessions this week.", "explanation": "Inconsistent answers and talk of “typical patterns” are signs of context degradation. Persist findings in a scratchpad file that later sessions and colleagues can read, and delegate verbose searches to subagents.", "eli5": {"question": "After a long session, Claude is getting vague and contradicting itself. The work goes on for days, and other people will join. The clue: the findings must survive <i>beyond this session</i>.", "answer": "After a long day of detective work, your memory of the morning gets fuzzy. The fix isn't to squeeze your memory harder; it's a case file. Write down what you have confirmed, so tomorrow-you and your colleagues start from it. Send helpers to do the messy searching and report back. That is a <b>scratchpad file</b> plus <b>subagents</b>. <b>Rule: degrading and multi-session → write findings down, delegate the noisy searching.</b>", "options": ["Tidying your desk helps today, but your colleagues can't read your desk tomorrow.", "Rereading everything piles more paper onto a desk that is already overflowing.", "Picking up tomorrow exactly where you left off brings back the same fog.", "Correct. A case file everyone can read, and helpers for the noisy searches."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full, confused session just needs compacting</b>. <code>/compact</code> frees space inside one session. Here the signs are <i>degradation</i> (inconsistent answers, “typical patterns” instead of the specific file), and the work spans sessions and people. The guide's answer is a scratchpad file for key findings plus subagents for verbose output.", "mapTitle": "Context trouble: what kind, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Vague answers; work spans sessions or people", "Degradation, no saved state", "Scratchpad file + subagents"], ["Nearly full, answers still specific, ends today", "Space only", "<code>/compact</code>"], ["Continue the same healthy session later", "Nothing wrong", "<code>--resume</code>"], ["Search output floods the context", "Verbose discovery", "Subagent returns a summary"], ["Work must survive a crash", "No saved state", "Manifest of progress"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Claude now gives different answers about where requests are authenticated", "means": "Inconsistent answers: a sign of degradation, not just a full context."}, {"quote": "It talks about “typical middleware patterns” instead of the file it named earlier", "means": "The textbook sign. Re-reading (<b>[[1]]</b>) adds volume; resuming (<b>[[2]]</b>) keeps the fog."}, {"quote": "The exploration will continue over several more sessions this week", "means": "The decider. <code>/compact</code> (<b>[[0]]</b>) helps only inside this session."}, {"quote": "Two other engineers will pick up parts of it", "means": "Findings must live in a file others can read: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Context degrading and work spans sessions or people → scratchpad file for findings, subagents for verbose search.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries; subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s2-08", "was": "s2-08", "sid": "s2", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Subagent isolation", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer needs every place the code opens a database connection outside the shared pool. A text search returns thousands of matching lines. Most come from generated clients spread over many folders with no common naming pattern. Once that output entered the conversation, Claude lost track of which sites in the refactor plan were already checked. What is the <b>best way</b> to run this search?", "options": [{"t": "Rerun the search with exclusion globs for the generated-client folders the team knows about.", "why": "Works, but only partly. Excludes cut the noise, but with no common naming pattern a hand-made list will miss some folders or drop real call sites.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Send the search output to a file, and have Claude read that file when it needs the results.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The main context stays clear until Claude reads the file, and then the same thousands of lines arrive.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Hand the search to an Explore subagent that reports only real matches by file and line.", "why": "Best. The subagent absorbs the noisy output in its own context and returns only what qualifies, so the main session keeps the plan."}, {"t": "Run the search as before, then use <code>/compact</code> once the results have been reviewed.", "why": "Works, but too late. Compacting shrinks the flood after it lands, and it may also condense the record of which sites were already checked.", "trap": "too late"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Once that output entered the conversation, Claude lost track of which sites in the refactor plan were already checked.", "explanation": "An Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output in its own context and returns a summary, which protects the main session's working state.", "eli5": {"question": "A search dumps thousands of lines into the chat, and Claude loses its place in the plan. The clue: the noise <i>entering the conversation</i> is what broke things.", "answer": "If you ask for every receipt that mentions coffee, you don't want the whole shoebox tipped onto your desk. You send an assistant to sort the shoebox in another room and bring back only the right receipts. That assistant is an <b>Explore subagent</b>: it reads the noisy output in its own context and returns the short list. <b>Rule: discovery that floods the context → Explore subagent returns a summary.</b>", "options": ["Hiding some shoeboxes helps, but you don't know where they all are.", "Putting the shoebox in the hall only delays the moment it lands on your desk.", "Correct. Someone else sorts the shoebox and hands you only what matters.", "Clearing the desk after the shoebox lands may sweep away your own notes too."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the fix is a smarter search</b>. Filters help when the noise sits in predictable places. Here it doesn't, and the damage came from the output entering the main conversation. An <b>Explore subagent</b> takes the noise in its own context and returns only a summary.", "diagram": "main session (refactor plan) ── ask ─▶ Explore subagent\n                                     grep → 1000s lines\n        ◀── 12 call sites by file:line ──", "mapTitle": "Noisy discovery. Where should the noise go?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Best fix", "Why"], "rows": [["Noise everywhere, main context must stay clean", "Explore subagent", "Isolates output, returns a summary"], ["Noise sits in a few known folders", "Exclusion globs", "Cheap and precise"], ["Session already full but still accurate", "<code>/compact</code>", "Frees space"], ["Findings must last beyond the session", "Scratchpad file", "Persists key facts"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "A text search returns thousands of matching lines", "means": "Far too much to read in the main context, now or later (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Most come from generated clients spread over many folders with no common naming pattern", "means": "A hand-made exclude list (<b>[[0]]</b>) will miss some."}, {"quote": "Once that output entered the conversation, Claude lost track of which sites in the refactor plan were already checked", "means": "The decider. Keep the output out of the main context; compacting afterwards (<b>[[3]]</b>) is too late."}, {"quote": "What is the best way to run this search?", "means": "Run it somewhere else and get a summary: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Discovery that would flood the main context → Explore subagent that returns a summary.", "guide": {"obj": "3.4 Plan mode vs direct execution", "quote": "Explore subagent isolates verbose discovery output, returns summaries"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-05", "was": "s3-05", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Provenance through synthesis", "trap": "Treating the symptom", "fam": 3, "select": 1, "question": "Each analysis summary keeps the paper, section and page in separate fields. The synthesis subagent turns the summaries into flowing prose. The reporting subagent then cites each sentence by finding the summary that reads most like it. A committee member found a dosing claim cited to a paper that never mentions dosing. Spot checks find more claims cited to the wrong paper. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the citation-check subagent compare every cited claim with its paper before release.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. A full check would catch wrong citations before they go out, but the pipeline would still guess them, and each one caught means rework.", "trap": "treats the symptom"}, {"t": "Have the reporting subagent match more strictly, and leave a sentence uncited when unsure.", "why": "Works, but only usually. Stricter matching swaps some wrong citations for missing ones, and every claim must be cited. It is still a guess made after the link was lost.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Have analysis add a short supporting quote to each finding, which makes the matching step easier.", "why": "Works, but still guessing. Quotes give the matcher more to go on, but citations are still rebuilt by resemblance instead of carried through.", "trap": "still guessing"}, {"t": "Have synthesis tag each claim with its source summary IDs, and build citations from them.", "why": "Best. The claim-to-source link is lost when synthesis writes prose. Carrying the link through synthesis removes the guesswork where it starts."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The reporting subagent then cites each sentence by finding the summary that reads most like it.", "explanation": "Source attribution is lost when synthesis compresses content without keeping claim-to-source mappings. Preserve those mappings through every stage instead of rebuilding them downstream by similarity.", "eli5": {"question": "Claims end up cited to the wrong papers. The summaries had the sources right. The clue: citations are added afterwards by guessing which summary a sentence looks like.", "answer": "A cook mixes ingredients from labelled jars into one soup, then tries to guess from the taste which jar each flavour came from. Better to write down which jars go into each spoonful as you cook. In the system, synthesis should keep a <b>claim-to-source mapping</b>: each claim carries the IDs of the summaries behind it. <b>Rule: carry the source link through each step; don't rebuild it afterwards.</b>", "options": ["A taster checking every bowl catches mistakes, but the kitchen is still guessing.", "Guessing more carefully means fewer wrong labels but more missing ones.", "Better tasting notes help the guess, but it's still a guess.", "Correct. Note the jars for each spoonful as you cook."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to <b>add a checker</b> when citations go wrong. But the summaries already hold the right sources. The link breaks at <b>synthesis</b>, which writes prose without saying which summary each claim came from. After that, the reporter can only guess by resemblance.", "diagram": "summaries (paper, section, page) ✓\n  synthesis → prose          ✗ link dropped\n  reporter  → cite by look-alike = guess\nfix: synthesis emits claim + [summary IDs]", "mapTitle": "Citations are wrong. Where does the link break?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Where the fault is", "Fix"], "rows": [["Sources right in summaries, wrong in the report", "Synthesis drops the claim-source link", "Claim plus source IDs through synthesis"], ["Summaries mix content with source details", "Analysis output format", "Separate structured fields"], ["Wrong citations reach readers this week", "No check before release", "Independent check before release"], ["Sources disagree", "Not an attribution fault", "Show both, with sources"], ["Numbers or dates blurred in summaries", "Lossy summarising", "Keep exact facts as fields"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each analysis summary keeps the paper, section and page in separate fields", "means": "Analysis already does its part; the link is lost later."}, {"quote": "The synthesis subagent turns the summaries into flowing prose", "means": "Prose with no tags is where the claim-to-source link disappears."}, {"quote": "The reporting subagent then cites each sentence by finding the summary that reads most like it", "means": "The decider. Citations are guesses. Stricter matching (<b>[[1]]</b>) or quotes (<b>[[2]]</b>) are still guesses; <b>[[3]]</b> removes the guess."}, {"quote": "Spot checks find more claims cited to the wrong paper", "means": "A wider problem, not a one-off. A release check (<b>[[0]]</b>) catches errors but leaves the cause."}], "rule": "Keep claim-to-source links through synthesis; never rebuild citations by similarity.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "Preserve structured claim-source mappings through synthesis"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s3-06", "was": "s3-06", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.6 Conflicting & temporal sources", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "In a summary on a blood-pressure drug, synthesis gave one figure for how often a side effect occurs. Two other good studies in the run gave lower figures. The committee found them only in raw subagent output. The analysis summaries record each figure and its paper, but not when the data were collected. The chair wants members, not the system, to weigh disputed figures. Which <b>TWO</b> changes best meet this?", "options": [{"t": "Show all the disagreeing figures side by side, each with its paper and a conflict note.", "why": "Best. Annotating conflicting figures with their sources, instead of picking one, puts the dispute in front of the members who are meant to weigh it."}, {"t": "When figures disagree, have the coordinator search for one more study before synthesis goes on.", "why": "Works, but wrong action. More evidence can help, but the system would still present one settled figure, and members would still not see the dispute.", "trap": "wrong action"}, {"t": "Carry each figure's data-collection date from analysis all the way through to the summary.", "why": "Best. Without dates, an older study and a newer one can look like a contradiction. Dates let members tell real disagreement from change over time."}, {"t": "Report the figure from the largest study as the headline, and list the other figures in a footnote.", "why": "Works, but still picks one. Members can find the others, but the system has chosen the headline figure, which is the choice the chair kept for members.", "trap": "still picks one"}], "answer": [0, 2], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "The chair wants members, not the system, to weigh disputed figures.", "explanation": "Annotate conflicting statistics with their sources instead of picking one, and carry publication or collection dates so differences over time aren't misread as contradictions. Both give readers what they need to judge for themselves.", "eli5": {"question": "Three studies gave different side-effect figures, and the summary showed only one. The clue: the chair wants the committee, not the system, to weigh them, and the summaries have no dates.", "answer": "Three thermometers give three readings. A good report lists all three readings and which thermometer gave each, and says when each was read: a morning reading and an afternoon reading can both be right. That is <b>showing conflicts with sources</b> plus <b>keeping collection dates</b>. <b>Rule: when sources disagree, show them all with sources and dates; let the reader decide.</b>", "options": ["Correct. List every reading with its thermometer, and say they disagree.", "Fetching a fourth thermometer can help, but the report would still show one answer.", "Correct. Note when each reading was taken.", "Headlining the biggest thermometer is still the report choosing for you."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the system should settle the dispute</b>, by more searching or by picking the strongest study. The chair has said the opposite: members weigh disputed figures. The system's job is to <b>show the conflict clearly</b>, with sources, and with the dates that tell real disagreement from change over time.", "mapTitle": "Sources disagree on a number. What should the summary do?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Right handling", "Why"], "rows": [["Readers must judge disputes themselves", "Show all figures with sources and a conflict note", "Puts the dispute in front of them"], ["Figures may come from different years", "Carry collection or publication dates", "Change over time isn't a contradiction"], ["The system must settle it, with no reader choice", "Search for more evidence first", "More data before deciding"], ["One figure chosen, others hidden or footnoted", "Avoid when readers decide", "The system has picked"], ["Model scores its own confidence", "Weak signal", "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Two other good studies in the run gave lower figures", "means": "A real conflict between credible sources, so it should be shown, not resolved silently."}, {"quote": "The committee found them only in raw subagent output", "means": "The summary hid the conflict. Side by side with sources: <b>[[0]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The analysis summaries record each figure and its paper, but not when the data were collected", "means": "Missing dates make old and new data look contradictory: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "The chair wants members, not the system, to weigh disputed figures", "means": "The decider. A footnote under one chosen figure (<b>[[3]]</b>) and a search to settle it (<b>[[1]]</b>) both let the system decide."}], "rule": "Disagreeing sources → show all, with sources and dates; don't pick one.", "guide": {"obj": "5.6 Information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis", "quote": "annotate conflicting statistics with source attribution (don't arbitrarily pick one)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s5-10", "was": "s5-10", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Confidence calibration", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "A reviewer labelled a sample of recent review findings, and overall precision came out at 94%. The platform team now wants findings to block merges. Most findings in the sample are about null safety. Concurrency findings are rare but tied to past production incidents, and only a handful were in the sample. Only the overall figure was computed. What is the <b>best next step</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Let findings block for a trial period, with an easy override, and track how often it is used.", "why": "Works, but too late. Override rates show some errors, but bad blocks hit real merges first, and rare categories may never show up.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Label a new sample with a quota per category, and let each block once its own precision is high.", "why": "Best. 94% is an average dominated by one common category. A quota per category measures each one, including the rare ones that matter most, before it can block."}, {"t": "Let only null-safety findings block for now, as they make up most of the well-scored sample.", "why": "Works, but not measured yet. It is a sensible start, but only the overall figure exists, so null safety's own precision is still unknown.", "trap": "not measured yet"}, {"t": "Let a finding block only when two independent review runs both report it.", "why": "Works, but measures agreement. Two runs agreeing cuts some noise, but agreement is not correctness, and it doubles the cost of a blocking check.", "trap": "wrong measure"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "Only the overall figure was computed.", "explanation": "Aggregate accuracy can hide weak performance in specific categories. Measure each category with stratified sampling before letting it drive an automatic decision such as blocking a merge.", "eli5": {"question": "The review scores 94% overall, and the team wants it to block merges. The clue: <i>only the overall figure</i> exists, and the rare, risky category barely appears in it.", "answer": "A school's average grade can look great while one class is failing. Before giving a class a privilege, you check that class's own grades. Here, label a new sample with a fixed number of findings from each category (<b>stratified sampling</b>), and let a category block merges only when its own precision is high. <b>Rule: an average can hide a weak category; measure each one before automating it.</b>", "options": ["Trying it on real students and counting complaints finds problems only after they hurt someone.", "Correct. Check each class's own grades before giving that class the privilege.", "The big class is probably fine, but nobody has actually checked its grades.", "Two teachers agreeing on a grade doesn't mean the grade is right."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>94% overall means the review is ready to automate</b>. An average is dominated by its biggest group. The rare concurrency category, the one tied to incidents, barely shows in it. The exam guide says to <b>validate accuracy by type before automating</b>, using stratified sampling.", "diagram": "overall 94%  = mostly null-safety findings\nconcurrency  = a handful → precision unknown", "mapTitle": "Is this automated decision safe to turn on?", "map": {"head": ["What you know", "Risk", "Next step"], "rows": [["Only an overall figure", "Weak categories hidden", "Stratified sample, measure each category"], ["Precision measured per category", "Some categories below bar", "Let only the strong ones block"], ["Model reports its own confidence", "Uncalibrated", "Calibrate on a labelled set first"], ["Categories measured and good", "Drift over time", "Keep sampling after launch"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "overall precision came out at 94%", "means": "A single average, not a per-category result."}, {"quote": "Concurrency findings are rare but tied to past production incidents, and only a handful were in the sample", "means": "The category that matters most is barely measured. A trial on live merges (<b>[[0]]</b>) may never see it."}, {"quote": "Only the overall figure was computed", "means": "The decider. Even null safety's own figure is unknown, so gating on it (<b>[[2]]</b>) is still a guess. Agreement between runs (<b>[[3]]</b>) is not precision."}, {"quote": "What is the best next step?", "means": "Measure each category first: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Before an automated decision, measure accuracy per category with a stratified sample; an average hides weak spots.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Validate accuracy by document type and field before automating"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-06", "was": "s6-06", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.5 Field-level calibration", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The team measured 97% field accuracy on 200 certificates labelled during a quiet month. Now they want to post results without review. That month held mostly standard certificates. Long policy schedules arrive mainly at renewal time, so only a handful were in the set. Insured names were almost never wrong, and most errors were in limits on those schedules. What should happen <b>before review is removed</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Keep a random sample of posted results under review each week, and stop once a quarter passes cleanly.", "why": "Works, but too late. Ongoing sampling is useful, but a random sample has the same blind spot, and it checks after posting rather than deciding what may post.", "trap": "too late"}, {"t": "Calibrate a confidence score per field on the labelled set, and route fields under a threshold to review.", "why": "Works, but too early. Calibrated field confidence is the right routing tool once the labelled set covers each type. A handful of schedules can't calibrate schedules.", "trap": "too early"}, {"t": "Label a new sample with a fixed quota per document type, and report accuracy for every type and field.", "why": "Best. The first sample barely covers schedules and averages easy fields with hard ones. A quota per type gives each type and field its own figure, which is what decides what can skip review."}, {"t": "Run a second extraction on long schedules, and post only the limits on which both runs agree.", "why": "Works, but measures the wrong thing. Agreement shows repeatability, not accuracy, and it changes the extraction instead of measuring it.", "trap": "different problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Long policy schedules arrive mainly at renewal time, so only a handful were in the set.", "explanation": "Aggregate accuracy can hide poor performance on specific document types and fields. Measure by type and field, with stratified sampling so rare types are covered, before automating; calibrated confidence comes after.", "eli5": {"question": "A test said 97% accurate, but it was taken in a quiet month with almost no long schedules, and those are where the errors are. The clue: <i>only a handful</i> of schedules were in the set.", "answer": "If you test a new driver only on quiet Sunday roads, a great score says little about rush hour. Test them on a fixed number of trips of each kind, and score each kind separately. That is <b>stratified sampling</b>, with accuracy reported <b>per document type and field</b>. <b>Rule: one big average can hide a weak spot. Measure each type and field before trusting automation.</b>", "options": ["Spot-checking some trips later helps, but uses the same quiet roads, and the driver is already alone.", "Asking the driver how confident they feel needs rush-hour practice first to mean anything.", "Correct. Test each kind of road a set number of times, and score each kind separately.", "Two drivers agreeing doesn't prove either one drove well."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>97% accuracy means the system is ready</b>. An average over a sample that barely contains the hard documents hides where errors live. Before removing review, measure accuracy <b>by document type and by field</b>, using a sample with a quota per type. Calibrated confidence routing comes after, once there is enough labelled data for each type.", "diagram": "random sample:  standard ████████████  schedules █\nstratified:     standard ██████  schedules ██████", "mapTitle": "Ready to remove review? What evidence do you need?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Next step", "Why"], "rows": [["High average, rare types barely sampled", "Stratified sample, report per type and field", "Average hides weak spots"], ["Each type well covered by labels", "Calibrate field confidence, route low ones", "Threshold now means something"], ["Automation already live", "Ongoing sampled review", "Catches drift over time"], ["Model rates its own confidence", "Don't route on it uncalibrated", "Self-confidence is unreliable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "The team measured 97% field accuracy on 200 certificates labelled during a quiet month", "means": "One average from one kind of month."}, {"quote": "Long policy schedules arrive mainly at renewal time, so only a handful were in the set", "means": "The decider. Schedules are barely measured, so calibrating on this set (<b>[[1]]</b>) or random checks (<b>[[0]]</b>) inherit the gap."}, {"quote": "Insured names were almost never wrong, and most errors were in limits on those schedules", "means": "Errors cluster by type and field, so they must be measured that way. Two runs agreeing (<b>[[3]]</b>) is not a measure of accuracy."}, {"quote": "What should happen before review is removed?", "means": "Measure each type and field first: <b>[[2]]</b>."}], "rule": "Before automating, validate accuracy per document type and field with a stratified sample. An overall figure can hide the weak spot.", "guide": {"obj": "5.5 Human review workflows and confidence calibration", "quote": "Validate accuracy by document type and field before automating"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "m4p-s6-07", "was": "s6-07", "sid": "s6", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Tool-result bloat & lost-in-the-middle", "trap": "Right idea, wrong layer", "fam": 4, "select": 2, "question": "Scanned policy schedules reach the model through an OCR tool. It returns over forty fields per text block, such as a box for every character. Extraction uses only each block's text and page number. A long schedule fills most of the context this way. The misses are rows from vehicle and location lists, and they cluster on the middle pages. Which <b>TWO</b> changes fit best?", "options": [{"t": "Switch to a model with a larger context window, so the full OCR output sits well inside the limit.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. More room helps a little, but the output already fits, and a bigger window makes a bigger middle.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Trim each OCR result to its text and page number before the tool result goes back to the model.", "why": "Best. Tool results use tokens far beyond their value. Two fields out of forty are needed, so trimming shrinks the context sharply and loses nothing."}, {"t": "Open the input with a summary of the key fields, and mark every page with a clear page heading.", "why": "Works, but carries too little. Key points up front with headings help a few facts survive, but dozens of rows per middle page can't fit in an opening summary.", "trap": "only key facts"}, {"t": "Extract rows a few pages at a time in separate passes, then merge the row lists in a final step.", "why": "Best. Misses in the middle of a long input are the “lost in the middle” effect. Short passes keep every page near the start or end of some input."}], "answer": [1, 3], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "The misses are rows from vehicle and location lists, and they cluster on the middle pages.", "explanation": "Two forces are at work: verbose tool results filling the context, and the middle of a long input being processed less reliably than its ends. Trim tool results to the fields used, and split the work so detailed rows never sit deep in the middle.", "eli5": {"question": "A bloated OCR output fills the context, and rows in the middle pages go missing. The clues: extraction needs only <i>two fields</i> out of forty, and the misses <i>cluster on the middle pages</i>.", "answer": "Reading a phone book where every name comes with forty lines of printing notes, you skim, and the middle pages blur. First strip each entry to the name and page. Then read a few pages at a time and combine your lists. That is <b>trimming tool results</b> plus <b>splitting into passes</b>. <b>Rule: cut tool output to what's used, and don't leave detail buried in the middle.</b>", "options": ["A bigger table to spread the phone book on doesn't stop the middle pages blurring.", "Correct. Strip each entry to what you need before reading.", "A cover note helps you remember a few facts, not dozens of rows per page.", "Correct. Read a few pages at a time, then combine the lists."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a full context means the model needs more room</b>. Two separate things are wrong. Tool output is mostly fields nobody uses, and long input loses detail in its middle (“lost in the middle”). Trim the output, and split the reading so no page sits deep in the middle.", "diagram": "OCR block: 40+ fields → keep text, page\nschedule: pp 1-3 | pp 4-6 | ... → merge rows", "mapTitle": "Long input, missed details. What is the cause?", "map": {"head": ["What you observe", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Context full of fields never used", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the fields used"], ["Middle detail missed, many rows", "Lost in the middle", "Short passes, then merge"], ["A few key facts lost in the middle", "Lost in the middle", "Key summary first, clear headings"], ["Input truly too large to fit", "Size", "Split into passes"], ["Numbers blurred after summarising", "Lossy summary", "Keep a facts block"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "It returns over forty fields per text block, such as a box for every character", "means": "Bloated tool results."}, {"quote": "Extraction uses only each block's text and page number", "means": "Two fields are enough, so trim the rest: <b>[[1]]</b>."}, {"quote": "A long schedule fills most of the context this way", "means": "The output already fits, so a bigger window (<b>[[0]]</b>) adds room, not attention."}, {"quote": "The misses are rows from vehicle and location lists, and they cluster on the middle pages", "means": "The decider. Lost in the middle, with many rows. A summary up front (<b>[[2]]</b>) can't carry them; short passes can: <b>[[3]]</b>."}], "rule": "Trim tool results to the fields used, and split long detailed input into passes so nothing sits deep in the middle.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Tool results accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately (40+ fields when 5 are relevant)"}}, "source": "Mock 4"}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-1", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Trim verbose tool results", "trap": "More room instead of less noise", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Customers with several boxes often ask about each one in turn. Each <code>lookup_order</code> result holds about forty fields, such as packing station, nutrition data and courier scans. The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price. In these chats the context fills early, and older turns are summarised after a few lookups. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Move the agent to a model with a larger context window, so summaries start much later.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The window still fills with fields nobody reads, just later, and every turn re-sends all of them.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Keep a case-facts block with each box's items, dates and refund amounts outside the summary.", "why": "Works, but treats the symptom. The facts survive the summaries, but the window still fills with unused fields and the summaries keep coming.", "trap": "different problem"}, {"t": "Cut each order lookup down to the few values the agent needs before Claude reads it.", "why": "Best. It removes the cause: about forty fields shrink to the few the agent needs, so the context stops filling early."}, {"t": "Ask the summariser for a longer, more detailed summary that keeps every order field.", "why": "Works, but makes room tighter. A longer summary keeps more, but it takes space the order fields already crowd, so passes still come early.", "trap": "delays the problem"}], "answer": [2], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price.", "explanation": "Tool results pile up in the context and use tokens out of all proportion to what the agent needs. Trim them to the relevant fields before they accumulate.", "eli5": {"question": "Every order lookup brings back a huge record, and the chat runs out of room. The clue: the agent only ever uses four of the fields.", "answer": "If a nurse stapled every lab printout into the handover folder, the folder would be too thick to read. Copy the four values that matter instead. <b>Trimming tool results</b> is that copy. <b>Rule: if a tool returns far more than the agent uses, cut it down before it enters the context.</b>", "options": ["A bigger folder holds more printouts. It is still full of pages nobody reads.", "Writing the key facts on a chart keeps them safe, but the folder is still overflowing.", "Correct. Copy the values that matter; leave the printout out.", "A longer retelling takes even more room in a folder that is already full."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>the window is too small</b>. Here the window is full of things nobody uses. The guide's line: tool results <b>accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately</b>. Fix what goes in, not how much fits.", "diagram": "lookup_order → ~40 fields → [trim to 4] → context\nbefore: window full after a few lookups\nafter:  window stays low, no summary pass", "mapTitle": "Where is the context going, and what fixes it?", "map": {"head": ["What you see", "Cause", "Fix"], "rows": [["Window fills after a few tool calls", "Verbose tool results", "Trim results to the relevant fields"], ["Amounts and promises drift after a summary", "Facts retold by each summary", "Case-facts block outside the summary"], ["One long input; middle facts missed", "Lost in the middle", "Key facts first + section headers"], ["Claude Code session full of old output", "Old output still in the window", "/compact, plus a scratchpad file"], ["Wish the window were bigger", "Usually one of the above", "Find which one first"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Each lookup_order result holds about forty fields", "means": "The weight is in the tool results, not the chat."}, {"quote": "The agent uses only the items, the delivery date, the status and the price", "means": "The decider: most of each result is unused. Cut it: <b>[[2]]</b>."}, {"quote": "older turns are summarised after a few lookups", "means": "A bigger window (<b>[[0]]</b>) or longer summary (<b>[[3]]</b>) leaves the unused fields in place. A facts block (<b>[[1]]</b>) protects facts but not the room."}], "rule": "Window filling with tool output? Trim the results to the fields the agent uses.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Tool results accumulate and consume tokens disproportionately (40+ fields when 5 are relevant)"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-2", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.1 Facts block across progressive summarisation", "trap": "Better retelling instead of a facts block", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "Long evidence runs pass through many search and analysis steps. To stay inside the context limit, the coordinator replaces older steps with a running summary. Committees now read lines such as “a large trial showed a modest benefit”. The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section. Those details are gone by the time the report is written. Which change <b>best fixes</b> this?", "options": [{"t": "Tell the summariser to copy every number, date and citation exactly as it appears.", "why": "Works, but only usually. A clear instruction cuts the slips, but the summary is still a model rewriting the facts, pass after pass.", "trap": "only usually works"}, {"t": "Give the coordinator a model with a larger window so it can skip the running summary.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. Longer runs still hit the limit, and until then the findings sit in the middle of a very long context.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have the report subagent rerun the analysis for each paper it cites in the summary.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It recovers the numbers by doing the analysis twice, at the cost of a second pass over every paper.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Keep each finding's numbers, dates and source in a block passed on unsummarised.", "why": "Best. The exact figures live outside the running summary, so no pass can blur them, and the report reads them as they were."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section.", "explanation": "Progressive summarisation condenses numbers, dates and sources into vague text. Keep transactional facts in a persistent structured block that is carried forward unchanged.", "eli5": {"question": "The numbers were right after analysis, then turned into “a large trial” after a few summaries. The clue: the exact details existed and were lost on the way.", "answer": "Retelling a patient's dose each shift turns “40 mg at 6 pm” into “some pain relief”. Writing it on the chart keeps it exact. A <b>facts block</b> passed on unsummarised is the chart. <b>Rule: facts that must stay exact go somewhere no summary rewrites.</b>", "options": ["Asking each nurse to retell carefully helps, but it is still a retelling.", "A longer shift means fewer handovers, not none.", "Re-examining the patient finds the numbers again, but that's a lot of work to redo.", "Correct. Write it on the chart; nobody retells the chart."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a better summary keeps the numbers</b>. Each pass is still a model compressing text, and numbers, dates and sources are the first to go. Put them where no summary touches them.", "diagram": "analysis → finding {n, effect, date, paper §}\n  ├─ running summary: rewritten each pass\n  └─ facts block: copied forward unchanged → report", "mapTitle": "Which part of the context keeps a fact exact?", "map": {"head": ["Where the fact lives", "What happens to it", "Use it for"], "rows": [["Running summary", "Rewritten on every pass", "The story of the run"], ["Facts block outside the summary", "Copied forward word for word", "Numbers, dates, sources, promises"], ["Recent turns", "Exact until the next pass", "Current step only"], ["Middle of a very long context", "Easy to miss", "Nothing you need exact"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the coordinator replaces older steps with a running summary", "means": "Every fact in the history gets retold, again and again."}, {"quote": "The analysis results gave the exact patient count, the effect size and the section", "means": "The decider: the details existed. They are lost in the retelling, so give them a home outside it: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Those details are gone by the time the report is written", "means": "A firmer instruction (<b>[[0]]</b>) lowers the loss rate; a bigger window (<b>[[1]]</b>) postpones it; redoing the analysis (<b>[[2]]</b>) is costly."}], "rule": "Numbers blurring after summaries? Keep them in a facts block the summary never rewrites.", "guide": {"obj": "5.1 Preserve critical information across long interactions", "quote": "Progressive summarization risks condensing numbers, %, dates, customer expectations into vague summaries"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-context-meter-3", "sid": "s4", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.4 Scratchpad and /compact in long exploration", "trap": "More room instead of saved findings", "fam": 5, "select": 1, "question": "An engineer has spent a long Claude Code session tracing how lessons are priced. Early on, Claude named the exact classes that apply discounts and taxes. Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other. The context is nearly full of earlier output. Several pricing questions are still left. What should the engineer <b>do next</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have Claude save its key findings to a notes file, then /compact the session.", "why": "Best. The class names and decisions are kept exact in a file, and /compact clears the old output that is crowding them."}, {"t": "Switch the session to a model with a larger context window and keep asking.", "why": "Works, but delays the problem. The early findings stay buried in the middle of a longer session, and the answers keep drifting.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Start a fresh session and ask Claude to explore the pricing code from scratch.", "why": "Works, but throws away work. The early findings were right; a fresh start pays to rediscover them and may drift again later.", "trap": "throws away work"}, {"t": "Add a CLAUDE.md line asking Claude to name the specific classes in each answer.", "why": "Works, but only usually. The instruction asks for names, but the names it found early are what is getting lost.", "trap": "only usually works"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 1, "decider": "Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other.", "explanation": "Inconsistent answers and talk of typical patterns are signs of context degradation. Persist key findings in a scratchpad file and reduce context use with /compact.", "eli5": {"question": "Claude found the exact classes early, and now speaks vaguely and contradicts itself. The clue: the session is nearly full of old output.", "answer": "When a long meeting runs on, write the decisions on the whiteboard, then clear the table of old papers. A <b>notes file</b> is the whiteboard; <b>/compact</b> clears the table. <b>Rule: save what you found, then make room.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Write the findings down, then clear the clutter.", "A bigger table holds more papers; the decisions are still buried among them.", "Starting the meeting again repeats all the work done so far.", "A rule to quote names helps only if the names are still in front of Claude."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>Claude has forgotten because the window is too small</b>. Vague, contradictory answers are context degradation: early findings are buried under later output. The guide's fix is structure: persist findings in a file, then compact.", "diagram": "early findings → buried under test output\n  → write notes file → /compact → answers cite notes", "mapTitle": "A long Claude Code session is degrading. What fits?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Move", "Why"], "rows": [["Key findings must survive a reset", "Scratchpad file", "Persists across boundaries"], ["Window full of old output", "/compact", "Reduces context usage"], ["Exploration will produce lots of output", "Delegate to a subagent", "Isolates verbose output"], ["Long task may crash midway", "State manifest", "Resume where it stopped"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "Early on, Claude named the exact classes that apply discounts and taxes", "means": "The findings exist; they are worth keeping, not rediscovering (<b>[[2]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "Now its answers mention “the usual service objects” instead, and two recent answers contradict each other", "means": "The decider: classic context degradation. More room (<b>[[1]]</b>) or a rule (<b>[[3]]</b>) does not bring the buried findings back."}, {"quote": "The context is nearly full of earlier output", "means": "Save the findings, then compact: <b>[[0]]</b>."}], "rule": "Answers turning vague in a long session? Save findings to a scratchpad file, then /compact.", "guide": {"obj": "5.4 Manage context in large codebase exploration", "quote": "Scratchpad files persist key findings across boundaries; subagent delegation isolates verbose output"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-1", "sid": "s3", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Report access failures with partial results", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The document-analysis subagent fetches each paper's full text through the journals' MCP server. Some papers sit behind paywalls the hospital does not subscribe to, and the server refuses them. The subagent analyses the rest and returns only those findings. Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists. How should the subagent <b>report this</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Return its findings plus a structured error naming each refused paper and the reason.", "why": "Best. The coordinator gets the work that succeeded and knows exactly what is missing and why, so it can ask the library, try another source or publish with the gap stated."}, {"t": "Retry refused papers with backoff, then return the findings and “some papers unavailable”.", "why": "Works, but it hides the detail. A paywall refusal won't change on retry, and “some papers” doesn't say which ones or why.", "trap": "hides the context"}, {"t": "Stop with an error until every paper can be fetched, and return no findings before then.", "why": "Works, but it throws away good work. One refused paper shouldn't end a run whose other analyses are finished and useful.", "trap": "ends the run"}, {"t": "Return its findings, and add a line to each summary that some sources may be missing.", "why": "Works, but vaguely. The committee is warned, yet nobody knows which papers are missing, so nobody can fetch them or judge how much they matter.", "trap": "loses which ones"}], "answer": [0], "runnerUp": 3, "decider": "Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists.", "explanation": "Report failures with structured context: what failed, why, what was tried and the partial results. Silently returning only the successes makes a gap look like a finding.", "eli5": {"question": "Some papers couldn't be opened, and the report pretends they don't exist. The clue: the summaries read as if there's no other evidence.", "answer": "If an assistant can't get into three of the files, you want to hear “here are 17, and these 3 were locked”, not just the 17. Then you can ask for a key. <b>Report what worked and exactly what didn't, with the reason.</b> <b>Rule: never let a failure look like an empty result.</b>", "options": ["Correct. Here's what I have, and here's exactly what's locked.", "Knocking on a locked door again won't open it, and “some files” doesn't say which.", "Refusing to hand over anything because three files were locked wastes the 17.", "“Some may be missing” worries people without telling them what to fetch."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>returning only what worked is the clean, safe option</b>. But silence about a failure reads as “nothing else exists”. The coordinator can only recover from a failure it can see: what failed, why, and what is already done.", "diagram": "server refuses paywalled papers\ntoday:  findings only -> gap looks like no evidence\nbetter: findings + refused list + reason\n        -> coordinator decides", "mapTitle": "How the failure reaches the coordinator", "map": {"head": ["Report", "What the coordinator knows", "Outcome"], "rows": [["Findings only", "Thinks the set is complete", "Gap reads as “no evidence”"], ["Findings + “some missing”", "Something is missing, not what", "Can't fetch or weigh the gap"], ["Exception, run ends", "Nothing; finished work lost", "Committee gets nothing"], ["Findings + structured failure", "Which papers, why, no retry", "Ask library, other source, or state the gap"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "the server refuses them", "means": "An access failure, not an empty result. A paywall refusal won't change on retry (<b>[[1]]</b>)."}, {"quote": "The subagent analyses the rest and returns only those findings", "means": "The failure is dropped on the way up. Stopping the run (<b>[[2]]</b>) would swing too far the other way."}, {"quote": "Summaries then read as if no other evidence exists", "means": "The decider: lost information has turned into a false impression. A vague warning (<b>[[3]]</b>) doesn't say what to fetch."}, {"quote": "every claim must cite a paper and section", "means": "The committee needs to know which papers were never read, by name."}], "rule": "Report a failure with its type, what was tried and the partial results. Silence reads as “no evidence”.", "guide": {"obj": "5.3 Error propagation across multi-agent systems", "quote": "Structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, alternatives) enables coordinator recovery."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-tool-failure-3", "sid": "s5", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.3 Local recovery, propagate only unresolved errors", "trap": "Losing or fabricating information", "fam": 7, "select": 1, "question": "The weekly dependency-risk report runs one subagent per module. Each subagent looks up every package in an advisory database over MCP. A few lookups per run time out, and they succeed on a second try seconds later. Today each timeout goes straight to the coordinator, which restarts that module's scan from the start. Runs now take most of the night. What is the <b>best fix</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the coordinator wait a minute before it restarts a module after a timeout.", "why": "Works, but it still throws away finished lookups. Each restart redoes the whole module for one failed package.", "trap": "redoes finished work"}, {"t": "Have the subagent retry blips itself and pass up only lookups that still fail.", "why": "Best. Short blips are fixed where they happen, finished lookups are kept, and the coordinator hears only about failures it has to decide on."}, {"t": "Raise the advisory client's timeout to give slow lookups longer to finish.", "why": "Works, but it hides the problem. Fewer timeouts, yet a hung call now holds the run for longer, and any timeout that remains still restarts the module.", "trap": "delays the problem"}, {"t": "Have the subagent skip timed-out packages and list them in the report as not checked.", "why": "Works, but it gives up too soon. A lookup that would succeed seconds later is left as a gap in the report every week.", "trap": "gives up too soon"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "they succeed on a second try seconds later", "explanation": "Recover locally from transient errors in the subagent, and propagate only unresolved errors, with the partial results, to the coordinator.", "eli5": {"question": "A few lookups hiccup and work on a second try, but each hiccup restarts a whole module. The clue: a second try seconds later works.", "answer": "If one page of a 40-page print job jams, the printer reprints that page, not the whole document, and only calls you if it keeps jamming. A subagent should do the same: <b>retry the blip itself</b>, keep what's done, and <b>report up only what is still broken</b>. <b>Rule: recover locally, propagate only unresolved errors.</b>", "options": ["Waiting before reprinting the whole document still reprints the whole document.", "Correct. Reprint the jammed page; call for help only if it keeps jamming.", "A longer wait for each page means a stuck page holds everything up longer.", "Leaving the jammed page out works, but the document has a hole every week."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>every error should go straight to the coordinator, so nothing is hidden</b>. But a blip that a quick retry fixes isn't a decision for the coordinator. Handle transient errors locally; send up only what is still unresolved, together with the partial results.", "diagram": "timeout -> subagent retries (seconds) -> ok\nstill failing -> error + results so far\n              -> coordinator", "mapTitle": "Who handles which failure", "map": {"head": ["Failure", "Who handles it", "How"], "rows": [["Brief timeout, fine on retry", "The subagent", "Retry locally, keep the results"], ["Still failing after retries", "The coordinator", "Structured error + partial results"], ["Business or permission refusal", "The coordinator", "Not retryable; report the reason"], ["Bad input to the tool", "The subagent", "Fix the input and call again"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "they succeed on a second try seconds later", "means": "The decider: these are transient blips that a local retry fixes. Skipping them (<b>[[3]]</b>) gives up on lookups that would work."}, {"quote": "Today each timeout goes straight to the coordinator, which restarts that module's scan from the start", "means": "Finished lookups are thrown away. Waiting first (<b>[[0]]</b>) still restarts the module."}, {"quote": "Runs now take most of the night", "means": "The cost of handling every blip at the top. A longer timeout (<b>[[2]]</b>) trades restarts for longer waits."}, {"quote": "What is the best fix?", "means": "Retry in the subagent, propagate only what is still failing: <b>[[1]]</b>."}], "rule": "Recover from blips inside the subagent; send up only unresolved errors, with partial results.", "guide": {"obj": "2.2 Implement structured error responses for MCP tools", "quote": "local recovery in subagents, propagate only unresolved errors with partial results"}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-1", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Calibrating when to escalate", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "The agent's prompt says to “escalate when needed.” Upset customers with simple problems, such as one missing item, are often handed to a person. Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone. In some of those chats it promises refunds that policy does not allow. Which change best improves <b>when the agent escalates</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Have the agent rate its confidence in each case and escalate when the rating is low.", "why": "Works, but an unreliable signal. The agent is often most sure of itself on exactly the cases it gets wrong, such as the calm policy exceptions.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "Train a separate classifier on past tickets to decide which chats go to a person.", "why": "Works, but overbuilt. It needs labelled history and retraining, and it learns past habits, including the bad ones described here.", "trap": "overbuilt"}, {"t": "Escalate when a sentiment check finds the customer upset, and let the agent handle the rest.", "why": "Works, but the wrong signal. Mood is not complexity: it sends more simple cases to people and still misses the calm exceptions.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "State escalation criteria in the prompt, with examples of escalate versus resolve.", "why": "Best. The problem is an unclear boundary. Stating the triggers and showing a few cases on each side fixes that directly, at prompt cost."}], "answer": [3], "runnerUp": 0, "decider": "Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone.", "explanation": "When escalation is miscalibrated, define explicit escalation criteria and show few-shot examples of escalate versus resolve. Sentiment and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity.", "eli5": {"question": "A new shop assistant calls the manager whenever a customer frowns, but quietly bends the rules for polite customers.", "answer": "Don't hand them a mood meter or ask how sure they feel. Tell them <b>when to call the manager</b> (“anything the refund policy doesn't cover; anyone who asks for the manager”) and walk through a few real cases. <b>Rule: wrong escalations → clear criteria plus a few examples, not mood or self-confidence.</b>", "options": ["Asking “how sure are you?” fails: they feel surest when they're bending the rules.", "Studying years of old cases works, but it's a big job and copies old mistakes.", "A mood meter calls the manager for frowns, not for hard cases.", "Correct. Say when to call the manager, and show a few cases."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>escalation needs a signal the model can't fake</b>: a mood score, a confidence number, a trained classifier. The real problem is that nobody told the agent where the line is. Upset is not the same as complex, and the model's own confidence is poorly calibrated.", "diagram": "\"escalate when needed\" → mood decides → wrong cases\ncriteria + examples:\n  policy gap? asked for a person? → escalate\n  simple, even if upset → resolve", "mapTitle": "Which signal tells you to escalate?", "map": {"head": ["Signal", "Reliable?", "Why"], "rows": [["Customer asks for a person", "Yes: escalate at once", "An explicit trigger"], ["Policy exception or gap", "Yes: escalate", "Needs a human decision"], ["Agent cannot make progress", "Yes: escalate", "Stuck is a trigger"], ["Customer sounds upset", "No", "Sentiment is not complexity"], ["Agent says it is unsure", "No", "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "escalate when needed", "means": "The cause: no criteria. The fix is to write them down and show examples: <b>[[3]]</b>."}, {"quote": "Upset customers with simple problems, such as one missing item, are often handed to a person", "means": "Mood is already driving escalation. A sentiment check (<b>[[2]]</b>) makes that worse."}, {"quote": "Calm customers who ask for something policy does not cover are usually handled by the agent alone", "means": "The decider: the hard cases look calm and feel easy, so neither mood nor self-rated confidence (<b>[[0]]</b>) catches them."}, {"quote": "Which change best improves when the agent escalates?", "means": "The proportionate fix is in the prompt. A classifier (<b>[[1]]</b>) is a bigger build."}], "rule": "Escalating the wrong cases? Explicit criteria plus examples, not sentiment or self-rated confidence.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Sentiment-based escalation and self-reported confidence are unreliable proxies for complexity."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}, {"id": "tg-who-escalates-3", "sid": "s1", "domain": "D5 · Context Management & Reliability", "obj": "5.2 Several customer matches", "trap": "Unreliable proxy", "fam": 6, "select": 1, "question": "A customer writes in from a new email address and gives only their name. <code>get_customer</code> returns two active accounts with that name. The agent picks the account with the most recent order and carries on. Last week this sent a refund to the wrong customer. How should the agent handle a lookup that returns <b>more than one customer</b>?", "options": [{"t": "Escalate each lookup with several matches to a person, who checks which account it is.", "why": "Works, but costs resolution. A person checks what the customer could confirm in one reply, and the queue grows.", "trap": "escalates what it could resolve"}, {"t": "Ask the customer for an identifier, such as a box number or postcode, first.", "why": "Best. The customer knows which account is theirs. One question settles it, and the agent can then resolve the case itself."}, {"t": "Pick the match whose postcode and recent boxes fit the chat best, and note the choice.", "why": "Works, but still a guess. Better clues pick right more often, and a wrong pick still refunds a stranger.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}, {"t": "Have Claude rate how sure it is of the match, and ask the customer when it is unsure.", "why": "Works, but an unreliable signal. Claude was already sure enough to refund the wrong account.", "trap": "unreliable proxy"}], "answer": [1], "runnerUp": 2, "decider": "get_customer returns two active accounts with that name.", "explanation": "When a lookup returns several customers, ask the customer for an identifier rather than choosing by heuristic. A guess, however good, sometimes acts on the wrong account.", "eli5": {"question": "Two people called Sam Lee have parcels at the post office. The clerk hands over the newer parcel to whoever says “Sam Lee”.", "answer": "The clerk should ask “what's your postcode?” One question, and the right Sam gets the right parcel. <b>Rule: several matches → ask for an identifier, don't pick.</b>", "options": ["Calling a supervisor works, but the customer could answer it in one line.", "Correct. Ask for something only the right Sam knows.", "A smarter guess is still a guess.", "The clerk felt sure last time too."]}, "deep": {"premise": "It is tempting to think <b>a smarter rule can pick the right account</b>. Any rule is a proxy, and the cost of a wrong pick is a refund to a stranger. The customer has the missing fact, so ask them.", "diagram": "get_customer(\"Priya Shah\") → 2 accounts\n  ask: box number or postcode? → 1 account → resolve", "mapTitle": "Unsure who or what? Ask, guess, or escalate?", "map": {"head": ["Situation", "Agent's move", "Why"], "rows": [["Several customer matches", "Ask for an identifier", "The customer knows; don't guess"], ["Request is unclear", "Ask a clarifying question", "Cheap and exact"], ["Policy exception or gap", "Escalate", "Needs a human decision"], ["Customer asks for a person", "Escalate at once", "Explicit trigger"], ["Agent feels unsure", "Not a trigger by itself", "Self-rated confidence is unreliable"]]}, "evidence": [{"quote": "get_customer returns two active accounts with that name", "means": "The decider: the lookup is ambiguous, and both accounts are live."}, {"quote": "The agent picks the account with the most recent order", "means": "A heuristic. Better clues (<b>[[2]]</b>) or a confidence rating (<b>[[3]]</b>) are still guesses."}, {"quote": "Last week this sent a refund to the wrong customer", "means": "A wrong guess costs money, so guessing better is not enough."}, {"quote": "How should the agent handle a lookup that returns more than one customer?", "means": "Ask the customer, then resolve it: <b>[[1]]</b>. Escalating (<b>[[0]]</b>) hands over a case one question would settle."}], "rule": "Several matches? Ask the customer for an identifier; never pick by heuristic.", "guide": {"obj": "5.2 Escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns", "quote": "Multiple customer matches → ask for identifiers (not heuristic selection)."}}, "source": "Trap gym"}]};
